window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-08-20T05:26:02.105240Z","count":241,"pages":[{"title":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","url":"/self-improvement-that-works/","description":"Self-improvement that works is usually less glamorous than self-help marketing suggests.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? Self improvement that works is usually less glamorous than self help marketing suggests. Self improvement that works is usually less glamorous than self help marketing suggests. The strongest evidence does not point to a particular morning routine, personality type or motivational philosophy. Self Improvement That Works self improvement that works Introduction That matters because motivation is unreliable. A person can sincerely want to exercise , study, save money or become more organised while still doing none of those things consistently. Effective self improvement therefore treats intention as the beginning of change rather than the mechanism that completes it. The useful question is not merely “How badly do I want this?” but “What will make the right action more likely when the moment arrives?” Illustrative overview Turn goals into actions Goals are useful, but they work best when they tell you what to do rather than merely describing the person you hope to become. Decades of goal setting research generally find advantages for appropriately challenging, clearly defined goals over vague instructions such as “do your best”, although effects vary with the task and context. More recent work also cautions against assuming that specificity or extreme difficulty is automatically better; for unfamiliar or complex tasks, learning goals and realistic challenge may be more useful than rigid performance targets. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... A useful goal therefore has two levels. First comes the outcome: “improve my fitness”, “finish the qualification” or “reduce unnecessary spending”. Underneath it comes a controllable behaviour: “walk for 25 minutes after lunch on weekdays”, “complete 30 minutes of practice before opening social media” or “review tomorrow’s purchases before placing an order”. Outcomes provide direction; behaviours provide something you can actually execute. One particularly well supported technique is the implementation intention : an if–then plan linking a situation to a response. Instead of “I will exercise more”, the plan becomes: “If I finish work on Monday, Wednesday or Friday, then I will change immediately and walk to the gym.” A classic meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found that these plans improved goal attainment with a medium to large average effect, although later work makes clear that they help most when the underlying goal is genuinely wanted. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The principle is simple: make fewer decisions at the moment when action is required. Decide beforehand. Build a system that does not depend on willpower People often frame inconsistency as a character problem: “I need more discipline.” Behavioural research suggests a more useful interpretation. Actions are influenced heavily by cues, friction, convenience, repetition and immediate rewards . A systematic review of digital habit interventions found that commonly used techniques included prompts and cues, goal setting, self monitoring, feedback and social support. Laboratory research also shows how repeated responses to recurring cues can develop stronger cue–response associations, allowing behaviour to become less dependent on deliberate motivation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That gives environmental design an important role in self improvement. Instead of repeatedly trying to overpower temptation, change what happens before the choice. Put running shoes beside the door rather than in a cupboard. Keep the book you intend to read on the sofa and the distracting device elsewhere. Open the document you need for tomorrow’s work before finishing today. Remove saved payment details from a shopping site if impulsive buying is the problem. Prepare ingredients in advance if cooking well regularly breaks down at the “what shall I eat?” stage. None of these measures guarantees success. Their value is that they reduce the number of occasions on which success requires an unusually motivated version of you. Immediate rewards can help as well. In a field experiment involving 6,792 participants, researchers tested “temptation bundling”: pairing exercise with enjoyable audiobooks. Giving participants audiobooks and encouraging the pairing increased weekly exercise during the intervention, with smaller effects persisting afterwards. An earlier experiment found strong initial increases in gym attendance from the same basic idea, although the effect declined over time. The broader lesson is not that everyone needs an audiobook; it is that delayed benefit behaviours become easier when some reward arrives now rather than months later. [ScienceDirect] Add an immediate reward where appropriate. Open source on sciencedirect.com. Explanatory illustration 1 Measure behaviour, not intentions One of the clearest findings in self regulation research is that tracking progress can improve results. A meta analysis of 138 studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring produced a meaningful improvement in goal attainment. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded or reported to others. [PubMed] nih.gov Pub Med Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedDoes monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed Tracking works partly because memory is a poor management system. “I think I exercised quite a lot” is difficult to act on. “I planned three sessions and completed one” identifies a problem. The same applies to spending, study time, applications, reading, sleep routines or almost any other repeated behaviour. Good tracking is deliberately small. Record the measure that changes your next decision rather than constructing an elaborate personal dashboard. Depending on the goal, that might simply be: sessions completed; pages or problems finished; days on which the target behaviour occurred; money spent in one problem category; bedtime and wake time; minutes of deliberate practice. Feedback can make monitoring more useful. A 2023 systematic review and meta analysis examining self monitoring interventions found that adding feedback improved physical activity outcomes, although the evidence was heterogeneous and researchers could not identify one universally superior feedback format. A broader meta review similarly found support for tools such as goal setting, monitoring and personalised feedback, while warning that none works consistently for every behaviour or population. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is an important corrective to self help certainty: evidence supports mechanisms, not magic formulas. Meet Wendy Wood, Author of Good Habits, Bad Habits Channel: USC Price Open on YouTube Give habits enough time to form Popular advice has often claimed that a habit takes 21 days to form. Research does not support a universal deadline. A 2024 systematic review of health related habit formation found substantial variation between people and behaviours. Across the small number of studies that measured time directly, medians were about 59–66 days, while individual estimates ranged from four to 335 days; some more complex behaviours took considerably longer. The review also noted significant limitations in the evidence, including a relatively small literature and substantial risk of bias in many studies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The influential earlier study behind the widely quoted 66 day figure found a range of 18 to 254 days and, importantly, reported that missing one opportunity did not materially derail habit formation. Consistency mattered; perfection did not. [OpenAIRE Explore] explore.openaire.eu Open source on openaire.eu. This changes how a useful self improvement plan should be judged. A habit is not “failing” because it still requires effort after three weeks. Nor does one missed workout, study session or early bedtime reset progress to zero. A better strategy is to make the repeated action modest enough to survive ordinary life. “Read ten pages after breakfast” is usually more sustainable than “become someone who reads two hours every day”. Once a behaviour is reliably happening, it can be expanded. When it repeatedly fails, changing the cue, time, difficulty or environment is often more productive than increasing self criticism. Explanatory illustration 3 Protect the capacity to improve Behavioural techniques have"},{"title":"Sleep and Mood","url":"/sleep-and-mood/","description":"Sleep loss can reduce positive mood and increase negative mood, changing the emotional conditions in which habits are attempted.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Sleep and Mood How Poor Sleep Changes the Mood Behind Your Habits Better Habits Sleep and Mood Sleep and Mood How Poor Sleep Changes the Mood Behind Your Habits Sleep loss can reduce positive mood and increase negative mood, changing the emotional conditions in which habits are attempted. Sleep loss can reduce positive mood and increase negative mood, changing the emotional conditions in which habits are attempted. Self Improvement That Works governance case family dataset evidence What sleep loss meta analyses report Positive and negative mood effects Planning demanding habits around recovery self improvement that works sleep and mood Introduction Poor sleep changes more than alertness. It changes the emotional conditions in which behaviour change has to happen. Experimental research shows a particularly consistent reduction in positive affect — feelings such as enthusiasm, attentiveness and energy — after sleep loss. Negative mood can also increase, but that effect is less uniform and depends more on how sleep is lost and how mood is measured. The most comprehensive recent meta analysis, covering 154 studies and 5,717 participants, found that total sleep deprivation, partial sleep restriction and sleep fragmentation all reduced positive affect, while effects on negative affect were more mixed. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep loss and emotion: A systematic review and meta analysis of over 50 years of experimental research PubMedDecember 21, 2023... Published: December 21, 2023 Illustrative overview That distinction matters for self improvement. A tired person may not simply feel “worse”. They may feel less interested, less energised and less willing to invest effort in something whose benefits arrive later. Habits still have to be chosen and executed under those conditions. Sleep therefore belongs inside the design of behaviour change: demanding actions should not be planned as though mood, motivation and perceived effort remain constant from one day to the next. What sleep loss meta analyses actually report Two major quantitative reviews give the clearest picture, and together they show why “lack of sleep makes you grumpy” is too crude a summary. A 2021 meta analysis combined 241 effect sizes from 64 studies examining sleep deprivation or sleep restriction. Sleep loss produced a large reduction in positive mood (Hedges’ g = −0.94) and a moderate increase in negative mood (g = 0.45). Total sleep deprivation produced a larger increase in negative mood than partial restriction. The same review also found a small reduction in adaptive emotion regulation, although those emotion regulation studies were concentrated in younger participants, which limits how confidently that result can be generalised to adults. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effect of sleep deprivation and restriction on mood, emotion, and emotion regulation: three meta analyses in one PubMedJune 1... A larger preregistered meta analysis published in Psychological Bulletin in 2024 widened the evidence base to 1,338 effect sizes from 154 experiments involving people aged 7 to 79. Across total deprivation, restricted sleep and fragmented sleep, positive affect consistently fell, with standardised mean differences ranging from −0.27 to −1.14. Anxiety symptoms increased, and emotional arousal in response to stimuli was blunted. By contrast, effects on negative affect and depressive symptoms varied according to the sleep manipulation and other study characteristics. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep loss and emotion: A systematic review and meta analysis of over 50 years of experimental research PubMedDecember 21, 2023... Published: December 21, 2023 The important point is therefore asymmetric. Losing positive mood is one of the most dependable emotional consequences of losing sleep. Becoming markedly more negative is possible, but not inevitable. A person trying to change behaviour may consequently encounter a quieter problem than obvious irritability: exercise seems less appealing, studying feels flatter, meal preparation has less pull, and a useful activity offers less immediate emotional reward. This asymmetry is also visible in controlled laboratory datasets. Research comparing chronic sleep restriction, total sleep deprivation and deliberately mistimed sleep found systematic reductions in attentiveness, joviality, self assurance and other positive mood states. Negative moods changed less consistently, although hostility and general negative affect rose under some conditions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Contrasting Effects of Sleep Restriction, Total Sleep Deprivation, and Sleep Timing on Positive and Negative Affect PubMedAugust... These estimates should not be treated as a formula for predicting how one individual will feel after one poor night. The studies differ substantially in sleep schedules, participant ages, laboratory conditions, measurement instruments and timing of mood assessments, and meta analyses report considerable heterogeneity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. What they establish more securely is the direction of the population level risk: insufficient sleep tends to make desirable emotional states harder to sustain. Explanatory illustration 1 Why losing positive mood can undermine a habit Positive affect is not simply a pleasant extra added after self regulation has succeeded. It helps determine whether activities feel worth approaching in the first place. That makes the reduction in positive mood especially relevant to habits that require initiation, concentration or repeated effort before they become automatic. Consider a person who intends to spend 45 minutes revising after work. On a well rested day, the task may still be demanding, but interest, attentiveness and confidence help offset its cost. After curtailed sleep, the objective task can be identical while its subjective economics have changed: exerting effort feels more expensive and the prospective reward may feel less motivating. Experimental work supports that interpretation. In one study of 77 healthy young adults, total sleep deprivation increased effort discounting : participants became more inclined to choose smaller rewards that required less effort rather than larger rewards requiring more work. Their preference for immediate rather than delayed rewards did not change in the same way. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 Another experiment found that sleep deprivation increased the perceived cost of sustained attentional effort. Participants performed worse on a vigilance task, and sleep loss further reduced the value they assigned to rewards that required longer periods of attention. Higher incentives could partly improve performance, showing that tired performance reflects an interaction between impaired capacity and willingness to allocate effort rather than a simple on/off loss of ability. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Partial restriction can produce a similar pattern. In an experiment in which 24 healthy young adults received either three nights with three hours of sleep opportunity or three nights with nine hours, restriction reduced willingness to expend cognitive effort for reward. Interestingly, willingness to expend physical effort was not significantly reduced. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation That finding argues against describing tiredness as a universal collapse in motivation. Behaviour change is more likely to suffer selectively. A mentally demanding evening habit — planning finances, learning a language, writing, studying or making a complicated food choice — may become disproportionately difficult even when the person remains capable of carrying out simpler or more physical actions. The Link Between Sleep and Mental Health (With Psychiatrist Sleep Hygiene Tips) Channel: Priory Open on YouTube A bad night can change how the day feels Laboratory deprivation studies establish causality more cleanly, but ordinary life rarely involves staying awake for 24 or 40 hours. Everyday sleep varies in smaller increments, often alongside work, relationships and other stressors. Naturalistic research helps bridge that gap. A systematic review of prospective daily life studies found reciprocal associations between subjective sleep and next day mood: sleep quality, sleep duration and time taken to fall asleep were linked with subsequent affect, while mood could also predict later sleep. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2024 systematic review of 121 studies using digital technologies and wearables similarly found broadly bidirectional sleep–affect relationships, but reported that sleep was more often"},{"title":"Missed Days","url":"/missed-days/","description":"Missing one opportunity does not erase habit formation, so recovery matters more than treating a lapse as a total reset.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Missed Days Does Missing One Day Ruin a Habit? Better Habits Missed Days Missed Days Does Missing One Day Ruin a Habit? Missing one opportunity does not erase habit formation, so recovery matters more than treating a lapse as a total reset. Missing one opportunity does not erase habit formation, so recovery matters more than treating a lapse as a total reset. Self Improvement That Works evidence decision cluster event time window What habit research found about lapses Why perfection is the wrong standard Returning at the next opportunity self improvement that works missed days Introduction Missing one day does not mean a habit has gone back to zero. The clearest direct evidence comes from a 12 week habit formation study in which people repeated a chosen eating, drinking or activity behaviour in the same daily context. Habit automaticity generally strengthened with repetition, and missing a single opportunity did not materially alter that growth process . What did matter was the broader pattern: people who performed the behaviour more consistently were more likely to develop the expected habit trajectory. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Illustrative overview That distinction is important. Habit formation is not best understood as an unbroken streak in which every successful day earns a permanent point and one failure deletes the score. It is a gradual learning process in which repeated responses become increasingly associated with recurring cues. A lapse costs an opportunity for reinforcement, but the evidence does not support treating one missed occasion as a complete reset. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 The practical priority after a missed day is therefore not to reconstruct a perfect streak. It is to return to the intended behaviour at the next suitable opportunity, preferably in the familiar context that has been training the habit . Explanatory illustration 3 What habit research found about lapses The study behind the widely repeated claim about missed days was conducted by Phillippa Lally and colleagues at University College London. Ninety six volunteers selected a new daily behaviour, such as eating fruit, drinking water or exercising, and linked it to a recurring situation such as after breakfast. They recorded both their performance and their experience of automaticity for 12 weeks. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers found that automaticity tended to rise rapidly at first and then increase more slowly towards a personal plateau. Among participants for whom the growth curve could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of that plateau varied enormously, from 18 to 254 days. The same study found that omitting one opportunity did not materially affect the habit formation process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That finding is stronger than the popular idea that missing one day automatically “breaks” a habit, but it should not be stretched into the opposite claim that missed days never matter. Lally herself summarised the result more carefully: one missed opportunity was not significant, whereas people who were very inconsistent generally did not succeed in developing habits. The study could not establish an exact percentage of successful repetitions that everyone must achieve. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College London... More recent evidence reinforces that qualification. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis covering 20 experimental or quasi experimental studies and 2,601 participants concluded that habit development varies substantially between people and behaviours. Repetition remains a central determinant, while stable contexts appear to support stronger habits; the review also noted that researchers still do not know precisely how much consistency is necessary. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is also some evidence that the length and pattern of interruptions may matter. In a six week app based study of study habits, 530 of 2,483 repetitions were preceded by a pause of at least a day. In that dataset, the length of the pause did not significantly predict automaticity, although pauses longer than one day were uncommon, limiting what can be concluded about longer breaks . A later analysis involving two habit building datasets reported a negative association between time since the previous repetition and automaticity, illustrating that the science is not precise enough to declare every interruption harmless. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)App Based Habit Building Reduces Motivational Impairments During Studying – An Event Sampling Study PMCFebruary 7... The sensible interpretation is therefore narrower and more useful: a single lapse is compatible with continued habit formation; repeated or prolonged inconsistency can slow or undermine it. Explanatory illustration 1 Why perfection is the wrong standard A streak counts consecutive performances. A habit, by contrast, is usually defined in behavioural research by the growing tendency for a cue or context to prompt a response automatically. Those are related concepts, but they are not the same measurement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Imagine that someone has walked for 20 minutes after lunch on 24 of the past 27 working days. Missing Tuesday because of an appointment changes the streak from perhaps ten days to zero, but it does not erase the previous repetitions linking finishing lunch with going for a walk . Treating the numerical streak as though it were the underlying psychological habit exaggerates what one lapse means. The shape of habit growth helps explain why. Lally's work found diminishing gains from successive repetitions: early repetitions tended to produce larger increases in automaticity, while later ones added progressively less as the behaviour approached a plateau. Habit strength therefore did not behave like a simple counter that increased by one each day and returned to zero after an omission. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Later work also shows why the setting of repetitions matters. In one 2022 study, 95 students deliberately formed study habits over six weeks. Participants assigned to repeat their behaviour in a stable place and time developed greater automaticity and achieved more of their repetition goals than those instructed to keep changing their context. A second dataset involving 218 users and 308 habits showed the same general association between context stability, automaticity and performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... That changes how a missed day should be interpreted. The key question is not “Is my streak still intact?” but “Will I continue reinforcing the same useful cue–response relationship?” If the usual cue appears tomorrow and the behaviour resumes, the learning process can continue. If the lapse becomes the beginning of weeks of irregular performance, the problem is no longer one missed day but the loss of consistent repetition. There is also a behavioural reason to avoid turning a lapse into a verdict on personal discipline. Research on relapse in other behaviour change settings distinguishes a lapse , a temporary departure from the intended behaviour, from a sustained return to an unwanted pattern. The evidence comes especially from addiction research, so it should not be treated as direct proof about ordinary habits; nevertheless, it demonstrates an important general point: progression from an initial lapse to continuing failure is not inevitable. In smoking research, people who engaged in restorative coping after an initial lapse were less likely to lapse again on the same day, while feeling like giving up predicted faster progression to another lapse. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For everyday self improvement, this argues against the familiar “I have already ruined it, so today no longer counts”"},{"title":"If Then Plans","url":"/if-then-plans/","description":"If-then plans connect a clear situation with a prepared response so less decision-making is needed when action is due.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"If Then Plans Why If Then Plans Make Goals Easier to Start Better Habits If Then Plans If Then Plans Why If Then Plans Make Goals Easier to Start If then plans connect a clear situation with a prepared response so less decision making is needed when action is due. If then plans connect a clear situation with a prepared response so less decision making is needed when action is due. Self Improvement That Works evidence pillar policy intervention How implementation intentions work Writing useful situation response plans When they are most likely to help self improvement that works if then plans Introduction An if then plan decides in advance what you will do when a recognisable situation occurs: “If X happens, then I will do Y.” Psychologists call this an implementation intention . Unlike a goal such as “ exercise more” or “study tonight”, it links a particular cue to a particular response, reducing the need to remember, deliberate and choose again when the moment for action arrives. Illustrative overview This is one of the better supported techniques for turning an existing intention into behaviour. A landmark meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found a medium to large average improvement in goal attainment, although effects differ considerably across behaviours, populations and plan designs. The important qualification is that implementation intentions are primarily an action control technique , not a way of creating motivation: they work best when someone already has a meaningful goal and needs help acting on it. The practical target, then, is quite specific. If you know what you want to do but repeatedly miss the right moment, hesitate when it arrives or get pulled into an alternative behaviour, an if then plan can pre decide the next move. How implementation intentions work Consider the difference between these two statements: Goal: “I intend to work on my course this evening.” Implementation intention: “If I put my dinner plate in the dishwasher, then I will sit at the desk and complete the next practice exercise.” The first establishes an aim. The second identifies a future situation and links it to an executable response. That distinction matters because intending to act and initiating action are separate problems. In the 2006 meta analysis, implementation intentions were associated not only with greater overall goal attainment but with better initiation of intended behaviour and greater resistance to distractions and competing responses. Research into the mechanism suggests that planning changes what happens when the critical situation appears. The chosen cue becomes more mentally accessible, making it easier to notice, and the association between that cue and the intended response becomes stronger. Experiments by Thomas Webb and Paschal Sheeran found that both processes helped explain improved goal achievement after people formed implementation intentions. This is sometimes described as strategic automaticity . The behaviour has been chosen deliberately, but part of its later initiation is handed over to the cue. Instead of arriving at the planned moment and reopening the entire decision — Should I do it now? Could I do it later? What exactly should I start with? — encountering the cue can prompt the prepared response more directly. Experimental studies have found faster or more effective initiation even when participants were simultaneously dealing with substantial cognitive demands. That does not mean an if then plan turns someone into an unthinking robot. It means that one decision is shifted from a difficult future moment to an earlier moment when it can be made deliberately. Explanatory illustration 1 It is not mainly a motivation boost A useful feature of the research is that it separates implementation intentions from techniques designed to strengthen enthusiasm or confidence. A meta analysis of 66 tests found negligible effects of forming implementation intentions on the strength of people's goal intentions or their self efficacy. In a separate experiment, improved performance was instead mediated by greater accessibility of the planned cue and a stronger cue response connection. That explains both the technique's value and its limitation. If the problem is “I want to act but I keep failing to do so at the relevant moment” , planning directly addresses the bottleneck. If the real problem is “I do not actually want this goal enough to pursue it” , a more precise cue may accomplish little. Research supports that distinction. In experiments examining the interaction between goals and implementation intentions, if then plans improved attainment when the underlying goal intention was strong or active, but not when it was weak. Studies of physical activity have likewise identified existing intention and perceived ability to act as important conditions for effectiveness. Writing useful situation response plans The formula is simple, but not every sentence containing “if” and “then” is equally useful. The plan has to specify a situation you can recognise and a response you can actually perform. Compare: Vague: “If I have some spare time, then I will study.” Usable: “If I finish breakfast on Saturday, then I will open the statistics workbook and complete the next five questions.” The second plan removes several decisions. The trigger is observable; the starting action is clear; and success does not depend on interpreting an indefinite phrase such as “some spare time”. A practical way to construct a plan is: 1. Start with a goal you genuinely intend to pursue. The plan is there to execute the goal, not to manufacture commitment to it. 2. Identify the critical moment. Find the situation where action should begin, or where it normally goes wrong. 3. Choose a recognisable cue. A time, place, preceding action, event or clearly detectable obstacle usually works better than an indistinct state such as “when I feel motivated”. 4. Specify an executable response. “Be productive” is not a useful response; “open the draft and write the next paragraph” is. 5. Link the two explicitly. State the plan as “If X occurs, then I will Y.” 6. Test it against real life. If the cue rarely occurs, is routinely missed or does not leave enough time for the response, revise the plan rather than treating failure as proof that the technique does not work. Evidence from applied studies suggests that these details matter. In a study of 764 people attempting to stop smoking, more specific coping plans predicted better abstinence outcomes than relatively unspecific plans; importantly, extremely detailed plans did not outperform moderately specific ones. The useful standard is therefore specific enough to guide action , not maximum possible detail. The explicit cue response link also appears to matter. In a randomised study of 557 young adults, an intervention using the if then format increased fruit and vegetable intake by about 0.50 portions, compared with 0.31 portions for a more general planning condition and virtually no change in the control group. The result does not mean every if then intervention will produce that magnitude of change, but it illustrates why implementation intentions are more than generic advice to “make a plan”. Implementation Intentions Increase Goal Completion by 40% Channel: Sam Martin Peak Performance Open on YouTube Choose cues that will actually be noticed A plan cannot trigger reliably if its “if” condition is difficult to detect. Good cues tend to be distinctive enough to recognise and closely connected to the moment when the response is possible . For example: “If it is sometime after work, I will exercise” leaves a wide and ambiguous window. “If I close my work laptop on Monday, Wednesday or Friday, I will put on my trainers” identifies a concrete transition. “If I feel distracted, I will concentrate” requires a vague internal judgement. “If I open a social media site while the report document is on screen, I will close the site and write for five minutes” identifies an observable event and response. The research supports the underlying principle rather than a universal requirement to use clock times. Prospective memory experiments show that implementation intentions can improve detection of event based opportunities, while mechanism studies find that representing the relevant situation strengthens its accessibility. The best cue is therefore not necessarily the most precise cue imaginable. It is the cue most likely to occur at a moment when the planned response can realistically follow . Explanatory illustration 2 Plan for opportunities and obstacles Implementation intentions can solve two different timing problems. An opportunity plan prepares a response for a favourable moment: If I get off the train at my home station, then I will walk home instead of taking the bus. An obstacle plan prepares a response for something that normally disrupts progress: If I notice that I have opened social media while writing,"},{"title":"Precommitment","url":"/precommitment/","description":"Deciding in advance can protect a goal by shrinking the number of choices you must make in a tempting or tiring moment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Precommitment Make the Decision Before Temptation Arrives Better Habits Precommitment Precommitment Make the Decision Before Temptation Arrives Deciding in advance can protect a goal by shrinking the number of choices you must make in a tempting or tiring moment. Deciding in advance can protect a goal by shrinking the number of choices you must make in a tempting or tiring moment. Self Improvement That Works implementation pillar mechanism Why in the moment choices are fragile Ways to precommit without overcomplicating life Matching precommitment to the problem self improvement that works precommitment Introduction Precommitment is a way of protecting a goal by making an important decision before temptation, fatigue or procrastination can distort it. Rather than planning to resist an unwanted choice later, you change the situation now: block the distracting option, lock away the money, impose a deadline, automate the desired action or attach a consequence to backing out. In behavioural research, the defining feature is that your calmer present self deliberately restricts what your future self can do. Illustrative overview The attraction is straightforward. Many self improvement failures are not failures of knowledge: people already know they would rather save than impulse buy, work than scroll, or exercise than postpone. The difficulty is that the balance of incentives changes when an immediate reward becomes available. Precommitment moves the decisive moment to an earlier point, when the long term goal has more influence. Experimental and field evidence shows that this can improve behaviour, although commitment devices vary greatly in effectiveness and can become counterproductive when they remove flexibility that turns out to be genuinely useful. Present Bias Explained (Why We Procrastinate and Avoid Saving) Channel: PlainSense Open on YouTube Why in the moment choices are fragile Imagine deciding on Sunday evening that you will study for an hour after work on Monday. At 7 pm on Monday, however, studying is no longer being compared abstractly with the benefits of finishing a qualification. It is being compared with a sofa, takeaway food, messages and immediately entertaining content. The options have not merely become more concrete; their rewards occur at different times. Economists describe one contributor to this problem as present bias : immediate costs and rewards can receive disproportionately high weight compared with future ones. The strength of present bias varies considerably between people, studies and types of reward, so it should not be treated as a universal explanation for every lapse. But the broader pattern it describes is familiar: a choice that looks worthwhile in advance can lose against a competing option once that option becomes immediately available. Precommitment changes this contest. Instead of asking your future self to choose the long term option while temptation remains available, you remove or penalise the alternative in advance. In laboratory research comparing these strategies directly, participants were more successful at choosing larger delayed rewards when they could make a binding advance choice than when they had to continue resisting a smaller immediate reward during the waiting period. The study therefore distinguished two routes to self control : resisting temptation after it appears and preventing the tempting decision from arising in the first place . That distinction is more important than it initially sounds. An implementation intention such as “if it is 7 pm, I will start studying” specifies what you intend to do. A precommitment such as “the distracting sites will be inaccessible from 7 to 8 pm” alters what you will be able to choose. The two methods can complement one another, but they solve different problems. Precommitment can also reduce the mental cost of repeatedly rejecting an attractive option. Research on temptation has found that people sometimes value menus from which tempting alternatives have already been removed, consistent with the idea that simply having to reject an option carries a subjective cost. In everyday terms, it may be easier not to have biscuits in the cupboard than to conduct a fresh internal debate each time you walk into the kitchen. Explanatory illustration 1 Ways to precommit without overcomplicating life A useful commitment device does not need to involve contracts or money. Its essential job is to make a predictable future failure harder to enact. The strongest design depends on why the behaviour normally breaks down. Remove access to the competing option. If the same temptation repeatedly wins, eliminating it during the vulnerable period is the most literal form of precommitment. A person who loses an evening to entertainment sites might schedule an automatic block before the evening begins. Someone trying not to buy convenience food might simply avoid bringing it home. Someone prone to online impulse purchases might remove stored card details or access to shopping apps. These measures work by shrinking the future choice set rather than strengthening an intention. The principle has been tested experimentally in digital environments. In a 2023 experiment, participants doing a tedious productivity task could pay to remove access to tempting YouTube content. Researchers found both undercommitment and overcommitment: some people restricted themselves unnecessarily, but more underestimated how costly temptation would prove, and the losses associated with undercommitting were larger overall. That result is a useful corrective to two simplistic views: neither “you should always lock yourself down” nor “you should just learn discipline” fits the evidence particularly well. Make future money unavailable for impulsive use. Savings provides unusually clear field evidence because commitment can be built into the account itself. In a randomised study in the Philippines, bank customers were offered an account in which they chose a savings target or date and restricted their ability to withdraw until the condition was met. About 28% of those offered the product opened one. After a year, assignment to the treatment increased average savings balances by 81% relative to the control group. The mechanism was not motivational messaging: the product deliberately reduced liquidity. The everyday version need not be so restrictive. Moving money automatically on payday into an account that is deliberately inconvenient to access is a milder form. The important design feature is timing: the saving decision is executed before the money competes with dozens of attractive purchases. Use deadlines when delay is the temptation. Procrastination differs from an obvious vice because the tempting choice is usually “not yet”. A deadline can create a cost for repeated postponement and therefore act as a commitment device. A well known 2002 study by Dan Ariely and Klaus Wertenbroch found that people voluntarily imposed costly deadlines on themselves and that these could improve performance, although externally imposed evenly spaced deadlines performed better. That finding became an influential example of self control through precommitment. It now requires an important qualification. A preregistered replication published in Psychological Science in July 2026 did not reproduce the original Study 2 result: changing deadline structures produced negligible differences on the main performance measures, and evenly spaced deadlines did not emerge as especially effective. This does not show that deadlines never help procrastination, but it does weaken the case for treating one famous experiment as definitive evidence that self imposed scheduling reliably solves the problem. For practical purposes, this suggests using deadlines because they change a real constraint — a submission, booking, meeting or promised delivery — rather than because merely writing an arbitrary date in a diary is expected to transform behaviour. Put something at stake. A harder commitment device makes failure costly. You might make a deposit that is returned only if you complete a behaviour, arrange accountability where a missed action has a predetermined consequence, or enter a structured commitment contract. The approach has produced substantial effects in some settings. In a randomised smoking cessation study in the Philippines, smokers could deposit their own money for six months and recover it only by passing a biochemical test showing they had stopped smoking. Only 11% of smokers offered the programme took it up, but those offered it were about three percentage points more likely than controls to pass the six month test, and the effect persisted in surprise testing at 12 months. The low uptake is as informative as the positive effect: a demanding device can work for some people while being unattractive to most. More recent exercise research reaches a similar conclusion about consequences. A 2024 randomised study"},{"title":"Study Systems","url":"/study-systems/","description":"A study routine is easier to protect when practice has a fixed cue and distracting devices are made less convenient.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Study Systems Make Studying the Default Before Distraction Starts Better Habits Study Systems Study Systems Make Studying the Default Before Distraction Starts A study routine is easier to protect when practice has a fixed cue and distracting devices are made less convenient. A study routine is easier to protect when practice has a fixed cue and distracting devices are made less convenient. Self Improvement That Works critique risk example portfolio population context Setting a repeatable study trigger Removing digital friction in reverse Tracking practice that actually happened self improvement that works study systems Introduction A study routine that starts before social media works by changing the order of the first decision. Instead of opening a feed and later trying to disengage, the learner gives studying the first reliable cue: after breakfast, after arriving at the library, or after sitting at a particular desk, a defined study action happens before social media is opened. The strongest case for this approach is not that phones uniquely destroy attention, but that stable cues , good advance plans, reduced access to competing activities and simple self monitoring can make intended behaviour easier to repeat. Research on university students building study habits found greater automaticity and goal attainment when studying occurred in a stable context rather than at continually changing times and places. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Illustrative overview The important qualification is that none of these ingredients is magic. Studies of implementation intentions and smartphone distraction produce mixed results, and simply putting a phone out of sight cannot compensate for ineffective learning methods. The useful system is therefore modest: create a repeatable trigger, make distraction slightly less convenient during the opening study block, record whether the practice actually happened, and adapt the routine from observed behaviour rather than good intentions. Setting a repeatable study trigger The most useful cue is one that already occurs predictably. “Study more in the mornings” still requires a decision; “after I put my breakfast plate in the sink, I sit at the desk and complete 20 minutes of vocabulary practice before opening social media” specifies the situation and the response. This is close to an implementation intention , an if–then plan designed to connect a recognised situation with a preselected action. Evidence supports the underlying idea, but with important limits. In a six week study involving 95 university students and 2,482 repetitions of newly chosen study habits, participants were instructed either to keep the time and location relatively stable or to vary them. Stable contexts were associated with stronger automaticity and better attainment of the students’ habit repetition goals. A second dataset from a habit building app showed the same broad pattern. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 This matters for a “study before social media” routine because the cue can gradually become associated with opening the book, flashcards or problem set rather than with checking a feed. Recent experimental work points in the same direction while showing that plan quality matters. A 2025 micro randomised trial used mobile prompts to help young learners specify when and where they would study vocabulary. The researchers found that planning could promote studying, but benefits depended substantially on whether the learners had made workable, sufficiently specific plans. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Planning promotes studying – The higher the plan quality the better: A micro randomized trial ScienceDirectDecember 1, 2025... Published: December 1, 2025 The lesson is more precise than “make a plan”: a plan needs a cue the learner will actually encounter and an action that can start immediately. A practical trigger might therefore be: 1. Cue: “When I sit down with breakfast finished at 7.30 on a weekday…” 2. Opening action: “…I put my phone on the shelf, open the prepared question set and answer the first five questions.” 3. Minimum completion rule: “Social media stays unopened until the first 25 minute block is finished.” The opening action should be concrete. “Revise chemistry” leaves the brain another planning problem; “complete questions 1–5 from yesterday’s acid base set” already contains a starting move. A short block also lowers the stakes. The objective is initially to establish the sequence — cue, study, then optional social media — rather than to prove heroic endurance. Implementation intentions should not be oversold, however. In a three week experiment with 177 undergraduates, neither SMART goal exercises nor implementation intentions significantly reduced academic procrastination. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Academic procrastination and goal accomplishment: A combined experimental and individual differences investigation Science... A newer 2026 randomised trial of 81 undergraduates found more encouraging results when implementation intentions were combined with “mental contrasting”, a process in which people consider a desired outcome alongside the obstacles likely to interfere with it: the combined intervention reduced task aversiveness and increased willingness to initiate academic work relative to a positive thinking control. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Beyond positive thinking: A randomized trial of mental contrasting with implementation intentions to curb academic procrasti... Those findings argue against treating an if–then sentence as a behavioural spell. The plan is most useful as part of a wider system. If the student’s predictable obstacle is “I pick up my phone while making tea and lose 40 minutes”, the routine should alter that point of contact as well as state an intention. Explanatory illustration 1 Removing digital friction in reverse Behavioural advice often says to make good habits easy. For studying before social media, the equally useful move is to make the competing behaviour slightly harder to start. This is friction in reverse : the textbook, notebook or practice set is prepared in advance, while the distracting device requires an extra action. That extra action does not need to be extreme. Useful versions include leaving the phone charging outside the study area, logging out of the most automatic social media account, disabling non essential notifications during the first study block, or using a computer browser profile that opens the learning platform without already being signed into entertainment sites. The aim is not punishment. It is to prevent a habitual check from becoming the default action during the few seconds when the learner is trying to initiate work. There is good reason to focus on actual phone use and interruptions. An experimental study of university students found that receiving push notifications during a smartphone game conducted alongside a brief lecture was associated with worse learning performance; notably, participants’ subjective assessment of their performance did not reliably reveal the objective cost. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. In another undergraduate study, students who reported being distracted for a larger share of their study time tended to perform worse on examinations even after several relevant factors were considered. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)To What Extent Do Study Habits Relate to Performance? Pub Med Central (PMC)To What Extent Do Study Habits Relate to Performance? These results do not isolate social media as the only problem, but they reinforce the narrower point that competing digital activity during learning can matter. Moving the phone helps, but the evidence is not absolute The stronger claim — that a silent phone merely sitting nearby reliably drains cognition — is less secure. One experiment involving 119 undergraduates found better memory recall when smartphones were absent and also found that consciously thinking about the phone predicted poorer recall. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Mobile phones: The effect of its presence on learning and memory Pub Med Mobile phones: The effect of its presence on learning and memory Other experiments have likewise reported increased difficulty on demanding attention tasks when a phone was available. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Yet the larger evidence base is inconsistent. A 2024 meta analysis covering 56 studies and 7,093 participants found a statistically significant pooled negative effect of smartphone presence for working memory capacity, but not"},{"title":"Complex Habits","url":"/complex-habits/","description":"Behaviours with more steps, effort, or variability may take much longer to feel automatic than simple repeated actions.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Complex Habits Why Some Habits Take Far Longer Than Others Better Habits Complex Habits Complex Habits Why Some Habits Take Far Longer Than Others Behaviours with more steps, effort, or variability may take much longer to feel automatic than simple repeated actions. Behaviours with more steps, effort, or variability may take much longer to feel automatic than simple repeated actions. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative theme cluster population context Simple versus complex behaviours Sources of variability in formation time Setting realistic expectations self improvement that works complex habits Introduction Some habits take far longer to feel automatic because the behaviour itself is demanding. Drinking a glass of water after breakfast is a short, repeatable action with an obvious cue. Exercising, cooking a healthy meal, completing a study session or following an evening routine may involve preparation, several decisions, changing circumstances and multiple linked actions. Habit research suggests that these differences matter: automaticity can develop over weeks or months, but formation times vary enormously between people and behaviours, and greater behavioural complexity is associated with weaker or less reliable habit development in several studies. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Illustrative overview This is why there is no scientifically defensible countdown such as “21 days” or even “66 days” that applies to every habit. A 2024 systematic review found individual formation times ranging from 4 to 335 days across the small number of health habit studies that measured duration directly. More importantly for complex habits, researchers increasingly distinguish between making the start of a behaviour automatic and making an entire multi step sequence automatic. For self improvement that lasts, that distinction produces much more realistic expectations. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 3 Simple versus complex behaviours The classic real world habit formation study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates why behaviour type cannot be ignored. Participants chose daily eating, drinking or exercise behaviours and repeated them in a consistent context. Among participants whose automaticity trajectories could be modelled, reaching 95% of their eventual automaticity took between 18 and 254 days. Automaticity rose quickly at first and then more slowly towards a plateau rather than suddenly appearing on a particular day. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... An important detail is often lost when the study is reduced to its famous 66 day average. Simpler actions, such as drinking water, tended to reach their automaticity plateau more quickly than more elaborate behaviours such as exercise routines. Researchers translating the findings into clinical habit advice subsequently emphasised choosing small, manageable actions partly because simpler actions appeared easier to automate. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 Complexity is not simply a question of how physically difficult something is. A behaviour becomes complex when successful performance depends on coordinating several sub actions or responding to variable conditions. “Take a vitamin after breakfast” has a fairly consistent structure. “Eat healthily every evening” might require deciding what to cook, checking ingredients, preparing food, resisting convenient alternatives and adapting to changing work or family schedules. “Exercise three evenings a week” may require packing equipment, travelling somewhere, choosing a workout and completing the session. Research across 25 behaviours, including exercise, handwashing and medication adherence, found that greater perceived behavioural complexity was associated with lower automaticity overall. Frequency, stable contexts and perceived rewards were all positively associated with automaticity. Interestingly, stable context and reward were especially strongly related to automaticity for behaviours perceived as complex, suggesting that complexity does not make habit impossible; it may make reliable cues and favourable conditions more important. Because this study was observational, however, it cannot establish that complexity itself caused slower habit development. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Behavioral Automat... More recent longitudinal evidence strengthens the case that complexity is one source of variation without turning it into a universal law. A preregistered 2026 reanalysis followed 254 people over six months and identified trajectories in which habits either strengthened or degraded. Membership in those trajectories differed according to behavioural complexity, frequency and context stability, while perceived difficulty was negatively associated with habit strength. The study reinforces a broader conclusion: the behaviour being attempted helps shape how smoothly automaticity develops. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsWhat Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation Sebastian Bürgler, Marina Milyavskaya, Gail Mc... Explanatory illustration 1 A complex routine may never become automatic in the simple sense There is a deeper problem with saying that an entire complex behaviour “becomes a habit”. Modern habit theory usually defines a habit as a learned association in which a contextual cue automatically triggers an impulse to act. That definition fits compact behaviours quite neatly. It becomes less straightforward when the target is a 45 minute workout, a study routine or cooking dinner, because those episodes contain many actions that still require attention and adjustment. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. Researchers therefore distinguish habitual instigation from habitual execution . Instigation means that a cue automatically prompts someone to begin: finishing work may generate an almost immediate impulse to change into gym clothes. Execution means that the subsequent sequence itself unfolds automatically. Research on exercise found that habitual instigation was more predictive of exercise frequency than habitual execution. In practical terms, the most useful habit may be “starting the workout automatically”, not performing every movement without deliberation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This distinction also resolves an apparent contradiction. Complex behaviours can plainly become routine — experienced runners go running, experienced cooks start dinner and regular students open their materials with little internal debate — yet the whole episode need not operate like an automatic reflex. Contemporary reviews describe complex behaviours as sequences in which some components can be habitually triggered while other components remain deliberate and responsive to changing circumstances. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. That is often desirable. An exercise session should change when a person is injured or unusually tired. A study session should adapt to the material that proves difficult. A healthy meal cannot be prepared identically when ingredients, time and company change. For such behaviours, successful habit formation may mean automating the recurring gateways — starting, preparing or showing up — while retaining conscious control over the variable parts. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. How Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit? Science Explains Channel: Lifestyle Science Open on YouTube Why formation time varies so widely Complexity is only one reason two apparently similar habits can develop at very different speeds. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis identified 20 experimental or quasi experimental studies involving 2,601 participants. Only four studies directly supplied habit formation times, which is an important limitation, but those studies showed striking dispersion: reported medians were 59–66 days, reported means included 106–154 days, and individual estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days. The authors concluded that behaviour type, frequency, timing, individual choice and other contextual factors all contributed to habit strength. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Several factors are particularly relevant when the target behaviour has many moving parts. Fewer usable"},{"title":"Learning Goals","url":"/learning-goals/","description":"When you are still learning how to succeed, goals about practicing skills or strategies may beat rigid performance targets.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Learning Goals When Learning Goals Beat Performance Targets Better Habits Learning Goals Learning Goals When Learning Goals Beat Performance Targets When you are still learning how to succeed, goals about practicing skills or strategies may beat rigid performance targets. When you are still learning how to succeed, goals about practicing skills or strategies may beat rigid performance targets. Self Improvement That Works implementation theme cluster population context Why complex tasks need a different approach Examples of useful learning targets Moving from learning to performance self improvement that works learning goals Introduction When a task is unfamiliar and complex, a demanding performance target can be premature. If you do not yet know the best way to perform the task, simply pushing for a higher score, faster completion or better output can consume attention without teaching you what to do differently. In that stage, a learning goal — a target focused on discovering, practising or applying useful strategies — can be more effective. Illustrative overview This is not an argument against ambitious performance goals. Research on goal setting broadly supports challenging goals when people already possess the relevant knowledge and skills. The important qualification is task stage . Experiments on complex scheduling, business simulations and skill acquisition suggest that beginners often perform better when their immediate target is to learn how the task works and identify effective strategies. Once those strategies become sufficiently reliable, performance goals become much more useful. [aom.org] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... Explanatory illustration 3 Why complex tasks need a different approach A performance goal specifies an end result: finish the analysis in two hours, make ten sales, score 80 per cent, run five kilometres in a particular time. Such goals can work well when the path to the result is already reasonably clear. They direct attention, increase effort and encourage persistence. An unfamiliar complex task presents a different problem. The limiting factor may not be effort at all. You may lack a mental model of the task, misunderstand which variables matter or possess no dependable strategy for handling them. Goal setting researchers Gerard Seijts and Gary Latham therefore distinguish situations that require working harder from those that initially require learning how to work effectively . A learning goal redirects attention from proving that you can already produce the result towards discovering the processes that make the result possible. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... A classic experiment by Dawn Winters and Gary Latham illustrates the distinction. Business students completed either a simple or complex scheduling task. On the simple version, an outcome goal produced better performance than merely telling participants to do their best. On the complex version, however, participants assigned a learning goal performed significantly better than those given either an outcome goal or a do your best instruction. They also reported higher self efficacy than the do your best group and used more effective task strategies. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... Earlier work with more than 1,000 US Air Force trainees performing simulated air traffic control tasks reached a related conclusion. Kanfer and Ackerman found that the effects of goal setting depended on where people were in skill acquisition. Early in a cognitively demanding task, regulating yourself around a difficult performance target can compete for the same limited attention needed to understand and execute the task itself. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLMotivation and cognitive abilities: An integrative/aptitude treatment interaction approach to skill acquisition. EBSCOhost... That helps explain the apparently paradoxical result: trying harder to hit a number can sometimes make a beginner worse at the task . The number is not inherently harmful. The problem is that the learner has been given an output standard before possessing an adequate method for reaching it. Research on complex tasks also warns about narrowed attention. A difficult performance target may encourage people to focus heavily on the measured outcome, prematurely commit to a strategy or keep changing tactics without evaluating what they are learning from each attempt. The risk rises when successful performance requires coordinating several pieces of information or adapting as conditions change. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. A quantitative review of learning goal research concluded that the advantage is especially relevant when tasks are complex for the person performing them. A separate meta analysis of experimentally induced motivational states similarly found an overall advantage for learning or mastery goals over performance goals, with the difference appearing particularly important on relatively complex tasks. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Learning goals: A qualitative and quantitative review ResearchGate(PDF) Learning goals: A qualitative and quantitative reviewJanuary 1, 2013... Published: January 1, 2013 The phrase “complex for the person” matters. Complexity is not just an inherent property of an activity. A spreadsheet model may be difficult for a first time analyst and routine for an experienced finance professional. Writing a sales proposal may demand substantial learning from someone entering sales while requiring little strategic discovery from a veteran. The appropriate goal can therefore change even though the nominal task stays the same. Explanatory illustration 1 Examples of useful learning targets A useful learning goal is more concrete than “learn more” but does not demand an outcome that the person does not yet know how to produce. It specifies something to discover, practise, test or understand. Suppose someone is learning data analysis. An early performance goal might be, “Finish this analysis in 90 minutes with no mistakes.” That combines speed and accuracy before the learner necessarily understands the workflow. A learning goal could instead be: identify three reliable ways to check the dataset for missing or implausible values; practise choosing an appropriate chart for five different analytical questions; complete the analysis once while documenting every validation step; compare two methods and explain when each is appropriate. The same distinction applies to public speaking. “Deliver a presentation that scores 9/10” is an output target. A novice may obtain more value initially from goals such as rehearsing three opening techniques, learning to replace filler words with pauses, or reviewing a recording to identify two specific changes for the next attempt. For learning a complicated software package, “complete the project twice as fast” may be less useful initially than “discover and practise five shortcuts that remove repeated manual steps” or “complete three representative tasks without consulting the tutorial”. For a new manager, “raise team productivity by 15 per cent” can coexist as a long term objective, while the immediate learning goals concern conducting useful one to ones, diagnosing workflow bottlenecks and testing ways of delegating clearly. The crucial feature is that the target should lead to task relevant knowledge or strategy , rather than simply lowering expectations. Learning goals are not an instruction to take it easy. In the research tradition discussed here, they can themselves be specific and challenging: identify a certain number of effective strategies, master a set of procedures, test alternative approaches or accurately explain why one approach works better than another. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... One laboratory study involving a moderately complex scheduling task makes this especially clear. Participants given a specific difficult learning goal performed better than those told simply to do their best, and the number of task relevant strategies discovered was positively associated with performance. The benefit came not from abandoning goals but from aiming goal directed effort at strategy acquisition . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. A later complex business simulation study reached much the same conclusion. A specific learning goal produced better"},{"title":"Scale Up","url":"/scale-up/","description":"Expanding a routine is safer after the basic behaviour happens reliably, rather than demanding maximum effort from the start.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Scale Up When Should You Make a Habit More Ambitious? Better Habits Scale Up Scale Up When Should You Make a Habit More Ambitious? Expanding a routine is safer after the basic behaviour happens reliably, rather than demanding maximum effort from the start. Expanding a routine is safer after the basic behaviour happens reliably, rather than demanding maximum effort from the start. Self Improvement That Works implementation decision cluster event time window Signs a routine is stable enough to grow Increasing duration or difficulty gradually Backing off when expansion breaks consistency self improvement that works scale up Introduction A habit is usually ready to become more ambitious when the basic behaviour is happening reliably in its intended context and no longer requires the same amount of negotiation, remembering or effort to get started. That is a better signal than waiting for a mythical number of days. Research on habit formation shows large differences between people and behaviours: in one influential real world study, the estimated time to approach peak automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days, while a recent systematic review found similarly wide variation and no single scientifically established cut off for when a habit is “formed”. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Illustrative overview Once that stability exists, increasing duration, frequency or difficulty can make the routine more useful. The safest approach, however, is to expand the dose of the behaviour without unnecessarily changing the cue and structure that made it reliable . In practical terms, keep the dependable “when and where”, raise one demand at a time, and treat falling consistency as information that the expansion may have been too large. How can you tell when a habit is stable enough to grow? Habit strength is not the same thing as having completed a 21 , 30 or 66 day streak. In psychological research, a habit is generally understood as a behaviour that becomes increasingly prompted by contextual cues because it has been repeated in association with them. Frequency matters, but repetition in a reasonably stable context matters too. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Psychology of Habit Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsPsychology of Habit Annual ReviewsJanuary 4, 2016... Published: January 4, 2016 That distinction gives a more useful test for scaling. Suppose you want to read every evening. “I have read for 30 days” tells you how long the project has existed. “After I make tea, I usually pick up the book without debating whether to do it” tells you something more relevant about behavioural stability. Research supports paying attention to this cue–behaviour relationship. A 2022 study of deliberate habit building found that greater context stability was associated with stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. In experimental data involving study habits, people instructed to keep their performance context stable developed more automaticity than those asked to vary rooms and times; similar associations appeared in real world app data. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Useful signs that a routine may be ready to expand include: It happens on most intended opportunities. You are not repeatedly renegotiating whether today counts. The starting cue still works. The behaviour follows something recognisable — after breakfast, on arriving home, after opening the laptop — rather than depending on remembering at a random moment. Starting feels easier than it did initially. Qualitative research on habit formation has found that behaviours can feel cognitively demanding early on and easier as automaticity develops. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study PubMedAugust 1, 2011... Published: August 1, 2011 Ordinary bad days do not routinely erase the behaviour. A busy day might shorten it, but the routine itself survives. A missed occasion is followed by a return rather than abandonment. This matters because maintenance is better understood as continuing or recovering across disruptions than as preserving a flawless streak. Reviews of physical activity maintenance explicitly distinguish sustainable maintenance from uninterrupted performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Physical Activity Maintenance: A Critical Narrative Review and Directions for Future Research PMCSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 None of these signs provides a clinical threshold. Habit researchers themselves use different measures and definitions of automaticity, and a recent systematic review noted that studies have applied varying thresholds for deciding when habit formation has occurred. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical decision is therefore probabilistic: scale when the existing version looks robust enough that added demand is unlikely to destroy the repetition pattern. A useful rule is: do not increase a habit merely because it feels easy once; increase it when ease and reliability have become normal. Explanatory illustration 1 Increase the dose without rebuilding the whole routine The most conservative expansion changes one dimension while preserving the established behavioural architecture. If ten minutes of walking after lunch is reliable, move towards fifteen minutes before deciding that the new routine must also involve running, a gym membership and a different time of day . If five minutes of language practice after breakfast is established, extend the session before replacing it with an elaborate evening study schedule. If saving £20 on payday is automatic, a higher transfer may be easier to absorb than moving the entire process to a new account, date and budgeting system simultaneously. This approach is consistent with the behaviour change technique known as graded tasks : progressively increasing the difficulty of a behaviour rather than requiring the final target immediately. Evidence is strongest in physical activity research rather than across every possible self improvement habit, so it should not be treated as proof of a universal formula. Still, several systematic reviews have found graded tasks among techniques associated with stronger physical activity outcomes or maintenance. A review of interventions for inactive adults found that programmes including graded tasks tended to show larger effects both immediately and at follow up, while a separate review of adults with overweight or obesity identified graded tasks among techniques associated with improved physical activity in digital interventions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The broader evidence is not perfectly consistent. For example, graded tasks have not improved every outcome in every population, and one review of computerised cognitive training found lower adherence in studies using them. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is a useful warning against turning “increase gradually” into another rigid self help rule. The important idea is not endless progression. It is matching the next challenge to what the existing system can carry . Change one variable at a time For many routines, ambition can be raised along several dimensions: Duration: read for 20 minutes rather than 10. Volume: write 500 words rather than 250. Frequency: practise four days per week rather than three. Difficulty: move from simple problems to harder ones. Quality: keep the same session length but work with greater concentration or better technique. Changing one of these at a time makes the result easier to interpret. If consistency remains intact, the new version may be sustainable. If performance collapses, you know which added demand caused trouble. Physical exercise offers a particularly concrete example because progression is deliberately managed rather than maximised immediately. American College of Sports Medicine guidance in some exercise contexts recommends gradual progression and explicitly warns against large simultaneous increases in frequency, intensity, time and other training demands. [ACSM] acsm.org Exercise for the Prevention and Treatment of Hypertension Exercise for the Prevention and Treatment of Hypertension The exact exercise prescription should not be generalised to reading, studying or saving money, but the implementation principle transfers well: avoid increasing every dimension at once. Habit Stacking: How to Keep Up With Everything (When You Don't Have Time) Channel: Scott Young Open on YouTube Preserve the cue where possible Scaling should generally enlarge the"},{"title":"Behaviour Goals","url":"/behaviour-goals/","description":"A useful self-improvement goal names the behaviour you can perform, not only the outcome you hope to reach.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Behaviour Goals Turn Big Goals Into Actions You Can Do Better Habits Behaviour Goals Behaviour Goals Turn Big Goals Into Actions You Can Do A useful self improvement goal names the behaviour you can perform, not only the outcome you hope to reach. A useful self improvement goal names the behaviour you can perform, not only the outcome you hope to reach. Self Improvement That Works implementation pillar policy intervention Outcome goals versus behaviour goals Choosing actions you can control Examples for fitness, study, and spending self improvement that works behaviour goals Introduction A useful self improvement goal describes a behaviour you can perform, not just an outcome you would like to achieve. “Get fitter”, “improve my grades” and “save more money” provide direction, but they do not tell you what to do when an ordinary Tuesday arrives. Behaviour goals make the next move explicit: “walk for 30 minutes after lunch”, “complete 20 practice questions before opening social media” or “transfer £50 to savings on payday”. Illustrative overview This distinction matters because intentions and actions are not the same thing. People can sincerely intend to change yet fail to initiate or sustain the behaviour required. Research on goal setting, implementation intentions and progress monitoring suggests that a practical response is to translate the desired outcome into a controllable action, specify the situation in which it will occur and then monitor whether it happened. Goal setting alone has a positive but modest average effect on behaviour; planning the execution can add the missing bridge between wanting and doing. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... Outcome goals versus behaviour goals An outcome goal identifies the result you care about. A behaviour goal identifies an action that contributes to that result and can be performed directly. That difference is easy to miss because many outcome goals sound impressively concrete. “Lose five kilograms”, “get a first class mark” and “save £3,000” are measurable. But none can be carried out as an action. Weight changes depend on many influences; examination marks depend on learning, assessment design and performance on the day; savings depend partly on income and unavoidable costs. You can influence these outcomes without controlling them completely. Behaviour goals move one step closer to execution: Desired outcomeBehaviour goalImprove fitnessWalk briskly for 30 minutes after work on Monday, Wednesday and FridayImprove an exam resultComplete and mark 20 retrieval practice questions on four evenings each weekReduce unnecessary spendingDelay every non essential purchase above £30 for 24 hoursBuild savingsTransfer a fixed amount to savings immediately after each paydayFinish a long writing projectDraft for 30 minutes before opening email each weekday This does not mean outcomes should disappear. They remain useful for deciding whether the behaviour is worth doing and, over time, whether it appears to be working. The important move is to avoid asking an outcome to do the job of an action plan. Research on goal setting interventions supports goal setting in general, but it also warns against treating any single formula as universally optimal. A systematic review covering 141 papers and 16,523 participants found a small positive independent effect of goal setting on behaviour, with substantial variation in how goals were designed and delivered. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... Even the familiar SMART formula — goals that are specific, measurable and time bound, among other criteria — has weaker scientific foundations than its popularity sometimes implies. A 2022 review of SMART goals in physical activity promotion argued that the acronym is inconsistently defined, does not adequately distinguish different types of goals and should not be treated as an evidence based recipe in itself. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The (over)use of SMART goals for physical activity promotion: A narrative review and critique PubMedJanuary 31, 2022... Published: January 31, 2022 The useful question is therefore not merely “Is my goal specific?” but “Does this goal specify an action that I can actually perform?” Choose an action you can control The most useful behaviour goals sit within what might be called the person’s zone of direct action . They describe something you can start or stop yourself rather than a response you need from the world. “Get three job offers” is an outcome. “Submit four tailored applications each week” is a behaviour. “Make my manager appreciate my work” depends on someone else; “send a concise Friday update showing completed work, decisions and blockers” is under your control. Three tests are particularly useful. Can you perform it deliberately? “Write for 25 minutes” passes. “Become motivated” does not. Can you tell whether it happened? “Complete one timed practice paper” gives a clear answer. “Study seriously” leaves room for reinterpretation. Would repeating it plausibly help the outcome? A behaviour can be measurable without being useful. Colour coding notes for two hours is controllable; that does not make it an effective learning strategy. The behaviour should have a credible connection to the result. This last test matters because self improvement can easily become a form of productive looking displacement. Tracking, planning and organising are useful only when they support the behaviour that produces the desired change. Learning research illustrates the point. In one randomised study of medical students learning wound closure skills, participants who set goals focused on the process of performing the skill showed better transfer than those focused on performance outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How effective is self guided learning of clinical technical skills? It's all about process PubMedJune 1, 2009... Published: June 1, 2009 A later study found a much smaller and less consistent advantage, although process focused students were better calibrated when judging their own performance during training. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Assigning Medical Students Learning Goals: Do They Do It, and What Happens When They Don't? PubMedApril 16, 2019... Published: April 16, 2019 The broader lesson is not that an outcome goal is always inferior, but that process focused targets become particularly useful when the challenge is learning or executing the actions that generate the outcome. There is a further caution: the behaviour you choose can shape how you perform. In a randomised surgical skills experiment, students given a speed goal worked faster, but produced fewer leak free ligatures than those given a quality goal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Speed and quality goals in procedural skills learning: A randomized experiment PubMedOctober 9, 2019... Published: October 9, 2019 Goals direct attention. Choosing a measurable action therefore requires deciding what kind of performance you actually want, not merely what is easiest to count. Explanatory illustration 1 Turn the behaviour into an if–then plan A behaviour goal becomes more actionable when it answers a second question: when will I do it? This is the purpose of an implementation intention , an if–then plan that connects a recognisable situation to a specified response: If situation X occurs, then I will perform behaviour Y. Instead of: “I will exercise more.” use: “If I finish work on Monday, Wednesday or Friday, I will change into my exercise clothes and begin my planned session.” Instead of: “I need to revise every day.” use: “If I finish dinner on a weekday, I will put my phone in the kitchen and complete 20 practice questions.” Peter Gollwitzer and Paschal Sheeran’s influential 2006 meta analysis examined 94 independent tests and reported a medium to large average effect of implementation intentions on goal attainment, d = 0.65. The review found benefits across several self regulatory problems, including starting intended behaviour and protecting ongoing goal pursuit from interference. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The technique is useful because it does more than make an intention detailed. It associates the intended action with a cue. Experimental work suggests that implementation intentions can increase the accessibility of the specified situation and make the planned response more readily initiated when that situation appears — sometimes described as “strategic automaticity ”. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Metacognition in action: the importance of implementation intentions Metacognition in action: the importance of implementation intentions PubMed... A separate meta analysis examining prospective"},{"title":"Goal Difficulty","url":"/goal-difficulty/","description":"Goals tend to work better when they are meaningfully challenging but still realistic for the task and your current skill.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Goal Difficulty How Hard Should a Good Goal Be? Better Habits Goal Difficulty Goal Difficulty How Hard Should a Good Goal Be? Goals tend to work better when they are meaningfully challenging but still realistic for the task and your current skill. Goals tend to work better when they are meaningfully challenging but still realistic for the task and your current skill. Self Improvement That Works critique risk decision cluster mechanism Why challenge can improve performance When difficulty becomes counterproductive Adjusting challenge as skill grows self improvement that works goal difficulty Introduction A good goal should be difficult enough to demand more attention, effort or skill than your normal performance, but not so difficult that it removes a credible route to success. Research on goal setting has repeatedly found that specific, challenging goals can outperform easy goals and vague instructions such as “do your best”. But that finding has important limits: the effect depends on ability, task complexity, feedback , commitment and whether you already know how to perform the task. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... Illustrative overview That qualification matters for self improvement. A demanding target can be useful when the challenge is mainly doing more or doing better at something you understand. When the challenge is figuring out how to do something unfamiliar, simply raising the performance target can interfere with learning. The practical aim is therefore not maximum ambition. It is productive difficulty : enough challenge to change your behaviour without overwhelming the skills and mental resources you currently have. Why challenge can improve performance The basic case for challenging goals is unusually well established by behavioural research standards. A major meta analysis covering goal setting studies conducted between 1966 and 1984 found strong support both for hard goals over easy goals and for specific, hard goals over general “do your best” instructions or no goal. Later reviews of goal setting theory continued to identify specific, difficult goals as a reliable influence on performance across many kinds of participants and settings. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... The mechanism is more useful than the slogan “aim high”. Challenging goals can alter several parts of performance at once. They direct attention towards goal relevant activity, encourage greater effort, promote persistence and can stimulate people to search for better strategies. In other words, a difficult target can provide a reason to organise behaviour differently rather than merely wishing for improvement. [DOI] doi.org New Directions in Goal Setting Theory New Directions in Goal Setting Theory Edwin A. Locke, Gary P. Latham, 2006... Consider someone who can already run five kilometres comfortably in roughly 30 minutes. “Go for a run sometime this week” leaves considerable room for drift. A target of completing three planned runs, or gradually improving a familiar pace, creates a clearer demand. The person already possesses the basic skill; the goal mainly asks for more consistent or more demanding execution. In that type of situation, greater difficulty can translate fairly directly into greater effort. But difficulty is not an objective property of the number written in the goal. Goal setting theory treats ability as an important moderator: a target that stretches one person can be routine for another and effectively impossible for a third. Research synthesising the antecedents of personal goal levels has likewise found that previous performance and ability are associated with the goals people adopt. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsMeta Analysis of the Antecedents of Personal Goal Level and of the Antecedents and Consequences of Goal Commitment J. C. W... This is one reason rules such as “your goal should have a 70% chance of success” should be treated cautiously. There is no single validated success probability that defines optimal difficulty across self improvement tasks. The useful question is whether the goal demands a meaningful increase in effort or skill while remaining credible given your present ability, resources and understanding of the task . Feedback also matters. The classic goal setting meta analysis found additional support for combining specific, difficult goals with feedback rather than setting the goal without feedback. Feedback tells you whether the difficulty is actually producing better performance or merely repeated misses . Without that information, an ambitious target can remain psychologically impressive while being badly calibrated in practice. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirectFebruary 1, 1987... Published: February 1, 1987 Explanatory illustration 1 When difficulty becomes counterproductive The biggest qualification to “hard goals work better” appears when a task is unfamiliar or cognitively complex. On a familiar task, the main problem may be effort. On a new task, however, you must simultaneously discover strategies, notice patterns, learn rules and decide what deserves attention. Those learning demands compete for limited mental resources. Research on skill acquisition has demonstrated why timing matters. Kanfer and Ackerman studied more than 1,000 US Air Force trainees learning a complex air traffic control task. Their work examined how cognitive ability, motivation and goal setting interact at different stages of learning, showing that the demands placed on a learner change substantially as practice develops. This helped establish task knowledge and stage of skill acquisition as important boundary conditions for goal setting. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLMotivation and cognitive abilities: An integrative/aptitude treatment interaction approach to skill acquisition. EBSCOhost... An especially clear demonstration came from Gerard Seijts and Gary Latham in a laboratory scheduling task that required participants to acquire knowledge before they could perform well. Participants given a specific, difficult outcome goal performed worse than those simply told to do their best. Yet participants given a specific, difficult learning goal performed better than the “do your best” group. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. The difference is crucial. An outcome goal asks, “How well must I perform?” A learning goal asks, “What must I discover or master in order to perform well?” When the method is already known, the first question can be motivating. When the method is still unclear, the second can be far more useful. Imagine learning to edit video for the first time. “Produce a polished ten minute documentary by Sunday” is specific and difficult, but it gives little guidance about the actual bottleneck if you do not yet understand editing software, audio mixing, pacing or colour correction. A challenging learning goal — for example, mastering cuts and transitions, completing two short practice sequences and diagnosing what makes them look amateurish — directs effort towards acquiring the capability on which the larger performance ultimately depends. Experiments on complex learning tasks have likewise found that the familiar advantage of difficult, specific goals from simple tasks does not automatically generalise to complex performance. Research examining explicit and implicit learning concluded that task complexity can alter the goal–performance relationship because performance depends on acquiring the right knowledge, not merely trying harder. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. A useful distinction is therefore between effort difficulty and strategy difficulty . Effort difficulty: You know reasonably well what works, but reaching the target requires more concentration, practice, persistence or output. Increasing the performance challenge can be productive. Strategy difficulty: You do not yet know reliably what works. Increasing the outcome target risks adding pressure without solving the knowledge problem. The distinction explains why ambition can sometimes create the appearance of seriousness while making learning worse. If a novice repeatedly measures themselves against an expert level outcome, attention may become dominated by the performance gap rather than by experimenting with the smaller skills needed to close it. Applied evidence is less tidy than laboratory theory. A 2021 systematic review of 27 goal setting interventions in sport found mixed evidence for several predicted features of goal setting theory, including difficulty. Only eight studies explicitly considered goal difficulty, and between group"},{"title":"Simple Metrics","url":"/simple-metrics/","description":"The best personal metric is often the smallest one that tells you whether to continue, adjust, or simplify the plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Simple Metrics Track Less, but Track What Actually Matters Better Habits Simple Metrics Simple Metrics Track Less, but Track What Actually Matters The best personal metric is often the smallest one that tells you whether to continue, adjust, or simplify the plan. The best personal metric is often the smallest one that tells you whether to continue, adjust, or simplify the plan. Self Improvement That Works governance decision cluster mechanism Choosing decision relevant measures Examples for common goals Avoiding personal dashboard overload self improvement that works simple metrics Introduction For self improvement, the best metric is usually not the one that produces the richest dashboard. It is the smallest measure that tells you what to do next. If you are trying to establish a repeated behaviour, “Did the planned session happen?” may initially be more useful than tracking duration, intensity, mood, streak length and half a dozen outcome measures. Experimental evidence shows that monitoring progress can improve goal attainment, particularly when progress is recorded, but it does not establish that collecting more variables produces better results. A meta analysis of 138 studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions which increased progress monitoring also improved goal attainment, with a moderate overall effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Illustrative overview The practical rule is simple: track enough to decide whether to continue, adjust or simplify the plan — and require every extra metric to justify its cost. Choosing decision relevant measures A useful personal metric is part of a feedback loop, not merely a description of your life. You act, observe what happened, compare it with what you intended, then decide whether anything should change. NICE's behaviour change guidance explicitly separates monitoring a behaviour from monitoring its outcome and recommends reviewing goals and making further plans in light of progress. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 That distinction solves many tracking problems. Suppose your goal is better fitness and you have planned three runs each week. There are at least two different questions you could be asking: Am I actually following the plan? Track completed planned runs. Is the plan improving my fitness? Track an appropriate performance outcome. Those measures are not interchangeable. If you complete only one run out of three, adding resting heart rate, kilometres, pace zones and estimated aerobic fitness may tell you interesting things, but the immediate governance problem is adherence. The plan is not happening often enough for its effectiveness to be the first question. If you consistently complete three runs each week but performance remains unchanged over a meaningful period, the decision changes. Adherence is no longer the obvious bottleneck; an outcome metric now matters more. This leads to a useful principle: measure the current uncertainty . Do not permanently track everything that could conceivably matter. A metric deserves a place in your system when you can answer three questions: 1. What decision will this measure influence? 2. What result would cause me to do something differently? 3. Could a simpler measure answer the same question? If “pages read”, “minutes meditated” or “daily productivity score” rises or falls without ever altering your plan, it is functioning as personal trivia rather than feedback. There is good evidence for the broader value of monitoring. In the large Harkin and colleagues meta analysis, interventions increased both monitoring frequency and subsequent goal attainment; monitoring had larger effects when progress was physically recorded or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 NICE consequently includes feedback and monitoring among evidence based behaviour change techniques and advises people using diet or physical activity interventions to use self monitoring to review progress towards their goals. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: digital and mobile health interventions Guidance NICEOctober 7, 2020... Published: October 7, 2020 But that evidence should not be stretched into a claim that maximal tracking is optimal. A systematic meta review covering 66 meta analyses found support for components including self monitoring, goal setting and personalised feedback, while also concluding that none worked consistently across every behaviour and population. It also identified methodological limitations in much of the self regulation evidence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulation mechanisms in health behavior change: a systematic meta review of meta analyses, 2006 2017 PubMed... The defensible conclusion is that monitoring can help; the ideal amount and form depend on what you need the information to decide. Explanatory illustration 1 Let the metric change when the bottleneck changes One of the most useful ways to keep measurement minimal is to stop treating metrics as permanent. Imagine somebody trying to study consistently after work. During the first month, the useful measure might be: Planned study sessions completed: 2 of 4. That already tells them something important. They do not first need a concentration score, total minutes, chapter count, recall percentage and daily motivation rating. If sessions repeatedly fail to happen, the next decision is likely to concern the plan itself: perhaps four sessions are unrealistic, the timing conflicts with family responsibilities, or the starting task is too large. Now imagine that, after adjusting the routine, the person reliably completes four sessions every week. “Sessions completed” still confirms adherence, but it cannot answer the new question: Is this study producing learning? A small outcome measure can now take priority — for example, performance on a weekly set of previously unseen practice questions or the proportion of material successfully recalled after a delay. The measurement changes because the decision has changed. The same logic applies elsewhere. An early metric often establishes whether the behaviour is occurring; a later metric tests whether that behaviour is producing the desired result. NICE's behaviour change framework explicitly distinguishes behavioural goals from resulting outcomes and recommends reviewing goals in light of experience rather than treating a plan as fixed. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 This avoids a common failure of personal dashboards: keeping every metric ever introduced. If a measure has answered its question, it can be retired, sampled less often or replaced. Lead vs Lag Measures: The Secret to Achieving Your Goals Channel: Adam N S Open on YouTube Examples for common goals Minimal metrics work best when they match the controllable part of the problem. The precise measure will vary, but the decision logic remains consistent. Exercise . If consistency is the problem, record planned sessions completed: “2 of 3” tells you much more about the immediate bottleneck than a detailed fitness dashboard. Once adherence is stable, use one outcome appropriate to the goal — perhaps time over a standard distance, repetitions at a standard load, or another simple performance measure. Avoid assuming that every statistic produced by a wearable deserves attention merely because it is automatically available. Learning. When starting reliably is difficult, record whether the planned practice session happened. Once the study routine is established, move towards a measure of learning rather than effort alone. Forty minutes spent highlighting notes proves that forty minutes passed; it does not demonstrate retention. A small set of delayed retrieval questions can answer a different and more useful question: whether the method is producing recall. Writing. If avoidance is the main obstacle, “Did I begin the planned writing session?” can be enough. Word count becomes useful only when producing a certain volume actually matters. For revision heavy work, completed sections or resolved revision tasks may be a better indicator than raw words, which can reward producing text that later has to be deleted. Spending. A person trying to reduce a particular kind of discretionary purchase might track the number or total value of purchases in that category each week. There is little reason to construct a large personal finance dashboard if the next decision is simply whether a specific spending rule is being followed. A bedtime routine."},{"title":"Reduce Friction","url":"/reduce-friction/","description":"Small reductions in effort, delay, or inconvenience can make a desired behaviour much more likely to happen repeatedly.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Reduce Friction Make Good Habits Easier Before You Need Them Better Habits Reduce Friction Reduce Friction Make Good Habits Easier Before You Need Them Small reductions in effort, delay, or inconvenience can make a desired behaviour much more likely to happen repeatedly. Small reductions in effort, delay, or inconvenience can make a desired behaviour much more likely to happen repeatedly. Self Improvement That Works mechanism pillar mechanism Where friction hides in everyday routines Reducing setup and decision costs Testing whether convenience changes behaviour self improvement that works reduce friction Introduction Friction is any small cost that sits between an intention and an action: finding equipment, opening an app, travelling somewhere, making another decision, waiting for a page to load, filling in a form or clearing a workspace before beginning. These obstacles can look trivial, yet behavioural research shows that changing effort can materially change what people actually do. A meta analysis of 184 field experiments involving more than 2.2 million participants found that interventions altering effort produced stronger behavioural effects, on average, than those aimed at intrinsic motivation, although the authors caution that publication bias may inflate the apparent advantage. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PubMed Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PMCNovember 29, 2023... Published: November 29, 2023 Illustrative overview For self improvement, the practical lesson is not that every good habit should become effortless. It is that unnecessary effort before the useful part of the behaviour can prevent repetition. Removing that effort makes starting more likely; repeated starts in a stable context then give habit formation a chance to occur. Friction reduction is therefore best understood as a mechanism for making good behaviour easier to repeat, rather than as a substitute for repetition itself. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Where friction hides in everyday routines The most obvious friction is physical. Exercise that requires a long journey, cooking that begins with an empty fridge and study that requires clearing a cluttered desk all impose extra actions before the intended behaviour can begin. Those actions consume time and create additional opportunities to abandon the plan. Distance provides a concrete illustration. In an observational study of an exercise programme for older adults, every additional kilometre between home and the gym was associated with an 8% reduction in the probability of adhering to the programme. Another German study found that greater distance from a gym was associated with lower participation in relevant sporting activities among rural girls, although the relationship was not consistent across all groups or facility types. These findings do not prove that moving a gym closer will automatically create an exercise habit, but they show why travel cost should not be dismissed as a minor inconvenience. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to a Community Based Exercise Program is a Strong Predictor of Improved Back Pain Status in Older Adults: A... Friction can also be remarkably local. A large workplace field study examined whether employees were more likely to take snacks when the snacks were close to the drinks station they used. Among men, the proportion taking a snack was 23% at the nearer station compared with 12% at the more distant one; among women it was 17% versus 13%. A difference of only a few steps altered behaviour. The important self improvement lesson is symmetrical: greater friction can suppress an unwanted action, while lower friction can favour a wanted one. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Proximity of snacks to beverages increases food consumption in the workplace: A field study ScienceDirectAugust 1, 2016... Published: August 1, 2016 Not all friction is measured in steps. Setup friction appears when equipment must be found, charged, assembled or prepared. Decision friction appears when the person still has to decide what workout to do, which chapter to study or what meal to cook. Process friction appears when an action requires unnecessary clicks, forms, passwords or intermediate stages. Timing friction appears when the opportunity is badly matched to the day, such as a gym that requires a special journey rather than sitting along the normal route. These costs can accumulate. “Go for a run” may actually mean find clean kit, locate headphones, decide a route, fill a bottle, change clothes and leave the house. None of those steps is individually difficult. Together, they make the behavioural threshold higher precisely when energy or motivation is low. Research on people beginning a new daily routine points in the same direction. In a study in which participants took vitamin C for three weeks, people often chose cues partly because they wanted to minimise effort — for example, by keeping the relevant object nearby or visible. The researchers concluded that cue selection is influenced not only by memory but by how easily the behaviour fits the surrounding routine. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What influences the selection of contextual cues when starting a new routine behaviour? An exploratory study PubMedMarch 30, 2020... Published: March 30, 2020 Explanatory illustration 1 Reduce setup before the moment of action The useful distinction is between productive effort and access effort . Productive effort is the work the habit is meant to contain: lifting the weight, practising the scales, solving the problems or writing the paragraph. Access effort is everything required merely to reach that work. Friction reduction mainly targets the second category. A difficult workout can still be a sensible goal. What need not be difficult is finding your trainers. Challenging study can be valuable; repeatedly searching for notes and deciding what to study is not part of that value. Preparing the environment in advance moves these incidental costs to a time when motivation is less fragile. In practical terms, that can mean: laying out exercise clothes the night before rather than locating them at 6.30 am; keeping medication or vitamins beside an existing reliable routine, where appropriate and safe; leaving the book being read in the place where reading normally happens; opening tomorrow’s working document before finishing today; keeping frequently used healthy ingredients available and easy to reach; storing distracting devices away from the workspace while keeping necessary tools immediately accessible; choosing an exercise venue that lies on an existing route rather than one that requires a separate expedition. This is more than tidiness. The aim is to alter the sequence of actions so that the desired behaviour occupies the shortest, clearest path. The importance of accessibility is visible in settings where compliance matters greatly. Hand hygiene research treats visibility, unobstructed access, proximity to the point of care and placement along the normal workflow as relevant features of sanitiser design. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The principle transfers cautiously to ordinary habits: put the useful action where the routine already passes, rather than expecting yourself to make a detour for it. There is an important limit, however. More convenience is not always better once a genuine barrier has already been removed. In a Zurich emergency department where mounted hand sanitiser dispensers were already available within two metres of every patient bed, giving staff wearable dispensers did not further increase observed hand hygiene compliance. The extra convenience apparently did little because accessibility was no longer the main constraint; ergonomic issues and other barriers remained. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That finding guards against a common mistake in self improvement: endlessly optimising the environment after the significant friction has already disappeared. Moving a notebook from a drawer to the desk may matter. Spending an hour designing the perfect notebook arrangement may simply become another way of avoiding writing. The secret habits that control your life Wendy Wood Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Reduce decisions as well as physical effort Some routines fail not because the action is physically inconvenient but because it still requires a miniature planning session every time. Consider exercise. “Exercise after work” leaves multiple unresolved questions: where, for how long, which movements, at what intensity and whether today is really suitable. “On Monday, Wednesday and Friday, change into the clothes already in my bag and start the saved 25 minute programme” removes much of that"},{"title":"Sleep and Focus","url":"/sleep-and-focus/","description":"Even one short night can increase sleepiness and worsen sustained attention, slowing responses and increasing lapses.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Sleep and Focus What One Short Night Does to Your Attention Better Habits Sleep and Focus Sleep and Focus What One Short Night Does to Your Attention Even one short night can increase sleepiness and worsen sustained attention, slowing responses and increasing lapses. Even one short night can increase sleepiness and worsen sustained attention, slowing responses and increasing lapses. Self Improvement That Works critique risk case family dataset evidence Findings from sleep restriction studies Slower responses and attentional lapses Why this matters for self improvement plans self improvement that works sleep and focus Introduction One short night of sleep can be enough to make attention less reliable. The strongest recent synthesis of the evidence, a 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of 44 studies, found that restricting adults to roughly two to six hours of sleep for a single night increased subjective sleepiness and impaired sustained attention. On average, people responded more slowly and had more attentional lapses on vigilance tasks after the restricted night. The same review did not find clear impairments across every cognitive domain, which is an important distinction: sleep restriction does not simply make all thinking equally worse. Its most consistent early cost appears to be the ability to stay alert and respond reliably over time. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... Illustrative overview That matters for self improvement because attention is a prerequisite for carrying out plans. Remembering the next step, noticing a cue, staying with a dull task and resisting the drift towards distraction all become harder when vigilance is unstable. A short night therefore creates a practical risk: a good plan can fail because the cognitive system needed to execute it is temporarily less dependable. Findings from sleep restriction studies The clearest evidence comes from studies that deliberately shorten sleep and then test vigilant attention. A common tool is the psychomotor vigilance test , or PVT, a simple reaction time task in which a person responds to visual signals appearing at unpredictable intervals. A traditional PVT lasts ten minutes and records not only average speed but also unusually slow responses, typically counting responses longer than 500 milliseconds as attentional lapses. It is widely used in sleep research because performance is highly sensitive to sleep loss and has relatively little practice effect. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 In the 2024 meta analysis, one night of sleep restriction produced a standardised mean difference of 0.512 for slower sustained attention reaction times and 0.489 for attentional lapses . Subjective sleepiness showed an even larger average increase, with a standardised mean difference of 0.986 . These are group level effects rather than guarantees about any particular individual, but they make the central point unusually clear: a single curtailed night can produce a measurable deterioration in vigilance by the following day. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... The underlying studies are not perfectly uniform. They vary in the amount and timing of sleep restriction, the tests used and the characteristics of participants. The meta analysis also found that the size of the sustained attention impairment was not neatly predicted by whether the restricted sleep opportunity was at the longer or shorter end of the two to six hour range. That means the evidence should not be reduced to a simplistic rule such as “four hours produces exactly twice the impairment of six”. Sleep loss interacts with factors such as time awake, circadian timing and individual vulnerability. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... Individual experiments nevertheless point in the same direction. In a study of 61 healthy adults aged 20–29, a night restricted to four hours in bed increased errors on a sustained attention task compared with a normal night. Interestingly, four prolonged night time awakenings produced similar decrements, suggesting that attention can be disrupted by fragmented sleep as well as by simply shortening the sleep window. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Effects of one night of induced night wakings versus sleep restriction on sustained attention and mood: a pilot study Scie... Explanatory illustration 1 Slower responses and attentional lapses Sleep restriction does not merely shift reaction time downwards by a small, uniform amount. A more characteristic pattern is instability : performance can look normal for stretches and then suddenly fail. Reviews of sleep deprivation research describe attention becoming erratic, with periods of competent performance interrupted by markedly slow responses or failures to respond. These intermittent breakdowns are the reason lapses are often more informative than an overall average. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The sleep deprived human brain PubMed Central (PMC)The sleep deprived human brain PMCMay 18, 2017... Published: May 18, 2017 This distinction matters in ordinary behaviour. Someone may be able to read several paragraphs efficiently and then realise they have stopped processing the words. They may follow most of a meeting but miss one instruction, or work competently on a repetitive task before overlooking a signal that would normally have been obvious. The problem is not necessarily that the person has become incapable of performing the task. It is that the ability to maintain the required level of attention has become less stable. Repeated short nights intensify this problem. In a classic laboratory study by Van Dongen and colleagues, healthy adults were restricted to four, six or eight hours in bed for 14 consecutive nights. PVT lapses accumulated significantly in both the four and six hour groups, with deterioration greater under the more severe restriction. Crucially, the impairment built across days rather than resolving once participants had spent a few nights on the restricted schedule. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A separate sleep dose study by Belenky and colleagues assigned 66 volunteers to three, five, seven or nine hours in bed each night for seven days. The three hour group showed steadily slower PVT responses and more lapses across the week. The five and seven hour groups showed smaller but still measurable degradation, while the nine hour group remained near baseline. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. These findings are consistent with a broader meta analysis of 61 sleep restriction studies involving 71 study populations. Across cognitive domains, the largest average effects appeared in sustained attention, and within that category attentional lapses were especially sensitive. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Why You Can’t Sleep (and How to Fix It) Dr. Michael Grandner Channel: FoundMyFitness Open on YouTube Feeling adapted is not the same as being unimpaired One of the more consequential findings in chronic sleep restriction research is that subjective adaptation and objective performance can diverge . In the Van Dongen experiment, people initially reported becoming sleepier when their sleep was shortened, but their ratings then changed relatively little even as objective vigilance performance continued to deteriorate. After repeated nights of four or six hours in bed, participants could therefore be substantially more impaired than their own sleepiness ratings suggested. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Reviews of this work describe the same mismatch: people may feel that they have “got used to” a restricted sleep schedule because the subjective sensation of sleepiness stops increasing sharply, while their sustained attention remains degraded. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This is a particular risk for self improvement because self assessment is often used to decide whether a system is working. If someone sleeps less to create extra time for studying, exercise or a side project, the arrangement may initially appear efficient: they have gained an extra hour or two and may not feel progressively worse each day. Yet the attention required to use those hours effectively can continue to deteriorate. The implication is not that people are incapable of judging their own state, nor that every short sleeper is dangerously impaired. It"},{"title":"Stable Contexts","url":"/stable-contexts/","description":"Repeating a behaviour in a similar place and situation can strengthen the association between the cue and the action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Stable Contexts Why Doing It in the Same Context Helps Better Habits Stable Contexts Stable Contexts Why Doing It in the Same Context Helps Repeating a behaviour in a similar place and situation can strengthen the association between the cue and the action. Repeating a behaviour in a similar place and situation can strengthen the association between the cue and the action. Self Improvement That Works mechanism pillar population context How context links cues and responses Choosing a repeatable setting What to do when routines vary self improvement that works stable contexts Introduction Habits become automatic more readily when a behaviour is repeated in a recognisably similar situation. The important ingredient is not simply doing something many times, but repeatedly pairing the action with cues that recur: finishing breakfast, arriving at a particular desk, putting the kettle on, or reaching a predictable point in an evening routine. With repetition, those cues can begin to prompt the behaviour with less conscious deciding. Reviews of habit psychology therefore define habits as learned responses that become linked to recurring contexts. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Illustrative overview This is why “same context” is useful advice for self improvement, although it needs interpreting sensibly. A context is not necessarily one exact room at one exact minute. It can be a stable configuration of time, place, preceding action, people or other noticeable cues. The practical goal is to create a dependable relationship between a cue and a response, while choosing a cue that survives ordinary variations in life. How context links cues and responses Early repetitions of a new behaviour usually require conscious control: you remember the goal, notice an opportunity and decide to act. Habit formation gradually changes that relationship. By repeatedly performing the same response when a particular cue is present, the cue and action become associated in memory. Eventually, encountering the cue can make the response more readily accessible, reducing how much deliberate thought is needed to get started. This cue driven mechanism is central to contemporary psychological accounts of habit. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... A classic real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates the process. Ninety six participants chose a simple eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context, such as “after breakfast”, for 12 weeks. Among participants whose data could be modelled, automaticity generally increased rapidly at first and then more slowly towards a plateau. The estimated time to reach 95% of a person's eventual automaticity varied enormously, from 18 to 254 days. Missing a single opportunity did not materially derail the process. The useful lesson is therefore not that a habit takes a magical number of days, but that repeated enactment in a consistent context gradually strengthens automaticity. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... More direct evidence for context stability came from a 2022 study of intentional habit building. In one experiment, 95 university students completed 2,482 study habit repetitions over six weeks. Some were instructed to keep the place and time stable, whereas others deliberately varied them. A second dataset examined 308 habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals; automaticity partly explained the relationship between stable context and goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 That finding is important because it suggests that a familiar context may do more than remind someone to begin. The researchers found evidence that matching the context in which earlier repetitions had occurred was also associated with smoother, more automatic performance once the behaviour was under way. In other words, familiar surroundings can support both the triggering and execution of a routine. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... Recent longitudinal evidence reinforces the overall picture without implying that context is the only determinant. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis following 254 people over six months found that context stability, behavioural frequency, autonomous motivation and several experiential factors were associated with habit strength. Participants showed different trajectories, including both habit formation and habit degradation. Stable context therefore appears to be one contributor within a dynamic learning process, not a guarantee that repetition will become automatic. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation PubMedApril 29, 2026... Published: April 29, 2026 Explanatory illustration 1 Choosing a repeatable setting The best context is usually one that occurs frequently, is easy to recognise and fits naturally into everyday life. “Read for ten minutes after I put my breakfast plate in the dishwasher” may provide a clearer recurring cue than “read more in the morning”. “Stretch after shutting down my work computer” may be more dependable than waiting until motivation to stretch appears. Crucially, the cue does not have to be a clock time. In a randomised trial involving 192 adults, participants linked a nutrition behaviour either to a daily routine or to a specific time and then reported their behaviour and automaticity for 84 days. Both approaches produced increases in automaticity, with no significant difference between routine based and time based plans. Among those who successfully formed habits, median time to peak automaticity was 59 days. What mattered especially was repeatedly carrying out the planned behaviour in response to the chosen cue. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That makes existing routines particularly useful anchors. They already happen reliably, so they provide ready made points at which another behaviour can be attached. An exploratory flossing study of 50 people found that participants told to floss after tooth brushing tended to develop stronger flossing automaticity than those told to floss before brushing; the difference was also evident at an eight month follow up. The researchers cautioned that the study was exploratory, but it illustrates why the position of a new action within an established routine may matter. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A useful context can therefore be built from several kinds of cue: A preceding action: meditate after making coffee; prepare tomorrow's lunch after clearing dinner. A place: begin focused work when sitting at a particular desk. A time or part of the day: take medication with breakfast or practise after arriving home. A recurring social setting: walk with the same colleague at lunchtime. A bundle of cues: the same approximate time, location and preceding routine occurring together. There is no strong basis for insisting that one cue type is universally superior. Research on physical activity shows why. A study of 1,244 Australian adults examined the consistency of time, people, activity, routine, location and mood cues. Greater consistency in routine and mood was associated with stronger physical activity automaticity, while consistent time and people were associated with more reported activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A smaller 2024 prospective study produced a somewhat different pattern: habit played a stronger role in subsequent physical activity when participants exercised at a consistent time, did the same activity or were in a similar mood, but consistent place, people and position within a routine did not significantly strengthen that relationship. The sample contained only 68 students, so this is better treated as a warning against oversimplification than as proof that place or routine cues are unimportant. Different behaviours may depend on different configurations of cues. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For practical purposes, a good cue has three qualities: it happens often enough, you reliably notice it, and it normally permits the intended response . A cue that repeatedly occurs when the action is impossible will not provide clean, dependable practice. “After lunch” may work well for a"},{"title":"The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","url":"/the-idea-that-inconsistent-people/","description":"Inconsistency is often easier to change by altering cues and friction than by treating every lapse as a shortage of character.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more... Do You Really Need More Willpower? Better Habits The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more... The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more... Do You Really Need More Willpower? Inconsistency is often easier to change by altering cues and friction than by treating every lapse as a shortage of character. Inconsistency is often easier to change by altering cues and friction than by treating every lapse as a shortage of character. Self Improvement That Works critique risk theme cluster population context Why character explanations are incomplete How environments influence behaviour Replacing self blame with system changes self improvement that works willpower myths Introduction The idea that inconsistent people simply need “more willpower” is attractive because it turns behaviour into a character story: disciplined people resist temptation; undisciplined people give in. Behavioural research gives a less moralising and more useful account. Self control matters, but success often depends on what happens before a difficult choice — which cues are present, how easy each option is, whether a useful response has been repeated in that setting, and whether temptation can be avoided rather than repeatedly defeated. Illustrative overview This distinction matters for self improvement because effortful resistance is only one form of self control. People who are good at pursuing long term goals do not necessarily spend their days winning heroic internal battles. Studies instead associate effective self control with encountering fewer problematic temptations, using anticipatory strategies and developing beneficial habits that reduce the amount of inhibition required in the first place. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 The practical lesson is not that willpower is imaginary or useless. It is that treating every lapse as evidence of weak character gives too much explanatory weight to the person at the exact moment of failure and too little to the system that repeatedly creates that moment. Why character explanations are incomplete One reason the popular willpower story has been so influential is the ego depletion hypothesis: the proposal that exerting self control uses up a limited internal resource, leaving less available for the next challenge. An influential 2010 meta analysis reported a substantial average depletion effect, helping establish the image of willpower as something rather like a muscle that becomes temporarily exhausted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis PubMedJuly 1, 2010... Published: July 1, 2010 Later evidence made that simple account much harder to defend. Reanalyses found evidence consistent with publication and small study biases, while a 2016 preregistered project involving 23 laboratories and 2,141 participants estimated an ego depletion effect of only d = 0.04, with a confidence interval spanning zero. A subsequent preregistered 36 laboratory project involving 3,531 participants again found no significant effect in its confirmatory analysis, estimating d = 0.06. Another multilab replication, using a different protocol, did detect an effect, but it was small: d = 0.10 in the full sample and d = 0.16 after specified exclusions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Publication bias and the limited strength model of self control: has the evidence for ego depletion been overestimated? PubMedJul... The scientific dispute is not completely settled. A 2024 review by proponents of limited willpower theories argues that improved methods, including stronger and longer fatigue manipulations, support the reality of depletion and that the original model should be refined rather than discarded. That makes “ego depletion has been disproved” an overstatement. But the evidence no longer justifies the stronger popular claim that ordinary self control failures can generally be understood as a predictable emptying of a single willpower tank. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Everyday data also resist an exclusively character based interpretation. In an experience sampling study, 205 adults supplied 7,827 reports about desires during a week. Whether people resisted successfully was associated not only with individual differences but with situational and interpersonal conditions. The presence and behaviour of other people, for example, were among the factors related to how desires developed and whether they were enacted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This suggests a better distinction. Capacity matters, but exposure matters too. Someone who checks social media five times during a working afternoon may indeed need to inhibit an urge at each encounter. But the behaviour is also being shaped by whether the phone is visible, notifications appear, the site is already open and checking requires almost no effort. Calling the fifth lapse “poor discipline” describes the end of the chain while ignoring most of the chain itself. Explanatory illustration 1 How environments influence behaviour The alternative to a character explanation is not environmental determinism. People still make choices. The important point is that choices do not arrive in a vacuum: their probability changes when the surrounding cues, costs and opportunities change. A useful framework is the process model of self control , which distinguishes strategies by how early they intervene. At the earliest point, a person can select or alter a situation; later, they can shift attention or reinterpret what is happening; only towards the end do they rely on direct inhibition of an already active impulse. Researchers argue that earlier interventions can be advantageous because they prevent a temptation from becoming as strong in the first place. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Daily life research supports that anticipatory view. In two studies of strategic self control, planning predicted whether people initiated self control, while situation selection, situation modification, distraction and reappraisal each independently predicted goal progress. Direct response inhibition — the strategy closest to the everyday image of “using willpower” — did not show consistent associations with progress. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Nipping Temptation in the Bud: Examining Strategic Self Control in Daily Life PubMed... Baumeister's \"Ego Depletion Theory\" affected by the replication crisis in psychology? Channel: Loyola Productions Munich Visualizing Minds Open on YouTube Small amounts of friction can change what people do “Friction” means the effort, inconvenience or number of steps required to perform an action. It can be physical — walking farther for something — or procedural, such as needing to log in again instead of opening an already authenticated app. A workplace field study at Google provides a concrete illustration. Employees using a drinks station nearer the snack area were more likely to take food than those using a more distant station. Among men, the recorded likelihood rose from 12% to 23%; among women, from 13% to 17%. Nobody had been forbidden to eat, instructed to diet or given a test of discipline. A change of a few metres altered the behavioural context. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Choice architecture research shows the same broad principle in other forms, although effects are neither universal nor permanent. A meta analysis of 58 studies involving 73,675 participants found sizeable average effects from making one option the default, but also substantial variation between studies and a few cases where defaults had no effect or moved behaviour in the opposite direction. A systematic review of choice architecture interventions targeting physical activity likewise found that many interventions changed behaviour while they were in place, but evidence of persistence after their removal was weaker. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Open source on cambridge.org. That caveat is important. “Design your environment” should not become another self help slogan promising effortless transformation. Environmental changes work differently across behaviours and people. A prompt may help someone take the stairs while it remains visible but cease to matter once removed. Defaults can be powerful in some decisions and weak in others. Systems alter probabilities; they do not abolish agency or guarantee outcomes. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org"},{"title":"Evidence Limits","url":"/evidence-limits/","description":"Useful findings still come with limits, including heterogeneous results, small literatures, and substantial variation between studies.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Evidence Limits How Strong Is the Evidence Behind Better Habits? Evidence Limits Evidence Limits How Strong Is the Evidence Behind Better Habits? Useful findings still come with limits, including heterogeneous results, small literatures, and substantial variation between studies. Useful findings still come with limits, including heterogeneous results, small literatures, and substantial variation between studies. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative pillar dataset evidence Meta analyses and systematic reviews Heterogeneity and risk of bias Using evidence without overclaiming self improvement that works evidence limits Introduction Popular self improvement claims often contain a real research finding wrapped in a stronger promise than the evidence can support. “If–then” plans can help people act on intentions; self monitoring can improve goal attainment; repeated behaviour in stable contexts can become more automatic; mindfulness and other structured practices can improve some measures of wellbeing. But the size and reliability of those benefits vary sharply across behaviours, populations, study designs and comparison groups. Reviews of self regulation research repeatedly find useful average effects alongside substantial heterogeneity and uneven methodological quality. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Illustrative overview The most credible version of evidence led self improvement therefore makes a narrower claim: some techniques improve the odds of change under particular conditions. It does not promise that one routine works for everyone, that an average effect predicts an individual result, or that a memorable number such as “21 days” represents a biological rule. Understanding how systematic reviews, heterogeneity, bias and replication affect those claims is what separates a research backed mechanism from a self help slogan. Meta analyses help — but the headline number is not the whole finding Meta analysis is valuable because it combines results from multiple studies rather than treating one striking experiment as definitive. Yet the pooled effect is an average across studies that can differ in participants, interventions, outcomes and methods. Cochrane guidance stresses that when such heterogeneity exists, a random effects meta analysis estimates an average across a distribution of effects; it does not establish that the average is what any particular person should expect. Prediction intervals, where appropriate, can be more informative because they show how widely the underlying effects may vary. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane That distinction changes how several familiar self improvement findings should be read. Implementation intentions are supported, but not as a universal productivity hack. A landmark 2006 meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large average effect, d = 0.65, for plans that specify in advance when, where or how a goal directed action will occur. This is good evidence for the basic proposition that linking a cue to an intended response can help translate intentions into behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Later evidence, however, shows why the original number should not be treated as a constant. A 2026 registered meta analysis of implementation intentions in children, drawing on 42 studies and 12,957 participants, found a smaller average effect, Hedges’ g = 0.31, while heterogeneity was high at I ² = 65.2%. Effects appeared stronger in some groups, including younger children. The underlying mechanism remains credible, but “if–then planning works” is more defensible than “if–then planning produces a 0.65 effect wherever it is used”. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Goal setting shows a similar pattern. A systematic review of 141 papers examining the unique effect of goal setting across behaviour change contexts found a small positive average effect, d = 0.34. That is meaningful evidence that goals can help, but it is far removed from the stronger popular claim that simply choosing a highly specific or ambitious goal reliably transforms performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Other meta analytic work has produced larger effects in particular settings: for example, specific difficult goals improved group performance relative to non specific goals in one review. The contrast is useful rather than contradictory. It suggests that effects depend partly on the task, comparison and social setting being studied. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The effect of goal setting on group performance: a meta analysis Pub Med The effect of goal setting on group performance: a meta analysis Progress monitoring has unusually broad experimental support, yet moderators still matter. A 2016 meta analysis identified 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions increased monitoring substantially and produced a more moderate improvement in goal attainment, d = 0.40. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded or reported publicly. The useful lesson is not merely “track everything”; it is that monitoring appears to help on average, and its design influences how much it helps. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 This is typical of credible behavioural science: the more closely the evidence is examined, the more conditional the message becomes. Explanatory illustration 1 The “66 day habit” shows how research turns into a rule it never claimed Habit formation is a particularly clear example of evidence being simplified beyond recognition. The influential real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues followed 96 volunteers who repeated a chosen eating, drinking or activity behaviour in a consistent context. Among participants for whom the automaticity curve could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of the eventual automaticity level ranged from 18 to 254 days . Sixty six days was a central value from the study, not a deadline built into human psychology. Missing one performance opportunity also did not materially derail the process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. A newer systematic review makes the uncertainty even clearer. It included 20 studies and 2,601 participants but found that only four studies directly reported the time required to reach habit formation. Reported medians were about 59–66 days, means in other studies reached 106–154 days, and individual estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days. Eleven of the 20 studies were rated at high risk of bias. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The evidence therefore supports the mechanism — repeated behaviour in a sufficiently stable context can become more automatic — considerably better than it supports a universal timetable. The familiar “21 day” claim fares worse still. Reviews tracing it back have linked it to anecdotal observations about adaptation following plastic surgery rather than to controlled research on behaviour becoming automatic. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This historical progression is instructive. A catchy fixed number circulated first; a comparatively small but careful field study later revealed extensive variation; and a subsequent systematic review confirmed both the basic phenomenon and the thinness of the literature on precise timing. Better evidence did not produce a more exact self help rule. It produced a less exact, more defensible one. Heterogeneity asks “for whom and when?”, not simply “does it work?” When studies disagree, researchers call the variation heterogeneity . Some variation is expected because interventions are delivered differently and because people, settings and outcome measures differ. Statistical measures such as I ² can indicate how much observed variability exceeds what would be expected from sampling error, but Cochrane cautions against treating I ² thresholds as rigid classifications, especially when few studies are available. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane For self improvement claims, heterogeneity is often the most practically important result. A pooled positive effect can coexist with small effects in some circumstances, substantial effects in others and little benefit elsewhere. That is exactly what a large meta review of self regulation mechanisms in health behaviour found. Across 66 meta"},{"title":"Exercise","url":"/exercise/","description":"Exercise becomes easier to repeat when the time, trigger, equipment, and first step are decided before the workout moment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Exercise Build an Exercise Routine That Starts More Easily Better Habits Exercise Exercise Build an Exercise Routine That Starts More Easily Exercise becomes easier to repeat when the time, trigger, equipment, and first step are decided before the workout moment. Exercise becomes easier to repeat when the time, trigger, equipment, and first step are decided before the workout moment. Self Improvement That Works governance example portfolio population context Choosing a reliable exercise cue Reducing preparation friction Tracking sessions rather than intentions self improvement that works exercise systems Introduction Exercise is easier to repeat when the routine is designed before the workout moment arrives. The most useful system fixes four things in advance: the cue that starts the session, the location and equipment, the first action , and the record that shows whether the session happened . This shifts the task from repeatedly deciding whether to exercise towards responding to a prepared situation. Illustrative overview Research on physical activity supports several parts of this approach. Implementation intentions — plans that link a specific situation to a specific response — produce small but meaningful improvements in physical activity on average. Habit research suggests that repeating exercise in a stable context can make starting feel more automatic, while studies of the built environment consistently find that accessible, convenient opportunities for activity matter. Self monitoring through step counters, wearables or other records can also increase physical activity, especially when combined with goals or additional support. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Impact of implementation intentions on physical activity by MAV da Silva · 2018 · Cited by 105 — Impact of implementation intentions on physical activity practice in adults: A systematic revi... The practical lesson is not that a clever cue or fitness tracker makes motivation irrelevant. It is that a good exercise routine governs the moments in which motivation normally has to do too much work. Choose a cue that survives ordinary life “I’ll exercise three times this week” leaves an important problem unresolved: when exactly does exercising begin? An implementation intention answers that question by specifying a recognisable situation and an action: “When I finish work on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, I will put on my trainers and walk for 20 minutes.” A systematic review and meta analysis of randomised trials found that interventions based on implementation intentions increased physical activity compared with controls. Earlier meta analytic work reached the same broad conclusion, although the average effect was relatively modest rather than transformative. Planning therefore appears useful, but it should be treated as one component of an exercise system rather than a guaranteed behavioural switch. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Impact of implementation intentions on physical activity by MAV da Silva · 2018 · Cited by 105 — Impact of implementation intentions on physical activity practice in adults: A systematic revi... For exercise, a cue works best when it is specific, easy to notice and already reliably present . Calendar time can work, but existing transitions are often easier to detect: after putting a work laptop away; after the school run; immediately after breakfast; on arriving home; after changing out of work clothes; after a regular Tuesday or Thursday meeting. Compare “exercise after work” with “when I close my laptop at 17:30 on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, I put on the clothes beside my desk and go outside”. The second arrangement governs more of the sequence. It identifies the trigger and prevents several small decisions — when to start, what to wear and what to do first — from accumulating at the point of action. This matters because exercise is not a single behaviour. Going for a run may require deciding to stop working, finding clothes, changing, locating headphones, choosing a route and finally walking out of the door. Research distinguishing exercise instigation from exercise execution is particularly relevant here. In a study using daily exercise diaries, the strength of the habit associated with starting exercise predicted exercise frequency more strongly than habitual performance of the workout itself. In practical terms, automating “begin the session” may matter more than making every part of the session identical. [King's College London] kclpure.kcl.ac.uk King's College LondonHabitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency King's College LondonJan... That means a cue does not need to dictate the entire workout. “After breakfast, I put on my walking shoes and leave the house” can remain stable even if the distance, pace or route changes. Explanatory illustration 1 Reduce preparation friction before motivation is tested Convenience is sometimes dismissed as laziness, but in exercise behaviour it is better understood as part of the environment governing the action. A systematic review examining motives and barriers across walking environments, cycling infrastructure, parks and sports facilities found easy accessibility repeatedly appearing as a facilitator of physical activity, while poor accessibility was a recurring barrier. Reviews of built environment interventions likewise find that improved walkability, recreation opportunities and accessible facilities can increase activity or use of activity settings, although results depend on context and study design. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Motives and Barriers Related to Physical Activity within Different Types of Built Environments: Implications for Heal... For an individual routine, the equivalent is to shorten the path between the cue and the first movement. Someone who wants to strength train after work could leave resistance bands beside the desk rather than storing them in a loft cupboard. Someone who walks in the morning could place shoes, coat and keys together the night before. A gym member deciding between an excellent facility requiring a complicated journey and a perfectly adequate gym passed on the commute should take convenience seriously rather than assuming enthusiasm will compensate indefinitely. The goal is not merely to save minutes. It is to remove decision points and failure points . Consider the difference between two nominally identical 30 minute workouts: High friction version: find clean kit → decide which workout to do → travel to a facility → discover a needed item is missing → improvise. Low friction version: cue occurs → put on prepared kit → start a predetermined session in an immediately available place. The exercise dose may be identical. The behavioural task is not. Broader environmental research reinforces the principle. Reviews have linked physical activity with access to recreational facilities, parks, destinations and walkable environments. Among older adults, a systematic review and meta analysis found positive associations between physical activity and factors including walkability, access to destinations, recreational facilities and parks. Such research cannot prove that moving a pair of dumbbells into the living room will produce the same effect as redesigning a neighbourhood, but it does support the larger point that opportunity and accessibility shape how much activity people actually perform. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A useful exercise system therefore asks a surprisingly concrete question: What has to happen between the cue and the first repetition or first step? Every unnecessary answer is a candidate for removal. Hack Your Habits for Lasting Change: Insights from Wendy Wood and Adam Grant Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Make the first step smaller than the whole workout Preparation becomes more powerful when the first required action is deliberately easy. “Complete an hour long workout” is a poor starting instruction because it asks the person to commit to the full cost of the session before beginning. “Put on the prepared shoes and walk outside” governs a much smaller action. This does not mean the workout itself must always be tiny. It separates starting criteria from performance criteria . A runner might usually complete five kilometres but define the routine’s minimum successful start as putting on running clothes and walking for five minutes. A strength training routine might begin with setting out the mat and completing the warm up. Once exercise has begun, continuing may be straightforward; on a difficult day, the smaller version still preserves the cue to action sequence. Habit theory provides a reason to care about that repetition. Habits are generally understood as learned associations between recurring contextual cues and responses. Repeatedly performing an"},{"title":"Meal Prep","url":"/meal-prep/","description":"Preparing ingredients or meals ahead can prevent healthy eating plans from collapsing when hunger meets an empty kitchen.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Meal Prep Make Better Meals Easier Before Hunger Hits Better Habits Meal Prep Meal Prep Make Better Meals Easier Before Hunger Hits Preparing ingredients or meals ahead can prevent healthy eating plans from collapsing when hunger meets an empty kitchen. Preparing ingredients or meals ahead can prevent healthy eating plans from collapsing when hunger meets an empty kitchen. Self Improvement That Works governance example portfolio policy intervention Where food routines commonly break down Preparing ingredients and portions Keeping meal preparation realistic self improvement that works meal prep Introduction Meal preparation works as a self improvement tool when it moves an important food decision away from the moment when that decision is hardest. Instead of arriving home hungry and asking, “What shall I eat?”, you arrange in advance for a reasonable option to be the easiest option: ingredients are ready, portions are available, or a complete meal only needs heating. The point is not culinary perfection. It is to reduce the number of choices, steps and delays standing between hunger and eating well. Illustrative overview That principle fits the evidence better than the stronger claim that everyone must cook extensively from scratch. Meal planning is associated with better diet quality and greater food variety, while more home food preparation is often associated with healthier eating. But much of this evidence is observational, so it cannot prove that preparation itself causes the improvement. British data also show that people can achieve a high quality diet without consuming large amounts of home prepared food. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical lesson is narrower and more useful: make tomorrow's acceptable meal easier before tomorrow's hunger arrives. Explanatory illustration 3 Where food routines commonly break down Healthy eating intentions often fail at a predictable handover point. The problem is not necessarily deciding that vegetables, pulses, wholegrains or balanced meals are desirable. It is converting that intention into food at 7 pm when someone is hungry, tired, short of time and looking at an unprepared kitchen. Time pressure is a well established part of this problem. Research on food choice has linked perceived time scarcity with less home preparation and greater reliance on fast food, restaurant meals and convenience foods. A population study of 1,319 US adults likewise found that people spending the least time on food preparation tended to be working adults who placed a high priority on convenience; those spending more time preparing food reported more frequent consumption of foods such as vegetables and fruit. These are associations rather than proof of cause, but they identify a realistic constraint: any eating system that repeatedly requires substantial effort at the end of a busy day is vulnerable to failure. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Time scarcity and food choices: An overview Time scarcity and food choices: An overview ScienceDirect... Meal preparation addresses that constraint by changing when the work happens. Washing vegetables while energy is available, cooking several portions together or choosing tomorrow's lunch tonight does not eliminate effort. It shifts effort from a high friction moment to a planned one. In behavioural terms, the evening meal becomes less of an open decision and more of an execution step. There is a useful parallel with implementation intentions: plans that connect a situation with a predetermined response. A meta analysis of 23 studies found that such plans had a moderate effect on increasing healthier foods and a smaller effect on reducing unhealthy eating. That asymmetry matters. It may be easier to ensure that a useful food is ready to be eaten than to rely on a rule forbidding every competing choice. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For meal preparation, that could mean replacing “I should eat better this week” with something operational: weekday lunches will be taken from the portions prepared on Sunday , or when I come home late, I will use one of the two freezer meals rather than decide from scratch . The improvement is not greater enthusiasm. It is clearer governance of the vulnerable moment. Explanatory illustration 1 Preparing ingredients and portions Meal preparation does not have to mean producing seven identical boxed dinners. The useful intervention is whatever removes enough work from the next meal to make the intended choice competitive with the easiest alternative. One approach is ingredient preparation . Vegetables can be washed or chopped, a grain or pulse can be cooked in advance, and a protein source can be portioned so that several different meals can be assembled quickly. This preserves more day to day variety than complete batch cooking while still removing much of the preparation barrier. A second approach is component preparation . One cooking session might produce rice, roasted vegetables, lentils and a sauce that can appear in different combinations during the week. The decision becomes “which prepared combination?” rather than “what meal can I invent, shop for and cook now?” This is especially useful when eating the same finished meal repeatedly would become tedious. A third approach is complete portions . Soups, stews, curries, pasta sauces and similar foods can be prepared in several servings, divided and refrigerated or frozen. The portion itself can then act as the default. NHS material explicitly recommends batch cooking, portioning meals and freezing them for later as a practical way of making home prepared food easier to use. [West London NHS Trust] westlondon.nhs.uk West London NHS Trust Food preparation and portions:: West London NHS Trust West London NHS Trust Food preparation and portions:: West London NHS Trust Portioning also changes the structure of the decision. Instead of facing a large container and deciding how much to serve while hungry, the serving decision has already been made. Experimental evidence illustrates why this can matter, although it should not be overgeneralised. In a 12 week randomised weight loss trial involving 183 adults with overweight or obesity, participants receiving portion controlled pre packaged lunch and dinner meals alongside behavioural counselling lost more weight on average than those selecting their own foods. That study tested commercial pre portioned meals within a weight loss programme, not ordinary household meal prep, so it does not prove that home portioning produces the same result. It does show that controlling the meal before the eating moment can materially alter behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The nutritional target does not need to be redesigned meal by meal either. The UK Eatwell Guide is intended to describe the balance of food across a day or even a week, rather than requiring every individual plate to reproduce its proportions exactly. That makes it well suited to advance planning: a small set of repeatable breakfasts, lunches and dinners can collectively cover fruit and vegetables, starchy carbohydrates, protein foods, dairy or alternatives and appropriate fats without demanding a fresh nutritional calculation every evening. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The Eatwell Guide The Eatwell Guide Your Food Isn't the Problem, This Is Channel: prep and purpose Open on YouTube Keeping meal preparation realistic The easiest way to make meal prep fail is to turn it into a second self improvement project. Elaborate weekly menus, large Sunday cooking sessions and perfectly measured containers can work for some people, but they also create a new burden. The better test is whether preparation reduces total friction across the week. That qualification is supported by the wider evidence on cooking. A systematic review of 38 observational studies found that home cooking is shaped by much more than cooking skill, including time availability, employment, relationships and cultural factors. The authors concluded that the evidence connecting home cooking itself with improved diet and health remained uncertain, in part because much of the literature was cross sectional. Reviews of cooking interventions have similarly noted weaknesses in study design and limited evidence about long term effects. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. So “cook everything yourself” is a poor universal rule. UK National Diet and Nutrition Survey research offers a useful counterexample. Among 1,063 adults who already had high quality diets, researchers found no adjusted difference in overall dietary quality or obesity prevalence between those consuming relatively high and low amounts of home prepared food. The low preparation group achieved its diet differently. The researchers therefore cautioned against presenting home preparation as a prerequisite for healthy eating."},{"title":"No Magic Formula","url":"/no-magic-formula/","description":"Evidence supports useful mechanisms, but no single routine, feedback style, or habit formula works equally well for everyone.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"No Magic Formula Why One Perfect Self Improvement Formula Does Not Exist Better Habits No Magic Formula No Magic Formula Why One Perfect Self Improvement Formula Does Not Exist Evidence supports useful mechanisms, but no single routine, feedback style, or habit formula works equally well for everyone. Evidence supports useful mechanisms, but no single routine, feedback style, or habit formula works equally well for everyone. Self Improvement That Works mechanism pillar dataset evidence What evidence supports consistently Where individual differences matter How to personalize without guessing blindly self improvement that works no magic formula Introduction Self improvement research supports useful behaviour change mechanisms, but it does not support a single routine, habit timetable or feedback formula that works equally well for everyone. Goal setting, self monitoring, implementation planning, environmental cues and timely feedback can all improve behaviour on average. Yet their effects vary with the behaviour being changed, the person using them, the situation and the way the technique is delivered. Even apparently simple processes such as forming a habit show striking individual variation. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Illustrative overview The practical implication is not that “anything goes”. It is almost the opposite. A useful personalised system begins with mechanisms that have evidence behind them, then treats their exact implementation as something to test and adjust. Instead of hunting for the perfect universal formula — a 21 day habit rule, an ideal morning routine or one motivational method — the aim is to discover which evidence based combination works reliably for a particular goal in a particular life. The Science of Motivation with Gabriele Oettingen The Science of Well Being Channel: Dr. Laurie Santos Open on YouTube What evidence supports consistently Behaviour change research rarely produces a universal prescription, but several tools have enough support to make sensible starting points. Clear goals help, but their design matters. A meta analysis of 141 papers and 16,523 participants found a small positive independent effect of goal setting on behaviour, with an average standardised effect size of 0.34. Effects differed according to characteristics of the goal and intervention: difficult goals, public goals and group goals were associated with stronger effects in that evidence base. That makes “set a goal” a defensible principle, but not a complete formula for deciding what kind of goal every person should use. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... Monitoring and feedback can close the gap between intention and action. In interventions targeting physical activity , diet and weight, researchers have repeatedly used self monitoring to make behaviour visible and feedback to guide subsequent choices. A 2024 systematic review found that adding feedback to physical activity self monitoring produced a positive pooled effect compared with self monitoring without feedback, although the underlying studies differed considerably and there was not enough evidence to declare one feedback format universally best. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and weight: a syste... Behavioural techniques tend to matter more than simply supplying information. A large meta analysis of interventions to increase physical activity, covering more than 99,000 participants, found modest overall improvements and suggested greater effects for interventions emphasising behavioural rather than purely cognitive strategies. Crucially, substantial unexplained variation remained between studies even after moderators were examined. The lesson is therefore more useful than “use technique X”: design the conditions for action, then measure whether the design is working. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Interventions to increase physical activity among healthy adults: meta analysis of outcomes PubMed... Tailoring can help, but personalisation itself is not magic. Meta analyses of computer tailored health interventions have generally found improvements over comparison conditions, and programmes that repeatedly updated their tailoring sometimes performed better over time than interventions based on a single initial assessment. Yet the size of the advantage depends on what is tailored, how often, for which behaviour and against what comparison. An earlier meta analysis of tailored print interventions, for example, found only a small overall benefit and substantial moderation by intervention and population characteristics. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)DEFINING WHAT WORKS IN TAILORING: A META ANALYSIS OF COMPUTERTAILORED INTERVENTIONS FOR HEALTH BEHAVIOR CHANGE PMCJ... This is an important distinction. Evidence supports a toolbox of mechanisms , not a universally optimal assembly of those tools. Explanatory illustration 1 Where individual differences matter The clearest reason to distrust rigid self improvement formulas is that averages conceal variation. An intervention can produce a useful average effect while helping some people substantially, helping others slightly and doing little for another group. A population result tells us what is promising; it does not automatically reveal the best configuration for one individual. N of 1 research exists precisely because between person trials and within person questions are not identical. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Personalized (N of 1) Trials: A Primer Pub Med Central (PMC)Personalized (N of 1) Trials: A Primer Habit formation offers a memorable example. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ real world study, participants selected a simple eating, drinking or activity behaviour and repeated it in a consistent context for 12 weeks. Among those whose automaticity curves could be modelled, the estimated time required to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days . That result is difficult to reconcile with any fixed claim that habits reliably form in 21, 30 or 66 days. The process had a broadly recognisable shape, but its speed varied enormously. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Variation also appears at the intervention level. A meta analysis of computer delivered health programmes found that their effects differed according to characteristics of participants, studies and interventions. More recent reviews of personalised mobile interventions likewise report promising average effects while describing the research field as immature and the evidence for particular tailoring choices as incomplete. One review of 31 personalised mobile interventions found positive pooled effects on lifestyle behaviours, but the interventions differed in what data they used and what they personalised. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The same caution applies even when personalisation sounds sophisticated. A 2023 review of computer tailored physical activity interventions for people with or at risk of long term conditions found small to medium benefits over generic or no information. However, it did not find strong evidence that any particular tailoring strategy, behaviour change theory or implementation method was consistently superior. The studies also showed medium to high heterogeneity. In other words, tailoring was promising, but research could not yet supply a recipe saying exactly how everybody should be tailored. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Individual differences are not limited to stable personality traits. The same person changes across situations. A reminder that is useful at 18:00 on a quiet weekday may be irritating during a meeting. A demanding exercise target may be productive after adequate sleep and unrealistic during an exhausting week. A study plan may work at home but collapse when travel removes the usual cue. Behaviour therefore depends not only on who the person is , but on what opportunities, constraints and competing demands exist at the moment of action. That is why newer behaviour change research increasingly distinguishes personalisation from static profiling. The objective is not simply to label someone as “a morning person”, “an obliger” or another fixed type. More sophisticated approaches change support according to context, timing, recent behaviour or current vulnerability. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Angela Duckworth and Katy Milkman on"},{"title":"Sleep Foundation","url":"/sleep-foundation/","description":"Sleep protects the attention and energy needed to carry out almost every other self-improvement plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Sleep Foundation Protect Sleep Before Adding More Productivity Tricks Better Habits Sleep Foundation Sleep Foundation Protect Sleep Before Adding More Productivity Tricks Sleep protects the attention and energy needed to carry out almost every other self improvement plan. Sleep protects the attention and energy needed to carry out almost every other self improvement plan. Self Improvement That Works evidence pillar population context Why sleep affects behavioural capacity What restricted sleep does to attention Building improvement plans around adequate rest self improvement that works sleep foundation Introduction Sleep is not merely another item on a self improvement checklist. It is part of the operating capacity that makes the rest of the checklist possible. Plans to study, exercise , resist distractions, prepare meals or finish difficult work all depend on being able to maintain attention, notice mistakes and keep exerting effort when the task stops being rewarding. Restricted sleep weakens those capacities, particularly sustained attention, and repeated short nights can produce deficits that accumulate even when a person no longer feels dramatically sleepier. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Illustrative overview That makes sleep a useful priority rule for self improvement: before adding another productivity technique, ask whether the plan is being built on adequate rest. For most healthy adults, expert guidance recommends at least seven hours of sleep regularly, while the NHS describes seven to nine hours as the average adult requirement. Individual need varies, but routinely cutting sleep to create more “productive” waking time can be self defeating if the extra hours come with poorer attention and less reliable follow through. [AASM] aasm.org Recommended Amount of Sleep for a Healthy Adult: A Joint Consensus Statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine and Sleep Rese... Why sleep affects behavioural capacity Much self improvement advice assumes that the main obstacle is deciding what to do. In practice, knowing the right action and reliably carrying it out are different problems. Execution requires sustained attention: the ability to remain engaged long enough to notice the relevant cue, keep the goal active and respond before attention drifts elsewhere. Sleep loss is unusually disruptive to this form of attention. Research commonly measures it with the psychomotor vigilance test, or PVT, a simple task in which people respond to unpredictable visual signals. Sleep deprived participants do not necessarily become uniformly slow. Instead, performance becomes unstable: many responses remain normal, but they are increasingly interrupted by unusually slow responses and outright lapses. This pattern helps explain why tired people may feel capable for stretches of time yet still make consequential errors. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 For ordinary self improvement, those brief lapses matter. A study session can be undermined by repeatedly losing the thread. A planned workout can fail at the transition between finishing work and getting changed. Administrative work can take longer because small mistakes require correction. The practical problem is therefore not simply “feeling tired”. It is that behaviour becomes less consistently governed by the goal from moment to moment. Sleep may also affect how willing people are to keep investing effort. A review of sleep deprivation and effort allocation found that sleep loss can make cognitive performance feel more effortful and reduce willingness to expend effort in pursuit of a goal. Strong incentives can sometimes produce compensatory effort, but compensation is incomplete and itself costly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance PubMedApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 This helps explain a familiar self improvement failure pattern: a demanding plan that seems reasonable when rested can feel disproportionately difficult after several short nights. There is also evidence linking sleep with self control . A systematic review and meta analysis covering experimental and observational research found an overall relationship between sleep and self control, although the literature varies by how sleep and self control are measured. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis Pub Med Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis One laboratory study went further, finding that a night without sleep shifted participants towards more habitual responding and away from goal directed control in an instrumental learning task. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That result should not be inflated into a claim that tired people automatically “lose discipline”, but it fits a broader principle: when cognitive capacity is constrained, relying on deliberate control becomes less dependable. Explanatory illustration 1 What restricted sleep does to attention The strongest practical evidence is not based on extreme all nighters alone. Controlled studies show that modest but repeated sleep restriction can accumulate into meaningful impairment. In a landmark laboratory experiment, healthy adults were assigned to four, six or eight hours of time in bed each night for 14 days. Performance on attention and other cognitive tasks deteriorated progressively in the four and six hour groups, whereas the eight hour group remained comparatively stable. By the end of the experiment, the cognitive deficits produced by repeated six hour nights were comparable on some measures to those seen after roughly one full night without sleep. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The especially important finding was that participants did not fully perceive this deterioration. Their objective performance continued to worsen across the restricted sleep period, while subjective sleepiness rose initially and then changed much less. People appeared to become accustomed to how sleepy they felt without becoming equivalently adapted in performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That mismatch is a problem for anyone using “I feel fine” as the test of whether sleep is sufficient. Separate controlled work has likewise found that subjective alertness can underestimate objective impairment under sleep restriction, particularly during the biological night. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Another inpatient experiment found that chronic short sleep increased reaction time impairment and attention lapses even when researchers prevented unusually long bouts of continuous wakefulness, strengthening the case that restricted sleep itself — not only marathon waking periods — degrades vigilance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Why Sleep Matters Matthew Walker Talks at Google Channel: Talks at Google Open on YouTube Attention is affected more consistently than every other ability The evidence does not support the exaggerated claim that one bad night uniformly damages every aspect of cognition. In a 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of 44 studies, restricting sleep to two to six hours for one night clearly increased sleepiness and impaired sustained attention: reaction times became slower and attentional lapses increased. Yet the same analysis did not detect significant overall effects on working memory, inhibitory control, choice reaction time or several other outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Individual experiments sometimes find little change in particular executive tasks after modest restriction. For example, one study of healthy young adults found no significant impairment on tests of response inhibition, selective attention or decision making after a single partial night of sleep restriction. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Another randomised trial in which participants lost approximately one hour of sleep nightly for six nights found an effect on working memory capacity but not its tests of sustained attention, inhibition or decision making. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These apparent inconsistencies are useful rather than inconvenient. “Cognition” is not one thing, and different tasks vary in difficulty, sensitivity and the amount of compensatory effort people can bring to them. Across broader bodies of research, sustained attention emerges as one of"},{"title":"Spending Systems","url":"/spending-systems/","description":"Adding friction before checkout can create time to reconsider unnecessary purchases instead of relying on restraint at the final click.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Spending Systems Add Friction Before an Impulse Purchase Better Habits Spending Systems Spending Systems Add Friction Before an Impulse Purchase Adding friction before checkout can create time to reconsider unnecessary purchases instead of relying on restraint at the final click. Adding friction before checkout can create time to reconsider unnecessary purchases instead of relying on restraint at the final click. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative example portfolio population context Why instant checkout encourages impulsive action Creating a review step before buying Tracking one problem spending category self improvement that works spending systems Introduction Impulsive spending is difficult to control precisely because modern buying systems are designed to remove pauses. Saved card details, mobile wallets and one click checkout compress the interval between wanting something and owning it. A useful spending system reverses that design: it deliberately adds a small amount of friction before payment, then makes the cost visible enough for the buyer to reconsider. Illustrative overview The principle is not that every purchase should become laborious. It is to identify the kinds of spending that repeatedly produce regret — clothes bought from social media adverts, takeaway orders, beauty products, hobby equipment or another personal trouble spot — and place a review step between desire and checkout. Research stretching from early studies of credit cards to newer experiments with digital shopping suggests that payment convenience can weaken spending restraint, while delays, reflection and timely spending feedback can sometimes restore it. [oup.com] academic.oup.com Oxford AcademicEffects of Payment Mechanism on Spending Behavior: The Role of Rehearsal and Immediacy of Payments Journal of Consumer R... The strongest practical lesson is therefore modest: do not expect willpower to arrive at the checkout button. Change the checkout process before the temptation appears. Stop Impulse Buying in 2026 (5 Simple Tricks) Channel: Primerica Open on YouTube Why instant checkout encourages impulsive action The problem predates smartphones. Researchers have long observed that payment mechanisms change how spending feels. In a 2001 study, Drazen Prelec and Duncan Simester found that participants instructed to pay by credit card were willing, in some situations, to pay substantially more than those using cash. Separate work by Dilip Soman found that previous spending influenced later purchase intentions more strongly when payments were immediate and when people had to record the amount, making the expenditure easier to remember. [CiNii] cir.nii.ac.jp CiNiiAlways Leave Home Without It: A Further Investigation of the Credit Card Effect on Willingness to Pay CiNii ResearchFebruary 1, 2001... Published: February 1, 2001 That distinction is often described in terms of payment transparency or payment coupling . Cash makes the loss conspicuous: money physically leaves the wallet at the same time as the purchase. Credit separates those events and makes the transaction less tangible. Experiments by Priya Raghubir and Joydeep Srivastava found greater willingness to spend with less cash like forms of payment, including gift certificates, and showed that increasing the salience of what was being spent could reduce the difference. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Monopoly money: the effect of payment coupling and form on spending behavior PubMed... Digital commerce pushes this historical trend further. Instead of finding cash, entering card numbers or even visiting another website, a shopper may move from advert to payment in seconds. A 2025 study of 1,162 digital payment users reported an association between the reduced psychological visibility and ease of digital payments and greater impulse buying. It is useful contemporary evidence, although its observational survey design means it cannot prove that digital payments themselves caused each extra purchase. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Spendception: The Psychological Impact of Digital Payments on Consumer Purchase Behavior and Impulse Buying PMCMarc... Nor should the argument be exaggerated into “faster checkout always causes impulse buying”. An experimental study of social commerce checkout found that keeping checkout inside Instagram did not, by itself, increase impulse buying as the researchers had predicted; brand familiarity and social cues mattered as well. The important point is narrower: removing transactional effort eliminates one opportunity to reconsider, but purchasing behaviour still depends on the product, shopper and surrounding cues. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Checkout button and online consumer impulse buying behavior in social commerce: A trust transfer perspective ScienceDirect... For someone trying to change their own spending, that is actually helpful. The goal is not to reconstruct a cash only economy. It is to identify where convenience is working against a prior intention — “I want to spend less on clothes this month” — and restore a deliberate decision point there. Explanatory illustration 1 Creating a review step before buying The simplest countermeasure is postponement : convert “buy now” into “decide later”. That can mean putting an item on a wish list rather than purchasing it, leaving it in the basket, or requiring a set waiting period for non essential purchases. Research on e commerce self control gives this idea more support than the familiar “24 hour rule” might suggest, but it also shows that the details matter. Carol Moser and colleagues studied interventions designed to interrupt impulse purchases. In one experiment, an approximately 25 hour delay reduced both the reported urge to buy and purchase intention. Another experiment prompted people to spend a few minutes listing reasons for and against a purchase; reflection also reduced purchase urge and intention. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Open source on umich.edu. A much shorter interruption did not work nearly as well. In a laboratory experiment in the same research programme, delaying Amazon purchases for ten minutes did not produce a statistically significant reduction in impulse items bought or money spent. One likely reason was that participants simply kept shopping during the delay. Friction works poorly if the waiting period leaves the person immersed in the same stream of tempting products. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Open source on umich.edu. That suggests a better design than merely staring at a countdown clock. A useful review step changes both time and context : 1. Capture rather than purchase. Put the item on a wish list, note or dedicated “later” basket. The desire is recorded, so postponement does not feel like forgetting it. 2. Leave the shopping environment. Close the app or browser instead of continuing to browse while the timer runs. 3. Review after the emotional peak has passed. For ordinary non essential purchases, reviewing the following day is more defensible than pretending there is a scientifically established universal number of hours. 4. Ask a concrete comparison question. “Would I choose this over the other things I intend to spend £60 on this month?” is more informative than “Do I really need it?”, which is easy to rationalise. 5. Buy deliberately if it still passes review. Friction is a filter, not a prohibition. Earlier qualitative work with online shoppers found that postponement was already one of the strategies people commonly described as successful: adding an item to a basket or wish list and returning later often allowed the initial urge to subside. In the same research, many participants expressed interest in tools that would impose a pause before checkout. [yardi.people.si.umich.edu] yardi.people.si.umich.edu Impulse Buying: Design Practices and Consumer Needs Impulse Buying: Design Practices and Consumer Needs Removing saved payment details applies the same idea in a cruder form. Instead of manufacturing a long waiting period, it inserts a small task — finding the card and entering its details — between desire and purchase. There appears to be much less direct experimental evidence for “delete your saved card” as a standalone intervention than popular advice sometimes implies, so it should be understood as an application of the wider friction principle rather than a proven treatment in its own right. The older payment research nevertheless supports the underlying logic that spending behaviour can change when payment becomes more salient and less automatic. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Monopoly money: the effect of payment coupling and form on spending behavior PubMed... Why You Can't Stop Spending Money (It's Not What You Think) Channel: Stackers University Open on YouTube Tracking one problem spending category Friction addresses the moment before a purchase. Monitoring addresses the repeated pattern"},{"title":"Work Prep","url":"/work-prep/","description":"Preparing the next task before finishing today can make tomorrow's work easier to begin without another round of setup decisions.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Work Prep Set Up Tomorrow's Work Before You Stop Today Better Habits Work Prep Work Prep Set Up Tomorrow's Work Before You Stop Today Preparing the next task before finishing today can make tomorrow's work easier to begin without another round of setup decisions. Preparing the next task before finishing today can make tomorrow's work easier to begin without another round of setup decisions. Self Improvement That Works mechanism example portfolio event time window Why starting friction matters Preparing the first visible next step Keeping end of day setup brief self improvement that works work prep Introduction A small amount of preparation before stopping work can make tomorrow’s first task materially easier to begin. The useful move is not to plan the whole next day in detail. It is to remove the first few decisions tomorrow will otherwise demand: decide what you will start, identify the first visible action, leave the relevant materials ready, and record anything that must be remembered. This shifts effort from the moment of initiation to a calmer moment when you are already engaged with the work. Illustrative overview The evidence comes from several related strands of research. Specific plans can improve goal initiation; external reminders can reduce reliance on prospective memory ; daily work planning can support performance; and making concrete plans for unfinished work can reduce the tendency for those tasks to keep intruding after hours. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The practical implication is narrow but useful: before you finish today, leave tomorrow with an obvious place to begin. Do You Have Any Tips for Closing Out My Work Day? Channel: Cal Newport Open on YouTube Why starting friction matters Starting a work task is often harder than continuing one because the opening minutes can contain several hidden decisions. Which task comes first? Which document is current? What was I trying to work out? Which tab, file or dataset do I need? What counts as the next action ? Each question is small, but together they create a miniature planning session before any substantive work has begun. Research on implementation intentions helps explain why advance decisions can matter. An implementation intention links a future situation to a specified response — in effect, deciding in advance what action follows when a particular cue appears. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall benefit for goal attainment and reported effects specifically on initiating goal directed behaviour. The proposed mechanism is not simply stronger motivation: specifying the cue and response makes the relevant opportunity easier to recognise and the intended response more automatic. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... For tomorrow’s work, the same principle can be used without turning the evening into a formal planning ritual. “Work on the report tomorrow” still leaves initiation unresolved. “When I sit down at 09:00, I will open the draft and rewrite the first paragraph of the findings section” has already settled the cue and the first action . The distinction is between preserving an intention and preparing its execution. There is also a memory problem. Remembering that something must be done later is known as prospective memory, and research shows that it becomes less reliable when attention and executive resources are occupied by demanding ongoing tasks. Externalising an intention — into a reminder, calendar, note or strategically positioned object — can substantially improve the chances that the intended action is carried out. Reviews of “intention offloading” conclude that external reminders are highly effective, while experimental work has found benefits from both self generated and externally supplied reminders. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Outsourcing Memory to External Tools: A Review of ‘Intention Offloading’ PMC... That makes a prepared workspace more than tidiness. A note at the top of a document saying “Next: compare the June and July figures” is an external memory cue. So is leaving the relevant spreadsheet open, putting the notebook on the keyboard, or placing the file you need at the top of tomorrow’s working folder. The environment carries part of the remembering burden. Explanatory illustration 1 Prepare the first visible next step The most useful end of day preparation usually answers one question: what should be visibly ready when work resumes? A good answer is concrete enough that tomorrow’s self can act before reconsidering the whole project. Suppose you are halfway through writing a proposal. Weak preparation would be to add “continue proposal” to a task list. Better preparation would be to leave the proposal open at the exact section that needs attention and add a short note: “Next: turn the three client objections into headings.” Tomorrow’s first action is then obvious. The same method transfers easily across different kinds of knowledge work: Writing: stop with a sentence describing the next paragraph, argument or source to add. Analysis: record the next comparison, calculation or data cleaning step, and leave the relevant file identifiable. Coding: leave a short note naming the failing test, function or next experiment rather than only “continue debugging”. Administration: pre select the first form, email thread or document that needs action. Creative work: leave a rough sketch, placeholder, question or unfinished transition that gives the next session something concrete to respond to. Meeting heavy work: prepare the first independent task before closing down, so the next open period does not begin with another round of prioritising. These examples are applications of the same mechanism rather than claims that every workplace should use an identical routine. The goal is to convert a broad project state into a small executable action. Research on daily planning supports the broader value of specifying tasks, priorities and when work will be done. In a two week experience sampling study, Parke and colleagues distinguished ordinary time management planning — making task lists, setting priorities and deciding how and when to perform tasks — from contingent planning, which anticipates interruptions. Time management planning was associated with greater engagement and performance, although its benefit weakened when interruptions were frequent; contingent planning was more resilient under interruption. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When daily planning improves employee performance: The importance of planning type, engagement, and interruptions PubMed... That finding adds an important qualification. Preparing tomorrow’s first task is most useful when the plan survives contact with the actual day. If mornings are routinely disrupted, “write for two uninterrupted hours at 09:00” may be brittle. A better setup could be: “When I get my first 25 minute uninterrupted block, open the draft at the marked section and write the three objection headings.” The preparation remains concrete without pretending that the schedule is fully controllable. Lower Stress With an End of Day Ritual Dr. Cal Newport & Dr. Andrew Huberman Channel: Huberman Lab Clips Open on YouTube Unfinished work does not need to remain mentally open End of day preparation may also help with what happens after work. Unfinished tasks are associated with continued work related thinking during off job time. A 2026 meta analysis covering 17 between person samples and 14 within person samples found that unfinished work was positively related to off job work thoughts, with the strongest associations for affective rumination. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unfinished work tasks and work related thoughts during off job time: meta analysis of the Zeigarnik effect in a work recovery conte... This does not mean every unfinished task will ruin an evening, nor that all work should be completed before stopping. Many jobs make that impossible. The more interesting evidence is that an unfinished goal can become easier to set aside once a credible plan exists for resuming it. In one influential series of experiments, people with activated but unfinished goals showed more intrusive thoughts and poorer performance on unrelated activities. When participants were allowed to form specific plans for completing those goals, the cognitive interference effects disappeared. The researchers argued that making a plan can satisfy some of the mental processes that would otherwise keep the unfulfilled goal active. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Workplace research reaches a similar conclusion. Brandon Smit followed 103 employees pursuing 1,127 work goals and found that people had greater difficulty mentally"},{"title":"Immediate Rewards","url":"/immediate-rewards/","description":"A small reward available now can make healthy or productive actions more attractive when their main payoff is far away.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Immediate Rewards Why Good Habits Need Some Reward Now Better Habits Immediate Rewards Immediate Rewards Why Good Habits Need Some Reward Now A small reward available now can make healthy or productive actions more attractive when their main payoff is far away. A small reward available now can make healthy or productive actions more attractive when their main payoff is far away. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative theme cluster mechanism The problem with delayed benefits Choosing rewards that fit the behaviour When rewards fade or backfire self improvement that works immediate rewards Introduction Many worthwhile habits have an awkward timing problem: the effort is immediate, while the main reward is distant. Exercise can feel tiring today while its health benefits accumulate over months and years. Studying costs an evening now while qualifications and career gains arrive later. Saving means giving something up today for financial security that may be decades away. In these situations, relying entirely on the future benefit asks the present self to keep paying for a reward it cannot yet enjoy. Illustrative overview A practical solution is to give the behaviour some value now . The immediate reward does not need to be large, expensive or artificial. Enjoyable music during exercise, a favourite coffee while doing difficult paperwork, an appealing study environment, visible progress, praise, or access to an absorbing audiobook only while walking can all change how the behaviour feels in the moment. Modern research suggests that these near term rewards can be disproportionately important for persistence, even when people say their real reason for acting is the distant payoff. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 The aim is not to bribe yourself forever. It is to narrow the gap between what is good for you later and what feels worth doing now . The problem with delayed benefits The idea that consequences shape repeated behaviour is much older than modern self improvement advice. At the turn of the twentieth century, psychologist Edward Thorndike developed what became known as the law of effect : responses followed by satisfying consequences become more likely to recur in similar circumstances. Later theories of reinforcement changed the terminology and mechanisms, but retained the central insight that what follows an action can affect whether that action happens again. [APA Dictionary] dictionary.apa.org Dictionary APA Dictionary of Psychology APA DictionaryAPA Dictionary of PsychologyApril 19, 2018... Published: April 19, 2018 That classical learning tradition helps explain a problem that long term goals create. The natural reinforcement for many valuable behaviours is badly delayed. One workout does not produce cardiovascular health. One revision session does not produce a degree. One pension contribution does not produce a comfortable retirement. The behaviour and its most important consequence are separated by so much time that the distant outcome can be excellent at justifying the goal while remaining relatively weak at making tonight's action appealing. Contemporary goal research makes this distinction unusually clear. In a series of studies, Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach examined people pursuing activities such as exercise, studying and New Year's resolutions. Participants generally said they pursued these goals for delayed benefits, yet immediate rewards such as enjoyment were more strongly associated with how long they persisted . The pattern appeared within individual exercise and study sessions and across periods ranging from a week to several months. The researchers' synthesis was not that future benefits are unimportant, but that delayed rewards seem particularly useful for deciding which goals are worth pursuing, whereas immediate rewards are more closely connected with continuing the activity once it begins. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 This helps resolve an everyday puzzle. Someone may be completely convinced that exercise is important and still repeatedly skip it. There is no contradiction. The distant reward may have successfully generated the intention while failing to make the next workout sufficiently attractive. The imbalance also fits broader research on delay discounting : people tend to value outcomes less when they are further away in time, although the strength of this tendency varies considerably between individuals and situations. A systematic review of experimental work found that preferences for sooner versus later rewards are not completely fixed; they are sensitive to framing, attention and context. For habit design, however, an attractive implication is simpler than trying to eliminate present bias altogether: instead of demanding that the distant reward win every contest, give the beneficial behaviour a worthwhile present component too. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral trainings and manipulations to reduce delay discounting: A systematic review PMCJuly 3, 2019... Published: July 3, 2019 Explanatory illustration 1 Choosing rewards that fit the behaviour The strongest version of this strategy is usually not “do something unpleasant, then pay yourself”. It is to make the activity itself more rewarding, or to attach a pleasant experience closely enough that the two begin to function as a package. Woolley and Fishbach demonstrated this distinction experimentally. In research on exercise and healthy eating, directing people towards more enjoyable versions of an instrumental activity increased persistence. Choosing an exercise because it was enjoyable rather than merely useful, for example, could support longer engagement even though fitness remained the long term objective. Their work suggests that people often optimise too heavily for delayed usefulness when choosing how to pursue a goal and underestimate how much the immediate experience will determine whether they continue. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) For the Fun of It: Harnessing Immediate Rewards to Increase Persistence in Long Term GoalsJanuary 5, 2016... Published: January 5, 2016 A useful hierarchy follows from that evidence. First, improve the activity itself. If several behaviours produce similar long term benefits, prefer the version you genuinely like. Walking with a friend may be more sustainable than forcing yourself onto a treadmill you dislike. A learner may persist longer with interesting practice material than with an allegedly optimal resource they dread opening. Healthy food that is both nutritious and enjoyable is generally a better behavioural proposition than food chosen solely because it appears virtuous. Second, pair the behaviour with something immediately pleasant. Music, podcasts, comfortable surroundings, companionship or a favourite drink can supply a modest reward without changing the underlying task. The closer the positive experience occurs to the target action, the clearer the behavioural association is likely to be. Third, use an external reward when the activity offers little natural enjoyment. A small point score, streak marker, financial incentive or planned treat can make an otherwise unrewarding action more attractive. Such rewards have evidence behind them, but they require more care because they may disappear, become expensive or shift attention away from the activity itself. Experiments comparing reward timing add an important refinement. Woolley and Fishbach found that receiving a reward sooner rather than later could increase participants' reported intrinsic motivation for an activity, even when the reward itself was external. Their proposed mechanism was associative: when reward and activity occur close together, the positive experience becomes more tightly connected with doing the activity. The timing effect was separable from simply increasing the size of the reward. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 That makes “I will buy myself something at the end of the month if I exercise enough” a rather different design from “my favourite podcast is what I listen to on my evening walk”. Both offer incentives, but only the second makes each individual repetition immediately better. An Introduction to 'Get It Done' by Ayelet Fishbach Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Temptation bundling turns a"},{"title":"Motivation Limits","url":"/motivation-limits/","description":"Wanting a result matters, but motivation fluctuates too much to serve as the only mechanism behind consistent action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Motivation Limits Why Motivation Alone Rarely Carries a Habit Better Habits Motivation Limits Motivation Limits Why Motivation Alone Rarely Carries a Habit Wanting a result matters, but motivation fluctuates too much to serve as the only mechanism behind consistent action. Wanting a result matters, but motivation fluctuates too much to serve as the only mechanism behind consistent action. Self Improvement That Works evidence pillar mechanism Intention versus execution Why motivation changes from day to day Building supports for low motivation moments self improvement that works motivation limits Introduction Wanting a result matters, but wanting it strongly is not the same as reliably doing the behaviour that produces it. Research repeatedly finds an intention–behaviour gap : people can form sincere intentions to exercise , study, eat differently or follow another self improvement goal without consistently acting on them. In experimental evidence, even fairly large increases in intention have produced much smaller changes in behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does changing behavioral intentions engender behavior change? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... Illustrative overview This does not make motivation irrelevant. Better quality, more autonomous motivation is associated with greater persistence, and motivation can be especially important when a behaviour is new. The limitation is that motivation is a changing internal state, while long term improvement requires action on ordinary days as well as enthusiastic ones. Effective behaviour change therefore uses motivation to choose and begin a direction, then builds ways of acting that require less motivation each time. Explanatory illustration 3 Intention is not execution A useful distinction in self improvement is between deciding to act and successfully initiating action when the opportunity arrives . An intention such as “I am going to exercise three times this week” settles the first problem. It does not automatically solve remembering , choosing the right moment, stopping another activity, tolerating inconvenience or beginning when the anticipated reward feels distant. The size of this gap is visible in experimental research. Webb and Sheeran reviewed 47 tests in which an intervention successfully changed people's behavioural intentions and then measured what they actually did. The average change in intention was medium to large, with an effect size of 0.66, but the corresponding behavioural change was only 0.36. In other words, making people want or intend something more strongly helped, but much of the additional intention did not become action. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does changing behavioral intentions engender behavior change? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... Studies following people in daily life make the problem more concrete. In one ecological momentary assessment study, adults repeatedly reported short term intentions to be physically active and wore accelerometers afterwards. Participants performed at least ten minutes of moderate to vigorous activity after only 16% of prompts at which they had reported an intention to exercise. The exact figure should not be generalised to every behaviour or population, but it demonstrates how rapidly intention and execution can separate when measured at the moment action is supposed to occur. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Momentary assessment of physical activity intention behavior coupling in adults PubMed... A later study using 2,341 momentary assessments found a smaller but still clear gap: intended physical activity was not carried out in about a quarter of relevant episodes. More specific planning, stronger self efficacy and intrinsic motivation all increased the probability that an intention became action. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Within person association of volitional factors and physical activity: Insights from an ecological momentary assessment stud... The important point is not that intentions are weak or useless. It is that an intention leaves several practical problems unresolved. That is why planning can outperform simply trying to intensify desire. Implementation intentions specify a response to a future situation: “If situation X occurs, I will do Y.” A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large average effect on goal attainment, while a separate meta analysis found that implementation intentions improved prospective memory — remembering to carry out intended future actions. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... These plans do not manufacture endless motivation. They reduce how much has to be decided at the critical moment. Explanatory illustration 1 Motivation changes from day to day Treating motivation as a stable personal resource creates a second problem: the same person does not have the same motivational state every day . Energy, mood, competing rewards, stress, perceived effort and the attractiveness of an outcome can all change between occasions. Recent intensive longitudinal research measured motivation repeatedly in everyday life rather than treating it as a fixed personality characteristic. In a 15 day study involving 3,344 momentary assessments, researchers found substantial within person fluctuations in motivation. Those momentary states predicted willingness to exert effort over and above participants' more stable motivational characteristics. Higher state motivation increased sensitivity to prospective rewards and made effortful choices more attractive. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Physical activity research shows a similar pattern. Momentary desire for activity varies across the day, rather than remaining fixed at the level captured by a one off questionnaire. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. This matters for self improvement because a plan that works only when the desired behaviour feels appealing is exposed to every downward fluctuation. Consider two approaches to evening study. One person relies on the thought, “I will revise when I feel sufficiently motivated.” Another has already chosen a 20 minute task, placed the materials on the desk and linked starting to finishing dinner. Both may value the qualification equally. The second arrangement simply leaves fewer parts of execution dependent on how attractive studying happens to feel at 7.30 pm. This is not an argument for ignoring the quality of motivation. Self determination research distinguishes relatively autonomous motivation — acting because something is valued or intrinsically rewarding — from behaviour driven mainly by external pressure. A systematic review of 66 exercise studies found more autonomous forms of motivation consistently associated with exercise participation; there was also evidence that intrinsic motivation may become particularly important for longer term adherence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2025 review of reviews likewise found competence, intrinsic regulation and identified regulation positively associated with physical activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. But this strengthens rather than overturns the main point. Motivation has degrees and qualities , and better motivation improves the odds of persistence. It still does not remove the execution problem. In a meta analysis of 65 independent tests, interventions based on self determination theory produced a statistically significant but small average effect on health behaviour, about d=.23, with autonomous motivation and perceived competence helping to explain behavioural improvement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Motivation is one mechanism in sustained action, not a substitute for all the others. The gap between planning and doing Kirsten Rohde TEDxErasmusUniversity Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube Build for low motivation moments The practical test of a self improvement system is therefore not whether it feels inspiring on the day it is designed. It is whether the intended behaviour remains easy enough to initiate when enthusiasm is ordinary or low. One way to reduce motivational dependence is to attach behaviour to predictable cues . Habit research describes habits as learned associations through which recurring contexts can activate responses with relatively little deliberate thought. Once such associations become strong, action need not be reconstructed from motivation and conscious decision making on every occasion. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Pub Med Psychology of Habit The transition is important: motivation often helps create the repetitions from which a habit can develop, but greater automaticity can subsequently make repeated performance less sensitive to motivational"},{"title":"Track Progress","url":"/track-progress/","description":"Recording whether a planned behaviour actually happened can reveal patterns that memory and good intentions often hide.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Track Progress Why Tracking What You Did Changes Results Better Habits Track Progress Track Progress Why Tracking What You Did Changes Results Recording whether a planned behaviour actually happened can reveal patterns that memory and good intentions often hide. Recording whether a planned behaviour actually happened can reveal patterns that memory and good intentions often hide. Self Improvement That Works evidence pillar dataset evidence What progress monitoring changes What the meta analysis found Keeping tracking simple enough to continue self improvement that works track progress Introduction Progress tracking works best when it answers a simple question: did the behaviour you planned actually happen? Memory is a poor substitute. After a busy week, it is easy to remember the two workouts you completed and overlook the three you skipped, or to feel that you have been studying “quite regularly” without knowing whether that means six sessions or two. Recording behaviour turns a vague impression into information that can guide the next decision. Illustrative overview The strongest broad evidence comes from a meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions that deliberately increased monitoring produced better goal attainment, with an average standardised effect of d = 0.40 . Importantly, the effect was stronger when people physically recorded their progress or reported it to somebody else. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That does not mean elaborate dashboards or obsessive measurement are necessary. The useful part of tracking is the feedback loop: record what happened, compare it with the plan, notice a gap early and adjust what you do next. What progress monitoring changes A goal such as “ exercise regularly” or “write more” exists partly as a mental standard. To regulate behaviour, however, you need some way of comparing that standard with reality. Models of self regulation describe progress monitoring as a recurring process: attend to relevant information, update your picture of where you are, compare it with the target and recognise any discrepancy. That discrepancy can then prompt more effort, a change in strategy or, where appropriate, a revision of the goal. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The neural basis of monitoring goal progress PubMed Central (PMC)The neural basis of monitoring goal progress PMCSeptember 10, 2014... Published: September 10, 2014 Consider someone who intends to walk for 30 minutes on four weekdays. Without records, Friday’s assessment might be, “I did reasonably well this week.” With four boxes marked Monday to Thursday, the situation is harder to blur: perhaps only Monday and Wednesday happened. The tracker has not supplied motivation by itself. It has supplied accurate feedback while there is still something that can be done about it . That distinction matters. Tracking is not primarily a way to document success after the fact. It gives a person repeated opportunities to detect a widening gap between intention and action. Researchers studying avoidance of goal information have described an “ostrich problem”: people sometimes avoid information about progress precisely when it is uncomfortable to see. Yet avoiding that information also removes one of the signals that could trigger corrective action. [DOI] doi.org DOI‘The Ostrich Problem’: Motivated Avoidance or Rejection of Information About Goal Progress Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psyc... The most useful thing to monitor is therefore usually close to the behaviour you can control. If your goal is to improve fitness, “went for planned walk: yes/no” may be more actionable than continually checking changes in body weight. If you want to write a book, tracking writing sessions or words drafted gives quicker feedback than repeatedly estimating how close you feel to “becoming an author”. Outcome measures can still be useful, particularly where they genuinely inform decisions. But behavioural tracking answers the question that matters most for follow through: am I repeatedly doing the thing I said I would do? Explanatory illustration 1 What the meta analysis found Benjamin Harkin and colleagues’ 2016 Psychological Bulletin meta analysis is particularly useful because it did not merely compare people who happened to track themselves with people who did not. It examined randomised interventions designed to increase progress monitoring , and required studies to report both whether monitoring increased and whether goal attainment subsequently changed. Across 138 studies, the interventions produced a very large increase in monitoring frequency, d = 1.98 , and a smaller but meaningful improvement in goal attainment, d = 0.40 . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The analysis also found that increases in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the improvement in goal attainment. In other words, interventions that succeeded in getting people to monitor more tended to produce larger improvements in results. This strengthens the case that monitoring itself matters rather than being an incidental feature of successful programmes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Two moderators are especially practical. Recording mattered. Effects on goal attainment were larger when people physically recorded the information they obtained from monitoring rather than merely noticing it mentally. A tick on a calendar, an entry in a notebook or an automatically logged step count creates a record that can be revisited. The original researchers suggested several possible explanations, including better encoding and retrieval of progress information, although the moderator analysis cannot by itself establish exactly which mechanism is responsible. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence White Ros... Reporting progress mattered too. Monitoring had larger effects when progress was reported or made public rather than kept entirely private. Accountability is one possible explanation: knowing that progress will be reported may increase effort or commitment. But the evidence does not mean everyone needs to broadcast personal goals online. A private weekly check in with a friend, coach or colleague can also turn an otherwise invisible intention into something that will be revisited. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The headline effect should still be interpreted with some restraint. The Harkin meta analysis found substantial variation between studies, and statistical tests indicated possible small study or publication related bias. One adjustment method reduced the estimated goal attainment effect from d = 0.40 to d = 0.19 , although the authors cautioned that such methods can themselves be unreliable when studies are highly heterogeneous. They also noted that much of the evidence concerned health related behaviour, leaving a thinner base for some other kinds of personal goals. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. A later meta review of 66 meta analyses on self regulation and health behaviour reached a similarly qualified conclusion. It found support for self monitoring in several contexts but stressed that behaviour change techniques were not uniformly successful across all behaviours and populations. It also rated the methodological quality of the Harkin review as limited on several contemporary review criteria, including protocol registration and risk of bias reporting. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Regulation Mechanisms in Health Behaviour Change: A Systematic Meta Review of Meta Analyses, 2006–2017 PMC... So the sensible reading is not “tracking guarantees success”. It is that deliberately increasing progress monitoring has a reasonably strong experimental evidence base as a way of improving follow through, while the size of the benefit varies substantially by goal and implementation . The Science of Setting and Achieving Goals Emily Balcetis Knowledge Project Podcast Channel: The Knowledge Project Podcast Open on YouTube Tracking works when it stays connected to action More measurement is not automatically better. A tracker becomes useful when its information changes what you do. Suppose someone plans five"},{"title":"Fewer Decisions","url":"/fewer-decisions/","description":"Good systems remove unnecessary choices at the point of action so hesitation has fewer opportunities to derail the plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Fewer Decisions Make Fewer Choices When It Is Time to Act Better Habits Fewer Decisions Fewer Decisions Make Fewer Choices When It Is Time to Act Good systems remove unnecessary choices at the point of action so hesitation has fewer opportunities to derail the plan. Good systems remove unnecessary choices at the point of action so hesitation has fewer opportunities to derail the plan. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative theme cluster policy intervention Why decision points create friction Planning the action sequence in advance Simplifying recurring routines self improvement that works fewer decisions Introduction Self improvement becomes easier to execute when fewer choices remain at the exact moment action is required. The useful move is not to eliminate choice from life, but to make important choices earlier: decide the cue, the first action and, where useful, the sequence that follows. Then the real world moment becomes less like “What should I do now?” and more like “This is the situation I planned for; begin.” Illustrative overview Research on implementation intentions — specific if–then plans linking a future situation to a response — provides the clearest evidence for this approach. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found that such plans improved goal attainment with a medium to large average effect, while later studies have shown smaller but still useful effects in particular domains such as physical activity . Crucially, the mechanism is not well described as simply “saving willpower”. Planning appears to make the relevant cue easier to notice and the planned response easier to retrieve and initiate. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The practical lesson is narrower and more defensible than the popular claim that every trivial decision drains a fixed mental energy reservoir: where hesitation repeatedly disrupts a behaviour you already want, move the decision upstream. Why decision points create friction There is a difference between deciding what you value and deciding, under pressure, whether to act on it. Someone may genuinely intend to exercise after work, study in the evening or make an important appointment, yet still reach the relevant moment without a settled answer to several smaller questions: When exactly? Where? What comes first? What if I feel tired? Do I start now or after one more task? Each unanswered question creates another point at which the planned behaviour can be delayed, renegotiated or forgotten. This is one form of the long studied intention–behaviour gap : wanting an outcome does not reliably produce the action needed to obtain it. Implementation intention research developed specifically to address that gap by supplementing a goal — “I intend to do X” — with a predetermined response to a recognisable situation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Promoting the translation of intentions into action by implementation intentions: behavioral effects and physiologica... That approach matters because ordinary goal execution often occurs while attention is occupied elsewhere. Psychologists studying prospective memory — remembering to perform an intended action later — distinguish forming an intention from successfully recognising the later opportunity and executing it. A 2015 meta analysis of 36 comparisons found that implementation intention procedures improved prospective memory performance overall, with an estimated medium sized effect. The evidence therefore fits a familiar everyday failure: sometimes the problem is not deciding that something matters but retrieving the right action at the right moment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: A systematic and meta analytic review ScienceDirectMarch 30... Experimental work also gives a more precise explanation than “less thinking is always better”. Implementation intentions appear to strengthen both the mental accessibility of the specified cue and the connection between that cue and the selected response. In one test of these component processes, people who planned how to perform a task achieved their goals more successfully, and the advantage was statistically mediated by greater cue accessibility and stronger cue–response links. In other words, planning changes what happens when the situation arrives: the relevant trigger is more likely to stand out, and the response is more readily available. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do implementation intentions promote goal attainment? A test of component processes ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 1 The “decision fatigue” explanation is too simple This distinction is important because practical advice about reducing decisions is often justified with a stronger claim: that making choices consumes a finite self control resource and that subsequent decisions therefore become progressively worse. That theory became highly influential, but evidence for its classic ego depletion formulation has been contested. In a preregistered replication involving 23 laboratories and 2,141 participants, the estimated ego depletion effect was very small and its confidence interval included zero. A later 12 laboratory replication involving 1,775 participants did obtain a statistically significant effect, but it was small, at about d=0.10, rising to d=0.16 after an exclusion analysis. The current evidence therefore does not justify treating willpower as a simple battery that is predictably emptied by each preceding choice. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceA Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect Perspectives on Psychological Sci... Reducing action time decisions still makes sense, but for firmer reasons. Pre deciding can remove ambiguity, prevent repeated reconsideration, improve recognition of a useful cue and make a desired response easier to retrieve. Those mechanisms do not require the stronger claim that choosing breakfast has literally used up a measurable stock of decision making energy. Nor does research on choice overload support the slogan that more options are always harmful. A meta analysis of 99 observations involving 7,202 participants found that the effects of large choice sets depended on conditions including how complex the options were, how difficult the decision was, how uncertain people were about their preferences and what they were trying to achieve. The practical target, then, is not “choices” in general. It is unnecessary or poorly timed choice when a known action already needs to occur. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta analysis ScienceDirect Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta analysis Why 99% Of People Will Never Achieve Their Goals (According To Psychology) Channel: Mindozen Open on YouTube Plan the action sequence in advance The strongest version of decision reduction is a plan that identifies both a trigger and an action : If situation X occurs, I will do Y. That structure was developed in implementation intention research from the 1990s onwards. Its distinctive contribution was to separate two acts that everyday goal setting often merges together: first choosing the goal, and then specifying how the goal will be enacted when the relevant circumstance appears. The 2006 Gollwitzer and Sheeran meta analysis found positive effects not only on getting started, but also on maintaining goal pursuit in the face of distractions and changing course when an existing strategy was failing. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... A useful plan therefore converts a vague future deliberation into a recognisable hand off: “If I finish breakfast on a weekday, I open the language learning exercise before opening social media.” “If I shut down my work computer, I put on my walking shoes.” “If I notice I am postponing the difficult report, I open it and work only on the next unresolved paragraph.” “If my usual exercise time is disrupted, I do the shorter home session instead.” The point is not the wording. The point is that the person has already decided what event will count as “go”. Specificity can matter. A large workplace field experiment involving 3,272 employees tested planning prompts for free influenza vaccination clinics. Everyone received information about the clinics. One group was additionally prompted to write down a date; another was prompted to record a date and a time . Vaccination uptake in the control group was 33.1%. Adding a date alone raised uptake by 1.5 percentage points, a difference that was not statistically significant, whereas specifying both date and time increased vaccination by 4.2 percentage"},{"title":"Design Around Temptation","url":"/design-around-temptation/","description":"Changing what is visible, accessible, or convenient can reduce the number of times you must resist a tempting alternative.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Design Around Temptation Change the Environment Instead of Fighting Yourself Better Habits Design Around Temptation Design Around Temptation Change the Environment Instead of Fighting Yourself Changing what is visible, accessible, or convenient can reduce the number of times you must resist a tempting alternative. Changing what is visible, accessible, or convenient can reduce the number of times you must resist a tempting alternative. Self Improvement That Works mechanism pillar policy intervention Removing tempting defaults Making better choices more convenient Examples for devices, food, and spending self improvement that works design around temptation Introduction Environment design reduces everyday temptation by changing what is visible, close at hand, easy to start and selected by default. The aim is not to eliminate choice or to pretend that willpower never matters. It is to arrange ordinary settings so that fewer moments require a difficult act of resistance in the first place. Research on self control describes this as an antecedent focused strategy: intervene before an impulse has gathered strength rather than waiting until temptation is fully active and then trying to suppress it. Field experiments with students have found that modifying tempting situations can improve goal attainment and reduce experienced temptation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Illustrative overview This gives environmental design a distinctive place in self improvement that works . Instead of repeatedly asking, “Can I resist this?”, ask, “Why am I being asked to resist this so often?” Moving distracting apps, changing notification settings, rearranging food, adding steps to an impulse purchase or putting a useful alternative within immediate reach can shift behaviour before the internal argument starts. Remove tempting defaults A tempting option does not need to be irresistible to cause problems. It only needs to be repeatedly available at moments when attention is elsewhere, energy is low or the alternative requires slightly more work. Small differences in effort can accumulate because they affect dozens or hundreds of ordinary choices. This is the basic mechanism behind choice architecture : changing the structure of a decision without necessarily banning alternatives or substantially changing their price. A major meta analysis covering more than 200 studies found that choice architecture interventions changed behaviour on average, although results varied substantially by technique and domain. Interventions that altered the structure of choices — including defaults and option related effort — tended to outperform interventions that merely supplied information. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The effectiveness of nudging: A meta analysis of choice architecture interventions across behavioral domains PMCJan... For personal use, the relevant lesson is not that every “nudge” works. It is that friction and defaults are behavioural variables you can deliberately edit . If opening a social media feed requires one tap from the home screen while reading the document you care about requires finding a folder, locating the file and settling back into it, your environment has quietly made distraction the easier action. Removing a tempting default can therefore mean: taking entertainment apps off the first home screen or signing out of the versions that cause habitual checking; turning non essential notifications off rather than deciding whether to ignore each alert; keeping tempting food somewhere that requires a deliberate retrieval rather than within sight and reach; unsubscribing from promotional retailer alerts and emails that repeatedly manufacture purchase occasions; removing saved payment details or one click purchasing when instant checkout is part of an impulsive spending pattern. The important feature is not inconvenience for its own sake. It is selective friction : make an unwanted behaviour just difficult enough to interrupt automatic continuation while leaving genuinely useful access available. Digital commerce demonstrates how powerful removing friction can be. Research using quasi experimental data from an online retailer found that introducing one click buying led customers to purchase more frequently and buy more items, with effects persisting after adoption. The retailer's objective was naturally to make purchasing easier; a consumer trying to curb spontaneous buying can apply the same mechanism in reverse. [PubsOnLine] pubsonline.informs.org Pubs On Line Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023... Published: February 22, 2023 Defaults matter too. A 2024 UK Department for Business and Trade study examined 558 online consumer journeys and then ran an experiment with 5,889 participants. Preselected defaults substantially shifted choices: participants were about 60–70% more likely to choose a more expensive option when it had been preselected. That is a useful reminder that seemingly minor interface settings can shape spending without anyone explicitly deciding that the expensive option is preferable. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping Explanatory illustration 1 Make the better option easier Environmental design works best when it does more than obstruct temptation. Removing an easy bad option while leaving the intended behaviour cumbersome can simply create annoyance. A stronger design simultaneously raises friction on the behaviour you want less of and lowers friction on the behaviour you want more of . Suppose someone wants to read after dinner instead of scrolling. Deleting every distracting app might help, but the stronger arrangement also places the book on the chair where they normally sit, with the bookmark ready. Someone who wants to exercise before work might put training clothes beside the bed and pack the gym bag the previous evening. Someone who wants to cook rather than order a takeaway can keep ingredients for two low effort meals ready rather than relying on the aspiration to prepare something elaborate at 8 pm. This fits evidence from strategic self control research. In two field experiments involving students, those assigned to modify their situations — for example by reducing access to distractions — were more successful in meeting academic goals than comparison groups relying on downstream resistance. In the college experiment, lower feelings of temptation partly explained the advantage. [DOI] doi.org A stitch in time: Strategic self control in high school and college students A stitch in time: Strategic self control in high school and college students... The mechanism is easy to miss precisely because successful environmental design can feel uneventful. If the phone is in another room, you do not heroically resist it every four minutes; you may simply think about it less often. Researchers studying situational self control have argued that such strategies are often underappreciated because people associate “self control” with visibly fighting an impulse rather than arranging circumstances so that the fight rarely begins. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science Situational Strategies for Self Control Association for Psychological ScienceSituational Strategies for Self Control Perspectives on Psychological Science APSJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Convenience can also be attached to desirable behaviour through temptation bundling . In a field experiment, participants given access to compelling audiobooks only while at the gym initially visited the gym 51% more often than controls; participants merely encouraged to impose the same rule on themselves initially visited 29% more often. The effects weakened over time, particularly after a disruption around Thanksgiving, so this is not evidence for a permanent behavioural cure. It does show how access rules can restructure the immediate attractiveness of an activity rather than relying entirely on delayed benefits such as future fitness. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Beyond Willpower: How to Design Your Environment for Automatic Success Channel: Journey Through the Mind Open on YouTube Devices: stop inviting distraction Phones are unusually important environments because their cues travel with the user. A notification can turn “I will check later” into an immediate choice between the current task and a newly salient alternative. Environmental design therefore starts upstream of screen time limits: reduce the number of invitations. A randomised field experiment with 237"},{"title":"Useful Cues","url":"/useful-cues/","description":"Visible and well-placed cues can make a planned action easier to remember and more likely to begin automatically.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Useful Cues Can Your Environment Remind You to Act? Better Habits Useful Cues Useful Cues Can Your Environment Remind You to Act? Visible and well placed cues can make a planned action easier to remember and more likely to begin automatically. Visible and well placed cues can make a planned action easier to remember and more likely to begin automatically. Self Improvement That Works evidence theme cluster mechanism What makes a cue noticeable Placing cues where action starts Avoiding clutter and cue fatigue self improvement that works useful cues Introduction Environmental cues can make a desired behaviour easier to start because they move part of the job from memory and motivation into the situation itself. A book left on the pillow can prompt reading; a water bottle placed beside a laptop can prompt drinking; running shoes beside the front door can make an intended walk harder to overlook. The cue does not perform the behaviour for you. Its value is that, when encountered repeatedly at the right moment, it can become associated with a particular response. Illustrative overview That mechanism is central to habit research. Repetition in a stable context strengthens links between recurring cues and actions, so the behaviour can become increasingly automatic rather than requiring a fresh decision each time. Studies of everyday habit formation, laboratory learning and context stability all point in this direction. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical implication is more precise than “put reminders everywhere”. Effective cues tend to be noticeable, specific and encountered where action can actually begin. Too many prompts, by contrast, can become background noise. Richard H. Thaler on nudges and choice architecture Channel: Sydney Executive Plus Open on YouTube What makes a cue noticeable? A useful cue must first win enough attention to trigger the intended response. That sounds obvious, but visibility is not simply a matter of making something large or brightly coloured. A cue works best when it is distinctive relative to its surroundings and closely connected to a behaviour the person has already decided to perform. Research on point of decision prompts provides a clear real world example. Signs placed by lifts or escalators, precisely where people choose between them and the stairs, have repeatedly increased stair use. A systematic review commissioned for the US Community Guide found strong evidence that such prompts increased stair climbing, while a later review of choice architecture studies found significant physical activity effects in most studies using prompts such as signs, floor markings and stair riser messages. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of decision prompts to increase stair use. A systematic review update PubMed... Specificity can matter too. In one university worksite study involving nearly 3,000 observations, a specific stair use message significantly increased the odds of taking the stairs, whereas the more general message did not; the specific message also outperformed the general one directly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of Decision Signs and Stair Use in a University Worksite Setting: General Versus Specific Messages PubMedMarch 26, 2014... Published: March 26, 2014 This is a useful clue for personal environments: “ exercise more” is a weak environmental instruction, while seeing your resistance band attached to the chair where you intend to use it points much more clearly towards a particular action. Visual cues can influence behaviour beyond exercise. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis covering 66 studies found that visual cues and primes could shift food and drink choices or consumption in the expected direction, although their effectiveness varied by the type of cue, participant and outcome. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis of visual cues and primes for nudging consumption related behaviours PubMedDecember 5, 2024... Published: December 5, 2024 That variation is important. A cue is not a behavioural command; it changes the probability of noticing, considering or initiating a response. For self improvement, the strongest cues therefore tend to have three qualities: They are easy to perceive. The notebook is open on the desk rather than buried in a drawer. They imply one clear action. A prepared gym bag means “take this with you”, not merely “remember fitness”. They appear when the behaviour is feasible. A cue to stretch beside the kettle is more actionable than the same reminder on a noticeboard you see while rushing out of the house. The last quality is particularly important because cues acquire their power through association. Habit research suggests that repeated behaviour becomes linked to the contexts in which it occurs. In daily life studies, strongly habitual actions have been triggered by recurring performance contexts, such as locations, even when people’s immediate goals were less influential. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Explanatory illustration 1 Put the cue where action starts The most useful place for a cue is usually not where you plan the behaviour but where you can begin it. That is why point of decision prompts are such a revealing model. A poster encouraging stair use would be much less useful if placed outside the building; beside the lift, it appears at the decision point. The same principle can be applied at home or work. If you want to take medication after breakfast, placing it somewhere appropriate and safe within the breakfast routine is more closely linked to action than keeping a reminder in another room. If you want to practise guitar after dinner, leaving the instrument ready where you normally sit removes a memory step between the cue and the response. The behavioural mechanism is repetition in a recurring context. In a classic 12 week field study, participants chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and repeated it in the same context each day. Automaticity generally increased with repetition, although the time required varied enormously between people. Importantly, one missed opportunity did not materially derail the habit building process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... More recent research strengthens the case for contextual consistency . In one study of students building study habits, and another using data from a habit building app, greater stability of place and time predicted both stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... Experimental work has likewise found that practice strengthens measurable context–response associations, and that those associations subsequently help guide performance even when deliberate thinking is made harder by cognitive load. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMed... A cue need not be an object. It can be a recurring event: finishing breakfast, arriving home, turning on a computer or brushing your teeth. In a randomised trial of 192 adults building a nutrition habit, participants linked their behaviour either to an existing daily routine or to a particular time. Both strategies produced increases in automaticity, with no significant advantage for one cue type over the other; repeated enactment was the key predictor of habit development. Among participants who successfully formed habits, median time to peak automaticity was 59 days. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. This means there is little reason to search for a universally “best” cue. The better question is whether the cue is dependable enough to recur and close enough to the behaviour to be useful. For example: To read before bed: put the book on the pillow or bedside table before the evening begins. The bedroom and bedtime routine then repeatedly present the same behavioural opportunity. To practise a skill after work: leave the required materials set up before finishing the previous session. Opening the laptop or sitting at the desk can then expose the next action immediately. To take a lunchtime walk: place walking shoes, headphones or a jacket beside whatever you normally encounter when lunch begins. To prepare tomorrow’s priorities: leave a notebook and pen on top of the keyboard before ending the workday, making planning the first visible step on return. The common feature is not decorative organisation. It is reducing the distance between seeing"},{"title":"Useful Feedback","url":"/useful-feedback/","description":"Tracking becomes more useful when the information leads to timely feedback about what to keep, change, or investigate.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Useful Feedback Tracking Is Better When It Changes Your Next Move Better Habits Useful Feedback Useful Feedback Tracking Is Better When It Changes Your Next Move Tracking becomes more useful when the information leads to timely feedback about what to keep, change, or investigate. Tracking becomes more useful when the information leads to timely feedback about what to keep, change, or investigate. Self Improvement That Works implementation pillar dataset evidence What feedback adds to monitoring What physical activity reviews found Why no single feedback format wins self improvement that works useful feedback Introduction Self monitoring becomes more useful when it changes what you do next. A step count, study log, spending record or food diary can reveal what happened, but feedback turns that record into a decision: continue, adjust the target, change the environment, or investigate why progress stalled. Research on behaviour change broadly supports this pairing. A large meta analysis found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring improved goal attainment, with stronger effects when progress was physically recorded or reported to someone else. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Illustrative overview The more specific evidence on feedback is encouraging but less tidy. A corrected 2024 systematic review of self monitoring interventions found a small positive effect of adding feedback on physical activity , while comparisons between different feedback styles produced mixed results. [Springer] link.springer.com Correction: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... The practical lesson is not that one perfect dashboard, message style or coaching format exists. It is that tracking works better when the information is translated into a timely, understandable signal about the next useful move. Explanatory illustration 3 What Feedback Adds to Monitoring Monitoring answers “What did I do?” Feedback adds at least one further comparison: “How does that relate to what I intended to do?” That distinction matters because raw data can accumulate without guiding behaviour. Ten thousand recorded numbers are not necessarily ten thousand useful observations. The clearest feedback usually connects three pieces of information: Current behaviour: what actually happened, such as 5,800 steps or three planned practice sessions completed. A reference point: a goal, previous performance or relevant pattern against which the result can be interpreted. A possible response: what to maintain, alter or investigate next. That structure is consistent with self regulation theories in which people compare their present state with a desired state and then adjust their behaviour. The broader evidence suggests that self monitoring, goal setting and personalised feedback can all contribute to behaviour change, but their effectiveness varies across behaviours and populations rather than operating as universally successful ingredients. A meta review of 66 meta analyses found that several such techniques were associated with better outcomes in some circumstances, while none worked consistently across every setting. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulation mechanisms in health behavior change: a systematic meta review of meta analyses, 2006 2017 PubMed... This explains why a tracker can be highly informative without being especially useful. Imagine that someone records daily walking and repeatedly finishes at about 6,000 steps against an 8,000 step goal. The number alone says they missed the target. Useful feedback asks a more actionable question: Where is the shortfall occurring? If most of it comes from low activity after work, the next experiment might be a 15 minute walk immediately after arriving home. If weekends are responsible, the intervention should be different. Feedback earns its place when it narrows the distance between observation and adjustment. Timing can matter for the same reason. A monthly summary may reveal a pattern after it has become entrenched; feedback available while there is still an opportunity to act can support a different decision. A systematic review of 31 just in time diet and physical activity interventions found that most feedback was personalised and goal oriented, but only a small minority explicitly supplied an actionable recommendation. Among the nine studies that directly tested behaviour change effects, four found significant improvements; successful studies commonly included feedback that was continuously available, goal oriented or actionable. The reviewers regarded these characteristics as promising rather than definitively proven because study designs varied too much to isolate the best combination. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 1 What Physical Activity Reviews Found Physical activity provides unusually useful evidence because wearable devices can both measure behaviour and feed the result back rapidly. A 2022 BMJ systematic review examined 121 randomised controlled trials involving 16,743 participants. Interventions in which adults received feedback from physical activity monitors produced more activity than control conditions without monitor feedback. Across the studies, the estimated effect on overall physical activity was equivalent to roughly 1,235 additional steps per day , while moderate to vigorous activity increased by about 48.5 minutes per week . The authors rated the certainty of evidence as low for overall physical activity and moderate for moderate to vigorous activity, and cautioned that publication and small study bias might inflate the estimate. [BMJ] bmj.com bmj 2021 068047 Effectiveness of physical activity monitors in adults: systematic review and meta analysis The BMJJanuary 26, 2022... Published: January 26, 2022 Earlier evidence in healthcare populations points in the same direction. A 2019 meta analysis of randomised trials involving people receiving healthcare found a moderate positive effect from interventions that used objectively measured activity feedback from wearables. Again, however, the interventions varied considerably, preventing a confident conclusion about which accompanying feedback strategy was responsible for the improvement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The most direct evidence for feedback on top of self monitoring comes from a 2024 systematic review by Rebecca Krukowski and colleagues. It deliberately included trials in which both groups self monitored, but only one received feedback, or in which researchers compared different kinds of feedback. Across 19 studies and 3,261 participants, the evidence was heterogeneous. For physical activity, the corrected meta analysis found a statistically significant but modest benefit from adding feedback: Cohen's d = 0.29, 95% confidence interval 0.16 to 0.43 . [Springer] link.springer.com Correction: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... That corrected number is worth noting because the article was initially published with a substantially larger estimate, d = 0.73. The authors subsequently reported that two standard errors had mistakenly been treated as standard deviations and formally corrected the analysis in February 2024. The revised, smaller estimate is the one that should be used. [Springer] link.springer.com Correction: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... The review also shows why “feedback helps” should not be translated into “more feedback always helps”. Six of the feedback versus no feedback studies reported benefits including greater physical activity, improved self monitoring or better progress towards behavioural goals, while others found no significant advantage for particular outcomes. Effects on weight and diet could not be pooled confidently because too few comparable trials isolated feedback from other intervention components. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... There is an additional caution in reviews of wearable interventions: simple exposure to numbers may be insufficient . One systematic review of portable activity sensing devices found that merely providing step counts or activity data did not reliably increase activity in the trials it examined. Interventions combining the device with several behaviour change techniques — such as goals, exercise plans or motivational prompts — appeared more promising, although results remained mixed. [PubMed Central (PMC)]"},{"title":"Temptation Bundling","url":"/temptation-bundling/","description":"Pairing an effortful activity with something immediately enjoyable can make starting and repeating it more attractive.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Temptation Bundling Pair Something Useful With Something You Enjoy Better Habits Temptation Bundling Temptation Bundling Pair Something Useful With Something You Enjoy Pairing an effortful activity with something immediately enjoyable can make starting and repeating it more attractive. Pairing an effortful activity with something immediately enjoyable can make starting and repeating it more attractive. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative case family policy intervention How temptation bundling works What the exercise experiments found Building pairings that do not undermine the goal self improvement that works temptation bundling Introduction Temptation bundling is a way to make a beneficial but effortful activity more attractive now rather than relying on its distant rewards. The basic rule is simple: pair something you should do, such as exercising, with something you genuinely want to do, such as listening to an absorbing audiobook, and reserve the enjoyable activity for the useful one. Instead of asking future health, fitness or productivity benefits to outweigh immediate effort, the bundle supplies an immediate reason to begin. Researchers describe this as a response to “present bias”: the tendency for immediate costs and pleasures to carry disproportionate weight when people make choices. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 Illustrative overview The best direct evidence comes from exercise experiments. An early university study found large initial increases in gym attendance when participants could access tempting audiobooks only at the gym, although the effect weakened over time and was disrupted sharply by a holiday break. A later field experiment involving thousands of commercial gym members found smaller but more durable improvements. Together, the studies make temptation bundling useful but not magical: it can change the immediate experience of a difficult activity, yet good pairings must survive repetition, interruptions and the temptation to consume the reward elsewhere. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed... Explanatory illustration 3 How temptation bundling works Many worthwhile activities have an awkward timing problem. Their costs arrive first: exercise takes effort, studying demands concentration and tedious administration consumes attention. Their most important benefits may appear weeks, months or years later. That mismatch makes postponement attractive even when a person sincerely values the long term outcome. Research on time preferences has found an association between greater present bias and lower levels of physical activity , illustrating why appeals to future health alone may struggle against the immediate price of getting started. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Temptation bundling changes that calculation by attaching an immediate pleasure to the beneficial activity. In Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp's original formulation, the appealing component was a “want”, while exercise was a “should”. Crucially, the two activities were consumed together. An audiobook did not arrive as a prize after ten workouts; it made the workout itself more enjoyable and created curiosity about what happened next. The researchers argued that this can produce two benefits at once: making the useful activity more tempting while confining a potentially time consuming indulgence to periods when it accompanies something valuable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 That distinction separates temptation bundling from ordinary reward systems. “Exercise this evening and then have a treat” is a delayed reward. “Listen to this novel only while walking or exercising” is a bundle. The latter can affect both anticipation and experience: wanting the next chapter supplies a reason to start, while listening can make the session itself feel less unpleasant. In the first exercise experiment, the advantage of bundling was greater among participants who found the combined workout more enjoyable, which is consistent with that mechanism. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 More broadly, studies by Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach help explain why this approach is plausible. Across research involving exercise, studying, healthy eating and personal goals, immediate enjoyment was more closely associated with persistence than delayed benefits, even though people often said they pursued those activities because of their long term value. Other experiments found that choosing a more enjoyable form of a useful activity, or adding an immediate positive experience to it, could increase persistence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals PubMed... This does not mean long term benefits are unimportant. They help determine which goals are worth pursuing in the first place. The practical insight is that the forces that make someone choose a goal may differ from those that keep them doing it on a rainy Tuesday evening. A person can exercise because cardiovascular health matters to them while still needing something more immediate to make today's workout attractive. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 What the exercise experiments found The first major test was conducted at a university gym in 2011 and later published in Management Science . Participants chose several highly engaging audio novels. In the strongest treatment, those books were loaded onto an iPod that remained in a monitored gym locker, meaning the participant could listen only while exercising there. A second group received the audiobooks on their own devices and was encouraged to impose the same restriction voluntarily. A control group received an equivalently valued bookshop gift card. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 At first, the results were striking. Participants with gym only access to their audiobooks visited the gym 51% more often than controls, while those merely encouraged to restrict listening to workouts visited 29% more often. The experiment also found evidence that people valued the constraint itself: after the study, 61% chose to pay for the option of having continuing gym only access to the tempting audiobooks. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed... But the headline percentages are only half the story. The effects faded as the study progressed, particularly after the US Thanksgiving holiday interrupted normal routines. Milkman has subsequently described that closure and break from the programme as effectively wiping out the earlier advantage. The original experiment therefore demonstrated that a tightly enforced bundle could be powerful at first while also exposing an important weakness: a pairing that depends on one place, one device or an uninterrupted routine can be fragile. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The voluntary condition also matters. Its smaller initial effect suggests that knowing the rule and enforcing it are not the same thing. “I will listen only at the gym” is easy to revise when the story becomes gripping and the sofa is available. The locked away device turned the idea into a commitment mechanism: the enjoyable activity literally became unavailable elsewhere. That design feature helps explain why temptation bundling is more than simply making exercise pleasant. Restriction can preserve the motivational value of the temptation for the next workout. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 A much larger study published in 2020 tested whether the idea could work without monitored lockers. Researchers worked with 24 Hour Fitness on a four week programme. Across the broader research programme, 6,792 participants were involved;"},{"title":"Adapt the Plan","url":"/adapt-the-plan/","description":"A strong self-improvement plan changes when the same failure keeps recurring instead of treating the original design as sacred.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Adapt the Plan Treat Repeated Failure as Data, Not a Verdict Better Habits Adapt the Plan Adapt the Plan Treat Repeated Failure as Data, Not a Verdict A strong self improvement plan changes when the same failure keeps recurring instead of treating the original design as sacred. A strong self improvement plan changes when the same failure keeps recurring instead of treating the original design as sacred. Self Improvement That Works governance pillar event time window Recognizing repeated failure patterns Choosing the smallest useful adjustment Re testing the redesigned routine self improvement that works adapt the plan Introduction Adaptive self improvement means treating a repeatedly failing routine as a design problem rather than repeatedly demanding more effort from the same design. Monitoring is useful because it reveals a gap between intention and behaviour; the important next step is to use that information to change the conditions producing the behaviour. Research on self regulation describes this as a feedback process: compare what happened with what was intended, decide whether the present strategy still fits, and alter the strategy when progress remains inadequate. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Theoretical explanations for maintenance of behaviour change: a systematic review of behaviour theories PMCJuly 2... Illustrative overview This approach is deliberately different from changing everything after one bad day. Single lapses are noisy: illness, travel, an unusual deadline or simple chance can disrupt a sound routine. Repeated failures in a similar situation are more informative. They suggest that the plan may contain a predictable point of friction — the timing, cue, workload, environment, response to obstacles or even the target itself. The practical aim is therefore to identify that recurring failure window, make the smallest plausible adjustment, and re test the redesigned routine before escalating to a larger change. Recognise a pattern before redesigning the plan A useful self improvement system distinguishes an event from a pattern . Missing one planned evening run after an exceptionally long workday tells you little. Missing most evening runs specifically on days when work finishes late tells you much more: the routine appears vulnerable during a recognisable time window. Progress monitoring helps expose patterns of this kind. A meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring increased both monitoring frequency and goal attainment; changes in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the improvement in attainment. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded or reported to someone else. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That supports tracking, but it does not mean that merely collecting more data solves the problem. A wider meta review found that self monitoring, feedback and goal setting techniques work inconsistently across behaviours and populations. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Regulation Mechanisms in Health Behaviour Change: A Systematic Meta Review of Meta Analyses, 2006–2017 PMCMarc... The higher value question is therefore not just “Did I fail?” but “Under what recurring conditions did the behaviour fail?” A simple record can distinguish several different problems: Starting failure: the intended action never begins — for example, study sessions disappear whenever they are scheduled after dinner. Completion failure: the action starts but its planned size is repeatedly unrealistic — a 60 minute workout becomes ten minutes or nothing. Cue failure: the intended trigger is unreliable — “when I get some free time” never creates a dependable starting point. Obstacle failure: the same foreseeable interruption repeatedly defeats the plan — late meetings, childcare, tiredness, missing equipment or distracting devices. Recovery failure: one missed attempt turns into several because the system contains no clear rule for restarting. This classification matters because the appropriate adjustment depends on where the failure occurs. Moving the workout earlier will not solve an exercise routine whose real problem is that it requires equipment the person rarely has available. Reducing session length will not fix a cue that never occurs consistently. Behaviour change guidance reflects this distinction between tracking and revision. NICE describes behaviour goals as things that should be reviewed in light of experience, with further plans made according to previous progress; its framework places monitoring alongside action planning rather than treating either as sufficient alone. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 Repeated failure should also be interpreted locally before it is interpreted globally. Older relapse research provides an especially clear example: treating a lapse as proof of personal incapacity can increase the risk that the lapse becomes a broader relapse, whereas attributing it to an ineffective response to a specific high risk situation creates an opportunity to develop a better response for the next occurrence. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Although those findings come from addiction research and should not simply be generalised to every self improvement goal, the governance lesson is useful: diagnose the failed interaction between person, plan and situation before concluding that the entire goal or the person pursuing it is defective. Explanatory illustration 1 Make the smallest adjustment that could fix the failure Once a recurring pattern is visible, the temptation is to redesign everything: a new app, a stricter schedule, a different diet, a larger goal or a more complicated productivity system. That makes the next result difficult to interpret. If five things change simultaneously and performance improves, you do not know which change mattered; if performance worsens, you do not know what caused that either. A better rule is minimum useful adjustment : change the smallest element that plausibly targets the repeated failure. Suppose the intended routine is “write for 45 minutes at 19:00”, but tracking shows that it fails four evenings out of five because family responsibilities make that window unpredictable. Possible interventions range from tiny to radical. The first test might simply move the session to 07:30. If starting remains difficult, the next adjustment might prepare the document and notes the previous night. Only after smaller changes fail would it make sense to question the session length, frequency or larger goal. This mirrors a central idea in contemporary models of self regulation. Monitoring does not merely indicate whether a goal has been reached; it can signal whether the current strategy or tactic should be maintained, changed or stopped. When a selected tactic produces insufficient progress, the person can return to strategy selection and try a different response rather than simply repeating the ineffective one. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Compass Self‐control: An integrative framework Self‐control: An integrative framework Werner 2023 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online Library... The most useful adjustment usually targets the mechanism exposed by the record: If the problem is timing, change the window. A planned behaviour that repeatedly collides with predictable demands should be moved rather than protected indefinitely through willpower. “Exercise after work” might become “exercise before the first meeting” or “walk during lunch”. If the problem is scale, lower the entry requirement. A routine can be sound in principle yet too large to survive ordinary low energy days. The redesign may preserve the frequency while shortening the minimum session. If the problem is an obstacle, plan the response to that obstacle. Behaviour change research distinguishes action planning — specifying when, where and how a behaviour will occur — from coping planning, which specifies what to do when anticipated barriers arise. A systematic review found variable but generally supportive evidence for coping planning, particularly where people received help forming their plans, while a broader review of planning interventions found small to medium effects across health behaviours but considerable heterogeneity. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. In practice, “I will walk at lunch” becomes “I will walk at lunch; if rain makes the normal route impractical, I will use the covered route through the shopping centre.” If the environment repeatedly wins, change the environment."},{"title":"Consistency","url":"/consistency/","description":"Long-term progress depends more on returning repeatedly than on maintaining an unbroken record with no missed opportunities.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Consistency Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits Consistency Consistency Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Long term progress depends more on returning repeatedly than on maintaining an unbroken record with no missed opportunities. Long term progress depends more on returning repeatedly than on maintaining an unbroken record with no missed opportunities. Self Improvement That Works critique risk pillar event time window Why perfect streaks are a misleading target What occasional lapses mean Building a reliable return strategy self improvement that works consistency Introduction Long term habit building depends less on protecting a flawless streak than on returning to the behaviour often enough, in a sufficiently stable context, for repetition to accumulate. The clearest evidence comes from real world habit formation research: in a widely cited study, missing a single opportunity did not materially derail the development of automaticity , while repeated performance in the same context remained the important ingredient. Habit formation also varied enormously between individuals, taking far longer than popular “21 day” rules imply. Illustrative overview That does not mean missed days are irrelevant. Repeated omissions obviously reduce the repetitions from which a habit can form. The more useful distinction is between a lapse and a breakdown . An isolated missed opportunity is usually recoverable; turning that miss into evidence that “the streak is ruined” can make the interruption larger than it needed to be. Research on behavioural streaks shows why: streaks can motivate continued action, but highlighting a broken streak can also reduce subsequent engagement. The practical target, then, is not perfection. It is dependable return. Explanatory illustration 3 Why perfect streaks are a misleading target A streak is easy to measure: either the behaviour happened every scheduled day or it did not. Habit formation is less binary. Psychologists generally describe habits as responses that become increasingly automatic through repeated performance in recurring contexts. The process is gradual rather than a switch that flips after a fixed number of consecutive days. Philippa Lally and colleagues followed people attempting to establish everyday behaviours such as eating fruit, drinking water or exercising. Automaticity tended to rise along a curved trajectory: early repetitions produced relatively large gains, then further gains became progressively smaller as behaviour approached a plateau. Among participants whose data fitted the researchers' model, reaching 95% of the eventual automaticity level took anywhere from 18 to 254 days. Crucially for the perfection question, missing one opportunity did not materially affect the formation process. A 2024 systematic review makes the same broader point about time. Across 20 health habit studies involving 2,601 participants, the reported median formation times in the small subset that measured them were about 59–66 days, while means ranged from 106–154 days; individual estimates extended from 4 to 335 days. The review also noted substantial limitations, including a relatively small evidence base and high risk of bias in more than half of the included studies. There is therefore no defensible universal countdown after which a behaviour is “installed”. That matters because a perfect streak mindset can confuse the record of repetition with the mechanism of change . Consecutive performance is useful because it supplies repetitions and keeps cues stable. Consecutiveness itself is not magic. Streaks can still be valuable motivational tools. Seven studies reported in the Journal of Consumer Research found that people shown an intact behavioural streak were more likely to continue the target activity than people shown a broken one, even when their underlying past behaviour was held constant. In one experiment involving exercise , 66.23% of participants continued with another strength exercise when their log displayed an intact streak, compared with 57.86% when it displayed a broken one. The same finding exposes the risk. A streak can become a second goal layered on top of the real goal. Someone who originally wanted to read regularly, exercise or practise a language may start protecting the unbroken counter instead. Once the counter reaches zero, part of the motivation disappears even though almost none of the underlying progress has vanished. The researchers found that allowing people to perceive a broken streak as repairable reduced this discouraging effect. A streak is therefore best treated as feedback , not a verdict . Explanatory illustration 1 What occasional lapses actually mean A missed opportunity has two consequences that are easy to confuse. First, the person loses one repetition. Second, they may interpret the miss as evidence about themselves: “I have failed”, “I am back at the beginning”, or “I clearly cannot stick to this”. Only the first consequence is inherent in the missed behaviour. For an emerging habit, losing repetitions does matter if it happens frequently. Habits strengthen through repeated links between context and response, so a pattern of persistent non performance leaves fewer opportunities for that association to develop. Stable contexts also appear to help. In one six week study of 95 university students, alongside observational data from 218 app users covering 308 habits, greater context stability was associated with stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. That is why “consistency beats perfection” should not be translated into “frequency does not matter”. It means something narrower: high repetition with occasional interruption can still produce progress . The Lally study supports tolerance for an isolated omission, not the claim that someone can repeatedly skip intended opportunities with no cost. The useful unit of analysis is therefore a longer window than one day. Imagine two people intending to exercise three times each week for three months. One completes 34 of 39 planned sessions but occasionally misses one because of illness, travel or workload. The other protects a 20 session streak, misses once, decides the programme has been spoiled and largely stops. The second person had the better streak; the first accumulated far more of the behaviour that mattered. This difference between a temporary lapse and a larger collapse also appears in relapse prevention research, although much of that literature concerns addictive behaviours and should not simply be treated as direct evidence about ordinary habits. The conceptual lesson is still useful: research has long distinguished an initial lapse from a full return to the previous behavioural pattern, and has examined how treating a lapse as personal failure can help turn a small interruption into a larger one. For everyday self improvement, the safer interpretation is simple: one miss is information about one occasion . A repeated cluster of misses is information about the system. Breaking & Building Habits: Timelines, Difficulties & Solutions Charles Duhigg Channel: Peter Attia MD Open on YouTube Building a reliable return strategy A useful habit system should contain a plan not only for successful days but also for the first opportunity after failure. That changes the objective from “never miss” to “make interruptions short”. Start by defining what counts as the next normal opportunity . If the habit is “walk after lunch on weekdays” and Tuesday is missed, the recovery point is Wednesday after lunch—not an improvised punishment workout on Tuesday night, and not a ceremonial restart next Monday. This preserves the contextual cue that habit research suggests is important: the same situation once again triggers the same response. Next, separate occasional noise from systematic friction. A missed session because a train was cancelled may require no redesign at all. Four missed sessions because the planned behaviour always collides with childcare, exhaustion or a late meeting suggest that the plan itself is poorly fitted to real life. Consistency improves when the repeated action and its cue are practical enough to survive ordinary weeks, rather than only ideal ones. A reliable return strategy can be kept compact: Resume at the next planned cue. Avoid allowing one missed opportunity to create several more. Keep the original behaviour recognisable. A smaller version can preserve continuity when circumstances are difficult, but it should still represent the behaviour you intend to establish. Diagnose patterns, not individual blemishes. One miss may be random; repeated misses in the same situation point to predictable friction. Protect context where practical. Similar timing, location or preceding events can make repetition easier and support automaticity. Track return as well as streak length. “How quickly did I resume?” is often a more useful long term metric than “How"},{"title":"Automaticity","url":"/automaticity/","description":"Repeatedly responding to the same cue can strengthen a learned connection until an action needs less deliberate prompting.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Automaticity How Repetition Turns a Choice Into a Habit Better Habits Automaticity Automaticity How Repetition Turns a Choice Into a Habit Repeatedly responding to the same cue can strengthen a learned connection until an action needs less deliberate prompting. Repeatedly responding to the same cue can strengthen a learned connection until an action needs less deliberate prompting. Self Improvement That Works governance pillar mechanism From deliberate action to cue response Why repetition alone is not enough Recognizing growing automaticity self improvement that works automaticity Introduction A behaviour becomes more automatic when the same response is repeatedly performed in the presence of a recurring cue. At first, the cue may simply remind you of a decision you have already made: after breakfast, you deliberately take a vitamin; when you sit at your desk, you consciously open your study notes. With repeated pairings, the relationship can change. The situation itself becomes increasingly capable of activating the response, so less deliberate prompting is needed each time. Habit researchers describe this as the strengthening of a learned cue–response association. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Illustrative overview This matters for self improvement because deliberate control is expensive. Remembering , deciding and overcoming hesitation must be repeated every time an action depends entirely on intention. A useful habit reduces that recurring demand. The aim is not to make behaviour literally unconscious, nor to eliminate choice, but to make the desired response easier to initiate when the relevant situation appears. From deliberate action to cue response Habit formation begins with deliberate behaviour. A person chooses an action because it serves some goal, performs it in a particular situation, and then encounters broadly the same situation again. If the same response keeps following that situation, memory begins to encode the relationship between the context and the action. Reviews of habit research therefore distinguish habits from mere frequency: what matters is not simply that something has been done many times, but that it has been repeatedly performed in relation to recurring cues. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... Those cues need not be a single object such as an alarm or a sticky note. A cue can be a place, time, preceding action, group of people or recurring combination of circumstances. “After I put my breakfast plate in the dishwasher” is one cue configuration; “when I arrive at the office and sit at this desk” is another. The more reliably a particular context is followed by the same response, the more opportunity there is for the context to acquire predictive value. The practical consequence is a gradual change in what gets the behaviour started. Early in learning, the sequence may look roughly like this: Situation → remembering the goal → deciding to act → action. With stronger habit learning, it can increasingly resemble: Familiar situation → activated response → action. That does not mean goals become irrelevant. Goals often explain why a person starts practising a behaviour in the first place and why they continue arranging opportunities to perform it. But once cue–response learning becomes strong, moment to moment initiation can rely less heavily on renewed conscious decision making. Wood and Rünger’s major review describes habits as an efficient default form of responding that can operate alongside deliberate goal pursuit rather than simply replacing it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Recent experimental work provides unusually direct evidence for this mechanism. In two studies published in 2024, Jennifer Labrecque, Kristen Lee and Wendy Wood had participants practise a multi step simulated sushi making task. Greater practice produced stronger measured associations between task cues and the corresponding responses. Those associations, in turn, predicted better performance when participants later completed the task under cognitive load, when deliberative processing was deliberately made harder. Importantly, practice did not appear to improve performance merely by strengthening people’s intentions or motivation to do the task correctly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 That finding captures the distinctive value of automaticity. Repetition can alter not only how much someone wants to perform an action, but how readily the environment itself calls up the practised response. Explanatory illustration 1 Automaticity usually grows gradually Habit formation is better understood as a learning curve than as a switch that flips on a particular day. In a well known real world study, Phillippa Lally and colleagues asked 96 adults to select an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and repeat it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Among participants whose data could be modelled, automaticity generally rose quickly at first and then increased more slowly towards a plateau. Estimated times to reach 95% of each person’s eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This is why the popular claim that a habit takes a fixed number of days is misleading. A 2024 systematic review of 20 studies involving 2,601 participants found wide variation across people and behaviours; studies continued to show habit strengthening beyond the first month, and reported formation times varied substantially. The review identified repetition frequency , timing, behavioural characteristics and contextual stability among the factors associated with habit development. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The useful question, then, is not “Has it been 21, 30 or 66 days yet?” It is “Is this situation increasingly prompting the response with less deliberation than before?” Why repetition alone is not enough “Repeat it until it becomes a habit” is directionally right but incomplete. Repetition is most useful for cue–response learning when it repeatedly connects the same kind of cue with the same response . Doing an action 40 times in unrelated circumstances does not provide the same learning conditions as repeatedly responding to a recurring situation. Research on context stability supports this distinction. In a 2022 study, 95 university students deliberately developed new study habits over six weeks. One group was instructed to keep the place and time of performance stable, while another deliberately varied them. A second dataset examined 308 habits recorded by users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. Context stability should not be interpreted as a requirement to reproduce every detail perfectly. The useful unit is the cue that can be encountered reliably enough to guide action. For one person, a clock time may be dependable. For another, an existing routine is more predictable: “after breakfast”, “when I close my laptop after work” or “after I brush my teeth”. A randomised trial of 192 adults helps clarify this point. Participants chose an everyday nutrition behaviour and linked it either to a specific time or to an existing daily routine. Over 84 days, both groups increased in automaticity, with no meaningful advantage for routine based over time based cues. What predicted automaticity particularly strongly was repeated enactment of the planned response when the chosen cue occurred. Among those who successfully formed habits, the median time to peak automaticity was 59 days. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. The practical lesson is therefore broader than “always use an existing routine” or “always do it at 7 am”. The stronger principle is: choose a cue you can actually encounter consistently, then make the intended response repeatedly follow it. The Science of Habit Formation: Cue, Craving, Response & Reward Channel: Fran Palokaj Open on YouTube Missed repetitions are interruptions, not resets Stable repetition does not require perfection. In Lally and colleagues’ 12 week study, missing a single opportunity did not materially disrupt the overall automaticity curve. [Wiley Online"},{"title":"Habit Timelines","url":"/habit-timelines/","description":"Habit formation has no universal deadline, and research finds wide variation between people and behaviours.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Habit Timelines How Long Does a Habit Really Take? Better Habits Habit Timelines Habit Timelines How Long Does a Habit Really Take? Habit formation has no universal deadline, and research finds wide variation between people and behaviours. Habit formation has no universal deadline, and research finds wide variation between people and behaviours. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative pillar dataset evidence Why 21 days is not a rule What modern reviews estimate Why individual timelines vary so much self improvement that works habit timelines Introduction There is no scientifically established number of days after which a new behaviour suddenly becomes a habit. The familiar 21 day rule is not a finding from modern habit research, and even the better known figure of 66 days should not be treated as a replacement deadline. Studies instead show a wide spread: some relatively simple behaviours become substantially automatic within weeks, while others take many months, and some people do not reach the researchers’ chosen habit threshold during the study at all. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Illustrative overview The most useful lesson for self improvement is therefore not “repeat this for exactly X days”. It is to expect a gradual learning process whose speed depends on the person, the behaviour and the circumstances in which it is repeated . A 2024 systematic review of health related habits found median formation times of roughly two months in the studies that directly measured them, but individual estimates stretched from 4 to 335 days . Just as importantly, the review found that surprisingly few studies have actually measured how long habit formation takes. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Why 21 days is not a rule The 21 day claim can be traced largely to plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz and his 1960 book Psycho Cybernetics . Maltz described observations such as patients adjusting to changes in their appearance and argued that it generally took a minimum of about 21 days for a changed mental image to become familiar. The passage was about adjustment and self image rather than a controlled experiment testing how long everyday behaviours take to become habitual. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. That distinction became blurred as the idea travelled through self help culture. “A minimum of about 21 days” is easily transformed into the much simpler promise that doing something for 21 consecutive days creates a habit. But the two statements are not equivalent. Maltz was not establishing a universal behavioural threshold, and contemporary habit research does not support one. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. The deeper problem is the assumption that habit formation resembles crossing a finish line. In behavioural science, a habit is usually understood not merely as something done frequently but as a response that becomes increasingly automatically prompted by a recurring context or cue . Repeating an action in a sufficiently stable situation gradually strengthens the association between that situation and the response. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlinePromoting habit formation: Health Psychology Review: Vol 7, No sup1 Get AccessOctober 11, 2011... Published: October 11, 2011 That learning process does not ordinarily jump from “not a habit” on day 20 to “habit” on day 21. In the influential real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, participants’ increases in automaticity were better represented by an asymptotic curve : gains tended to be larger earlier and then became progressively smaller as automaticity approached a plateau. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This makes fixed challenges easy to misunderstand. Completing 21 days of walking, reading or stretching can certainly establish useful repetition. It simply does not mean that the behaviour is now permanently automatic, nor that needing deliberate effort on day 22 represents failure. Explanatory illustration 1 What modern research actually estimates The study responsible for the famous “66 days” figure followed 96 volunteers who selected a new eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour and tried to perform it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. They repeatedly rated how automatic the behaviour felt. Among participants whose data could be modelled appropriately, estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity varied from 18 to 254 days . Later summaries report a median of about 66 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The range matters more than the headline number. Sixty six days was a description of the middle of a highly variable distribution in a particular study, not a biological rule. Moreover, the mathematical habit formation curve fitted only 62 participants’ data, and fitted particularly well for 39, another reason not to turn the estimate into a universal countdown. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Subsequent studies have produced similarly varied estimates. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis examined 20 studies involving 2,601 participants and health related behaviours including physical activity, healthier eating, water consumption, flossing and reduced sedentary behaviour. Only four studies , however, directly reported a mean or median time to habit formation. Across those studies, medians were 59 to 66 days , while means ranged from 106 to 154 days . Individual estimates across relevant studies ranged from 4 to 335 days . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Some examples show why one average conceals so much: In one healthy eating study, those who reached the specified habit threshold did so in a median of 59 days , but estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days , and only about 23% of participants reached that threshold. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Lally and colleagues’ eating, drinking and activity study produced the frequently cited 66 day estimate, with individual modelled times ranging from 18 to 254 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... In a 90 day study of a daily stretching routine, researchers extrapolated the learning curves to estimate automaticity at roughly 106 days for morning stretching and 154 days for evening stretching . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit PubMedNovember 1, 2017... Published: November 1, 2017 Another study in the review, using participants’ own reports for healthy eating and self weighing, produced a mean estimate of about 91 days , with substantial variation around that average. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The 2024 review therefore concluded that health habits may commonly require roughly two to five months to develop substantial automaticity, while stressing the considerable person to person variation. That is a more defensible expectation than three weeks, but it still should not be interpreted as a guaranteed window. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is another reason for caution: the evidence base remains thinner than the confidence of popular habit advice might suggest. Of the 20 studies in that review, only four directly yielded formation time estimates, and 11 were rated as having a high risk of bias. The authors explicitly identified the scarcity of studies directly measuring time to habit formation as an important research gap. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. So “science says it takes 66 days” is only marginally better than “science says 21 days”. The more accurate summary is that habit formation is gradual, measurable timelines vary enormously, and current research does not justify a single countdown for everyone or every behaviour . Healthy Habits Take Time: Systematic Review Debunks 21 Day Habit Myth Channel: Medical Dialogues Open on YouTube Why individual timelines vary so much Variation is not statistical noise that can simply be averaged away."},{"title":"Fix the System","url":"/fix-the-system/","description":"Repeated failure is a signal to examine the cue, timing, difficulty, or environment rather than simply demand more discipline.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Fix the System What to Change When a Habit Keeps Failing Better Habits Fix the System Fix the System What to Change When a Habit Keeps Failing Repeated failure is a signal to examine the cue, timing, difficulty, or environment rather than simply demand more discipline. Repeated failure is a signal to examine the cue, timing, difficulty, or environment rather than simply demand more discipline. Self Improvement That Works critique risk pillar mechanism Diagnosing the point of failure Changing cues, timing, and friction Testing one adjustment at a time self improvement that works fix the system Introduction When a habit keeps failing in the same way, the most useful response is usually not to demand more discipline. Repeated breakdown is information about the design of the behaviour: the cue may be unreliable, the timing may clash with real life, the action may be too demanding at that moment, or the surrounding environment may make the alternative easier. Habit research consistently finds that repetition works best when behaviour is tied to a sufficiently stable context, while planning and monitoring can help bridge the gap between intention and action. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Illustrative overview Troubleshooting therefore starts with a different question. Instead of asking, “Why can’t I stick to this?”, ask, “At exactly what point does this system stop producing the behaviour?” That shift matters because different failure points need different repairs. Forgetting to start, repeatedly running out of time, abandoning a task halfway through and losing a routine after one missed day are not the same problem. Diagnose the Point of Failure A failed habit is easiest to fix when it is treated as a sequence rather than a verdict. Take a routine such as “study for 30 minutes after dinner”. Several quite different things can go wrong: dinner happens at inconsistent times; the person goes straight from eating to their phone; the study materials are in another room; 30 minutes feels too demanding after a long day; or one disrupted evening becomes the point at which the whole routine disappears. That distinction reflects how habits actually develop. In behavioural research, a habit is not simply something done frequently. It is a response that becomes increasingly associated with recurring contextual cues through repetition. A major review by Wendy Wood and Dennis Rünger describes habits as responses repeated in recurring contexts, while research tracking intentional habit building has found greater context stability associated with both stronger automaticity and better repetition of the intended behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMed... A practical diagnosis can therefore separate four common breakdowns: The behaviour never comes to mind. The cue is absent, vague or badly placed. The cue appears, but the behaviour is postponed. Timing, competing actions or start up friction are probably the stronger problem. The behaviour starts but repeatedly collapses. The task may be too difficult, too long or poorly matched to available energy and attention. The routine works until an interruption, then disappears. The system lacks a recovery rule for disrupted days. The value of this diagnosis is that it stops indiscriminate “motivation fixes”. An alarm cannot repair a task that is routinely impossible at 7 pm, and motivational reading cannot compensate for exercise clothes that are inaccessible when the intended workout starts. Explanatory illustration 1 Look for the repeated location of the breakdown The most revealing evidence is usually not the total number of failures but their pattern. If the same habit works on weekends but fails on weekdays, the target behaviour itself may be viable while its weekday timing is not. If it consistently fails after late meetings, the problem is probably not forgetting. If it succeeds whenever equipment is already prepared, preparation is part of the system rather than an optional extra. Context matters because habitual responses are strongly dependent on the circumstances in which they were learned. In a classic study of students moving to a new university, established behaviours such as exercising and newspaper reading were more likely to be disrupted when important features of their usual performance context changed. Broader work on “habit discontinuity” similarly suggests that changes in context can weaken established cue–response patterns. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... That principle works in both directions. A changed context can disrupt a useful routine, but it can also expose why a supposedly “bad discipline” problem is actually environmental. If a reading habit survives at home but disappears while travelling, rebuilding the familiar cue may be more productive than recommitting to the goal. Change Cues, Timing and Friction Once the failure point is visible, change the component closest to it. The aim is not to create the theoretically perfect routine; it is to make the desired response more likely under the conditions in which it actually has to occur. Repair an unreliable cue. “Exercise sometime after work” leaves considerable interpretation to the moment. A more dependable cue might be “after I put my work bag down” or “when I close my laptop”. Implementation intentions — advance if–then plans linking a situation with a response — are one of the better supported tools for converting intentions into action. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large average effect on goal attainment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... But an if–then plan is not magic. Research on unwanted habits found that implementation intentions were less effective when the competing habit was particularly strong; in a smoking field study, the planning intervention helped participants with weaker or moderate habits but not those with strong smoking habits. That is a useful warning against treating a better sentence as a substitute for changing the environment when the old response is deeply established. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Planning to break unwanted habits: habit strength moderates implementation intention effects on behaviour change PubMedOctober 11... Move the behaviour to a more reliable time. A habit scheduled for an aspirational version of the day will repeatedly collide with the real one. If a nightly routine fails because evenings vary dramatically, attaching it to a stable morning event may be a better experiment than increasing reminders. A recent systematic review of health habit formation found considerable variation between people and behaviours, but identified timing, behavioural regulation and preparatory routines among factors associated with habit strength; it also found some evidence that morning practices showed greater habit strength, although the evidence base remains limited and many included studies had high risk of bias. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Reduce the cost of starting. If the cue is noticed but action repeatedly stalls, inspect every step between intention and first movement. A 20 minute home workout may actually require finding clothes, clearing floor space, choosing a routine, connecting headphones and deciding which exercise to begin with. Those are small actions individually, but together they create several opportunities to abandon the plan. The repair is to move preparation outside the vulnerable moment: put the equipment where the behaviour happens, choose the session in advance, leave the document open, prepare ingredients earlier or make the first action obvious. This is not merely a productivity trick. Habit research increasingly treats context, preparatory behaviours and ease of execution as important influences on whether repetition becomes stable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Shrink a behaviour that repeatedly exceeds capacity. When a routine starts and then fails because it is consistently too demanding, simply protecting the original target can preserve the failure. The useful distinction is between the behaviour required to maintain the pattern and the larger performance goal. “Open the textbook and complete one problem” can preserve a study sequence on a difficult day even when a full session is unrealistic. That does not mean progressively lowering every standard. It means testing whether excessive difficulty is preventing repetition. The original real world habit formation study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues found enormous individual variation in"},{"title":"Goals vs Actions","url":"/goals-vs-actions/","description":"Outcomes provide direction, while controllable behaviours give you something specific to execute and measure.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Goals vs Actions Should You Track Outcomes or Daily Actions? Better Habits Goals vs Actions Goals vs Actions Should You Track Outcomes or Daily Actions? Outcomes provide direction, while controllable behaviours give you something specific to execute and measure. Outcomes provide direction, while controllable behaviours give you something specific to execute and measure. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative decision cluster mechanism What outcomes are good for Why behaviours are easier to manage Linking the two levels self improvement that works goals vs actions Introduction Outcome goals and daily behaviours do different jobs. An outcome such as losing 10 kilograms, passing an examination, running a faster 10K or saving £5,000 gives direction and a standard for judging progress. But it is not usually something you can perform today. A controllable behaviour — walking after lunch, completing a practice set, following a planned training session or transferring money on payday — is an action you can actually execute. Illustrative overview The most useful approach is therefore not to choose outcomes or behaviours, but to connect them. Research on goal setting shows that clear, appropriately challenging goals can improve performance, while research on implementation intentions and progress monitoring shows why goals become more useful when translated into specific actions and tracked. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... For self improvement, the practical distinction is simple: use outcomes to decide where you are going; use controllable behaviours to decide what you do next . What outcomes are good for Outcome goals describe a desired state rather than the immediate action that produces it. “Get promoted”, “reach a healthy weight”, “publish a novel” and “save three months of expenses” are all outcomes. They matter because they provide direction, establish priorities and make it possible to compare the present with a desired future. This role fits the long history of goal setting research. A major 1987 meta analysis found strong support for the performance benefits of specific, difficult goals compared with easy goals or vague instructions such as “do your best”. Goal setting theory subsequently explained these effects partly through greater focus, effort, persistence and strategy development. Feedback is important too: a target is much less useful when people cannot tell whether they are moving towards it. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... An outcome can therefore answer questions that a daily checklist cannot: What am I trying to change? “Build a £6,000 emergency fund.” How much change would count as success? “Complete the course by December.” Which competing activities deserve priority? Training for a half marathon implies different choices from simply “getting fitter”. Is the current approach working over time? If the outcome indicator is not moving, the behaviours beneath it may need to change. That makes outcomes useful as a navigation instrument . The problem begins when they are also treated as the primary unit of daily control. Imagine two people who both want to reduce their 5K time to under 25 minutes. They can set that target, care deeply about it and check their stopwatch repeatedly. None of those acts makes them faster. Improvement comes through the training they can perform — running planned sessions, recovering adequately and progressively adjusting the workload. The finishing time remains useful, but mainly as feedback on whether those actions are producing the intended result. There is also an important qualification to the familiar advice that goals should always be highly specific and difficult. Research on complex tasks has found that demanding performance targets do not reliably produce the benefits seen on simpler tasks, particularly while people are still learning how to perform the task. Work on learning goals suggests that when strategy is not yet known, focusing on acquiring knowledge or discovering effective methods can be preferable to fixating on a demanding performance number. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Knowing when to set learning versus performance goals Knowing when to set learning versus performance goals ScienceDirectJanuary 1, 2012... Published: January 1, 2012 So “get an A” may provide useful direction, but a novice learner may benefit more from asking which study method actually improves retrieval and understanding before optimising everything around the grade. Explanatory illustration 1 Why behaviours are easier to manage A behaviour is closer to the point at which personal control operates. You cannot directly perform “lose half a kilogram this week”, but you can prepare the lunch you planned. You cannot execute “receive a first class degree”, but you can complete an hour of retrieval practice. You cannot make somebody hire you, but you can submit a carefully prepared application. This distinction matters because outcomes commonly contain factors that sit partly or wholly outside the individual’s control. Body weight fluctuates with water and other short term influences. Investment balances move with markets. Race times depend partly on conditions. Sales depend on customers. Examination results depend on both preparation and the assessment itself. Daily behaviours are not perfectly controllable either — illness, caring responsibilities, emergencies and other constraints intervene — but they usually sit much nearer the person’s decision point. The psychology of implementation intentions helps explain why this matters. Rather than stopping at “I intend to exercise ”, an implementation intention specifies a cue and response: if situation X occurs, then I will perform behaviour Y . A landmark meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large average improvement in goal attainment from these plans. Experimental work suggests they operate partly by making the relevant cue more mentally accessible and strengthening the link between that cue and the intended response. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... That converts a distant aspiration into something executable: Outcome: finish my professional qualification. Behaviour: after breakfast on weekdays, complete 30 minutes of practice questions before checking social media. The first line explains why the effort exists. The second tells the person what counts as action. This is also why behaviour measures tend to produce more useful short term feedback. Suppose somebody wants to write a book. “Book completed: no” provides almost no diagnostic information for months. “Wrote for 45 minutes on four of five planned mornings” tells them immediately whether their working system is functioning. Progress monitoring research reinforces this point. A meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring improved goal attainment, with an average effect of d=0.40. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded and when outcomes were reported or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... For day to day self improvement, that makes a behaviour log potentially more informative than continually inspecting the distant result. “Did I do the planned session?” is often a cleaner question than “Am I successful yet?” Process Goals VS Outcome Goals: How to Set Goals You Can Actually Achieve Channel: Develop Good Habits Open on YouTube Process goals have an instructive sporting history Sport provides one of the clearest historical comparisons between outcome and process focused goals because athletes routinely work with both. An outcome goal concerns the end result — winning a match, finishing first or achieving a particular placing. A process goal concerns what the athlete focuses on while performing the skill. Researchers have spent decades examining whether these different forms of goal setting influence performance differently. Earlier reviews found the sporting literature more mixed than the strong findings initially established in industrial and organisational research. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Goal setting and performance in sport and exercise settings: a synthesis and critique PubMed... A 2022 systematic review and meta analysis of 27 sport studies sharpened the comparison. In the studies included, process goals produced the largest estimated performance effect (d=1.36), compared with performance goals (d=0.44) and outcome goals (d=0.09). Process goals were also associated with a large effect on self efficacy. The"},{"title":"Physical Capacity","url":"/physical-capacity/","description":"Physical activity belongs in self-improvement not only as a goal itself but as part of the basic capacity to function well.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Physical Capacity Why Movement Belongs in a Broader Improvement Plan Better Habits Physical Capacity Physical Capacity Why Movement Belongs in a Broader Improvement Plan Physical activity belongs in self improvement not only as a goal itself but as part of the basic capacity to function well. Physical activity belongs in self improvement not only as a goal itself but as part of the basic capacity to function well. Self Improvement That Works implementation theme cluster population context Exercise as both goal and foundation Making activity repeatable Using cues, rewards, and feedback together self improvement that works physical capacity Introduction Regular physical activity belongs in a self improvement plan for a reason that goes beyond fitness: it helps maintain the basic capacity needed to pursue other goals. Movement supports sleep, mood, cognitive function, cardiovascular and muscular fitness, and the ability to carry out everyday tasks. Some benefits appear after a single session, while others accumulate through regular participation. That makes physical activity less like an isolated project and more like infrastructure for studying, working, caring for others and remaining independent over time. [CDC] cdc.gov Benefits of Physical Activity Physical Activity Basics CDCDecember 4, 2025... Published: December 4, 2025 Illustrative overview The practical challenge is consistency . Nearly one third of adults worldwide did not meet recommended activity levels in 2022, according to World Health Organization estimates. A useful self improvement approach therefore does not depend on repeatedly becoming motivated enough to “work out”. It makes movement easier to repeat through realistic starting points, stable cues , convenient forms of activity, progress monitoring, feedback and gradual increases in difficulty. [World Health Organization] who.int World Health OrganizationNearly 1.8 billion adults at risk of disease from not doing enough physical activityJune 26, 2024... Published: June 26, 2024 Exercise as both goal and foundation Physical activity can be a goal in its own right: someone may want to run further, become stronger or meet public health recommendations. But its wider value is that improvements in physical and psychological functioning can make other parts of life easier to sustain. For adults, WHO recommends at least 150–300 minutes of moderate intensity aerobic physical activity each week, or 75–150 minutes of vigorous activity, or an equivalent combination, alongside muscle strengthening activity on at least two days. Crucially, WHO also emphasises that any amount of activity is better than none and that activity can come from transport, leisure, work or ordinary daily tasks rather than formal exercise alone. [World Health Organization] who.int World Health OrganizationWHO guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour: at a glanceMay 4, 2021... Published: May 4, 2021 Those recommendations describe a health target, not a threshold below which movement is worthless. This distinction matters for behaviour change. A person currently doing very little does not need to leap immediately to the full guideline before the effort “counts”. Smaller increases can provide benefits and create a workable base from which activity can grow. [CDC] cdc.gov Benefits of Physical Activity Physical Activity Basics CDCDecember 4, 2025... Published: December 4, 2025 Several effects are particularly relevant when physical activity is viewed as capacity for other goals. Sleep can improve. The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention lists better sleep quality among the immediate benefits of moderate to vigorous physical activity. A 2024 meta analysis of 81 randomised controlled trials involving 6,193 participants also found improvements in subjective sleep quality and objective sleep efficiency following exercise interventions. Better sleep does not automatically make someone productive or disciplined, but it removes one common obstacle to concentration, emotional regulation and sustained effort. [CDC] cdc.gov Health Benefits of Physical Activity for Adults Physical Activity Basics CDC Health Benefits of Physical Activity for Adults Physical Activity Basics CDC... Cognitive performance can benefit. A 2025 umbrella review covering 133 systematic reviews, 2,724 randomised trials and more than 258,000 participants found positive average effects of exercise on general cognition, memory and executive function. A separate meta review examining acute exercise found small to medium improvements across attention, executive function, memory and information processing, with benefits particularly evident when cognition was tested after the activity session. These are average effects rather than a guarantee that a walk will rescue every difficult afternoon, but they support the broader idea that movement can contribute to the mental machinery used for other tasks. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effectiveness of exercise for improving cognition, memory and executive function: a systematic umbrella review and meta meta analys... Mood and anxiety matter to capacity too. Physical activity is associated with reduced short term feelings of anxiety, while regular activity is linked with a lower risk of depression. An umbrella review of 97 systematic reviews found beneficial effects of physical activity interventions on symptoms of depression, anxiety and psychological distress across a wide range of adult populations, although the methodological quality of many included reviews was weak. The sensible conclusion is not that exercise replaces treatment where treatment is needed, but that regular movement is one evidence supported component of psychological functioning. [CDC] cdc.gov Benefits of Physical Activity Physical Activity Basics CDCDecember 4, 2025... Published: December 4, 2025 Physical capability protects options. Regular activity strengthens muscles and bones and improves the ability to perform everyday activities. In later life it also supports balance, lowers fall risk and can help people remain independent for longer. From a self improvement perspective, this is a long time horizon worth noticing: maintaining the capacity to walk, lift, travel and manage daily life preserves the ability to pursue other aims rather than becoming an end in itself. [CDC] cdc.gov older adults older adults This does not mean that more exercise is always better. WHO's evidence synthesis indicates that benefits for many outcomes begin to flatten at higher volumes, while training that is excessive for a person's current capacity can produce fatigue, soreness or injury that competes with rather than supports the rest of life. A broader improvement plan therefore asks not “How much exercise can I tolerate?” but “What amount of activity improves my capacity and remains sustainable alongside everything else?” [Iris] iris.who.int 9789240015128 eng 9789240015128 eng Explanatory illustration 1 Making activity repeatable The biggest gap in physical activity is often not knowing what to do but continuing to do it. Long term maintenance research points towards a combination of planning, manageable progression, environmental support and repetition rather than reliance on inspiration. A systematic review of interventions for healthy but inactive adults found that physical activity programmes produced a modest improvement in behaviour and that some of the effect persisted six months or more after interventions ended. Techniques associated with longer term effectiveness included action planning, prompts or cues, behavioural practice, graded tasks and self reward. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis Are physical activity interventions for healthy inactive adults effective in promoting behavior change and maintenance, and which b... That translates into a simple design principle: make the first version of the routine easy enough to repeat. A person aiming eventually to exercise for an hour several times a week might begin with a 15 minute walk after lunch. Someone rebuilding strength might schedule two brief sessions rather than designing an elaborate six day programme. The initial routine is doing two jobs at once: providing some physical activity and teaching the person when, where and how the behaviour fits into life. Attach movement to something dependable. “I should exercise today” leaves both timing and execution undecided. “After I put my lunch plate away, I walk for 15 minutes” gives the behaviour a cue. Implementation intention research in physical activity supports linking intended action to a defined situation, while broader maintenance evidence also identifies action planning and prompts as useful components. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Reduce start up friction. A walk beginning outside the workplace, a bicycle already prepared for commuting or a home strength routine requiring little equipment"},{"title":"Small Habits","url":"/small-habits/","description":"Modest actions are easier to repeat through busy, tiring, and imperfect days, making them useful foundations for larger routines.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Small Habits Start Small Enough That the Habit Survives Better Habits Small Habits Small Habits Start Small Enough That the Habit Survives Modest actions are easier to repeat through busy, tiring, and imperfect days, making them useful foundations for larger routines. Modest actions are easier to repeat through busy, tiring, and imperfect days, making them useful foundations for larger routines. Self Improvement That Works critique risk pillar policy intervention Why modest actions improve repeatability Finding a sustainable minimum Avoiding goals built for ideal days self improvement that works small habits Introduction A small habit is useful not because it is impressive, but because it can still happen on an ordinary Wednesday when work runs late, energy is low and the day does not resemble the plan. For self improvement that lasts, the most useful starting behaviour is often the smallest version that remains genuinely worthwhile and repeatable under normal conditions. Illustrative overview Research on habit formation supports this emphasis on repeatability. Habits strengthen when a behaviour is repeated in a sufficiently stable context, and simpler behaviours generally demand less deliberate regulation than complex ones. Crucially, repetition need not be perfect: in a well known real world habit study, missing one opportunity did not materially derail the formation process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical policy is therefore not “do as little as possible”. It is: set a minimum that survives bad but normal days, repeat it reliably, and expand only when the larger version can coexist with ordinary life . Why modest actions improve repeatability Habit formation depends partly on repeated encounters between a context and a response. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ 12 week study, 96 people selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it in the same daily context, such as after breakfast. Automaticity generally rose with repetition, although the rate varied enormously between individuals: among those whose trajectories could be modelled, reaching 95% of their eventual automaticity took between 18 and 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That wide range matters more than the popular idea that a habit should be established after some fixed number of days. A behaviour intended to survive for months needs enough opportunities for repetition. If its minimum version is so demanding that it routinely disappears whenever life becomes inconvenient, the person loses precisely the repetitions from which a habit can grow. Complexity creates another problem. Research distinguishing habitual instigation — automatically getting started — from habitual execution — automatically carrying out all the component steps — suggests that complex behaviours can still require considerable deliberate self regulation even when their initiation is habitual. A 2025 study found that strategy use increased with behavioural complexity, including for strongly instigated habits. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This helps explain why “go for a run” may become a reliable cue driven impulse while the complete routine of finding equipment, travelling somewhere, warming up, exercising, showering and returning home remains vulnerable to time pressure. For a new habit, reducing the required action can therefore reduce the number of things that have to go right. Compare: “Read for an hour every evening” with “read two pages after getting into bed”. “Do a full gym workout four times a week” with “begin ten minutes of movement after arriving home”. “Completely tidy the kitchen every night” with “clear and wipe the worktop after dinner”. “Write 1,000 words every morning” with “open the document and write one paragraph before checking messages”. The smaller versions are not necessarily the eventual ambitions. Their value is that they provide a dependable behavioural floor. Clinical habit formation guidance has made a similar argument. A review aimed at general practice recommended small, manageable behavioural changes partly because failure can discourage further effort and simpler actions may become habitual more quickly. Its example was deliberately unheroic: rather than telling a sedentary person to walk an entire journey, begin by walking one or two additional stops. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 The relevant question is therefore not whether a small action sounds ambitious enough. It is whether it is sufficiently useful to count and sufficiently modest to recur. Explanatory illustration 1 Finding a sustainable minimum A sustainable minimum is the version of a behaviour that you can plausibly execute across the range of days that make up your real life. It should survive ordinary fatigue, meetings, childcare, commuting, mild stress and changes of mood without becoming meaningless. There is no scientifically established universal formula such as “two minutes” or “one repetition”. Different behaviours impose different costs, and people have different constraints. A useful minimum is better treated as an individual design decision than as a magic number. Three tests help locate it. First, remove optional complexity. Ask which part of the routine actually needs to become dependable. If the goal is to establish reading after lunch, the core habit may be “open the book and read”; a particular chair, cup of tea, playlist and 45 minute uninterrupted session are extras. If the goal is strength training, the repeatable core may be starting a short exercise sequence rather than completing an elaborate programme every time. This distinction is supported by work on habitual instigation. In a study of behaviours ranging from checking a phone to using public transport, habitual initiation predicted subsequent behaviour frequency more consistently than habitual execution. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Habit and behavioural complexity: habitual instigation and execution as predictors of simple and complex behaviours Scienc... A useful implication is that, for complex routines, automating the start may be more realistic than expecting the entire performance to run on autopilot. Second, test the behaviour against an ordinary difficult day. Imagine the day when you arrive home late, have less energy than expected and still need to prepare dinner. Could you perform the minimum without requiring a motivational speech? If not, the minimum may still be built around ideal conditions. Third, preserve meaningful direction. “Put on my running shoes” is easy, but it has limited value if it repeatedly ends there. A sustainable minimum should normally contain at least a small dose of the desired behaviour: walk for five minutes, practise one passage, review five flashcards, write a paragraph. Starting cues can be useful, but the minimum should not become a ritual that substitutes for the activity it was supposed to support. This is also why difficulty should be calibrated rather than simply minimised. In research on health goals delivered through a mobile system, perceived difficulty and confidence in completing a behaviour every day were strongly negatively correlated. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Implementation Intention and Reminder Effects on Behavior Change in a Mobile Health System: A... Making an action less demanding can therefore raise perceived feasibility — but reducing it indefinitely eventually produces a behaviour too trivial to advance the underlying aim. A useful rule is to set two levels: the floor and the fuller version . The floor is what preserves continuity; the fuller version is what you do when circumstances permit. Someone might have a floor of ten minutes of walking while normally aiming for 30, or a floor of one paragraph while ordinarily writing for an hour. The minimum protects repetition without imposing an artificial ceiling. Habit Expert, Wendy Wood, breaks down the three bases of habit formation! (Behavioral Science) Channel: Pete Judo Open on YouTube Design for normal disruption, not perfect streaks Small habits work best when they are tolerant of imperfection. That means resisting one of the most counterproductive interpretations of consistency : the idea that a single missed performance destroys the routine. Lally and colleagues specifically examined missed opportunities and found that missing one did not materially affect the habit formation process. [Wiley Online"},{"title":"Accountability","url":"/accountability/","description":"Reporting progress to another person can make monitoring more consequential and may strengthen follow-through.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Accountability Does Telling Someone Else Help You Follow Through? Better Habits Accountability Accountability Does Telling Someone Else Help You Follow Through? Reporting progress to another person can make monitoring more consequential and may strengthen follow through. Reporting progress to another person can make monitoring more consequential and may strengthen follow through. Self Improvement That Works evidence theme cluster population context Why reporting can change behaviour When accountability is useful Keeping support from becoming pressure self improvement that works accountability Introduction Yes — telling someone else how you are actually progressing can strengthen follow through, particularly when the reporting is regular, concrete and tied to behaviour you can control. The strongest evidence comes from research on progress monitoring. A meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions which increased monitoring produced a moderate improvement in goal attainment overall, and that effects were larger when monitoring results were reported to someone else or made public rather than kept entirely private. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Illustrative overview The important word is progress . Accountability is not simply announcing, “I’m going to get fit” or asking a friend to motivate you. It means creating a situation in which another person will later know whether you did the agreed behaviour. That visibility can make tracking more consequential, bring useful support into the process and make missed actions harder to quietly overlook. The Science of Setting & Achieving Goals Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Why reporting can change behaviour Progress monitoring works partly because it repeatedly confronts you with the gap between what you intended to do and what you actually did. The 2016 meta analysis found that interventions designed to increase monitoring substantially increased how often people checked their progress, and that increases in monitoring helped explain subsequent improvements in goal attainment. The overall effect on attainment was d=0.40, a meaningful but far from miraculous improvement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Adding another person appears to strengthen that loop. In the meta analysis, monitoring had larger effects when results were either reported to somebody else or publicly observable. The authors also found better results when progress was physically recorded rather than merely considered mentally. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence White Ros... This points to a useful distinction: private awareness says “I know what happened”; reported monitoring says “someone else will know what happened too”. That does not necessarily require publicity. In the underlying analysis, monitoring that was done privately but subsequently reported was also associated with stronger goal attainment than private monitoring that was never reported. The publicly monitored category was comparatively small, so the research should not be read as evidence that posting progress to a large audience is uniquely powerful. The more defensible conclusion is that making monitoring socially consequential may add value . A recent experimental example illustrates the idea more concretely. In a 12 week behavioural weight loss study, 322 adults were assigned either to let their coaches see digital records of weight, diet and physical activity or to keep those data inaccessible to the coach. Participants whose coaches could see the records lost 6.2% of their initial weight on average, compared with 5.3% in the comparison condition. They also recorded eating and physical activity more consistently and reported a stronger sense of supportive accountability. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Coach access to digital self monitoring data: An experimental test of short term effects in behavioral weight loss tr... The absolute difference was modest, and weight management programmes contain many interacting components, so this is not proof that data sharing alone transforms behaviour. It does, however, show the basic accountability mechanism under reasonably controlled conditions: knowing that a coach could actually see the record changed both monitoring behaviour and, to a smaller extent, the eventual outcome. Explanatory illustration 1 When accountability is useful Reporting is most useful when it turns a vague intention into a recurring, observable commitment. “Ask me whether I’m being more productive” gives an accountability partner very little to work with. “Every Friday I’ll tell you how many of my four planned study sessions I completed” creates a much clearer feedback loop. Research on “supportive accountability”, originally developed to explain why human support can improve adherence to digital health programmes, makes a similar argument. The model proposes that accountability works best when expectations are clear, focused on processes and agreed with the person doing the behaviour. The person receiving the report should also be regarded as trustworthy and helpful rather than merely as an enforcer. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth... In ordinary self improvement, that suggests several useful design choices: Report behaviour, not personality. “I practised Spanish on four of five planned days” is more useful than “I was disciplined this week”. Agree the measure beforehand. Decide what counts as completion before there is any temptation to reinterpret a weak week. Use a predictable rhythm. A weekly message or conversation creates a real future point at which progress will become visible. Keep the report small. A number, tick sheet or short summary usually creates more clarity than a long explanation. Include failures as data. If missed actions disappear from the report, accountability gradually becomes impression management rather than monitoring. Accountability can also interact with other forms of interpersonal support. In the Opt IN weight management experiment, 562 adults were randomised across combinations of intervention components including coaching, a trained “buddy” and progress reports sent to their primary care clinician. The optimised package included both buddy training and clinician progress reports, with the researchers finding that the effect of buddy support was enhanced when progress was also reported to the clinician. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Factorial Experiment to Optimize Remotely Delivered Behavioral Treatment for Obesity: Results of the Opt IN Study PubMedJuly 12... That study concerned obesity treatment rather than everyday self improvement, but it reinforces the idea that reporting can operate as part of a wider support system rather than as a stand alone motivational trick. There is no clear evidence that everyone needs this structure. People who already monitor reliably and act consistently may gain little from another reporting layer. The supportive accountability model likewise predicts that people with stronger intrinsic motivation may require less external support. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Accountability is therefore best treated as a tool for a specific problem: when intentions repeatedly disappear without consequences between the moment they are made and the moment action is required. The Power of Accountability (Why You Need a Partner to Reach Your Goals) Channel: Minds 2Minds Open on YouTube Reporting progress is not the same as announcing a goal One reason advice about “telling people your goals” seems contradictory is that researchers have studied several quite different behaviours under that broad description. A well known 2009 series of four experiments found that people whose identity related intentions were noticed by others sometimes pursued them less vigorously afterwards. For example, publicly signalling an intention associated with becoming a particular kind of professional could produce a premature feeling of possessing that desired identity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That finding is often simplified online into “never tell anyone your goals”, but it does not test the same intervention as repeatedly reporting completed behaviour. More recent work further complicates any blanket rule. Four longitudinal studies published online in"},{"title":"Written Records","url":"/written-records/","description":"Physically recording progress creates a clearer record than relying on impressions such as feeling that you were fairly consistent.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick?","level":2,"text":"Written Records Write It Down Instead of Trusting Memory Better Habits Written Records Written Records Write It Down Instead of Trusting Memory Physically recording progress creates a clearer record than relying on impressions such as feeling that you were fairly consistent. Physically recording progress creates a clearer record than relying on impressions such as feeling that you were fairly consistent. Self Improvement That Works governance decision cluster policy intervention Why memory gives weak feedback Physical records and stronger effects Choosing a recording method self improvement that works written records Introduction Self improvement becomes easier to judge when progress leaves a record. “I was fairly consistent this month” is an impression; a calendar with twelve completed sessions, six missed sessions and the dates of each is information. That distinction matters because memory is not a neutral ledger. Retrospective reports are vulnerable to forgetting, reconstruction and other recall biases, particularly when people are trying to summarise repeated behaviour over days or weeks. Research methods designed to capture experience close to the time it happens were developed partly to reduce precisely this problem. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ecological momentary assessment Ecological momentary assessment PubMed... Illustrative overview There is also evidence that recording progress can do more than improve recollection. A large meta analysis of experimental studies found that interventions which increased progress monitoring improved goal attainment, with stronger effects when the information was physically recorded rather than merely monitored without a record. The practical lesson is modest but useful: do not make memory the sole authority on whether the plan is working. Create a small, visible record that can settle the question. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 James Clear's Analog Habit System Revealed Channel: Matt Ragland Open on YouTube Why memory gives weak feedback The problem with relying on memory is not simply that people occasionally forget individual events. Self improvement usually requires reconstructing a pattern: How often did I exercise ? Have I really been studying four evenings a week? Is my spending improving, or did this week merely feel better? The longer and more repetitive the period, the more the answer depends on summarising many similar episodes after the fact. Research comparing retrospective reports with measurements made closer to events shows why that is risky. Reviews of physical activity measurement, for example, find that questionnaire based reports can overestimate or underestimate activity and are affected by inaccurate recollection and response biases. Studies comparing self reported exercise with device records have likewise found discrepancies, including tendencies towards over reporting. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A comparison of direct versus self report measures for assessing physical activity in adults: a systematic review P... The relevant comparison is not “memory is useless, records are perfect”. Records can also be wrong. The useful distinction is between reconstructing behaviour later and capturing evidence nearer to the event. Ecological momentary assessment, a research approach that repeatedly records what people are doing or experiencing in daily life, exists partly because shorter recall periods can reduce the distortions associated with global retrospective reports. A 2022 systematic review found that the accuracy of such momentary self reports still varied substantially between behaviours and studies, so contemporaneous recording should not be treated as objective truth. It simply removes one important source of uncertainty: having to remember an entire period afterwards. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Ecological Momentary Assessment: A Systematic Review of Validity Research PMC... That changes the quality of personal feedback . Suppose someone intends to practise a language five days a week. At the end of a disappointing month, “I think I practised most days” offers little guidance. A record showing 17 sessions, with most omissions on Fridays and weekends, reveals a different problem: the weekday system may be working reasonably well while the weekend plan is failing. The record does not merely issue a verdict. It shows where the next decision belongs. This is why written tracking is best understood as a small governance mechanism for personal behaviour. It establishes a rule about evidence: when judging progress, use recorded occurrences rather than whichever weeks happen to be easiest to remember. Explanatory illustration 1 Physical records can strengthen the effect The strongest broad evidence comes from Benjamin Harkin and colleagues' meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions designed to increase the monitoring of goal progress produced a moderate improvement in goal attainment overall, and increases in monitoring statistically mediated the improvement. Crucially for written records, the researchers found larger effects when participants physically recorded the information they obtained from monitoring: the reported effect on goal attainment was about d=0.43 with recording versus d=0.29 without it. The difference remained when goal attainment itself was measured objectively. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 “Physically recorded” should not be read as “paper is scientifically superior to a phone”. In this research, the important contrast was between creating a persistent record and merely checking progress without recording it. The authors suggested that a stored record allows people to examine progress over time and identify actions that help or hinder the goal. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. That interpretation fits the wider behaviour change literature. The widely used Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy treats “self monitoring of behaviour” as a distinct intervention component: establishing a way for a person to monitor and record their own behaviour as part of a change strategy. It is grouped with feedback and monitoring rather than with inspiration or general goal setting because its practical role is to make performance observable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The behavior change technique taxonomy (v1) of 93 hierarchically clustered techniques: building an international consensus for the... Explanatory illustration 3 A record turns progress into something inspectable Once behaviour has been recorded, several questions become answerable without reconstructing the past from memory: Did the action happen? A tick, tally or dated entry settles the basic question. How often did it happen? Frequency becomes countable rather than impressionistic. When does the system break down? Missed entries can reveal particular days, locations or circumstances associated with failure. Is the trend actually improving? Several weeks of records can distinguish a persistent change from one unusually good or bad week. Does the plan need changing? A repeated mismatch between the intended schedule and the recorded pattern provides evidence for revising the system rather than simply recommitting to it. This does not require elaborate data analysis. In many cases the most useful information is binary: done or not done. Someone trying to read each evening may gain more from thirty boxes on a sheet of paper than from a sophisticated dashboard recording pages, reading speed, mood, genre and time of day . The purpose is not to produce impressive data. It is to prevent vague recollection from overruling visible evidence. There is another possible effect: measurement itself can influence behaviour. A 2022 systematic review of digital, in the moment health measurements found small but meaningful evidence of “measurement reactivity” — behaviour changing partly because it is being measured. Proposed mechanisms include greater awareness of the behaviour and noticing discrepancies between actual and desired performance. Earlier reviews also found that asking people questions about their behaviour can sometimes change what they subsequently do, although effects are heterogeneous and the evidence is not clean enough to predict when this will happen. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For self improvement, that is not necessarily a flaw. If marking each workout makes the intended behaviour more salient, the record is functioning partly as an intervention. But it does mean that “tracking” should not be"},{"title":"If Then Plans","url":"/if-then-plans-8234f6/","description":"Linking a clear situation to a prepared response can make intended actions easier to start when the cue appears.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Behaviour Goals","level":3,"text":"If Then Plans Why If Then Plans Make Starting Easier Behaviour Goals If Then Plans If Then Plans Why If Then Plans Make Starting Easier Linking a clear situation to a prepared response can make intended actions easier to start when the cue appears. Linking a clear situation to a prepared response can make intended actions easier to start when the cue appears. Self Improvement That Works evidence single policy policy intervention How implementation intentions connect cues and actions What research says about goal attainment and memory How to write an if then plan for everyday goals self improvement that works behaviour goals if then plans Introduction An if then plan turns a behaviour goal into a prepared response to a recognisable situation: “If X happens, then I will do Y.” In research this is called an implementation intention . Instead of relying on yourself to remember the goal, notice the opportunity and make a fresh decision at the crucial moment, you decide in advance what cue should trigger what action. Explanatory illustration 1 That apparently small change matters. A major 2024 review covering 642 independent tests found that implementation intentions improved behavioural, cognitive and emotional outcomes, with effects varying by outcome and study conditions. Plans worked better when they genuinely used a contingent if then structure, when people were motivated to pursue the underlying goal and when the plans were rehearsed. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of Soc DOIThe when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... For self improvement, the practical lesson is straightforward: once you know what behaviour you genuinely intend to perform, give it a clear moment at which to begin. How cues become actions A goal intention states what you want: “I intend to exercise regularly” or “I want to study more consistently”. An implementation intention adds a trigger and response: “If I finish work on Monday, Wednesday or Friday, then I will put on my trainers and go for a 30 minute walk.” This is more than making the original goal more detailed. The distinctive feature is the contingent link between the situation and the response. The 2024 meta analysis found larger effects for plans explicitly formulated contingently, while an earlier experiment on fruit and vegetable consumption found that an if then format produced a larger behavioural increase than a more general plan that did not explicitly connect a situation with an action. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of Soc DOIThe when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... Research points to two complementary processes. Specifying the “if” can make the critical situation more mentally accessible, increasing the chance that it is noticed when it arrives. Linking that cue to the “then” response can in turn make initiation of the intended action more efficient. The original large scale review of 94 independent tests found support for both greater accessibility of specified opportunities and more automatic initiation of the associated responses. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... Consider the difference between these plans: Vague: “I will read more.” Scheduled but still deliberative: “I will try to read in the evening.” If then: “If I put my mug in the dishwasher after dinner, then I will sit in the armchair and read for 20 minutes.” Obstacle plan: “If I feel tempted to check my phone while reading, then I will leave it on the kitchen counter until I finish.” The third and fourth plans identify events that can actually be encountered. Once dinner ends or the temptation to check the phone appears, the person does not need to invent the next move from scratch. That does not mean behaviour literally becomes an uncontrollable reflex. Experiments support faster or more efficient cue linked responding, but the strongest version of the “ automaticity ” explanation remains debatable. In prospective memory experiments, implementation intentions improved remembering but did not always protect performance when cognitive demands became high. [Guilford Journals] guilfordjournals.com Open source on guilfordjournals.com. A useful everyday interpretation is therefore that an if then plan reduces the amount of fresh deliberation needed at the decision point , rather than eliminating conscious control altogether. What is Implementation Intention Explained in 2 min Channel: Productivity Guy Open on YouTube What research says about attainment and memory The evidence base is substantial, although the size of the benefit varies considerably between settings. Gollwitzer and Sheeran's influential 2006 meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, d = 0.65. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The much larger review published online in 2024 examined 642 independent tests accumulated across roughly 25 years of research and reported beneficial effects across behavioural, cognitive and affective outcomes, with effect sizes ranging from d = 0.27 to 0.66. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of Soc DOIThe when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... Those broad averages should not be interpreted as a promise that every if then plan will produce a large change. Domain specific reviews often find smaller effects. A meta analysis of physical activity studies, for example, reported an overall standardised effect of 0.31 immediately after interventions and 0.24 at follow up. Another review of randomised physical activity trials found benefits particularly where planning was reinforced and included ways of dealing with barriers. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. Diet research illustrates why the details matter. A review of 23 studies found a larger effect for increasing healthy eating ( d = 0.51) than for reducing unhealthy eating ( d = 0.29), while also warning that features of study design could inflate some estimates. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do implementation intentions help to eat a healthy diet? A systematic review and meta analysis of the empirical evidence PubMedFe... A newer meta analysis restricted more tightly to genuine if then interventions and active control groups found that participants reported eating about 0.29 additional portions of fruit and vegetables per day. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These findings are better read as evidence for a useful behavioural tool than as evidence for a universal transformation. How to Automate Your Focus: The Science of If Then Rules Channel: The Research File Open on YouTube Remembering to act at the right moment One especially relevant line of evidence concerns prospective memory : remembering to carry out an intended action later. That is precisely the problem behind everyday failures such as remembering an important task only after leaving the office or recalling the intention to take something with you once you are already on the train. A meta analysis of prospective memory experiments found a medium improvement among healthy younger adults ( d = 0.445), with larger effects when verbal if then planning was combined with imagery. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Laboratory work that held the cue and intended response constant while changing the planning strategy also found that the if then formulation strengthened the cue response link and improved prospective memory performance, whereas ordinary rehearsal did not produce the same benefit. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. This helps explain why the technique can be valuable even when motivation is not the main problem. Sometimes you already want to perform the behaviour; you simply fail to retrieve that intention at the moment when it could be performed. An implementation intention gives the environment a more active role in reminding you. Explanatory illustration 2 A plan still needs a goal you actually want If then planning is mainly a tool for translating motivation into action, not manufacturing motivation that is absent. In two studies examining this distinction, implementation intentions improved goal"},{"title":"Longer Breaks","url":"/longer-breaks/","description":"Evidence on longer interruptions is less certain, but repeated or prolonged inconsistency poses a greater risk than an isolated missed day.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Missed Days","level":3,"text":"Longer Breaks When Does a Habit Break Become a Problem? Missed Days Longer Breaks Longer Breaks When Does a Habit Break Become a Problem? Evidence on longer interruptions is less certain, but repeated or prolonged inconsistency poses a greater risk than an isolated missed day. Evidence on longer interruptions is less certain, but repeated or prolonged inconsistency poses a greater risk than an isolated missed day. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic event time window What studies can tell US about longer pauses Why evidence beyond single day lapses is limited When an interruption becomes a pattern of inconsistency self improvement that works missed days longer breaks Introduction There is no scientifically established number of missed days after which a habit suddenly “breaks”. Research supports a much narrower conclusion: one missed opportunity is usually not enough to derail habit formation, whereas longer or repeated interruptions become more concerning because they reduce repetition and may separate the behaviour from the cues that normally trigger it . Exactly where that change becomes harmful is not known. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That uncertainty matters because popular rules such as “never miss twice” can sound like research backed thresholds when they are better understood as practical heuristics. The evidence suggests a continuum rather than a cliff edge. A weekend away, an illness or a short holiday may interrupt performance without erasing an established cue–behaviour association. By contrast, repeated gaps, declining frequency or a lasting change in routine can prevent a developing behaviour from becoming reliably automatic. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study PubMedAugust 1, 2011... Published: August 1, 2011 What Studies Can Tell Us About Longer Pauses The landmark real world habit study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues followed 96 people attempting to establish daily eating, drinking or activity behaviours over 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition, and missing one opportunity did not materially disrupt that process. But the same research also found that people who performed their behaviour more consistently were more likely to show the expected habit development trajectory. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That gives researchers a strong comparison point for an isolated lapse, but it does not tell us what happens after three, seven or fourteen missed days. Later work has tried to get closer to that question. A six week study of university students building study habits recorded 2,483 repetitions after participants' initial attempts. Of these, 530 — about 21% — followed a pause of at least one day. The researchers explicitly tested whether the number of days since the previous repetition predicted automaticity and found no statistically significant effect. The apparent reassurance has an important limitation, however: pauses longer than one day were rare , leaving the study with relatively little evidence about genuinely prolonged interruptions. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)App Based Habit Building Reduces Motivational Impairments During Studying – An Event Sampling Study PMCFebruary 7... A separate app based dataset produced a less reassuring result. Users were building habits in areas including studying, exercise and mental focusing practices. Here, roughly 16% of recorded repetitions followed a one day pause and about 21% followed pauses of two days or more. In analyses using these data, time since the previous repetition negatively predicted automaticity. A later paper examining both datasets therefore treated “habit pausing” — days since the previous performance — as a potentially important control variable and reported a negative relationship with automaticity in both datasets. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Efficacy in Habit Building: How General and Habit Specific Self Efficacy Influence Behavioral Automatization and... These findings should not be converted into a precise countdown. They come from different samples and designs, and much of the evidence is observational rather than an experiment randomly assigning people to, say, two day versus seven day breaks. They do, however, undermine the stronger claim that the length of an interruption simply does not matter. Habit Expert, Wendy Wood, breaks down the three bases of habit formation! (Behavioral Science) Channel: Pete Judo Open on YouTube Why There Is No Reliable “Maximum Break” A major obstacle is that habit formation studies have generally been designed to investigate repetition and the growth of automaticity , not to determine the maximum interruption a habit can survive. The 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of health related habit formation illustrates the problem. It identified 20 experimental or quasi experimental studies involving 2,601 participants. Only four reported mean or median times to habit formation, and individual estimates varied dramatically, from 4 to 335 days. Eleven of the 20 included studies were rated as having a high risk of bias. The review concluded that frequency and stable repetition matter but also explicitly noted that the degree of consistency required remains unclear. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This makes universal claims such as “a habit survives X days without practice” difficult to justify. Several variables are entangled: How developed the habit already is. A new behaviour is still acquiring automaticity, so a long gap deprives it of repetitions during a period when repetition is doing substantial learning work. Lally's study found that early repetitions produced larger automaticity gains before improvements gradually approached a plateau. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Whether the original cue remains available. A person may stop doing a workday habit during a holiday simply because the workday cue has disappeared. That is not necessarily equivalent to repeatedly encountering the normal cue and failing to respond to it. Whether the context changes permanently. Moving home, changing workplace or adopting a new daily schedule can remove or alter the cues associated with an established response. Research on “habit discontinuity” shows that behaviours can be disrupted when their usual contexts change. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... How frequently the behaviour was supposed to occur. Seven days without a daily behaviour means seven lost opportunities. Seven days without a Saturday only behaviour may contain no missed opportunity at all. Counting elapsed days therefore makes less sense than considering missed repetitions relative to the behaviour's intended frequency. The research consequently offers no defensible equivalent of an expiry date. The evidence supports increasing concern as interruptions become repeated or prolonged, not a universal day on which previous progress disappears. Explanatory illustration 2 A Holiday Can Pause Performance Without Erasing the Habit One particularly useful distinction is between not performing a habit because its cue is temporarily absent and repeatedly failing to perform it when its normal cue is present. A small qualitative study by Lally, Jane Wardle and Benjamin Gardner provides a concrete example. Ten participants in a habit based weight management intervention described how their new behaviours developed in everyday life. Many habits became tied to work based contexts. Weekends and holidays disrupted performance because the familiar cues were absent — yet participants commonly resumed the behaviours when they returned to work. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study PubMedAugust 1, 2011... Published: August 1, 2011 This suggests that a temporary interruption in behaviour does not necessarily mean that the underlying association has vanished. A habit can effectively become dormant when the situation that normally triggers it is missing, then reappear when that situation returns. The study was qualitative and involved only ten people, so it cannot establish how long such dormancy lasts. It nevertheless illustrates why elapsed"},{"title":"Daily Stress","url":"/daily-stress/","description":"A shorter-than-usual night can leave positive mood less resilient when ordinary stressors pile up the next day.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Mood","level":3,"text":"Daily Stress Why Bad Sleep Makes Setbacks Hit Harder Sleep and Mood Daily Stress Daily Stress Why Bad Sleep Makes Setbacks Hit Harder A shorter than usual night can leave positive mood less resilient when ordinary stressors pile up the next day. A shorter than usual night can leave positive mood less resilient when ordinary stressors pile up the next day. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Short sleep and next day emotional reactivity Stressful events and reduced positive affect Protecting habits on difficult days self improvement that works sleep and mood daily stress Introduction A short night can make ordinary setbacks hit harder the next day, but the effect is subtler than simply becoming irritable. Daily life research suggests that when people sleep less than is usual for them, routine stressors are followed by a larger loss of positive affect — feelings such as enthusiasm, interest, energy and attentiveness. The same short sleep also appears to reduce the emotional lift people get from positive events. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep duration and affective reactivity to stressors and positive events in daily life PMCSeptember 7, 2020... Published: September 7, 2020 Explanatory illustration 1 That combination matters during behaviour change. A delayed train, difficult meeting, argument, unexpected bill or disrupted plan does not have to become objectively more serious after poor sleep. Instead, the person may have less positive emotional capacity left once the disruption occurs, while rewarding moments do less to restore it. For a new habit that still requires deliberate effort, this is a useful definition of reduced day to day resilience: the setback takes more out of you, and the good parts of the day may give less back . Short sleep changes the response to an ordinary bad day One of the clearest demonstrations comes from the US National Study of Daily Experiences. Researchers followed 1,982 adults aged 33 to 84 for eight consecutive days, asking about nightly sleep duration, everyday stressors, positive events and emotions. Crucially, the analysis examined fluctuations within the same person . The question was therefore not merely whether habitual short sleepers differed from long sleepers, but whether someone responded differently to events after sleeping less or more than their own usual amount. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep duration and affective reactivity to stressors and positive events in daily life PMCSeptember 7, 2020... Published: September 7, 2020 They did. On days containing a stressor, positive affect declined. But the decline was larger when the previous night's sleep had been shorter than usual. The researchers illustrated the difference by comparing nights one hour below and one hour above a person's normal duration: the estimated fall in positive affect associated with a stressor was −0.17 after the shorter night versus −0.10 after the longer night on the study's affect scale. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep duration and affective reactivity to stressors and positive events in daily life PMCSeptember 7, 2020... Published: September 7, 2020 The result is particularly relevant because the stressors were not extreme laboratory challenges. The study concerned naturally occurring daily experiences, including the sorts of interpersonal disagreements and work demands that routinely interfere with plans. It therefore resembles the environment in which habits actually succeed or fail far more closely than a test of behaviour under ideal conditions. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep duration and affective reactivity to stressors and positive events in daily life PMCSeptember 7, 2020... Published: September 7, 2020 There is an important qualification. Short sleep did not significantly alter negative affect reactivity to stressors in this study. In other words, its distinctive finding was not that a bad night's sleep reliably made people much more distressed by every problem. It was that stressors consumed more of their positive mood . That distinction fits broader experimental evidence: a large meta analysis of 154 sleep loss experiments found reductions in positive affect across total sleep deprivation, partial restriction and fragmented sleep, while effects on negative affect varied more with the form of sleep loss and study characteristics. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep loss and emotion: A systematic review and meta analysis of over 50 years of experimental research PubMedDecember 21, 2023... Published: December 21, 2023 So the everyday experience may be less dramatic than “everything makes me angry when I am tired”. A more evidence aligned description is: ordinary friction can leave less enthusiasm and attentiveness available for whatever comes next . Dr. Matt Walker: Improve Sleep to Boost Mood & Emotional Regulation Huberman Lab Guest Series Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Small stressors can become disproportionately costly Laboratory research provides a useful complement to the daily diary evidence. In two experiments involving 53 healthy adults, researchers compared rested participants with people kept awake for a night and then exposed them to cognitive tasks whose difficulty, time pressure and performance feedback created either relatively mild or high stress. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation and stressors: evidence for elevated negative affect in response to mild stressors when sleep deprived PubMedFe... After the mild stressor condition, sleep deprived participants reported greater stress, anxiety and anger than rested controls. Yet sleep deprivation did not selectively magnify reactions to the high stress condition, which produced strong responses in both groups. The authors interpreted this pattern as evidence that sleep deprivation may lower the threshold at which cognitive demands are experienced as stressful. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation and stressors: evidence for elevated negative affect in response to mild stressors when sleep deprived PubMedFe... That finding should not be treated as proof that a modestly shorter night has the same effect as total sleep deprivation. It does, however, help explain why sleep loss can be especially relevant to mundane friction . When rested, a minor scheduling problem or difficult task may remain background noise. When depleted, the same demand can consume a larger share of the day's emotional resources. Evidence from medical residents points in a similar direction under real working conditions. Researchers examined residents' sleep alongside their emotional responses to events that either disrupted or advanced their work goals. Sleep loss intensified negative emotions and fatigue following goal disruptive events while weakening the positive emotional response to goal enhancing events. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effects of sleep loss on medical residents' emotional reactions to work events: a cognitive energy model PubMed... For behaviour change, that asymmetry is consequential. Imagine that the intended behaviour is an evening workout. A difficult meeting does not directly prevent exercise , but it is a goal disruptive event elsewhere in the day. After adequate sleep, the person may absorb it and still retain enough energy and positive anticipation to train. After short sleep, the same event may extract more emotionally, leaving the workout feeling like another demand rather than the rewarding action originally planned. Stress takes more away while good events give less back The daily life findings reveal a second effect that is easy to overlook. Sleep duration changed not only responses to stressors but also responses to positive events . After longer than usual sleep, positive events produced a larger rise in positive affect; after shorter than usual sleep, that emotional benefit was smaller. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep duration and affective reactivity to stressors and positive events in daily life PMCSeptember 7, 2020... Published: September 7, 2020 This matters because positive emotions appear to play a role in recovery from everyday stress. In a separate analysis of 1,588 adults completing eight days of diary reports, people who experienced higher than usual positive emotion on a stressor day showed a smaller same day rise in negative emotion. They also reported less negative emotion the following day and were less likely to report another stressor the next day. These observational associations do not prove that deliberately producing positive emotion will cause those outcomes, but they show why losing positive affect is not a trivial side effect. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Positive Emotions Experienced on Days of Stress"},{"title":"Effort Cost","url":"/effort-cost/","description":"After too little sleep, demanding tasks can feel less worth the effort even when the intended reward has not changed.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Mood","level":3,"text":"Effort Cost Why Tired Tasks Feel Harder to Start Sleep and Mood Effort Cost Effort Cost Why Tired Tasks Feel Harder to Start After too little sleep, demanding tasks can feel less worth the effort even when the intended reward has not changed. After too little sleep, demanding tasks can feel less worth the effort even when the intended reward has not changed. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Effort discounting after sleep loss Cognitive effort versus physical effort What this means for demanding habits self improvement that works sleep and mood effort cost Introduction After too little sleep, a demanding task can become less attractive even when its reward has not changed. The useful concept here is effort discounting : as the effort required to obtain a reward rises, the reward becomes less valuable in practical terms. Controlled experiments suggest that sleep loss can steepen this discounting, particularly for cognitive effort . A tired person may still value passing an exam, finishing a report or keeping a learning habit, yet become less willing to pay the immediate mental cost required to get there. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for behaviour change. Sleep loss does not simply remove goals or make every reward less appealing. Instead, it can alter the calculation between what an action is worth and what it seems to cost to perform . This helps explain the familiar experience of knowing that a planned behaviour matters while finding its first demanding step unusually difficult to start. Effort discounting after sleep loss Effort discounting describes a simple trade off. Suppose one option pays £5 for an easy task while another pays £10 for a substantially harder one. The £10 remains £10, but the work needed to obtain it reduces its subjective value. Increase the required effort far enough and a person will eventually prefer the smaller, easier reward. Sleep deprivation appears capable of shifting that crossover point. In a 2013 laboratory study involving 77 healthy young adults across three experiments, Camilo Libedinsky and colleagues compared choices made after normal sleep with choices made after a night without sleep. Their effort task offered monetary rewards in return for differing amounts of work, such as typing different numbers of letter strings backwards. After sleep deprivation, participants discounted rewards more strongly as required effort increased. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 The comparison with delay discounting made the result especially informative. The researchers also examined whether tired participants became more inclined to take smaller rewards immediately rather than wait for larger ones. Sleep deprivation did not significantly change that preference, even though it increased effort discounting. In other words, the result was not simply that tired people valued future rewards less or became uniformly impatient. The cost attached specifically to working for the reward had changed. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 A 2025 systematic review of human effort discounting research places these experiments within a wider literature showing that effort and delay are meaningfully different costs. It notes both the sleep deprivation result and subsequent evidence that fatigue manipulations can change cognitive effort discounting. At the same time, effort discounting measures show appreciable within person variability, so they should not be treated as perfectly stable measurements of motivation. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressA systematic review of effort discounting research in humans: Current knowledge, recommendations, and future di... The Real Reason You Feel Lazy (It's Not Your Fault) Channel: Doodle Mind Open on YouTube Duration becomes part of the price A later experiment made the mechanism more concrete by asking participants how much sustained attention they were prepared to provide for money. Participants first completed a vigilance task after normal sleep and after sleep deprivation, with different monetary incentives for fast responses. They were then offered rewards for maintaining attention for periods ranging from one to 30 minutes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation increases the costs of attentional effort: Performance, preference and pupil size PubMed... Longer attention already reduced the attractiveness of the reward when people were rested. Sleep deprivation made that duration related discounting steeper. Put simply, 30 minutes of concentration carried a larger subjective price when participants had not slept . Pupil measurements also tracked variations in arousal and performance, supporting the interpretation that effort allocation under sleep deprivation reflects an interaction between physiological state, incentives and task demands rather than a simple decision to stop caring. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation increases the costs of attentional effort: Performance, preference and pupil size PubMed... This fits a broader review of sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance. Sleep loss reduces arousal and increases lapses and other constraints on cognitive performance; maintaining performance can therefore require greater compensatory effort. At the same time, willingness to provide that effort can decline. Performance after sleep loss can consequently reflect two interacting problems: reduced capacity and a higher subjective cost of mobilising the remaining capacity . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance ScienceDirect... That is an important correction to everyday interpretations of tired behaviour. Choosing the easy task does not necessarily mean that the underlying goal suddenly matters less. If concentration has become more costly, the same reward may simply no longer clear the person's threshold for investing the required effort. Cognitive effort is especially vulnerable The evidence does not support the stronger claim that insufficient sleep makes all effort equally unattractive. One particularly revealing experiment directly compared willingness to invest cognitive and physical effort in the same people. Twenty four healthy young adults completed laboratory sessions after either three consecutive nights with only a three hour sleep opportunity or three nights with a nine hour opportunity. Before making their choices, participants were trained on six difficulty levels of separate cognitive and physical tasks. This mattered because the researchers wanted to distinguish genuine reluctance to expend effort from a rational belief that a tired participant would simply fail the harder task. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMC... Sleep restriction reduced willingness to choose high cognitive effort, high reward options, particularly at the greatest effort levels. Yet it did not significantly reduce willingness to select physically effortful options for reward. Participants had also demonstrated that they could perform the cognitive effort levels successfully, making reduced ability alone an inadequate explanation for the choices. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMC... The result argues against treating motivation as one tank that simply empties when someone sleeps badly. Cognitive and physical effort have partly distinct motivational properties even in rested people, and research using parallel effort tasks finds only a moderate relationship between willingness to expend the two kinds of effort. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is also an important distinction between willingness to choose physical effort and how physically strenuous exercise feels once underway . A 2025 systematic review and meta analysis covering 45 studies found that sleep deprivation impaired several measures of sporting performance and, across 12 studies assessing ratings of perceived exertion, increased subjective exertion overall. The effect varied by population and form of sleep deprivation, so this does not mean every workout will feel harder after every short night. It does show why the finding that cognitive motivation can be selectively reduced should not be misread as evidence that sleep is irrelevant to"},{"title":"Positive Mood","url":"/positive-mood/","description":"Reduced enthusiasm, energy and attentiveness are among the most consistent emotional effects of insufficient sleep.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Mood","level":3,"text":"Positive Mood The Mood Loss You May Notice Least Sleep and Mood Positive Mood Positive Mood The Mood Loss You May Notice Least Reduced enthusiasm, energy and attentiveness are among the most consistent emotional effects of insufficient sleep. Reduced enthusiasm, energy and attentiveness are among the most consistent emotional effects of insufficient sleep. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Why positive affect falls reliably How reduced energy changes habit appeal Why negative mood is less consistent self improvement that works sleep and mood positive mood Introduction Insufficient sleep does not have to make you obviously miserable to alter your mood. One of its most reliable emotional effects is quieter: positive affect falls . Enthusiasm, interest, energy, attentiveness and the feeling of being ready to engage can all weaken, even when anger, sadness or other negative feelings change little. Explanatory illustration 1 That asymmetry is unusually well supported. A 2024 meta analysis covering 154 experimental studies, 1,338 effect sizes and 5,717 participants found that total sleep deprivation, restricted sleep and fragmented sleep all reduced positive affect. Effects on negative affect and depressive symptoms were considerably more mixed. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep loss and emotion: A systematic review and meta analysis of over 50 years of experimental research PubMedDecember 21, 2023... Published: December 21, 2023 An earlier meta analysis likewise estimated a large reduction in positive mood after sleep loss (Hedges' g = −0.94), compared with a smaller increase in negative mood (g = 0.45). [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effect of sleep deprivation and restriction on mood, emotion, and emotion regulation: three meta analyses in one PubMedJune 1... For behaviour change, that distinction matters. After poor sleep, the problem may not be “I feel terrible”. It may simply be that today's workout, study session or other useful action has lost some of its pull. Why positive affect falls so reliably Researchers use positive affect more broadly than everyday words such as happiness. Common questionnaires capture states including being active, inspired, interested, excited, enthusiastic, energetic, alert, attentive, determined, confident and strong. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This makes the sleep loss effect easier to recognise in ordinary life: it can look less like unhappiness and more like diminished psychological activation. The strongest synthesis comes from the preregistered 2024 Psychological Bulletin review. Across experiments involving people aged 7 to 79, every major form of experimentally induced sleep loss reduced positive affect, with standardised mean differences ranging from −0.27 to −1.14. There was also evidence that high arousal positive states were particularly vulnerable. In other words, insufficient sleep appears especially capable of suppressing the energetic end of feeling good rather than merely shifting people from happiness towards sadness. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep loss and emotion: A systematic review and meta analysis of over 50 years of experimental research PubMedDecember 21, 2023... Published: December 21, 2023 The finding is not simply a consequence of combining many different mood outcomes into one score. Laboratory work that separated positive mood into more specific components found changes in attentiveness, general positive affect, joviality and self assurance. During prolonged wakefulness, attentiveness showed a particularly strong decline with time awake. Chronic restriction to a six hour nightly sleep opportunity also reduced attentiveness relative to a ten hour opportunity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Contrasting Effects of Sleep Restriction, Total Sleep Deprivation, and Sleep Timing on Positive and Negative Affect PubMedAugust... There is an important qualification. Effect sizes vary substantially between experiments, and different sleep manipulations are not interchangeable. The earlier 2021 meta analysis found considerable heterogeneity in positive mood effects and a methodological influence of study design. Its striking overall estimate of g = −0.94 should therefore not be read as a prediction of how much any particular person's enthusiasm will decline after losing a particular number of hours. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP Academiceffect of sleep deprivation and restriction on mood, emotion, and emotion regulation: three meta analyses in one SLEEP Ox... The robust conclusion is the direction of the effect, not a universal dose response formula. How Light Impacts Your Sleep and Mood: Easy Daily Tactics from Dr. Andrew Huberman Channel: Tim Ferriss Open on YouTube Broken sleep can drain positive mood Total hours asleep are not the whole story. Experiments that repeatedly awaken participants provide an unusually clear demonstration of how disrupted sleep can selectively weaken positive feeling. In one controlled study, healthy adults experienced a night of forced awakenings as well as a night of uninterrupted sleep. Positive affect scores averaged 22.12 after the disrupted night compared with 26.44 after uninterrupted sleep, a medium sized difference (d = −0.53). Negative affect, by contrast, did not differ significantly between conditions. The reduction extended across positive states including joviality, serenity, self assurance and attentiveness. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicPartial Sleep Deprivation Attenuates the Positive Affective System: Effects Across Multiple Measurement Modalities SLEEP ... An earlier experiment compared several nights of forced awakenings with sleep loss produced by delaying bedtime. Although the groups experienced comparable reductions in total sleep time, people subjected to repeated awakenings showed a larger deterioration in positive mood. They also lost more slow wave sleep, and the difference in slow wave sleep statistically mediated the difference in positive mood. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Effects of Sleep Continuity Disruption on Positive Mood and Sleep Architecture in Healthy Adults PubMedNovember 1, 2015... Published: November 1, 2015 A subsequent crossover experiment strengthened the case that this is not merely a questionnaire effect. One night of disrupted sleep reduced self reported positive affect, weakened the normal pain inhibiting effect associated with positive emotion and altered attentional bias towards positive material, while corresponding negative affect measures did not change significantly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These studies do not establish that loss of slow wave sleep is the single mechanism behind every positive mood effect of insufficient sleep. They do show something more practically useful: two nights containing a similar quantity of sleep need not have identical emotional consequences if their continuity and architecture differ . Explanatory illustration 2 How reduced energy changes habit appeal The relevance to self improvement becomes clearer when positive affect is treated as part of the motivational environment rather than simply as an emotional outcome. Many desirable behaviours have an immediate effort cost and a delayed payoff. Starting a difficult piece of work, practising a skill or preparing something rather than choosing the easiest alternative requires the action to feel sufficiently worth engaging with. Experimental evidence suggests that sleep loss can change this calculation. In one study, 24 healthy young adults underwent three nights with either a three hour or nine hour sleep opportunity before making choices about how much effort to expend for rewards. Sleep restriction reduced willingness to invest cognitive effort , although motivation to invest physical effort was preserved. The researchers designed the task to distinguish this motivational change from participants simply being less capable of performing the task. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation A smaller study following five nights with five hours in bed found meaningful individual differences too. Participants whose subjective motivation deteriorated more under sleep restriction also showed worse aspects of attentional performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These experiments caution against the sweeping claim that poor sleep destroys all motivation. Its effects can depend on the kind of effort involved and on the individual. Positive mood may also matter for how rewards themselves are processed. In a crossover experiment of 42 healthy adults, forced awakenings did not produce a significant overall decline in reward learning. But the participants whose positive affect fell after disrupted sleep showed"},{"title":"Mental Habits","url":"/mental-habits/","description":"Mental habits such as studying, planning and writing may become harder after restricted sleep even when simpler physical actions remain manageable.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Mood","level":3,"text":"Mental Habits Why Tiredness Can Hit Mental Habits First Sleep and Mood Mental Habits Mental Habits Why Tiredness Can Hit Mental Habits First Mental habits such as studying, planning and writing may become harder after restricted sleep even when simpler physical actions remain manageable. Mental habits such as studying, planning and writing may become harder after restricted sleep even when simpler physical actions remain manageable. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case aspect population context Evidence from partial sleep restriction Cognitive effort versus physical effort Choosing lower friction actions after poor sleep self improvement that works sleep and mood mental habits Introduction Partial sleep restriction can make mental habits harder before it makes every kind of action feel equally difficult. The clearest controlled evidence comes from an experiment in which healthy young adults were still capable of completing demanding cognitive tasks after three nights of severely restricted sleep, yet became less willing to choose those tasks for reward. Their willingness to undertake comparable physical effort did not fall. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. After poor sleep, the problem may not simply be that studying, planning or writing has become impossible. The person may still be capable of doing the work while becoming more inclined to choose an easier alternative. Meanwhile, a straightforward physical action may remain comparatively approachable. Longer running sleep restriction experiments add another complication: objective cognitive deficits can accumulate faster than people recognise them subjectively. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The practical lesson is not that mental habits should be abandoned whenever sleep is imperfect. It is that their activation cost matters more on tired days . Reducing unnecessary cognitive friction can help preserve the habit without pretending that restricted sleep has left the decision making environment unchanged. Restricted sleep changed which effort people chose A particularly useful experiment published in Nature and Science of Sleep in 2022 asked a more precise question than whether tired people perform worse: does restricted sleep change what kind of effort they are willing to undertake? Twenty four healthy young adults completed two laboratory conditions. In the rested condition they received nine hours of sleep opportunity on each of three nights; in the restricted condition, only three hours on each night. The same participants therefore made decisions under both conditions. Before the critical choice phase, researchers trained them on six levels of cognitive effort and six levels of physical effort. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 The cognitive task involved monitoring rapidly presented streams of letters, with additional streams increasing the mental workload. The physical task involved maintaining specified percentages of each participant's maximum handgrip force. During the subsequent choice phase, participants repeatedly chose between a low effort, low reward option and alternatives offering larger rewards in return for greater effort. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 Restricted sleep produced an unusually informative split. Participants became less willing to select higher cognitive effort , with the effect appearing at all but the lowest of the higher effort levels. Yet their willingness to choose greater physical effort did not significantly change. The interaction between sleep condition, effort domain and effort level was statistically significant (p = 0.001). [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 This was not simply a case of tired participants discovering that they could no longer perform the mental task. Their reinforcement rates on the cognitive task remained at ceiling, and the researchers found no consistent sleep related performance reduction capable of explaining their choices. A control analysis likewise found that performance at a particular cognitive effort level did not significantly predict whether participants chose it. Effort and reward did predict their choices. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 That makes the finding particularly relevant to habits. Restricted sleep can apparently alter the decision to invest mental effort even when basic capacity to perform the task remains available . A tired student may therefore be able to study but choose something easier; a writer may be capable of drafting but keep postponing the document; someone intending to plan tomorrow's work may instead perform a simpler routine task. Those everyday examples are reasonable applications of the experimental finding, not behaviours directly tested in the study. The experiment was also small, involved healthy young adults and used an unusually severe restriction of only three hours' sleep opportunity for three nights. It therefore demonstrates a controlled effect rather than establishing exactly how much ordinary short sleep will change anyone's studying or writing behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PubMed... What Happens To Your Body And Brain If You Don't Get Sleep The Human Body Channel: Insider Tech Open on YouTube Cognitive effort and physical effort are not interchangeable The experiment challenges an intuitively appealing model of tiredness: that sleep loss simply turns down one general purpose supply of motivation. At least under these laboratory conditions, participants did not become uniformly unwilling to work. Their choices depended on what kind of work was being offered . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PMCNovember 4, 2022... Published: November 4, 2022 That result fits a broader literature showing that restricted sleep does not affect every cognitive measure identically. A 2017 meta analysis covering 61 studies and 71 study populations found an overall adverse effect of experimentally restricted sleep on cognition (Hedges' g = −0.383). Significant effects appeared for executive functioning (g = −0.324), sustained attention (g = −0.409) and long term memory (g = −0.192), while the evidence was insufficient to establish effects in several other measured domains. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The neurocognitive consequences of sleep restriction: A meta analytic review PubMedJuly 28, 2017... Published: July 28, 2017 More recent evidence reinforces the importance of distinguishing cognitive functions. A 2024 meta analysis of 44 studies examined a single night restricted to two to six hours of sleep opportunity. Sleepiness increased substantially and sustained attention deteriorated, including slower responses and more attentional lapses. Yet the pooled analysis did not find significant effects on working memory, inhibitory control, choice reaction time or cognitive throughput after that one restricted night. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... The timescale matters too. In the classic 2003 dose response experiment, 48 healthy adults were assigned to four, six or eight hours in bed per night for 14 days, with a separate total sleep deprivation comparison. Cognitive deficits accumulated progressively in the four and six hour groups. The researchers concluded that six hours or less per night could produce substantial cumulative neurobehavioural impairment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Together, these findings argue against treating “tired” as a single performance state. One short night may particularly expose sustained attention; repeated restriction can accumulate broader costs; and under severe multiday restriction, the"},{"title":"Self Control","url":"/self-control/","description":"A failed habit after a poor night may reflect lower positive mood, impaired attention and higher effort costs rather than one simple loss of willpower.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Mood","level":3,"text":"Self Control Is Poor Sleep Really a Willpower Problem? Sleep and Mood Self Control Self Control Is Poor Sleep Really a Willpower Problem? A failed habit after a poor night may reflect lower positive mood, impaired attention and higher effort costs rather than one simple loss of willpower. A failed habit after a poor night may reflect lower positive mood, impaired attention and higher effort costs rather than one simple loss of willpower. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism What sleep and self control studies show Why mood is only part of the pathway Avoiding discipline only explanations self improvement that works sleep and mood self control Introduction A habit failure after a poor night’s sleep can look like a straightforward failure of willpower: you skip the planned workout, abandon the difficult piece of work, order the easier meal or give in to a distraction. Sleep and self control really are associated, but “less sleep equals less willpower” is too simple an explanation . Explanatory illustration 1 Sleep loss can change several ingredients of goal directed behaviour at once. It can weaken inhibitory control, destabilise attention, reduce positive mood and make cognitive effort feel less worthwhile. Those changes can produce the outward appearance of low self control even when the underlying problem is not a single depleted store of discipline. A 2021 meta analysis of 61 independent studies found that both sleep quality and sleep duration were associated with self control, including within person effects, but its authors explicitly identified the causal processes linking the two as an important unresolved question. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis PubMedJune 5, 2021... Published: June 5, 2021 For self improvement, that distinction matters. If yesterday’s plan becomes unusually difficult after poor sleep, the useful question is not only “Why wasn’t I disciplined enough?” but also “What changed in the machinery required to carry the plan out?” What sleep and self control studies actually show The broad association is reasonably well established. In the 2021 systematic review and meta analysis, better sleep quality showed correlations with better self control of 0.26 between individuals and 0.35 within individuals; sleep duration showed smaller corresponding associations of 0.14 and 0.20. The review included experimental as well as observational research, but it did not establish that every behavioural failure following short sleep is caused by a unitary reduction in “willpower”. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis Sleep and self control: A systematic review and meta analysis PubMedJune 5, 2021... Published: June 5, 2021 More specific cognitive evidence strengthens one part of the case. A 2025 meta analysis examined 24 studies involving 712 healthy participants using Go/No Go and Stop Signal tasks, which test the ability to withhold or stop a response. Sleep deprivation produced a moderate impairment in inhibitory control on both types of task. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A meta analytic investigation of the effect of sleep deprivation on inhibitory control PubMedDecember 8, 2024... Published: December 8, 2024 A broader 2025 meta analysis covering 79 publications likewise found that sleep loss affected several components of executive functioning, although the size and form of the impairment differed by task and outcome measure. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The impairments of sleep loss on core executive functions: General and task specific effects ScienceDirect... That makes a genuine inhibition pathway plausible. When someone who intended not to check social media automatically reaches for a phone, or struggles to resist an immediately attractive alternative to a planned activity, poorer response inhibition may contribute. But inhibition is only one mechanism. Sleep deprivation is particularly reliable at disrupting vigilant attention : the capacity to keep attention stably directed at a task. Performance becomes more variable and attentional lapses become more frequent, with deficits accumulating across repeated nights of restricted sleep. [Nature] nature.com Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review NeuropsychopharmacologyJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 In one controlled chronic sleep restriction experiment, shortened sleep increased attentional lapses fivefold even though participants did not uniformly report feeling less alert. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Chronic sleep curtailment, even without extended ( 16 h) wakefulness, degrades human vigilance performance PubMedJune 5, 2018... Published: June 5, 2018 That creates an important interpretive problem. Suppose someone sits down to study after insufficient sleep, repeatedly loses the thread, rereads paragraphs, checks unrelated tabs and eventually gives up. Calling the episode “low self control” describes the outcome, but may misidentify much of the mechanism. Maintaining the intended behaviour has become cognitively harder because attention itself is less stable. Sleep loss can even interfere with recognising errors. A systematic review and meta analysis of performance and error monitoring found relatively inconsistent evidence about people’s overall judgements of their performance, but more consistent evidence that sleep loss impairs error monitoring. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That matters during behaviour change because successful self regulation often requires noticing a small deviation early enough to correct it. How to Increase Your Willpower & Tenacity Huberman Lab Podcast Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube The hidden cost of effort One of the most useful alternatives to a discipline only explanation comes from research on effort discounting . People do not merely decide whether a goal is valuable; they continually weigh its expected benefit against what obtaining it seems likely to cost. Sleep can alter that calculation. In experiments comparing rested and sleep deprived participants, sleep deprivation changed choices involving effortful monetary rewards without producing the same effect on delay discounting. In other words, the critical shift was not simply greater impatience for immediate rewards : effort itself had become more important to the choice. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Research on sustained attention reaches a similar conclusion. One experiment found that sleep deprivation impaired vigilance and increased the extent to which rewards were discounted by the duration of the task. The authors interpreted the results as evidence that the perceived cost of attentional effort rises with sleep deprivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A wider review of sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance similarly concluded that sleep loss can reduce willingness to exert effort and make cognitive performance feel more effortful; sufficiently strong external incentives can sometimes partially compensate, which itself argues against a simple all or nothing loss of capacity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance A small controlled study makes the distinction especially concrete. Twenty four healthy young adults completed tasks after either three nights with only three hours of sleep opportunity per night or three nights with nine hours. Sleep restriction reduced their willingness to invest cognitive effort for reward, while motivation to invest physical effort was spared. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That does not mean poor sleep always leaves physical motivation untouched; the study was small and tested particular laboratory choices. It does show why “motivation” should not automatically be treated as one global quantity. Sleep loss may change the cost benefit calculation for some forms of effort more than others. For everyday behaviour change, this produces a familiar pattern. Preparing a meal, starting a complicated work task or organising everything needed for tomorrow can contain numerous small decisions and attentional demands. After poor sleep, the desired outcome may still matter just as much while the route towards it has become more expensive. Choosing the easier option can therefore resemble a sudden collapse in character when it is partly a shift in perceived effort. Explanatory illustration 2 Why mood is only part of the pathway Mood belongs in this explanation, but it should not absorb everything else. A major 2024 meta analysis covering 1,338 effect sizes from 154 studies and 5,717 participants found that total sleep"},{"title":"One Miss","url":"/one-miss/","description":"A single missed opportunity does not erase the automaticity built through earlier repetitions, although it forgoes one chance to reinforce it.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Missed Days","level":3,"text":"One Miss Does One Missed Day Really Break a Habit? Missed Days One Miss One Miss Does One Missed Day Really Break a Habit? A single missed opportunity does not erase the automaticity built through earlier repetitions, although it forgoes one chance to reinforce it. A single missed opportunity does not erase the automaticity built through earlier repetitions, although it forgoes one chance to reinforce it. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the 12 week habit study actually found Why automaticity does not reset to zero What one missed repetition does and does not cost self improvement that works missed days one miss Introduction Missing one day does not reset a developing habit to zero. The best known direct evidence comes from Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ 12 week real world habit study: after people repeatedly performed a chosen behaviour in the same daily context, a single missed opportunity produced only a very small change in measured automaticity and did not materially disrupt the overall formation process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That finding matters because habit strength is not a streak counter. It reflects learning: repeated encounters with a familiar context become associated with a particular response, making that response progressively easier to initiate without deliberate thought. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 One omission means one less opportunity to strengthen that association. It does not erase the learning produced by previous repetitions. The useful distinction, then, is between losing one repetition and losing the habit itself . The first happens whenever a day is missed. The available evidence does not show that the second follows from a single miss. What the 12 week habit study actually found Lally and colleagues recruited 96 volunteers and asked each person to choose a new eating, drinking or activity behaviour. Participants were to perform it once a day in response to a consistent contextual cue — for example, doing the behaviour after breakfast — for 12 weeks. They recorded whether they had performed it and completed daily measures from the Self Report Habit Index (SRHI), from which the researchers assessed automaticity. Eighty two participants supplied enough data for the main analysis. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Habit strength did not rise in a simple straight line. Instead, automaticity generally followed an asymptotic curve : early repetitions could produce relatively large gains, after which further gains became progressively smaller as a person's automaticity approached a plateau. Among participants for whom the researchers could model that trajectory, the estimated time to reach 95% of the eventual plateau ranged from 18 to 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers also examined missed opportunities directly. They defined a miss as an occasion when the participant did not perform the behaviour after having performed it on the preceding three opportunities. They identified 140 such misses across 55 participants . Immediately after an omission, automaticity fell by an average of just 0.29 points on the automaticity scale — a very small change. [Transformation Weight Control] transformationweightcontrol.com Transformation Weight Control European Journal of Social Psychology Transformation Weight ControlEuropean Journal of Social PsychologyApril 19, 2026... Published: April 19, 2026 For 67 of those missed opportunities, the researchers also had the necessary data after the participant resumed the behaviour. Across the interval containing the miss, automaticity was 0.55 points higher than before it. For comparison, when participants performed the behaviour consistently across the comparable period, the average increase was 0.79 points. The authors concluded that the difference was small enough that a single missed opportunity did not materially affect habit formation. [SciSpace] scispace.com how are habits formed modelling habit formation in the real 4iq5e623zp SciSpace(PDF) How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world (2010) Phillippa Lally 1734 CitationsOctober 1, 2010... Published: October 1, 2010 This is more informative than simply observing that some successful habit builders occasionally missed days. The researchers specifically looked at automaticity immediately around omissions and compared its movement with periods of uninterrupted performance. There is an important limit to the result. It does not establish that any number of missed days is harmless. Lally later emphasised through University College London that although one missed opportunity did not significantly affect formation, people who were very inconsistent generally failed to develop habits successfully. The researchers could not specify a universal minimum level of consistency . [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College London... The Habit Blueprint: How to Make Changes That Stick with Dr. Wendy Wood and Charles Good Channel: Charles Good Open on YouTube Why automaticity does not reset to zero The result makes sense once habit strength is separated from the visible behaviour on a particular day. Contemporary habit research generally describes habits in terms of learned associations between recurring contexts and responses. Repeatedly carrying out a response in a stable context strengthens that association until encountering the context itself can help activate the response. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Habit in Personality and Social Psychology Habit in Personality and Social Psychology PubMedJuly 24, 2017... Published: July 24, 2017 A missed performance is therefore not equivalent to deleting the association. Imagine that someone has repeatedly drunk a glass of water immediately after breakfast. Each successful pairing provides another experience of breakfast context → drink water . If they skip the water on Tuesday, Tuesday contributes no successful pairing. But the cue response learning accumulated on Monday and the preceding days has not thereby vanished. Experimental evidence published in 2024 strengthens the underlying learning account. Jennifer Labrecque, Kristen Lee and Wendy Wood had participants practise a sequential task and then objectively tested cue response associations. More practice produced stronger associations, which in turn supported performance; the improvement could not be explained simply by stronger intentions or motivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMed... This does not directly test missed days in everyday habit formation, but it helps explain why accumulated practice should not behave like a streak that disappears after one omission. The distinction between behaviour and habit strength is especially important here. A person can fail to perform a habitual response on a particular occasion even though the underlying cue response association remains. Reviews of the habit literature explicitly distinguish the occurrence of behaviour from the learned association capable of prompting it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is why “I did not do it today” and “I no longer have any habit strength” are not interchangeable statements. The first describes an event. The second makes a claim about learned automaticity — and one missed event is not evidence that all of that learning has disappeared. Explanatory illustration 2 What one missed repetition actually costs Saying that one miss does not reset a habit should not be turned into the stronger claim that the miss has literally no cost. Habit formation depends on repetition. When the behaviour is omitted, there is no new successful cue response pairing on that occasion, so the person has forgone one opportunity for further strengthening. A recent systematic review of health behaviour habit formation likewise identifies repeated performance and stable contexts as central determinants of increasing automaticity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The shape of the Lally study's learning curves adds an interesting"},{"title":"Repeated Misses","url":"/repeated-misses/","description":"Recurring misses in the same circumstances are more useful as diagnostic clues than as reasons to declare the entire habit lost.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Missed Days","level":3,"text":"Repeated Misses Repeatedly Missing the Same Habit? Change the Setup Missed Days Repeated Misses Repeated Misses Repeatedly Missing the Same Habit? Change the Setup Recurring misses in the same circumstances are more useful as diagnostic clues than as reasons to declare the entire habit lost. Recurring misses in the same circumstances are more useful as diagnostic clues than as reasons to declare the entire habit lost. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention How to distinguish isolated disruption from a pattern Finding recurring trouble spots in timing and context Adjusting the cue or effort without pretending misses did not happen self improvement that works missed days repeated misses Introduction A missed habit is not automatically a failed habit. But when the same miss keeps happening under the same conditions , it becomes useful information about the routine itself. Repeatedly skipping exercise after late meetings, forgetting a supplement on weekends, or abandoning a study session whenever it is scheduled for the evening suggests more than random disruption: the cue, timing, setting or required effort may be poorly matched to everyday life. Explanatory illustration 1 Habit research supports treating that pattern diagnostically rather than wiping progress back to zero. A single missed opportunity did not materially derail habit formation in Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ influential 12 week study, while greater consistency was associated with stronger habit development trajectories. More recent research likewise finds that repetition and stable contexts matter for automaticity . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical response to recurring misses is therefore neither “start again from day one” nor “the misses do not matter”. Keep the progress already made, identify what repeatedly prevents the behaviour, and redesign that part of the routine. When does a miss become a pattern? There is no scientifically established rule saying that two, three or five missed days require a redesign. Habit formation research varies substantially across behaviours and people, and a 2024 systematic review of 20 studies involving 2,601 participants concluded that the necessary degree of consistency remains uncertain. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A more useful distinction is therefore situational recurrence rather than a fixed number of misses . An isolated disruption has an obvious exceptional cause: illness, an emergency, travel or an unusually demanding day. A pattern appears when failures repeatedly cluster around a condition that is actually part of ordinary life. Suppose someone intends to walk for 30 minutes after lunch. Missing because of one unexpected appointment says little about the design. But if the walk disappears whenever lunch runs late, whenever the person works from home, or every Friday when the afternoon schedule changes, those misses identify a recurring weak point. This interpretation fits what researchers know about context dependent habits. Habits develop as repeated responses become associated with recurring contextual cues; changing those circumstances can disrupt habitual behaviour. In research on students moving between universities, established behaviours such as exercising and newspaper reading were more likely to survive the move when relevant aspects of their performance context remained stable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... A small qualitative habit formation study provides an especially concrete illustration. Participants often established behaviours in their working week routines, but weekends and holidays removed the cues associated with work and temporarily disrupted performance. The behaviours could return when the participants returned to their normal working context. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study PubMedAugust 1, 2011... Published: August 1, 2011 That is precisely why recurring weekend misses should not automatically be interpreted as evidence that someone “lacks discipline”. They may indicate that a habit has a workable weekday cue but no equivalent weekend setup. PAN Podcast 6 Dr. Phillippa Lally Habits and Health Channel: PAN Physicians Association for Nutrition Open on YouTube Look for the trouble spot, not a character flaw When misses cluster, the most useful question is “What is reliably different on the days this does not happen?” That shifts attention from judging motivation to finding a repeatable obstacle. Four features deserve particular scrutiny: Cue: Does the intended trigger reliably occur, and is it noticeable? “After I put my breakfast plate away” may provide a clearer event than “sometime in the morning”. Timing: Does the selected moment regularly collide with meetings, childcare, commuting, fatigue or other predictable demands? Context: Is the routine dependent on one place or schedule that disappears on weekends, travel days or work from home days? Effort: Does starting require too many preparatory steps — finding equipment, changing clothes, travelling somewhere, choosing what to do or setting everything up? Cue selection is not trivial. In a qualitative study in which 39 participants attempted to establish daily vitamin C use, researchers found that people did not necessarily choose effective cues. Participants often selected cues partly to minimise effort, for example by keeping relevant objects visible or close at hand. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What influences the selection of contextual cues when starting a new routine behaviour? An exploratory study PubMedMarch 30, 2020... Published: March 30, 2020 That makes repeated misses a kind of informal stress test. If a routine works only when the day is unusually orderly, the routine may have been designed around an ideal day rather than the person's normal environment. Context stability is particularly important. In a 2022 study, researchers examined intentional habit building in two datasets. One involved 95 university students and 2,482 study habit repetitions; another covered 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions recorded by app users. More stable performance contexts predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Other longitudinal work points in the same direction. In a study of flossing and vitamin C use, greater context stability — measured in terms of location and point within an existing routine — was associated with more frequent repetition, although some effects differed between the two behaviours. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Exploratory study of the impact of perceived reward on habit formation PMCDecember 20, 2018... Published: December 20, 2018 The important practical point is not that every behaviour must occur at precisely the same minute and location. It is that a cue needs enough regularity to become a dependable opportunity to act. How To Change with Prof. Katy Milkman Channel: Action for Happiness Open on YouTube Redesign the cue before abandoning the habit Once a recurring failure condition is visible, the smallest useful intervention is usually to change the setup around the behaviour , rather than replacing the goal. Imagine a person repeatedly intends to read for 20 minutes at 9 pm but rarely does so because that is also when household tasks accumulate. Restarting a streak on Monday leaves the underlying collision untouched. A redesign might attach reading to finishing dinner, to getting into bed, or to another event that occurs more reliably. Research suggests there is some flexibility in what kind of cue can work. In a randomised controlled trial involving 192 adults, participants linked a nutrition behaviour either to an everyday routine or to a specific time. Both approaches increased automaticity and plan enactment, with no significant group level advantage for one cue type over the other. Repeated enactment of the planned behaviour was the important predictor of automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That means a repeatedly failing clock time cue need not be defended merely because it was the original plan. A routine linked cue can replace it, and the reverse is also possible. A useful redesign follows the evidence revealed by the misses: If the cue is unreliable, make it concrete. Replace “exercise this evening” with a recognisable event such as changing out of work clothes. If the time repeatedly"},{"title":"Keep the Cue","url":"/keep-the-cue/","description":"Returning when the familiar cue next appears preserves the context that has been linking a situation with the intended behaviour.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Missed Days","level":3,"text":"Keep the Cue After a Miss, Keep the Habit Cue Missed Days Keep the Cue Keep the Cue After a Miss, Keep the Habit Cue Returning when the familiar cue next appears preserves the context that has been linking a situation with the intended behaviour. Returning when the familiar cue next appears preserves the context that has been linking a situation with the intended behaviour. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why stable contexts help habits become automatic Returning at the next normal cue When the usual cue no longer fits your life self improvement that works missed days keep the cue Introduction After a missed day, the most useful restart is usually the least dramatic one: when the familiar cue next appears, do the habit there again . If the behaviour has been paired with “after breakfast”, arriving at the office, finishing lunch or another recurring situation, returning at that same cue continues the learning process that was already under way. A lapse does not require a new start date, a compensatory session or a redesigned routine. Explanatory illustration 1 The reason is mechanical rather than motivational. Habits develop as repeated behaviour becomes associated with recurring features of its context. Eventually, encountering those features can help activate the response with less deliberate decision making. Research finds stronger automaticity when behaviours are repeated in stable rather than continually changing contexts, while one missed opportunity need not materially derail habit formation. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... For restarting after an ordinary lapse, then, the valuable thing to preserve is not the streak. It is the cue–behaviour relationship . Why stable contexts help habits become automatic In habit research, a “cue” is broader than an alarm or reminder. It can be a location, time of day , preceding action, social situation or other recurring feature present around the behaviour. Washing your hands after using the toilet is a straightforward example: the preceding event provides a reliable context in which the response has repeatedly occurred. With enough consistent pairings, exposure to contextual cues can increasingly activate the behaviour without the same degree of conscious prompting. [tandfonline.com] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: Developing habit based health behaviour change interventions: twenty one questions to guide future r... This makes context stability especially relevant after a lapse. The useful unit of continuity is not simply “I exercised yesterday and today”; it can instead be “when this situation occurs, this is what I do”. Missing one occurrence leaves that learned relationship available for the next occurrence. Returning to the normal cue supplies another pairing of the same situation with the same response. Evidence increasingly supports a causal role for that consistency . In a 2022 study, 95 university students formed study habits over six weeks. One group was instructed to vary places and times, while another kept its performance context stable. A second dataset examined 308 habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals; in the experimental study, the stable context group showed the expected context effects that were absent when place and time were deliberately varied. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMed... A later randomised study provides a useful real world example. Researchers assigned 127 insufficiently active working adults to walking plans involving consistent contexts, varied contexts or no required planning change. Both consistent and varied context planners increased walking during the four week intervention, but only the consistent context planners developed and maintained greater habit automaticity . That distinction matters: doing the behaviour and making its initiation habitual are related but not identical outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Confirming the Causal Role of Consistent Contexts in Developing a Walking Habit: A Randomized Comparison With Varied Contexts Pub... The same general pattern appears in a 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of health behaviour habit formation. Across the literature, repetition was central, but stable patterns of performing the target behaviour in a consistent context were associated with stronger habits. The authors also cautioned that research cannot yet specify a universal amount of consistency required. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. So “keep the cue” should not be interpreted as a magical formula in which precisely matching one time or place guarantees a habit. It is better understood as a way of giving associative learning a relatively consistent signal. The secret habits that control your life Wendy Wood Channel: The Well Open on YouTube Return at the next normal cue Suppose the intended habit is ten minutes of stretching after breakfast. Tuesday is missed because the morning goes badly. From a cue based perspective, the cleanest restart is not an elaborate recovery plan on Tuesday night. It is to stretch after breakfast on Wednesday , when the situation that has been associated with stretching naturally returns. That approach fits the original real world habit formation study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues. Ninety six participants selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repeated context consistent performance, while missing a single opportunity did not materially affect the formation process. More consistent performance was nevertheless associated with a better fitting habit development trajectory, so the finding supports tolerating an isolated miss rather than treating repeated inconsistency as irrelevant. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical restart rule can therefore be kept deliberately simple: Identify the cue you were already using. Think “after I make my morning coffee” or “when I finish lunch”, rather than merely “every day”. Wait for its next normal occurrence. An ordinary lapse does not create a need to manufacture an extra performance solely to repair the streak. Perform the intended behaviour when that cue occurs. This restores the same cue–response pairing instead of introducing an unnecessary new one. Repeat from there. The important pattern is renewed context consistent repetition, not retrospective correction of the missed occasion. A cue need not be an exact clock time. In a randomised trial of 192 adults building healthy eating habits, participants linked their chosen behaviour either to a daily routine, such as after breakfast, or to a particular time. Automaticity increased in both conditions, with no significant advantage for either cue type; frequent enactment of the behaviour in response to the chosen cue predicted greater automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 That result makes the restart principle more flexible than “always do it at 7.30 am”. A person whose mornings vary might have a more dependable cue in a sequence of actions: after brushing their teeth, after putting the kettle on, when arriving home or immediately after closing their work laptop. Researchers note that habit cues can include preceding actions as well as times and locations, and existing routines can provide particularly convenient stable contexts. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMC... The goal is therefore recognisable sameness, not perfect duplication . What needs to recur is enough of the meaningful context for the same behaviour to become predictably associated with it. Explanatory illustration 2 Why changing the cue after every miss can work against you A lapse can provoke unnecessary troubleshooting: perhaps the workout should move from morning to evening, the reading habit needs a different chair, or the reminder needs another time. Sometimes those changes are justified. But continually relocating a workable habit after ordinary misses can remove the very regularity that helps automaticity develop. Research on established habits illustrates why. In a classic study of students transferring universities, habitual exercising, newspaper reading"},{"title":"Streak vs Habit","url":"/streak-vs-habit/","description":"A streak can fall to zero after one miss even while the learned cue-response association remains substantially intact.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Missed Days","level":3,"text":"Streak vs Habit Your Streak Reset, but Did Your Habit? Missed Days Streak vs Habit Streak vs Habit Your Streak Reset, but Did Your Habit? A streak can fall to zero after one miss even while the learned cue response association remains substantially intact. A streak can fall to zero after one miss even while the learned cue response association remains substantially intact. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic mechanism What streaks measure and what habits measure Why a zero day streak can misrepresent progress Better ways to interpret consistency after a lapse self improvement that works missed days streak vs habit Introduction A streak can reset to zero after one missed day while the habit itself remains substantially intact. That is because the two measures describe different things. A streak records uninterrupted performance ; habit research is concerned with how strongly a recurring context comes to trigger a response automatically. One is a sequence in a log. The other is a learned behavioural association. Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters most immediately after a miss. In the classic 12 week habit formation study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, missing one opportunity did not materially disrupt the growth of automaticity . Yet newer research on streak tracking shows that displaying a streak as “broken” can reduce subsequent engagement even when people's underlying recent behaviour is held constant. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... So a zero day streak can be psychologically powerful without being a scientifically meaningful claim that habit progress has returned to zero. The useful question after a lapse is not simply “How many consecutive days have I done this?” but “How much evidence is there that this behaviour is becoming the normal response to its cue?” What streaks measure and what habits measure A consecutive day streak is deliberately simple. If you perform an activity every day for 40 days and miss day 41, the old streak is over. Your new streak is zero. Mathematically, that is correct: the tracker is measuring an uninterrupted run. Habit strength is different. In behavioural science, habits are generally understood as responses that become automatically activated by recurring contextual cues through repeated experience. Wendy Wood and Dennis Rünger’s major review describes habits as developing when people repeatedly perform the same responses in particular contexts; once established, these context response associations can efficiently guide behaviour without requiring the same degree of deliberate decision making each time. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Psychology of Habit Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsPsychology of Habit Annual ReviewsJanuary 4, 2016... Published: January 4, 2016 That gives streak length and habit strength very different properties after a lapse: After one missed dayStreak measureHabit progress interpretationConsecutive countResetsDoes not imply a resetPrevious repetitionsNo longer part of the current streakStill part of the learning historyCue response learningNot measuredCentral to what researchers mean by habitAutomaticityNot measured directlyMay remain substantially developedNext repetitionStarts a new streakCan continue reinforcing the existing association The difference is visible in Lally and colleagues’ influential longitudinal study. Ninety six participants selected a daily eating, drinking or activity behaviour and performed it in a recurring context, such as after breakfast. Automaticity generally increased rapidly at first and then more slowly towards a plateau. Crucially, missing one opportunity did not materially affect the habit formation process . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... UCL’s explanation of the research makes the qualification equally important: one missed opportunity was not significant, but people who were very inconsistent generally failed to form habits successfully. The researchers could not specify one universal level of consistency that everyone must achieve. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College LondonAugust 4, 2009... Published: August 4, 2009 That means “a miss does not reset your habit” should not be converted into “consistency does not matter”. Repetition matters enormously. What the evidence challenges is the much narrower assumption that consecutiveness itself is the underlying psychological quantity being accumulated . Streak Psychology: Why Breaking One Feels So Bad Channel: Design Trap Official Open on YouTube Why zero can misrepresent your progress The most revealing evidence comes from putting streak research beside habit research. Streak researchers Jackie Silverman and Alixandra Barasch found across seven studies that people were more likely to continue a behaviour when their behavioural log displayed an intact rather than a broken streak. Strikingly, this effect could occur independently of actual past behaviour : changing how the same behavioural history appeared in the log could change what participants did next. [DOI] doi.org On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 In one experiment involving exercise , participants had actually completed the same four consecutive strength exercises. Yet when the behavioural log represented those exercises as an intact streak, 66.23% chose another strength exercise, compared with 57.86% when the log represented the sequence as broken. The researchers designed the comparison specifically to separate the effect of the displayed streak from differences in participants' actual behavioural histories. [Oxford Academic] academic.oup.com Oxford AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 This exposes an important paradox. The streak display can influence future behaviour even though it is not itself a measure of the underlying habit. Why? Silverman and Barasch found evidence that maintaining the streak becomes a secondary goal. People do not necessarily see the number merely as a record of exercising, studying or practising a language; keeping the sequence alive can acquire value of its own. An intact streak can therefore increase accomplishment and motivation, while a broken one can make progress feel smaller than the behavioural record warrants. [Oxford Academic] academic.oup.com Open source on oup.com. Related research strengthens the point that people assign extra meaning to consecutive patterns. Across nine preregistered studies, Silverman, Barasch and Deborah Small found that observers predicted greater future goal adherence when recent successes formed a streak than when adherence rates were otherwise comparable. The streak was interpreted as evidence of greater commitment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Hot streak! Inferences and predictions about goal adherence ScienceDirect Hot streak! Inferences and predictions about goal adherence That inference can sometimes be useful: a streak really can reflect sustained effort. But it is not uniquely diagnostic of habit strength. Two people could both have completed 29 of the previous 30 planned repetitions. If one missed yesterday and the other missed two weeks ago, their displayed streaks might be 0 versus 13 despite almost identical overall adherence. The streak statistic magnifies the position of the miss; it does not directly tell us how automatic either person's behaviour has become. Explanatory illustration 2 The habit does not live in the counter Habit research gives a better explanation for why earlier repetitions do not simply disappear after one missed opportunity. Repeatedly performing a response in a particular situation strengthens the association between that context and the behaviour. A substantial research literature therefore treats cue contingent automaticity, rather than mere behavioural frequency, as central to habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Context stability is especially relevant. In research combining a six week study of university students with data from users of a habit building app, performing behaviours in more stable contexts predicted greater automaticity as well as better attainment of repetition goals. The two datasets covered thousands of habit repetitions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2024 systematic review and"},{"title":"Habit Floors","url":"/habit-floors/","description":"A habit floor is the smallest meaningful version you can still complete when time, energy, or attention are limited.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Small Habits","level":3,"text":"Habit Floors How Small Should Your Habit Floor Be? Small Habits Habit Floors Habit Floors How Small Should Your Habit Floor Be? A habit floor is the smallest meaningful version you can still complete when time, energy, or attention are limited. A habit floor is the smallest meaningful version you can still complete when time, energy, or attention are limited. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention What makes a minimum both easy and worthwhile Testing the floor against an ordinary difficult day Using a fuller version without raising the minimum self improvement that works small habits habit floors Introduction A habit floor is the smallest version of a behaviour that still counts as meaningful progress. It is not the ideal session and not a trick for claiming success after doing almost nothing. Its purpose is narrower: when time, energy or attention are unusually limited, the floor gives you a version you can still perform rather than abandoning the behaviour altogether. Explanatory illustration 1 Research does not establish a universal number of minutes, repetitions or pages that makes the perfect floor. What it does support is the logic behind one: habits develop through repeated behaviour in recurring contexts; simpler behaviours are easier to execute; complex behaviours continue to demand self regulation even when getting started has become habitual; and planning in advance for obstacles can improve follow through. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical rule is therefore make the floor easy enough to survive an ordinary difficult day, but substantial enough to remain connected to the result you actually want . Then treat anything above it as optional expansion, not as a new minimum. What makes a minimum both easy and worthwhile The most important distinction is between a floor and a symbolic gesture. “Open the book” is wonderfully easy, but if opening it and immediately closing it is repeatedly counted as a reading habit, the action contributes almost nothing to reading. “Read two pages” retains most of the convenience while producing an actual unit of reading. Likewise, putting on trainers can be a useful starting cue, but five or ten minutes of walking is a more defensible floor for a walking habit. This distinction follows from what habit research actually measures. In the influential study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, 96 participants chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition, although the rate differed greatly between people. Crucially, the behaviour being repeated was the target behaviour itself, not merely preparation for it. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Clinical guidance derived from this research similarly recommends choosing a simple action that moves towards the goal and can be repeated regularly. Gardner, Lally and Wardle give the example of increasing walking gradually rather than immediately demanding a much larger journey on foot. Their point is not that smaller is inherently better; it is that an achievable action provides opportunities for repetition that an overambitious prescription may lose. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit formation' and general practice PubMed... That suggests three tests for a useful habit floor: It produces a real unit of the desired behaviour. One paragraph written is writing; five minutes of practising scales is musical practice; clearing one defined surface is tidying. It removes avoidable complexity. The floor should require fewer steps, decisions and resources than the full version. It remains easy on the days for which it was designed. A “minimum” that regularly requires bargaining with yourself when tired is probably still an aspiration rather than a floor. The third point deserves emphasis. Research increasingly distinguishes habitual instigation — automatically getting started — from habitual execution , in which the steps of the activity themselves become automatic. In a 2022 study, habitual instigation predicted frequency across behaviours of different complexity, whereas habitual execution did not. More recent work found that complex behaviours continued to draw on deliberate self regulation strategies even when their initiation was strongly habitual. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Habit and behavioural complexity: habitual instigation and execution as predictors of simple and complex behaviours Scienc... That makes the floor particularly valuable for complicated goals. You may develop a reliable impulse to “work out after work”, but a 60 minute gym session still contains transport, equipment, exercise selection, multiple sets and recovery. A floor such as ten minutes of predetermined exercise strips away enough execution demands to give the starting habit somewhere realistic to go on a difficult day. MINI HABITS by Stephen Guise Core Message Channel: Productivity Game Open on YouTube Test the floor on an ordinary difficult day A habit floor should not be calibrated against your best day. Nor should it be designed around emergencies such as serious illness. The useful test is the bad but normal day : you slept poorly, work overran, the children need attention, concentration is patchy, or you have half the time you expected. Imagine the intended habit is 30 minutes of language study after dinner. On a normal evening, 30 minutes is comfortable. On a difficult evening, it repeatedly disappears. Possible floors might be: Possible floorBad day feasibilityMeaningful progressVerdictOpen the language appVery highVery lowProbably too smallComplete one short exerciseHighModest but realPlausible floorStudy for 15 minutesMediumStrongPossibly still too demandingComplete the normal 30 minutesLowStrongFull version, not a floor The correct row depends on the individual and the behaviour. There is no research backed rule saying that a floor must take two minutes, five minutes or one repetition. Treat such numbers as design suggestions rather than psychological thresholds. A better calibration method is empirical. Choose a candidate floor and observe what happens over several genuinely inconvenient opportunities. If you repeatedly postpone or skip it for predictable reasons, reduce the requirement or remove friction. If completing it feels virtually indistinguishable from doing nothing towards the goal, increase it slightly. Recent longitudinal evidence reinforces the importance of both feasibility and repetition. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis following 254 people over six months found habit strength was positively associated with behaviour frequency and context stability, while perceived difficulty was negatively associated with habit strength. Because this was observational longitudinal evidence rather than an experiment specifically testing “habit floors”, it should not be read as proving a particular minimum. It does, however, strengthen the case for designing repeated behaviours that are realistically executable rather than chronically difficult. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsWhat Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation Sebastian Bürgler, Marina Milyavskaya, Gail Mc... How to become 37.78 times better at anything Atomic Habits summary (by James Clear) Channel: Escaping Ordinary (B.C Marx) Open on YouTube Keep the cue when you shrink the action When possible, the fallback should occur in the same context as the normal behaviour. If the full habit is “read after getting into bed”, the difficult day version might be two pages rather than 30 — still after getting into bed. If the routine is “write after making morning coffee”, the floor might be one paragraph at the same cue rather than postponing the entire session until an uncertain gap appears later. That matters because habits are learned relationships between recurring contextual cues and responses. A 2022 study examined context stability across two longitudinal datasets: 95 students forming study habits over six weeks and 218 users of a habit building app. More stable contexts predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of the repetition specific goal; in both datasets, automaticity partially mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 A habit floor can therefore preserve two things simultaneously: some useful output and the cue to action repetition . On a"},{"title":"First Action","url":"/first-action/","description":"A tiny predefined first task can remove the extra decision that often comes between a study cue and actually beginning.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Study Systems","level":3,"text":"First Action Why the First Study Action Should Be Tiny Study Systems First Action First Action Why the First Study Action Should Be Tiny A tiny predefined first task can remove the extra decision that often comes between a study cue and actually beginning. A tiny predefined first task can remove the extra decision that often comes between a study cue and actually beginning. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism Why vague tasks create another decision at the worst moment Examples of first actions that can begin immediately How short opening blocks keep the routine easy to repeat self improvement that works study systems first action Introduction For a study before social media routine, the first study action should be so concrete that there is almost nothing left to decide. “Study chemistry” is still a planning problem. “Answer questions 1–5 from yesterday’s acid–base set” is an action. “Revise vocabulary” requires choosing material and a method; “test myself on the first ten prepared flashcards” does not. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because starting is itself a self regulation problem. Research on implementation intentions — plans linking a recognisable cue to a predetermined response — shows that specifying when and how an intended behaviour will happen can improve goal attainment, while newer educational research suggests that the quality and specificity of the plan matter. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... The practical implication is simple: prepare tomorrow’s first few minutes today. When the study cue arrives, the learner should be able to begin before opening social media and without first deciding what “studying” means. Why vague tasks create another decision at the worst moment A vague instruction such as “work on my essay” describes a goal but not the next physical or cognitive action. At the moment of starting, the learner must still choose which document to open, which section to tackle, whether to read or write, and how much work counts. Each unanswered question creates another opportunity to postpone the task or switch to something immediately available. That matters because procrastination is not explained simply by laziness or weak intentions. Piers Steel’s large meta analytic review, covering 691 correlations, identified task aversiveness, delay, self efficacy and impulsiveness among the stronger and more consistent predictors of procrastination. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The nature of procrastination: a meta analytic and theoretical review of quintessential self regulatory failure PubMed... A first action cannot remove every cause of delay, but it can remove one avoidable source of friction: having to design the study session at precisely the moment when the learner is supposed to start it. Implementation intention research provides a useful mechanism for doing this. Instead of retaining only an outcome such as “I want to revise before checking Instagram”, the learner connects a detectable situation with a response: “When I sit at my desk after breakfast, I answer the five questions on the card beside my laptop.” A 2006 meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect of implementation intentions on goal attainment and concluded that such plans can help with the particular problem of initiating goal directed behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... More recent evidence sharpens the point. A 2024 meta analysis covering 642 independent tests found beneficial effects across cognitive, affective and behavioural outcomes, but effects varied with how plans were constructed. Contingent if–then plans produced larger effects, as did rehearsing plans and having strong motivation for the underlying goal. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... In other words, “I should study tomorrow” and “After breakfast, I will answer these five questions” are not psychologically equivalent plans. A particularly relevant field test appeared in Contemporary Educational Psychology in 2025. In a 42 day study, 357 German fifth and sixth form pupils received prompts on randomly selected evenings to plan when and where they would use a vocabulary learning app the next day. Simply receiving the planning intervention did not produce a significant overall increase in studying. The important result was more specific: higher quality plans — those using cues that were specific, salient and appropriate — were associated with a greater likelihood of studying. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Planning promotes studying – The higher the plan quality the better: A micro randomized trial ScienceDirect... That is a useful warning against turning “make a plan” into another piece of generic self improvement advice. The plan has to leave the learner with something executable. Why you procrastinate even when it feels bad Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube First actions that can begin immediately The best opening action has three properties: its materials are already identifiable, its stopping point is obvious, and beginning it does not require another round of planning. The learner should be able to read the instruction and act. Useful examples include: Vocabulary: test yourself on the ten flashcards already marked for tomorrow. Mathematics: solve questions 1–3 on the prepared problem sheet. Chemistry: answer five retrieval questions from yesterday’s topic without looking at the notes. History: write from memory five facts about the topic on the card left on the desk, then check them. Languages: translate the first five prepared sentences, then correct them against the answers. Essay work: open the existing draft and write one paragraph answering the question already placed at the top. Reading: read the next three marked pages and write two retrieval questions about them. Exam revision: complete the first short question from a selected past paper, rather than beginning by browsing folders for a paper. These are intentionally more precise than “do some flashcards”, “work on maths” or “read for ten minutes”. A numerical boundary — five questions, three problems, one paragraph — makes completion visible. Preparing the exact material in advance removes the selection step as well. There is also a second reason to favour questions, problems and flashcards over merely opening a textbook: the first action can do useful learning immediately. Reviews of learning research consistently identify retrieval practice — attempting to recall information rather than merely rereading it — as an effective technique for improving later memory. A major review of ten common learning techniques rated practice testing and distributed practice as having particularly high utility, while a later review found retrieval benefits across different materials, ages and learner abilities. [DOI] doi.org Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques John Dunlosky, Katherine A. Rawson, Elizabeth J. Marsh, Mitchell J... A meta analysis comparing testing with restudy likewise found a reliable testing effect on retention. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effect of testing versus restudy on retention: a meta analytic review of the testing effect PubMedAugust 25, 2014... Published: August 25, 2014 This makes “answer five prepared questions” unusually suitable as a first action. It solves two problems at once: it provides an unambiguous behaviour to initiate, and the behaviour itself is educationally productive. That does not mean every session must begin with a quiz. For subjects involving problem solving, composition or extended projects, the first action should match the work. The governing test is more basic: could you start it immediately without deciding what to do first? If the answer is no, the action probably needs another level of specification. Explanatory illustration 2 Prepare the decision before the cue arrives A concrete first action works best when it is selected before the study period, not invented during it. This is the crucial difference between a routine and a morning negotiation. Suppose the intended sequence is breakfast → study → social media. The night before, the learner can place one mathematics sheet on the desk, mark questions 4–6 and put a pencil beside it. The next morning, the cue does not lead to “What shall I revise?” It leads to question 4. For digital work, the equivalent might be leaving the required document ready and recording the exact opening instruction somewhere that does not require visiting a distracting feed. Research on habit development supports making"},{"title":"Strong Habits","url":"/strong-habits/","description":"If-then plans can weaken some habitual responses, but deeply entrenched habits may require environmental changes or additional support.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / If Then Plans","level":3,"text":"Strong Habits Can If Then Plans Overcome a Strong Habit? Strong Habits Strong Habits Can If Then Plans Overcome a Strong Habit? If then plans can weaken some habitual responses, but deeply entrenched habits may require environmental changes or additional support. If then plans can weaken some habitual responses, but deeply entrenched habits may require environmental changes or additional support. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic population context How prepared responses can interrupt habitual behaviour Why stronger habits are harder to displace When to add environmental or behavioural support self improvement that works if then plans strong habits Introduction If then plans can interrupt an unwanted habit, but their power has limits. The key distinction is habit strength . A prepared response such as “If I reach for biscuits while watching television, then I will make tea instead” can give an alternative behaviour a better chance of winning when the familiar cue appears. Yet experiments suggest that this advantage shrinks as the competing habit becomes stronger. In a field study of smoking, implementation intentions reduced smoking among people with weak or moderate smoking habits, but not among those with strong habits. [DOI] doi.org Planning to break unwanted habits: Habit strength moderates implementation intention effects on behaviour change Webb 2009 Briti... Explanatory illustration 1 That matters for self improvement because repeated failure of an obstacle plan does not necessarily mean that the plan was pointless. It can mean that the old cue response relationship is sufficiently entrenched that a sentence written in advance is being asked to do too much. At that point, changing the environment, identifying the real trigger, strengthening the replacement behaviour or adding structured behavioural support may be more effective than simply rewriting the same if then plan. How prepared responses can interrupt a habit A habit and an implementation intention have an important feature in common: both can connect a situation with a response. A habit acquires that connection through repeated behaviour in recurring circumstances. An implementation intention deliberately creates a new connection — “when this situation occurs, perform this response” — before the situation arises. Reviews of habit research describe established habits as responses that recurring contextual cues can activate with relatively little dependence on a person's current intentions. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... This sets up something resembling competition between two prepared responses. Suppose arriving home has repeatedly led to sitting on the sofa and scrolling on a phone. A new obstacle plan might be: “If I put my keys down after work, then I will put my phone in the drawer and change into my running clothes.” The old context points towards one response; the deliberately prepared plan points towards another. Laboratory research supports the idea that such counter habitual plans can alter this competition. In three experiments, Marieke Adriaanse and colleagues found that implementation intentions removed the habitual response's normal advantage in mental accessibility when participants encountered the critical situation. Importantly, however, the alternative behaviour did not simply become automatically dominant. The researchers concluded that the plans appeared to create greater flexibility at the decision point, while actual departure from the habit still depended on having a sufficiently strong intention to perform the alternative behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Breaking habits with implementation intentions: a test of underlying processes PubMedApril 1, 2011... Published: April 1, 2011 That is a more useful model than thinking of an if then plan as an instant replacement habit. The plan can open a window in which another response becomes possible . What happens inside that window still depends on the strength of the old habit, the suitability of the alternative and the person's commitment to it. There is also experimental evidence that implementation intentions can improve suppression of previously learnt responses. A 2018 laboratory study using a task designed to detect habitual “slips of action” found better habit breaking with implementation intentions than with goal intentions alone, although effects varied with the type of outcome involved. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com An experimental investigation of breaking learnt habits with verbal implementation intentions ScienceDirectMarch 1, 2018... Published: March 1, 2018 Taken together, these studies support a qualified conclusion: planning can interfere with habitual responding, but it does not reliably erase the old response. How to Break Bad Habits Permanently (The 3 Step Scientific Method) English Podcast Channel: English Insight Podcast Open on YouTube Why stronger habits are harder to displace The clearest direct test comes from Thomas Webb, Paschal Sheeran and Aleksandra Luszczynska's study of habit strength. Their first experiment created relatively habitual responses in a laboratory task and then changed what participants needed to do. Forming implementation intentions reduced the harmful carry over of the old response, but the benefit was smaller among participants with stronger habits . [DOI] doi.org Planning to break unwanted habits: Habit strength moderates implementation intention effects on behaviour change Webb 2009 Briti... Their second experiment moved from a laboratory task to smoking. Here, implementation intentions reduced smoking for participants whose smoking habits were weak or moderate but did not significantly reduce it among participants classified as having strong smoking habits. The authors' central conclusion was therefore not that implementation intentions cannot break habits, but that habit strength moderates their effectiveness . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Planning to break unwanted habits: habit strength moderates implementation intention effects on behaviour change PubMed... That result fits the wider science of habits. Strong habits are particularly responsive to cues associated with previous performance. In experiments examining everyday habits, familiar contexts activated strongly habitual behaviours even when participants' current goals pointed elsewhere. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... A person may sincerely intend not to check their phone, snack, smoke or procrastinate, yet the familiar location, time, object or preceding action can still retrieve the practised response. The contrast between adding a desired behaviour and suppressing an established one is also visible in dietary research. A systematic review of 23 studies found implementation intentions more effective for increasing healthy eating, with an effect size of d = 0.51, than for reducing unhealthy eating, where the effect was d = 0.29. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A later meta regression covering 70 interventions and 9,689 participants likewise found a medium effect for promoting healthy eating ( d = 0.33) but only a small effect for reducing unhealthy eating ( d = 0.18). [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These results concern eating behaviour rather than habit strength alone, so they should not be treated as a direct replication of the smoking finding. They nevertheless illustrate a recurring practical difficulty: starting a useful response can be easier than stopping a well rehearsed competing response . Explanatory illustration 2 Replacement works better than telling yourself “not to” The wording of an obstacle plan matters particularly when it confronts a habit. A plan such as “If I am offered cake, then I will not eat cake” names the cue but leaves the person without a positive action to execute. Experiments on unhealthy eating found an additional problem. Adriaanse and colleagues tested plans with the form “if situation X occurs, then I will not perform habitual response Y”. These negation plans produced ironic cognitive and behavioural rebound effects compared with forming a goal intention or specifying a replacement response. In other words, explicitly linking the cue to not doing the habitual action could keep the unwanted response mentally active rather than successfully replacing it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For obstacle planning, the safer design principle is therefore to specify what happens instead : Weaker: If I feel like checking social media while working, then I will not check my phone. Stronger: If I reach for my phone while working, then I will put both hands back on the keyboard and finish the paragraph."},{"title":"Repetition Frequency","url":"/repetition-frequency/","description":"A routine done only a few times each week provides fewer cue-action repetitions, so automaticity may develop more slowly.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Complex Habits","level":3,"text":"Repetition Frequency Why Twice a Week Habits May Take Much Longer Complex Habits Repetition Frequency Repetition Frequency Why Twice a Week Habits May Take Much Longer A routine done only a few times each week provides fewer cue action repetitions, so automaticity may develop more slowly. A routine done only a few times each week provides fewer cue action repetitions, so automaticity may develop more slowly. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic mechanism Habit learning depends on repeated cue action pairings Calendar time versus number of repetitions What six month habit data suggest about frequency self improvement that works complex habits repetition frequency Introduction A routine performed only twice a week can take much longer to become automatic than one performed every day for a simple reason: habit learning happens through repetitions, not merely through the passage of calendar time . Each performance in a recurring context gives the brain another opportunity to strengthen the association between a cue and an action. A twice weekly routine supplies roughly eight such opportunities in a month; a daily routine supplies about 30. Research consistently links greater behavioural frequency with stronger automaticity , although frequency is not the only factor that matters. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beha... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is especially important for complex self improvement routines such as gym sessions, meal preparation or structured study. The familiar habit formation estimates measured in days mostly come from studies of behaviours attempted daily . They should not simply be transferred to routines performed two or three times a week. For low frequency behaviours, counting meaningful cue action repetitions is often more informative than counting days on the calendar. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Habit learning depends on repeated cue action pairings A habit is more than something a person does regularly. In contemporary habit research, the crucial feature is automaticity: encountering a familiar context begins to activate a learnt response with decreasing need for conscious deliberation. That relationship develops through repeated experience in which the relevant cue and behaviour occur together. Reviews of habit formation therefore describe the core process as repetition in a sufficiently consistent context. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlinePromoting habit formation: Health Psychology Review: Vol 7, No sup1 Get AccessOctober 11, 2011... Published: October 11, 2011 Experimental evidence supports the underlying learning mechanism. In two experiments involving a sequential sushi making task, researchers found that greater practice produced stronger cue response associations in memory. Those associations, in turn, predicted better performance when participants later carried out the task without instructions while under cognitive load. The researchers concluded that repetition helps recurring contextual cues automatically activate the responses practised in their presence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 This makes frequency important in a way that a calendar cannot capture. Suppose someone begins two new routines on 1 January. They take a vitamin after breakfast every day but go to the gym every Tuesday and Saturday. After six weeks, the vitamin behaviour has had as many as 42 opportunities to become associated with its cue; the gym routine has had about 12. Both routines are “six weeks old”, but their learning histories are radically different. The comparison is simplified: repetitions are not interchangeable units, and habit strength does not rise by a fixed amount every time. Context stability, reward, behavioural difficulty and individual differences all matter. Even so, a study covering 25 behaviours in 459 participants found that frequency and contextual stability were each independently positively associated with automaticity, while perceived complexity was negatively associated with it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beha... For complex low frequency routines, the disadvantage can therefore be compounded. There are fewer occasions on which to learn the routine, and each occasion may involve more decisions or sub actions than a simple behaviour does. PAN Podcast 6 Dr. Phillippa Lally Habits and Health Channel: PAN Physicians Association for Nutrition Open on YouTube Calendar time can hide the real amount of practice The best known real world habit study illustrates why “days to form a habit” can be misleading when applied to infrequent routines. Phillippa Lally and colleagues asked 96 participants to choose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and perform it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Among participants whose automaticity trajectories could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days. Automaticity followed a curve: gains were initially larger and then diminished as participants approached a plateau. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Those numbers are frequently treated as general purpose habit deadlines, but the study design matters. Participants had a daily opportunity for cue behaviour learning. A routine scheduled twice weekly cannot accumulate comparable numbers of repetitions at the same speed. The arithmetic shows the scale of the difference. At perfect adherence, 30 repetitions require about one month for a daily behaviour, around ten weeks for a three times weekly routine, and roughly fifteen weeks for a twice weekly routine. Sixty repetitions would require about two months, twenty weeks and thirty weeks respectively. These are illustrations of exposure counts, not scientifically established thresholds at which habits suddenly form . Research does not support a universal number of repetitions that guarantees automaticity. Indeed, researchers have specifically warned that habit formation studies often analyse change against elapsed time even though repetition is the proposed learning mechanism. A 2022 methodological review noted that the real world formation studies it examined analysed habit development over time rather than directly over repetitions. The authors also highlighted a case in which participants sometimes performed a behaviour more than once per day, demonstrating why days themselves can be an imperfect proxy for learning opportunities. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. This distinction also explains why an occasional missed performance is different from a routinely sparse schedule. In Lally's daily study, missing one opportunity did not materially derail the formation process; automaticity continued developing afterwards. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A twice weekly schedule, by contrast, is not a daily schedule with occasional lapses. Long gaps are built into it. Missing one Tuesday gym session could mean going a full week between two performances, sharply reducing the number of cue action pairings accumulated that month. Explanatory illustration 2 What six month habit data suggest about frequency Newer longitudinal evidence gives frequency a particularly important role. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis by Sebastian Bürgler and colleagues followed 254 people for six months, measuring behavioural automaticity monthly alongside ten possible determinants of habit development. The researchers identified trajectories of habit formation and habit degradation, and membership of those trajectories differed according to behavioural complexity, frequency and context stability. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation PubMed... In the multilevel analyses, behavioural frequency was positively associated with habit strength, as were context stability, autonomous motivation and positive feelings such as feeling pleased and energised; perceived difficulty was negatively associated with habit strength. Crucially for low frequency routines,"},{"title":"Preparation Barriers","url":"/preparation-barriers/","description":"Cooking and other multi-step habits can fail before the main action begins because ingredients, equipment and preparation all have to align.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Complex Habits","level":3,"text":"Preparation Barriers Why Complex Habits Often Fail Before They Start Preparation Barriers Preparation Barriers Why Complex Habits Often Fail Before They Start Cooking and other multi step habits can fail before the main action begins because ingredients, equipment and preparation all have to align. Cooking and other multi step habits can fail before the main action begins because ingredients, equipment and preparation all have to align. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context How extra steps multiply opportunities to stop Healthy cooking as a higher order habit Reducing preparation without oversimplifying the goal self improvement that works complex habits preparation barriers Introduction Healthy routines can fail before the behaviour a person actually cares about has begun. Cooking a nutritious dinner, for example, is not one action. Someone may first need to decide what to eat, check what is already at home, buy missing ingredients, find suitable equipment, allow enough time, prepare ingredients and only then cook. Exercise can have the same structure: clothes need washing, a bag may need packing, equipment must be available and travel may be required. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for complex habits . Preparation is not merely background administration; it is part of the behavioural chain. Research on physical activity explicitly describes preparatory action as a stage between planning and the target behaviour, while food research shows that time, facilities, cooking skills, ingredient availability and planning can all determine whether healthy eating is practical. [IAAP Journals] iaap journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com IAAP JournalsPreparing for Physical Activity: Pedometer Acquisition as a Self‐regulatory Strategy Koring 2013 Applied Psychology: H... The practical lesson is not that healthy routines should always be made tiny or effortless. It is that a routine with five prerequisites has five opportunities to become impossible before its main cue arrives. Effective self improvement therefore involves making the supporting actions reliable as well as repeatedly practising the headline behaviour. The universal hack: Why the friction principle applies everywhere Bob Sutton for Big Think+ Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube How extra steps multiply opportunities to stop Consider two evening habits: eating a piece of fruit and cooking a vegetable rich meal from basic ingredients. The first can be close to a single cue response relationship when fruit is already available: see or finish dinner, take fruit, eat it. The second is better understood as a small system. A home cooked dinner can depend on several conditions being satisfied at once: a meal has been chosen, its ingredients have been bought and stored, perishable ingredients are still usable, the necessary cookware is available, there is enough preparation and cooking time, and the person has the skills and energy to complete the recipe. The systematic review literature on home cooking accordingly finds that its determinants extend well beyond cooking ability itself. Time availability, employment and social and cultural circumstances all influence cooking behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Health and social determinants and outcomes of home cooking: A systematic review of observational studies ScienceDirectApr... This produces what might be called upstream failure . The intended behaviour at 7 pm may be “cook dinner”, but the decisive failure could have occurred at lunchtime, when no meal was chosen; yesterday, when shopping was postponed; or several days earlier, when the ingredients that would make a quick meal possible were never bought. By the time hunger is strong, the convenient alternative can require far fewer remaining steps. Time scarcity makes these chains particularly fragile. Longitudinal Australian research found that new episodes of time or income scarcity were associated with deterioration in health behaviours; time scarcity could contribute to lower fruit and vegetable consumption and more eating out, while combinations of income and time scarcity increased constraints further. The authors emphasised that nutritious eating consumes time through provisioning and preparation, not simply through eating itself. [DOI] doi.org Your money or your time? How both types of scarcity matter to physical activity and healthy eating ScienceDirectJanuary 1, 2017... Published: January 1, 2017 That is an important correction to the idea that preparation problems are simply failures of motivation. A person can strongly intend to eat well and still encounter a chain that cannot be completed with the resources available that evening. A 2024 review of healthy eating among disadvantaged adults in the UK identified lack of time, fatigue, cooking skills, food costs, access to shops and lack of facilities for storing, warming or cooking food among the barriers reported in the literature. Environmental context and resources were the most commonly represented theoretical domain across the studies reviewed. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Barriers and facilitators to healthy eating in disadvantaged adults living in the UK: a scoping review PMC... The UK Government's food strategy makes a similar point at policy level: dietary inequalities are not explained by food prices alone, because people also need equipment, skills and time to prepare healthier food rather than use convenient alternatives. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK ernment food strategy ernment food strategy GOV.UKJune 13, 2022... Published: June 13, 2022 Preparation barriers should therefore not automatically be interpreted as weak willpower. For someone working irregular shifts, caring for children or living without adequate kitchen facilities, removing one preparation step can have considerably more value than another reminder to “stay disciplined”. How to Build Habits That Actually Last: A Neuroscience Approach Channel: Dr. Tracey Marks Open on YouTube Healthy cooking is a higher order habit Cooking illustrates particularly well why some healthy behaviours resist becoming automatic. “Cook a healthy dinner” sounds like one habit, but in practice it contains several behaviours operating at different times. Planning is one of them. In a study of 40,554 adults from the French NutriNet Santé cohort, 57.4% reported planning meals at least occasionally. Meal planning was associated with better adherence to nutritional guidelines and greater food variety. Because the study was observational and cross sectional, it cannot establish that planning caused the healthier diets; people who are already more interested in healthy eating may simply be more inclined to plan. Even so, the association illustrates how an apparently peripheral preparation behaviour is connected with what eventually reaches the plate. [DOI] doi.org Meal planning is associated with food variety, diet quality and body weight status in a large sample of French adults International... Shopping is another. Deciding to cook vegetables does little at the critical moment if vegetables are not available. Research on preparatory planning has specifically treated actions such as ensuring that fruit is available as distinct from planning when and where to eat it. That distinction is useful beyond the particular experiment: action plans organise the target behaviour, while preparatory plans create the conditions under which that behaviour is possible . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of preparatory and action planning instructions on situation specific and general fruit and snack intake PubMedJanuary 1... Skills create another potential bottleneck. UK evidence identifies lack of food preparation skills as a barrier for some groups, including the difficulty of producing enough variety with the cooking skills people possess. But this evidence also cautions against treating cooking lessons as a complete solution: improved skills cannot compensate for unreliable access to affordable ingredients, time or adequate facilities. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Barriers and facilitators to healthy eating in disadvantaged adults living in the UK: a scoping review PMC... A population analysis of UK National Diet and Nutrition Survey data likewise notes the relationship between cooking skills, meal preparation and reliance on convenience foods, although these associations do not by themselves demonstrate causation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Finally, healthy cooking competes against alternatives designed to remove preparation. Convenience food can collapse planning, ingredient assembly, preparation and sometimes washing up into a much shorter sequence. Economic analyses consequently warn that the “cost” of a meal includes time as well as its supermarket price. One review notes that when researchers incorporated the value of"},{"title":"66 Day Myth","url":"/66-day-myth/","description":"Habit automaticity can take weeks or months, making 66 days a midpoint from one study rather than a universal deadline.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Evidence Limits","level":3,"text":"66 Day Myth Does It Really Take 66 Days to Form a Habit? Evidence Limits 66 Day Myth 66 Day Myth Does It Really Take 66 Days to Form a Habit? Habit automaticity can take weeks or months, making 66 days a midpoint from one study rather than a universal deadline. Habit automaticity can take weeks or months, making 66 days a midpoint from one study rather than a universal deadline. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case dataset evidence Where the 66 day number came from Why individual habit times vary so widely What newer reviews say about fixed deadlines self improvement that works evidence limits 66 day myth Introduction The “66 day rule” is best understood as a memorable summary of one influential study, not a countdown built into human psychology. Phillippa Lally and colleagues found that participants who successfully followed a model of increasing automaticity took a median of 66 days to reach 95% of their estimated maximum automaticity. But individual estimates ranged from 18 to 254 days , and the researchers never established 66 days as a universal deadline. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 Later research makes the case against a fixed deadline even stronger. A 2024 systematic review found reported median formation times of 59–66 days, but means of 106–154 days and individual estimates spanning 4 to 335 days . A large field study likewise found that different behaviours acquired predictable, habit like patterns on different timescales. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. So the useful lesson is not “repeat something for exactly 66 days”. It is that automaticity usually develops gradually through repetition, often over weeks or months, and its timing depends heavily on the behaviour, person and context. Where the 66 day number came from The number traces back to Lally and colleagues' study, first published online in 2009 and subsequently appearing in the European Journal of Social Psychology in 2010. The researchers recruited 96 volunteers and asked them to choose a new eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour — performed once each day in the same context, such as after breakfast — and follow it for 12 weeks. Participants recorded whether they performed the behaviour and completed daily measures of its automaticity. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That last detail matters. The researchers were not simply counting how many consecutive days somebody completed a task and declaring it a “habit”. They were interested in automaticity : the extent to which performing the behaviour came to require less conscious initiation. Habit researchers commonly distinguish this from mere behavioural frequency; habits are responses that become linked to recurring contexts through repetition. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Pub Med Psychology of Habit The researchers fitted a curved trajectory to each participant's automaticity scores. The proposed pattern was asymptotic: early repetitions should produce relatively large gains in automaticity, with each subsequent gain becoming smaller as the person approaches a plateau. In other words, habit development was modelled as a gradual learning curve, not as a switch that flips on a particular day. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This is where “66 days” needs careful qualification. Of the 96 recruited volunteers, 82 supplied sufficient data for analysis. The mathematical model could be fitted to 62, and only 39 were classified as showing a good fit. For people whose trajectories could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity varied from 18 to 254 days. The figure that became famous — 66 days — represented the middle of this highly dispersed distribution, not an experimentally demonstrated requirement. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Lally herself has since explicitly rejected the universal interpretation. In a 2026 University of Surrey interview, she said the important finding was the high variability in habit formation time rather than the idea that everyone needs 66 days. [University of Surrey] surrey.ac.uk Open source on surrey.ac.uk. That distinction changes the claim considerably: What the study supports: for certain daily eating, drinking and activity behaviours, automaticity often increased progressively over repeated performances, with 66 days being a useful description of the centre of one sample's distribution. What it does not support: every behaviour becomes a habit after 66 repetitions, everyone reaches automaticity on day 66, or failing to feel automatic by that date means habit formation has failed. Achieve Your Goals Through the Power of Habits Channel: USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences Open on YouTube Why real habit times vary so widely A fixed number of calendar days hides an important question: what actually happens during those days? Habit learning depends on repeated encounters between a context and a response. Two people can therefore spend the same 66 calendar days having very different learning experiences. Research on context stability illustrates the point. In one study of university students building study habits and another using data from a habit building app, greater stability in the circumstances surrounding repetition predicted stronger automaticity. The first study deliberately varied or stabilised participants' contexts; across both datasets, context stability was associated with greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Repetition itself is similarly important. In a 2021 randomised trial, 192 adults linked a chosen nutrition behaviour either to a daily routine or to a particular time. Both approaches increased automaticity, and repeated enactment of the planned behaviour was a key predictor of habit development. Among participants who successfully formed habits, the median modelled time to peak automaticity was 59 days — close to Lally's headline figure, but with individual estimates extending from 4 to 335 days in the later systematic review's synthesis. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. Even apparently small differences in a routine can be associated with very different timelines. In an experiment following 48 students performing a daily stretching exercise , participants assigned to stretch after waking were estimated to reach automaticity at about 106 days , compared with about 154 days for those assigned to stretch before bed. Those estimates were extrapolated from learning curves rather than direct observations of a precise “habit day”, which is another reason not to read them as exact deadlines. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit The broader point is more robust than any particular number: elapsed time is an imperfect proxy for habit learning . A 2025 cognitive neuroscience review emphasises that repetition and time need to be distinguished. Very simple laboratory behaviours can acquire habitual characteristics quickly when hundreds of repetitions are compressed into a short period, while complex real world behaviours may accumulate relevant repetitions much more slowly. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. This also explains why “doing something for two months” is not a sufficiently precise prescription. Drinking water after breakfast provides one clearly defined response to a frequently recurring cue. “Exercise regularly” can involve travel, clothes, equipment, scheduling, different movements and changing environments. Calendar duration alone cannot make those learning situations equivalent. The curve matters more than the deadline The original study contains another finding that tends to disappear when 66 days becomes a rule: habit formation was modelled as a curve with diminishing gains . The first repetitions in a stable context can produce comparatively large changes in automaticity. As the cue–behaviour relationship becomes better established, additional repetitions still contribute but yield progressively smaller increases, eventually approaching a plateau. [Wiley Online Library]"},{"title":"Choosing Cues","url":"/choosing-cues/","description":"A useful trigger is easy to notice and occurs at a moment when the planned action can realistically follow.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / If Then Plans","level":3,"text":"Choosing Cues Which Cues Make If Then Plans Easier to Follow? Choosing Cues Choosing Cues Which Cues Make If Then Plans Easier to Follow? A useful trigger is easy to notice and occurs at a moment when the planned action can realistically follow. A useful trigger is easy to notice and occurs at a moment when the planned action can realistically follow. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic mechanism Why distinctive cues are easier to notice Event cues versus vague internal states Testing whether a trigger works in real life self improvement that works if then plans choosing cues Introduction The best cue for an if then plan is not merely specific: it is recognisable when it happens and tied to a moment when the planned response can realistically begin . “If I have some free time” is weak because there is no clean point at which “free time” announces itself. “If I put my lunch plate in the sink, then I will walk for ten minutes” gives the mind a much clearer event to detect. Explanatory illustration 1 This matters because the cue is part of the mechanism of an implementation intention. Research indicates that forming an if then plan makes the specified situation more mentally accessible and strengthens its association with the intended response. Experiments have found that people can consequently recognise planned cues more quickly and accurately, while a recent meta analysis of 642 independent tests found benefits across a wide variety of cue types . [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryIdentifying good opportunities to act: Implementation intentions and cue discrimination Webb 2004 European Jour... The practical rule is therefore to choose a cue that passes three tests: Will it actually occur? Will I notice it? Can I act when it does? Why distinctive cues are easier to notice An if then plan effectively delegates part of action initiation to a future situation. That makes the “if” side more than a diary entry. In experimental work by Thomas Webb and Paschal Sheeran, the accessibility of the specified cue and the strength of the cue response association both helped explain better goal attainment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do implementation intentions promote goal attainment? A test of component processes ScienceDirect... Their earlier cue discrimination experiments are particularly relevant to choosing triggers. Participants who formed implementation intentions became better at detecting the specified cue. In subsequent experiments they responded to it more accurately or more rapidly, without becoming correspondingly worse at dealing with irrelevant or ambiguous stimuli. The researchers characterised the result as swift and precise discrimination of the critical cue. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryIdentifying good opportunities to act: Implementation intentions and cue discrimination Webb 2004 European Jour... For everyday planning, that argues for cues with a clear boundary . Compare: Weak: “If it is later this evening, I will practise.” Better: “If I finish washing the dinner dishes, I will practise.” Weak: “If I get a chance at work, I will send the difficult email.” Better: “If the Tuesday team meeting ends, I will return to my desk and send the email.” Weak: “If I am not too busy in the morning, I will stretch.” Better: “If I switch off the shower, I will do my two minute stretch.” The stronger examples do not require a second judgement about whether “later”, “a chance” or “not too busy” has arrived. A concrete transition supplies the signal. A cue also needs to occur at an opportune moment . The US National Cancer Institute's research summary describes the critical situation in an implementation intention as one in which the response is feasible, or as an anticipated obstacle that needs to be handled. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Cancer ControlImplementation Intentions Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences (DCCPS)... A highly noticeable cue is therefore still poorly chosen if it routinely fires when the behaviour is impossible. “If my 9 am alarm sounds, I will go for a run” is of little use if 9 am normally finds you on a train. There is an important qualification to the advice to make cues specific. Specificity should help identify the opportunity, not turn the plan into an elaborate specification. A physical activity study found a generic single situation intervention could perform as well as offering ten more specific situations, while a broader meta analysis found that adding extra detail about how or for how long an action would be performed did not improve the effect associated with specifying time and place. In other words, clarity is useful; maximal detail is not the objective . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Does Situation Specificity Affect the Operation of Implementation Intentions? ScienceDirect... What Is Implementation Intention For Building Habits? The Time Management Pro Channel: The Time Management Pro Open on YouTube Event cues versus vague internal states Events in an existing routine are often attractive triggers because the environment supplies the reminder. Finishing breakfast, shutting a laptop, entering the kitchen or hearing the end of a meeting all have observable boundaries. Research on prospective memory — remembering to carry out an intended future action — similarly distinguishes event based cues from time based intentions that may require monitoring the clock. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: A systematic and meta analytic review ScienceDirectMarch 30... This does not mean that every if then plan should use an external event. The large 642 test review by Paschal Sheeran, Olivia Listrom and Peter Gollwitzer found successful plans using many kinds of situations: time and place, task transitions, particular stimuli, thoughts, feelings and bodily sensations. Time plus place produced a larger estimated effect than time alone in the studies available for that comparison, and task junctures such as starting or ending a phase of work also performed well. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... Internal cues can be especially appropriate when the desired action is itself a response to an internal obstacle. For example: If I notice that I am distracted while reading, then I will put the phone out of reach and return to the paragraph. Here, distraction is not simply an inconveniently vague mood. Recognising distraction is precisely the situation in which the corrective action is needed. The recent meta analysis found effective plans using cues including recognising distraction, feeling tempted or anxious, and experiencing bodily sensations such as hunger or discomfort. It found no simple overall pattern showing that external cues were categorically superior to internal ones. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. The useful distinction, then, is not external good, internal bad . It is discriminable versus ambiguous . “If I feel bad, I will use my coping strategy” leaves considerable interpretation to the future self. What counts as bad? Mild frustration? Fatigue? Anxiety? A specific internal event can be much more actionable: “If I notice myself rereading the same sentence because my attention has wandered, then I will close the distracting tab.” The same principle applies to clock time. A time can be perfectly definite — “7 pm” — yet still be a poor real world trigger if you do not naturally check the time around 7 pm. An event already embedded in a routine can sometimes do more of the retrieval work for you. Conversely, a clock cue is useful when the action genuinely needs to happen at that time and the time will be visible or externally signalled. Prospective memory research provides a useful caution against turning this into an absolute rule. Detailed implementation intentions have improved self initiated remembering when obvious environmental prompts were absent, and experiments have also found benefits for difficult to detect event cues. The technique can therefore compensate to some extent for an imperfect cue; choosing a naturally conspicuous trigger simply reduces the amount of work the plan has to do. [psychologicalscience.org] psychologicalscience.org Open source on psychologicalscience.org. Explanatory illustration 2 Match the cue to the first executable action A cue is only useful if the “then” response fits the situation it creates. This is an easy point to miss because people often choose triggers by asking When should I ideally do this? A better question is At what reliably occurring moment can I actually start? Suppose the goal is to study after work. “If I leave work, then I will study for 45 minutes” contains a clear cue, but the action cannot literally follow while"},{"title":"Plan Specificity","url":"/plan-specificity/","description":"Plans work best when they are specific enough to guide action without becoming overloaded with unnecessary detail.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / If Then Plans","level":3,"text":"Plan Specificity How Specific Should an If Then Plan Be? If Then Plans Plan Specificity Plan Specificity How Specific Should an If Then Plan Be? Plans work best when they are specific enough to guide action without becoming overloaded with unnecessary detail. Plans work best when they are specific enough to guide action without becoming overloaded with unnecessary detail. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence What useful specificity looks like Why vague plans leave decisions unresolved Why maximum detail is not always better self improvement that works if then plans plan specificity Introduction An if then plan should be specific enough to remove the decision you would otherwise have to make in the moment, but not detailed merely for the sake of detail . A useful plan identifies a recognisable cue and an executable response: “If I finish lunch, then I will walk for ten minutes” gives the mind much more to work with than “If I have time, I will exercise ”. Explanatory illustration 1 Research broadly supports that distinction. Studies of physical activity , smoking cessation and studying have found that better specified plans tend to predict better follow through. Yet the evidence does not establish a universal rule that every extra detail improves a plan. In one smoking cessation study, medium and highly specific plans performed similarly, while experimental work has found that a generic situation can sometimes work as well as a collection of narrowly specified ones. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov From theory to practice: An explorative study into the instrumentality and specificity of implementation intentions PubMed... The practical target is therefore useful specificity : enough precision that you can recognise when to act and know what to do, without loading the plan with conditions that do not help either job. What useful specificity looks like The National Cancer Institute's summary of implementation intention research captures the central problem with vagueness. A plan such as “tomorrow I will exercise more” fails to identify an unambiguous opportunity or a particular action; some of the deciding still has to happen later. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Cancer ControlImplementation Intentions Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences (DCCPS)... Consider three versions of the same intention: Too vague: “If I get a chance tomorrow, then I will study.” Useful specificity: “If I sit down on the bus home, then I will review my vocabulary.” Possibly unnecessary detail: “If I sit in the left hand window seat on the 4.12 bus home and have at least 18 minutes before my stop, then I will review exactly 25 vocabulary cards.” The middle version specifies a concrete transition and a concrete response. The first leaves unresolved what counts as a “chance”. The third adds conditions that may make the plan apply less often without obviously making the desired action easier to recognise or perform. This distinction matters because an if then plan is supposed to establish a link between a cue and a response. Research on implementation intentions proposes that specifying the cue makes it more mentally accessible and that linking it to the response strengthens the cue response association. In other words, precision has a functional purpose: it should make the relevant moment easier to detect and the next action less ambiguous. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Cancer ControlImplementation Intentions Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences (DCCPS)... A 2011 study provides a particularly useful real world illustration. Emely de Vet and colleagues analysed plans made by 342 adults seeking to increase their physical activity. Participants could specify the activity, day, time of day , place and duration. They produced an average of 2.18 plans, yet more than 30% failed to make even one plan judged specific. Better specified plans, rather than simply a greater number of plans, were associated with greater physical activity two weeks later. The researchers particularly highlighted specifying when to act . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com More or better: Do the number and specificity of implementation intentions matter in increasing physical activity? Science... The lesson is not that every plan must contain five pieces of information. It is that details should close genuine decision gaps. “If I exercise tomorrow, I will run” still leaves when unresolved. “If I finish work, I will run” may already settle the relevant question. Adding the exact pavement, playlist and colour of running top probably does not. How to Achieve Any Goal with Science The WOOP technique Channel: Brain's Book Club Open on YouTube Why vague plans leave decisions unresolved Vagueness weakens an if then plan in two places. The if part can fail to tell you when the rule applies, while the then part can fail to tell you what to do. “If I have some free time, then I will work on my project” illustrates both problems. How much free time counts? Does ten minutes qualify? And what does “work on” mean: research, write, organise files or answer email? Encountering free time can therefore reopen the same choice the plan was meant to settle. That is important because the distinctive value of an implementation intention lies in contingency: a specified situation is linked to a specified response. A 2024 meta analysis covering 642 independent tests found positive effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with larger effects when plans actually used a contingent if then format. The review also classified a wide range of workable cues, including times and places, task transitions and other situations, rather than implying that one particular type of microscopic detail is required. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... Recent field evidence makes the distinction even clearer. In a 42 day study published in 2025, 357 fifth and sixth year pupils were prompted on randomly selected evenings to create if then plans for using a vocabulary learning app the following day. Simply receiving a planning prompt was not enough: effectiveness depended strongly on the quality of the plan the pupil actually produced. Higher quality cues were specific, noticeable and appropriate for studying. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Planning promotes studying – The higher the plan quality the better: A micro randomized trial ScienceDirectDecember 1, 2025... Published: December 1, 2025 The researchers' examples show why “specific” should not be confused with “long”. A cue tied to finishing a familiar activity, such as brushing one's teeth in the morning, could provide a clear moment for action. By contrast, “when I am at home” describes a potentially lengthy period rather than a particular transition, while “when I have time” supplies no definite situation at all. The study also treated contextual suitability as part of plan quality: an otherwise identifiable cue is not especially useful if it occurs when the intended action is impractical. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Planning promotes studying – The higher the plan quality the better: A micro randomized trial ScienceDirectDecember 1, 2025... Published: December 1, 2025 This suggests a simple test for specificity: when the if part becomes true, should you still have to decide whether “now” really counts? And after it becomes true, should you still have to decide what action to begin? If either answer is yes, the plan probably needs clarification. Explanatory illustration 2 Why maximum detail is not always better It is tempting to turn “specific plans work better” into “the most detailed possible plan works best”. The research does not justify that simple escalation. A study of 764 participants in a smoking cessation contest is especially relevant. Participants created three plans for dealing with difficult situations, and researchers independently classified their specificity as non specific, medium or high. Specificity predicted point prevalence smoking abstinence seven months later: medium to high specificity was associated with better outcomes than low specificity. Crucially, however, abstinence did not differ between the medium and highly specific groups. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov From theory to practice: An explorative study into the instrumentality and specificity of implementation intentions PubMed... The authors themselves raised the possibility of over specification. A cue described through too many conditions may occur rarely or may be harder to match to a real situation that differs slightly from the imagined one. Similarly, an action burdened with numerous prerequisites may become impossible when one component is unavailable. They therefore framed the issue as finding an effective level of specificity rather than assuming an unlimited “more is"},{"title":"Automatic Action","url":"/automatic-action/","description":"Linking a clear cue to a response can make the cue easier to notice and the intended action quicker to initiate.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / If Then Plans","level":3,"text":"Automatic Action Why Can If Then Plans Start Action So Quickly? Automatic Action Automatic Action Why Can If Then Plans Start Action So Quickly? Linking a clear cue to a response can make the cue easier to notice and the intended action quicker to initiate. Linking a clear cue to a response can make the cue easier to notice and the intended action quicker to initiate. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Making the critical cue more mentally accessible Strengthening the cue response link What strategic automaticity does and does not mean self improvement that works if then plans automatic action Introduction An if then plan can make action feel unusually quick because it changes what has to happen at the critical moment. Instead of noticing a situation, remembering a goal, deciding whether to act and choosing what to do, you have already linked one recognisable cue to one response: “If X happens, then I will do Y.” Psychologists call this an implementation intention. Explanatory illustration 1 Research points to two closely connected mechanisms. First, specifying the “if” makes the critical cue more mentally accessible and easier to detect. Second, explicitly pairing that cue with the “then” response strengthens their mental association, so encountering the cue can bring the intended action to mind rapidly. Experiments find characteristics associated with automatic action initiation: faster responding, relative efficiency under competing mental demands and, in some settings, less need for a fresh conscious decision at the moment of action. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com How do implementation intentions promote goal attainment? A test of component processes ScienceDirect... The important phrase is strategic automaticity . The plan does not remove choice. It uses a deliberate choice made earlier to reduce the amount of choice required later. Making the critical cue easier to notice Ordinary intentions can fail before a decision about action is even reached. You might genuinely intend to do something but fail to recognise the particular moment when doing it would make sense. An implementation intention tackles this recognition problem by giving one future situation special psychological importance. Suppose your aim is to practise a language regularly. “I will practise tonight” leaves you responsible for remembering that intention amid everything else happening that evening. “If I put my dinner plate in the dishwasher, then I will open my language exercise ” singles out a particular event. The dishwasher is no longer merely part of clearing up; it has become the signal for the next action . Experimental evidence supports this cue accessibility account. Webb and Sheeran found that implementation intentions increased the accessibility of the situation specified in the plan, and that this increase helped explain subsequent improvements in goal directed performance. In other words, the cue did not merely appear in the wording of the plan: its mental representation became more ready for activation. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do implementation intentions promote goal attainment? A test of component processes ScienceDirect... Earlier experiments by Webb and Sheeran examined whether this heightened sensitivity might have an obvious downside — perhaps people would become so primed for the planned cue that they would mistake irrelevant or ambiguous stimuli for it. Their studies instead found more accurate or faster discrimination of the specified cues without evidence of impaired responses to irrelevant or ambiguous stimuli. That fits a more useful interpretation of cue accessibility: the plan can help the right situation stand out rather than simply making the person indiscriminately trigger happy. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineIdentifying good opportunities to act: implementation intentions and cue discrimination White Rose Research O... This is one reason the wording of an if then plan matters. A vague cue such as “if I have some spare time” provides the mind with a poorly defined event to detect. A concrete transition — “when I close my laptop after my last meeting” — gives the plan a much more identifiable trigger. The mechanism depends not simply on having made a plan, but on having specified something that can actually be recognised when it occurs. How to Actually Stick to Your Goals: The Psychology of Implementation Intentions Channel: Mindset Psychology Open on YouTube Strengthening the cue response link Noticing the opportunity is only half of the mechanism. After recognising a cue, a person could still reopen the question of what to do: Should I start now? What should I start with? Perhaps later would be better. The “then” half of an implementation intention pre selects the response. Webb and Sheeran experimentally tested both sides of this mechanism and found that improved performance was mediated not only by greater cue accessibility but also by a stronger association between the specified cue and response. Their conclusion was that implementation intentions partly delegate behavioural control to the chosen situational cue. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do implementation intentions promote goal attainment? A test of component processes ScienceDirect... Later prospective memory research provides a useful illustration. In an experiment comparing implementation intentions with rehearsal and other forms of preparation, holding the relevant cue and response constant, the if then planning condition produced a stronger cue response link and better performance. This suggests that the benefit cannot always be reduced to simply thinking about the intended behaviour more often; explicitly encoding the contingency between this situation and this response can matter. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineImplementation intentions forge a strong cue–response link and boost prospective memory performance: Journal of Co... The practical difference is subtle but important. Consider: Intention: “When I get home, I should go for a run.” If then plan: “If I put my keys on the hall table, then I will put on my running shoes.” Both refer to essentially the same goal. But the second statement builds a direct bridge between a perceptible event and a specific first action . When the keys hit the table, there is less behavioural territory left to negotiate. That helps explain why implementation intentions can affect the speed of initiation, not merely whether an action eventually happens. Classic experiments found that people with implementation intentions initiated planned responses more immediately when the relevant situations occurred. The US National Cancer Institute's behavioural research summary describes this as helping people both “see” and “seize” opportunities to act: cue processing improves, while the planned response becomes more readily triggered. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Cancer ControlImplementation Intentions Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences (DCCPS)... Explanatory illustration 2 Why the response can feel automatic Psychologists use automaticity more carefully than everyday speech sometimes does. It does not necessarily mean a completely involuntary reflex. Processes can show particular features of automaticity, such as occurring quickly, requiring relatively few cognitive resources or proceeding without a new conscious intention immediately beforehand. Implementation intentions have shown several of these features. Brandstätter, Lengfelder and Gollwitzer tested action initiation in four studies, including experiments in which university students had to cope with experimentally imposed cognitive load. The benefits of implementation intentions persisted despite competing mental demands. The researchers interpreted this as evidence that the planned action could be initiated both immediately and efficiently when its cue appeared. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation PubMed... Other experiments pushed the idea further. Bayer and colleagues presented critical cues outside participants' conscious awareness and found evidence of stronger action preparation or faster initiation among participants who had previously formed the corresponding implementation intention. Such findings support the proposition that, once the association has been deliberately established, its later activation need not always involve consciously rehearsing the complete plan. [Guilford Journals] guilfordjournals.com Open source on guilfordjournals.com. Research on prospective memory — remembering to carry out an intended action later — points in a similar direction, although with important qualifications. One study found that implementation intentions improved prospective memory performance under"},{"title":"Weak Motivation","url":"/weak-motivation/","description":"If-then planning mainly helps people execute goals they already value; it does little when the underlying intention is weak.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / If Then Plans","level":3,"text":"Weak Motivation When an If Then Plan Will Not Be Enough If Then Plans Weak Motivation Weak Motivation When an If Then Plan Will Not Be Enough If then planning mainly helps people execute goals they already value; it does little when the underlying intention is weak. If then planning mainly helps people execute goals they already value; it does little when the underlying intention is weak. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Why implementation intentions do not create commitment Evidence from strong versus weak goal intentions Recognising when motivation is the real bottleneck self improvement that works if then plans weak motivation Introduction An if then plan can make it easier to act at the right moment, but it cannot reliably make an unwanted goal worth pursuing. That distinction is central to using implementation intentions well. The technique is designed to solve an execution problem : “I genuinely intend to do this, but I fail to start, forget, hesitate or get distracted.” It is much less suited to a motivation problem : “I keep saying I should do this, but I am not strongly committed to doing it.” Explanatory illustration 1 Research directly supports this boundary. Experiments have found that implementation intentions improve goal attainment when the underlying goal intention is strong or active, but can provide little or no benefit when it is weak. A large recent meta analysis likewise found larger effects among highly motivated participants. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The interplay between goal intentions and implementation intentions The interplay between goal intentions and implementation intentions PubMedJanuary 1, 2005... Published: January 1, 2005 For self improvement, this means that repeatedly refining an if then plan can be wasted effort when the real bottleneck comes earlier. Before asking When exactly will I act? , it can be more useful to establish whether I actually intend to act at all . Why planning does not create commitment Implementation intentions and goal intentions perform different jobs. A goal intention represents the outcome or behaviour a person means to pursue. An implementation intention connects a recognisable cue with an action that serves that goal: “If situation X occurs, then I will do Y.” The second is therefore downstream of the first. It organises the execution of a decision rather than necessarily creating the decision itself. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... That distinction can be seen in research on the mechanism. Thomas Webb and Paschal Sheeran conducted a meta analysis of 66 tests examining whether forming implementation intentions changed people's goal intentions or self efficacy — their confidence that they could perform the behaviour. The effects on both were negligible. Their accompanying experimental work instead indicated that implementation intentions helped by making the planned cue more accessible and strengthening the association between that cue and the intended response. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubMechanisms of implementation intention effects: The role of goal intentions, self‐efficacy, and accessibility of plan compon... In practical terms, suppose someone writes: “If it is 7 pm on Monday, I will go for a 30 minute run.” If that person genuinely wants to exercise but habitually gets absorbed in something else at 7 pm, specifying the cue can address the problem. But if they have little intention of running and wrote the plan merely because they feel they ought to exercise, greater sensitivity to 7 pm does not supply the missing commitment. The plan has made the opportunity clearer without necessarily making the action more desirable. This is why describing implementation intentions simply as a way to “increase motivation” is misleading. They can improve behaviour, sometimes substantially: the influential 2006 review of 94 independent tests reported an overall medium to large effect on goal attainment. But its theoretical starting point was precisely the problem of people who already hold goal intentions and nevertheless fail to translate them into action. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... How to Automate Your Focus: The Science of If Then Rules Channel: The Research File Open on YouTube Strong and weak intentions produce different results The most direct demonstration comes from work by Paschal Sheeran, Thomas Webb and Peter Gollwitzer. Their 2005 research deliberately tested whether implementation intentions operate independently of the motivational goal above them. They found that they did not. In the first study, the researchers measured the strength of participants' goal intentions and examined what happened when people did or did not make implementation intentions. The planning intervention improved attainment among people with strong goal intentions , but not among those whose intentions were weak. In a second experiment, implementation intentions influenced performance when the relevant underlying goal had been activated, but not when it had not. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The interplay between goal intentions and implementation intentions The interplay between goal intentions and implementation intentions PubMedJanuary 1, 2005... Published: January 1, 2005 The paper also reported a useful additional analysis using earlier data on attendance at workplace health and safety training. Implementation intentions were not associated with attendance among participants with low goal intentions; the relationship became strong and statistically significant among those with strong intentions. This gave the researchers another setting in which the same basic interaction appeared. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Interplay Between Goal Intentions and Implementation Intentions Paschal Sheeran, Thomas L. Webb, Peter M. Gollwitzer... More recent synthesis strengthens the case that motivation is a meaningful boundary condition rather than an incidental result from one old experiment. A review and meta analysis published online in 2024 analysed 642 independent tests of implementation intentions. The technique showed benefits across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, but effects were larger when participants were highly motivated to pursue the relevant goal. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... There is also evidence outside tightly controlled laboratory tasks. Research on entrepreneurial action found that stronger goal intentions were associated with implementation intentions and subsequent action, while the relationship between implementation intentions and action was stronger at higher levels of goal intention strength. [Springer] link.springer.com Implementation intentions in the entrepreneurial process: concept, empirical findings, and research agenda Small Business Econo... The sensible interpretation is not that an exact universal motivation threshold has been discovered. It has not. Nor does the evidence justify saying that planning never helps someone with less than perfect enthusiasm. Rather, motivation and implementation planning interact: the less genuine commitment there is for a plan to serve, the less reason there is to expect a powerful execution aid to solve the problem . Explanatory illustration 2 When motivation is the real bottleneck This boundary matters because weak motivation can masquerade as poor self control . Someone may repeatedly conclude, “I need a better system,” when their behaviour is actually revealing uncertainty about the goal itself. A useful diagnostic is to separate two experiences. An execution problem sounds like: “I definitely want to do this, and I am annoyed that I keep forgetting or failing to start.” The intended behaviour is valued; the difficulty appears when an opportunity for action arrives. That is a good fit for an if then plan. A motivation problem sounds more like: “I suppose I should do this,” “someone else wants me to do it,” or “I keep making plans, but when the time comes I would rather do almost anything else.” Here, repeatedly adding more precise cues may merely produce increasingly detailed plans for an action the person has not really chosen. Several signs therefore suggest that motivation deserves attention before another implementation plan: the goal is driven mainly by obligation or outside pressure rather than a personally accepted reason; enthusiasm for making plans is consistently greater than willingness to perform the planned action; there is persistent ambivalence about whether the outcome is worth the cost; the plan is repeatedly abandoned even when"},{"title":"Locked Savings","url":"/locked-savings/","description":"Commitment savings can raise balances by making money harder to withdraw before short-term spending competes with a longer-term goal.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Precommitment","level":3,"text":"Locked Savings Does Making Savings Harder to Reach Work? Precommitment Locked Savings Locked Savings Does Making Savings Harder to Reach Work? Commitment savings can raise balances by making money harder to withdraw before short term spending competes with a longer term goal. Commitment savings can raise balances by making money harder to withdraw before short term spending competes with a longer term goal. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case policy intervention How restricted access changes the spending decision What the Philippines commitment account trial found When mild separation may be better than a hard lock self improvement that works precommitment locked savings Introduction Commitment savings can work because they change when the spending decision is made. Instead of leaving saved money continuously available and relying on restraint whenever an impulse purchase appears, the saver chooses in advance to make some money difficult or impossible to withdraw until a date or target is reached. The strongest field evidence comes from a randomised trial in the Philippines: clients offered such an account accumulated substantially higher bank savings after a year, even though the account paid the same interest rate as ordinary savings. [isps.yale.edu] isps.yale.edu January 20, 2006... Published: January 20, 2006 Explanatory illustration 1 That is good evidence that restricted access can help some people save. It is not evidence that everyone should lock up their money. Hard commitments sacrifice liquidity, uptake can be limited, and other studies show that people facing financial shocks or existing debt can respond in ways that offset the apparent benefit. The practical question is therefore not simply whether making savings harder to reach works, but when the protection from impulsive withdrawal is worth the loss of flexibility . [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The limits of commitment: who benefits from illiquid savings products 13, 2021... How restricted access changes the spending decision An ordinary savings account leaves two decisions open. First, you decide whether to put money into savings. Later, whenever another use for the money appears, you decide whether to take it back out. The second decision can quietly undo the first. A commitment account removes or weakens that second decision. Once money crosses the boundary, the saver cannot simply reverse course because a purchase suddenly feels attractive. This is a particularly literal form of precommitment : rather than hoping to prefer the long term goal tomorrow, the saver deliberately gives tomorrow's self fewer options. The Philippine experiment makes the mechanism unusually clear. Researchers Nava Ashraf, Dean Karlan and Wesley Yin worked with the Green Bank of Caraga in Mindanao to create the SEED account. Clients could choose one of two withdrawal restrictions. They could specify a future date , such as a time when school fees or Christmas expenses were expected, or specify a target balance , such as the amount needed for a home improvement. Once chosen, the restriction could not simply be changed and the funds normally remained inaccessible until the target was met. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence From a Commitment Savings Product in the PhilippinesJuly 1, 2005... Published: July 1, 2005 That design matters because it separates commitment from financial reward. SEED paid the same 4 per cent annual interest as the bank's ordinary savings account. The special feature was therefore not a higher return for tolerating inconvenience; it was the inability to casually undo the saving decision. [J PAL] povertyactionlab.org Open source on povertyactionlab.org. Among the 202 people who opened SEED accounts, 140 chose a date based target and 62 an amount based target. The researchers regarded the amount target as potentially stronger: if access requires reaching a particular balance, stopping deposits early also postpones access to the money already saved. A date lock, by contrast, eventually expires whether or not the saver continues contributing. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence From a Commitment Savings Product in the PhilippinesJuly 1, 2005... Published: July 1, 2005 The product also linked the restriction to concrete purposes. Forty eight per cent of account holders named celebrations such as Christmas, birthdays or fiestas as their goal; 21 per cent named education, while another 20 per cent named business or home investment. That illustrates an important feature of commitment saving: the lock was not merely making money inconvenient to obtain. It was protecting money already assigned to a future use from competing demands in the meantime. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence From a Commitment Savings Product in the PhilippinesJuly 1, 2005... Published: July 1, 2005 Can Commitment Savings Help Americans Be Better Prepared for Retirement? Channel: John Wall Open on YouTube What the Philippines trial found The experiment began with 1,777 existing or former bank clients. Of 710 clients randomly offered the commitment product, 202 — 28.4 per cent — opened an account. After twelve months, the average savings balance of clients assigned to the commitment treatment group was 81 percentage points higher than that of the control group . Because assignment to the offer was random, the comparison is considerably more informative than simply observing that people who voluntarily choose locked accounts tend to save more. [isps.yale.edu] isps.yale.edu January 20, 2006... Published: January 20, 2006 The distinction between being offered the account and opening one is crucial. Only about 28 per cent of those offered SEED accepted it, yet the headline effect compares the whole treatment group with the control group. The experiment therefore provides evidence that making a commitment account available and actively offering it caused higher savings , rather than merely showing that unusually motivated savers accumulated money after choosing one. [isps.yale.edu] isps.yale.edu January 20, 2006... Published: January 20, 2006 There was also evidence that the people attracted to commitment were not random. In baseline questions designed to detect changing preferences between immediate and delayed rewards, women showing the pattern the researchers associated with time inconsistency were significantly more likely to take up SEED. This is consistent with the intended mechanism: at least some customers appeared to recognise value in restricting their own future access. It should not, however, be read as proof that present bias explains every account opened. Restricted personal control over money within a household was another plausible benefit of an individually controlled account. [isps.yale.edu] isps.yale.edu January 20, 2006... Published: January 20, 2006 The result is especially striking because the intervention did not require people to make continuing deposits. SEED restricted withdrawals but did not contractually force subsequent saving. In that sense, the intervention protected accumulated money rather than replacing the act of saving itself. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence From a Commitment Savings Product in the PhilippinesJuly 1, 2005... Published: July 1, 2005 Later research also found effects beyond the account balance. Among married women who initially had relatively little household decision making power, being offered the individually controlled commitment account affected measures of financial decision making and shifted spending towards some female oriented durable goods. Those findings underline that making savings inaccessible to other immediate claims can matter for reasons besides one's own impulse spending. They also caution against interpreting every effect of SEED as pure self control . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Female Empowerment: Impact of a Commitment Savings Product in the Philippines ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 2 A harder lock is not automatically a better lock If restricting withdrawals protects savings, it is tempting to conclude that stronger restrictions should always work better. The wider experimental literature gives a more complicated answer. In one experiment examining different withdrawal rules, participants allocated money between liquid savings and commitment accounts. When interest rates were equal, the account permitting no early withdrawal attracted more money than versions offering either emergency access or withdrawal with a 10 per cent penalty. The strict account received 53.7 per cent of participants' allocated funds, compared with roughly 45 per cent for the less restrictive alternatives. When both a no withdrawal account and a 10 per cent penalty account were simultaneously available, participants still put"},{"title":"Digital Blocks","url":"/digital-blocks/","description":"Scheduled access blocks can protect planned work by making distracting content unavailable during the hours when temptation usually wins.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Precommitment","level":3,"text":"Digital Blocks When Blocking Distractions Beats Resisting Them Precommitment Digital Blocks Digital Blocks When Blocking Distractions Beats Resisting Them Scheduled access blocks can protect planned work by making distracting content unavailable during the hours when temptation usually wins. Scheduled access blocks can protect planned work by making distracting content unavailable during the hours when temptation usually wins. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention How advance blocking changes the future choice set What the 2023 You Tube experiment found about commitment Choosing the smallest block that still protects focused work self improvement that works precommitment digital blocks Introduction Blocking digital temptations works by changing the choice you will face during focused work. Instead of opening a document and repeatedly deciding not to check social media, news or video, you decide beforehand that those options will be unavailable for a defined period. That distinction matters: the intervention is not a reminder to exercise self control but a form of digital precommitment that reduces the opportunities to abandon the task. Explanatory illustration 1 The evidence is promising but more qualified than the marketing around productivity blockers often suggests. Workplace research has found longer periods of focus when distracting sites were blocked, while other experiments show that restrictions can fail, become burdensome or remove content people genuinely need. A particularly revealing 2023 experiment using tempting YouTube videos found both overcommitment and undercommitment — but, after adjustment, undercommitment was the larger problem. [DOI] doi.org 3123024.3124558 DOIHow blocking distractions affects workplace focus and productivity Proceedings of the 2017 ACM International Joint Conference on Per... how to eliminate distractions on your computer (full guide) Channel: Reysu Open on YouTube How advance blocking changes the future choice set The important implementation detail is when the decision is made . Suppose someone intends to write from 09:00 to 10:30 but routinely drifts into social media at about 09:20. Simply deciding to “stay off social media” leaves the tempting option available at exactly the moment when the earlier plan is vulnerable. Scheduling a block at 08:55 changes the later situation: at 09:20 there is no ordinary choice between continuing the paragraph and opening the feed. This is why scheduled blocking is meaningfully different from noticing distraction and activating a blocker after it has already begun. Automatic scheduling moves the intervention upstream. Current blocking software can implement recurring periods in which selected sites or apps become unavailable without requiring the user to start a focus session at the vulnerable moment. [Focus Bear] support.focusbear.io Focus Bear Blocking Schedule: Automatically Block Apps and Websites by Time Focus Bear Blocking Schedule: Automatically Block Apps and Websites by Time The behavioural principle does not depend on a particular product: the useful feature is that access changes before the temptation driven decision would otherwise occur. There are at least two reasons this can help. First, digital interruptions can directly compete for attention. In an experimental study, receiving mobile phone notifications disrupted performance on an attention demanding task even when participants did not interact with the phone. Another experiment in which people could switch between a cognitive task and their own smartphones found naturally arriving notifications were the strongest predictor of switches from work to phone use. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification PubMedJune 29, 2015... Published: June 29, 2015 Blocking or silencing a known source of interruption can therefore prevent some attention shifts rather than requiring recovery afterwards. Second, blocking removes repeated opportunities for voluntary diversion. A small workplace field study illustrates the distinction. Thirty two information workers completed five baseline working days followed by five days with non work online distractions blocked. During the blocking period, participants reported significantly higher productivity and were able to focus for significantly longer; those most susceptible to social media distraction showed the greatest gains in focus. [DOI] doi.org 3123024.3124558 DOIHow blocking distractions affects workplace focus and productivity Proceedings of the 2017 ACM International Joint Conference on Per... That study was exploratory rather than a large randomised trial, so it should not be read as proof that blockers reliably increase objective output. It also uncovered a useful warning: some participants worked for longer stretches without taking physical breaks, with higher stress. Removing digital diversion can eliminate an unhelpful interruption without automatically creating a healthy working rhythm. [DOI] doi.org Effects of Individual Differences in Blocking Workplace Distractions Proceedings of the 2018 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Comp... A good block therefore protects work from predictable temptations; it need not prohibit breaks. Block EVERY distraction with 5 Apps! Channel: Tool Finder Open on YouTube What the 2023 YouTube experiment found about commitment Claes Ek and Margaret Samahita's 2023 experiment provides an unusually direct test of digital precommitment because participants faced an actual tempting alternative while doing tedious work. During a 15 minute transcription task, YouTube videos appeared in pop ups. Participants could state how much they were willing to pay for a commitment device that could remove the videos for the whole task. The design kept the pop ups themselves present even when videos were removed, helping the researchers separate the temptation of the videos from the mechanical interruption of closing a window. [UCD Research Repository] researchrepository.ucd.ie Research Repository UCD Research Repository... The temptation was not merely hypothetical. Among exposed participants, some clicked through to YouTube or watched videos in the pop ups; five spent more than half of the 15 minute task period on YouTube. Yet many resisted successfully. [UCD Research Repository] researchrepository.ucd.ie Research Repository UCD Research Repository... This variation allowed the researchers to ask a more interesting question than whether distracting video can hurt work: did people correctly judge how much protection they needed? Not always. After the researchers adjusted for learning between the two work tasks, 19.3% of exposed participants were classified as overestimating the commitment justified by their realised material losses, while 47.8% underestimated it. The estimated aggregate welfare loss associated with underestimation was also substantially larger than that associated with overestimation under the study's assumptions. [UCD Research Repository] researchrepository.ucd.ie Research Repository UCD Research Repository... Using a measure that also incorporated expectations about the psychological difficulty of resisting temptation produced a more conservative but similar direction: 17.5% were classified as overcommitters and 24.3% as undercommitters after the learning adjustment. [UCD Research Repository] researchrepository.ucd.ie Research Repository UCD Research Repository... The practical lesson is not that stronger blocking is always better. Some people really did seek more commitment than their subsequent behaviour appeared to justify. Rather, the experiment challenges the comforting assumption that people reliably know they will cope when distraction arrives. In this setting, insufficient commitment was at least as important a problem as excessive commitment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Too much commitment? An online experiment with tempting YouTube content ScienceDirect... There is also an important limit to what this experiment establishes. It involved a short, artificial transcription task and deliberately presented tempting YouTube content; everyday focused work can last hours and may require occasional access to the same platforms that also distract. The experiment is therefore stronger evidence for the mechanism of advance restriction than for any particular all day blocking regimen. Explanatory illustration 2 Blocking is not automatically better than a lighter intervention Other evidence makes that qualification important. In a randomised experiment involving students in a Stanford massive open online course, Richard Patterson tested several behavioural tools. A commitment device that let students precommit to how much distracting time they would allow themselves increased time spent working on the course by 24%, raised grades by 0.29 standard deviations"},{"title":"Money at Stake","url":"/money-at-stake/","description":"Putting personal money at risk can strengthen follow-through when softer promises have repeatedly failed, but demanding contracts may attract few users.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Precommitment","level":3,"text":"Money at Stake When Is Losing Money a Useful Commitment? Precommitment Money at Stake Money at Stake When Is Losing Money a Useful Commitment? Putting personal money at risk can strengthen follow through when softer promises have repeatedly failed, but demanding contracts may attract few users. Putting personal money at risk can strengthen follow through when softer promises have repeatedly failed, but demanding contracts may attract few users. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention How financial stakes change the cost of backing out What smoking and gym trials found When hard contracts become too costly or inflexible self improvement that works precommitment money at stake Introduction Putting your own money at risk can make a repeated behaviour failure expensive enough to change what happens when motivation weakens. A financial commitment contract typically lets you choose a target in advance — such as not smoking or visiting the gym — and requires you to forfeit some of your own money if you fail. Unlike a reward paid by somebody else, the incentive comes from avoiding a loss you deliberately created. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest case for this approach is narrow. Money at stake can improve follow through for some people who already want to change and have repeatedly failed with softer promises. Smoking and exercise trials show that such contracts can produce meaningful behavioural changes. But the same studies expose the central problem: many people decline hard commitments, while some who accept them subsequently lose their money. A stronger incentive is therefore not automatically a better self improvement tool. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic AssociationPut Your Money Where Your Butt Is: A Commitment Contract for Smoking Cessation American Economic Association... How financial stakes change the cost of backing out A conventional intention leaves the immediate economics of a lapse largely unchanged. Suppose someone decides on Sunday that they will exercise on Tuesday evening. When Tuesday arrives, skipping the gym offers an immediate benefit — more free time and less effort — while much of exercise's benefit remains distant. Repeating the intention does not alter that trade off. A money at stake contract does. If the person has already pledged £30 that will be lost unless an independently measurable target is met, skipping now carries an additional immediate cost. The contract does not need to make exercise more enjoyable or the long term health benefit more vivid. Its distinctive function is to bring a consequence of failure forwards in time. Economists therefore distinguish hard commitments , which impose genuine economic consequences, from softer devices such as promises or reminders. Hard commitments are particularly interesting because the person voluntarily creates a restriction that they may later wish did not exist. Reviews of commitment devices emphasise that effectiveness and demand are separate questions: a device can exert a strong influence once adopted while still being unattractive to many of the people who might theoretically benefit from it. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Commitment Devices Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsCommitment Devices Annual ReviewsSeptember 4, 2010... Published: September 4, 2010 That distinction matters for self improvement. The relevant comparison is not simply “financial penalty versus doing nothing”. A useful contract must clear at least three hurdles: the target must represent something the person genuinely wants, failure must be measured fairly, and the stake must be large enough to matter without making an ordinary setback needlessly damaging. Prof. Dean Karlan: Innovations for Poverty Action and Commitment Channel: Yale University Open on YouTube What smoking and gym trials found One of the clearest early field tests came from smokers in the Philippines. Xavier Giné, Dean Karlan and Jonathan Zinman tested a voluntary product called CARES. Smokers who accepted it deposited their own money into an account over six months. At the end they took a urine test for nicotine and cotinine: passing meant getting the deposited money back; failing meant forfeiting it to charity. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic AssociationPut Your Money Where Your Butt Is: A Commitment Contract for Smoking Cessation American Economic Association... The result illustrates both the promise and the limitation of hard commitment. Only 11% of smokers offered CARES accepted it . Yet random assignment to being offered the contract increased the probability of passing the six month test by about 3 percentage points relative to the control group. The researchers also found an effect in surprise testing at 12 months, after the original contractual period had finished, suggesting that the intervention did more than simply shift smoking until the day of the test. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic AssociationPut Your Money Where Your Butt Is: A Commitment Contract for Smoking Cessation American Economic Association... The participants who used CARES did not merely make a token wager. Study material reports an average opening balance of 57 Philippine pesos, followed by further contributions from 80% of clients; by six months, the average balance had risen to 553 pesos. In other words, users progressively accumulated something worth protecting. [TIME] time.com Put Your Money Where Your Butt Is Put Your Money Where Your Butt Is:December 4, 2025... Published: December 4, 2025 The low uptake is nevertheless crucial. The experiment does not establish that imposing monetary penalties on all smokers would work, nor that everyone offered the contract benefited. It shows that making a voluntary hard commitment available changed cessation outcomes across the group offered it, despite relatively few people choosing to participate. The selection problem is part of the intervention rather than an incidental detail. There is now strong evidence that financial incentives broadly construed can help smoking cessation, but this should not be mistaken for equally strong evidence specifically for self funded contracts. A 2025 Cochrane review found high certainty evidence that financial incentive programmes increased long term quitting: across 39 studies and 18,303 participants, about 10 in 100 people receiving incentives quit for six months or longer, compared with about 7 in 100 controls. However, those studies encompassed cash and vouchers as well as self deposits. The review found no clear evidence that programmes using redeemable self deposits were more effective than other incentive designs. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Can rewards help people quit smoking, and do they work in the long term? CochraneJanuary 13, 2025... Published: January 13, 2025 Carrots and Sticks by Ian Ayres: 10 Minute Summary Channel: SnapTale Audiobook Summaries Open on YouTube Exercise shows the same tension A workplace exercise experiment by Heather Royer, Mark Stehr and Justin Sydnor provides another instructive case. Employees first encountered a four week financial incentive for using their company gym; some were subsequently offered the opportunity to create self funded commitment contracts. The initial incentive produced strong short term responses but only modest persistence by itself. The group offered commitment contracts subsequently showed longer lasting changes in gym attendance. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic AssociationIncentives, Commitments, and Habit Formation in Exercise: Evidence from a Field Experiment with Workers at a... Again, however, demand for the hard device was limited. Among 346 people given the relevant commitment option, 12.4% took it up , putting about $58 on average at stake. Demand was considerably higher among people who were already gym members: 23% accepted. Among people who had not previously been members, overall uptake was only 6%, although it reached 21% among those who had visited at least once during the preceding incentive period. [IZA] iza.org Incentives, Commitments and Habit Formation in Exercise Incentives, Commitments and Habit Formation in Exercise:January 22, 2025... Published: January 22, 2025 That pattern suggests an important practical role for money at stake contracts: they may work better as an escalation after somebody has already begun changing a behaviour than as the first intervention offered to an entirely disengaged person. A financial contract cannot make somebody value a goal they do not really want. Its comparative advantage is making an already valued intention harder to abandon. More recent evidence reinforces the point that genuine stakes can add something beyond merely writing down a promise. A 2024 randomised experiment involving 1,629 gym members in Stockholm compared non incentivised “soft” contracts with “hard”"},{"title":"Real Deadlines","url":"/real-deadlines/","description":"Deadlines are more credible commitment devices when they create an external consequence, while recent replication evidence weakens claims about arbitrary","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Precommitment","level":3,"text":"Real Deadlines Do Self Imposed Deadlines Really Stop Procrastination? Precommitment Real Deadlines Real Deadlines Do Self Imposed Deadlines Really Stop Procrastination? Deadlines are more credible commitment devices when they create an external consequence, while recent replication evidence weakens claims about arbitrary Deadlines are more credible commitment devices when they create an external consequence, while recent replication evidence weakens claims about arbitrary Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Why deadlines can function as precommitment What changed after the 2026 replication Turning vague intentions into real delivery points self improvement that works precommitment real deadlines Introduction A deadline can be a precommitment , but simply putting a date in your diary is not necessarily one. The distinction is whether missing the date changes anything. A deadline tied to a client, submission system, appointment, financial loss or other consequence changes the future choice; an arbitrary private target can often be moved the moment it becomes inconvenient. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because a famous 2002 study is often treated as evidence that self imposed dates beat procrastination. In fact, its self imposed deadlines were binding and costly to miss . More importantly, a preregistered replication published in Psychological Science in July 2026 failed to reproduce the study's central result: different deadline arrangements had negligible effects on the measured outcomes. [upenn.edu] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Ariely Wertenbroch 2002 Wharton Faculty Platform1333... The useful lesson is therefore narrower than “set yourself deadlines”. A credible delivery point can still remove room for repeated postponement, but current evidence gives much less reason to believe that inventing intermediate dates — even neatly spaced ones — is a reliable anti procrastination technique by itself. Parkinson's Law Manage Your Time More Effectively Channel: Better Than Yesterday Open on YouTube Why deadlines can function as precommitment A genuine deadline changes what happens after a particular date. Before the deadline arrives, waiting remains possible; afterwards, waiting carries a cost. That cost need not be money. A missed university submission may lose marks, a late client deliverable may damage a relationship, and failing to send something before a scheduled meeting may mean arriving without the promised work. The common feature is that the consequence cannot simply be wished away when motivation falls. This is what made the classic experiments by Dan Ariely and Klaus Wertenbroch more interesting than ordinary goal setting. In their first field study, 99 professionals taking an MIT executive education course had three papers to complete. One section received evenly spaced deadlines. The other could choose its own dates — but had to declare them near the beginning of the course, could not subsequently change them, and lost 1 per cent of a paper's grade for every day it was late. Students nevertheless selected dates substantially earlier than the last possible submission date. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Ariely Wertenbroch 2002 Wharton Faculty Platform1333... That behaviour was evidence of demand for commitment : people were voluntarily surrendering flexibility and accepting a potential penalty. It was not evidence about writing “finish report: Friday” in a private calendar and retaining complete freedom to change Friday to Monday. The original paper's second experiment made the same point still more clearly. Sixty participants proofread three roughly ten page texts over three weeks. They earned money for finding errors but lost $1 for each day a task was submitted after its deadline. Participants were randomly assigned to an end deadline, externally imposed weekly deadlines, or deadlines they chose themselves. Again, the self chosen dates were not imaginary milestones: once selected, lateness cost money. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Ariely Wertenbroch 2002 Wharton Faculty Platform1333... In the 2002 results, performance followed an appealing pattern. Participants with externally imposed, evenly spaced deadlines found the most errors, incurred the least delay and earned the most; self imposed deadlines came next; leaving everything until the final deadline performed worst. The authors concluded that people recognised their tendency to procrastinate and could partially control it by precommitting, but did not choose their deadlines optimally. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceProcrastination, Deadlines, and Performance: Self Control by Precommitment Psychological Science... For years, that finding supplied an unusually tidy prescription: divide a long project into smaller pieces and give each one an advance deadline. The evidence accumulated since then makes that prescription considerably less tidy. The Secret to Kicking Procrastination: Reward Yourself Dan Ariely Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube What changed after the 2026 replication The most direct challenge arrived in 2026. Kyle Hyndman and Alberto Bisin replicated the proofreading experiment underlying Study 2 of Ariely and Wertenbroch. Their design again compared three conditions: no intermediate deadlines, externally imposed evenly spaced deadlines, and self imposed deadlines. The preregistration specified the original study's central outcome measures — errors detected, days late and overall payment — and planned 40 participants per treatment, with random assignment at the individual level. [ASPREDICTED] aspredicted.org q6vf 824t aspredicted.org... The result did not reproduce the original deadline advantage. Hyndman and Bisin reported that changing the deadline regime had a negligible effect on the three performance measures and on several survey measures. In particular, externally imposed, evenly spaced deadlines no longer stood out as an effective way of reducing procrastination. The replication was published online by Psychological Science on 15 July 2026. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceReplication of “Procrastination, Deadlines, and Performance: Self Control by Precommitment” Psycho... That is an important correction because it targets the experiment most directly associated with the idea that carefully spaced deadlines improve performance. It does not prove that deadlines never work, nor does one failed replication erase every observation in the original research. But the specific causal claim is now much less secure. The sensible reading in 2026 is that evenly spaced deadlines should not be treated as a generally established solution to procrastination . Nor did this doubt appear from nowhere. Hyndman and Bisin had already conducted a separate field experiment, published in 2020, in which students showed substantial demand for self imposed deadlines but those deadlines did not increase completion rates. Depending on the treatment, sizeable minorities or majorities voluntarily chose deadlines — yet wanting commitment and benefiting from it were clearly different things. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Present bias, procrastination and deadlines in a field experiment Present bias, procrastination and deadlines in a field experiment ScienceDirectJanuary 1, 2020... Published: January 1, 2020 That study also exposed a potential downside. Deadlines can encourage people to begin close to the cut off, leaving little room to recover when a task proves harder than expected. The authors identified three competing effects: a deadline may create useful commitment, but it also removes the option of waiting for a better moment and can magnify mistakes caused by overconfidence or failure to persevere after an unsuccessful attempt. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Present bias, procrastination and deadlines in a field experiment Present bias, procrastination and deadlines in a field experiment ScienceDirectJanuary 1, 2020... Published: January 1, 2020 Other field evidence is similarly mixed. Burger, Charness and Lynham found in a 2011 experiment that evenly spaced interim deadlines actually reduced completion of a lengthy task. [UCSB Economics Faculty] faculty.econ.ucsb.edu UCSB Economics FacultyJournal of Economic Behavior & Organization 77 (2011) 393–404November 8, 2025... Published: November 8, 2025 A much larger study involving vocational colleges in Israel introduced deadlines for students' final projects, with missing the date producing delay and uncertainty over rescheduling. It found no effect on either project defence or graduation rates. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Do Deadlines Affect Project Completion? Experimental Evidence from Israeli Vocational Colleges"},{"title":"Right Sized Commitment","url":"/right-sized-commitment/","description":"People can restrict themselves too much or too little, so effective precommitment depends on estimating temptation without sacrificing useful flexibility.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Precommitment","level":3,"text":"Right Sized Commitment Can You Precommit Too Much or Too Little? Precommitment Right Sized Commitment Right Sized Commitment Can You Precommit Too Much or Too Little? People can restrict themselves too much or too little, so effective precommitment depends on estimating temptation without sacrificing useful flexibility. People can restrict themselves too much or too little, so effective precommitment depends on estimating temptation without sacrificing useful flexibility. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why people misjudge the future cost of temptation What undercommitment and overcommitment look like How to choose the smallest reliable restriction self improvement that works precommitment right sized commitment Introduction Precommitment can fail in two opposite ways. Undercommitment leaves enough room for a predictable temptation to win; overcommitment removes more freedom than the problem actually requires, making legitimate changes of plan unnecessarily costly. The practical target is therefore not maximum restriction. It is the smallest restriction that reliably changes what happens at the vulnerable moment . Explanatory illustration 1 This is difficult because choosing a commitment requires a forecast about your future self. You must estimate how strong the temptation will be, whether the restriction will actually stop the lapse, and how valuable flexibility might become if circumstances change. Research shows that people make errors in both directions. In an experiment built around distracting YouTube videos, participants sometimes paid for commitment they did not need, but undercommitment was more common and produced larger aggregate losses. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Too much commitment? An online experiment with tempting YouTube content ScienceDirect... The lesson is not simply to “be stricter”. It is to calibrate the constraint to the failure you can actually predict. Why we misjudge the future cost of temptation A commitment decision contains an awkward forecasting problem. From a calm vantage point, you have to estimate what will happen later when an immediately attractive alternative is present. People who underestimate that future pull can choose a restriction too weak to work. People who overestimate it can buy protection against a failure that would never have occurred. Claes Ek and Margaret Samahita tested this unusually directly. Participants completed a tedious online task while entertaining YouTube videos offered an opportunity to procrastinate. They could pay for a commitment device that probabilistically removed the videos. Because the researchers could compare willingness to pay for protection with participants' realised losses when exposed to temptation, they could identify errors on both sides. A substantial group appeared to overcommit , being too pessimistic about how they would perform with temptation available. Yet more participants undercommitted , and the total losses associated with undercommitment were larger. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Too much commitment? An online experiment with tempting YouTube content ScienceDirect... That result matters because ordinary advice often assumes only one forecasting error: future me will be weaker than present me expects . That is important, but incomplete. Some people apparently make the opposite mistake and treat anticipated temptation as more dangerous than it turns out to be. Effective self improvement therefore requires calibration rather than a blanket preference for harder constraints. A related issue is awareness of one's own self control problem . Research distinguishing people who recognise their future self control difficulties from those who do not finds that this awareness predicts commitment behaviour. People who are relatively sophisticated about their limitations are more likely to use commitment devices than people who fail to anticipate their own inconsistency. [IZA] iza.org Sophistication about Self Control IZA@LISER Network Sophistication about Self Control IZA@LISER Network... But recognising that a problem exists is not the same as knowing exactly how much commitment it requires. Classic research on self imposed deadlines illustrates the distinction. Dan Ariely and Klaus Wertenbroch found that people were willing to impose costly deadlines on themselves and that those deadlines improved performance, suggesting genuine awareness of procrastination. Yet participants did not choose deadlines optimally: externally imposed, evenly spaced deadlines produced better performance in the relevant experiment. People could recognise the need for a constraint without accurately designing it. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsProcrastination, Deadlines, and Performance: Self Control by Precommitment Dan Ariely, Klaus Wertenbroch, 2002... Tying Your Own Hands: How Commitment Devices Outsmart Future You Channel: THE RATIONAL WALLET Open on YouTube What too little and too much commitment look like Undercommitment is easy to mistake for commitment because some obstacle has technically been created. The relevant question, however, is whether that obstacle is large enough to change behaviour when temptation arrives. A revealing field example comes from Anett John's research on commitment savings in the Philippines. Participants could choose a savings plan and their own penalty for falling behind. The product increased savings on average, but 55% of clients defaulted and incurred monetary losses. The evidence was consistent with many participants choosing penalties too small to overcome their self control problem: 80% selected the minimum permitted termination fee. [informs.org] pubsonline.informs.org When Commitment Fails: Evidence from a Field Experiment Management ScienceJune 12, 2019... Published: June 12, 2019 This is an important form of undercommitment disguised as prudence . A small penalty feels safer when chosen in advance, but if it is too small to affect the later decision, the person receives the disadvantages of commitment without its central benefit. In John's study, many people were not merely failing to save; they were failing under a contract that could also impose a loss. The weakest commitment was not necessarily the safest commitment. Everyday versions are less dramatic but structurally similar: A website blocker that can be dismissed instantly may add a click without stopping a predictable two hour distraction. Putting spending money in a separate account may accomplish little if transferring it back takes seconds. Setting a self imposed deadline with no consequence for moving it may simply create another date to renegotiate. Keeping a tempting option “just in case” can preserve exactly the choice that repeatedly defeats the original plan. Overcommitment has the opposite structure. The restriction works against temptation, but it also blocks choices that the person would reasonably want to make. A rigid evening internet shutdown may prevent distraction, for example, while also preventing an unexpected piece of urgent work. Locking too much cash away may control discretionary spending but create difficulties when an unforeseen expense appears. The general economic problem is that flexibility has value under uncertainty. David Laibson's analysis of the relatively limited real world demand for commitment emphasises that commitment itself has costs: variation in future opportunity costs, direct costs of restricting oneself and imperfect awareness of future behaviour can make a rigid constraint unattractive even for someone with present biased preferences. In his calibrated model, the perceived benefits of commitment are often outweighed by its costs. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic Association Why Don't Present Biased Agents Make Commitments? American Economic AssociationWhy Don't Present Biased Agents Make Commitments? American Economic Association... So declining a hard commitment is not necessarily evidence of weak motivation . Sometimes keeping an escape route is rational because the future contains genuine information that the present cannot know. Explanatory illustration 2 The real trade off is temptation versus useful flexibility This produces a more useful way to think about commitment strength. Imagine a spectrum running from unrestricted choice to complete lock in. Moving towards lock in provides more protection against predictable lapses, but it can also increase the cost of responding to legitimate surprises. The best position depends on the predictability of the reason you will want to change course . If the future urge to abandon the plan is highly predictable and repeatedly turns out to be something you later regret, flexibility has relatively little value at that point. If future circumstances genuinely vary in ways that matter, flexibility becomes more valuable. Consider two superficially similar plans to stop using distracting"},{"title":"Notifications","url":"/notifications/","description":"Notifications can disrupt learning even when students do not accurately notice how much their performance has suffered.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Study Systems","level":3,"text":"Notifications Can Notifications Hurt Learning Without You Noticing? Study Systems Notifications Notifications Can Notifications Hurt Learning Without You Noticing? Notifications can disrupt learning even when students do not accurately notice how much their performance has suffered. Notifications can disrupt learning even when students do not accurately notice how much their performance has suffered. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism What notification experiments found about learning performance Why subjective focus can miss objective disruption Practical ways to silence interruptions during the first study block self improvement that works study systems notifications Introduction Push notifications can hurt learning before a student ever opens the app that sent them. Experimental research shows that the sound, vibration or appearance of an incoming alert can redirect attention, slow performance and interfere with memory. More strikingly, students may not accurately notice the loss: in one university experiment, participants exposed to push notification driven interruptions learnt less from a short lecture but did not rate their own performance correspondingly lower. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Receiving push notifications from smartphone games reduces students learning performance in a brief lecture: An experimental... Explanatory illustration 1 That makes notifications particularly relevant to a study routine that begins before social media. The problem is not simply that an alert might tempt someone into spending ten minutes on a feed. An interruption can impose a cognitive cost even when the phone is not subsequently used. For the first focused study block, therefore, silencing notifications is less about demonstrating self control than about removing events that can compete for attention in the first place. How to Get Your Brain to Focus Chris Bailey TEDxManchester Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube What notification experiments found about learning One unusually direct experiment was conducted by Katharina Graben, Bettina Doering and Antonia Barke with 93 students. Participants watched a video designed to mimic a brief university lecture. A control group simply watched it, while two other groups periodically played a simple smartphone game for 20 seconds at two minute intervals. Crucially, one gaming group was prompted by push notifications, while the other played without those notifications. Afterwards, everyone completed multiple choice questions about the lecture. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Receiving push notifications from smartphone games reduces students learning performance in a brief lecture: An experimental... Students in the push notification condition performed worse than the control group, with a reported standardised effect size of d = 0.51. The researchers did not find the same significant difference between the no notification gaming group and the control group. This does not establish that every notification causes a fixed amount of lost learning, but it provides experimental evidence that the way an interruption is initiated matters: an externally generated alert can impose costs beyond the mere availability of a phone. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Receiving push notifications from smartphone games reduces students learning performance in a brief lecture: An experimental... The finding also needs an important qualification. The same researchers conducted another experiment in which 93 students read material rather than watched a lecture, again comparing gaming, push notification and control conditions. That study found no significant differences in quiz performance or reading time. The authors suggested that characteristics of the task and interruption may matter. Taken together, the two experiments argue against the simplistic claim that every notification inevitably damages every learning activity. [Springer Nature] link.springer.com Springer NaturePlaying smartphone games while studying: an experimental study on reading interruptions by a smartphone game Education a... A broader evidence base nevertheless supports the underlying attentional mechanism. In a 2015 experiment, Cary Stothart and colleagues found that receiving mobile phone call or text notifications disrupted performance on an attention demanding task even though participants did not interact with the phone. The researchers concluded that the notifications themselves could generate task irrelevant thoughts and attentional disruption. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification PubMed... Later laboratory work has added physiological evidence. An experiment measuring event related potentials — electrical brain responses associated with information processing — found slower responses when participants heard smartphone notification sounds rather than control sounds. The neural measurements were also consistent with notification sounds requiring additional cognitive control. In other words, ignoring an alert is itself something the cognitive system may have to do. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The hidden cost of a smartphone: The effects of smartphone notifications on cognitive control from a behavioral and e... Memory experiments point in the same direction. Across a pilot study and two experiments, researchers compared people receiving no simulated phone notifications with people who had to dismiss or read them while memorising categorised word lists. Memory disruption increased with the degree of notification interaction. Merely dismissing notifications particularly interfered with memory for relationships among the studied items, while reading them disrupted both relational and individual item processing. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Individual differences in memory disruption caused by simulated cellphone notifications PubMedSeptember 12, 2022... Published: September 12, 2022 The wider literature concerns mobile phone distraction rather than notifications alone, so it should not be treated as notification specific evidence. Still, a 2025 meta analysis synthesising 27 experiments concluded that mobile phone distraction during learning produced a medium sized negative effect on young adults' immediate recall. That broader result is consistent with the idea that repeatedly switching attention between learning and phone related information has measurable costs. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. The New Science of Finding Focus & Fighting Distraction Gloria Mark Talks at Google Channel: Talks at Google Open on YouTube Why feeling focused can be misleading The most useful result from the Graben experiment may not be the lower quiz score by itself. Participants also estimated how well they thought they had performed. Those subjective estimates did not significantly differ between experimental conditions, even though objective learning performance was poorer in the push notification group than in the uninterrupted control group. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Receiving push notifications from smartphone games reduces students learning performance in a brief lecture: An experimental... That creates a practical problem for self improvement. A student can use an apparently reasonable test — “I still feel focused” — and reach the wrong conclusion about whether notifications are harmless. The mismatch makes sense when the interruption is tiny. A notification does not necessarily feel like abandoning a study session. The student hears a buzz, perhaps notices who sent something, dismisses it and immediately returns to the page. From the learner's point of view, almost the entire period was spent studying. But the relevant cognitive event is not measured simply in seconds of phone use. Attention had to orient towards the unexpected stimulus, decide what it meant or whether it deserved action, suppress or pursue the response, and then continue processing the learning material. Experimental work showing slower responses to notification sounds and disrupted attention without phone interaction supports this interpretation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification PubMed... This is why “I never reply while studying” is not quite the same protection as “notifications cannot interrupt this block”. The first strategy relies on resisting an interruption after it has occurred. The second prevents many externally generated interruptions from entering the study environment. There is another complication: not every phone related distraction comes from an actual alert. A 2026 study of 84 Taiwanese undergraduates compared settings in which smartphone notifications were revealed or hidden. Visible notifications were"},{"title":"Phone Distance","url":"/phone-distance/","description":"Moving a phone away can be useful when habitual checking is the real obstacle, even though mere phone presence does not always impair learning.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Study Systems","level":3,"text":"Phone Distance When Moving Your Phone Actually Helps Studying Study Systems Phone Distance Phone Distance When Moving Your Phone Actually Helps Studying Moving a phone away can be useful when habitual checking is the real obstacle, even though mere phone presence does not always impair learning. Moving a phone away can be useful when habitual checking is the real obstacle, even though mere phone presence does not always impair learning. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention What experiments say about phone presence and memory Why active checking matters more than simple proximity Who is most likely to benefit from putting the phone farther away self improvement that works study systems phone distance Introduction Moving a phone farther away can help studying, but the strongest reason is more practical than the familiar claim that a nearby phone automatically “drains” the brain. Research on the mere presence of smartphones is mixed: some experiments find small costs to working memory, while preregistered replications and meta analyses find little or no reliable effect across many cognitive tasks. [doi.org] doi.org Brain Drain: The Mere Presence of One’s Own Smartphone Reduces Available Cognitive Capacity Journal of the Association for Consumer... Explanatory illustration 1 The clearer case for distance is behavioural. Smartphone checking can become a brief, repetitive habit triggered with little deliberate thought, and notifications or actual phone use can interrupt attention and memory. [doi.org] doi.org Habits make smartphone use more pervasive Personal and Ubiquitous Computing Springer Nature LinkJune 16, 2011... Published: June 16, 2011 Putting the phone across the room, in a bag that is not beside the chair, or outside the study space therefore works best as a precommitment : it changes the environment before the urge to check arrives. For a routine built around studying before social media, that distinction matters. Distance is useful not because every visible phone necessarily damages cognition, but because an inaccessible phone cannot be picked up automatically at the first moment of boredom. What experiments say about phone presence and memory The influential case for physical distance came from a 2017 study by Adrian Ward and colleagues. Participants were randomly assigned to keep their smartphones on the desk, in a pocket or bag, or in another room while completing cognitive tests. In the first experiment, the other room group performed better than the desk group on a combined measure involving working memory and fluid intelligence, with results following a desk to pocket/bag to other room gradient. The researchers argued that inhibiting attention to a salient personal device might itself consume some limited cognitive capacity. [DOI] doi.org Brain Drain: The Mere Presence of One’s Own Smartphone Reduces Available Cognitive Capacity Journal of the Association for Consumer... That finding produced the memorable “brain drain” idea, but subsequent evidence makes a universal version of the claim difficult to defend. A preregistered direct replication published in 2022 reproduced the desk, pocket/bag and outside the room conditions and also varied whether phones were switched on or off. It found no significant difference between phone location conditions on working memory or response inhibition performance. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Reexamining the “brain drain” effect: A replication of Ward et al. (2017) ScienceDirect... Another laboratory study using mobile eye tracking similarly failed to find a performance cost from having a smartphone present; participants rarely looked at it during the tasks, although the device attracted more attention during breaks and transitions. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Does a smartphone on the desk drain our brain? No evidence of cognitive costs due to smartphone presence in a short term and... Meta analyses reflect the same uncertainty rather than resolving it into a simple rule. A 2023 systematic review and set of six meta analyses covered 56 effects and 7,093 participants. It found a statistically significant pooled mere presence effect for working memory capacity, but null summary effects for the other cognitive functions examined. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. A separate meta analysis reported an overall negative smartphone presence/use effect but also substantial heterogeneity across cognitive domains and studies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Does the Brain Drain Effect Really Exist? A Meta Analysis Pub Med Central (PMC)Does the Brain Drain Effect Really Exist? A Meta Analysis The practical interpretation is narrower than “always put your phone in another room because seeing it makes you less intelligent”. The presence effect appears sensitive to what is being measured and how the experiment is conducted. Distance may sometimes reduce cognitive interference, particularly for working memory, but that benefit is not sufficiently consistent to make brain drain the main justification for a phone distance rule. For studying, this distinction prevents an environmental tactic from being oversold. A student who can leave a silent phone on the desk for 40 minutes without touching it should not assume that moving it three metres away will produce a large improvement in memory. A student who repeatedly unlocks the same phone without consciously deciding to do so has a much stronger reason to move it. Why You Can't Focus (It's Not Your Phone) The Real Science of Attention Residue Channel: TheBetterStudent Open on YouTube Why active checking matters more than simple proximity Actual interaction with a phone presents a different evidential picture because it introduces attention switching rather than merely the possibility of distraction. A 2025 meta analysis synthesising 27 experiments on mobile phone distraction during learning concluded that mobile multitasking had a negative, medium sized effect on young adults’ immediate recall. The relevant mechanism is straightforward: attention must be divided or switched between learning and an unrelated mobile activity. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Mobile multitasking in learning: A meta analysis of effects of mobilephone distraction on young adults’ immediate recall S... Even an interruption that stops short of opening the phone can matter. In a controlled experiment, receiving a mobile phone notification disrupted performance on an attention demanding task even when participants did not interact with the device. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification PubMed... Experiments on memory have likewise found increasing disruption as participants moved from receiving no notifications, to dismissing notifications, to reading them. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Another study of 105 undergraduates found that notifications slowed performance on an attention task while mere phone presence did not — a particularly useful contrast when deciding what a distance rule is actually meant to accomplish. [ERIC] eric.ed.gov Open source on ed.gov. Not every interruption experiment produces worse test scores. In one study, students periodically played a short smartphone game while reading, and neither the game interruptions nor notifications produced significant differences in quiz scores or reading time. [Springer Link] link.springer.com Open source on springer.com. In a related brief lecture experiment, however, participants who played after receiving push notifications performed worse than the no game control group, while the notification free gaming group did not differ significantly from controls. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. The mixed individual experiments are another reason to avoid claiming that every glance has a predictable numerical cost. What distance can do more reliably is remove the opportunity for an effortless check . Long before current debates over “brain drain”, researchers examining logged smartphone behaviour described a characteristic checking habit: brief, repetitive inspections of readily updated information. Their field work suggested that quickly available informational rewards can reinforce these checks and that an initial check can become a gateway to additional phone activity. [DOI] doi.org Habits make smartphone use more pervasive Personal and Ubiquitous Computing Springer Nature LinkJune 16, 2011... Published: June 16, 2011 That makes physical distance a form of friction. Consider two versions of the same opening study block. With the phone beside the textbook, a moment of uncertainty in a difficult problem can be followed almost immediately by reach, unlock and feed. With the phone in another room, the same sequence requires"},{"title":"Distraction Shift","url":"/distraction-shift/","description":"Restricting a few social apps may simply redirect attention to messaging or other digital distractions instead of improving academic outcomes.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Study Systems","level":3,"text":"Distraction Shift What Happens When Social Media Limits Just Shift Distraction? Study Systems Distraction Shift Distraction Shift What Happens When Social Media Limits Just Shift Distraction? Restricting a few social apps may simply redirect attention to messaging or other digital distractions instead of improving academic outcomes. Restricting a few social apps may simply redirect attention to messaging or other digital distractions instead of improving academic outcomes. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case policy intervention What the multi quarter social media restriction trial found How students substituted messaging for restricted platforms Why routines should target the distraction pattern, not one app self improvement that works study systems distraction shift Introduction Limiting a few social media apps can reduce use of those apps without reducing the underlying distraction. The clearest student experiment on this problem followed digital behaviour across three academic quarters and restricted Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat to ten minutes a day during the intervention period. Students did use those platforms less, but they shifted towards instant messaging, their overall device time did not fall, and their grades did not improve significantly. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for a study routine that is supposed to begin before social media. If the real habit is “reach for something socially or digitally rewarding whenever studying becomes uncomfortable”, blocking Instagram may simply turn WhatsApp, YouTube or another available screen into the next escape route. More recent UK research with teenagers has observed the same broader substitution pattern across messaging, gaming, streaming and other screens. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Social Media Intervention Research 2026: Social media restriction pilots: Qualitative research with 13 to 17 year olds in the UK GOV.UK... The practical lesson is therefore narrower than “screen limits do not work”. Limits can change behaviour. But a limit aimed at particular apps is not necessarily a limit on distraction, and reducing one measured category of screen use should not automatically be treated as evidence that more studying has taken its place. Smartphones: It’s Time to Confront Our Global Addiction Dr. Justin Romano TEDxOmaha Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube What the multi quarter restriction trial found Avinash Collis and Felix Eggers designed their randomised controlled trial to get beyond a common weakness in research on social media: relying on people to estimate their own use. Students at the University of Groningen installed RescueTime, allowing the researchers to observe digital activity on computers and phones. They also obtained objective academic measures, including grades and credit points, alongside surveys of wellbeing. The study covered eight months, with the restriction itself lasting roughly two and a half months. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students P... The first academic block established a baseline. During the second, students were randomly assigned to treatment and control groups; the treatment group could spend no more than ten minutes per day across Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat. A third block allowed the researchers to examine what happened after the restriction disappeared. The initial survey involved 191 students, although attrition reduced the samples available for later analyses; 122 students could be matched across the principal data sources used for the first two blocks. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... The intervention clearly changed its immediate target: restricted students spent less time on the specified social networks. But that did not translate into the expected academic payoff. The researchers found no significant treatment effect on grades during the restriction period. Students in the treatment group attempted courses carrying more credit, but they did not pass significantly more courses than the control group. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... That is an important distinction for self improvement advice. A successful app restriction and a successful study intervention are different outcomes. The former asks whether time on named services fell. The latter asks whether the student actually redirected attention towards learning strongly enough to improve useful academic behaviour or results. In this trial, the first happened; convincing evidence for the second did not. Nor did the restriction leave a durable reduction in social media use. Among the 106 students with data throughout all three blocks and all four surveys, the treatment group's social media use returned after the limit was removed, with no significant difference from the control group in the final block. There were also no significant post treatment differences in grades or credit points. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... The result should not be stretched into a claim that social media can never interfere with studying. The authors themselves noted limited statistical power for smaller effects, the restricted platforms reflected the digital environment of the period rather than today's full mix of services, and a university student sample cannot represent everyone. Their experiment tested a specific intervention against specific platforms. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... What it demonstrates particularly well is something more useful here: removing one obvious outlet for distraction does not tell you what people will do with the released attention. How to Disarm Internal Triggers and Improve Focus Channel: Nir Eyal Open on YouTube Where the displaced attention went The strongest clue comes from the activity logs. During the restriction, instant messaging increased significantly relative to the control condition. Among the relevant users, average daily instant messaging time in the treatment group rose from 25.1 minutes in the baseline block to 28.8 minutes during the restriction, while the control group's corresponding average fell from 21.8 to 15.2 minutes. About 92.9% of the activity classified as instant messaging was WhatsApp. The difference in differences result for this substitution was statistically significant. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students P... This makes the null academic result easier to understand. A student prevented from checking Instagram has not necessarily become a student who is revising. Messaging can satisfy some of the same immediate motives: social contact, novelty, relief from boredom or avoidance of an effortful task. Collis and Eggers consequently argued that social networks and instant messaging could function as substitutes, while noting that their student population might be particularly prone to substitution because social communication is an important part of their use. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Effects of restricting social media usage on wellbeing and performance: A randomized control trial among students PLOS OneAugust 24... Evidence beyond this single trial suggests that the substitution can spread much further than messaging. A seven day social media abstinence study found participants describing replacement activities such as games, digital books and web searches when the habitual social media option was unavailable. The researchers interpreted this as evidence that boredom relief may sometimes be attached less to one particular platform than to the availability of convenient digital stimulation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Fear of Missing Out, Mental Wellbeing, and Social Connectedness: A Seven Day Social Media Abstinence Trial PMCJune... That mechanism has also appeared in much newer UK evidence. In a 2026 Department for Science, Innovation and Technology commissioned qualitative pilot, 309 families with 13 to 17 year olds took part in interventions involving 15 minute app limits, an overnight social media curfew or app removal. The study was qualitative rather than a randomised academic performance trial, so it cannot"},{"title":"Stable Context","url":"/stable-context/","description":"Studying in the same reliable context can make starting before social media more automatic over repeated sessions.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Study Systems","level":3,"text":"Stable Context Does Studying in the Same Context Build Momentum? Study Systems Stable Context Stable Context Does Studying in the Same Context Build Momentum? Studying in the same reliable context can make starting before social media more automatic over repeated sessions. Studying in the same reliable context can make starting before social media more automatic over repeated sessions. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence Why repeated time and place cues can strengthen automaticity How to choose a study context you will actually encounter When an inconsistent schedule makes context stability harder self improvement that works study systems stable context Introduction Studying in the same reliable context can make a morning study routine easier to start repeatedly. The clearest evidence comes from a six week study of 95 university students: those asked to keep the time and place of a new study habit stable developed stronger automaticity and did better at meeting their repetition goals than students asked to keep changing those contexts. Across 2,482 logged study habit repetitions, greater context stability was associated with the behaviour feeling progressively more automatic. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Explanatory illustration 1 For a routine designed to begin before social media, the practical point is not that every morning must look identical. It is that a recurring, recognisable situation can become associated with starting study. “After breakfast, at my desk” gives the behaviour a more consistent setting than “whenever I find time this morning”. Repetition in stable contexts is a central mechanism in habit theory because recurring cues can gradually become associated with the action that follows them. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlinePromoting habit formation: Health Psychology Review: Vol 7, No sup1 Get AccessOctober 11, 2011... Published: October 11, 2011 The evidence is promising rather than definitive. The main university experiment was relatively small, heavily weighted towards psychology students and deliberately contrasted stable contexts with unusually variable ones. It therefore supports context consistency as a useful design principle, not a guarantee that a fixed morning schedule will automatically produce a lasting study habit. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Habits: How to be successful every day Dan Ariely, Gretchen Rubin & more Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Why repeated time and place cues can strengthen automaticity The important distinction is between repetition and context dependent repetition . Simply studying frequently gives you practice at studying. Repeatedly beginning under recognisable circumstances can additionally strengthen an association between those circumstances and the act of starting. Habit research describes automaticity as behaviour becoming less dependent on fresh deliberation because familiar cues increasingly activate the response. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... That distinction was tested unusually directly by Marco Stojanovic, Axel Grund and Stefan Fries. Their 2022 study randomly assigned university students building new study habits to different context instructions. The stable context group was told to perform its habit in the same place and at the same point in the course of the day. The variable context group was told to change both, including using different places and different times rather than repeatedly returning to recent ones. Participants then logged their repetitions over six weeks. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The final dataset contained 95 students and 2,482 repetitions, averaging 26.13 logged repetitions per participant. The manipulation produced a large difference in reported context stability between the two groups, confirming that students had meaningfully altered the consistency of their settings. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Greater context stability predicted greater automaticity. It also predicted higher habit repetition goal attainment — essentially, how well participants were achieving the repetition targets they had set. Automaticity partially mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment, meaning that part, but not all, of the advantage associated with stable contexts could statistically be accounted for by the behaviour becoming more automatic. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMed... A second dataset provided a useful check. The researchers analysed 2,368 repetitions of 308 self selected habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Context was measured rather than experimentally assigned, so this part cannot establish causality as cleanly. Nevertheless, the same broad pattern appeared: greater context stability predicted both greater automaticity and better repetition goal attainment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 That convergence makes the study especially relevant to morning consistency. A reliable morning context does more than reserve a convenient slot in a diary. Repeated encounters with the same cues may reduce the amount of initiation that has to be reconstructed each day. Instead of waking up and repeatedly deciding whether, where and when to study, the learner increasingly encounters a familiar situation in which studying is the practised response. This interpretation also fits the wider habit literature. Reviews describe habits as responses acquired through repeated behaviour in stable contexts, with context–behaviour associations strengthening as the pairing recurs. Once those associations are established, encountering the relevant context can help activate the behaviour with less dependence on conscious deliberation. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlinePromoting habit formation: Health Psychology Review: Vol 7, No sup1 Get AccessOctober 11, 2011... Published: October 11, 2011 A 2026 preregistered reanalysis of a separate six month longitudinal dataset also found context stability among the factors associated with habit strength, although that research covered behaviours more broadly rather than specifically morning study. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. For studying before social media, this suggests a useful change of emphasis. The aim is not merely to exercise enough willpower to ignore feeds each morning. It is to make starting study the repeatedly practised response to the opening context of the day . Achieve Your Goals Through the Power of Habits Channel: USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences Open on YouTube How to choose a study context you will actually encounter The strongest context is not necessarily the most perfectly controlled one. It is one that recurs often enough to provide repeated opportunities for the same behaviour. For a student with predictable mornings, that might mean studying at the same desk shortly after breakfast. The relevant context then contains several overlapping cues: the stage of the morning, the location, the preceding activity and perhaps the prepared study materials. A workable routine might therefore be: finish breakfast, sit at the usual desk, open the prepared question set and begin before opening social media. There is a reason to think beyond clock time alone. Habit researchers have argued that stable contexts need not be defined exclusively by an exact physical location and exact minute; functional cues such as a preceding action can also provide continuity. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. For someone whose breakfast finishes anywhere between 7.15 and 7.45, “after breakfast” may therefore be a more dependable cue than “at exactly 7.30”. This matters because an idealised context that rarely occurs produces few opportunities for context dependent repetition. A student who chooses “8.00 at the library” but reaches campus at 8.00 only twice a week has created a precise context without creating a"},{"title":"Act Earlier","url":"/act-earlier/","description":"Changing situations, attention, or interpretation early can reduce the need to suppress a fully developed impulse later.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","level":3,"text":"Act Earlier Why Self Control Often Works Best Before Temptation The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more Willpower” Act Earlier Act Earlier Why Self Control Often Works Best Before Temptation Changing situations, attention, or interpretation early can reduce the need to suppress a fully developed impulse later. Changing situations, attention, or interpretation early can reduce the need to suppress a fully developed impulse later. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism The process model of self control Situation selection, modification, distraction, and reappraisal Why direct inhibition is often a late stage strategy self improvement that works willpower myths act earlier Introduction Self control often works best before temptation becomes a contest of wills. Instead of waiting until an urge is strong and then trying to suppress it, people can intervene earlier: choose a less tempting situation, alter the situation they are already in, direct attention elsewhere, or change how they interpret the tempting option. This is the central insight of the process model of self control , which treats an impulse as something that develops over time rather than appearing fully formed. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction changes what “good self control” looks like. Leaving the phone outside the study room can be self control. Sitting away from the drinks at a party can be self control. Looking away from an enticing food, or deliberately thinking about its less appealing consequences, can be self control too. Research in classrooms and daily life suggests that these earlier, antecedent focused strategies can support goal progress without requiring people to repeatedly win a late stage internal battle. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Stitch in Time: Strategic Self Control in High School and College Students PubMed... The evidence does not establish a universal rule that earlier is always better. Strategy effectiveness depends partly on the person and the particular self control problem. But it does undermine the idea that inhibition at the moment of maximum temptation is the defining test of discipline. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov From willpower to strategies: Existing insights and outstanding issues in self control strategy use in daily life PubMedAugust 28... The process model: intervene while the impulse is developing The process model developed by Angela Duckworth, Tamar Gendler and James Gross organises self control around the sequence through which an impulse develops. A situation exposes us to relevant cues; we direct attention towards some features of it; we appraise what those features mean or how valuable they seem; and this process produces a response tendency . Crucially, the sequence is iterative: attending to a tempting cue can make it more salient, which can further strengthen the impulse. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 The model therefore identifies several points at which someone can intervene: Situation selection: choose where to go, what to encounter or what to avoid. Situation modification: remain in the situation but alter its tempting features. Attentional deployment: redirect attention away from temptation or towards something supporting the goal. Cognitive change or reappraisal: change the meaning or value assigned to what is happening. Response modulation: inhibit the resulting response once the impulse has already developed. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 The ordering is important. Imagine trying to finish an hour of concentrated work while a phone sits beside the keyboard. Situation selection could mean working somewhere without the phone. Situation modification might mean switching it off and putting it in a bag. Attentional deployment means noticing it but returning attention to the document. Reappraisal might mean interpreting a notification not as an interesting opportunity but as an interruption that can wait. Response inhibition occurs when the desire to check is already strong and the person has to stop their hand reaching for the device. All five can count as self control. The difference is where in the development of the impulse they act . Earlier strategies try to prevent or weaken the temptation producing process; inhibition tries to control its behavioural output. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Situational Strategies for Self Control Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Angela Duckworth, University of Pennsylvania Self Control Stategies for School Age Children Channel: PsychologicalScience Open on YouTube Change the situation before changing yourself Situation selection is the earliest intervention because it changes exposure itself. Duckworth and colleagues give deliberately ordinary examples: studying in a library rather than a noisy room, exercising at a gym rather than expecting oneself to start from the sofa, or choosing social surroundings that make the desired behaviour easier. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Avoidance can sound like weakness under a willpower centred view: surely a disciplined person ought to be able to face temptation and say no. The process model reverses that interpretation. If the goal is to behave in a particular way, deliberately avoiding a predictable obstacle can itself be skilled self regulation. The person is acting while preferences are comparatively calm rather than delegating the entire decision to a future version of themselves facing a stronger impulse. There is evidence that people conventionally described as having high self control already behave in something like this fashion. Research reviewed by Duckworth and colleagues found that higher trait self control was associated with greater proactive avoidance of temptation. In an experimental example, participants could perform an anagram task immediately in a distracting lounge or wait for access to a quieter laboratory; those higher in trait self control were more likely to choose the less distracting environment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 This fits an important finding from everyday life. In an experience sampling study, 205 adults produced 7,827 reports about their desires over a week. Subsequent analyses of this line of research found that people higher in trait self control reported fewer problematic temptations and less motivational conflict rather than simply reporting more frequent victories over powerful desires. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Everyday temptations: an experience sampling study of desire, conflict, and self control PubMed... When complete avoidance is impractical, situation modification offers the next move. A student can silence notifications rather than leave the building; someone trying not to overeat can move tempting food out of immediate reach rather than avoid the gathering; a partygoer can sit away from where drinks are being served. The common mechanism is reducing the potency, proximity or convenience of the tempting cue before it has generated its strongest response. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Attention and interpretation are earlier control points too Not every situation can be avoided or rearranged. At that point, self control can still act upstream of direct inhibition by changing what receives attention. Classic delay of gratification research illustrates why this matters. Children waiting for a preferred reward could make waiting easier by diverting attention from its arousing qualities. Later work continues to identify this comparatively “cool” attentional focus — shifting attention away from emotionally activating features — as an important mechanism in children's ability to delay gratification. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Delay of gratification in children Delay of gratification in children PubMedMay 26, 1989... Published: May"},{"title":"Action Goals","url":"/action-goals/","description":"A useful goal turns a desired result into a repeatable action you can deliberately carry out and check.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Behaviour Goals","level":3,"text":"Action Goals Can You Actually Perform Your Goal? Behaviour Goals Action Goals Action Goals Can You Actually Perform Your Goal? A useful goal turns a desired result into a repeatable action you can deliberately carry out and check. A useful goal turns a desired result into a repeatable action you can deliberately carry out and check. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic mechanism Why measurable outcomes are not always actionable How to translate results into controllable behaviours Examples from fitness, study, money and work self improvement that works behaviour goals action goals Introduction A goal is actionable when it names something you can deliberately do , rather than merely a result you hope will happen. “Lose 5 kg”, “get a distinction”, “save £3,000” and “win more clients” may all be measurable, but they are still outcomes. You cannot directly perform a kilogram lost, a mark awarded, a bank balance or another person’s decision. You can perform the behaviours that make those outcomes more likely. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is more than wordplay. Behaviour change researchers formally distinguish goals defined by outcomes from goals defined by behaviour, and separately distinguish both from action planning: specifying how, when, where or how often the behaviour will occur. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryThe Behavior Change Technique Taxonomy (v1) of 93 Hierarchically Clustered Techniques: Building an International Consensus f... The practical move is therefore to work backwards: desired result → useful behaviour → repeatable action → evidence that you did it . The outcome still matters. It supplies direction and tells you whether your strategy is working. But for day to day self improvement, the question that makes a goal executable is simpler: Can I actually perform my goal? Why measurable outcomes are not always actionable “Make it measurable” is common goal setting advice, but measurability and actionability solve different problems. A measurable outcome tells you how you will recognise success. An actionable goal tells you what to do next. Consider “lose 5 kg”. It is specific enough to measure precisely, yet weight is the downstream product of behaviour plus numerous other influences. “Walk briskly for 30 minutes after work on Monday, Wednesday and Friday” is different: when Monday evening arrives, there is a defined action available to perform. Behaviour change science preserves this distinction explicitly. The Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy developed by researchers at University College London classifies “goal setting (behaviour)” separately from “goal setting (outcome)”. It also treats action planning as another technique, requiring planning around features such as context, frequency, duration or intensity. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryThe Behavior Change Technique Taxonomy (v1) of 93 Hierarchically Clustered Techniques: Building an International Consensus f... This is useful because it prevents three different questions becoming muddled together: What result do I want? “Save £3,000.” What behaviour could influence it? “Save part of my income.” What can I actually perform? “Transfer £150 to savings on every payday.” Research supports goal setting as a behaviour change tool, but not as a magic formula. A systematic review and meta analysis covering 141 papers, 384 effect sizes and 16,523 participants found a small positive effect of goal setting on behaviour, with an average effect size of d = 0.34. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... The important implication is not that every aspiration should simply be given a number. A goal has to direct attention towards useful behaviour. This becomes particularly clear in sport. A systematic review and meta analysis of 27 studies distinguished outcome goals such as winning, performance goals based on personal standards, and process goals concerned with the actions used to perform. In that literature, process goals showed the largest estimated performance effect ( d = 1.36), compared with performance goals ( d = 0.44) and outcome goals ( d = 0.09). Those estimates come from a specific sporting evidence base and should not simply be assumed to apply identically to saving, studying or office work, but the contrast illustrates why moving attention towards controllable execution can matter. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: The performance and psychological effects of goal setting in sport: A systematic review and meta ana... Advantages of creating systems and not just setting goals Peter Attia, M.D. with James Clear Channel: Peter Attia MD Open on YouTube Translate the result into controllable behaviour A useful translation starts with the outcome but works backwards from it. Suppose the outcome is “improve my exam result”. Asking “How will I get a better mark?” can produce vague answers such as “study harder”. A stronger question is: What repeated action, under my control, has a credible chance of improving the result? For studying, that might produce “complete and check 20 retrieval practice questions four evenings a week”. That is not merely more concrete; it selects an activity with evidence behind it. A systematic review of 50 classroom experiments involving 5,374 learners found that retrieval practice generally improved learning across educational levels, subject areas and testing arrangements, with 57% of reported effects classed as medium or large. [DOI] doi.org Retrieval Practice Consistently Benefits Student Learning: a Systematic Review of Applied Research in Schools and Classrooms Educati... A broader review likewise concludes that practising retrieval can slow forgetting and improve later access to learned information. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Practicing Retrieval Facilitates Learning Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsPracticing Retrieval Facilitates Learning Annual ReviewsJanuary 4, 2021... Published: January 4, 2021 The same backwards translation can be applied elsewhere: Desired outcomeToo dependent on the resultAction you can performImprove fitnessRun 5 km in under 25 minutesComplete the planned three training sessions each weekImprove exam resultsGet at least 70%Complete and mark 20 retrieval questions on four evenings each weekBuild savingsSave £3,000Transfer £150 to savings on every paydayProgress at workGet promoted this yearComplete one high priority deliverable before routine email each working dayGenerate businessWin five new clientsSend five well researched prospect approaches each week The right hand column is not guaranteed to produce the middle or left hand result. That is precisely the point. A behaviour goal should be controllable, not guaranteed to succeed. Its job is to identify your contribution to an outcome whose final value may remain uncertain. A good conversion can be tested with four questions. Can you deliberately start the behaviour? Can you tell afterwards whether you did it? Can you repeat it? Is there a credible reason to believe that repeating it contributes to the desired result? If the answer to the first three is yes but the fourth is no, you have created a trackable activity rather than a useful action goal. Mediocre People Focus on the Outcome. Exceptional People Focus On the Process Channel: The Art of Improvement Open on YouTube Make the behaviour executable Even “ exercise three times a week” leaves some execution work undone. What counts as exercise? When will it happen? For how long? Behaviour change research calls the next refinement action planning : specifying the performance of the behaviour through details such as context, frequency, duration or intensity. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk Open source on ucl.ac.uk. So the translation can become progressively more executable: Outcome: Improve cardiovascular fitness. Behaviour: Run regularly. Action goal: Run for 30 minutes three times each week. Executable version: Run for 30 minutes after work on Monday, Wednesday and Friday. This is closely related to implementation intentions: plans that link a relevant situation to a response, commonly expressed as “if situation Y occurs, then I will do behaviour X”. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large positive effect on goal attainment ( d = 0.65), with evidence that such plans help people initiate goal directed action when the specified opportunity occurs. [Social Psychology and Motivation] socmot.uni konstanz.de Social Psychology and MotivationImplementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta analysis of effects and processes Social Psychol... Reviews of health behaviour planning also find generally favourable effects, while stressing heterogeneity between studies rather than treating planning as universally effective. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"title":"Starting Automatically","url":"/starting-automatically/","description":"For workouts, study and similar routines, the cue to begin may become automatic long before the full sequence does.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Complex Habits","level":3,"text":"Starting Automatically Why Starting the Routine May Be the Real Habit Complex Habits Starting Automatically Starting Automatically Why Starting the Routine May Be the Real Habit For workouts, study and similar routines, the cue to begin may become automatic long before the full sequence does. For workouts, study and similar routines, the cue to begin may become automatic long before the full sequence does. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Habitual instigation versus habitual execution Why automatic starts predict repeated behaviour Which gateways are worth making automatic self improvement that works complex habits starting automatically Introduction For a complex routine, the first part to become habitual may be the decision or movement that gets the routine under way, not the whole sequence. Habit researchers call this habitual instigation : a familiar cue begins to trigger action with less deliberation. By contrast, habitual execution means the later steps of the routine themselves unfold automatically. Research on exercise and other behaviours suggests that instigation is more closely tied to how often people actually perform a behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction changes what “making exercise a habit” or “making studying a habit” realistically means. A workout can still require choices about exercises, weights and effort after arriving at the gym. A study session can still require deciding which problems to tackle. The useful habit may instead be the reliable gateway: when a recurring cue appears, beginning becomes the default response. Instigation is different from execution Consider “going for a run”. At one level, the behaviour involves choosing to run rather than continuing with whatever else you are doing. At another, it contains a chain of smaller actions: changing clothes, putting on trainers, leaving home, choosing a route, adjusting pace and eventually stopping. Gardner, Phillips and Judah formalised this distinction between automatically instigating an action episode and automatically executing its component actions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMed... The two need not develop together. Someone might automatically reach for their running kit after finishing work but consciously decide almost everything about the run itself. Conversely, a highly practised gym goer might perform familiar lifts smoothly once inside the gym yet still have to persuade themselves to go there. Habit strength therefore does not have to be uniform across an entire routine. This helps resolve an apparent problem with applying habit theory to complex self improvement behaviours. A simple cue–response association seems capable of explaining “after breakfast, take a tablet”, but less capable of specifying every action in a 45 minute workout. It does not need to. The learned association can operate at the gateway, automatically selecting or initiating “work out now”, while conscious goal directed control handles much of what follows. As Gardner later argued, complex sequences are unlikely to unfold entirely through habit even when their initiation is strongly habitual. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Compass Does habit weaken the relationship between intention and behaviour? Revisiting the habit‐intention interaction hypothesis Gardner 2020 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online Library... That also explains why repetition of an identical routine is not the same thing as habit. Phillips and Gardner explicitly separated patterned exercise — repeatedly doing similar exercise actions — from the automaticity of instigating or executing exercise. Their study of 123 adults found that instigation habit was the only one of these measures that uniquely predicted exercise frequency. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 How To Change with Prof. Katy Milkman Channel: Action for Happiness Open on YouTube Why automatic starts predict repeated behaviour The central advantage of an instigation habit is that it acts at a decisive point: whether the behavioural episode happens at all . If arriving home at 6 pm reliably prompts changing into exercise clothes, or sitting down after dinner reliably prompts opening the material for a study session, one important choice has already been made before the difficult parts begin. The exercise evidence is particularly revealing. In Phillips and Gardner's month long study, participants reported both how automatically exercise was instigated and how automatically it was executed, while recording their exercise in daily electronic diaries. Instigation habit strength uniquely predicted exercise frequency; changes in exercise frequency were also associated with changes in instigation habit rather than execution habit or simply following a patterned workout. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Gardner, Phillips and Judah subsequently examined the distinction in 229 students across flossing, snacking and eating breakfast. Measures specifically targeting habitual instigation were consistently more predictive of later behaviour frequency than measures of habitual execution. Their results also suggested that widely used self report habit measures largely capture automatic instigation rather than automatic performance of every component of a behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMed... The same pattern has appeared when behavioural complexity was examined directly. A 2022 study assessed four activities ranging from drinking sugary drinks and checking a phone to using social media and public transport. Among 173 participants initially surveyed, with 118 providing follow up frequency data, habitual instigation predicted behaviour across the activities whereas habitual execution did not. Crucially, the relationship between instigation habit and behaviour held across different levels of perceived complexity. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Habit and behavioural complexity: habitual instigation and execution as predictors of simple and complex behaviours Scienc... The practical interpretation should remain modest. These studies do not establish that making a start automatic will inevitably cause someone to maintain every complex routine. Much of the evidence relies on self reported habit measures and observational associations, and the original instigation versus execution researchers explicitly called for more experimental work. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMed... What the evidence supports more strongly is a narrower proposition: automatic initiation appears to be an especially important component of the relationship between habit and repeated behaviour . Explanatory illustration 2 The gateway can be smaller than the routine There is a further useful refinement. The habit worth developing does not necessarily have to be “start exercising” as an abstract mental decision. It can involve a concrete preparatory behaviour that reliably moves the person towards the larger routine. A six week study of 181 adults recruited from nine gyms and recreation centres separated exercise preparation from exercise performance. Intention and habitual preparation predicted exercise and changes in exercise over the study period, whereas habit of exercise performance did not. Temporal consistency — performing the relevant behaviour at a consistent time — was particularly important in both phases. The researchers concluded that establishing a consistent preparatory routine could help establish exercise habits. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub The role of habit in different phases of exercise BPS Psych HubThe role of habit in different phases of exercise Kaushal 2017 British Journal of Health Psychology Wiley Online Lib... More recent experimental evidence points in a similar direction. A 2025 factorial study of new users of an online exercise class platform tested several motivational and habit focused intervention components. The preparation habit intervention produced the clearest effects on exercise participation and increased both preparation and instigation habit strength. The authors interpreted the findings as evidence that habitualising preparation may help people"},{"title":"Formation Times","url":"/formation-times/","description":"Research reports habit formation ranging from days to many months, making fixed rules like 21 or 66 days misleading for complex routines.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Complex Habits","level":3,"text":"Formation Times There Is No Universal Day When Habits Click Complex Habits Formation Times Formation Times There Is No Universal Day When Habits Click Research reports habit formation ranging from days to many months, making fixed rules like 21 or 66 days misleading for complex routines. Research reports habit formation ranging from days to many months, making fixed rules like 21 or 66 days misleading for complex routines. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Where the famous 66 day figure came from What the 4 to 335 day range actually shows Why complexity makes countdown rules especially unreliable self improvement that works complex habits formation times Introduction There is no universal day on which a complex behaviour suddenly “clicks” into being a habit. The familiar numbers — 21 days, 66 days, two months — describe very different things, and none is a scientifically established deadline. The strongest direct evidence instead shows a learning process whose duration varies sharply between people, behaviours and circumstances. A 2024 systematic review found individual habit formation times ranging from 4 to 335 days , while noting that only four of its 20 included studies directly reported how long formation took. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 1 For complex routines, that variation matters more than any average. Exercise , meal preparation or a multi step evening routine can contain several decisions and actions, and research increasingly distinguishes between automatically starting such a sequence and automatically executing every part of it. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... The useful lesson for self improvement is therefore not to find the correct countdown. It is to understand what researchers actually timed. Hack Your Habits for Lasting Change: Insights from Wendy Wood and Adam Grant Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Where the famous 66 day figure came from The 66 day figure comes primarily from a University College London study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, published online in 2009 and in the European Journal of Social Psychology in 2010. Ninety six volunteers selected a new eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in a consistent context for 12 weeks. Examples included performing an action “after breakfast”. Participants recorded both whether they performed the behaviour and how automatic it felt. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Crucially, the researchers were not measuring the day on which a behaviour switched from “not a habit” to “habit”. They modelled each person's repeated automaticity scores using an asymptotic curve: automaticity was expected to increase substantially at first, then improve by progressively smaller amounts as it approached a personal plateau. The reported formation time represented the estimated point at which someone had reached 95% of that eventual automaticity level . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That distinction changes the meaning of “66 days”. It was the median estimated time to reach that modelling criterion, not a biological threshold, a prescription to practise for exactly 66 days, or evidence that everybody should feel automatic on day 67. Individual estimates ranged from 18 to 254 days . The curve could be fitted for 62 participants and was judged a good fit for 39, another reason not to treat one summary statistic as a universal law. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The shape of the curve is arguably more useful than its midpoint. Habit formation was gradual and asymptotic rather than an on/off transition. Early repetitions could therefore produce meaningful gains in automaticity well before the estimated plateau, while later repetitions could continue strengthening the association without producing a dramatic subjective moment when the habit suddenly became effortless. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The same study also undermines the idea that a streak must be perfect. Missing one opportunity to perform the behaviour did not materially disrupt the formation process. Consistent repetition mattered, but a single omission did not reset an invisible habit clock to zero. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Healthy Habits Take Time: Systematic Review Debunks 21 Day Habit Myth Channel: Medical Dialogues Open on YouTube From 21 days to a 335 day range The older 21 day rule has an even weaker connection to behavioural habit research. Maxwell Maltz's 1960 Psycho Cybernetics discussed observations such as patients becoming accustomed to changed appearances after surgery and explicitly referred to a minimum of about 21 days for perceptible changes in a mental image. It was not an experiment establishing that repeated behaviours become automatic in three weeks. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. Modern evidence does not simply replace 21 with 66. The 2024 systematic review and meta analysis examined 20 health habit studies involving 2,601 participants. Across the four studies that directly reported formation time, the headline results included median times of 59–66 days and mean estimates of 106–154 days. At the individual level, however, observed estimates stretched from 4 to 335 days . [MDPI] mdpi.com Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants... That widest range came from a study of healthy eating habits in which the median among successful formations was 59 days, but only 23% of participants reached the study's predetermined formation threshold . This is an important qualification: a formation time statistic can describe those who crossed a particular measurement threshold without telling us that everyone attempting the behaviour eventually did so. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Other results show why averages should not be transferred casually between routines. In a 90 day study of a daily stretching behaviour, researchers modelled automaticity at about 106 days when the stretch was performed after waking and 154 days when performed before bed . Because those estimates extended beyond the observation period, they involved extrapolating the fitted learning curves rather than watching every participant reach a literal finishing line. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit The systematic review itself therefore deserves a careful reading. Its 4 to 335 day span is not a new universal “true range” within which every conceivable habit must fall. It is the range found in the available studies it reviewed. Only four studies directly supplied formation time estimates, the behaviours were predominantly health related, and the review judged most included studies to have a high risk of bias. The authors explicitly identified the limited direct evidence on formation duration as a research gap. [MDPI] mdpi.com Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants... In other words, the progression from “21 days” to “66 days” to “4–335 days” should not be read as science repeatedly discovering a better countdown. It reflects a move away from a simple countdown altogether. Explanatory illustration 2 Why complexity breaks countdown rules Complex behaviours make a single formation time particularly difficult to interpret because “the habit” may not be one indivisible action. Going to the gym, for example, can involve recognising a cue, deciding to exercise, changing clothes, travelling, beginning the session and then completing a sequence of exercises. Those components need not acquire the same degree of automaticity at the same rate. Research distinguishes habitual instigation — a cue automatically prompting the impulse to begin — from habitual execution , in which the sub actions involved in performing"},{"title":"Stable Contexts","url":"/stable-contexts-196b65/","description":"Repeating a demanding routine in the same time and place can strengthen the cue that prompts it and improve follow-through.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Complex Habits","level":3,"text":"Stable Contexts Why the Same Time and Place Can Matter Complex Habits Stable Contexts Stable Contexts Why the Same Time and Place Can Matter Repeating a demanding routine in the same time and place can strengthen the cue that prompts it and improve follow through. Repeating a demanding routine in the same time and place can strengthen the cue that prompts it and improve follow through. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention What context stability means for complex routines Study habit evidence from stable versus varied settings Making cues consistent when schedules are messy self improvement that works complex habits stable contexts Introduction Repeating a demanding routine in the same setting can make it easier for that routine to become automatic. The reason is not simply that a fixed time or place makes a behaviour easier to remember. Habit learning depends on repeatedly pairing contextual cues with a response, so that encountering those cues increasingly prompts the response with less deliberate decision making. For complex routines, which contain several steps and more opportunities to hesitate or stop, that stable cue structure may be especially useful. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 1 The clearest direct evidence comes from experiments in which people have been asked either to keep the context of a new routine stable or deliberately vary it. Students developing study habits showed greater automaticity when repetitions occurred in a consistent context, while a recent randomised walking study found that both consistent and varied context planning increased activity but only consistent context planning produced sustained increases in habit automaticity . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 For self improvement, the practical lesson is therefore more precise than “be consistent”. When a routine is complicated, make the circumstances that start and surround it as predictable as reasonably possible. What context stability means for a complex routine A stable context is a recurring configuration of cues around a behaviour. Location and time of day are obvious examples, but context can also include a preceding activity, social setting or another reliable part of a daily routine. Habit researchers describe habits as learned context–response associations: repeated performance in recurring circumstances makes those circumstances increasingly capable of activating the behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... That distinction matters for complex habits . Consider “study for 25 minutes” rather than “drink a glass of water”. Studying requires getting materials ready, choosing what to work on, beginning, maintaining attention and progressing through the task. If the person studies at a different time and place every day, each attempt arrives with a somewhat different set of cues and practical decisions. If studying repeatedly begins at the same desk after breakfast, the desk, the preceding meal and that part of the day can repeatedly become associated with starting the same behavioural sequence. Research involving 459 participants and 25 behaviours provides unusually relevant evidence here. Frequency, contextual stability and perceived rewards were all positively associated with automaticity, while perceived behavioural complexity was negatively associated with it. Crucially, the association between contextual stability and automaticity was stronger for behaviours perceived as more complex. The study was observational, so it cannot prove that stabilising context caused complex behaviours to become automatic. But it suggests that environmental consistency may become more , rather than less, important as a routine becomes complicated. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beh... There is also a useful distinction between automating the decision to start a complex routine and automating every action inside it. A higher order habit can use a stable cue to initiate behaviour while leaving the details flexible. Research on nutrition, for example, has examined “fill half the plate with vegetables at dinner” as a higher order habit: dinner supplies a recurring context, while the particular vegetables and meal can change. This is a useful model for complex self improvement routines because it avoids demanding that every repetition be identical. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of behavioral performance, intrinsic reward value, and context stability on the formation of a higher order n... The target might therefore be “when I finish breakfast at home, I begin my study session”, not “every study session must contain exactly the same sequence of tasks”. The first preserves a dependable context–action relationship while allowing the content of a demanding routine to respond to real needs. Routine vs. Habit: What’s the REAL Difference? Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Stable versus varied settings in study habit research A particularly informative six week study by Marco Stojanovic, Axel Grund and Stefan Fries followed 95 university students through 2,482 repetitions of newly chosen study habits. Participants selected activities lasting between three and 30 minutes, such as working through a textbook or summarising lecture notes. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The manipulation was deliberately concrete. Students in the stable context condition were instructed to perform their study habit in the same place and at the same point in the course of the day. Those in the variable context condition were told to vary both, including changing locations so that their recent repetitions occurred in different places. The manipulation produced a large difference in perceived context stability between the groups. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Across the analyses, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity. It also predicted better attainment of the goal set for each repetition: in other words, participants were more likely to complete what they had intended to accomplish during the session when the context was stable. Part of the association between context stability and goal attainment was statistically mediated by automaticity. A second dataset involving 218 users of a habit building app, covering 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions, produced a similar general pattern. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 That completion finding is particularly relevant to complex habits. Context may do more than remind someone to begin. Stojanovic and colleagues argue that familiar circumstances can support smoother execution after the routine has started. Earlier experimental work on multi step routines similarly found that disrupting context during a practised coffee making task produced more action errors. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The result should not be overstated. Participants assigned to varying times and places still developed some automaticity, so an identical setting is not a prerequisite for learning. The student experiment also involved a predominantly young sample, incentives and deliberately planned once daily habits; the authors caution against assuming that the findings generalise unchanged to every kind of behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Even so, the experiment addresses an important practical question that observational studies cannot: changing the stability of the circumstances changed how habit development unfolded. For a demanding study routine, repeatedly returning to a recognisable starting situation appears preferable to deliberately scattering repetitions across unrelated times and locations. The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Consistency helps beyond"},{"title":"Explain It","url":"/explain-it/","description":"Explaining why a method works, where it fails and what to change next can reveal whether practice has produced usable understanding.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Learning Goals","level":3,"text":"Explain It Can You Explain Why Your Method Works? Learning Goals Explain It Explain It Can You Explain Why Your Method Works? Explaining why a method works, where it fails and what to change next can reveal whether practice has produced usable understanding. Explaining why a method works, where it fails and what to change next can reveal whether practice has produced usable understanding. Self Improvement That Works evidence single mechanism mechanism Why explanation exposes shallow understanding Questions that turn experience into usable knowledge Using explanations to choose the next practice step self improvement that works learning goals explain it Introduction Practice can make a procedure familiar without making it understandable. You may learn to reproduce the steps that worked last time yet still be unable to say why they worked, recognise when they no longer apply, or adapt them when the task changes. An explanation goal addresses that gap: after practising a method, aim to explain its mechanism, its conditions of use, its likely failure modes and the evidence that would make you change it. Explanatory illustration 1 Research on self explanation suggests that this is more than a test of whether learning happened. Generating an explanation can itself improve learning by forcing connections between actions, principles and prior knowledge. A 2018 meta analysis covering 64 research reports found a moderate overall benefit from prompting self explanation, although effects vary with the task, learner and prompt. [ERIC] eric.ed.gov EJ1186664 Inducing Self Explanation: A Meta Analysis, Educational Psychology Review, 2018 Sep... The practical aim is therefore not to become eloquent about what you did. It is to turn repeated experience into knowledge that can guide the next unfamiliar case. Why explanation exposes shallow understanding Successful repetition provides weak evidence about what has been learnt. Suppose you follow a particular sequence for analysing a spreadsheet, diagnosing a technical fault or solving an equation. After several successful attempts, at least three different things could be true: you understand the underlying relationships; you have memorised a useful sequence; or the practice problems have been similar enough that the same sequence keeps working. Performance alone may not distinguish them. Self explanation research provides a useful model of the difference. In a landmark study of students learning mechanics from worked examples, Michelene Chi and colleagues found that stronger learners did more than recount the displayed solution. They explained how solution steps related to underlying principles and elaborated the conditions under which actions were appropriate. Their knowledge subsequently depended less on the examples themselves. Weaker learners generated fewer such explanations and relied more heavily on examples during later problem solving. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Self explanations: How students study and use examples in learning to solve problems ScienceDirectApril 1, 1989... Published: April 1, 1989 That distinction matters for unfamiliar and complex tasks because usable understanding contains conditions, not merely actions . “When I see X, do Y” is useful only while the new problem resembles the old one. A stronger representation is closer to: “Y works here because X implies Z; if Z is absent, I need a different method.” Explanation pushes the learner towards that second form. There is evidence that deliberately prompting this process helps. Bisra and colleagues' 2018 meta analysis identified 69 effect sizes from 64 research reports and estimated an overall weighted effect of g = 0.55 for induced self explanation. The studies covered activities including problem solving, studying worked problems and learning from text, suggesting that the mechanism is not confined to a single school subject. [ERIC] eric.ed.gov EJ1186664 Inducing Self Explanation: A Meta Analysis, Educational Psychology Review, 2018 Sep... In classroom experiments using a computer based Cognitive Tutor, students required to explain their problem solving steps subsequently showed better understanding and greater success on transfer problems than students who practised without explaining their steps. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAn effective metacognitive strategy: learning by doing and explaining with a computer‐based Cognitive Tutor Aleven... The feeling of understanding is not enough Explanation is particularly valuable because subjective familiarity can be misleading. Rozenblit and Keil demonstrated what they called the illusion of explanatory depth : people often believe that they understand causal systems in substantially greater detail than they can actually explain. The illusion was especially pronounced for knowledge involving mechanisms and causal relationships. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The misunderstood limits of folk science: an illusion of explanatory depth PMCSeptember 1, 2002... Published: September 1, 2002 That produces a simple diagnostic for practice. Do not ask only, “Does this method feel clear now?” Try to produce the mechanism without leaning on the worked example, notes or interface that normally supplies cues. A smooth procedure can conceal missing reasoning; an attempted explanation makes some of those gaps observable. The important signal is not whether the explanation sounds polished. It is where it breaks . If you can describe steps one through four but cannot explain why step three is necessary, you have located a learning target. If you can explain why the method works in the practice case but cannot state what would make it inappropriate, you have identified a boundary that needs testing. This Really Makes a Difference (The Self Explanation Effect) Channel: Realistic Learning Open on YouTube Questions that turn experience into usable knowledge A useful explanation goal should demand more than “explain what you did”. That prompt can produce little more than a verbal replay of the procedure. Research on mathematics learning makes the distinction especially clear: explanations can help learners develop conceptual and procedural knowledge and transfer, but their quality and focus matter. A 2024 review reports strong evidence for immediate benefits in these areas while noting that evidence for delayed procedural transfer is more limited. It also concludes that carefully structured prompts and support for higher quality explanations can increase their usefulness. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Encouraging students to explain their ideas when learning mathematics: A psychological perspective ScienceDirectDecember 1... After practising a method, four questions provide a more demanding test: Why did this work? Identify the principle, causal relationship or constraint that made the important steps effective. “Because those are the instructions” is not yet a mechanism. When should I use it? State the cues or features of a new problem that justify choosing this method rather than another one. Where would it fail? Change an assumption, input or constraint. Predict whether the method still works and explain why. What would I change next? Use the explanation to specify the next variation, correction or experiment needed to strengthen the method. These questions probe different weaknesses. “Why?” looks beneath the sequence. “When?” tests whether you know the method's conditions of application. “Where would it fail?” searches for boundaries rather than merely accumulating successes. “What next?” converts the resulting uncertainty into deliberate practice. Consider someone learning a data analysis procedure. Ten successful repetitions of “remove unusual values, calculate the average, compare the groups” may create procedural fluency. An explanation goal asks something harder: Why are those observations being removed? What evidence makes them errors rather than genuine extreme cases? Why is the mean appropriate for these data? What happens if the distribution is strongly skewed? The moment those questions become difficult, practice has revealed what it has not yet taught. Comparison can sharpen the same process. When two examples look different but share an underlying structure, explaining what remains invariant can help the learner abstract beyond surface details. Research on contrasting cases and self explanation suggests that learners can use comparison to extract common relational structures and connect procedures to their conceptual basis. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Instead of merely asking, “Can I solve another one?”, ask, “What is the same about these two cases that makes the same method appropriate?” Explanatory illustration 2 Explanation should predict, not merely justify There is a trap in explaining successful practice: hindsight makes almost any successful sequence easy to"},{"title":"Measure Learning","url":"/measure-learning/","description":"Learning targets work best when their measures capture genuine understanding or skill rather than rewarding an arbitrary count.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Learning Goals","level":3,"text":"Measure Learning When Does a Learning Metric Become Meaningless? Learning Goals Measure Learning Measure Learning When Does a Learning Metric Become Meaningless? Learning targets work best when their measures capture genuine understanding or skill rather than rewarding an arbitrary count. Learning targets work best when their measures capture genuine understanding or skill rather than rewarding an arbitrary count. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention Why numerical specificity can mislead Choosing measures that represent real learning Examples of useful and distorted learning targets self improvement that works learning goals measure learning Introduction A learning goal becomes meaningless when its metric rewards counting learning like activity rather than demonstrating learning . “Find five strategies”, “read ten papers” or “complete twenty practice problems” sounds admirably specific, but the number may be only loosely connected to competence. Once hitting the quota becomes the objective, a learner can succeed on paper while understanding little. Explanatory illustration 1 This is an important qualification to research showing that learning goals can outperform performance goals on unfamiliar, complex tasks. Those studies support directing attention towards acquiring useful knowledge, strategies and procedures rather than demanding an immediate result. But they do not establish that every learning target needs an arbitrary numerical quota. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... For self improvement that works , the better principle is specificity about the capability you want to acquire, combined with evidence that the capability has actually improved . Count strategies when the number genuinely represents useful learning; otherwise measure explanation, selection, application, correction, retention or transfer. Why numerical specificity can mislead Goal setting research has good reasons for emphasising specificity. A vague intention such as “learn more about negotiating” provides little guidance about what to do or how to judge progress. In experiments on complex tasks, specific learning goals have successfully directed people towards discovering task relevant strategies, and the strategies discovered have been positively associated with performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... The crucial detail is what the number represented in those experiments. In one study of learning goal difficulty , participants completed a tightly specified class scheduling task for which researchers had identified four task effective strategies . Learning could therefore be operationalised as how many of those particular strategies participants identified and used. The researchers compared goals such as discovering and implementing one or two strategies with discovering and implementing three or four. In that controlled setting, a strategy count had substantive meaning because the strategy universe was unusually constrained and researchers could inspect whether each strategy was actually used. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. Everyday learning is rarely so tidy. Suppose you are learning data analysis and set a target to “discover ten ways to analyse a dataset”. Ten weak, overlapping or inappropriate techniques do not necessarily constitute more learning than three methods you understand deeply enough to select and apply correctly. A quota can turn strategy discovery into strategy collection . That is a version of a wider measurement problem often described through Goodhart's law: a useful indicator can become less informative once people optimise explicitly for the indicator. In education, commentators have applied the principle to measures that become targets even after their credibility as representations of learning has weakened. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)“When a Measure Becomes a Target, It Ceases to be a Good Measure” PMCFebruary 13, 2021... Published: February 13, 2021 The practical warning is not that numerical goals are inherently bad. It is that a metric is only useful to the extent that success on it remains evidence of the capability you actually care about . A strategy quota is particularly vulnerable to three distortions: Duplication: one underlying method can be divided into several nominally different “strategies” to inflate the count. Quality blindness: the metric treats effective and ineffective strategies alike unless effectiveness is independently checked. Stopping at the target: once the fifth strategy has been found, the learner has technically succeeded even if a sixth would be crucial or the first five have not been mastered. The irony is that a poorly designed learning metric can recreate the very problem learning goals are meant to solve: attention shifts away from understanding the task and towards hitting a predetermined number. Goodhart's Law: Why Hitting Your Targets Can Wreck Your Company Channel: KnowHow Open on YouTube Measure evidence of learning, not the appearance of it A stronger learning target starts by asking: what would I be able to do if I genuinely understood this? The answer supplies the success criterion. The Organisation for Economic Co operation and Development's discussion of formative assessment makes a similar distinction. Learning goals describe what learners are aiming to master, while success criteria make clear what they should be able to demonstrate; learners then monitor their progress against those goals. Assessment and feedback are useful because they provide evidence about the gap between present and desired capability. [OECD] oecd.org using formative assessment and feedback 189fb6dc Using formative assessment and feedback: Unlocking High Quality Teaching OECDApril 3, 2025... Published: April 3, 2025 For an unfamiliar complex task, useful evidence commonly falls into several forms. Explain it . Can you describe why a strategy works, which assumptions it depends on and where it can fail? This distinguishes possession of a rule from understanding of the rule. Choose it. Given several plausible approaches, can you identify an appropriate strategy and explain the choice? This matters because competence in complex work often consists not merely of knowing procedures but knowing when to use them. Apply it independently. Can you execute the method without copying an example or following step by step prompts? Research on worked examples illustrates why this distinction matters. Detailed examples are especially useful to novices, but guidance should be reduced as knowledge develops so that learners increasingly perform the task themselves. Studies of fading instructional guidance have found advantages on delayed and transfer tests, making independent performance a more informative signal than simply counting examples completed. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk supporting pupils with worked examples EEFSupporting pupils with worked examples EEFJune 20, 2022... Published: June 20, 2022 Diagnose errors. Can you spot why an attempted solution fails and repair it? Identifying and correcting mistakes can probe understanding that successful repetition alone may conceal. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk eef blog mistakes and explanations eef blog mistakes and explanations Transfer the learning. Can you use the principle when the surface details change? Transfer — applying knowledge in a new context — is especially valuable evidence because memorising a familiar procedure can produce apparent mastery without flexible understanding. Research has found that retrieving and applying concepts across different examples can improve later transfer to new examples. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. More recent experimental work with complex research methods concepts likewise found benefits of sufficient retrieval practice on delayed retention and application measures. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. These measures need not eliminate numbers. They simply attach numbers to meaningful demonstrations . “Correctly diagnose four out of five unfamiliar cases and justify the chosen method” is numerical, but its quantity is anchored to competence. “Learn five strategies” may not be. Explanatory illustration 2 A useful metric should survive a change of example One practical test for a learning metric is to ask whether somebody could maximise it without becoming substantially better at the underlying task. If the answer is yes, the measure needs revision. Consider a novice learning spreadsheet modelling. A goal such as “identify six modelling techniques this week” is easy"},{"title":"Find Strategies","url":"/find-strategies/","description":"A strategy discovery goal directs beginner effort toward finding workable methods before demanding a high performance result.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Learning Goals","level":3,"text":"Find Strategies What Should Beginners Try to Discover First? Learning Goals Find Strategies Find Strategies What Should Beginners Try to Discover First? A strategy discovery goal directs beginner effort toward finding workable methods before demanding a high performance result. A strategy discovery goal directs beginner effort toward finding workable methods before demanding a high performance result. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism What counts as a useful task strategy Setting a specific strategy discovery target Evaluating which discovered strategies actually help self improvement that works learning goals find strategies Introduction For a beginner facing an unfamiliar, complex task, a useful first goal is often not “perform brilliantly” but discover several workable ways of doing the task well . This is a strategy discovery goal: a specific learning target that directs attention towards finding, testing and implementing task relevant methods before demanding a high outcome. Goal setting researchers Gerard Seijts and Gary Latham describe this as shifting attention from the end result to the strategies and processes capable of producing it. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because complexity changes what effort can accomplish. When a beginner does not yet know how to succeed, greater persistence with a weak method may simply produce more weak attempts. Experiments with scheduling tasks, business simulations and novice surgical training instead suggest a different route: make strategy acquisition itself the immediate target, use performance as evidence about whether those strategies work, and only later give the outcome target greater prominence. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... What counts as a useful task strategy? A strategy is more than an intention to concentrate, practise or “try harder”. It is a repeatable way of approaching some consequential part of the task : a procedure, decision rule, sequence, information gathering method or technique that changes how the work is performed. Research on complex task performance distinguishes strategy identification, selection and implementation precisely because knowing that a method exists is not the same as choosing it appropriately or carrying it out successfully. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineGoal Levels, Complex Tasks, and Strategy Development: A Review and Analysis: Human Performance: Vol 4, No 1Novembe... Imagine someone learning to conduct a difficult client interview. “Be more confident” is not much of a strategy. “Begin with broad questions, note unexplained claims, then use follow up questions to test each one” is closer to one because the learner can actually apply and evaluate it. Likewise, a beginner learning data analysis might discover that checking variable definitions before choosing a statistical method prevents a recurring class of errors. The useful discovery is not merely a fact about the subject; it is knowledge that changes what the learner does . This is why a strategy discovery goal should not be confused with a vague instruction to learn. Seijts and Latham's account of learning goals explicitly centres the discovery of effective strategies or processes. In a 2001 class scheduling experiment involving 96 participants, the number of task relevant strategies discovered was positively associated with performance, while a specific, difficult learning goal outperformed an instruction merely to do one's best. Strategy development also helped explain the relationship between self efficacy and performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... The distinction appeared earlier in Winters and Latham's experiment with 114 business students. On a straightforward scheduling task, an outcome goal worked well. When the task became complex enough to require knowledge acquisition, however, participants given a learning goal performed better and used more effective task strategies than those assigned either an outcome goal or a do your best instruction. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... So the practical test for a discovered strategy is not whether it sounds clever. It should be specific enough to execute, relevant to an important part of the task, and capable of being tested against feedback . What Are Learning Goals Vs Performance Goals? Social Success Club Channel: Social Success Club Open on YouTube Set a strategy discovery target, not just an intention to learn “Learn how to do this” leaves a beginner with almost as much ambiguity as “do your best”. A strategy discovery goal makes the learning target observable. It can take forms such as: discover and test three ways to identify the most important information before making a decision; identify four techniques that reduce recurring errors, then use each on a fresh attempt; find three different approaches to the hardest stage of the task and record when each works; identify five task relevant strategies, implement them, and retain those that improve subsequent attempts. The numbers are not universal prescriptions. Their value is that they turn an abstract ambition into a search target. Experimental research has explicitly operationalised learning goals in terms of the number of unique, task relevant strategies participants were asked to identify and implement. In a study of 146 participants, Latham, Seijts and Crim varied the difficulty of such learning goals and found that higher learning goal levels were associated with better performance; cognitive ability and commitment also mattered. [Rotman School of Management] www 2.rotman.utoronto.ca Rotman School of Management Canadian Journal of Behavioural Science Rotman School of ManagementCanadian Journal of Behavioural ScienceNovember 29, 2025... Published: November 29, 2025 That finding should not be converted into the simplistic rule that “more strategies are always better”. The target needs to stimulate meaningful exploration rather than strategy collecting for its own sake. The object is to discover effective methods. A beginner who lists ten superficial tips but never tests them has not accomplished the important part of the goal. There is also a useful distinction between discovering and implementing . A learner can read about a good method without becoming capable of using it. The 2001 scheduling study is instructive because it examined task relevant strategies actually implemented, not merely ideas participants could name. Participants assigned a learning goal together with proximal goals implemented more task relevant strategies than those in the other goal conditions. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... A strong strategy discovery target therefore contains an action verb that goes beyond exposure: identify and test , discover and implement , compare and refine . That wording keeps the target attached to actual task behaviour. Explanatory illustration 2 Why strategy search can outperform harder striving The mechanism is largely about where limited attention goes . On a familiar task, a difficult performance goal can encourage greater effort and persistence because the performer already possesses plausible methods. On an unfamiliar complex task, the missing resource may instead be procedural knowledge. Seijts and Latham argue that a learning goal redirects attention towards acquiring that knowledge rather than continually comparing present performance with a demanding outcome. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... This can prevent a common beginner failure mode: treating every disappointing result as evidence that more effort is required. If the method is poor, extra effort does not necessarily repair it. Strategy discovery changes the question from “How can I push harder?” to “What could I do differently on the next attempt?” There is evidence that this change affects more than reported motivation. In a randomised study of 90 third year medical students, nearly all of whom had little or no previous experience with the relevant techniques, learners given learning goals performed better at knot tying and"},{"title":"When to Switch","url":"/when-to-switch/","description":"Performance goals become more useful once effective strategies are reliable enough that attention can shift from discovery toward execution.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Learning Goals","level":3,"text":"When to Switch When Are You Ready for a Performance Goal? Learning Goals When to Switch When to Switch When Are You Ready for a Performance Goal? Performance goals become more useful once effective strategies are reliable enough that attention can shift from discovery toward execution. Performance goals become more useful once effective strategies are reliable enough that attention can shift from discovery toward execution. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic population context Signs that strategy discovery is becoming reliable How performance targets change the task demand Using a gradual handoff from learning to results self improvement that works learning goals when to switch Introduction The right time to switch from a learning goal to a performance target is not simply “after enough practice”. The more useful threshold is when you have a dependable way of doing the task . Early on, the main problem is usually discovery: finding effective strategies, understanding cause and effect, and learning which procedures work. Later, once that knowledge is sufficiently reliable, the problem increasingly becomes execution: applying the method consistently, efficiently and under demanding standards. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because performance goals work best when effort and persistence can be channelled through relevant task knowledge. Goal setting research has long found benefits from specific, challenging targets, but research on complex tasks qualifies that finding: when people do not yet know how to achieve the result, an ambitious outcome target can compete with the learning needed to develop the method. Once useful knowledge and skills exist, that constraint weakens and performance targets become more appropriate. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... The practical implication is a handoff rather than a fixed date . Do not ask only, “How long have I been practising?” Ask, “Am I still searching for a workable strategy, or am I now mainly trying to execute a strategy I understand?” When strategy discovery is becoming reliable A useful way to recognise the transition is to look at what is causing your remaining errors. If failures still routinely reveal that you misunderstood the task, chose the wrong method or cannot explain why one approach works better than another, learning remains the bottleneck. If failures increasingly come from inconsistent execution, insufficient effort, lapses of attention, pacing or failure to apply an already known procedure, a performance target has more to work with. This follows from an important distinction in the goal setting literature between motivation and task knowledge . Edwin Locke's analysis of studies linking goals, strategies and performance argued that direct benefits from performance goals arise when people already possess relevant task knowledge. In effect, a demanding target can tell an experienced person to mobilise and apply strategies that are already available; it cannot substitute for strategies that have not yet been acquired. [IAAP Journals] iaap journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com IAAP JournalsMotivation, Cognition, and Action: An Analysis of Studies of Task Goals and Knowledge Locke 2000 Applied Psychology... Experience matters for the same reason. A field study involving 347 employees found that experience moderated relationships between goal setting, strategy quality and performance on jobs with substantial component complexity. This does not produce a universal number of hours after which somebody becomes “ready”, but it reinforces the underlying principle: the same objective can impose different cognitive demands on a novice and an experienced performer. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryJoint moderating effects of job experience and task component complexity: Relations among goal setting, task strategi... For self improvement, several practical signs therefore point towards readiness for a stronger performance focus: You can describe the method, not merely the desired result. You know the main steps, decisions or techniques that generate good performance. The strategy survives repetition. A successful attempt no longer feels like a lucky exception; similar decisions produce reasonably similar results across several attempts. Feedback mostly refines execution. Corrections concern speed, precision, consistency or application rather than repeatedly exposing a missing mental model. You can diagnose mistakes. When performance drops, you can usually identify what went wrong and what to change on the next attempt. Extra effort has somewhere useful to go. Working faster, harder or for longer now means applying known behaviours more consistently rather than simply intensifying an uncertain approach. These are diagnostic indicators, not a validated five part test. Research gives much stronger support to the underlying principle — use learning goals when relevant knowledge and skill still need to be acquired — than to any universal numerical switching threshold. A recent expert statement on goal setting in exercise and physical activity makes the same distinction explicitly: specific, challenging performance goals are appropriate when the necessary ability is present, whereas people should avoid such targets while they still lack the knowledge or skill needed to perform the task. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Goal Setting in Exercise and Physical Activity: An Expert Statement on Behalf of Exercise and Sports Science Australi... Confidence can be informative too, but it should be treated as supporting evidence rather than proof. In a complex business simulation, a specific learning goal improved performance relative to a performance goal or vague goal, with information search and self efficacy helping to explain the effect. Someone who feels confident because they have discovered and successfully applied effective strategies is in a different position from someone who merely feels confident before those strategies have been tested. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsGoal Setting and Goal Orientation: An Integration of Two Different Yet Related Literatures Academy of Man... Eduardo Briceño: How to get better at the things you care about TED Channel: TED Open on YouTube What changes when you adopt a performance target? Switching goals changes what deserves your limited attention. A learning goal might be: identify three ways of structuring this analysis and determine which produces the fewest errors . A performance goal might become: complete the analysis accurately within 90 minutes . The second target makes sense once the first problem has largely been solved. This is why performance goals should not be understood as the “advanced” version of learning goals in a simple hierarchy. They perform a different job. Learning goals direct attention towards discovering strategies, procedures and task knowledge. Performance goals can then direct established capability towards a specified result through greater focus, effort and persistence. Seijts and Latham summarised the distinction by arguing that learning goals are particularly useful when acquiring knowledge and skills is more important than simply increasing effort. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... Historically, this qualification emerged against a large body of goal setting research showing that specific, difficult goals often outperform vague “do your best” instructions. A major meta analysis of studies conducted from 1966 to 1984 found strong support for difficult and specific goals, while also identifying moderators of their effects. Later work on complex tasks helped clarify why the familiar prescription cannot simply be transferred unchanged to a novice who is still learning what to do. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirectFebruary 1, 1987... Published: February 1, 1987 The difference is especially visible when knowledge acquisition is experimentally important. In research using a complex business simulation, participants assigned a specific learning goal performed better than those assigned a specific performance goal or a vague goal; information search helped mediate the learning goal advantage. Another experiment on a complex knowledge acquisition task found a significant performance effect for a primed learning goal but not for a primed performance goal. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsGoal Setting and Goal Orientation: An"},{"title":"Test Methods","url":"/test-methods/","description":"Comparing two approaches under similar conditions can turn repeated attempts into evidence about which method works better and when.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Learning Goals","level":3,"text":"Test Methods Can Two Small Tests Reveal a Better Method? Learning Goals Test Methods Test Methods Can Two Small Tests Reveal a Better Method? Comparing two approaches under similar conditions can turn repeated attempts into evidence about which method works better and when. Comparing two approaches under similar conditions can turn repeated attempts into evidence about which method works better and when. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Choosing two approaches worth comparing Keeping comparisons similar enough to learn from Recording what changed and what to try next self improvement that works learning goals test methods Introduction For an unfamiliar, complex task, a useful learning goal is not simply “practise more”. It can be: test two plausible methods under similar conditions, compare what happens, then use the result to choose the next test . That turns repeated attempts into evidence. Instead of asking only whether performance improved, the learner asks which approach worked better, on what kind of problem, and why. Explanatory illustration 1 This approach follows directly from research showing that learning goals are valuable when effective strategies still have to be discovered. It also draws on research into contrasting cases, deliberate practice, reflection and single case experiments. Together, these fields support a practical principle: comparisons become informative when the alternatives are genuinely plausible, the conditions are similar enough to make the contrast meaningful, and the learner records enough detail to explain the result rather than merely declaring a winner. [aom.org] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... Choosing two approaches worth comparing A test and compare goal is most useful when there is real uncertainty about method . If the learner already knows that one procedure is correct and the other is plainly defective, comparison adds little. The interesting case is when two reasonable strategies could work but their relative strengths are unclear. Goal setting research provides the underlying rationale. Seijts and Latham argue that when a task principally requires acquiring knowledge or skill, a challenging learning goal should direct attention towards discovering effective strategies rather than merely demanding a difficult outcome. In an experiment using a moderately complex scheduling task, a specific difficult learning goal produced better performance than a “do your best” instruction, and the discovery and implementation of task relevant strategies were positively associated with performance. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... A test and compare goal makes that strategy search concrete. Suppose someone is learning to analyse unfamiliar reports. Instead of setting “finish the next report 20% faster”, they might compare: Method A: read the whole document before taking notes; Method B: first identify the decision question, then read selectively and record evidence against a fixed set of headings. The goal for two comparable reports becomes something such as: try each method once, measure time and the number of important points missed, then explain which method should be tested again . The immediate target is knowledge about the process, not a flattering performance number. The alternatives should differ in something that could plausibly cause a useful difference. “Work normally” versus “try harder” is a poor comparison because the intervention is vague. “Retrieve answers from memory before checking notes” versus “reread notes before answering” is much more diagnostic because the behavioural difference is clear. There is also a strong case for comparing methods side by side rather than learning each in isolation . In a study of 70 seventh grade pupils learning algebra, Bethany Rittle Johnson and Jon Star randomly assigned pupils either to compare alternative solution methods or to reflect on the same methods separately. The comparison group made greater gains in procedural knowledge and flexibility, while conceptual gains were similar. The finding is especially relevant to complex task learning because flexibility — knowing that more than one procedure exists and selecting appropriately — is different from becoming faster at one familiar routine. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLDoes comparing solution methods facilitate conceptual and procedural knowledge? An experimental study on learning to solve e... Comparison is not automatically beneficial, however. Research on fraction division found that simply presenting contrasting cases without prompting learners to explain them did not improve conceptual understanding. Self explanation did. That is an important qualification: a test and compare goal should require the learner to articulate what differed and why that difference might have produced the result , rather than merely placing two scores next to each other. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do contrasting cases and self explanation promote learning? Evidence from fraction division ScienceDirectDecember 1, 2015... Published: December 1, 2015 Eduardo Briceño: The Performance Paradox Channel: FranklinCovey Open on YouTube Keeping the comparison similar enough to teach you something The hardest part of a personal comparison is not trying two methods. It is making the attempts comparable enough that the result means anything. Imagine using Method A on a short, familiar assignment on Monday morning and Method B on a difficult new assignment late on Friday afternoon. If Method A wins, the learner cannot tell whether the method mattered or whether task difficulty, prior knowledge, fatigue or some other difference produced the gap. A test and compare goal therefore needs a simple version of experimental control: change the method while keeping important surrounding conditions reasonably similar . This principle is well established in single case experimental research. Alternating treatments designs compare two or more interventions within the same individual through repeated, closely spaced exposure to the different conditions. Their value comes from making the conditions comparable and looking for a consistent difference across repeated observations rather than relying on one before and after impression. Randomisation or constrained alternation of condition order can further reduce the risk that time or sequence systematically favours one treatment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Quantitative Techniques and Graphical Representations for Interpreting Results from Alternating Treatment Design PM... A self improvement exercise does not need to pretend to be a clinical experiment, but it can borrow the underlying discipline. Useful controls include giving each method tasks of similar difficulty, using the same time allowance and success criterion, alternating which method goes first, and repeating each approach rather than treating one unusually good or bad attempt as decisive. For learning tasks, equivalence matters more than literal sameness . You often cannot perform exactly the same novel task twice because the first attempt changes what you know. Research on adapted alternating treatments designs faces a similar problem when studying skills that are acquired and therefore cannot simply be reset. One solution is to compare methods using different but carefully matched sets of learning material. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryExperimental control in the adapted alternating treatments design: A review of procedures and outcomes Cariveau 2... That suggests a practical pattern. If someone wants to compare two ways of learning vocabulary, programming concepts or technical procedures, each method should receive a different set of items matched as closely as possible for difficulty and prior familiarity. Later, swap or repeat the comparison with fresh matched sets. The aim is not laboratory perfection; it is to prevent obvious differences between the tasks from overwhelming the difference between the methods. The same logic explains why changing several things simultaneously is usually a bad learning test. Suppose Method A involves morning study, retrieval practice and a quiet room, while Method B involves evening study, rereading and background music. A difference in performance cannot identify the useful ingredient. Small comparisons are more informative when they isolate one strategically important contrast. There are limits. Rapid alternation is inappropriate when one method has strong carry over effects into the next condition, and single case research explicitly recognises interference between"},{"title":"Gradual Growth","url":"/gradual-growth/","description":"Small progressive increases often protect repetition better than abrupt leaps, although research does not support one universal progression formula.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Scale Up","level":3,"text":"Gradual Growth How Big Should Your Next Habit Increase Be? Scale Up Gradual Growth Gradual Growth How Big Should Your Next Habit Increase Be? Small progressive increases often protect repetition better than abrupt leaps, although research does not support one universal progression formula. Small progressive increases often protect repetition better than abrupt leaps, although research does not support one universal progression formula. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic policy intervention What graded tasks mean in practice Why there is no universal percentage increase Choosing a step large enough to matter but small enough to test self improvement that works scale up gradual growth Introduction Once a habit is reliably established, the next increase should usually be large enough to create a meaningful new challenge but small enough that repetition remains realistic . Behaviour change research supports the general idea of graded tasks: start from what a person can already do and increase the demand through achievable steps. A 2026 systematic review of physical activity interventions found that programmes containing graded tasks produced meaningful increases in activity, although the evidence was heterogeneous and graded tasks were always combined with other techniques such as goal setting and action planning. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effects of graded tasks on physical activity: a systematic review and meta analysis PubMedFebruary 5, 2026... Published: February 5, 2026 Explanatory illustration 1 What research does not establish is a universal progression rule such as “increase every habit by 10% each week”. The appropriate jump depends on what is being increased, the person's present capacity and what happens to consistency after the change. The useful principle is therefore not always make tiny increases . It is increase experimentally rather than assuming that a large leap will be sustainable . What graded tasks mean in practice “Graded tasks” has a more precise meaning in behavioural science than simply taking things slowly. In the Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy, the technique involves setting tasks that begin easy enough to perform and then become progressively more difficult while remaining achievable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Linking behaviour change techniques to mechanisms of action: Using the Theory and Techniques Tool alongside the Behav... This resembles the older behavioural principle of shaping , in which a target behaviour is approached through successive approximations rather than demanding the finished performance immediately. The American Psychological Association describes shaping as progressively reinforcing responses that come closer to the desired behaviour. [APA Dictionary] dictionary.apa.org Dictionary APA Dictionary of Psychology APA DictionaryAPA Dictionary of PsychologyApril 19, 2018... Published: April 19, 2018 Applied to an established personal habit, grading means changing the criterion in stages. Someone who already reads reliably for ten minutes after breakfast might try fifteen minutes before considering thirty. A regular 15 minute walk could become 20 minutes, rather than immediately becoming an hour long outing. A person consistently completing one focused study session might extend its duration or add a second session on selected days rather than simultaneously doubling both frequency and length. The important feature is not that each numerical increase must be small. It is that the next demand is chosen in relation to demonstrated performance . Work on shaping in rehabilitation illustrates this adaptive logic particularly clearly: tasks are progressively made harder as capability improves, with difficulty adjusted through variables such as time, repetitions or task demands. One influential constraint induced movement therapy protocol describes the appropriate task as slightly beyond what the patient can already accomplish easily, while stressing that progression must be individualised. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Behavior Analytic Origins of Constraint Induced Movement Therapy: An Example of Behavioral Neurorehabilitation PMC... Behavioural activation provides another practical precedent. Graded task assignment breaks activities that feel difficult into manageable steps so that people can practise rather than repeatedly fail or avoid the activity. In a UK behavioural activation trial for people with post stroke depression, for example, a difficult shopping goal could begin with visiting a familiar local shop before progressing to a larger, more distant one. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioural Activation Therapy for Depression after Stroke (BEADS): a study protocol for a feasibility randomised con... Although that clinical setting is not the same as everyday habit scaling, the progression principle transfers cleanly: the next step should stretch current behaviour without making successful repetition implausible. Tiny Habits. Big Changes: BJ Fogg Channel: FranklinCovey Open on YouTube Why gradual progression has an advantage over a sudden leap The case for grading is strongest when the objective is not a one off peak performance but continued repetition at a higher level . A dramatic target can produce a dramatic day; it does not necessarily produce a durable new baseline. The strongest recent evidence comes from physical activity. A 2026 systematic review identified 53 randomised controlled trials involving 10,060 adults in which graded tasks were used to increase physical activity. Thirty nine studies entered the meta analysis, which found a medium overall effect, with a standardised effect size of g = 0.61. Intriguingly, the number of graded task increments explained substantial variation in outcomes. However, certainty of evidence was rated low, heterogeneity was high, and every graded task intervention also used behavioural goal setting and action planning. The review therefore supports graded progression as a useful component of intervention, not as an independently proven formula for every habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effects of graded tasks on physical activity: a systematic review and meta analysis PubMedFebruary 5, 2026... Published: February 5, 2026 Smaller change interventions also show why plausibility matters. In the ASPIRE randomised trial, researchers deliberately tested modest, cumulative, participant chosen changes in eating and physical activity rather than relying on large initial behavioural changes. The small change group lost more weight than comparison groups over the short study and maintained that initial loss at three month follow up. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Small changes in nutrition and physical activity promote weight loss and maintenance: 3 month evidence from the ASPIRE randomized t... But longer studies provide an important corrective: a two year trial in sedentary adults found that a small change intervention involving roughly 2,000 extra daily steps and a 100 kcal dietary reduction did not produce a significant weight advantage over monitoring alone at two or three years. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That mixed record matters. “Small” is not automatically superior. A change can be so modest that its cumulative benefit is limited, or it can still prove difficult to maintain. In a more recent remote small change trial, 62% of participants described the approach as helpful for weight management, yet adherence failed the researchers' predefined feasibility criterion. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The more defensible conclusion is narrower: graded progression gives you repeated opportunities to discover a sustainable level before committing to a much larger demand . It is a method for calibrating difficulty, not evidence that tiny actions inevitably compound into large outcomes. B.F. Skinner's Theory of Shaping Explained Channel: Psychology Exposed Open on YouTube There is no universal percentage increase Habit advice often becomes more precise than its evidence. A rule such as “add 10% each week” sounds attractive because it converts a judgement call into arithmetic. But different behaviours impose very different constraints, and even exercise science does not provide a universal percentage that can simply be exported to reading, writing, meditation, studying or household routines. Running is a useful warning. The familiar “10% rule” recommends limiting increases in training volume, yet reviews have questioned the evidence for that particular threshold. A systematic review of transitions to minimalist running footwear, for example, noted that the 10% training volume rule had not been validated as a reliable injury prevention boundary and concluded that an optimal progression rate was unclear. [PubMed Central"},{"title":"When Scaling Fails","url":"/when-scaling-fails/","description":"If a scaled habit starts collapsing on normal days, the added demand may be too large and the previous reliable version remains a useful floor.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Scale Up","level":3,"text":"When Scaling Fails What to Do When a Bigger Habit Stops Sticking Scale Up When Scaling Fails When Scaling Fails What to Do When a Bigger Habit Stops Sticking If a scaled habit starts collapsing on normal days, the added demand may be too large and the previous reliable version remains a useful floor. If a scaled habit starts collapsing on normal days, the added demand may be too large and the previous reliable version remains a useful floor. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Warning signs that the expansion is too demanding Separating a bad day from a broken repetition pattern Using the reliable floor behaviour to recover self improvement that works scale up when scaling fails Introduction When a habit becomes less consistent soon after you make it harder, the most useful interpretation is usually not that the original habit has failed. It is that the new dose of the behaviour may exceed what your existing routine can reliably carry . A ten minute walk that happened almost every day and a newly prescribed 40 minute walk are not behaviourally identical simply because both are called “walking”. Explanatory illustration 1 Habit research does not provide a precise rule for how large an increase is “too large”. It does, however, point in a consistent direction: repetition, stable contexts and manageable behaviour support automaticity , while greater perceived difficulty and behavioural complexity can work against it. A 2026 longitudinal study, for example, found perceived difficulty negatively associated with habit strength, while behaviour frequency and context stability were positively associated with it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation PubMedApril 29, 2026... Published: April 29, 2026 The practical response is therefore diagnostic rather than punitive. Distinguish an isolated bad day from a new pattern of missed repetitions . If the bigger version repeatedly disappears under ordinary conditions, return to the smaller version that was already reliable, preserve its cue and rebuild upwards in smaller increments. Warning Signs the Expansion Is Too Demanding A scaled habit has to work on normal days, not merely demonstrate what you can do when time, energy and motivation all cooperate. This distinction matters because increasing a habit can quietly change more than its apparent size. Consider someone who reliably reads for ten minutes after making evening tea. Moving to 15 minutes mostly changes duration. Moving to an hour may also require an earlier start, fewer interruptions, more sustained attention and a different bedtime. The cue may be unchanged, but the behaviour now makes substantially greater demands on the rest of the evening. Research supports treating that added difficulty as consequential. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis followed 254 participants over six months and identified trajectories of both habit formation and habit degradation. Behavioural complexity, frequency and context stability differed between those trajectories, and greater perceived difficulty was associated with weaker habit strength. The authors emphasised that habits are dynamic rather than something that simply becomes permanent once established. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation PubMedApril 29, 2026... Published: April 29, 2026 This fits broader evidence that behavioural complexity matters for habitual control. Reviews of health behaviour research report stronger habit effects for behaviours lower in complexity, while a 2024 systematic review of health related habit formation found that frequency, behavioural regulation, type of behaviour and other practical characteristics can influence habit strength. [Springer Nature] link.springer.com Springer NatureProgress on theory of planned behavior research: advances in research synthesis and agenda for future research Journal o... The clearest warning signs after an increase are therefore behavioural: The habit starts requiring favourable days. The expanded version works at weekends or when work is quiet, but repeatedly disappears on ordinary busy days. Starting requires renewed negotiation. A previously near automatic “I do this now” becomes “Do I have enough time or energy for this?” Completion frequency falls noticeably. More behaviour per successful session is being bought at the price of fewer sessions. The increase creates secondary requirements. A longer workout now requires changing clothes, travelling somewhere, showering or finding an uninterrupted hour. The old cue stops producing a practical response. “After breakfast” may still trigger the thought of exercising, for example, but a 45 minute session no longer fits between breakfast and leaving home. That last distinction is especially important. Stable contexts help both habit development and performance. In a six week study of university students and a separate real world dataset from a habit building app, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 If scaling forces the behaviour into different times, places or circumstances, the change may inadvertently weaken part of the structure that made the smaller habit dependable. So “make the habit harder” should not mean “make each successful repetition as impressive as possible”. A useful increase is one that raises the behavioural dose without causing the repetition pattern to collapse . Why You Keep Failing At Habit Changes @bjfogg Ep. 306 Channel: The Jordan Harbinger Show Open on YouTube Is It a Bad Day or a Broken Pattern? One missed repetition is weak evidence that an expansion has failed. A sequence of misses tied to the increased demand is much more informative. This distinction prevents two opposite mistakes. The first is overreacting to every lapse by immediately reducing the habit. The second is protecting an unrealistic target for weeks because each failure can individually be explained away. The classic real world habit formation study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues followed people performing a chosen behaviour in the same daily context for 12 weeks. Missing a single opportunity did not materially disrupt the habit formation process. What mattered more was the broader pattern of repetition in a consistent context. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Although that study examined formation rather than the specific problem of scaling an established habit, it provides a useful caution against treating one missed day as catastrophic. Maintenance research makes a similar conceptual distinction between a lapse , meaning a single deviation, and relapse , in which lapses accumulate into a return towards an earlier behavioural pattern. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Applied to habit scaling, the question is not “Did I miss Tuesday?” but “What happened to the probability that I perform this behaviour after increasing its demands?” A practical test is to compare the enlarged habit with its former baseline. Suppose 15 minutes of language practice happened on six evenings out of seven. You raise it to 45 minutes and, over the next few weeks, manage three or four sessions in a typical week. The new target produces impressive sessions, but it has also damaged the repetition pattern. That is useful evidence about the design of the expansion. By contrast, if the 45 minute version usually happens and disappears only during an unusually difficult week, there is less reason to conclude that the increase itself is wrong. The problem may be temporary conditions rather than the normal behavioural demand. The relevant test is therefore ordinary condition survivability . Ask whether the bigger version can repeatedly coexist with the workload, tiredness, interruptions and imperfect motivation that make up normal life. This is stricter than asking whether the target is physically possible, but more useful for a habit intended to last. Explanatory illustration 2 Why “Push Through It” Can Misdiagnose the Problem When consistency falls, it is tempting to assume that maintaining the harder target will eventually force adaptation. Sometimes persistence is appropriate. But this argument confuses two different challenges: gradually increasing capacity and repeatedly failing to initiate an oversized routine. Behaviour change guidance generally favours progression through manageable steps rather than simply prescribing a large end state. NICE recommends planning change through easy steps over time, using coping plans for relapse and"},{"title":"Scaling Signs","url":"/scaling-signs/","description":"A habit is safer to expand when starting feels routine, cues still work, and ordinary disruptions no longer derail repetition.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Scale Up","level":3,"text":"Scaling Signs When Is a Habit Ready to Grow? Scale Up Scaling Signs Scaling Signs When Is a Habit Ready to Grow? A habit is safer to expand when starting feels routine, cues still work, and ordinary disruptions no longer derail repetition. A habit is safer to expand when starting feels routine, cues still work, and ordinary disruptions no longer derail repetition. Self Improvement That Works evidence single mechanism mechanism Reliability matters more than a day count Signs that starting has become more automatic Why recovery after missed days matters self improvement that works scale up scaling signs Introduction A habit is ready to grow when the existing version is becoming reliably cue driven rather than repeatedly decision driven . The strongest practical signs are not a particular streak length but a cluster of changes: the behaviour happens on most intended occasions, its usual cue reliably gets it started, initiation requires less conscious remembering or negotiation, and an ordinary interruption does not turn into prolonged abandonment. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because habit formation varies enormously. A 2024 systematic review found substantial differences between behaviours and individuals, with reported habit formation times ranging from 4 to 335 days across the relevant studies. The classic real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues likewise found estimates ranging from 18 to 254 days to approach peak automaticity . [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For scaling, therefore, the useful question is not “Have I done this for long enough?” It is: “Has the small version become dependable enough that adding demand is unlikely to dismantle the pattern that produces it?” Reliability matters more than a day count The familiar idea that a habit becomes established after a fixed number of days does not fit the research particularly well. In Lally and colleagues' 12 week study, 96 participants chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour to repeat daily in the same context. Automaticity generally rose with repetition towards a plateau, but people reached that plateau at markedly different rates. The often repeated figure of 66 days was a median estimate, not a universal deadline. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The more recent systematic review reinforces the point. Across 20 studies involving 2,601 participants, researchers used different behaviours, measures and criteria for habit formation. Only four studies specifically reported time to automaticity, and their results varied substantially. The review also judged most included studies to have a high risk of bias, which makes a precise universal threshold even harder to defend. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants PubMed... For deciding whether to scale, behavioural stability is therefore more informative than elapsed time . Someone who has read for ten minutes after breakfast for six weeks but still needs an alarm, repeated self persuasion and frequent catch up sessions may have accumulated plenty of repetitions without developing a particularly strong cue–behaviour link. Someone else may find that finishing breakfast now naturally leads to reaching for the book. The second pattern is closer to what habit researchers mean by automaticity. This follows from the underlying mechanism. Habits develop as responses become associated with recurring contextual cues. Wendy Wood and Dennis Rünger describe habits as an efficient default form of responding that emerges through repeating responses in recurring contexts. Frequency alone is therefore not the whole story: what increasingly triggers the behaviour matters. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Psychology of Habit Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsPsychology of Habit Annual ReviewsJanuary 4, 2016... Published: January 4, 2016 A sensible scaling decision consequently looks for repeated evidence rather than a calendar milestone. One easy session proves little. Several weeks of effortless looking repetition do not prove complete automaticity either, but they provide much better evidence when the same cue continues to initiate the behaviour and missed opportunities remain exceptions rather than the beginning of collapse. Signs that starting has become more automatic The most useful automaticity signal is often found at the start of the routine. Habit researchers distinguish between habitual instigation — automatically selecting and initiating an action — and habitual execution , in which the sequence of actions itself proceeds automatically. In a study covering breakfast consumption, snacking and flossing, Benjamin Gardner and colleagues found that measures of habitual instigation were consistently more predictive of subsequent behaviour frequency than measures of habitual execution. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... That makes initiation especially relevant to scaling. A 10 minute walk need not become something performed in a trance before it can be expanded. What matters more is whether the usual situation increasingly produces the transition into walking: lunch ends, shoes go on, and the person leaves. The behaviour can still involve conscious choices once under way. Several observable signs point in that direction: The cue regularly produces a start. After breakfast, arriving home or shutting the work laptop has become a dependable prompt rather than merely a reminder of something you ought to do. Remembering requires less work. The routine is less dependent on alarms, notes or suddenly remembering late in the day. There is less negotiation before beginning. Starting no longer routinely requires weighing whether you feel motivated enough. The behaviour feels increasingly like the normal response in that situation. You may notice yourself beginning with comparatively little deliberation. This pattern is repeated, not occasional. A single unusually motivated week is weaker evidence than the same low friction initiation across ordinary weeks. These signs closely resemble the dimensions researchers actually use to measure automaticity. The validated four item Self Report Behavioural Automaticity Index focuses on whether a behaviour feels automatic, occurs without conscious remembering or thinking, and can begin before the person fully notices that they have started. Crucially, the scale deliberately separates automaticity from simple behavioural frequency. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Towards parsimony in habit measurement: Testing the convergent and predictive validity of an automaticity subscale of... That does not mean a person needs to satisfy every item at its extreme before increasing a routine. There is no validated “scale your habit now” score. Rather, these dimensions provide better diagnostic questions than “How many days is my streak?” How Does Habit Formation Impact UX Design Choices Channel: IxDF Interaction Design Foundation Open on YouTube Does the cue still work on ordinary days? Automaticity is not simply an internal feeling of ease. It reflects a learned relationship between a context and a response, so the reliability of that context is another important readiness signal. A 2022 study by Marco Stojanovic, Axel Grund and Stefan Fries examined this directly. In one experiment, 95 university students completed 2,482 repetitions of study habits over six weeks. Participants assigned to the stable context condition were asked to use the same place and point in the day, while another group deliberately varied those conditions. A second dataset examined 308 habits from 218 users of a habit building app. Across the datasets, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals; automaticity partially mediated the association between context stability and goal attainment. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. For scaling, that suggests a useful test: does the established cue continue to do its job when life is merely ordinary rather than ideal? Consider someone building a writing habit. During the first weeks, writing 100 words after making the first morning coffee may demand considerable attention. Later, coffee preparation itself may begin to lead naturally towards opening the document. At that point, moving from 100 to 150 words changes the amount of work while leaving the learned starting structure intact. By contrast, if the writer still completes 100 words only by moving the session around the day, relying on repeated notifications and making"},{"title":"One Variable","url":"/one-variable/","description":"Raising duration, frequency, volume, difficulty, or quality one at a time makes it easier to see what the routine can sustain.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Scale Up","level":3,"text":"One Variable Change One Part of a Habit at a Time Scale Up One Variable One Variable Change One Part of a Habit at a Time Raising duration, frequency, volume, difficulty, or quality one at a time makes it easier to see what the routine can sustain. Raising duration, frequency, volume, difficulty, or quality one at a time makes it easier to see what the routine can sustain. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention The five common ways to make a habit harder Why simultaneous increases hide the cause of failure How to test whether the new version is sustainable self improvement that works scale up one variable Introduction Once a habit is consistent, the cleanest way to make it more ambitious is to change one demand at a time while leaving the rest of the routine as stable as practical. A 10 minute walk might become 15 minutes while staying on the same days and route; three weekly study sessions might become four while their length and difficulty remain unchanged. The point is not that research has established a universal “one variable rule” for habits — it has not — but that gradual, isolated changes combine several evidence backed principles: stable contexts support automaticity , graded tasks keep increases achievable, and monitoring one altered demand makes it easier to judge whether the new version can actually be maintained. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers Frontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022 Explanatory illustration 1 This matters after consistency has been established because scaling creates a different problem from starting. The question is no longer simply “Can I do this behaviour?” but “How much more can I ask of this routine without making reliable repetition disappear?” Changing one variable turns that question into a practical test rather than a wholesale redesign. Explanatory illustration 3 The five common ways to make a habit harder A stable routine can usually be expanded along five useful dimensions: duration, frequency, volume, difficulty and quality . These categories overlap in some activities, but separating them is useful because each imposes a somewhat different demand. Duration: make each occasion longer. A 10 minute reading session becomes 15 minutes; a 20 minute walk becomes 25. Frequency: add another occasion. Strength training twice a week becomes three times; reviewing vocabulary on four mornings becomes five. Volume: do more work within an occasion. Write 300 words instead of 200, practise 15 problems instead of 10, or complete four sets rather than three. Difficulty: keep roughly the same amount of activity but make it more demanding. Read harder material, use a more challenging exercise variation or practise more difficult problems. Quality: retain the broad workload while raising the standard of execution. Writing might include a deliberate editing pass; music practice might place greater emphasis on accuracy; study might shift from rereading towards retrieval and explanation. These are practical distinctions rather than five scientifically standardised categories of habit progression. Behaviour change research does, however, explicitly treats goals in terms such as frequency, duration and intensity, and the Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy includes graded tasks : beginning with achievable tasks and making them progressively more difficult. [NCBI] nih.gov NCBIBehaviour change techniques used in included studies (Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy version 1 An evidence synthesis and economic analysis NCBI Bookshelf The useful policy is therefore not “always increase by 10 per cent” or any other universal formula. The appropriate increment depends heavily on the behaviour. Exercise progression is constrained by physical adaptation, whereas adding five minutes to reading mainly creates a time and attention demand. UK physical activity guidance, for example, recommends beginning with low durations and intensities and building up as the body adjusts, rather than abruptly imposing a full workload on someone unaccustomed to it. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UKJuly 10, 2026... Published: July 10, 2026 The underlying habit research also argues against treating difficulty as irrelevant. A 2019 study covering 25 behaviours found that perceived behavioural complexity was negatively associated with automaticity, while frequency and context stability were positively associated with it. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis of a six month longitudinal dataset similarly found habit trajectories differed with behavioural complexity, frequency and context stability, and that greater perceived difficulty was associated with weaker habit strength. These findings do not prove an ideal progression schedule, but they make a sudden jump in several kinds of difficulty at once a questionable way to protect an established routine. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beh... 9:21 What People Get Wrong About Deliberate Practice Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD · Views: 377.3K · Uploaded: October 2021 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why simultaneous increases hide the cause of failure Suppose someone reliably runs for 20 minutes on Monday, Wednesday and Friday. They decide to improve by running for 35 minutes, adding Saturday, choosing a hillier route and trying to run faster. If adherence collapses two weeks later, what went wrong? Perhaps four sessions do not fit the week. Perhaps 35 minutes creates too much recovery demand. Perhaps the hills make the session unpleasant. Perhaps the faster pace is the real problem. Because all four variables changed together, the result supplies surprisingly little useful information. Changing only duration — 20 minutes to 25, for example — creates a much more interpretable test. If the person continues starting reliably and completing the sessions, the established routine appears able to carry the extra five minutes. If consistency deteriorates repeatedly, duration is an obvious variable to reconsider. This is essentially a practical behavioural experiment: preserve as much of the existing system as possible, introduce one meaningful change and observe what happens. That preservation matters because context is part of what makes habitual behaviour dependable. In a six week study of 95 university students, alongside data from 218 users of a habit building app, greater context stability predicted both stronger automaticity and greater attainment of the goal attached to each repetition. The experimental portion deliberately varied versus stabilised participants' places and times, giving stronger grounds than a simple cross sectional association for treating context stability as useful during habit building. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers Frontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022 So if the established pattern is “study after breakfast at my desk”, increasing study from 20 to 30 minutes is a relatively contained change. Moving it to the evening, switching location, increasing it to 45 minutes and replacing familiar exercises with substantially harder material is not merely a larger version of the same habit. It changes several elements that may have helped the old version work. This distinction becomes especially important with complex behaviours. Research differentiates habitual instigation — automatically getting started — from habitual execution , in which the subsequent actions themselves are automatic. In a 2022 study spanning behaviours of differing complexity, habitual instigation predicted subsequent behaviour frequency whereas habitual execution did not. More recent work likewise found that complex behaviours can continue to require deliberate self regulation even when their initiation is strongly habitual. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Habit and behavioural complexity: habitual instigation and execution as predictors of simple and complex behaviours Scienc... That means an established cue can remain valuable even when the activity after the cue becomes more sophisticated. Someone may automatically sit down to practise the piano at 18:00 while still having to think carefully through an increasingly difficult practice session. Scaling does not require making every component automatic; preserving the dependable start may be particularly valuable while deliberately upgrading what happens afterwards. How to become 37.78 times better at anything Atomic Habits summary (by James Clear) Channel: Escaping"},{"title":"Keep the Cue","url":"/keep-the-cue-ebc4ad/","description":"Keeping the same reliable cue while enlarging the response can reduce the need to rebuild a routine from scratch.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Scale Up","level":3,"text":"Keep the Cue Why Scaling Works Better When Cues Stay Stable Scale Up Keep the Cue Keep the Cue Why Scaling Works Better When Cues Stay Stable Keeping the same reliable cue while enlarging the response can reduce the need to rebuild a routine from scratch. Keeping the same reliable cue while enlarging the response can reduce the need to rebuild a routine from scratch. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How cue behaviour links support automaticity Expanding the response without changing the context When a new time or place creates a new habit self improvement that works scale up keep the cue Introduction Once a habit is reliable, the simplest way to make it bigger is often to increase the response while keeping its established cue intact . If five minutes of reading reliably follows breakfast, the next version might be ten minutes after breakfast — not ten minutes at a new time, in a new room, under a new set of conditions. The reason is mechanical rather than motivational: habits develop partly because repeated behaviour becomes associated with recurring features of its context, allowing those features to prompt action with less deliberate decision making. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Explanatory illustration 1 This makes a stable cue a useful piece of behavioural infrastructure. Scaling the action while retaining that infrastructure changes one problem at a time: the person has to adapt to doing more , but does not also have to learn when to start . Research has not directly established a universal formula for scaling an already formed habit, so this principle should not be treated as a proven optimisation rule. But it follows closely from evidence showing that stable contexts support automaticity , that contextual changes can disrupt established habits, and that automatic initiation can remain important even when the behaviour itself is complex. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 How cue–behaviour links support automaticity In habit research, a cue is not merely a reminder pasted onto an otherwise unchanged decision. Through repetition, features of a recurring situation can become associated with a response. Wood and Neal's influential account describes habits as emerging from learned associations between responses and features of the contexts in which those responses have repeatedly occurred — including physical settings and preceding actions. Later reviews similarly describe a habit as a process in which encountering a contextual cue automatically activates an impulse to act. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... That context can be something quite ordinary. Breakfast can precede taking a vitamin; arriving home can precede changing into exercise clothes; opening a laptop can precede reviewing the day's priorities. The important property is not that the cue is unusual or psychologically powerful in isolation. It is that it is encountered reliably enough for the same response to be repeatedly paired with it. A classic real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates the principle. Ninety six participants selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context — for example, “after breakfast” — for 12 weeks. Repetition in that consistent context was associated with increasing automaticity, although the rate of development varied substantially between people. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... More direct evidence comes from a 2022 study of context stability. In one experiment, 95 university students building study habits over six weeks were instructed either to keep their time and place stable or deliberately vary them. A second dataset covered 308 habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals; automaticity partly mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The practical point for scaling is therefore easy to miss: the cue is part of what has been learned . When a routine has become dependable after breakfast, “after breakfast” is not incidental packaging around the behaviour. It may be helping to initiate it. The Power of Habit Animated Summary Channel: Wisdom for Life Open on YouTube Expand the response, not the starting context Suppose someone has reached the point where opening their laptop in the morning reliably leads to five minutes of language practice. They now want 15 minutes. There are at least two ways to make that change: keep opening the same laptop in the same part of the morning and extend the practice from five to 15 minutes; or move the longer session to 7 pm, use a different device and expect themselves to remember it independently. Both produce a nominally larger goal, but the second also removes much of the context associated with starting the established routine. It is therefore not simply a change in quantity. From a habit learning perspective, it changes the cue conditions as well. This distinction becomes especially useful when separating habitual instigation from habitual execution . Habitual instigation means that a cue automatically prompts the selection or initiation of an activity; habitual execution concerns automaticity within the sequence after it has begun. In a study covering flossing, snacking and breakfast consumption, instigation habit was more consistently predictive of behaviour frequency than execution habit. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... Exercise provides an especially clear example because the activity itself can be long and complicated. In a month long study of 123 healthy adults, habitual exercise instigation — automatically getting started — was the unique habit related predictor of exercise frequency, whereas automatic execution of the whole exercise sequence was not. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMed... This helps explain why keeping the start of a scaled routine recognisable can matter even when what follows becomes more ambitious. The cue does not necessarily have to automate every repetition, kilometre, page or study problem. Its useful job may simply be to get the larger episode under way. A sensible scaling pattern therefore looks like this: Established version: After breakfast → read for 10 minutes. Scaled version: After breakfast → read for 20 minutes. Or: Established version: Arrive home → change clothes → walk for 15 minutes. Scaled version: Arrive home → change clothes → walk for 30 minutes. Or: Established version: Open laptop → review five flashcards. Scaled version: Open laptop → complete a 15 minute review session. In each case, the response grows while the familiar gateway remains recognisable. This is not a claim that the larger behaviour will instantly inherit all the automaticity of the smaller one. The extra work may still require conscious effort and repeated experience. The narrower advantage is that the person is not deliberately discarding an already learned starting condition while asking themselves to tolerate a greater behavioural demand. Explanatory illustration 2 Stable does not have to mean an exact clock time Keeping a cue stable should not be confused with performing a behaviour at precisely the same minute every day. Context can include location, preceding activities, other people and other recurring features of a situation. Researchers still do not know exactly which contextual features dominate habit triggering in every real world setting, and their importance can vary between behaviours and individuals. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... For many routines, an event can provide the anchor: after breakfast , when I arrive at work , after I put the children to bed or when I open my laptop for the day . A practical review of habit formation notes that an existing daily routine can provide a convenient, stable context because it supplies an event that is repeatedly encountered. [PubMed Central (PMC)]"},{"title":"Time of Day","url":"/time-of-day/","description":"The same amount of sleep restriction can produce different vigilance deficits depending on when during the day attention is tested.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Focus","level":3,"text":"Time of Day When Sleep Loss Hits Attention Hardest Sleep and Focus Time of Day Time of Day When Sleep Loss Hits Attention Hardest The same amount of sleep restriction can produce different vigilance deficits depending on when during the day attention is tested. The same amount of sleep restriction can produce different vigilance deficits depending on when during the day attention is tested. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why morning vigilance can deteriorate more sharply How circadian alerting can mask sleep loss effects What timing means for demanding daily tasks self improvement that works sleep and focus time of day Introduction Sleep restriction does not impair attention by the same amount at every hour of the day. The clearest laboratory evidence suggests that, after repeated short nights, sustained attention failures can be much more visible in the morning and substantially less visible in the late afternoon or early evening. In one controlled study, after eight days restricted to four hours of sleep per day, participants averaged 8.3 more psychomotor vigilance test (PVT) lapses at 08:00 than at 18:00 . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Time of day effects on neurobehavioral performance during chronic sleep restriction PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 This matters because an afternoon period of apparently good concentration does not necessarily mean that insufficient sleep has done little harm. Circadian alerting can temporarily support performance even while sleep debt remains. Conversely, the first hours of the morning can expose impairment particularly strongly, especially when a person's biological clock is still promoting sleep. For practical self improvement, the useful question is therefore not simply “How much sleep did I get?” but also “When am I asking my attention to perform?” Why morning vigilance can deteriorate more sharply A particularly informative experiment followed 90 healthy adults aged 21–49 during a 14 day laboratory protocol. Participants were assigned to different sleep schedules providing between 4.2 and 8.2 hours of total daily sleep opportunity, sometimes divided between night time sleep and a daytime nap. During ten days of restricted sleep, researchers administered the PVT every two hours during scheduled wakefulness. The PVT measures sustained attention by recording unusually slow responses, or “lapses”, to unpredictable stimuli. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Time of day effects on neurobehavioral performance during chronic sleep restriction PubMed... The amount of sleep mattered, but so did the clock. With less sleep, deficits accumulated more rapidly across successive days, yet this deterioration was strongest at 08:00. The rate became progressively smaller later in the day and was particularly reduced between 16:00 and 20:00. After eight days at roughly four hours of sleep a day, the difference between 08:00 and 18:00 reached 8.3 additional PVT lapses. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Time of day effects on neurobehavioral performance during chronic sleep restriction PubMed... Crucially, the researchers had excluded tests performed immediately after nocturnal sleep or a nap from their main analysis, specifically to avoid simply measuring sleep inertia — the temporary grogginess immediately after waking. Their analysed morning tests began at 08:00 rather than immediately upon awakening. That strengthens the interpretation that the morning disadvantage was not merely a few minutes of post sleep sluggishness. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. Other work nevertheless shows that sleep inertia can add another layer of morning vulnerability. In a study measuring vigilant attention during partial chronic sleep restriction, PVT lapses were generally highest during the morning session and declined later in the day. Differences between the sleep restricted and control groups were significant in the morning and at midday but not in the afternoon. The investigators considered stronger sleep inertia in the restricted group an important contributor to the early morning result. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Heart Rate Variability for Evaluating Vigilant Attention in Partial Chronic Sleep Restriction PMCJuly 1, 2014... Published: July 1, 2014 So “morning” can contain several overlapping disadvantages: residual sleep inertia soon after waking, accumulated pressure from inadequate prior sleep, and a biological clock that may not yet be supplying its strongest wake promoting signal. Which component dominates depends on precisely when someone wakes, when they are tested and where that wake time falls within their own circadian cycle. 2021. 6. 10. Andrew Philips, Towards individualized predictions of human sleep and circadian timing Channel: IBS Biomedical Mathematics Group Open on YouTube How circadian alerting can mask sleep loss effects Human alertness reflects at least two interacting processes. Homeostatic sleep pressure builds while we are awake and is reduced by sleep. The circadian system , meanwhile, produces a roughly 24 hour rhythm that promotes sleep at some biological times and wakefulness at others. Performance at any particular moment reflects their combined influence rather than sleep duration alone. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 That interaction produces a counter intuitive pattern. After inadequate sleep, someone may perform poorly in the morning but improve during the afternoon even though they have now been awake for longer . A major review of vigilant attention research describes the afternoon as relatively protected during sustained sleep restriction because the circadian drive for wakefulness partially counteracts rising homeostatic sleep pressure. This period is sometimes called the wake maintenance zone . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 The effect is not merely a feature of partial sleep restriction. During prolonged total sleep deprivation, vigilance typically becomes particularly poor during the biological morning and can then improve during the subsequent afternoon despite the person having accumulated still more time awake. This apparent recovery is therefore not evidence that the underlying need for sleep has disappeared. It reflects the changing balance between sleep pressure and circadian support. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 That distinction is important outside the laboratory. A productive spell late in a sleep deprived afternoon can create the impression that a short night “didn't affect me much”. The evidence supports a narrower conclusion: performance at that particular circadian phase may be partially protected . It does not follow that attention would have been equally reliable earlier in the day, overnight, or after additional days of restriction. There are also limits to the afternoon protection idea. Circadian effects vary with the task, the amount of prior wakefulness and the severity of sleep debt. In forced desynchrony experiments — protocols designed to separate biological time from time spent awake — simulated driving deteriorated independently with poor circadian timing, extended wakefulness and reduced sleep. More importantly, those factors interacted: combining sleep debt or long prior wakefulness with the early biological morning produced particularly poor driving performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Simulated driving under the influence of extended wake, time of day and sleep restriction PubMedMarch 1, 2012... Published: March 1, 2012 The circadian boost should therefore be understood as a buffer, not immunity . Even during a relatively favourable biological period, sufficiently severe sleep restriction can still impair performance. Explanatory illustration 2 Clock time is not the same as biological time An 08:00 test does not occur at precisely the same biological time for everyone. People's internal circadian phases differ, so two people who wake at the same clock time may be waking at different points in their biological night to day transition. A study of 43 healthy young adults illustrates why this matters. After two nights of sleep restriction, participants completed a PVT two hours after waking. Researchers also measured dim light melatonin onset (DLMO) , a standard laboratory marker of circadian phase. Later melatonin timing — meaning participants had awakened at an earlier point in their internal circadian cycle — was associated with more PVT lapses, greater subjective sleepiness and more slow eye"},{"title":"One Short Night","url":"/one-short-night/","description":"Even one night with only a few hours of sleep can make sustained attention slower and less reliable the next day.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Focus","level":3,"text":"One Short Night What One Short Night Does to Attention Sleep and Focus One Short Night One Short Night What One Short Night Does to Attention Even one night with only a few hours of sleep can make sustained attention slower and less reliable the next day. Even one night with only a few hours of sleep can make sustained attention slower and less reliable the next day. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic event time window How reaction times change after one restricted night Why attentional lapses matter more than average speed Which mental abilities may remain relatively intact self improvement that works sleep and focus one short night Introduction One short night can make attention measurably less reliable the next day, even when the person has slept rather than stayed awake all night. The clearest evidence comes from a 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of 44 studies in which adults were restricted to roughly two to six hours of sleep opportunity for one night. The next day, sustained attention reaction times were slower and attentional lapses were more frequent. Subjective sleepiness increased even more strongly. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... Explanatory illustration 1 The important word is sustained . A short night does not necessarily produce an obvious collapse across every mental ability. Instead, one of its most reproducible effects is difficulty maintaining consistently prompt attention over minutes of repetitive or monotonous activity. That makes the cost easy to underestimate: much of the time performance can still look competent, while occasional slow responses or missed signals become more common. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Thomas Andrillon Can sleep intrusions in waking explain lapses of attention? Channel: GIGA Consciousness Seminars Open on YouTube How reaction times change after one restricted night Researchers often measure this effect with the psychomotor vigilance test (PVT) . It is deliberately simple: a stimulus appears at unpredictable intervals and the participant responds as quickly as possible. Because there is little reasoning to do, worsening performance is difficult to explain away as failure to understand a complicated task. Researchers can examine overall response speed, the slowest responses and conspicuously delayed responses — commonly those taking more than 500 milliseconds — classified as lapses. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Across the 44 studies included in the 2024 meta analysis, a single restricted night produced a standardised mean difference of 0.512 for slower reaction times on sustained attention tasks and 0.489 for attentional lapses . Both effects were statistically significant. Subjective sleepiness increased more strongly, with a standardised mean difference of 0.986. These standardised effect sizes describe average differences across studies rather than a prediction that every person will slow by a particular number of milliseconds. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... A controlled experiment provides a more concrete illustration. In one study, 24 participants averaged about 7.8 hours of sleep in the normal sleep condition and 5.0 hours after sleep restriction . Following the shorter night, overall PVT performance slowed, the slowest 10% of responses deteriorated, and lapses increased significantly. Yet another measure of brief, transient attention in the same experiment — the “attentional blink” — showed no significant effect of sleep restriction. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The impact of sleep loss on sustained and transient attention: an EEG study PMC... A different experiment involving 61 healthy adults aged 20–29 compared normal sleep with either four hours in bed or a night containing four deliberately prolonged awakenings. Both disrupted conditions increased omission and commission errors on a sustained attention task. The finding is useful because it shows that next day attention can be vulnerable not only when the sleep window is drastically shortened, but also when a night's sleep is substantially interrupted. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Effects of one night of induced night wakings versus sleep restriction on sustained attention and mood: a pilot study Scie... The evidence nevertheless does not support a precise one night dose formula. In the 2024 synthesis, sustained attention impairment was not significantly associated with sleep duration across the two to six hour restriction range. That does not mean that two hours and six hours of sleep are equivalent. It means these studies did not establish a sufficiently clean relationship to justify predicting next day impairment from sleep duration alone. Differences in protocols, testing times and individuals make the response more complicated. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... Effects of Sleep Deprivation on Attention and Mood Channel: The BioClock Studio Open on YouTube Why lapses matter more than average speed An average reaction time can hide the characteristic problem created by insufficient sleep. Sleep loss research has long described state instability : instead of every response becoming uniformly slower, normal looking responses are increasingly mixed with unusually slow responses and failures to respond promptly. In other words, performance becomes less dependable from one moment to the next. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 That distinction changes how a short night's consequences should be interpreted. Imagine that most responses during a monotonous task remain reasonably fast but a handful become dramatically delayed. The average may move only modestly, yet those rare failures could be the responses that matter most if the task involves spotting a road hazard, noticing an alarm, monitoring equipment or catching an error before acting. This is one reason vigilance tests examine the tail of the reaction time distribution rather than reporting only a mean. In the five hour sleep experiment, for example, researchers found deterioration not just in overall PVT speed but specifically in the slowest 10% of responses and in the number of lapses. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The impact of sleep loss on sustained and transient attention: an EEG study PMC... The broader sleep loss literature reinforces this interpretation. Reviews describe vigilant attention impairment as increased moment to moment variability, with exceptionally slow responses interspersed among faster ones. Experiments involving more severe sleep deprivation likewise find that both average reaction time and within person variability rise as sleep pressure increases. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 For self improvement, this makes a short night particularly relevant to tasks that demand consistency rather than occasional peak performance . Being capable of a fast response does not demonstrate that attention is fully restored; the problem is that the fast response may not arrive reliably on every occasion. A person may therefore have a productive looking morning while still being more vulnerable to intermittent attention failures. The same distinction helps explain why monotonous activities deserve particular caution. Driving research has used sustained attention measures precisely because sleepiness and prolonged, repetitive performance can interact. In a counterbalanced study of 47 healthy adults, researchers compared normal sleep with a four hour sleep opportunity and assessed participants using two sustained attention tasks alongside a computer based fitness to drive battery and subjective and physiological measures of sleepiness. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effect of partial sleep deprivation on computer based measures of fitness to drive PubMed... Not every driving related measure necessarily deteriorates after one restricted night. Another simulator experiment found clear increases in subjective sleepiness after four hours of sleep but more limited effects attributable specifically to sleep restriction"},{"title":"Usable Cues","url":"/usable-cues/","description":"A useful habit cue must recur often, be noticeable and usually leave enough opportunity to perform the intended behaviour.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Stable Contexts","level":3,"text":"Usable Cues Choose Habit Cues That Leave Room to Act Stable Contexts Usable Cues Usable Cues Choose Habit Cues That Leave Room to Act A useful habit cue must recur often, be noticeable and usually leave enough opportunity to perform the intended behaviour. A useful habit cue must recur often, be noticeable and usually leave enough opportunity to perform the intended behaviour. Self Improvement That Works mechanism bounded topic mechanism The three qualities of a workable habit cue Why frequent but inconvenient cues create poor practice Testing whether a cue fits the behaviour in real life self improvement that works stable contexts usable cues Introduction A habit cue is useful only if it arrives when the behaviour is genuinely possible. A cue may be frequent and memorable, but if it usually appears while your hands are full, you are travelling, the necessary equipment is elsewhere, or there is no time to act, it repeatedly creates a prompt without a performance. That matters because habits are learned through repeated cue–behaviour pairings, not through repeated reminders alone. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com What is habit and how can it be used to change real‐world behaviour? Narrowing the theory‐reality gap Gardner 2024 Social an... Explanatory illustration 1 For practical habit building, therefore, a workable cue needs three qualities: it should recur often enough to provide practice, be noticeable enough to register, and coincide with a real opportunity to perform the intended action. The third requirement is easy to overlook. Behavioural science distinguishes a prompt from the physical and social opportunity that makes behaviour possible; a good habit design brings the two together. [Health Education England] hee.nhs.uk Health Education EnglandFurther COM B information NHS England Workforce, training and educationSeptember 26, 2022... Published: September 26, 2022 How to get ANYONE to do ANYTHING! (COM B explained) Channel: Pete Judo Open on YouTube The three qualities of a workable habit cue Habit formation depends on learning an association between a recurring context and a response. Repetition strengthens that association, so that encountering the context can eventually trigger an impulse to act with less deliberate decision making. The practical implication is that cue selection should be judged by the number of usable repetitions it is likely to generate, rather than by how clever or memorable the cue sounds. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicHabit Formation and Behavior Change Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Psychology Oxford AcademicApril 26, 2019... Published: April 26, 2019 It recurs often enough. A cue that appears only occasionally offers relatively few chances to practise the cue–behaviour pairing. For an intended daily behaviour, an everyday event such as finishing breakfast may therefore be more useful than an irregular event such as receiving a particular type of email. Frequency is not valuable by itself, however: what matters is repeatedly performing the behaviour when that cue occurs . In a randomised trial of 192 adults forming healthy eating habits, repeated enactment of the planned cue–behaviour link predicted increasing automaticity . Simply doing the desired behaviour at unrelated moments is not the same learning experience. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 It is noticeable. A recurring circumstance cannot reliably guide action if it routinely passes unnoticed. Reviews of habit formation consequently emphasise cues that are salient, perceptible and accessible. Existing events in the flow of daily life can work well because their occurrence is already apparent: finishing lunch, putting away a toothbrush or arriving home provides a clearer transition than an indistinct intention such as “sometime this afternoon”. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Applying the Science of Habit Formation to Evidence Based Psychological Treatments for Mental Illness PMCMarch 1, 2022... Published: March 1, 2022 Implementation intention research supports the underlying mechanism: specifying a future situation in an if–then plan can make that situation easier to identify when it occurs. Experiments by Thomas Webb and Paschal Sheeran found faster or more accurate detection of specified cues without corresponding costs for irrelevant or ambiguous stimuli. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. It leaves room to act. This is the crucial filter. Opportunity in the COM B model of behaviour refers to external circumstances that make an action possible or prompt it; physical opportunity includes such basics as time, resources and an accessible location. [Health Education England] hee.nhs.uk Health Education EnglandFurther COM B information NHS England Workforce, training and educationSeptember 26, 2022... Published: September 26, 2022 A cue can therefore be excellent as a reminder but poor as a habit building context. “When I get on the train, I will practise the piano” is unmistakable and frequent but usually unusable. “After I put my bag down at home, I will practise for five minutes” is more promising because cue and opportunity coincide. This distinction also prevents a common design error: treating a notification as though it were the habit itself. A phone can reliably announce 6 pm, but the notification does not create the privacy needed to meditate, the trainers needed to run or the kitchen needed to prepare food. In a four week study of technology supported habit formation, reminders supported repetition but reliance on reminders did not support habit development as well as event based cues; the authors argued that apps should connect behaviour more closely to contextual cues rather than simply issuing alerts. [University of Bristol] research information.bris.ac.uk Open source on bris.ac.uk. Boost motivation (and ability) by starting tiny Channel: Dr. BJ Fogg Open on YouTube Why frequent but inconvenient cues create poor practice Imagine trying to establish a ten minute walk using “when I finish lunch” as the cue. On paper it looks excellent: lunch happens daily, its ending is obvious, and the cue is naturally tied to an existing routine. But suppose lunch usually ends five minutes before the next meeting. The cue occurs perfectly while the intended behaviour is regularly impossible. The problem is not primarily weak motivation . The design has separated the signal to act from the opportunity to complete the response. Habit formation requires cue linked behavioural repetitions; if the cue repeatedly occurs without the planned response, the learner loses precisely the practice that is supposed to establish the association. Research tracking real world habit formation accordingly treats changes in motivation, capability or opportunity as prerequisites for the early performances from which habits can develop. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. This is why “choose something that happens every day” is incomplete advice. A cue can score highly on frequency yet produce few usable repetitions: The cue arrives during a competing obligation. A 9 am stretching cue is weak in practice if 9 am usually means joining a meeting. The required resources are elsewhere. Arriving at work cannot reliably cue taking a medicine that is normally left at home. The setting restricts the action. Finishing a commute may be predictable, but it is unsuitable for an action requiring privacy or specialised equipment. The available window is shorter than the behaviour. A predictable five minute gap cannot consistently support a 30 minute routine. Other people control the opportunity. A cue may recur reliably while access to a shared room, equipment, childcare or social permission varies. These are not minor inconveniences. Opportunity is one of the conditions required for behaviour in the COM B framework, alongside capability and motivation. Research applying the model in real settings repeatedly identifies time, resources, accessible locations and social circumstances as factors that determine whether an intended action is feasible. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com A deductive framework analysis using the Capability, Opportunity, Motivation and Behaviour (COM‐B) behavioural science model Stocker... There is also an important difference between a cue triggering an impulse and the behaviour actually happening. Contemporary habit theory does not require the cue to force behaviour mechanically; rather, a learned cue–behaviour association can activate an impulse to act. Whether that impulse becomes behaviour still depends on circumstances and competing responses. Gardner's 2024 review gives the converse example: adding enough friction to an unwanted behaviour can make it"},{"title":"Use Friction","url":"/use-friction/","description":"Adding or removing a few steps can shift behavior by changing how easy an action is at the moment of choice.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","level":3,"text":"Use Friction How a Few Extra Steps Can Change Behavior The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more Willpower” Use Friction Use Friction How a Few Extra Steps Can Change Behavior Adding or removing a few steps can shift behavior by changing how easy an action is at the moment of choice. Adding or removing a few steps can shift behavior by changing how easy an action is at the moment of choice. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention What behavioral friction means The workplace snack station example Where friction helps and where its effects fade self improvement that works willpower myths use friction Introduction A habit can become easier or harder without changing the person’s motivation at all. Sometimes the decisive change is mundane: putting an object within reach, moving food a few metres away, removing a login step, preparing equipment in advance, or inserting a short delay before an impulsive action. Behavioural researchers often describe these small physical and procedural obstacles as friction costs . They matter because intentions have to survive the practical effort required at the moment of action. The OECD notes that even minor obstacles can encourage delay and inertia, while reducing clicks, questions or other required actions can increase follow through. [OECD] oecd.org component 6 The BASIC Manual: Tools and Ethics for Applied Behavioural Insights: The BASIC Toolkit OECDJune 18, 2019... Published: June 18, 2019 Explanatory illustration 1 This is one reason effective self improvement need not depend on repeatedly winning battles of willpower. Instead of asking only, “How do I become more disciplined?”, it can be useful to ask a narrower implementation question: Which behaviour is currently easiest to perform, and can a few steps be moved from the behaviour I want to the behaviour I do not? What behavioural friction means Friction is the effort separating an intention from an action. In its most literal form it is physical: standing up, walking across a room, opening a cupboard or retrieving something from another floor. But the same principle can apply procedurally. Finding a password, navigating several screens, completing another form or waiting through a delay all make an action slightly more troublesome. The important point is that friction need not make an action impossible. It changes its relative convenience at the moment of choice . The UK Behavioural Insights Team’s EAST framework therefore recommends reducing the “hassle factor” when trying to encourage behaviour. Its examples include sending taxpayers directly to the relevant form rather than making them navigate through an intermediate webpage. The OECD similarly recommends examining the actual sequence people must follow and reducing unnecessary actions, clicks and questions. [Behaviour Change] behaviourchange.hee.nhs.uk Behaviour Change EASY TIMELY ATTRACTIVE SOCIAL EAST Four simple wayApril 14, 2014... Published: April 14, 2014 For personal habits, this suggests a practical distinction between two kinds of environmental change: Reduce friction for the behaviour you want. Put running shoes where they are encountered before a planned run; keep the document you need to work on readily accessible; prepare ingredients before the point when cooking competes with ordering food. Add modest friction to the behaviour you want less of. Put tempting food somewhere that requires getting up; keep a distracting device out of immediate reach; log out of an attention grabbing service rather than leaving it continuously available. These interventions are deliberately small. Their purpose is not to create an impregnable barrier. It is to alter what happens during the brief interval in which an intention either becomes behaviour or disappears. That distinction also prevents “friction” becoming a vague synonym for every influence on behaviour. A motivational speech may change how much somebody wants to exercise ; a reminder may make the intention salient; a social commitment may create accountability. Friction specifically concerns how much effort or inconvenience lies on the path to acting. Overview of the BASIC and the EAST Frameworks Channel: Applying Behavioural Insights In Public Policy Open on YouTube A few metres changed workplace snacking One unusually concrete illustration comes from a 2016 field study conducted in a heavily used micro kitchen at Google’s New York office. Employees could obtain drinks from two otherwise identical beverage stations. One was about 6 feet 5 inches (roughly 2 metres) from a snack station; the other was 17 feet 6 inches (roughly 5.3 metres) away. The snacks remained available either way. The researchers were testing whether those additional metres were enough to influence what employees did. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Proximity of snacks to beverages increases food consumption in the workplace: A field study ScienceDirectAugust 1, 2016... Published: August 1, 2016 They were. Among men, the observed likelihood of taking a snack was 23% when they used the nearer beverage station versus 12% at the farther station. Among women, it was 17% versus 13%. Nothing had been banned, nobody had to pay more and the distant station was hardly an expedition. The relevant environmental difference was simply that pairing a drink with a snack required a little more movement. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Proximity of snacks to beverages increases food consumption in the workplace: A field study ScienceDirectAugust 1, 2016... Published: August 1, 2016 That makes the study useful for understanding friction rather than merely workplace nutrition. An additional eleven feet or so seems trivial when considered abstractly. At the exact moment when somebody already has their drink, however, those feet separate an effortless add on from a separate action. The intervention therefore works upstream of a classic self control struggle: fewer people arrive at the point of physically taking the snack. The finding is consistent with a broader experimental literature on food proximity. A 2019 Cochrane review identified 18 randomised studies manipulating proximity. In a meta analysis of 15 comparisons from 12 studies involving 1,098 participants, placing food farther away was associated with moderately lower consumption. Greater distances tended to produce larger effects. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Altering the availability or proximity of food, alcohol, and tobacco products to change their selection and consumption Cochran... That broader evidence matters because a memorable workplace experiment can otherwise invite an overly tidy conclusion. The Cochrane authors rated the certainty of the evidence for the consumption result as low , noting risk of bias concerns, the predominance of laboratory research and a need for better long term real world studies. The safer conclusion is therefore not that moving something a precise number of metres reliably produces a particular percentage reduction. It is that proximity is capable of changing consumption, while the size and durability of that effect depend on context . [Cochrane] cochrane.org Altering the availability or proximity of food, alcohol, and tobacco products to change their selection and consumption Cochran... Explanatory illustration 2 Friction works beyond physical distance The same implementation logic can operate in digital environments. A distracting action may require virtually no physical movement, yet designers can still change the number of moments separating impulse from consumption. A preregistered field experiment involving 112 participants compared strategies for reducing smartphone screen time. A design friction intervention using greyscale produced an immediate significant reduction in objectively measured screen time relative to a self monitoring control, whereas goal setting produced a smaller, more gradual reduction. The study did not find corresponding immediate improvements in subjective wellbeing or academic performance, which is an important boundary: changing a behaviour is not automatically the same thing as producing every hoped for downstream benefit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Digital Strategies for Screen Time Reduction: A Randomized Field Experiment PubMedDecember 26, 2022... Published: December 26, 2022 Research on the self nudge app One Sec provides another example of inserting a break into digital consumption. Experimental work testing features including a delay before content consumption found that time based friction reduced consumption behaviour; importantly, offering users an explicit opportunity to dismiss the content was particularly effective. A message alone was not. The result fits the broader implementation principle: changing the action sequence can matter more than simply telling somebody what they ought to do. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Directing smartphone use through the"},{"title":"Temptation Exposure","url":"/temptation-exposure/","description":"Successful restraint depends partly on which temptations appear, how often they appear, and what other people are doing around us.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","level":3,"text":"Temptation Exposure Self Control Depends on What You Keep Encountering The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more... Temptation Exposure Temptation Exposure Self Control Depends on What You Keep Encountering Successful restraint depends partly on which temptations appear, how often they appear, and what other people are doing around us. Successful restraint depends partly on which temptations appear, how often they appear, and what other people are doing around us. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic population context Why exposure matters alongside personal capacity What daily life desire studies reveal Reducing repeated encounters with the same temptation self improvement that works willpower myths temptation exposure Introduction Self control does not operate in a vacuum. Whether a person sticks to a goal depends partly on what they have to resist in the first place: how often a temptation appears, how visible and accessible it is, and whether the people around them make indulging seem normal. This changes the usual picture of willpower. Two people with similar intentions can face very different self control problems because one encounters a tempting cue once while the other encounters it repeatedly. Explanatory illustration 1 Daily life research supports that distinction. Temptations are common, but their consequences vary with the situation, and people who are effective at self control appear to do more than simply win difficult internal battles. They also avoid or alter situations that generate those battles. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Everyday temptations: an experience sampling study of desire, conflict, and self control PubMed... For self improvement, the implication is practical rather than fatalistic: reducing unnecessary encounters with temptation can be a form of self control itself. A system that makes the unwanted option less frequent, less salient or less socially reinforced can reduce the number of moments in which willpower has to rescue the goal. Why exposure matters alongside personal capacity Imagine two people trying to concentrate for an hour. One leaves their phone in another room. The other keeps it beside the keyboard, sees incoming notifications and repeatedly notices the possibility of checking messages. If both eventually complete the work, the second person has had to negotiate more potential conflicts to achieve the same result. That distinction between resisting temptation and reducing exposure to temptation is central to contemporary models of self control. Duckworth, Gendler and Gross's process model argues that people can intervene at different points as an impulse develops. They can select a different situation, modify the situation they are already in, redirect attention, reinterpret the temptation or, at the final stage, inhibit their response. Earlier strategies can be valuable precisely because they prevent a strong impulse from developing. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 This makes exposure an important part of the denominator in everyday self control. A person who successfully resists nine temptations out of ten still experiences one lapse; somebody whose environment produces only two comparable temptations may need far less successful inhibition to achieve the same or better result. The numbers are illustrative, but the principle matters: behavioural outcomes reflect both what happens when temptation occurs and how often a person encounters it. Research on trait self control fits this interpretation. Michael Ent, Roy Baumeister and Dianne Tice found that people higher in trait self control reported more behaviours aimed at minimising temptation. In experimental studies, they were also more likely to choose a distraction free working environment over a more distracting alternative. High self control, in other words, was associated not simply with greater resistance but with greater avoidance of situations requiring resistance. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Trait self control and the avoidance of temptation Trait self control and the avoidance of temptation ScienceDirect... This helps explain an initially counter intuitive observation from experience sampling research: people high in trait self control do not necessarily report constantly exercising more moment to moment resistance. A review of situational self control research notes that they instead tend to encounter fewer problematic desires. What looks from the outside like effortless discipline may therefore partly reflect better positioning before the decisive moment arrives. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PubMed Central (PMC)Situational Strategies for Self Control PMCJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 How to Break Unwanted Habits and How to Create New Desired Ones with Adam Grant and Wendy Wood Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube What daily life desire studies reveal Laboratory tasks can isolate self control mechanisms, but everyday life poses a different question: what actually happens as people move through ordinary environments? A landmark experience sampling study by Wilhelm Hofmann and colleagues followed 205 adults for a week. Participants responded to prompts about their desires, eventually producing 7,827 reports. The study found that desire was a recurring feature of daily life and varied considerably in strength, conflict with other goals, resistance and enactment. Crucially for understanding temptation exposure, outcomes were associated not just with personality but with characteristics of the immediate situation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Everyday temptations: an experience sampling study of desire, conflict, and self control PubMed... The social findings are particularly revealing. The mere presence of other people generally inhibited the enactment of desires. But when those other people were already doing the thing the participant wanted to do — effectively modelling the tempting behaviour — resistance was lower and enactment became more likely. Thus, “being around people” was not a uniform influence. What those people were doing changed the self control context. [Carlson School of Management] carlsonschool.umn.edu Carlson School of Management Journal of Personality and Social Psychology Carlson School of ManagementJournal of Personality and Social PsychologyAugust 19, 2024... Published: August 19, 2024 More recent research strengthens the case that social context can affect temptation before a person reaches the stage of deliberate resistance. Across five main studies with 5,488 participants, researchers examined how social norms shape which behaviours come to mind. In one study, people trying to eat more healthily judged the same unhealthy foods as less tempting when eating them would violate the norm of the situation. In a classroom field study, establishing a norm against laptop use reduced both temptation to multitask and actual multitasking over the term; simply encouraging students to exercise individual self control did not produce the same result. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Social norms govern what behaviors come to mind And what do not Social norms govern what behaviors come to mind And what do not PubMedNovember 17, 2022... Published: November 17, 2022 That is a deeper point than saying that peer pressure matters. A context can change the subjective problem itself. If an option feels abnormal or is barely considered, there may be less temptation to overcome. If everyone around you is doing it, the same action can become more psychologically available and easier to justify. Playing the Long Game: The Power of Self Control Channel: Charles Schwab Open on YouTube Repeated temptation can matter more than repeated resistance Another strand of daily life evidence challenges the assumption that the important variable is how hard somebody fights once a temptation has appeared. Marina Milyavskaya and Michael Inzlicht followed university students using experience sampling, nightly reports and longer term measures of progress towards personally chosen goals. Their results linked goal attainment to the experience of temptation rather than to the amount of effortful self control participants reported exerting. Experiencing more temptation was also associated with greater feelings of depletion, which in turn predicted poorer goal attainment. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals What’s So Great About Self Control? Examining the Importance of Effortful Self Control and Temptation in Predicting Real Life Depletion and Goal Attainment Marina Milyavsk... The study does not prove that resisting temptation is useless. In a particular moment, resistance can plainly"},{"title":"Control Test","url":"/control-test/","description":"Strong behaviour goals can be performed deliberately, verified clearly and repeated in a way that plausibly helps the outcome.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Behaviour Goals","level":3,"text":"Control Test Is Your Goal Really Under Your Control? Behaviour Goals Control Test Control Test Is Your Goal Really Under Your Control? Strong behaviour goals can be performed deliberately, verified clearly and repeated in a way that plausibly helps the outcome. Strong behaviour goals can be performed deliberately, verified clearly and repeated in a way that plausibly helps the outcome. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism The deliberate action test The clear yes or no completion test The usefulness test for productive looking habits self improvement that works behaviour goals control test Introduction A behaviour goal is truly controllable when you can deliberately perform the action, tell clearly whether you performed it, and reasonably expect repeated performance to help the outcome you care about . Specificity and measurability are not enough. “Get seven hours of sleep”, “feel confident in meetings” and “receive three job offers” are all measurable, but none is fully produced by an act of will. By contrast, “switch off screens at 10.30 pm”, “ask one prepared question in the meeting” and “submit four tailored applications this week” describe actions you can actually initiate. Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters because goal setting directs attention and effort. A poorly chosen target can therefore make you diligently pursue something you cannot control — or become extremely consistent at an activity that contributes little to the result. Research on goal setting, implementation intentions and progress monitoring supports moving the target towards observable action, while research on poorly specified targets shows why the right thing must be measured, not merely something easy to count. [DOI] doi.org The Intention–Behavior Gap The Intention–Behavior Gap Sheeran 2016 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online LibrarySeptember 5, 2016... Published: September 5, 2016 The deliberate action test The first test is simple: could you perform the goal deliberately, on command, when the relevant opportunity arrives? This separates an action from an outcome, reaction or internal state. Consider these pairs: “Fall asleep by 11 pm” → “Get into bed at 10.45 pm with my phone outside the bedroom.” You can create conditions favourable to sleep; you cannot directly command sleep to begin. “Be confident during presentations” → “Deliver my opening paragraph without rushing and make eye contact with three parts of the room.” Confidence may follow, but it is not itself a reliable voluntary action. “Make my manager recognise my contribution” → “Send a Friday update summarising completed work, decisions and blockers.” Your communication is controllable; your manager's judgement is not. “Get an interview” → “Submit one carefully tailored application to a suitable vacancy.” An employer controls the invitation. “Run a personal best” → “Complete the planned interval session at the prescribed effort.” Training behaviour is directly available to you; race day performance also depends on conditions, health and other variables. This does not mean outcomes are irrelevant. They tell you where you want the behaviour to lead. The mistake is treating an outcome as though it were the action itself. The distinction fits the broader evidence on the intention–behaviour gap: wanting a result does not guarantee that the required behaviour occurs. Implementation intentions address precisely this problem by linking a recognisable situation with a specified response — in effect, defining what the person will do when the opportunity appears. A major 2006 meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect of implementation intentions on goal attainment, while a much larger 2024 synthesis of 642 tests found benefits across behavioural, cognitive and affective outcomes, with stronger effects for genuinely contingent if–then plans. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... There is an important qualification. “Under your control” should not be interpreted as “guaranteed to happen”. Illness, emergencies, caring responsibilities, equipment failure and other constraints can prevent even a well defined behaviour. The useful distinction is direct agency rather than certainty . “Walk for 20 minutes after lunch” passes because walking is an action you can normally initiate. It does not fail the test merely because a sprained ankle could occasionally make it impossible. A useful diagnostic question is therefore: If the outcome went badly despite my doing exactly what this goal says, could I still truthfully say that I completed the goal? If the answer is no, the wording probably still contains an outcome. You can complete four applications and receive no interviews. You can practise piano for 30 focused minutes and still play badly at tomorrow's recital. You can prepare carefully for a difficult conversation and still receive an unfavourable response. A controllable behaviour goal separates execution , which you can judge immediately, from effectiveness , which has to be evaluated over time. Process Goals VS Outcome Goals: How to Set Goals You Can Actually Achieve Channel: Develop Good Habits Open on YouTube The clear yes or no completion test A behaviour can be voluntary yet still be too vague to regulate action. “Work on my dissertation”, “eat better”, “be more organised” and “practise properly” all describe desirable directions, but they leave enough interpretive room for the standard to change after the event. The second test is therefore: at the end of the relevant period, can a reasonable observer answer yes or no about whether the behaviour occurred? “Study hard tonight” fails. “Complete and mark 20 retrieval practice questions before 8 pm” passes. “Network more” fails. “Send one personalised message to a professional contact on Tuesday” passes. “Spend less online” is ambiguous; “wait 24 hours before completing any non essential online purchase above £30” gives a much cleaner completion rule. That clarity is valuable partly because self regulation depends on feedback . The American Psychological Association's summary of goal setting theory notes that goals focus activity and that feedback about performance or progress is important to their effectiveness. More directly, a meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring increased both monitoring and goal attainment; effects on attainment were larger when progress was physically recorded or reported publicly. [APA Dictionary] dictionary.apa.org Dictionary APA Dictionary of Psychology APA DictionaryAPA Dictionary of PsychologyApril 19, 2018... Published: April 19, 2018 A binary completion rule does not , however, mean reducing every activity to a crude number. This is where popular advice about making goals “SMART” can mislead. A review of SMART goals in physical activity concluded that the familiar acronym has much weaker scientific foundations than its ubiquity suggests and, importantly, does not adequately distinguish between different types of goals. The review also found that empirical evidence does not support treating every element of the acronym as universally necessary. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The (over)use of SMART goals for physical activity promotion: A narrative review and critique PubMedJanuary 31, 2022... Published: January 31, 2022 The control test is asking something more fundamental than “Is it measurable?” It asks what the measurement represents . “Read 30 pages” is beautifully measurable, for example, but it does not establish that you understood anything. If understanding matters, a better completion criterion might be “read the section and answer five retrieval questions without looking at the text”. Similarly, “write 1,000 words” may be useful during a rough first draft but potentially counterproductive during final editing, when deleting 500 unnecessary words could be better work. The completion rule should therefore be precise enough to prevent self deception without becoming so crude that the metric replaces the purpose. Explanatory illustration 2 The usefulness test The hardest test comes after controllability and clarity: if you repeatedly completed this behaviour, is there a credible reason to expect it to improve the desired outcome? This catches the most deceptive failure mode because low value behaviours often feel productive. They can be scheduled, measured and ticked off with satisfying precision. Suppose someone wants to improve an examination result. All of these are under their control: spend 45 minutes colour coding notes; reorganise the revision timetable; watch three study advice videos; complete and mark a set of practice questions; retrieve key ideas from memory and check errors afterwards. Controllability"},{"title":"Goal Metrics","url":"/goal-metrics/","description":"What you choose to count can change how you perform, so a convenient metric should not reward speed or volume at the expense of quality.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Behaviour Goals","level":3,"text":"Goal Metrics Are You Measuring the Wrong Behaviour? Behaviour Goals Goal Metrics Goal Metrics Are You Measuring the Wrong Behaviour? What you choose to count can change how you perform, so a convenient metric should not reward speed or volume at the expense of quality. What you choose to count can change how you perform, so a convenient metric should not reward speed or volume at the expense of quality. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence How goals direct attention during performance What speed versus quality experiments reveal Choosing measures that match the result you value self improvement that works behaviour goals goal metrics Introduction The easiest behaviour to count is not always the behaviour you should optimise. A target such as “write 1,000 words”, “answer 40 questions” or “finish the workout in 30 minutes” can make action concrete, but it also tells you what deserves attention. If the number rewards output while ignoring accuracy, technique or usefulness, you can become better at hitting the metric while becoming worse at the performance the metric was meant to improve. Explanatory illustration 1 That is not an argument against measurable behaviour goals . Goal setting has a small but reliable positive effect on behaviour, and monitoring progress can improve goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... The important qualification is that measurement is part of the intervention . A useful behaviour metric therefore needs two properties: it must be easy enough to guide action, and it must preserve the quality that makes the action worthwhile. How goals direct attention during performance Goals do more than record what happened afterwards. Goal setting theory proposes that they direct attention and action towards goal relevant activities, increase effort and persistence, and encourage the development of strategies for reaching the target. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Work Motivation and Satisfaction: Light at the End of the Tunnel Sage JournalsWork Motivation and Satisfaction: Light at the End of the Tunnel Edwin A. Locke, Gary P. Latham, 1990July 1, 1990... Published: July 1, 1990 This is one reason specific goals can outperform vague instructions such as “do your best”. It is also the reason specificity can have unintended effects. Suppose two people are practising the same skill. One aims to “complete 30 repetitions”; the other aims to “complete 20 correct repetitions using the required technique”. The first target makes throughput conspicuous. The second makes acceptable performance part of what counts. Both are measurable behaviour goals, but they define success differently. This quantity–quality problem has appeared in experimental research for decades. In six experiments involving 338 undergraduates, Janet Bavelas and Eric Lee examined tasks in which participants could vary both how much they produced and the quality of what they produced. On several tasks, higher quantitative goals generated more responses but responses further from the defined ideal. The researchers argued that the goal helped define the task for participants, producing a systematic trade off between quantity and quality rather than simply increasing effort indiscriminately. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Effects of goal level on performance: A trade off of quantity and qualityDecember 1, 1978... Published: December 1, 1978 Research on more complex decisions points in the same general direction while making the picture less tidy. In an experiment involving 160 students making simulated investment decisions, Stephen Gilliland and Ronald Landis manipulated quantity and quality goals and found effects not only on performance but also on participants' information search strategies. [University of Arizona] experts.arizona.edu University of ArizonaQuality and Quantity Goals in a Complex Decision Task: Strategies and Outcomes University of ArizonaOctober 1, 1992... Published: October 1, 1992 The implication is important for self improvement: a metric can change the route you take through a task, not merely the final number you record . This is why a seemingly innocent target can misfire. “Read 50 pages” can encourage moving through pages rather than understanding an argument. “Clear 30 emails” can privilege easy messages over important difficult ones. “Do 100 practice questions” can reward guessing quickly unless checking mistakes is included. In each case, the problem is not measurement itself. It is that the chosen measure represents only one dimension of good performance. Why the secret to success is setting the right goals John Doerr TED Channel: TED Open on YouTube What speed versus quality experiments reveal A particularly clear demonstration comes from a randomised experiment by David Cook and colleagues involving 80 secondary school students learning a simulated surgical procedure. Participants repeatedly tied ligatures around a model blood vessel. After four repetitions, they were randomly assigned either a speed goal or a quality goal , and researchers measured both completion time and whether the resulting ligatures were leak free. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Speed and quality goals in procedural skills learning: A randomized experiment PubMedOctober 9, 2019... Published: October 9, 2019 The goal changed what improved. By the final repetition, the speed goal group was 18% faster than the quality goal group. But its odds of producing a high quality, leak free ligature were substantially lower: the reported odds ratio was 0.36. Even when performance time was taken into account, participants given the speed goal had higher odds of leaks after the intervention. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Speed and quality goals in procedural skills learning: A randomized experiment PubMedOctober 9, 2019... Published: October 9, 2019 That experiment is unusually useful because the metric and the neglected quality dimension were both directly observable. A stopwatch could truthfully report that someone had become faster while the leak test simultaneously showed that the product had become worse. The speed number was not inaccurate; it was incomplete . The lesson should not be exaggerated into “speed goals are bad”. Another randomised study of laparoscopic skills training found benefits from achievable time goals among students. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. And experimental warehouse research involving 212 participants found that quantity feedback could improve productivity without necessarily reducing quality; in a robustness experiment, quantity feedback was associated with improvements in both. [Tilburg University Research Portal] research.tilburguniversity.edu Open source on tilburguniversity.edu. So there is no universal law saying that increasing quantity or speed must reduce quality. The risk depends on the task, the person's skill, the room available for shortcuts and whether quality is independently protected. The surgical ligature experiment demonstrates the danger particularly clearly, rather than proving that every productivity metric creates the same trade off. This distinction matters when choosing personal goals. If faster performance can be achieved only after the underlying skill is secure, speed may be a sensible later metric. If rushing creates errors, injuries, shallow learning or rework, speed is a poor primary target. A metric should reflect the current bottleneck in performance, not merely the dimension that produces the neatest graph. Explanatory illustration 2 When a useful measure becomes the wrong target The wider problem is sometimes described through Goodhart's law : once a measure is placed under strong pressure as a target, the relationship between that measure and the broader result it was supposed to represent can deteriorate. The idea originated in economic policy but has since been applied to areas such as education and healthcare assessment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. At an organisational level, research on performance measurement documents familiar failure modes: concentrating on easy cases, under reporting problems, “teaching to the test”, increasing quantity while reducing quality and otherwise improving the reported indicator without equivalent improvement in the underlying objective. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com Open source on oup.com. Analyses of target driven public services have similarly warned against allowing a measurable part of performance to stand in for the whole. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. Personal behaviour tracking is usually much lower stakes, but the underlying design problem is recognisable. A person can optimise a proxy: counting books finished rather than ideas understood; counting study hours rather than successful retrieval; counting"},{"title":"Coping Plans","url":"/coping-plans/","description":"When the same obstacle repeatedly derails a goal, a preplanned fallback can preserve action without relying on fresh willpower.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Behaviour Goals","level":3,"text":"Coping Plans What Will You Do When the Plan Breaks? Behaviour Goals Coping Plans Coping Plans What Will You Do When the Plan Breaks? When the same obstacle repeatedly derails a goal, a preplanned fallback can preserve action without relying on fresh willpower. When the same obstacle repeatedly derails a goal, a preplanned fallback can preserve action without relying on fresh willpower. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Action planning versus coping planning How to identify the obstacle that usually wins Designing realistic fallback behaviours self improvement that works behaviour goals coping plans Introduction A behaviour goal works best when it survives ordinary disruption. If the same obstacle repeatedly defeats a useful action — rain cancels the walk, a late meeting wipes out the study session, tiredness turns cooking into takeaway, or an interruption consumes the only planned work period — the answer need not be another burst of motivation. A coping plan decides in advance what to do when that predictable barrier appears. Explanatory illustration 1 The basic structure is simple: “If this obstacle occurs, then I will use this fallback.” Research distinguishes this from an action plan, which specifies when, where and how the intended behaviour will happen. Coping planning instead anticipates threats to that plan and prepares a response. Studies suggest that the technique can help, particularly when people receive support in making useful plans, although effects vary substantially across settings. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAction planning and coping planning for long‐term lifestyle change: theory and assessment Sniehotta 2005 Europe... The practical lesson is not to prepare for everything that might go wrong. Identify the obstacle that actually tends to win, then design a fallback realistic enough to use on a difficult day. Action planning versus coping planning An action plan answers: How will I do the behaviour when things go normally? A coping plan answers: What will I do when the normal plan becomes difficult or impossible? In the foundational work separating the two, action planning specified when, where and how to exercise , whereas coping planning involved mentally anticipating barriers and ways of overcoming them. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAction planning and coping planning for long‐term lifestyle change: theory and assessment Sniehotta 2005 Europe... Consider a goal of exercising three evenings a week: Behaviour goal: exercise three times this week. Action plan: on Monday, Wednesday and Friday at 18:00, go for a 30 minute run from home. Coping plan for weather: if heavy rain makes the run impractical, do the 20 minute indoor workout instead. Coping plan for a late meeting: if work runs beyond 18:30, take a 20 minute walk after dinner rather than cancelling the session. Coping plan for low energy: if I am too tired for the planned 30 minutes, start the 10 minute version and allow myself to stop after it. The distinction matters because the two forms of planning solve different problems. Action planning helps turn a general intention into an executable behaviour. Coping planning protects that behaviour once circumstances start interfering. Research with cardiac rehabilitation patients found action planning was more influential earlier in behaviour change, while coping planning became more important later; greater coping planning after discharge was associated with more exercise at four months. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAction planning and coping planning for long‐term lifestyle change: theory and assessment Sniehotta 2005 Europe... A randomised cardiac rehabilitation study provides a more direct test. Patients received standard care, action planning alone, or action planning combined with coping planning. Two months after discharge, the combined planning group reported significantly more exercise than either of the other groups. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubAction plans and coping plans for physical exercise: A longitudinal intervention study in cardiac rehabilitation Sniehotta... Another rehabilitation study involving 373 adults found evidence of a delayed role for coping planning, consistent with the idea that dealing with barriers becomes especially relevant to maintaining a behaviour after the initial start. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Adoption and maintenance of physical activity: Planning interventions in young, middle aged, and older adults PubMed... This makes coping plans particularly relevant to a familiar self improvement puzzle: “I know how to do this and I genuinely intend to do it, so why do I keep stopping?” Once initiation is no longer the main problem, recurring friction may be. The WOOP process Mental contrasting Channel: Hannah Louise Open on YouTube Find the obstacle that usually wins Good coping planning starts with diagnosis, not creativity. The useful question is not “What could conceivably go wrong?” but “What has actually stopped this behaviour before?” Behaviour change frameworks treat barrier identification as a specific process: identify an obstacle to performing the intended behaviour and plan a way of overcoming it. The Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy developed through University College London distinguishes these techniques precisely so that interventions can specify what people are actually being asked to do rather than simply telling them to “plan better”. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryThe Behavior Change Technique Taxonomy (v1) of 93 Hierarchically Clustered Techniques: Building an International Consensus f... Look backwards before looking forwards. Take the last several occasions on which the behaviour was supposed to happen but did not. What happened immediately beforehand? The pattern is often more concrete than the explanation people initially give themselves. “I lack discipline”, for example, is difficult to plan around. “When I arrive home hungry at 19:30, I abandon the planned meal and order food” is actionable. So is “when my first meeting overruns, I never return to the morning writing session” or “when it rains, I skip the walk because I have not decided what counts as a substitute”. Barriers can come from several places: competing demands, environmental conditions, physical states, thoughts or emotions, and other people. Earlier behaviour change classifications explicitly included behavioural, cognitive, emotional, environmental, social and physical barriers. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Standardising Behaviour Change Intervention Reports and ManualsJanuary 1, 2008... Published: January 1, 2008 The important distinction is whether the barrier is sufficiently predictable to justify a prepared response. A useful diagnostic sequence is: 1. Name the planned behaviour precisely. “Study” is too broad; “complete 20 retrieval practice questions after dinner” is easier to troubleshoot. 2. Review recent failures. Identify what was happening when the behaviour was missed, shortened or postponed. 3. Separate recurring barriers from one offs. A once a year emergency does not need a standing coping plan. A meeting that overruns twice a week probably does. 4. Describe the barrier as an observable situation. Replace “I lose motivation” with something such as “after a poor night's sleep, I decide at 06:30 that I am too tired to train”. 5. Start with the barrier responsible for the most failures. One robust fallback for a common problem is generally more useful than a catalogue of elaborate responses to unlikely ones. Digital behaviour change interventions have used much the same process. In the MyPlan programme, participants first identified difficult situations they expected during the coming week and then selected or generated a solution relevant to that particular barrier. One example was an unusually busy working week creating a perceived lack of time for physical activity . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Which behaviour change techniques are effective to promote physical activity and reduce sedentary behaviour in adults... That specificity prevents coping planning from deteriorating into positive thinking. “I will try harder when things get difficult” does not remove a decision. “If my lunchtime meeting overruns, I will take the 15 minute walking route home” does. Explanatory illustration 2 Design a fallback you will actually use The best fallback is not necessarily an equally demanding replacement for the original behaviour. Its job is to preserve useful action under worse conditions. This point is easy to miss. Suppose the normal plan is an hour at the gym. A fallback of “go to a different gym for an hour” solves only a narrow location problem. It does nothing when the real barrier is insufficient time or"},{"title":"Shopping Defaults","url":"/shopping-defaults/","description":"Preselected options can steer shoppers toward costlier choices even when they never actively decide that the upgrade is better.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Design Around Temptation","level":3,"text":"Shopping Defaults How Preselected Options Quietly Change What You Buy Design Around Temptation Shopping Defaults Shopping Defaults How Preselected Options Quietly Change What You Buy Preselected options can steer shoppers toward costlier choices even when they never actively decide that the upgrade is better. Preselected options can steer shoppers toward costlier choices even when they never actively decide that the upgrade is better. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the UK online shopping experiment found Why preselection changes consumer decisions How to review defaults before completing a purchase self improvement that works design around temptation shopping defaults Introduction A preselected shopping option can change what people buy even when they never consciously decide that the preselected choice is better. In a large UK government commissioned experiment, shoppers shown a more expensive option already selected were about 60% more likely to choose the costlier protection plan and 70% more likely to choose the costlier shipping option than shoppers required to make an active choice. The same experiment found that people moved through the shopping journey faster when an option was preselected. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture Explanatory illustration 1 That makes shopping defaults a useful example of environment design in self improvement. The practical lesson is not that online shops are universally manipulating customers: most defaults observed in the accompanying UK survey actually pointed towards cheaper or similarly priced choices. Rather, the starting state of a decision deserves scrutiny because accepting it can feel like doing nothing, while rejecting it requires an active decision . [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping Explanatory illustration 3 What the UK online shopping experiment found The Department for Business and Trade commissioned Alma Economics to examine defaults in UK e commerce. The research, conducted in 2024, had two linked stages. First, researchers manually followed consumer journeys through 558 popular UK shopping websites and apps. They recorded 412 instances of defaults, spread across 49% of the sampled services. Defaults were particularly common in retail: 69% of the retail websites and apps examined contained at least one. Shipping and delivery were the most common defaults within the customer service category. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping Importantly, this observational exercise did not show that online defaults were generally designed to make shoppers spend more. About 64% directed people towards a cheaper or similarly priced option. The researchers therefore described the real world picture as more nuanced than a simple story about harmful design. Defaults can save effort when they select an option that suits the shopper; the problem arises when the default and the shopper's interests diverge. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture The second stage isolated that possibility experimentally. A randomised controlled trial involved 5,889 participants using a simulated shopping platform designed to resemble a familiar e commerce site. Participants encountered decisions involving either a protection plan or shipping. In the control condition they had to choose actively; in treatment conditions, the more expensive choice was either genuinely preselected or merely made to look visually prominent in a way that mimicked a default. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture The distinction produced the study's most striking result. Actual preselection strongly increased selection of the expensive option: roughly 60% for the protection plan and 70% for shipping relative to the control condition. By contrast, merely making an option look like a default had much weaker effects. For protection plans, the effect of the mimicking treatment was statistically indistinguishable from zero; for shipping, participants were only about 5% more likely to choose the visually prominent expensive option. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture That comparison matters. It suggests that the result was not simply “people buy whatever catches their eye”. There was something consequential about having the decision already made on the shopper's behalf. The preselected choice became the option that had to be actively rejected. There was another revealing difference: preselection reduced the overall time participants spent on the shopping journey by about 12%. The researchers interpreted the time results as consistent with shoppers spending less time deliberating when a default was available, whereas the control condition forced them to select an option themselves. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture Yet the study did not find statistically significant effects of preselection on its self reported measure of willingness to pay or on its estimated consumer surplus measure. Nor did most participants report feeling badly about their decisions afterwards. The evidence is therefore strongest for a change in observed choice , not for a claim that everyone influenced by a default later regrets the purchase or suffers measurable financial harm. Participants exposed to preselection were, however, less likely to say they would choose the same option again. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture Prof. Eric J. Johnson: Choice Architecture and Financial Decisions Rational Reminder 240 Channel: The Rational Reminder Podcast Open on YouTube Why preselection changes consumer decisions Defaults work partly because they change the task. Compare two checkout screens. On the first, neither standard nor premium delivery is selected, so the shopper must answer, “Which delivery option do I want?” On the second, premium delivery is already selected. Now the immediate task can become, “Is there enough reason to change this?” Those are not psychologically identical decisions even though exactly the same alternatives remain available. A broader meta analysis of 58 default studies, covering 73,675 participants, found a substantial average default effect but also considerable variation between settings. Its analysis highlighted several possible channels. A default may be treated as an implicit endorsement — a signal that the choice architect considers the option appropriate — or as an existing status quo that the person becomes reluctant to give up. Staying with a default can also be easier than changing it, particularly when the shopper is moving quickly through several decisions. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressWhen and why defaults influence decisions: a meta analysis of default effects Behavioural Public Policy Cam... The UK experiment offers unusually useful evidence about attention. For shipping, people who noticed the default were 31 percentage points less likely to select the expensive default than those who did not notice it. The researchers therefore concluded that making defaults more noticeable could reduce their influence. This does not prove that awareness completely neutralises defaults, but it supports a simple practical point: recognising that a choice has been made for you changes the decision environment . [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice architecture Age also mattered. The experiment found that older participants had a greater probability of selecting the expensive option when it was preselected. The detailed analysis found an interaction between age category and the preselection treatment in both the protection plan and shipping experiments. The authors consequently highlighted older consumers as a group that may warrant particular attention when retailers or regulators assess default design. [GOV.UK Assets] service.gov.uk UK Assets Research into potentially harmful online choice architecture AssetsResearch into potentially harmful online choice"},{"title":"Growth Mindset","url":"/growth-mindset/","description":"Growth mindset research shows how different study filters and statistical choices can turn the same literature into sharply different conclusions.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Evidence Limits","level":3,"text":"Growth Mindset Why Growth Mindset Meta Analyses Disagree So Much Evidence Limits Growth Mindset Growth Mindset Why Growth Mindset Meta Analyses Disagree So Much Growth mindset research shows how different study filters and statistical choices can turn the same literature into sharply different conclusions. Growth mindset research shows how different study filters and statistical choices can turn the same literature into sharply different conclusions. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence Why overall effect estimates became controversial How study quality and publication bias change the result What targeted effects and prediction intervals add self improvement that works evidence limits mindset debate Introduction Growth mindset is a particularly revealing test case for evidence based self improvement because serious meta analyses of broadly the same research literature have produced strikingly different messages. One concludes that interventions have, at most, a tiny average effect on academic achievement and that the apparent benefit disappears under stricter quality and publication bias checks. Another argues that averaging everybody together answers the wrong question: well implemented interventions targeted at students facing academic difficulty show a small but meaningful benefit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do growth mindset interventions impact students' academic achievement? A systematic review and meta analysis with recommendations f... Explanatory illustration 1 The disagreement is not simply “one side believes in growth mindset and the other does not”. It turns on consequential methodological choices: which studies and outcomes enter the dataset, how multiple results from the same study are handled, how study quality and publication bias are assessed, whether moderators are analysed separately or jointly, and whether researchers focus on a grand mean or on variation between populations and settings. That makes growth mindset unusually useful for understanding why a meta analysis is not a mechanical truth machine. Why overall effect estimates became controversial A growth mindset, in this literature, means believing that intellectual ability can develop rather than being permanently fixed. The practical claim goes further: teaching this belief might change how students respond to difficulty and thereby improve achievement. The distinction matters because evidence that mindset correlates with achievement is not equivalent to evidence that an intervention changing mindset will improve grades. An influential 2018 synthesis by Victoria Sisk and colleagues already suggested caution. Its first meta analysis covered 273 samples and 365,915 participants and examined the association between mindset and achievement; its second covered 43 intervention studies and 57,155 participants. Overall relationships and intervention effects were weak, although some analyses suggested that students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds or those academically at risk might benefit more. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov To What Extent and Under Which Circumstances Are Growth Mind Sets Important to Academic Achievement? Two Meta Analyses PubMedMarc... The argument sharpened with two systematic reviews published together in Psychological Bulletin in 2023. Macnamara and Burgoyne examined 63 studies with 97,672 participants and estimated an overall academic achievement effect of d = 0.05 , with a 95% confidence interval from 0.02 to 0.09. That is a very small standardised difference. More importantly, their progressively stricter analyses made the result smaller rather than larger. Among 13 studies in which the intervention demonstrably changed students' mindsets, the estimated effect was d = 0.04 , with a confidence interval crossing zero. Restricting the analysis to six studies judged to provide the highest quality evidence produced d = 0.02 , again statistically non significant. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do growth mindset interventions impact students' academic achievement? A systematic review and meta analysis with recommendations f... Burnette and colleagues approached the literature differently. Rather than treating heterogeneity mainly as noise around a single average, they asked whether effects differed in theoretically predicted ways. Their review covered 53 independent samples across academic, mental health and social outcomes. For academic achievement, their broader estimate was about d = 0.09 ; when analyses focused on targeted populations expected to benefit and interventions delivered with high fidelity, the estimate was d = 0.14 , 95% CI 0.06 to 0.22. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis of growth mindset interventions: For whom, how, and why might such interventions work? PubMed... Those numbers are all small, but they imply different conclusions. An effect near 0.02–0.05 supports scepticism about using growth mindset as a general achievement intervention. An effect around 0.14 in a properly specified target population supports a narrower proposition: a cheap intervention might be worthwhile for some students in some circumstances. The key question therefore became less “Which meta analysis has the correct headline number?” than what population and intervention does each number actually describe? Growth Mindsets & Academic Improvement Channel: Dr Justin Coulson's Happy Families Open on YouTube The same literature can become different datasets Meta analysis involves many decisions before a pooled effect is calculated. Researchers define what counts as a growth mindset intervention, which designs qualify, which achievement measures count, whether unpublished work enters the synthesis and how multiple outcomes or subgroups within one experiment are represented. Different defensible rules can therefore produce datasets that overlap substantially without being identical. More importantly, the two 2023 reviews handled the structure of those data differently. Tipton and colleagues' subsequent methodological commentary argued that Macnamara and Burgoyne aggregated effect sizes within studies before pooling them and tested moderators largely through separate subgroup analyses. Burnette and colleagues instead retained more within study variation and used multilevel meta regression, allowing related effects from different outcomes and subgroups to be modelled without pretending they were independent studies. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That technical difference matters when the theory itself predicts heterogeneity. Imagine an intervention has essentially no effect on students already thriving academically but a modest effect on students struggling with setbacks. Average the groups first and the targeted effect is diluted. Preserve subgroup variation and model risk status, and the difference may become visible. Tipton and colleagues tested precisely this issue by applying methods similar to Burnette's multilevel approach to Macnamara and Burgoyne's dataset. Their exploratory re analysis again found substantial heterogeneity and a significant effect for focal, at risk groups, including after adjustment for measures of study quality or publication bias. They therefore argued that the disagreement could not be reduced simply to the two teams having selected different studies. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Macnamara and Burgoyne rejected the implication that modelling heterogeneity resolved the deeper evidential problems. Their reply stressed that they had preregistered numerous moderator tests and argued that methodological weaknesses and bias in the underlying studies remained consequential regardless of the statistical model. They reported, among other concerns, that 94% of interventions contained what they classified as confounds and that researchers with a known financial incentive were substantially more likely to report positive results. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com Open source on ebsco.com. So the dispute exposes two different risks. Over aggregation can conceal a real conditional effect; subgroup modelling can identify effects that matter only in particular circumstances. But increasingly elaborate moderator analyses cannot rescue an underlying evidence base if its positive results are substantially driven by weak designs, selective reporting or researcher degrees of freedom. Both problems have to be considered. How study quality and publication bias change the result The strongest sceptical argument is not merely that the average effect is small. It is that estimated effects appear to shrink as the evidence is filtered for credibility. Macnamara and Burgoyne reported shortcomings involving intervention design, analysis and reporting across the literature. They also found evidence consistent with publication and researcher bias. Their uncorrected estimate across all studies was d = 0.05 ; after"},{"title":"Effort vs Strategy","url":"/effort-vs-strategy/","description":"Harder goals help most when you know what works; when the real problem is finding a strategy, extra pressure may solve the wrong problem.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goal Difficulty","level":3,"text":"Effort vs Strategy Do You Need More Effort or a Better Strategy? Goal Difficulty Effort vs Strategy Effort vs Strategy Do You Need More Effort or a Better Strategy? Harder goals help most when you know what works; when the real problem is finding a strategy, extra pressure may solve the wrong problem. Harder goals help most when you know what works; when the real problem is finding a strategy, extra pressure may solve the wrong problem. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism What effort difficulty looks like What strategy difficulty looks like Choosing the right response to each kind of challenge self improvement that works goal difficulty effort vs strategy Introduction A difficult goal can fail for two very different reasons. Sometimes you already know what works but are not doing enough of it: the difficulty is effort . Sometimes you are working hard but do not yet know which actions will reliably produce the result: the difficulty is strategy . Treating the second problem as if it were the first can make a goal harder without making success more likely. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because challenging, specific goals have a strong record of improving performance, but their benefits become less straightforward as tasks become complex, novel or knowledge intensive. Research on complex tasks shows that pressure for a better outcome can sometimes consume attention that would be better spent learning how the task works. In those circumstances, a learning goal — discovering, testing or mastering an effective method — can outperform a demanding outcome goal. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... The practical question is therefore not simply, “Is this goal difficult?” It is: What kind of difficulty is stopping me? What effort difficulty looks like Effort difficulty occurs when the route from action to result is reasonably well understood. You possess the relevant knowledge and basic skill; the challenge is supplying enough attention, intensity, consistency or persistence to execute what you already know. This is the situation in which conventional challenging goals are most at home. Goal setting research has found that specific, difficult goals can direct attention, regulate effort and encourage persistence, while feedback lets people compare their actual performance with the target. The classic meta analysis of studies from 1966 to 1984 found strong support for difficult goals over easy goals and for specific, difficult goals over vague “do your best” instructions or no goal. It also found support for pairing difficult goals with feedback. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com A meta analytic study of the effects of goal setting on task performance: 1966–1984 ScienceDirect... Imagine that you already know how to run five kilometres comfortably but want to improve your time. Your training plan is sensible, your pacing is understood and completing the sessions reliably improves your fitness. If you repeatedly skip the harder workouts, the principal bottleneck may genuinely be execution. A more demanding but attainable training target can increase the amount or quality of work directed at the result. The same pattern can appear in less physical goals. You may know how to prepare a standard report but keep leaving it until the deadline, or know the revision technique that works for a familiar subject but fail to put in sufficient sessions. In such cases, the gap between present and desired performance can plausibly be closed largely by doing more consistently, for longer, or to a higher standard . A useful diagnostic is to ask what would happen if someone offered you a compelling incentive to succeed tomorrow. If you could immediately describe the actions you would take — and those actions have already been shown to work — the problem probably contains a substantial effort component. More motivation can change execution because the necessary knowledge is already available. Experience matters here. A field study of 347 people found that both job experience and task complexity affected relationships among goal setting, strategy quality and performance. More generally, skill acquisition research shows that the cognitive demands of a task change with practice: what initially requires substantial conscious processing can become increasingly procedural with experience. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryJoint moderating effects of job experience and task component complexity: Relations among goal setting, task strategi... That means “effort problem” is not a permanent description of an activity. A task that is strategically difficult for a beginner may become predominantly an execution challenge for an expert. Deliberate Practice: Why Most Practice Does Not Build Mastery (Ericsson Science) Channel: Habits, Backed by Science Open on YouTube What strategy difficulty looks like Strategy difficulty begins when the connection between effort and results is uncertain. You may be highly motivated and busy, yet still have unanswered questions about what to do, what to attend to, which method to choose, or why your current approach is failing . Novel and complex tasks are particularly likely to create this problem. A meta analysis of 125 goal setting studies specifically examined task complexity as a moderator of goal effects, reflecting the fact that the effectiveness of demanding goals cannot simply be assumed to remain constant as a task requires more information processing and interdependent actions. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. An especially revealing set of experiments used a stock prediction task in which participants had to work out how several cues related to outcomes. Difficult performance goals did not merely ask participants to work harder: they increased strategy search activity. Yet that extra searching could actually reduce prediction accuracy. The researchers argued that specific, difficult goals can lose their advantage on novel tasks when people have multiple possible strategies but do not know which one is effective. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Goals, Strategy Development, and Task Performance: Some Limits on the Efficacy of Goal SettingFebruary 1, 1989... Published: February 1, 1989 A follow up study makes the risk clearer. Researchers testing interventions for the same sort of strategy problem warned that difficult goals can produce excessive strategy search when an effective approach is not readily identifiable. Helping people search more intelligently — by restricting the search space or teaching search methods — changed what the demanding goal could accomplish. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsTask Strategy Interventions in Goal Setting: The Importance of Search in Strategy Development P. Christopher Earley, Terry... This is an important correction to the familiar idea that a hard goal automatically makes you “try harder”. On a strategy limited task, greater pressure can instead make you try more things . That is useful only if the search process helps distinguish good strategies from bad ones. Rapidly switching approaches without evaluating them can generate considerable activity with little learning. A strategy problem therefore often has recognisable signs: More of the same produces little improvement. Additional hours mostly repeat the same errors. You cannot explain the mechanism of success. Good and bad performances occur, but you are unsure what caused the difference. You keep changing methods. Effort is high, but experimentation is unsystematic. Feedback surprises you. You regularly predict that an action will work and discover that it does not. A knowledgeable person could probably save you substantial time. Instruction, examples or a better model of the task would be more valuable than encouragement. You cannot yet write a credible process goal. “Get a better result” is clear, but the behaviours most likely to produce it are not. The dividing line is knowledge, not how tiring the work feels. Strategy discovery itself can require enormous effort. Someone spending ten intense hours debugging an unfamiliar system may be working much harder than an expert who fixes the same fault in twenty minutes. High effort does not prove that effort is the bottleneck. Explanatory illustration 2 Why more pressure can solve the wrong problem The danger of misdiagnosis is asymmetric. If you have a good strategy but apply it half heartedly, insufficient challenge can leave performance below your capabilities. But if you lack a good strategy, raising the outcome target can increase pressure on a system that does not yet know how to deliver the outcome. Seijts and Latham describe this distinction directly: where a task primarily requires acquisition of knowledge and skill rather than"},{"title":"Stretch Goals","url":"/stretch-goals/","description":"A goal designed to seem nearly impossible can push people away from proven methods and make risky shortcuts more attractive.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goal Difficulty","level":3,"text":"Stretch Goals When Does a Stretch Goal Become Too Extreme? Goal Difficulty Stretch Goals Stretch Goals When Does a Stretch Goal Become Too Extreme? A goal designed to seem nearly impossible can push people away from proven methods and make risky shortcuts more attractive. A goal designed to seem nearly impossible can push people away from proven methods and make risky shortcuts more attractive. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention How extreme stretch goals differ from ordinary challenge Why impossible looking targets can encourage risky exploration Deciding whether radical ambition fits your situation self improvement that works goal difficulty stretch goals Introduction A stretch goal becomes too extreme when reaching it no longer looks like a matter of applying a proven method with greater effort, but instead requires a large, uncertain leap beyond your present capabilities. That distinction matters because an ordinary difficult goal can focus effort and persistence, whereas a seemingly impossible goal can force you to search for entirely new methods — and expose you to the costs of experiments that fail. Research on stretch goals therefore treats extreme difficulty and radical novelty as important distinguishing features, rather than simply describing stretch goals as unusually ambitious targets. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org Using Stretch Goals for Idea Generation Among Employees: One Size Does Not Fit All! Organization ScienceJune 30, 2021... Published: June 30, 2021 Explanatory illustration 1 For self improvement, the lesson is not that radical ambition is inherently bad. It is that ambition changes character once the reliable route disappears. At that point, the relevant question is no longer simply, “How hard am I willing to work?” It becomes, “Can I afford the experimentation, failure and risk that this target may require?” The evidence suggests that extreme goals are most defensible when there is room to experiment safely, and least defensible when failure is costly or resources are already strained. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. How extreme stretch goals differ from ordinary challenge The conventional evidence for challenging goals should not be confused with evidence that the most extreme possible goal is always best. Goal setting research generally finds benefits from specific, difficult goals when people have the ability, commitment, feedback and resources needed to pursue them. Goals can direct attention, increase effort, extend persistence and encourage the development of strategies. [DOI] doi.org New Directions in Goal Setting Theory New Directions in Goal Setting Theory Edwin A. Locke, Gary P. Latham, 2006... A genuine stretch goal goes further. Research by Sim Sitkin and colleagues defines these goals around targets that appear practically unattainable with current capabilities. Later research emphasises two connected features: extreme difficulty in the desired outcome and extreme novelty in the means needed to reach it. If current methods could plausibly produce the result simply by being applied more consistently or intensely, the target may be difficult without being a true stretch goal. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. Imagine, for example, that you reliably complete two hours of focused study each weekday. Raising that to two and a half hours may require better scheduling and more discipline, but the underlying method remains familiar. By contrast, deciding that you must somehow achieve in three months an outcome that normally takes several years may create a completely different problem. Your established methods cannot plausibly close the gap, so the goal implicitly demands that you discover a breakthrough. That difference explains both the attraction and the danger of extreme stretch goals. They can disrupt complacency precisely because they make existing routines look inadequate. Sitkin and colleagues argue that such targets can provoke exploratory search rather than incremental improvement. But exploration is intrinsically uncertain: abandoning a known route for an untested one creates the possibility of unusually large gains and unusually poor outcomes. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. This is why “10% better” and “10 times better” should not automatically be treated as points on the same motivational scale. The latter may require a different strategy, different resources and different tolerance for failure. Once a goal demands invention rather than merely improved execution, increasing difficulty is no longer equivalent to increasing productive challenge. The Dark Side of Stretch Goals: Why Aiming Too High Can Backfire 🚀🔥 Channel: Psywork Open on YouTube Why impossible looking targets can encourage risky exploration The central problem with an extreme stretch goal is not simply that you might miss it. Missing a harmless aspirational target may be perfectly acceptable. The more important concern is that an apparently impossible target can change which actions seem reasonable while you are pursuing it . When conventional methods obviously cannot produce the required outcome, continuing to use them appears pointless. That can make unusually speculative alternatives attractive. The stretch goal literature describes this as a move towards exploratory search: the target can push decision makers away from familiar routines and towards radically different possibilities. That mechanism is exactly what proponents hope will generate breakthroughs, but it also increases uncertainty. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. Experimental work using management simulations gives the concern some empirical support. Researchers studying moderate, stretch and seemingly impossible goals found that increasing goal difficulty was associated with greater perceived risk taking, lower goal commitment, reduced self efficacy and greater anxiety. Moderate and stretch goals did not produce significantly different financial performance in that study, while seemingly impossible goals reduced performance. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsManagerial Responses at the Interface between Stretch Goals and Organizational Performance Academy of Man... The risk can become especially important when a target is treated as mandatory rather than aspirational. Research on goal setting more broadly has warned that tightly specified goals can narrow attention, distort risk preferences and encourage people to neglect consequences that the goal itself does not measure. Ordóñez and colleagues argued that these effects mean goals should be treated as interventions with potential side effects rather than automatically beneficial motivational devices. [Harvard Kennedy School] hks.harvard.edu Harvard Kennedy SchoolGoals Gone Wild: The Systematic Side Effects of Overprescribing Goal Setting Harvard Kennedy School... There is also experimental evidence that an unmet target can affect ethical decisions. In a laboratory study by Maurice Schweitzer, Lisa Ordóñez and Bambi Douma, participants with unmet goals were more likely to behave unethically than people instructed simply to do their best; the relationship appeared with and without financial incentives and was particularly strong among people who fell just short of their target. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. That does not establish that ambitious personal goals generally cause unethical behaviour, nor does it mean every extreme goal produces reckless decisions. The broader critique of goal setting has itself been disputed. Goal setting researchers Edwin Locke and Gary Latham argued that some prominent criticisms relied too heavily on anecdotes, used unrepresentative evidence and presented known limitations of goal setting as if they undermined the theory as a whole. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. The sensible conclusion is narrower. Extreme goals deserve additional scrutiny because they can create circumstances in which ordinary methods seem inadequate, while the pressure to reach the outcome remains. The larger the gap between the target and proven methods, the more important it becomes to decide in advance which risks are acceptable rather than allowing desperation near the deadline to make that decision for you. Research for Entrepreneurs: When & How to Pursue Stretch Goals Channel: Duke Innovation and Entrepreneurship (Duke I&E) Open on YouTube When experimentation is useful — and when it is expensive Radical exploration is not automatically a mistake. Sometimes abandoning the familiar method is exactly the point. A person experimenting with a new creative technique, business model or way of learning may deliberately choose a target that cannot be reached through"},{"title":"Feedback","url":"/feedback/","description":"Feedback reveals whether a demanding goal is producing improvement or simply generating repeated misses that call for adjustment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goal Difficulty","level":3,"text":"Feedback When Should You Make a Hard Goal Easier? Goal Difficulty Feedback Feedback When Should You Make a Hard Goal Easier? Feedback reveals whether a demanding goal is producing improvement or simply generating repeated misses that call for adjustment. Feedback reveals whether a demanding goal is producing improvement or simply generating repeated misses that call for adjustment. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why difficult goals need performance feedback Distinguishing productive misses from poor calibration Adjusting targets without abandoning challenge self improvement that works goal difficulty feedback Introduction A hard goal should become easier when feedback shows that the difficulty is no longer producing useful adaptation. One disappointing result is rarely enough: challenging goals are supposed to generate some misses. The stronger warning is a pattern in which the gap between target and actual performance stays large, performance is not improving, and further effort or reasonable strategy changes do not close it. Explanatory illustration 1 Feedback makes that distinction possible. Research on progress monitoring shows that tracking performance improves goal attainment on average, while newer research on goal revision finds that people systematically use the gap between goals and performance to adjust later targets. The practical lesson is not to lower a goal whenever it feels uncomfortable. It is to treat the target as a working calibration rather than a promise carved in stone. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Why difficult goals need performance feedback A difficult goal creates a discrepancy: where you are is deliberately below where you intend to be. That gap can motivate attention, effort, persistence and strategy search, but only if you can see what happens to the gap over time. Feedback therefore turns goal difficulty from a guess into something that can be tested against actual performance. This idea has deep roots in goal setting research. In Miriam Erez's 1977 experiment, the relationship between participants' self set goals and performance was substantially stronger when they received performance feedback than when they did not. Later summaries of goal setting theory likewise identify feedback as an important condition because it enables people to monitor progress and adjust goal directed behaviour. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. There is also broader experimental evidence that monitoring itself matters. Benjamin Harkin and colleagues synthesised 138 studies involving 19,951 participants in which people were randomly assigned to interventions intended to increase progress monitoring or to control conditions. Monitoring interventions increased both the frequency of monitoring and subsequent goal attainment; the average effect on goal attainment was moderate, at d = 0.40. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded and when outcomes were reported or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 For recalibrating a difficult goal, however, the useful unit is not simply “success” or “failure”. It is the goal–performance discrepancy : the distance between the target and what actually happened. Suppose your target is to complete five demanding practice sessions each week. Weeks of four, four, five and four sessions tell a different story from weeks of two, one, two and two, even though both records contain misses. The first target looks demanding but plausible; the second may be substantially beyond present capacity. Recent research directly supports paying attention to that distance. A 2025 synthesis of 28 studies on goal revision found that people generally raised goals following success and lowered them following failure. More importantly, the size of the goal–performance discrepancy predicted the magnitude of subsequent goal revision, with a correlation of r =.51. The relationship was stronger when feedback explicitly provided information about both the goal and performance rather than performance alone. [DOI] doi.org Full article: How (much) do people revise their goals after success and failure? The Triple A model of goal revisionJuly 23, 2025... Published: July 23, 2025 That makes a simple written record surprisingly useful. “I did badly” is an emotional judgement. “My target was 20; my last four results were 13, 14, 14 and 15” is calibration information. Self Regulation Theory: Positive and Negative Feedback Loops Channel: Engineering Psychology Open on YouTube Productive misses or a badly calibrated goal? Not every miss means a hard goal is too hard. If you immediately lower a target whenever you fall short, difficulty disappears precisely when it begins demanding adaptation. The more useful question is whether misses contain evidence of movement towards the target . Research with competitive athletes illustrates why the size and pattern of the gap matter. In an eight week study of 100 varsity track and field athletes, goal–performance discrepancies predicted goal revision: athletes were more likely to lower competition and season goals after missing them when the discrepancy was large. But the response also depended on how athletes interpreted the cause of the miss and how much time remained for reaching the goal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Missing the mark: effects of time and causal attributions on goal revision in response to goal performance discrepancies PubMed... This suggests that a miss should trigger diagnosis before adjustment. Three patterns are especially informative: The gap is shrinking. If performance is trending towards the target, the difficulty may be doing its job. A runner aiming for a demanding time might miss it repeatedly while moving from 27:00 to 26:20 to 25:50. The binary result remains “goal not reached”, but the feedback says something important is improving. The gap is stable despite sensible changes. If repeated attempts produce roughly the same shortfall even after adequate practice and reasonable strategy adjustments, the target increasingly looks poorly calibrated to present capacity. The gap is widening or performance is deteriorating. That is stronger evidence for intervention, particularly when the target is causing compensatory behaviour that undermines the activity itself — for example, rushing work to hit an unrealistic volume target while quality falls. This distinction also guards against a common error: interpreting all negative feedback as proof that you need more effort. Feedback research does not support the idea that feedback automatically improves performance. Kluger and DeNisi's influential meta analysis covered 607 effect sizes and 23,663 observations. Feedback interventions improved performance on average, but more than one third produced worse performance. Their Feedback Intervention Theory argues that feedback is more useful when attention stays close to the task and less useful when it shifts towards self focused concerns. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLThe effects of feedback interventions on performance: A historical review, a meta analysis, and a preliminary feedback inter... In practice, “You failed again” is poor calibration information. “Your accuracy improved from 72% to 81%, but speed has stopped improving” tells you what needs changing. Feedback should help decide whether to change effort, strategy or target , rather than merely delivering a verdict on the person. Explanatory illustration 2 Diagnose the shortfall before lowering the number The same gap can have very different meanings. Missing a target because you practised inconsistently is not equivalent to missing it despite consistent practice. Nor is failure caused by a poor strategy equivalent to evidence that the final target is inherently unrealistic. Research on goal revision supports this more careful interpretation. Experiments by Adam Tolli and Aaron Schmidt found that performance feedback interacted with people's explanations for their outcomes to influence self efficacy — their belief that they could perform the task — which in turn influenced subsequent goal revision. Feedback therefore does not mechanically produce a new goal. What you think the result says about future performance matters. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The role of feedback, casual attributions, and self efficacy in goal revision PubMed... Before making a difficult goal easier, separate four possibilities: 1. Execution problem: the target may still fit, but the planned behaviour is not actually happening. 2. Strategy problem: effort is present, but the method is not producing improvement. 3."},{"title":"Right Difficulty","url":"/right-difficulty/","description":"A useful stretch goal demands more than your normal performance without making success implausible at your present skill level.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goal Difficulty","level":3,"text":"Right Difficulty How Hard Should Your Goal Actually Be? Goal Difficulty Right Difficulty Right Difficulty How Hard Should Your Goal Actually Be? A useful stretch goal demands more than your normal performance without making success implausible at your present skill level. A useful stretch goal demands more than your normal performance without making success implausible at your present skill level. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why the same target can be easy, challenging or impossible Using recent performance to set a credible stretch Warning signs that difficulty is badly calibrated self improvement that works goal difficulty right difficulty Introduction The right goal is not simply “hard”. It is hard relative to you . A target can be routine for someone who already performs near that level, a useful stretch for someone a little below it, and effectively impossible for a beginner. Research on goal setting therefore makes most practical sense when difficulty is judged against current ability, recent performance, available resources and understanding of the task—not against an impressive looking number in isolation. A meta analysis of 78 goal setting studies found that both prior performance and ability were significantly related to the goals people set for themselves. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsMeta Analysis of the Antecedents of Personal Goal Level and of the Antecedents and Consequences of Goal Commitment J. C. W... Explanatory illustration 1 For self improvement, that points to a practical rule: use your recent demonstrated performance as the starting point , then choose a target that requires a credible improvement. When your performance changes, recalibrate. And when you do not yet know how to perform the task well, the problem may be skill acquisition rather than insufficient ambition. Why the same target can be easy, challenging or impossible Numbers hide an important distinction between goal level and goal difficulty . Goal level is the objective target: running five kilometres in 25 minutes, completing 20 press ups, writing 1,000 words or scoring 80% on an examination. Goal difficulty is what achieving that number demands from a particular person. Suppose three people set a target of running five kilometres in 25 minutes. One currently runs it in 24 minutes, another in 27 minutes and the third cannot yet run five kilometres continuously. They have identical targets but very different goals in practical terms. The first person has little stretch at all. The second needs a meaningful but potentially credible improvement. The third may first need to develop basic endurance before a 25 minute performance target becomes useful. This distinction appears directly in research comparing uniform and ability adjusted goals. In a laboratory study of 138 university students, researchers compared an “ability based” approach with assigning everybody the same performance target. Ability based goals produced better results overall, with particularly clear benefits for lower ability participants; lower and moderate ability participants given the common target also showed larger performance declines in later rounds. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe Performance Effects of an Ability Based Approach to Goal Assignment: Journal of Organizational Behavior Manage... The implication is not that people with lower current performance should permanently receive easier goals. It is that a stretch should start from where performance actually is. Once ability improves, the appropriate target should move with it. That also explains why copying another person's goal can be misleading. “Read 50 books this year”, “run a sub four hour marathon” or “practise for two hours every day” says little about appropriate difficulty without knowing the person's starting point, relevant skills, time and constraints. Difficulty has to be calibrated rather than borrowed. Why Most Goals Fail (and How to Set Goals That Actually Stick) Channel: Mental Health Academy Open on YouTube Use recent performance to set a credible stretch The most useful benchmark is usually not what you believe you ought to be capable of. It is what you have recently demonstrated under reasonably representative conditions. Evidence on self efficacy reinforces the value of this approach. A meta analysis examining changes within individuals found that previous performance was a stronger predictor of subsequent self efficacy than self efficacy was of subsequent performance. In other words, confidence partly follows evidence of what you have actually managed to do. [DOI] doi.org A Meta‐Analytic Investigation of the Within‐Person Self‐Efficacy Domain: Is Self‐Efficacy a Product of Past Performance or a Driver of... Start by collecting a small baseline. Depending on the goal, that might mean your last several runs rather than your lifetime personal best, the number of focused study sessions you actually complete in a normal week, or the amount of uninterrupted practice you can currently sustain. A single unusually good or bad day is a weak calibration point; recent repeatable performance gives you a better picture of present capacity. Then ask what improvement would require you to change your behaviour without requiring an unexplained leap in ability. Research illustrates why that distinction matters. In a muscular endurance study involving secondary school students, participants received goals ranging from no specific target through improvements of 10%, 20% and 40%. The 20% target was designed as difficult but realistic, while 40% was treated as improbable or unattainable. Across practice periods, the difficult but realistic group showed the largest gains; the improbable group performed substantially worse than that group after four and six weeks, although the difference was no longer present after eight weeks. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of goal difficulty, goal specificity and duration of practice time intervals on muscular endurance performance PubMed... That does not establish 20% as a universal formula. The percentage came from one task and population. Indeed, experiments examining different ways of defining difficulty have found that results depend partly on whether goals are expressed as absolute performance levels or improvements over previous performance. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The Effects of Varying Goal Difficulty Operationalizations on Goal Setting Outcomes and Processes ScienceDirectJanuary 1... The useful lesson is the method: anchor the target to evidence about current performance rather than adopting a fixed percentage that supposedly works for everybody. A simple calibration cycle is: 1. Establish your current range. Look at several recent attempts under normal conditions. 2. Choose a genuine stretch. The target should demand more than your normal performance, not merely formalise something you already do. 3. Check the route to improvement. You should be able to identify plausible changes—more practice, better consistency , improved technique or another concrete strategy—that could close the gap. 4. Run the goal long enough to generate evidence. One miss does not prove that a goal is excessive, just as one exceptional success does not prove that it is too easy. 5. Recalibrate from the new evidence. Consistent success is a reason to raise the challenge; persistent failure despite appropriate effort is a reason to examine the target, strategy or skill gap. The point is adaptive difficulty. A well calibrated goal is temporary because your ability should not remain fixed. Explanatory illustration 2 Attainability matters without requiring easy goals “Attainable” can sound like permission to avoid ambitious targets, but that is not what the evidence implies. The useful distinction is between a goal whose difficulty stimulates extra effort and one whose distance from current performance makes the target cease to function as a credible guide. A revealing real world example comes from Boston Marathon qualifying standards. Researchers analysed marathon performances from 1970 to 2015 and exploited changes in the qualifying times runners faced as they entered different age categories. After accounting for previous performance, runners tended to improve when the Boston qualifying target became more attainable; where it became more challenging, performance declined. The study therefore provided evidence that attainability itself can influence performance rather than merely reflecting who happens to be a stronger runner. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Goal attainability and performance: Evidence from Boston marathon qualifying standards ScienceDirectFebruary 1, 2017... Published: February 1, 2017 The case is especially useful because the qualifying target is objectively identical for everyone in an age and sex category but subjectively very different depending on"},{"title":"Novice Goals","url":"/novice-goals/","description":"When you are still figuring out how a complex task works, a demanding performance target can compete with the learning needed to improve.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goal Difficulty","level":3,"text":"Novice Goals Why Hard Goals Can Backfire for Beginners Goal Difficulty Novice Goals Novice Goals Why Hard Goals Can Backfire for Beginners When you are still figuring out how a complex task works, a demanding performance target can compete with the learning needed to improve. When you are still figuring out how a complex task works, a demanding performance target can compete with the learning needed to improve. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What changes when the method is still unknown The scheduling task evidence on difficult outcome goals How to recognize when performance pressure is premature self improvement that works goal difficulty novice goals Introduction Hard goals are often useful when you already know how to do the task and mainly need more effort, persistence or focus. For a beginner facing a complex task, however, the bottleneck is different: you are still discovering what works. A demanding outcome such as “complete ten correct schedules” or “reach this score” can make immediate results compete for attention with the experimentation and knowledge building needed to improve. Explanatory illustration 1 That is not merely a theoretical exception to goal setting advice. In a controlled scheduling experiment with 114 business students, a difficult outcome goal helped on the simple version of the task, but on the complex version a difficult learning goal produced better performance and more effective task strategies. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... A related scheduling experiment went further: when participants had to acquire knowledge to perform correctly, a vague “do your best” instruction actually produced better performance than a specific, difficult outcome target, while a difficult learning goal performed best of all. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... The important distinction, then, is not between ambition and timidity. It is between asking a novice to produce a demanding result now and asking them to master the methods that will eventually make that result attainable . What changes when you do not know the method yet A performance or outcome goal specifies the result to achieve: finish a certain number of correct cases, reach a particular score, capture a given market share. A learning goal instead specifies something that must be discovered, understood or mastered on the way to good performance. That difference becomes important when performance depends on strategies the learner does not yet possess. Researchers Gerard Seijts and Gary Latham argue that challenging outcome goals are most appropriate when better results primarily require increased effort and persistence. When the central requirement is acquiring knowledge and skill, directing attention towards the end result can be counterproductive; a learning goal instead directs attention towards discovering effective processes and strategies. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsLearning versus performance goals: When should each be used? Academy of Management PerspectivesFebruary 1... The problem can be understood as competition for limited attention. Early in complex skill acquisition, people have to think consciously about information that experienced performers can handle more automatically. Research on skill acquisition describes attentional demands as changing substantially with practice: beginners must first build declarative knowledge, compile that knowledge into usable procedures and eventually develop more proceduralised performance. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsDeclarative and Procedural Knowledge in Skill Acquisition: An Aptitude — Treatment Interaction Framework for Training Phil... In three experiments involving 1,010 US Air Force trainees performing computerised air traffic control tasks, Ruth Kanfer and Phillip Ackerman specifically examined how goal setting interacts with these changing information processing demands. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLMotivation and cognitive abilities: An integrative/aptitude treatment interaction approach to skill acquisition. EBSCOhost... This helps explain why “try harder” can be the wrong intervention at the beginning. If the learner already knows the effective procedure, extra effort can be channelled into executing it. If the learner does not know the procedure, more pressure to hit an outcome does not supply the missing knowledge. Research reviewing difficult goals on complex tasks has consequently treated strategy development as a central part of the problem rather than assuming that performance shortfalls simply require greater effort. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineGoal Levels, Complex Tasks, and Strategy Development: A Review and Analysis: Human Performance: Vol 4, No 1Novembe... There is an important qualification. Complexity does not mean hard outcome goals invariably damage learning. DeShon and Alexander's experiments found that difficult, specific goals failed to improve performance when processing of a complex task was implicit, but produced gradual improvements when task processing was explicit; their strategy analyses indicated that better strategies helped account for the gains. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Goal Setting Effects on Implicit and Explicit Learning of Complex Tasks ScienceDirectJanuary 1, 1996... Published: January 1, 1996 The more defensible conclusion is therefore narrower: outcome pressure is especially risky when a novice still needs attention and experimentation to discover how the task works . How to use performance development to drive real growth Leadership wellbeing Part 6 Channel: SACS Consulting Open on YouTube The scheduling task shows the problem clearly The Winters and Latham scheduling experiment is particularly useful because it compared goal types while also varying the complexity of the underlying task. The researchers recruited 114 business students and examined performance on simple and complex versions of a scheduling problem. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... On the simple task, the conventional goal setting result appeared: assigning an outcome goal produced significantly more correct schedules than telling participants merely to do their best. Once the task became complex, the pattern changed. Participants given a specific learning goal performed significantly better than those given an outcome goal or a “do your best” instruction. Crucially, the learning goal participants also used significantly more effective task strategies than participants in either of the other conditions. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... That strategy result matters because it points towards a mechanism. The learning goal did not simply make people feel better about a difficult assignment. It changed what they did while confronting it. Instead of concentrating primarily on reaching the immediate output number, they were directed towards discovering useful ways of performing the task. A subsequent experiment by Seijts and Latham used another class scheduling task with 96 participants who needed to acquire knowledge to perform it correctly. Here the contrast was even sharper: a “do your best” outcome instruction produced higher performance than a specific, difficult outcome goal. Yet the specific, difficult learning goal outperformed “do your best”. Participants pursuing the learning goal discovered and implemented more task relevant strategies, and the number of strategies discovered was positively related to performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... Those comparisons prevent an easy misreading of the evidence. The lesson is not that beginners should have vague, easy goals. In the second experiment, the strongest condition was still specific and difficult; what changed was what the difficulty was attached to . Instead of demanding an ambitious immediate output, the researchers made learning itself the demanding target. Explanatory illustration 2 The wider evidence supports a complexity boundary The scheduling experiments sit within a broader body of research showing that task complexity changes how reliably difficult performance goals help. An earlier meta analysis by Robert Wood, Anthony Mento and Edwin Locke examined 125 goal setting studies"},{"title":"Pre Portioning","url":"/pre-portioning/","description":"Dividing meals before hunger arrives removes a later serving decision, although trial evidence does not prove home portioning causes weight loss.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Meal Prep","level":3,"text":"Pre Portioning Does Pre Portioning Change What You Eat? Meal Prep Pre Portioning Pre Portioning Does Pre Portioning Change What You Eat? Dividing meals before hunger arrives removes a later serving decision, although trial evidence does not prove home portioning causes weight loss. Dividing meals before hunger arrives removes a later serving decision, although trial evidence does not prove home portioning causes weight loss. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence Why serving decisions become harder when hungry What the portion controlled meal trial actually found What can and cannot be inferred for home meal prep self improvement that works meal prep pre portioning Introduction Pre portioning a meal changes the timing of one deceptively important decision: how much food to serve . Instead of making that choice while hungry, the amount is decided earlier, when cooking or packing the meal. Research supports the underlying logic. Hunger can alter portion perception and is associated with choosing larger ideal portions, while experimental evidence shows that the amount served strongly influences how much adults actually eat. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 1 That makes pre portioning a plausible way to remove a vulnerable decision from the hungry moment. But the evidence needs careful interpretation. Trials of commercially pre portioned meals show that smaller pre portioned amounts can reduce energy intake, and one prominent weight loss trial found better short term results with packaged entrées. Yet the broader evidence does not establish that dividing your own batch cooked food into containers causes weight loss. A recent systematic review concluded that the evidence linking portion strategies to body composition or obesity outcomes is too inconsistent for a firm conclusion. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Randomized Clinical Trial of Portion Controlled Prepackaged Foods to Promote Weight Loss PMCJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 Mastering Portion Control for Weight Loss Finding Your Perfect Fit Channel: Dr Helen McCarthy Open on YouTube Why serving decisions become harder when hungry A serving looks like a simple physical quantity, but before eating it is also a prediction: How much of this food will I need to feel satisfied? Research on “expected satiation” — the fullness someone expects a food to produce — suggests that such predictions play an important role in selecting meal size. In one laboratory study of 28 adults assessing 17 foods, ideal portion size was strongly associated with expected satiation: foods expected to be less filling per calorie tended to be selected in larger energy portions. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow Many Calories Are on Our Plate? Expected Fullness, Not Liking, Determines Meal‐size Selection Brunstrom 2009... Current appetite can shift these judgements. In a randomised crossover study of 27 non obese men, participants assessed eight foods and drinks after an overnight fast and after breakfast. When hungry, they estimated significantly smaller quantities as constituting a portion than when full. In other words, the same amount of food could be perceived differently depending on appetite state. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A much larger pair of studies involving hundreds of participants also found hunger among the factors influencing ideal portion choices, with hungrier respondents tending to select larger ideal portions. Those studies relied partly on online judgements rather than observing actual consumption, so they should not be read as proof that hunger inevitably makes a person over serve dinner. They do, however, reinforce the narrower proposition that serving size decisions are not independent of the state in which they are made. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Gender, Age, Hunger, and Body Mass Index as Factors Influencing Portion Size Estimation and Ideal Portion Sizes PMC... That distinction makes pre portioning interesting. Suppose a pot contains four intended dinners. Leaving it as one large pot means that each evening still contains a serving decision: How much should I put on my plate tonight? Dividing it into four containers immediately after cooking turns the later task into something closer to take one container . The amount is no longer being generated from scratch at the same moment as hunger. This matters because serving size itself affects consumption. The US Department of Agriculture's 2024 systematic review, prepared for the 2025 Dietary Guidelines Advisory Committee, examined 43 adult portion size articles comprising 51 interventions. It judged the evidence strong that serving larger portions increases adults' food and energy intake. Its separate analysis of eight randomised trials involving pre portioned foods found that smaller pre portioned amounts consistently produced lower energy intake, although effect sizes differed; that conclusion received a moderate evidence grade. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Pre portioning therefore does more than provide a visual reminder. It fixes one of the environmental inputs that can influence consumption. Portion sizes: Am I eating the right amount? The Food Chain podcast, BBC World Service Channel: BBC World Service Open on YouTube What the portion controlled meal trial actually found A useful concrete test comes from a 2016 randomised clinical trial led by Cheryl Rock at the University of California, San Diego. It enrolled 183 adults with overweight or obesity in a 12 week behavioural weight loss programme. Participants were prescribed a reduced energy diet, but the key comparison was between people receiving portion controlled prepackaged lunch and dinner entrées and people selecting their own foods. One packaged meal group received regular entrées and another received higher protein versions. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Randomized Clinical Trial of Portion Controlled Prepackaged Foods to Promote Weight Loss PMCJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 At 12 weeks, average weight loss was 8.6% of initial body weight in the regular entrée group, 7.8% in the higher protein entrée group and 6.0% in the self selected food control group. When the two packaged meal groups were considered together, they lost significantly more weight than controls. Seventy four per cent of intervention participants achieved at least 5% weight loss, compared with 53% of controls. Intervention participants also lost more body fat: an average 5.7 kg compared with 4.4 kg in the control group. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Randomized Clinical Trial of Portion Controlled Prepackaged Foods to Promote Weight Loss PMCJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 The result is relevant to the hungry serving decision because the packaged entrées supplied a predetermined amount of food. Participants did not have to decide the entrée portion each time they ate lunch or dinner. The study therefore provides experimental evidence that a dietary programme incorporating predetermined portions can outperform an otherwise self selected reduced energy approach over a short period. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Randomized Clinical Trial of Portion Controlled Prepackaged Foods to Promote Weight Loss PMCJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 But “pre portioned meals caused the extra weight loss” is already a stronger statement than the experiment warrants for ordinary home meal preparation . Participants received intensive behavioural counselling, reduced energy prescriptions and advice about physical activity . The intervention supplied commercially prepared foods free of charge, and the researchers did not collect standardised dietary recalls that could show exactly what participants ate outside the supplied entrées. The authors themselves noted that adherence to the reduced energy prescription could not be directly linked to the observed response. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Randomized Clinical Trial of Portion Controlled Prepackaged Foods to Promote Weight Loss PMCJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 There is also a potential conflict of interest consideration. The trial was funded by Nestlé USA; one author was formerly employed by Nestlé Research, and the lead author reported institutional research funding from Nestlé and Jenny Craig. The paper states that the university investigators retained responsibility for data management, analysis and publication and that the sponsor did not control the conduct, analysis or publication decision. Sponsorship does not invalidate the findings, but it is relevant context when weighing a commercially supplied food trial. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open"},{"title":"Behaviour First","url":"/behaviour-first/","description":"When a plan is not happening reliably, tracking whether the behaviour occurred is usually more useful than tracking its downstream results.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Simple Metrics","level":3,"text":"Behaviour First Track the Behaviour Before Judging the Outcome Simple Metrics Behaviour First Behaviour First Track the Behaviour Before Judging the Outcome When a plan is not happening reliably, tracking whether the behaviour occurred is usually more useful than tracking its downstream results. When a plan is not happening reliably, tracking whether the behaviour occurred is usually more useful than tracking its downstream results. Self Improvement That Works governance single mechanism mechanism Why weak adherence makes outcome data hard to interpret Choosing one behaviour measure that answers the immediate question When stable adherence justifies switching to an outcome metric self improvement that works simple metrics behaviour first Introduction When a self improvement plan is not happening reliably, track the behaviour before judging the outcome . If the plan is to exercise three times a week but only one session usually happens, changes in fitness, weight or mood cannot yet tell you much about whether three weekly sessions would work. The immediate uncertainty is simpler: can you execute the plan consistently enough to test it? Explanatory illustration 1 Behaviour change research formally distinguishes monitoring a behaviour from monitoring its downstream outcomes, while research on implementation fidelity makes the diagnostic point especially clear: weak implementation can make an effective intervention look ineffective. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 This is a rule for choosing the next useful metric , not an argument that outcomes never matter. While adherence is unstable, use one simple measure of whether the intended action occurred. Once the behaviour is reliably happening for long enough to produce a meaningful result, promote the outcome metric. Why weak adherence makes outcomes hard to interpret Imagine that you plan four 30 minute study sessions each week because you want to improve your exam performance. After six weeks, your test scores have barely changed. There are at least two very different explanations: 1. Execution failure: you rarely completed the four sessions, so the intended study routine was never properly tested. 2. Strategy failure: you reliably completed the sessions, but that amount or method of studying did not improve learning. The same disappointing outcome appears under both explanations, but the appropriate response is different. In the first case, changing study methods may be premature; you first need to make the routine executable. In the second, continuing to optimise reminders and scheduling may miss the point; the routine is happening, so its effectiveness deserves scrutiny. This resembles the problem of implementation fidelity in intervention research. The Medical Research Council's process evaluation framework separates what was implemented and how from the outcomes subsequently observed. Its guidance notes that an intervention can have limited effects because its design is weak or because it was not properly implemented, which is why measures such as fidelity and dose help researchers interpret outcome results. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Process evaluation of complex interventions: Medical Research Council guidance PMCMarch 19, 2015... Published: March 19, 2015 Public Health England's evaluation guidance makes the practical consequence explicit: understanding what was actually delivered is important because interpretation of outcomes depends on it. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Process evaluation: evaluation in health and wellbeing Process evaluation: evaluation in health and wellbeing A personal plan is not a clinical trial, but the diagnostic logic transfers well. If your intended “dose” is four study sessions and you complete one, you have mainly learnt that the present system produces about one session. You have not obtained a clean test of what four sessions would do. Outcomes also have more competing causes Downstream measures are often influenced by much more than the behaviour being changed. Body weight can move with fluid balance as well as eating and activity. Running performance can vary with sleep, illness, weather and accumulated fatigue. A weekly productivity rating may reflect workload as much as a new planning routine. The further a measure sits downstream from your action, the more opportunities there are for other factors to affect it. Logic model guidance therefore distinguishes implementation — what gets delivered in practice — from mechanisms and eventual outcomes. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Creating a logic model for an intervention: evaluation in health and wellbeing Creating a logic model for an intervention: evaluation in health and wellbeing When adherence itself is uncertain, moving immediately to the end of that causal chain creates an avoidable interpretation problem. This does not mean behavioural measures are inherently superior. It means they are closer to the decision you currently need to make. Process Goals VS Outcome Goals: How to Set Goals You Can Actually Achieve Channel: Develop Good Habits Open on YouTube Choose the behaviour measure that answers one question The useful question during weak adherence is usually: Did the behaviour I intended to perform actually happen often enough? Behaviour change science treats this as a distinct measurement target. The Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy separates “self monitoring of behaviour” from “self monitoring of outcomes of behaviour”. The former records the action itself; the latter records consequences produced by that action. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The behavior change technique taxonomy (v1) of 93 hierarchically clustered techniques: building an international consensus for the... NICE similarly distinguishes monitoring behaviours from outcomes and recommends goals, action plans, coping plans and review in light of progress. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 For personal use, that distinction can produce deliberately boring metrics: Exercise: planned sessions completed out of planned sessions. Studying: planned study blocks started. Writing: planned writing sessions completed. Meditation: days the planned practice occurred. Bedtime routine: nights the routine began within the chosen window. The aim is not to construct a comprehensive record. It is to make one decision legible. Suppose you plan three gym visits a week. A weekly entry of 1/3, 2/3, 1/3, 2/3 is already highly actionable. Before worrying about strength gains or body composition changes, you know that the planned frequency is not being achieved reliably. That directs attention towards the plan's execution: its timing, difficulty, cues, competing commitments or minimum viable version. Research supports monitoring as a useful self regulation technique, although it does not support the stronger claim that more tracking is always better. A meta analysis covering 138 experimental studies and 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring increased both monitoring and goal attainment; effects on attainment were larger when progress was physically recorded or reported publicly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 A later meta review of 66 meta analyses found support for components including self monitoring, goal setting and personalised feedback , but stressed that none worked consistently for every behaviour and population and that much of the evidence had methodological limitations. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That uncertainty strengthens rather than weakens the case for minimalism: use monitoring to resolve a specific uncertainty instead of assuming that a larger dashboard must produce better self regulation. Explanatory illustration 2 Measure occurrence before optimising quality There is another useful hierarchy inside behaviour measurement itself. When initiation is the bottleneck, measuring fine details of performance can be premature. Someone who repeatedly misses planned writing sessions probably does not first need a sophisticated “writing quality” score. Someone who cannot establish a walking routine may gain little from analysing pace zones. If meditation happens twice in a planned seven days, detailed ratings of depth or concentration can distract from the more basic failure mode. A good first measure therefore often has a binary or countable form: happened/did not happen , or completed opportunities/planned opportunities . Once occurrence becomes dependable, duration, volume or quality can become relevant if they answer the next question. This emphasis on enactment fits broader evidence on the"},{"title":"Current Uncertainty","url":"/current-uncertainty/","description":"A metric earns its place when a different result would lead you to continue, adjust or simplify what you are doing.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Simple Metrics","level":3,"text":"Current Uncertainty Measure the Question That Could Change Your Plan Simple Metrics Current Uncertainty Current Uncertainty Measure the Question That Could Change Your Plan A metric earns its place when a different result would lead you to continue, adjust or simplify what you are doing. A metric earns its place when a different result would lead you to continue, adjust or simplify what you are doing. Self Improvement That Works mechanism bounded topic mechanism Linking each metric to a specific decision Testing whether different results would trigger different actions Replacing interesting trivia with decision relevant information self improvement that works simple metrics current uncertainty Introduction A measurement earns its place in a self improvement plan when a different result could lead to a different next action . The practical question is not “What can I track?” but “What am I uncertain about that could make me continue, adjust or simplify this plan?” If every plausible result would leave the plan unchanged, collecting the number adds information without resolving a decision. Explanatory illustration 1 Decision science gives this principle a formal name: the value of information . Information is valuable when reducing uncertainty can improve a choice; if no possible finding would change the preferred action, its decision value is effectively zero. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Applied to self improvement, this suggests a deliberately narrow measurement strategy: identify the live uncertainty, decide in advance what different findings would imply, and collect only enough evidence to distinguish between the actions you are genuinely considering. Link each metric to a specific decision The same goal can require different measurements depending on what is currently uncertain. Suppose you intend to study four evenings a week but feel that progress is poor. One possibility is that the routine rarely happens; another is that you study consistently but the method produces weak learning. Those are different uncertainties, and they call for different metrics. If the first question is “Am I following the plan often enough to give it a fair test?” , the most useful measure may simply be planned sessions completed. If four sessions are scheduled and only one regularly happens, a recall score or sophisticated concentration measure is unlikely to change the immediate decision. The plan first needs to become more feasible. If four sessions are reliably happening, however, adherence is no longer the main uncertainty. A delayed recall test, practice problem or other outcome measure may now help answer whether the method itself should change. NICE's behaviour change guidance makes a related distinction between monitoring a behaviour and monitoring its outcome. It recommends reviewing progress and tailoring subsequent plans accordingly rather than treating measurement as an end in itself. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 That distinction matters because a metric can be perfectly accurate while answering the wrong question. Knowing precisely how many minutes you studied does not tell you whether poor retention is caused by an ineffective study method if the real problem is that most planned sessions never happened. A useful way to design a metric is therefore to work backwards from the decision: 1. What are the plausible next actions? For example, continue unchanged, alter the schedule, or simplify the routine. 2. What uncertainty prevents you choosing confidently between them? 3. What is the smallest observation that could reduce that uncertainty enough to choose? This makes measurement conditional rather than permanent. “Sessions completed” might be crucial for three weeks and almost irrelevant a month later. A metric should follow the question, not become part of the furniture simply because it was once useful. Test whether different results would trigger different actions Before collecting a metric, imagine several plausible results. If you cannot say how they would lead to materially different actions, the measurement is probably not ready to earn your attention. Consider a person experimenting with a morning writing routine. They are unsure whether the schedule is sustainable. Before tracking anything, they could define three broad cases: Finding after a suitable trialDecision implicationMost planned sessions happenKeep the scheduleAbout half happenAdjust timing or frequencyVery few happenSimplify the plan substantially The thresholds need not pretend to be universal scientific cut offs. Their purpose is to make the decision logic explicit . Without that step, it is easy to accumulate numbers and decide afterwards that whatever happened somehow supports the plan you already preferred. This is closely related to the logic behind formal value of information analysis. Such methods compare what can be achieved with current information against what could be achieved if some uncertainty were reduced. Crucially, when no possible information outcome would alter the decision, gathering more information has no expected decision value. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The everyday implication is powerful: a metric does not become useful merely because its value varies. The variation must cross a boundary that matters for action. This also reveals a common failure in personal experiments: collecting evidence before defining what evidence would count . Suppose someone tracks mood after meditation but has no idea what improvement would make them continue, what lack of improvement would make them change the practice, or how long they need to observe before judging. Almost any pattern can then be rationalised. By contrast, deciding beforehand that “after four weeks, I will keep this routine if it is feasible and there is a noticeable improvement in the outcome I care about; otherwise I will alter one element” gives the measurement an actual job. Pre specifying the decision does not eliminate judgement. Personal data are often noisy, and rigid responses to single observations can be counterproductive. The point is to decide the kind of evidence that could change the plan and the observation window over which you will judge it, rather than reacting to every daily fluctuation. Decision Analysis 4 (Tree): EVSI Expected Value of Sample Information Channel: Joshua Emmanuel Open on YouTube The useful metric sits at the current bottleneck Measurement becomes especially efficient when it targets the part of the plan that is currently most uncertain rather than attempting to describe the whole system. Imagine someone trying to exercise three times a week. There are several possible bottlenecks: the sessions are rarely started; sessions happen but are routinely cut short; the programme is followed but performance does not improve; the programme works but is so inconvenient that it is unlikely to last. Tracking every relevant variable at once — workouts, sets, repetitions, heart rate, sleep, soreness, motivation, steps and body weight — may produce a detailed picture without revealing which of those bottlenecks deserves action. A smaller approach works sequentially. First ask whether planned sessions happen. If not, the next experiment concerns feasibility. Once adherence is dependable, ask whether the programme produces the intended outcome. If it does, the remaining uncertainty may be whether the routine is sustainable. Each stage calls for different evidence. This does not mean self monitoring itself is unhelpful. A large meta analysis covering 138 experimental studies and 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase goal progress monitoring also improved goal attainment on average, with an effect size of d = 0.40. Effects were larger when progress was physically recorded or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 What that evidence supports is the usefulness of progress monitoring as a self regulation technique. It does not establish that collecting more variables, monitoring them more often or keeping them indefinitely produces better decisions. A broader meta review of 66 meta analyses reinforces the need for caution. It found support for techniques including self monitoring, goal setting and personalised feedback , but none was consistently effective for every behaviour and population, and the authors identified substantial limitations in the evidence base. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulation mechanisms in health behavior change: a systematic meta review of meta analyses, 2006 2017 PubMed... The useful question is therefore not whether tracking is good in the abstract. It is whether this"},{"title":"Retire Metrics","url":"/retire-metrics/","description":"Once a metric has resolved the uncertainty it was introduced to test, it can often be retired, sampled less often or replaced.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Simple Metrics","level":3,"text":"Retire Metrics When a Useful Metric Has Outlived Its Purpose Simple Metrics Retire Metrics Retire Metrics When a Useful Metric Has Outlived Its Purpose Once a metric has resolved the uncertainty it was introduced to test, it can often be retired, sampled less often or replaced. Once a metric has resolved the uncertainty it was introduced to test, it can often be retired, sampled less often or replaced. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention How temporary metrics prevent dashboards from expanding forever Signals that a measure has answered its original question Choosing whether to retire, reduce or replace the measure self improvement that works simple metrics retire metrics Introduction A personal metric should have an exit condition. If you began counting missed study sessions to discover whether an ambitious timetable was realistic, and several weeks of data have already shown that the timetable is the problem, continuing to count misses indefinitely adds little. The metric has done its job. The useful next move is to change the plan, then either retire the measure, check it less often or replace it with one that answers the next uncertainty. Explanatory illustration 1 This is not an argument against self monitoring. Experimental evidence shows that progress monitoring can improve goal attainment. The narrower point is that evidence for monitoring does not imply that every useful measure should become permanent. Research on personal informatics shows that people commonly stop, suspend or change tracking after learning what they wanted to know, while newer research finds that users adjust their mix of metrics as their circumstances and purposes change. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Temporary metrics stop the dashboard growing forever The easiest way for a personal dashboard to become unwieldy is to treat every useful measure as a permanent addition. A sleep experiment adds bedtime and wake time. A study experiment adds sessions completed. A running plan adds weekly mileage. A distraction problem adds phone pickups. None is individually unreasonable, but if measures are only added and never removed, yesterday's questions eventually compete with today's for attention. Research on personal informatics offers a better model. The original stage based framework described tracking as an iterative process involving collection, integration, reflection and action rather than data collection as an end in itself. Later research explicitly incorporated lapsing, stopping and resuming into the picture. In surveys covering physical activity , finance and location tracking, Epstein and colleagues found that people could stop because tracking had served its purpose, because its upkeep was burdensome or because their needs had changed. Some documentary tracking was deliberately short term: people accumulated enough information to reflect on it and then stopped. [personalinformatics.ianli.com] personalinformatics.ianli.com Model / Personal Informatics Lab Model / Personal Informatics Lab That matters for self improvement because collection has a cost. It may require entering information, wearing or charging a device, checking an application, interpreting charts or simply spending attention on a number that would otherwise have remained in the background. In one six month study involving repeated mobile assessments and photographic food records, adherence to both forms of self monitoring declined over time, particularly for the more demanding photographic records. Qualitative personal informatics research likewise identifies the effort of collecting and integrating data as a reason people abandon tracking. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to self monitoring healthy lifestyle behaviours through mobile phone based ecological momentary assessments... Measurement is not necessarily neutral either. A 2022 systematic review of digital, in the moment health measurements found evidence that measurement itself can alter behaviour; among the predominantly physical activity studies suitable for quantitative synthesis, pooled effects were small but meaningful. A much larger meta analysis of the broader “question behaviour effect” also found a small effect of measurement on health related behaviour, although with substantial variation between studies. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis of studies of reactivity to digital in the moment measurement of health behaviour PubMedMar... Sometimes that reactivity is precisely why tracking helps: seeing the number prompts correction. But it also means that a metric is an intervention on attention, not just an innocent record. Keeping a measure permanently visible can continue directing attention towards what it counts even after that is no longer the most useful thing to optimise. A practical design rule follows: when introducing a metric, write down the question that justifies it and the condition under which you expect to stop measuring it. For example: “Track planned workouts completed for four weeks to find out whether this schedule is realistic.” “Record bedtime for two weeks to see whether late nights are concentrated on particular days.” “Count unplanned phone checks during study sessions until the main trigger is clear.” “Measure practice test accuracy until there is enough evidence to identify the weakest topic.” This converts tracking from an accumulating autobiography into a sequence of small investigations. The Psychology of Bad Targets Goodhart's Law Explained Channel: Know your Theory Open on YouTube How to tell when a metric has answered its question A metric has not outlived its purpose merely because the number improved. The stronger test is whether additional observations are still likely to change the decision . Suppose you track whether four planned study sessions happen each week. The first two weeks show two sessions completed; the next three show only one or two. Your notes repeatedly identify the same obstacle: the planned 7 pm start conflicts with childcare. More weeks of identical counts may make the graph longer without materially increasing what you know. The uncertainty was “Can this schedule actually be followed?” The evidence now says “not reliably under present conditions”. The appropriate intervention is to redesign the schedule. Several signals are particularly useful. The result repeatedly points to the same action. If another week of data would lead to the same decision under almost any plausible result, the marginal value of daily tracking is low. The original uncertainty has been resolved. You introduced a measure because you genuinely did not know whether the problem was adherence, timing, workload, recovery or something else. Once the evidence distinguishes the plausible explanations well enough to act, collection can pause while you act. The behaviour has become sufficiently predictable for the present purpose. If you needed a daily count to establish whether a routine was happening and it has now been stable for months, daily confirmation may no longer be worth the attention. This does not prove that the behaviour can never relapse; it means continuous high frequency measurement may no longer be necessary. The decision has moved downstream. Once study attendance is reliable, the important uncertainty may become whether the sessions produce durable learning. Once a running schedule is consistently followed, the relevant question may become whether performance or recovery is changing. Keeping the old metric as the centrepiece because it once mattered confuses historical usefulness with current usefulness. This fits NICE's behaviour change guidance, which treats monitoring as part of an adaptive process: goals should be reviewed in light of experience, further plans should follow progress, and interventions should be tailored at planned review points. NICE also distinguishes monitoring a behaviour from monitoring its outcomes. The implication for personal use is not that monitoring should cease on a fixed timetable, but that the measure should remain connected to the question and decision it serves. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 Recent research makes the same point at the level of individual metrics. A 2025 study of fitness tracker users found that people used metrics for different purposes — including checking in, goal setting, self comparison and curiosity — and reselected metrics as their circumstances and purposes changed. The researchers describe an evolving “metric ecology”, rather than a fixed set of numbers that remains equally useful throughout a person's tracking journey. [DOI] doi.org The Framework of"},{"title":"Simpler Tracking","url":"/simpler-tracking/","description":"Weight-management trials suggest that reducing recording detail can preserve usefulness while making self-monitoring easier to sustain.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Simple Metrics","level":3,"text":"Simpler Tracking Can Simpler Tracking Work as Well as Detailed Logs? Simple Metrics Simpler Tracking Simpler Tracking Can Simpler Tracking Work as Well as Detailed Logs? Weight management trials suggest that reducing recording detail can preserve usefulness while making self monitoring easier to sustain. Weight management trials suggest that reducing recording detail can preserve usefulness while making self monitoring easier to sustain. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What abbreviated food and exercise diaries changed What the simplified calorie monitoring trial found Why these studies challenge maximal tracking without proving minimalism always wins self improvement that works simple metrics simpler tracking Introduction Weight management trials offer a useful test of a broader self improvement question: does a useful record have to be a detailed one? The evidence so far says not necessarily . Small randomised studies have found that abbreviated food and exercise diaries or tracking only selected high calorie foods can produce weight loss outcomes similar to detailed daily logging, while in some studies making recording easier to sustain. Explanatory illustration 1 That does not establish that simplified tracking is universally as effective as complete calorie logging. The direct comparison trials are small, and simplified systems deliberately discard information that may sometimes matter. What they do challenge is a common assumption: that a self monitoring system becomes more useful simply by capturing more detail. For a metric intended to guide the next decision, a somewhat coarse record that gets completed may sometimes outperform a theoretically richer record that people gradually stop keeping . What abbreviated diaries changed An unusually direct experiment appeared in 2007. Diane Helsel, John Jakicic and Amy Otto randomly assigned 42 adults in a 16 week correspondence based weight loss programme either to continue traditional detailed self monitoring of eating and physical activity or to transition to an abbreviated method. Everyone received the same broad targets: dietary intake of 1,200–1,500 kcal a day, less than 30% of energy from fat, and physical activity progressing towards 200 minutes a week. The variable of interest was how much participants had to record. The simplified approach did something important before weight loss was even considered: participants transitioning to abbreviated recording returned significantly more diaries than those using the traditional detailed method. Yet there was no significant difference in weight loss. Among participants who completed the intervention, average losses were 7.6 kg with abbreviated monitoring and 7.5 kg with detailed monitoring. Intention to treat results, which retain participants regardless of whether they completed the programme, were likewise similar: 4.3 kg versus 3.9 kg respectively. More revealingly, weight loss was correlated with the number of diaries completed (r=0.53), leading the investigators to argue that the process of continuing to self monitor might be more important than recording everything in detail. That distinction matters. Detailed logging can theoretically produce better information about calories, portions and patterns. But a self monitoring system has two stages before it can influence behaviour: the person must first record the information, then use what they recorded. If demanding more detail reduces completion sufficiently, extra precision can carry a behavioural cost. The result should not be treated as a definitive head to head victory for abbreviated diaries. Forty two participants constitute a small trial, the intervention lasted only 16 weeks, and the study bundled eating and exercise self monitoring together. It is better read as an early demonstration that removing recording requirements did not automatically remove the apparent benefit of monitoring . Eric Helms: Nutrition, Bodybuilding, & Supplementation for Strength and Aesthetics RP 114 Channel: Robinson Erhardt Open on YouTube What simplified calorie monitoring found A later randomised trial tested the same idea using smartphones and a much sharper contrast. The 2022 PATH study enrolled 72 parents with overweight or obesity who had children aged 2–12 at home. One group used conventional calorie monitoring: participants received a daily calorie target and entered the calories from their foods and drinks into an app. The simplified group instead used a traffic light system and recorded only “red” foods — energy dense items such as sweets, fried foods, crisps and sugar sweetened drinks. This was genuine information reduction. The standard group attempted to account for its whole diet. The simplified group was given a daily limit of three to five red foods, depending on starting weight, and recorded those rather than calculating total daily calories. Both groups otherwise received a six month smartphone delivered behavioural programme, an activity tracker, wireless scales, lessons, text messages and personalised weekly feedback . At six months, the standard group had lost an average 5.7% of initial body weight and the simplified group 4.0%; the between group difference was not statistically significant (p=0.25). Average losses in kilograms were 5.9 kg and 3.5 kg respectively, again without a statistically significant between group difference. Most strikingly, almost identical proportions achieved at least 5% weight loss: 43.2% with standard calorie monitoring and 42.9% with simplified monitoring. Nor did simplified recording merely produce weight change without a corresponding dietary shift. Independent dietary assessments indicated that both groups reduced average energy intake by roughly 250–300 kcal a day from baseline to six months, with no significant difference in the change between groups. There is an important wrinkle. The researchers had expected the easier system to improve adherence, but the two groups did not differ significantly in the number of days on which they tracked their diets. The experiment therefore does not demonstrate a simple chain in which “less detail → much more logging → better weight loss”. Instead, it shows something narrower and arguably more interesting: recording every calorie was not demonstrably necessary to produce useful behaviour change in this particular intervention. Explanatory illustration 2 When simplification improves engagement Another 2022 randomised pilot provides stronger evidence for the burden side of the argument. The Spark study recruited 38 racial and ethnic minority adults for a three month remote weight loss intervention. Eighteen were assigned to detailed daily recording of all food and drinks through the Fitbit app; 20 used a web checklist to record only high calorie, nutritionally limited “red zone” foods. Weight and steps were also monitored in both groups. Here the difference in dietary tracking was substantial. The median participant in the detailed group recorded diet on 49% of intervention days. In the simplified group, the median was 97%. Of the eight people across the study who recorded their diet every day, seven were in the simplified group. Despite that large engagement gap, average three month weight changes were almost indistinguishable: −3.41 kg in the detailed group and −3.29 kg in the simplified group. The study was designed primarily to assess feasibility and acceptability rather than to establish whether the two methods were statistically equivalent, so those numbers should remain descriptive. Still, they illustrate the trade off unusually clearly: roughly twice the frequency of dietary recording did not require collecting a complete dietary record . The simplified group met all 12 of the investigators' predefined feasibility benchmarks, whereas the detailed group met nine. Overall retention was 92%, and 89% of participants said they would recommend the intervention. This is especially useful evidence because earlier self monitoring research often relied heavily on predominantly white, female samples; a 2011 systematic review explicitly identified limited population diversity as a weakness of the literature. Yet Spark is also a warning against reading too much into tracking frequency alone. Within this tiny sample, dietary monitoring frequency was not significantly associated with three month percentage weight change in either treatment arm. More recording is therefore not automatically evidence of more effective self regulation. The record still has to capture something that can influence behaviour. 1741: Break Through a Training Plateau, Tips to Get Better Results from Your Workouts, & More Channel: Mind Pump Show Open on YouTube Why more detail is not automatically better The wider literature supports self monitoring much more confidently than it supports any particular amount of self monitoring. A systematic review of 59 behavioural weight loss intervention"},{"title":"Learning Checks","url":"/learning-checks/","description":"Once study sessions happen consistently, a small delayed-recall or unseen-practice test can reveal whether the routine is producing learning.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Simple Metrics","level":3,"text":"Learning Checks When Study Time Stops Being the Right Metric Simple Metrics Learning Checks Learning Checks When Study Time Stops Being the Right Metric Once study sessions happen consistently, a small delayed recall or unseen practice test can reveal whether the routine is producing learning. Once study sessions happen consistently, a small delayed recall or unseen practice test can reveal whether the routine is producing learning. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single case aspect population context Why completed sessions answer an adherence question rather than a learning question Using delayed recall or unseen practice to test learning Knowing when to keep, reduce or replace session count tracking self improvement that works simple metrics learning checks Introduction Once study sessions are happening reliably, counting them can become the wrong metric. “Four sessions completed this week” answers an adherence question: did the routine happen? It does not answer the question that now matters: did the routine produce learning that remains available after the material is no longer in front of you? Explanatory illustration 1 At that point, a small learning check is usually more informative than another week of session counts. The useful check is not an elaborate mock examination. It can be a few questions answered from memory after a delay, or several unfamiliar problems that require the same knowledge in a new form. Delaying the check matters because performance during or immediately after study can give a misleading impression of durable learning. Research on retrieval practice repeatedly finds that studying can look better in the short term while retrieval based conditions produce better retention later. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science Test Enhanced Learning Association for Psychological ScienceTest Enhanced Learning Psychological Science APS... The metric should therefore change when the uncertainty changes: establish that study happens, then test whether learning survives. Why completed sessions stop answering the important question A session count is a measure of behaviour , not a direct measure of its educational result. If somebody plans four study sessions and completes only one, that distinction hardly matters yet: adherence is the obvious problem. But after several weeks of reliably completing four out of four, another “4/4” supplies very little new information. The routine has demonstrated that it can happen. What remains unknown is whether those sessions produce knowledge that can later be retrieved or used. This is a particularly important distinction because performance during study and learning are not the same thing . Soderstrom and Bjork's review of the learning–performance distinction argues that observable performance during acquisition can be an unreliable indicator of the relatively lasting changes that constitute learning. Some conditions make practice feel or look successful without producing correspondingly durable knowledge; other, more difficult conditions can depress immediate performance while improving later retention. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science Learning Versus Performance Association for Psychological ScienceLearning Versus Performance Perspectives on Psychological Science APS... A classic experiment by Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke makes the problem concrete. Students either repeatedly studied prose passages or practised recalling them. Five minutes later, repeated study produced better performance. But when the final test came two days or a week later, previous testing produced substantially better retention. Students who repeatedly studied were also more confident about how much they would remember. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science Test Enhanced Learning Association for Psychological ScienceTest Enhanced Learning Psychological Science APS... That creates a measurement trap for a consistent student. Hours studied, chapters reviewed and sessions completed can all rise while the important outcome remains uncertain. Familiarity with notes can also make material feel accessible because the answer is present or has just been encountered. A learning check changes the task from recognising familiar material to demonstrating what remains available without that support. The shift is therefore not “stop caring about consistency ”. It is stop using an adherence metric as though it were an outcome metric . The Critical Distinction Between Learning and Performance, Dr. Robert Bjork Channel: LastingLearning.com Open on YouTube Use delayed recall to find out what survived The simplest replacement is a small delayed recall check. Study normally, put the material away, allow enough time for immediate familiarity to fade, and then try to produce the important knowledge from memory before looking at the answer. The delay does not have to turn every study cycle into an examination. Its purpose is diagnostic: to introduce some forgetting so that the check distinguishes information that remains retrievable from information that merely felt fluent while studying. The appropriate interval should reflect the goal. Material needed tomorrow warrants a shorter check than knowledge expected to remain useful weeks or months later. The evidence for making retrieval central is unusually substantial. In Roediger and Karpicke's prose experiments, the advantage of retrieval emerged on delayed rather than five minute tests. Karpicke and Roediger subsequently found that repeatedly testing already recalled material substantially improved recall one week later, whereas repeatedly studying already recalled material did not provide the same benefit. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Repeated retrieval during learning is the key to long term retention ScienceDirect... A 2021 systematic review of 50 experiments conducted in schools and classrooms, involving 5,374 learners, likewise found retrieval practice benefits across different education levels, subjects, test formats and delays, although the authors noted that the geographical diversity of the evidence was limited. [DOI] doi.org Retrieval Practice Consistently Benefits Student Learning: a Systematic Review of Applied Research in Schools and Classrooms Educati... A larger meta analysis of classroom quizzing, covering 222 studies and 48,478 students, estimated a medium overall achievement benefit, while also finding that the size of the effect varied with factors such as feedback , repetition and test format. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Testing (quizzing) boosts classroom learning: A systematic and meta analytic review PubMedApril 1, 2021... Published: April 1, 2021 For personal tracking, however, there is an important wrinkle: a learning check is both measurement and practice . Attempting retrieval can itself strengthen later memory. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Practicing Retrieval Facilitates Learning Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsPracticing Retrieval Facilitates Learning Annual ReviewsJanuary 4, 2021... Published: January 4, 2021 That is not a reason to avoid it. It simply means a five question recall check is not a chemically pure measurement instrument. In self improvement, that is often an advantage: the same few minutes can reveal weaknesses and contribute to learning. A minimal version might look like this: 1. After studying a topic, choose a small number of important prompts rather than testing every detail. 2. Wait until a meaningful delay has passed. 3. Answer without notes, answer keys or the source material visible. 4. Record something simple, such as 4/6 recalled correctly , rather than building a detailed scoring system. 5. Check the answers and correct important errors. Corrective feedback is especially sensible when recall fails, because an incorrect or missing answer should not simply be rehearsed as though it were correct. Reviews of retrieval research generally find that feedback can strengthen the benefits of testing, although the exact contribution varies across conditions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The critical role of retrieval practice in long term retention The critical role of retrieval practice in long term retention PubMed... Interestingly, failed retrieval need not make the attempt worthless: experimental work has found that an unsuccessful attempt followed by exposure to the correct answer can itself improve subsequent learning. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Unsuccessful retrieval attempts enhance subsequent learning Pub Med Unsuccessful retrieval attempts enhance subsequent learning The score's job is not to produce a flattering percentage. It is to reveal whether the present study method leaves important knowledge accessible after the study context has disappeared."},{"title":"Receptivity","url":"/receptivity/","description":"People can need behavioural support most when stress or competing demands leave them with the least attention available to use it.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / No Magic Formula","level":3,"text":"Receptivity What If the Best Moment to Help Is Wrong? No Magic Formula Receptivity Receptivity What If the Best Moment to Help Is Wrong? People can need behavioural support most when stress or competing demands leave them with the least attention available to use it. People can need behavioural support most when stress or competing demands leave them with the least attention available to use it. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context The difference between need and receptivity Why high pressure moments can defeat well timed support Designing prompts that respect attention and context self improvement that works no magic formula receptivity Introduction Help can be most necessary at exactly the moment a person is least able to use it. Stress, time pressure, competing tasks and emotional strain can raise the risk of abandoning a plan while simultaneously reducing the attention, working memory or practical freedom needed to respond to a prompt. Research on just in time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) therefore distinguishes need from receptivity : being vulnerable to an unwanted behaviour does not necessarily mean being willing and able to process support. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Healt... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement systems. A reminder that detects the “right” psychological moment can still be badly timed in practical terms. Effective personalisation therefore cannot simply ask, “When is this person most at risk?” It must also ask, “What, if anything, can this person realistically do right now?” Sometimes the best intervention is a tiny prompt, sometimes a different form of help, and sometimes no interruption at all. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicJust in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Health Behavi... Need and receptivity are different In behavioural intervention research, receptivity is more demanding than merely receiving a notification. It concerns whether someone is presently able and willing to receive, process and use the particular support being offered. That state can change quickly with mood, location, activity and social circumstances. Crucially, it also depends on the intervention itself: someone can be receptive to one form of help but unreceptive to another at the same moment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMC... Consider a stressful afternoon at work. Stress might make an old avoidance habit more tempting, so a behaviour system correctly identifies increased need. Yet the person might simultaneously be dealing with an urgent deadline or sitting in a meeting. A discreet reminder requiring almost no action might be usable; a five minute exercise demanding reflection, reading and several decisions might not be. The latest conceptual work on JITAIs explicitly treats receptivity as intervention specific rather than a simple on/off characteristic of the person. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMC... This creates a fundamental tension for adaptive self improvement: High behavioural risk does not imply high capacity to respond. Indeed, the two can move in opposite directions. Stress is a particularly clear example. A meta analysis covering 51 studies and 2,486 participants found that acute stress impaired working memory and cognitive flexibility overall, although its effects on inhibition were more complicated. In other words, some of the cognitive capacities useful for considering alternatives and implementing advice can deteriorate under the conditions in which behavioural support might appear most necessary. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effects of Acute Stress on Core Executive Functions: A Meta Analysis and Comparison with Cortisol PMCJune 28, 2016... Published: June 28, 2016 The implication should not be overstated: stress does not make people universally incapable of deliberate action, and laboratory findings about acute stress cannot be mechanically applied to every difficult moment in daily life. But the evidence gives a sound reason to reject the assumption that greater distress automatically justifies a more demanding intervention. A system that detects vulnerability but ignores available attention has solved only half of the timing problem. Adaptive Interventions in Digital Health: Misconceptions & Opportunities Inbal Nahum Shani – Mich Channel: mDOT Center Open on YouTube Why the “perfect moment” can be the wrong moment The problem becomes clearer when behavioural support is treated as an interruption rather than as abstract information. A notification competes with whatever the person is already doing. A field study of mobile notifications collected 10,372 notifications from 20 participants. Both response time and perceived disruption varied with factors including the task the person was performing, its complexity and how far through it they were. Importantly, useful or important notifications could still be disruptive. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk Open source on ucl.ac.uk. That finding exposes a weakness in simplistic “right message, right time” thinking. Relevance and receptivity are not synonyms. A message can be highly relevant to the user's goal while arriving at a moment when attending to it imposes an unreasonable switching cost. This is why JITAI frameworks consider both states of vulnerability or opportunity and states of receptivity . Researchers give straightforward examples: driving can make a text based intervention inappropriate; being in a meeting can make a phone call unusable even when a text might be acceptable; bad weather can make an outdoor exercise suggestion poorly matched even if an indoor alternative remains feasible. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicJust in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Health Behavi... There is a subtler version of the same problem. The circumstances producing the behavioural risk may themselves produce the lack of receptivity. A difficult work event can create a need for emotion regulation support while also occurring during a meeting. Morning time pressure can make a routine more likely to fail while leaving little attention for a digital intervention. Stress associated with smoking relapse risk may also impair cognitive processes needed to engage with the support being delivered. Recent JITAI scholarship highlights these conflicts as a central design problem rather than an edge case. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMC... So “intervene at the moment of maximum need” is not a safe general rule. The more useful target is an intersection: enough need to make support worthwhile + enough receptivity to make that particular support usable. And sometimes that intersection does not occur at the moment of peak vulnerability. Explanatory illustration 2 More prompts can create their own problem Poorly timed support has consequences beyond one ignored notification. Repeated demands can contribute to intervention burden, fatigue, frustration and habituation. JITAI researchers therefore treat “provide nothing” as a genuine intervention option rather than a system failure. Withholding a prompt can preserve attention and reduce unnecessary intrusion. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Building health behavior models to guide the development of just in time adaptive interventions: A pragmatic framewor... HeartSteps, an adaptive physical activity intervention, illustrates why restraint matters. In a six week micro randomised trial involving 44 adults, contextually tailored activity suggestions could be delivered up to five times a day. Walking suggestions increased subsequent steps on average, but their effect was much stronger near the beginning of the study and declined with time. For walking prompts specifically, the estimated effect on 30 minute step counts was initially a 107% increase relative to no suggestion, but the detectable advantage disappeared by about day 29. Participants also reported that suggestions became boring after several weeks, supporting habituation as one plausible explanation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That does not prove that every reminder inevitably wears out. It demonstrates something more useful: the effectiveness of a prompt is time varying . A message that worked repeatedly last week is not guaranteed to deserve the same share of someone's"},{"title":"Enough Convenience","url":"/enough-convenience/","description":"Once the main access barrier is gone, further convenience may add little and can turn optimization into avoidance.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Reduce Friction","level":3,"text":"Enough Convenience When Making a Habit Easier Stops Helping Reduce Friction Enough Convenience Enough Convenience When Making a Habit Easier Stops Helping Once the main access barrier is gone, further convenience may add little and can turn optimization into avoidance. Once the main access barrier is gone, further convenience may add little and can turn optimization into avoidance. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single case aspect policy intervention Evidence that extra convenience can hit diminishing returns How to identify the real remaining barrier Avoiding friction reduction as productive procrastination self improvement that works reduce friction enough convenience Introduction Reducing friction is useful when friction is actually what is stopping a good habit. Once the behaviour is already easy to start, however, another shortcut, app, piece of equipment or round of preparation may produce little benefit. At that point the limiting factor may be something else: weak motivation , an unclear cue, lack of skill, competing priorities, an unstable routine or simply too little repetition. Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters because behavioural research strongly supports removing genuine effort barriers, but it does not establish that making an already accessible behaviour progressively easier will keep producing comparable gains. Habit formation depends on repeated behaviour in sufficiently stable contexts, and behaviour more broadly depends on capability, opportunity and motivation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PubMed Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PMCNovember 29, 2023... Published: November 29, 2023 The practical stopping rule is therefore simple: remove friction until the main access barrier is no longer the problem, then diagnose what is actually preventing repetition. Otherwise, optimising the conditions for a habit can quietly replace doing the habit. Extra convenience eventually solves the wrong problem The evidence for reducing genuine friction is substantial. A meta analysis covering 184 field experiments and more than 2.2 million participants found that interventions changing effort had stronger effects, on average, than interventions targeting intrinsic motivation. The authors also warned that publication bias may have inflated the estimated advantage. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PubMed Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PMCNovember 29, 2023... Published: November 29, 2023 Governments use the same logic in “sludge audits”, which identify unnecessary time, procedural and psychological costs that prevent people from completing useful actions. [OECD] oecd.org fixing frictions sludge audits around the world 5e9bb35c en Fixing frictions: ‘sludge audits’ around the world OECDJune 12, 2024... Published: June 12, 2024 Concrete cases show why. UK behavioural insights work found that something as mundane as having to clear a loft could obstruct uptake of loft insulation, prompting a trial specifically designed to remove that hassle. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Open Policy Making toolkit Sensitive policy tools Guidance GOV.UKFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Another government report describes how removing a single unnecessary click from an online tax payment journey increased timely payment by 20%. [GOV.UK Assets] assets.publishing.service.gov.uk An exploration of potential behavioural biases in project delivery in the Department for TransportJuly 12, 2017... Published: July 12, 2017 In cases like these, convenience changes behaviour because it removes an identifiable obstacle between intention and action. But that evidence should not be stretched into a universal rule that less effort is always better . The relevant comparison in these interventions is usually a burdensome process versus a meaningfully easier one. It does not follow that reducing a four step routine to three steps, then three to two, then buying specialised equipment to shave another ten seconds off it, will generate comparable improvements. Research on habit formation points to a different source of diminishing returns. Habit automaticity typically develops through repetition in a consistent context, and its growth has often been modelled as an asymptotic process: gains are initially larger and then tend to become smaller as automaticity strengthens. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The crucial input is repeated performance, not an indefinitely optimised environment. A recent preregistered reanalysis of a six month longitudinal habit study reinforces that broader picture. Habit strength was associated with several factors, including behaviour frequency, context stability, autonomous motivation, positive feelings and perceived difficulty. In other words, difficulty matters, but it is one variable among several rather than a master lever that explains every stalled habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That gives friction reduction a natural boundary. If putting running shoes beside the door changes “I cannot get myself organised in the morning” into regular runs, the intervention has done useful work. Once the shoes are already there and leaving takes a minute, rearranging them again is unlikely to fix a problem that is now about skipped sessions, poor scheduling or reluctance to run. Dr. Susan Michie, The Behaviour Change Wheel Channel: Knowlege Translation Open on YouTube Find the barrier that remains A useful way to decide whether more convenience will help is to stop asking, “How can I make this easier?” and ask, “At exactly what point does the behaviour fail?” Behaviour change research provides a disciplined reason for doing this. The COM B model treats behaviour as the product of interacting conditions involving capability, opportunity and motivation. Intervention design is supposed to begin with diagnosis rather than automatically applying the same solution to every failure. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions PMC... That distinction can be applied to ordinary habits: The behaviour rarely starts because preparation is genuinely cumbersome. Friction is still a plausible target. Put the equipment where it is used, reduce unnecessary steps, pre select the task or move the activity into an existing route or routine. Everything is ready, but you forget. The problem is more likely to be cueing or prospective memory than access. A stable trigger or an explicit if–then plan may be more useful than another convenience. You remember and have access, but repeatedly choose something else. Motivation, competing rewards or priorities deserve attention. Making the target behaviour another few seconds easier may not change that competition. You begin but cannot perform the behaviour effectively. The bottleneck may be capability: knowledge, physical ability or skill. Training and appropriately scaling the task address a different problem from environmental convenience. You perform it sometimes but not consistently. Look at context stability and scheduling. In two longitudinal datasets, more stable contexts predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of habit repetition goals. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 You know exactly what to do but repeatedly fail at the decisive moment. Pre committing to a response can help. A classic meta analysis of 94 tests found that implementation intentions — specific if–then plans connecting a situation to an action — improved goal attainment, while a much larger 2024 synthesis of 642 tests found positive effects across behavioural and other outcomes. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. This is why a stalled habit should not automatically trigger another environmental redesign. COM B explicitly recognises that opportunity is only part of the behavioural system. Capability and motivation can be limiting conditions too, while habits themselves sit within motivation in the model. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions PMC... Habit research also cautions against expecting any single mechanism to carry too much weight. A 2024 state of the field review argues that habit is only one influence on behaviour and that habit formation may"},{"title":"Add Friction","url":"/add-friction/","description":"The same friction that blocks good habits can be deliberately placed in front of unwanted actions to make them less convenient.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Reduce Friction","level":3,"text":"Add Friction Make Bad Habits Harder to Reach Reduce Friction Add Friction Add Friction Make Bad Habits Harder to Reach The same friction that blocks good habits can be deliberately placed in front of unwanted actions to make them less convenient. The same friction that blocks good habits can be deliberately placed in front of unwanted actions to make them less convenient. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single mechanism mechanism What proximity studies reveal about small barriers Adding distance and steps to unwanted actions Keeping barriers proportionate and practical self improvement that works reduce friction add friction Introduction Adding friction means deliberately putting a small obstacle between yourself and an unwanted action: moving tempting food out of reach, keeping a distracting device in another room, logging out of an absorbing website, or introducing a short delay before an app opens. The principle is the mirror image of reducing friction for good habits. When an unwanted behaviour becomes even slightly less convenient, fewer automatic impulses make it all the way to action. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest evidence comes from research on proximity, food consumption and digital behaviour. Experiments suggest that surprisingly small increases in distance or delay can reduce consumption, although the size and durability of the effect vary and the evidence is not equally strong across behaviours. A broad meta analysis of behavioural field experiments also found that interventions changing effort had comparatively large effects, while warning that publication bias makes precise estimates uncertain. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PubMed Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PMCNovember 29, 2023... Published: November 29, 2023 The practical aim, then, is not to make ordinary life punitive. It is to place a modest, repeatable cost exactly where an unwanted behaviour would otherwise happen almost automatically. Habit Expert, Wendy Wood, breaks down the three bases of habit formation! (Behavioral Science) Channel: Pete Judo Open on YouTube What proximity studies reveal about small barriers Physical distance is one of the clearest demonstrations of behavioural friction because it can be measured in centimetres or footsteps. A snack within arm's reach can be taken almost without interrupting another activity. Move it far enough away that someone must reach, stand or walk, and the decision acquires a small cost. An influential 2006 office experiment illustrates the idea vividly. Forty participants were given chocolates in bowls that varied in visibility and location. When the sweets sat on the participant's desk rather than two metres away, participants ate an average of 1.8 more chocolates per day. Visibility had a separate effect: visible chocolates produced an additional 2.2 sweets consumed per day on average. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The office candy dish: proximity's influence on estimated and actual consumption PubMed... That individual study should not carry the argument by itself. Later randomised research has also manipulated snack distance. In one UK experiment, for example, a bowl of chocolate was positioned either 20 centimetres or 70 centimetres from seated participants, explicitly testing whether moving food from easy reach reduced consumption. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of snack food proximity on intake in general population samples with higher and lower cognitive resource PubMedFebruary 1... A subsequent experiment examined the effect when healthier and less healthy foods were simultaneously available, addressing the more realistic situation in which increasing friction for one option leaves another option accessible. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Impact of proximity of healthier versus less healthy foods on intake: A lab based experiment PubMedFebruary 1, 2019... Published: February 1, 2019 A larger workplace study provides an especially useful real world example. Researchers examined snack taking around beverage stations at Google offices. Among men, 23% of those using the drinks station nearer the snacks took a snack, compared with 12% at the more distant station. Among women, the corresponding figures were 17% and 13%. Nothing had been forbidden or priced differently; the relationship between the normal walking route and the tempting option had changed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Proximity of snacks to beverages increases food consumption in the workplace: A field study ScienceDirectAugust 1, 2016... Published: August 1, 2016 The broader evidence points in the same direction, but with an important qualification. A Cochrane systematic review identified 18 randomised studies of food proximity interventions. Across 15 comparisons from 12 studies involving 1,098 participants, placing food farther away produced a moderate reduction in consumption, with a standardised mean difference of −0.60. However, the reviewers rated the certainty of this evidence as low , partly because many studies had methodological limitations and much of the research occurred in laboratories rather than sustained everyday settings. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Altering the availability or proximity of food, alcohol, and tobacco products to change their selection and consumpti... That qualification matters for self improvement. The evidence supports “distance can influence behaviour” much better than it supports a precise rule such as “move something two metres away and consumption will fall by X per cent”. The useful mechanism is the interruption of easy access, not a magic distance. The secret habits that control your life Wendy Wood Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Add distance and steps before the unwanted action The proximity findings suggest a simple design principle: identify the shortest route from impulse to behaviour and lengthen that route slightly. Friction works particularly well when the unwanted action currently has virtually no starting cost. Consider a packet of biscuits beside a desk. Eating one requires a hand movement. Put the packet in a kitchen cupboard and the same impulse now requires standing up, leaving the room, opening the cupboard and returning. None of those actions is difficult. Their value lies in creating a boundary between wanting and doing. The same reasoning applies beyond food. A phone sitting beside the bed is available at the first hint of boredom; a phone charging outside the bedroom requires getting up. A bookmarked distracting site is one click away; logging out introduces authentication before entry. A television that automatically opens a streaming service presents a shorter path than one where the user must deliberately choose the service and programme. These changes need not make the unwanted behaviour impossible. Indeed, that distinction is useful. Friction preserves a choice while changing its convenience. If you genuinely want the biscuits, phone or website, you can still reach it. What becomes less likely is the low intention behaviour that occurs because the option was immediately available. Research on precommitment provides a related explanation. In behavioural science, precommitment means voluntarily restricting future access to temptation before encountering it. Experimental work has found that people can use such restrictions as an alternative to resisting temptation in the moment. The underlying logic is to make the earlier, goal aligned decision constrain the later choice made under temptation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Restricting Temptations: Neural Mechanisms of Precommitment Pub Med Central (PMC)Restricting Temptations: Neural Mechanisms of Precommitment For everyday habits, however, full restriction is often unnecessary. There is a continuum: Increase reach: put the object in a drawer rather than on the desk. Increase distance: store it in another room rather than beside you. Add a step: log out, remove a shortcut or require a deliberate confirmation. Add a delay: make access possible, but not instantaneous. Restrict access: use a stronger precommitment device when weaker barriers repeatedly fail. The best starting point is usually the smallest barrier that reliably interrupts automatic behaviour. Explanatory illustration 2 Digital friction shows that time can work like distance Modern digital habits make the mechanism particularly easy to see because opening an app can require almost no physical effort. Removing an icon from the first screen is a weak form of friction; requiring an extra action or waiting period is stronger. A 2023 study of the self nudging app one sec provides unusually direct evidence. The intervention intercepted attempts to open selected apps and inserted several features, including a short delay and"},{"title":"Gym Distance","url":"/gym-distance/","description":"Travel distance can become meaningful exercise friction, with research linking longer journeys to lower programme adherence.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Reduce Friction","level":3,"text":"Gym Distance Is Your Gym Too Far Away to Stick With? Reduce Friction Gym Distance Gym Distance Is Your Gym Too Far Away to Stick With? Travel distance can become meaningful exercise friction, with research linking longer journeys to lower programme adherence. Travel distance can become meaningful exercise friction, with research linking longer journeys to lower programme adherence. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What exercise adherence studies found about distance Why detours raise the threshold for starting Choosing exercise that fits an existing route self improvement that works reduce friction gym distance Introduction A gym can be excellent on paper and still be a poor choice for consistency if getting there requires a separate, inconvenient journey. Exercise research does not support a universal cut off at which a facility becomes “too far away”, but it does show a recurring pattern: greater travel distance is associated with lower attendance or adherence, particularly when exercise depends on repeatedly reaching a fixed facility. A systematic review of prescribed exercise in older adults judged greater distance from the exercise facility to be a negative predictor of adherence with moderate quality evidence. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Predictors of adherence to prescribed exercise programs for older adults with medical or surgical indications for exe... Explanatory illustration 1 One study makes the size of that friction unusually concrete. Among older adults enrolled in a community exercise programme, each additional kilometre between home and the gym was associated with an 8% lower probability of adherence after other measured predictors were considered. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to a Community Based Exercise Program is a Strong Predictor of Improved Back Pain Status in Older Adults: A... That does not mean everyone's adherence falls by exactly 8% per kilometre. It does mean that location can be part of the behaviour itself, rather than a trivial detail surrounding it. Motivational Monday Purposeful Friction – How to Design Your Environment for Success Channel: Rob Sumner Open on YouTube What exercise adherence studies found about distance The clearest single dataset example comes from an observational study of a community based adaptive physical activity programme for older adults with chronic lower back pain. Researchers examined factors associated with adherence and found that distance from home to the gym remained significant in their multivariable model. The odds ratio was 0.92 for each additional kilometre: in the authors' interpretation, every extra kilometre was associated with an 8% decrease in the probability of adhering to the programme. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to a Community Based Exercise Program is a Strong Predictor of Improved Back Pain Status in Older Adults: A... That result is especially useful because it concerns follow through , not merely people's stated attitudes towards exercise. Participants already had access to an organised programme; the question was who actually adhered. Distance was one of five variables that remained statistically significant after adjustment, alongside physical function, self rated health, duration of back pain and ratings of the trainer. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to a Community Based Exercise Program is a Strong Predictor of Improved Back Pain Status in Older Adults: A... In other words, the journey still mattered even though it was plainly not the only thing that mattered. A much larger English dataset points in the same direction. The Leeds Let's Get Active programme offered free access to specified sessions at 15 council leisure centres. Researchers analysed a year of attendance data covering 25,745 registered people and 185,245 visits before exclusions and cleaning. When predicting whether someone returned to the same leisure centre after an initial visit, distance from home was the most influential variable in the study's random forest analysis. For the estimated number of return visits, the difference between living 300 metres and 15 kilometres from a centre amounted to roughly seven to ten visits over a year. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Investigating the environmental, behavioural, and sociodemographic determinants of attendance at a city wide public health p... The relationship was not simply “near equals attendance, far equals none”. Age became more important when predicting the total number of return visits, and calendar month was most important for predicting attendance on a particular day. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Investigating the environmental, behavioural, and sociodemographic determinants of attendance at a city wide public health p... That distinction matters. Distance appears to be one ingredient in follow through, rather than a master variable capable of explaining exercise behaviour by itself. Evidence from prescribed exercise programmes strengthens that interpretation. A 2022 systematic review included 23 studies and 5,785 older adults prescribed exercise for cardiac, pulmonary, surgical, medical or neurological indications. After grading the available evidence, the reviewers concluded that greater distance from an exercise facility predicted poorer adherence with moderate quality evidence . They described distance as the only clear programme level factor predicting adherence in their review, while also noting that the relevant distance evidence came specifically from cardiac and pulmonary rehabilitation studies. [Springer Nature] link.springer.com Springer NaturePredictors of adherence to prescribed exercise programs for older adults with medical or surgical indications for exercise... There are also smaller examples in other populations. In a community exercise and support programme for African American breast cancer survivors, participants attending at least 70% of sessions lived an average of 6.6 miles from the facility, compared with 10.8 miles among the less adherent group; the difference was statistically significant. The sample was small enough that this should be treated as supporting rather than definitive evidence. [DOI] doi.org ClinMed International Library A Community Based Exercise and Support Group Program Improves Quality of Life in African American Brea... Why Consistency Beats Motivation (And How to Build the Habit) Catalyst Fitness Quickcast Channel: The Catalyst Method Open on YouTube Distance matters, but there is no universal “too far” It would be easy to turn these findings into an attractive rule such as “never join a gym more than five kilometres away”. The research does not justify such precision. First, much of the evidence is observational. People living nearer and farther from facilities can differ in income, transport access, health, neighbourhood design and other characteristics. The chronic back pain study, for example, adjusted for multiple measured factors and still detected an association with distance, but adjustment cannot prove that distance itself caused the difference. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Adherence to a Community Based Exercise Program is a Strong Predictor of Improved Back Pain Status in Older Adults: A... The systematic review likewise found useful but still limited evidence and explicitly called for more research across different exercise populations. [Springer Nature] link.springer.com Springer NaturePredictors of adherence to prescribed exercise programs for older adults with medical or surgical indications for exercise... Second, proximity does not behave identically across every activity or population. A German study of 1,768 adolescents found that girls in rural areas living farther from the nearest gym were less likely to participate in indoor sports, but proximity to tennis courts and indoor pools did not show equivalent associations with their corresponding activities. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Proximity to Sports Facilities and Sports Participation for Adolescents in Germany PMCMarch 27, 2014... Published: March 27, 2014 Research on children has similarly found that the distance participation relationship varies according to the type of sports facility; participation did not simply decline at the same rate with every additional kilometre. [Springer Nature] link.springer.com Springer NatureThe closer the sportier? Children’s sports activity and their distance to sports facilities European Review of Aging and... A Swedish study of 7,358 working adults adds another useful qualification. Greater distance from home to paid indoor and outdoor physical activity facilities was associated with exercising less frequently, whereas associations involving free outdoor facilities were not robust."},{"title":"Prepare Ahead","url":"/prepare-ahead/","description":"Moving setup work earlier can make a useful habit easier to start when time, energy or motivation is low.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Reduce Friction","level":3,"text":"Prepare Ahead Prepare Now So Good Habits Start Easier Reduce Friction Prepare Ahead Prepare Ahead Prepare Now So Good Habits Start Easier Moving setup work earlier can make a useful habit easier to start when time, energy or motivation is low. Moving setup work earlier can make a useful habit easier to start when time, energy or motivation is low. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Separating access effort from productive effort What to prepare before the moment of action When preparation turns into procrastination self improvement that works reduce friction prepare ahead Introduction Preparing your environment before a good habit begins means doing the access work while you have time and attention, so that the later moment contains as little unnecessary effort as possible. Put the book where you will read it, lay out exercise clothes, charge the equipment, fill the water bottle, open the working document, portion tomorrow’s ingredients, or decide the first task before the session starts. The point is not elaborate organisation. It is to make the useful action the next obvious action. Explanatory illustration 1 This fits a broader finding in behavioural science: changing effort can change behaviour, sometimes substantially. A meta analysis of 184 field experiments involving more than 2.2 million participants found that interventions targeting effort produced stronger effects, on average, than interventions targeting intrinsic motivation, although the authors warn that publication bias may exaggerate the difference. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PubMed Central (PMC)A meta analytic cognitive framework of nudge and sludge PMCNovember 29, 2023... Published: November 29, 2023 Preparation applies that principle at a very small, personal scale: move avoidable effort out of the vulnerable moment when the habit is supposed to begin. Separate access effort from productive effort A useful distinction is between access effort and productive effort . Productive effort is inherent in the habit: running the kilometres, practising the instrument, reading the chapter, doing the exercises or cooking the meal. Access effort merely gets you into a position to do that work: locating shoes, assembling equipment, clearing a desk, finding a file, choosing a recipe or deciding what to practise. Preparation should mainly attack the second category. Making a workout easier to start is different from making the workout physically undemanding. Likewise, opening tomorrow’s study materials tonight removes administrative effort without removing the intellectual effort of studying. This matters because environmental restructuring is recognised as a distinct behaviour change technique: the Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy defines restructuring the physical environment as changing surroundings to facilitate a wanted behaviour, while “adding objects to the environment” covers placing useful objects so that behaviour becomes easier to perform. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov An evidence synthesis and economic analysis NCBI BookshelfMay 1, 2018... Published: May 1, 2018 Evidence from choice architecture makes the underlying mechanism unusually concrete. A systematic review of interventions affecting NHS and other healthcare staff found that proximity interventions — making options easier or harder to reach — were frequently effective at changing food purchasing. In one included hospital intervention, margarine was placed in several accessible positions while butter had to be retrieved from a refrigerator; sales shifted accordingly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Choice architecture interventions to improve diet and/or dietary behaviour by healthcare staff in high income countries: a systemat... A separate systematic review likewise concluded that changing the order or proximity of foods can influence food choice, while noting that stronger long term research is needed. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Nudging consumers towards healthier choices: a systematic review of positional influences on food choice PubMedJune 1, 2016... Published: June 1, 2016 Those studies concern food environments rather than personal habit preparation, so they do not prove that putting trainers beside the door will establish an exercise habit. They demonstrate the narrower and more defensible principle: small differences in accessibility can alter what people do. Preparation exploits that principle by arranging accessibility in advance. There is a second benefit. Preparation can convert a vague intention into a concrete starting condition. Research on implementation intentions — plans linking a specified situation to a specified response — has repeatedly found that deciding in advance when, where and how to act can improve goal attainment. A classic meta analysis of 94 tests reported a medium to large overall effect, while a much larger 2024 meta analysis covering 642 independent tests found beneficial effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with stronger effects for contingent “if–then” plans. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... Environmental preparation and implementation intentions are not identical. But they can reinforce each other. “I will study tomorrow” still leaves tomorrow’s self to decide where, when and how to begin. “After breakfast, I will sit at the kitchen table and work through the three questions already laid beside my laptop” has both a defined cue and a prepared first action . How to Build Good Habits and Break Bad Ones with James Clear Channel: The Psychology Podcast Open on YouTube What to prepare before the moment of action The best preparation is usually the minimum that removes a predictable obstacle. Rather than trying to create a perfectly optimised environment, work backwards from the first minute of the habit and ask what could otherwise delay it. A practical preparation policy is: Put required objects at the point of use. Leave the book on the chair where you read, the instrument ready to play, the walking shoes beside the door or the notebook on the desk. This reduces retrieval effort and can also make the object itself a reminder. Make equipment operational, not merely available. Charge the headphones, refill the bottle, replace missing supplies, load the dishwasher before tomorrow’s cooking session or install the software before the planned work period. Remove preliminary decisions. Choose tomorrow’s workout, reading section, practice exercise or first work task beforehand. The later session can then begin with execution rather than selection. Prepare the first physical action. A document can already be open at the relevant section; ingredients can be grouped together; exercise kit can be laid out. Preparing the beginning is generally more valuable than trying to script every later step. Attach the setup to a stable context. If possible, leave the prepared materials where and when the behaviour is meant to happen rather than creating a separate reminder that must itself be remembered. Reset after finishing. Where practical, make restoring the environment part of the previous repetition: put the next tablet beside the breakfast bowl, return the book to the reading chair or replenish supplies after using them. Preparation then becomes part of a repeating loop rather than a separate project. Research into how people actually choose cues for new routines supports this approach. In a three week UK study, 39 participants trying to take vitamin C daily commonly selected cues that minimised effort, such as keeping the tablets nearby or visible. The researchers also found that vague plans and reliance on a single cue could be unreliable, and that participants often needed trial and error before finding a cue arrangement that worked in everyday life. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)What influences the selection of contextual cues when starting a new routine behaviour? An exploratory study PMCMar... That last finding is important because environmental preparation is sometimes presented as though there must be one clever setup that eliminates self control problems. Real routines are messier. A book placed on a bedside table may work until bedtime becomes irregular; gym clothes laid out at night may help until morning exercise starts conflicting with childcare or commuting. Preparation should therefore be treated as a small behavioural experiment: notice where the start keeps failing, remove that particular obstacle, and see whether repetition becomes easier. Explanatory illustration 2 Prepare cues as well as equipment A prepared environment does more than save seconds. Repeated behaviour can become associated with recurring contextual cues. A major review of habit psychology describes"},{"title":"Fewer Decisions","url":"/fewer-decisions-09247a/","description":"Preselecting known answers can remove the miniature planning sessions that make familiar routines harder to begin.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Reduce Friction","level":3,"text":"Fewer Decisions Stop Re Deciding Habits You Already Planned Reduce Friction Fewer Decisions Fewer Decisions Stop Re Deciding Habits You Already Planned Preselecting known answers can remove the miniature planning sessions that make familiar routines harder to begin. Preselecting known answers can remove the miniature planning sessions that make familiar routines harder to begin. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How repeated decisions add friction Preselecting workouts, meals and study tasks When keeping a choice is worth the effort self improvement that works reduce friction fewer decisions Introduction A routine is harder to start when it quietly contains a planning meeting. “ Exercise ” can still require deciding which workout, for how long and where. “Study” can mean choosing a subject, chapter and first task. Dinner can begin with deciding what to cook. One useful form of friction reduction is therefore simple: make recurring decisions before the moment when the habit is supposed to begin . Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest evidence does not support the popular idea that every trivial choice drains a fixed reservoir of “decision making energy”. Research on decision fatigue and choice overload is more mixed than that slogan suggests. The better supported mechanism is narrower: decisions become troublesome when options are difficult to compare, preferences are uncertain, or deciding competes with simply getting started. Preselecting a sensible default removes that deliberative step. Implementation intention research also shows that specifying what response will follow a particular cue can improve goal attainment, while stable contexts help repeated behaviours become more automatic. [doi.org] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... What Behavioral Science Says About Changing Habits Around the New Year Channel: Knowledge at Wharton Open on YouTube How repeated decisions add friction The crucial distinction is between deciding what you want to achieve and repeatedly deciding how to enact a decision you have already made. The first can be valuable. The second can become avoidable friction. Suppose someone has already decided to exercise three mornings a week. If Tuesday morning still presents a menu — run or lift weights, home or gym, 20 or 40 minutes, legs or upper body — the person has not yet reached the exercise itself. They have reached another decision. On a good morning that may barely matter. On a rushed morning it creates another point at which the routine can stall. Implementation intentions offer particularly relevant evidence. These plans connect a specified situation with a specified response: in effect, deciding in advance what happens when a cue occurs. A 2006 meta analysis covering 94 independent tests reported a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, and proposed that specifying the response in advance makes the relevant opportunity more accessible while making initiation more automatic. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... A much larger 2024 meta analysis, covering 642 independent tests, found beneficial effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with effect sizes ranging from d = 0.27 to 0.66. Effects were stronger when plans took a contingent if–then form, when people were motivated to pursue the goal and when plans were rehearsed. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... This does not mean every routine needs to be written as a formal if–then statement. The practical principle is to turn an open question into a prepared response. Instead of reaching 7am and asking, “Should I exercise, and what should I do?”, the relevant answer might already be: “On Tuesday after breakfast, I do workout A.” That distinction matters because the evidence on choice itself is nuanced. A 2010 meta analysis of 50 experiments found that simply increasing the number of options had an average choice overload effect close to zero, with substantial variation between studies. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicCan There Ever Be Too Many Options? A Meta Analytic Review of Choice Overload Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academic... A later meta analysis found that overload was more likely when the decision was difficult, the options were complex, the chooser was uncertain about their preferences, or the aim was to minimise decision effort. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta analysis Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta analysis ScienceDirectApril 1, 2015... Published: April 1, 2015 So “choices are exhausting” is too crude. A more defensible rule is: remove recurring choices when they add complexity without adding useful adaptation . There is another reason preselection fits habit building particularly well. Habit research describes habits as responses that become increasingly cued by recurring contexts rather than requiring fresh deliberation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... In a six week study of university students, greater stability in the context of study habit repetitions predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals; a second dataset from users of a habit building app showed the same pattern. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... A default response therefore does two jobs: it removes an immediate decision and helps preserve the consistency from which automaticity can develop. How to change your behavior for the better Dan Ariely Channel: TED Open on YouTube Preselect workouts, meals and study tasks The most useful defaults are concrete enough to start immediately but not so rigid that they survive after circumstances change. Three familiar routines show what that looks like. Workouts: decide the session, not merely that you will exercise. A calendar entry saying “exercise” leaves much of the behavioural work unresolved. “Monday: workout A; Wednesday: workout B; Saturday: 30 minute run” converts several recurring choices into previously selected answers. The person can still deviate when necessary, but deviation becomes the exception rather than the starting procedure. Evidence from physical activity interventions supports advance planning, although it should not be oversold as a universal solution. A 2022 systematic review and meta analysis covering 41 randomised trials, with 35 included in the quantitative synthesis, found that planning interventions produced a small to moderate improvement in physical activity ( SMD = 0.35, 95% CI 0.25–0.44). Results varied considerably between studies, and interventions using reinforcement during follow up tended to work better. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Effectiveness of Planning Interventions for Improving Physical Activity in the General Population: A Systematic Review and Meta... An earlier review likewise found that reinforced implementation intention interventions improved physical activity, while another systematic review emphasised that results depend partly on factors such as existing motivation and self efficacy. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of implementation intentions on physical activity practice in adults: A systematic review and meta analysis of... The practical target is consequently not maximal scheduling. It is eliminating the predictable question that appears at the start of every session. A beginner might use a small rotation of established workouts; an experienced athlete whose training genuinely needs day to day adjustment has more reason to retain choice. Meals: decide from a small repertoire before hunger becomes the planning system. Meal planning is a particularly intuitive application because eating requires repeated decisions every day. A weekly plan can preselect several breakfasts, lunches and evening meals while still leaving room for social occasions, leftovers or changed appetites. The evidence here is useful but less causal. In the French NutriNet Santé study, researchers examined meal planning among 40,554 adults. Fifty seven per cent reported planning meals at least occasionally. Meal planning was associated with greater adherence to nutritional guidelines and greater food variety; planners also had lower odds of obesity, although the study's cross sectional design means it cannot establish that planning caused those outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open"},{"title":"Broken Sleep","url":"/broken-sleep/","description":"Repeated awakenings can impair sustained attention even when total time in bed is not dramatically reduced.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Focus","level":3,"text":"Broken Sleep Interrupted Sleep Can Hurt Attention Too Sleep and Focus Broken Sleep Broken Sleep Interrupted Sleep Can Hurt Attention Too Repeated awakenings can impair sustained attention even when total time in bed is not dramatically reduced. Repeated awakenings can impair sustained attention even when total time in bed is not dramatically reduced. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single case aspect mechanism What repeated nighttime awakenings do to vigilance How fragmented sleep compares with shortened sleep Why uninterrupted sleep opportunity matters self improvement that works sleep and focus broken sleep Introduction Sleep can be long enough on the clock and still leave attention less reliable the next day. Sleep fragmentation means repeated arousals or awakenings that break otherwise continuous sleep. Experimental studies show that these interruptions can increase daytime sleepiness and impair sustained attention even when total sleep time changes little or, in some protocols, is deliberately preserved. In one controlled experiment, participants obtained about 396 minutes of sleep during a fragmented night versus 400 minutes during undisturbed sleep, yet fragmentation made it harder to remain awake and worsened measures that included sustained attention. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicEffect of Sleep Fragmentation on Daytime Function American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine Oxford Acade... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. Counting hours is useful, but it does not capture whether those hours were consolidated. A nominally adequate sleep window can therefore coexist with poorer next day vigilance if sleep is repeatedly interrupted. The practical target is not merely enough opportunity to sleep, but enough uninterrupted sleep opportunity for sleep to remain restorative. What repeated awakenings do to vigilance The strongest evidence for an effect independent of substantially shorter sleep comes from experiments that deliberately fragment sleep while keeping total sleep time similar. A particularly clear example involved 16 healthy participants whose sleep was disturbed with sound pulses approximately every two minutes. The sounds were adjusted to produce brief electroencephalographic, or EEG, arousals. Despite this intensive disruption, total sleep time was essentially unchanged: 396 ± 24 minutes after fragmentation versus 400 ± 20 minutes after undisturbed sleep , a statistically non significant difference. The next day, however, participants became sleepy more quickly on the Multiple Sleep Latency Test and were less able to maintain wakefulness on the Maintenance of Wakefulness Test. Performance measures also indicated poorer mental flexibility and sustained attention. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicEffect of Sleep Fragmentation on Daytime Function American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine Oxford Acade... An even subtler experiment suggests that disruption need not always become an obvious awakening. Twelve healthy participants were exposed to sounds approximately every minute, most of which produced autonomic responses — such as transient changes in heart rate or blood pressure — without a conventionally visible EEG arousal. Total sleep time again barely changed: 414 ± 32 minutes during fragmentation versus 419 ± 27 minutes during undisturbed sleep . Nevertheless, slow wave sleep fell and participants were objectively sleepier the following day on both sleep latency and wakefulness maintenance tests. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The effect of nonvisible sleep fragmentation on daytime function The effect of nonvisible sleep fragmentation on daytime function PubMed... Other laboratory work has found electrophysiological signs of impaired attention after fragmentation without reduced total sleep time. Measurements of waking EEG activity and auditory event related potentials — brain responses to stimuli — indicated reduced arousal and impaired information processing after experimentally fragmented sleep. The investigators concluded that this relatively subtle disruption produced sustained impairment of alertness and attention despite no reduction in total sleep time. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These experiments expose the weakness of treating nightly duration as a complete measure of restorative sleep. Two nights can contain roughly the same number of scored minutes asleep while differing considerably in how those minutes are organised. Why You Wake Up Tired Even After 8 Hours : Fix These Morning Killers Fast Channel: Medical Centric Podcast Open on YouTube Why the same number of hours can work differently Sleep is not simply an accumulation of interchangeable unconscious minutes. During a normal night, the brain moves through stages of non rapid eye movement and rapid eye movement sleep in an organised pattern. Repeated arousals can force transitions towards lighter sleep, interrupt deeper stages and alter the continuity of that architecture. In the experiment involving autonomic fragmentation, for example, slow wave sleep declined from 24% to 20% even though total sleep time remained virtually unchanged. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The effect of nonvisible sleep fragmentation on daytime function The effect of nonvisible sleep fragmentation on daytime function PubMed... A broader review of physiological markers of sleep quality found that greater fragmentation generally tracked poorer perceived sleep quality, while slow wave sleep showed moderate associations with better performance on vigilance, motor speed and executive function tasks. The review also emphasised that these relationships are inconsistent across studies, so no single sleep stage should be treated as a simple explanation for next day performance. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Physiological markers of sleep quality: A scoping review Physiological markers of sleep quality: A scoping review ScienceDirectAugust 1, 2022... Published: August 1, 2022 Frequent arousals also repeatedly shift the sleeping brain towards wakefulness. An older physiological experiment found that brief, frequent arousals increased overnight metabolism and reduced morning performance, mood and alertness even though total sleep time was only minimally reduced. The authors did not claim that the metabolic changes themselves caused the impairment, but the findings illustrate that apparently small interruptions are physiologically active events rather than neutral gaps in an otherwise restorative night. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Metabolism during normal, fragmented, and recovery sleep Metabolism during normal, fragmented, and recovery sleep PubMed... This helps explain the basic mechanism without overstating it. Fragmentation can reduce the restorative value of a given quantity of scored sleep by changing its continuity and composition. The downstream expression may be greater sleep pressure during the day, reduced alertness and less dependable information processing. There is, however, an important scientific qualification. Researchers have debated whether “continuity” is truly an independent restorative property or whether fragmentation simply reduces the amount of effective restorative sleep. A review and reanalysis argued that some apparent continuity effects could be explained by fragmentation increasing stage 1, or very light, sleep: conventional total sleep time calculations count those minutes as sleep even though they may contribute relatively little to recuperation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Does sleep fragmentation impact recuperation? A review and reanalysis Does sleep fragmentation impact recuperation? A review and reanalysis PubMed... For everyday purposes, the distinction does not overturn the practical finding. Whether interruptions damage attention because continuity itself is necessary, because they replace deeper sleep with less restorative light sleep, or through a mixture of these processes, hours alone can conceal a meaningful loss of sleep quality . Explanatory illustration 2 Fragmented sleep versus shortened sleep Fragmentation and sleep restriction are different manipulations. Restriction reduces the opportunity to sleep; fragmentation repeatedly interrupts sleep within an available sleep window. Both can impair next day function, but they should not automatically be treated as physiologically identical. A useful head to head experiment studied 61 healthy adults aged 20–29. One group experienced a four hour sleep opportunity; another had an eight hour opportunity interrupted by four awakenings lasting roughly 15 minutes each. Compared with their normal nights, both conditions increased omission and commission errors on a sustained attention task . Both also increased fatigue and confusion and reduced vigour. The researchers detected no significant difference"},{"title":"Sleep Debt","url":"/sleep-debt/","description":"Several nights of restricted sleep can produce steadily more attention lapses even when the schedule starts to feel familiar.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Focus","level":3,"text":"Sleep Debt Why Attention Gets Worse Across Short Nights Sleep and Focus Sleep Debt Sleep Debt Why Attention Gets Worse Across Short Nights Several nights of restricted sleep can produce steadily more attention lapses even when the schedule starts to feel familiar. Several nights of restricted sleep can produce steadily more attention lapses even when the schedule starts to feel familiar. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic event time window How vigilance declines across repeated restricted nights What four , six , and seven hour sleep schedules show Why impairment can build instead of stabilizing self improvement that works sleep and focus sleep debt Introduction Repeated short sleep does not necessarily settle into a harmless new normal. Controlled laboratory studies show that when sleep is restricted night after night, failures of sustained attention can accumulate across days. The clearest example comes from a 14 night experiment in which healthy adults given four or six hours in bed developed progressively more lapses on the psychomotor vigilance test (PVT), whereas an eight hour group did not show the same deterioration. The shorter the sleep opportunity, the faster the impairment accumulated. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Explanatory illustration 1 The particularly important finding for self improvement is that feeling accustomed to short sleep is not the same as performing normally on it . In the same experiment, participants' subjective sleepiness rose early and then changed relatively little, even while objective attention continued to deteriorate. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... That mismatch makes repeated restriction different from an obviously exhausting all nighter: a person can become familiar with the feeling of the schedule while vigilance failures continue to accumulate. How vigilance declines across repeated restricted nights The landmark 2003 experiment led by Hans Van Dongen randomised healthy adults to four, six or eight hours of time in bed per night for 14 consecutive nights . Participants repeatedly completed the PVT, a simple sustained attention task in which they respond as quickly as possible to unpredictable signals. One of its most useful measures is the number of unusually slow responses, conventionally called lapses . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The result was a dose response pattern across both sleep duration and time . Four hour nights produced the steepest deterioration. Six hour nights produced a slower but still continuing build up. Eight hour nights did not produce a significant accumulation of PVT lapses. A later review of the experiment reports that by the seventh restricted day, participants in the six hour condition averaged about 54 lapses across that day's nine PVT sessions, compared with about 70 in the four hour condition. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMC... That time course is crucial. If people simply adapted to reduced sleep, performance should deteriorate initially and then settle at a stable level as they became accustomed to the schedule. Instead, in this experiment the four and six hour groups continued to accumulate cognitive deficits across the two weeks. The original researchers found that chronic restriction to six hours or less eventually produced performance deficits comparable in magnitude to those observed after as much as two nights of total sleep deprivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... A later review of vigilant attention describes the four hour condition as reaching PVT impairment comparable with roughly two to three days of acute total sleep deprivation by the end of the restriction period. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PubMedJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 The comparison should not be read as saying that two weeks of four hour nights and several sleepless nights are biologically identical. Rather, it illustrates how severe the measured vigilance deficit from repeated partial sleep loss can eventually become. This cumulative pattern also helps distinguish repeated restriction from a single bad night. A 2024 meta analysis of 44 studies found that even one night restricted to two to six hours can increase sustained attention reaction times and lapses. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis Scien... Chronic experiments add the more consequential finding: when the short nights keep coming, the next day's deficit need not simply repeat yesterday's. It can build on it. PEBL Psychomotor Vigilance Task (PVT) Channel: PEBL Video Tutorials Open on YouTube What four , six and seven hour schedules show Two influential sleep dose experiments published in 2003 approached the question with somewhat different schedules. Together, they provide stronger evidence than either experiment alone — but they also show why it is too simple to claim that attention always deteriorates at exactly the same rate whenever sleep falls below a particular number of hours. Four hours: In the Van Dongen experiment, four hours in bed for 14 nights produced a pronounced, continuing rise in vigilance lapses. The deficit was greater than in the six hour group, establishing a clear dose response effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Four hours is therefore not merely an example of feeling very sleepy after a short night; under repeated laboratory restriction, it produced progressively less reliable attention over a fortnight. Six hours: Six hours is arguably the more instructive condition because it resembles a schedule that some people may regard as sustainable. Yet PVT lapses also accumulated across the 14 night experiment. The six hour group deteriorated more slowly than the four hour group, but it did not simply return to baseline performance after becoming accustomed to the routine. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMC... Seven hours: The picture becomes less uniform near the milder end of restriction. Gregory Belenky and colleagues assigned 66 volunteers to three, five, seven or nine hours in bed each night for seven days. PVT speed and lapses worsened steadily in the three hour group. In the five and seven hour groups, response speed initially deteriorated and then appeared to stabilise at a reduced level; lapses were elevated in the five hour condition, while the seven hour condition showed reduced response speed without the same significant increase in lapses. The nine hour group remained near baseline. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The two experiments therefore should not be compressed into a rigid claim that every restricted schedule produces an indefinitely linear decline. Belenky's study found evidence of lower level stabilisation under milder restriction , whereas Van Dongen's study found continuing cumulative impairment during six hour nights across 14 days. A recent theoretical review explicitly notes this difference between the experiments. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The Sleep Opportunity, Need and Ability (SONA) Theory Pub Med Central (PMC)The Sleep Opportunity, Need and Ability (SONA) Theory Their broader conclusions nevertheless converge. Across controlled sleep dose studies, giving healthy adults progressively less sleep produces progressively poorer vigilant attention, and repeated restriction can leave performance substantially below the level maintained with adequate sleep opportunity. Reviews of this literature conclude that sustained attention is particularly sensitive to chronic restriction and that significant deficits are more likely to accumulate when healthy adults repeatedly receive less than about seven hours of sleep. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMC... Importantly, these are experimental time in bed conditions , not precise prescriptions for every"},{"title":"False Adaptation","url":"/false-adaptation/","description":"People may stop feeling progressively sleepier during chronic sleep restriction even while objective attention performance continues to worsen.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep and Focus","level":3,"text":"False Adaptation Feeling Used to Less Sleep Can Mislead You Sleep and Focus False Adaptation False Adaptation Feeling Used to Less Sleep Can Mislead You People may stop feeling progressively sleepier during chronic sleep restriction even while objective attention performance continues to worsen. People may stop feeling progressively sleepier during chronic sleep restriction even while objective attention performance continues to worsen. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism How subjective sleepiness and objective performance diverge Why feeling normal does not prove vigilance has recovered What this mismatch means for self assessment self improvement that works sleep and focus false adaptation Introduction Repeatedly sleeping too little can create a deceptive sense of adaptation. After the first few restricted nights, people may stop feeling progressively sleepier even though their ability to sustain attention is still impaired or continuing to deteriorate. In the classic 14 day laboratory study behind this finding, participants restricted to four or six hours in bed showed cumulative cognitive deficits, while their subjective sleepiness rose sharply at first and then changed relatively little. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement because “I feel fine on this amount of sleep” is not a reliable performance test. Feeling accustomed to short sleep can reflect adaptation in how the sleep loss feels , without equivalent recovery in how reliably attention works . Later experiments have reproduced the broader mismatch between subjective alertness and objective vigilance, including situations in which sleep restricted people reported alertness comparable with controls despite markedly worse reaction times and attentional lapses. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Chronic sleep curtailment, even without extended ( 16 h) wakefulness, degrades human vigilance performance PMCJune... How feeling sleepy and performing poorly diverge The clearest demonstration comes from Hans Van Dongen and colleagues' controlled sleep dose experiment. Healthy adults were assigned to four, six or eight hours of time in bed per night for 14 days, while another group underwent total sleep deprivation. Restricting sleep to four or six hours produced cumulative, dose dependent deficits across cognitive tests. On measures of behavioural alertness, impairment continued to build as restricted nights accumulated. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Subjective experience followed a different trajectory. Participants initially noticed the restriction: sleepiness increased rapidly. But subsequent increases were comparatively small. A later review of the experiment describes psychomotor vigilance test (PVT) lapses increasing approximately linearly across the 14 restricted nights, whereas subjective sleepiness and fatigue showed a much flatter trajectory. By the end, when objective performance was at its poorest, participants in the four and six hour conditions reported being only slightly sleepy. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMC... This is the phenomenon that can easily be mistaken for successful adaptation. The person has genuine evidence from experience that something has changed: getting through the day may no longer feel progressively harder. What does not follow is that their attention has returned to normal. A separate seven day sleep dose experiment illustrates why the word “adaptation” needs care. Belenky and colleagues assigned participants three, five, seven or nine hours in bed. PVT performance deteriorated sharply under severe restriction; with five or seven hours, some performance measures eventually stabilised, but at an impaired level rather than returning to baseline. The researchers therefore discussed physiological adaptation that could stabilise performance at reduced capacity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The two findings are compatible rather than contradictory. Under some schedules objective deterioration can accumulate; under others it can level off below baseline. The important point for self assessment is that neither a plateau in perceived sleepiness nor becoming accustomed to a schedule establishes that normal vigilance has been restored . Episode 175: Hans Van Dongen on how fatigue and sleep loss lead to cognitive deficits Channel: TheIHMC Open on YouTube Why feeling normal does not prove vigilance has recovered Subjective sleepiness and vigilant attention are related, but they are not interchangeable measurements. A sleepiness scale asks, in effect, “How sleepy or alert do I feel?” A vigilance task measures something different: whether attention can be maintained reliably enough to respond to unpredictable events without unusually slow responses or lapses. Experiments designed specifically to compare these signals show how far apart they can move. In one forced desynchrony study, 27 healthy young men underwent either a standard or sleep restricted schedule while researchers separated the effects of biological time from time awake. Under restricted sleep, objective PVT impairment exceeded the deterioration participants reported in subjective alertness. The mismatch was particularly pronounced during the biological night, leading the researchers to conclude that sleep restricted people were likely to underestimate their neurobehavioural impairment at that time. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Mismatch between subjective alertness and objective performance under sleep restriction is greatest during the biological night P... An especially revealing experiment later removed a possible alternative explanation. Perhaps chronic sleep restriction hurts performance simply because short sleepers spend unusually long periods awake each day. Researchers therefore used a 32 day inpatient protocol in which restricted participants received only 4.67 hours of sleep opportunity per cycle but were never awake for more than 15.33 hours at once. Even without unusually prolonged waking episodes, chronic short sleep produced reaction time impairment reaching roughly twice the control level and as much as a fivefold increase in PVT lapses. Yet there were no significant overall differences between restricted and control groups in subjective alertness or sleepiness. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is stronger evidence than simply observing that tired people occasionally misjudge themselves. It shows that objective vigilance and the subjective experience of alertness can follow substantially different responses to chronic insufficient sleep . The mismatch is not perfectly uniform from person to person. The same study found considerable individual variation in the relationship between subjective alertness and PVT performance. Another modelling study found that subjective alertness was informative about performance, but insufficient on its own: predictions of PVT performance improved when circadian phase, time awake and accumulated sleep loss were also included. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Chronic sleep curtailment, even without extended ( 16 h) wakefulness, degrades human vigilance performance PMCJune... So the correct lesson is not that feelings are meaningless. Someone who feels profoundly sleepy has useful information. The problem is the reverse inference: not feeling very sleepy cannot safely be translated into “therefore my attention is unimpaired”. Explanatory illustration 2 The mismatch can hide inside an apparently good day One reason false adaptation is persuasive is that sleep restriction does not necessarily make every moment feel or look dysfunctional. Circadian rhythms create periods when the biological clock promotes wakefulness, and motivation or engaging circumstances can also alter how alert someone feels. The resulting experience can be convincing: a person may work, talk and exercise without an obvious sensation of exhaustion while still being more vulnerable to attentional failures. Research separating circadian timing from sleep loss makes this particularly clear. Under chronic restriction, objective performance becomes substantially worse around the biological night, while subjective ratings do not necessarily change by the same magnitude. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Mismatch between subjective alertness and objective performance under sleep restriction is greatest during the biological night P... In the long inpatient experiment, the circadian system still produced"},{"title":"Routine Anchors","url":"/routine-anchors/","description":"A behaviour that already happens reliably can provide a stronger anchor for a new habit than a vague intention to act later.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Stable Contexts","level":3,"text":"Routine Anchors Why Existing Routines Make Powerful Habit Anchors Stable Contexts Routine Anchors Routine Anchors Why Existing Routines Make Powerful Habit Anchors A behaviour that already happens reliably can provide a stronger anchor for a new habit than a vague intention to act later. A behaviour that already happens reliably can provide a stronger anchor for a new habit than a vague intention to act later. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism Why established actions create dependable cues What the flossing after brushing study suggests How to choose the right position in a routine self improvement that works stable contexts routine anchors Introduction An existing routine can make a powerful anchor for a new habit because it supplies something a vague intention such as “I’ll do it later” does not: a concrete event that already happens reliably. Instead of repeatedly deciding when to act, you link the new behaviour to a recognisable point in something you already do — for example, flossing after brushing your teeth, taking a supplement after breakfast, or doing a brief stretch after shutting down your computer. Explanatory illustration 1 The mechanism is straightforward. Repeatedly encountering the established action and then performing the new one gives the brain a stable cue–response pairing to learn. Over time, the preceding routine can become a prompt for the added behaviour. Research supports this general approach, although it also adds an important qualification: routines are useful anchors because they can make repetition dependable, not because attaching one action to another automatically guarantees a habit. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com What is habit and how can it be used to change real‐world behaviour? Narrowing the theory‐reality gap Gardner 2024 Social an... Why established actions create dependable cues A routine anchor turns an intention into a specific contingency: when this familiar event happens, I do that . Psychologists study closely related plans as “implementation intentions”. Research on their mechanisms suggests that specifying a cue can make that opportunity easier to notice and strengthen the association between the situation and the intended response. A recent meta analysis covering 642 independent tests found implementation intentions beneficial across behavioural and other outcomes, with stronger effects when plans used an explicit contingent format and were rehearsed. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubMechanisms of implementation intention effects: The role of goal intentions, self‐efficacy, and accessibility of plan compon... An established routine has an additional practical advantage: the cue does not have to be created from scratch. If you reliably brush your teeth every evening, “after brushing” already occurs without needing another calendar entry or a fresh act of remembering . The new behaviour can effectively borrow the stability of the old one. That distinction helps explain why “after I make my morning coffee, I will write for five minutes” is potentially more useful than “I will write more often”. The first plan identifies an observable event at which action should begin. It also creates repeated opportunities to practise the same relationship between cue and response. Contemporary habit theory describes habits precisely in terms of cue–behaviour associations acquired through context consistent repetition. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com What is habit and how can it be used to change real‐world behaviour? Narrowing the theory‐reality gap Gardner 2024 Social an... There is evidence that event based cues can support this process. In a four week study of technology supported habit formation, researchers found that reminders helped participants repeat behaviours but reliance on reminders did not produce the same development of automaticity ; event based cues were associated with increased automaticity. That result should not be treated as proof that phone reminders are inherently bad, but it illustrates the difference between an external prompt that repeatedly tells you what to do and a recurring event that can itself become associated with the behaviour. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryBeyond Self Tracking and Reminders: Designing Smartphone Apps That Support Habit Formation UCL DiscoveryApril 19, 2015... Published: April 19, 2015 The broader context stability literature points in the same direction. In a 2022 study, 95 university students building study habits were assigned either to keep place and time stable or deliberately vary them; a second dataset covered 308 habits recorded by 218 habit app users. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. An established routine is therefore useful partly because it can provide one component of that stable context again and again. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 How To Actually Get Things Done (implementation intentions) Channel: Better Than Yesterday Open on YouTube What the flossing after brushing study suggests One unusually concrete experiment examined exactly where a new action should sit inside an established routine. Gaby Judah, Benjamin Gardner and Robert Aunger recruited 50 participants for a study of flossing habit formation. Everyone received an intervention intended to encourage flossing, but half were instructed to floss before brushing their teeth and half after brushing. Participants recorded their flossing for four weeks, and the researchers assessed automaticity after four weeks and again at an eight month follow up. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Forming a flossing habit: an exploratory study of the psychological determinants of habit formation PubMed... The after brushing group tended to develop stronger flossing habits. At the eight month follow up they also reported stronger habits and more frequent flossing than those assigned to floss before brushing. Behavioural frequency mattered too, as did factors including previous flossing and positive attitudes, so the location of the new behaviour was not the only influence. And with only 50 participants, the study was exploratory rather than a definitive test of the best possible anchoring strategy. The authors themselves called for more rigorous research. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Forming a flossing habit: an exploratory study of the psychological determinants of habit formation PubMed... Nevertheless, the finding is revealing because “before brushing” and “after brushing” sound superficially equivalent. They are not necessarily equivalent psychologically. Putting flossing after tooth brushing gives the new action a clear preceding event: brushing finishes, then flossing begins. Asking someone to floss before brushing means the person must remember the new behaviour before starting the familiar action. The established routine cannot serve as the preceding trigger in quite the same way. Researchers reviewing habit based interventions have consequently highlighted the position of a new behaviour within an existing routine as an unresolved but potentially important design question. The flossing result suggests that routine structure matters, while the wider evidence is not yet strong enough to support a universal rule that “after” is always superior to “before”. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. The practical lesson is narrower and more useful: choose a point where the old behaviour can clearly signal the beginning of the new one. “After I put my toothbrush down, I floss” has an identifiable transition. “Sometime during my bedtime routine, I floss” leaves more room for forgetting or postponement. Explanatory illustration 2 Routine anchors are useful, not uniquely superior The appeal of anchoring can easily outrun the evidence. A daily routine is not necessarily a better cue than every alternative. A randomised controlled trial led by Jan Keller tested routine based against time based cues directly. The researchers randomly assigned 192 adults to link a self selected healthy eating behaviour either to a daily routine, such as after breakfast, or to a specific time, such as 9 am. Participants then completed daily assessments for 84 days. Among those who successfully developed habits, the estimated median time to peak automaticity was 59 days. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMed... Both groups increased their automaticity and enactment of their plans, but there was no significant overall advantage for routine based cues over time based cues . What strongly predicted automaticity was repeatedly carrying"},{"title":"Flexible Context","url":"/flexible-context/","description":"Habit cues need recognisable consistency, not identical surroundings, so ordinary changes do not always erase a learned routine.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Stable Contexts","level":3,"text":"Flexible Context How Similar Does a Habit Context Need to Be? Stable Contexts Flexible Context Flexible Context How Similar Does a Habit Context Need to Be? Habit cues need recognisable consistency, not identical surroundings, so ordinary changes do not always erase a learned routine. Habit cues need recognisable consistency, not identical surroundings, so ordinary changes do not always erase a learned routine. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Recognisable consistency versus exact repetition Which contextual features may actually serve as cues Why travel and schedule changes disrupt some habits more than others self improvement that works stable contexts flexible context Introduction A habit context does not have to be identical every time. What matters is whether enough of the features that have become associated with the behaviour recur for the situation to be recognised, psychologically, as the sort of situation in which that behaviour normally happens. “After breakfast” can remain a useful context even when breakfast is 40 minutes later, the weather is different or somebody else is in the kitchen. Explanatory illustration 1 The difficult part is that research does not provide a universal tolerance threshold — no defensible rule says that changing one feature is harmless while changing two breaks a habit. Contexts are bundles of cues, and different behaviours become attached to different parts of those bundles. Physical location is often influential, but time of day , preceding actions, other people and even internal states can matter. The practical question is therefore not “How identical must today be to yesterday?” but “Are the cues that actually predict this behaviour still recognisable?” [frontiersin.org] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Recognisable consistency matters more than exact repetition Habit research sometimes uses phrases such as “stable context” or “same context”, but these should not be read as demanding a perfect replay of previous circumstances. In a 2022 longitudinal study of intentional habit building, Marco Stojanovic and colleagues explicitly defined context stability as the similarity of the current performance context to contexts in which the behaviour had routinely been performed before . Greater stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of participants' repetition goals. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The experiment makes the distinction between ordinary variation and substantial variation particularly useful. Ninety five university students built study habits for six weeks. Participants in the stable condition were asked to study in the same place and at the same point in the day. Those in the variable condition were told to change both, including using different places and different points in the day across recent repetitions. The manipulation produced a large difference in perceived context stability, and stable context participants developed more automaticity. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Yet even the deliberately variable group did not experience its contexts as completely unrelated. The researchers noted that changing from a living room to a kitchen could still preserve the broader feature “at home”, just as different clock times might still share a broader context such as “after school”. That is important for everyday self improvement: people appear capable of representing circumstances at more than one level of specificity. A context can change in detail while retaining a recognisable higher level structure. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... What the study cannot tell us is exactly how far that flexibility extends. Its authors explicitly identified this as an unanswered question: how far can a context be generalised before its similarity to the learned context disappears? The study compared markedly stable and deliberately variable conditions; it was not designed to discover a precise boundary between “similar enough” and “too different”. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... That limitation matters because simplistic advice can outrun the evidence. It is reasonable to say that consistency assists habit formation. It is not reasonable to claim that a morning routine must happen in the same chair, at exactly 07:30, or within some particular number of minutes of its normal time. Habit science has not established thresholds of that kind. Habits: How to be successful every day Dan Ariely, Gretchen Rubin & more Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Which parts of a context actually become cues? A real life context contains far more information than a person consciously notices: location, objects, sounds, lighting, people, time, preceding activities, mood and bodily states can all recur alongside an action. Only some need to acquire control over a particular habit. A useful demonstration comes from research by Mindy Ji and Wendy Wood on three familiar behaviours: buying fast food, watching television news and taking the bus. They assessed physical location, time of day, mood and the presence of particular people. Physical location supported habitual responding across all three behaviours , but the other cues differed. Fast food purchasing was associated with mood; television news habits were associated with all four types of context assessed; bus taking was associated with all except mood. [USC Dornsife] dornsife.usc.edu ji.wood.2007 habitual purchase and consumption USC DornsifeHJCP 17(4).book(HJCP A 254101.fm)... This helps explain why asking whether a habit can tolerate “a context change” is slightly misleading. Two changes that look equally large to an observer may not be equally important to the learned routine. Consider a person who habitually stretches after making their morning coffee. They might successfully perform the routine in a different kitchen because making coffee is the cue that matters. Another person might say that they stretch “in the morning” but actually depend heavily on seeing the exercise mat beside their bedroom door. Moving the mat could therefore be more disruptive than shifting the routine by half an hour. Research on cue selection reinforces the point. In a study in which 39 participants attempted to take vitamin C daily for three weeks, people commonly chose cues because they were convenient or because similar strategies had worked previously. The researchers found that vague plans and reliance on a single factor could be poor remembering strategies, while combining clearly defined cues could be more useful. People also often had to discover effective cues through trial and error rather than correctly predicting them in advance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What influences the selection of contextual cues when starting a new routine behaviour? An exploratory study PubMedMarch 30, 2020... Published: March 30, 2020 There is also reason to distinguish clock time from events in a routine . Habit researchers have argued that event based cues such as “after breakfast” can be easier to notice naturally than “at 08:15”, which requires monitoring the time. Evidence is not completely uniform: a review of habit based health interventions notes findings favouring consistency of preceding events for physical activity habits, but also research in which time and event based cue recommendations supported dietary habit development similarly. The broader literature therefore does not justify declaring one cue type universally superior. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: Developing habit based health behaviour change interventions: twenty one questions to guide future r... For flexible habits, this distinction can be valuable. “After I finish breakfast” survives a breakfast that moves from 07:30 to 08:10. “When I arrive at my desk” survives a commute that takes longer than usual. A cue defined by a recurring event can therefore preserve meaningful consistency while allowing irrelevant details to vary. Why some changes disrupt habits and others do not The clearest real world evidence comes from situations in which people's lives change substantially. Wood, Leona Tam and Melissa Guerrero Witt studied students transferring to a new university and followed behaviours including exercising, newspaper reading and television watching. Previously habitual"},{"title":"Missed Days","url":"/missed-days-40e24b/","description":"Missing one planned repetition appears far less damaging than abandoning the stable cue-response pattern over time.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Stable Contexts","level":3,"text":"Missed Days Does Missing One Day Break a New Habit? Stable Contexts Missed Days Missed Days Does Missing One Day Break a New Habit? Missing one planned repetition appears far less damaging than abandoning the stable cue response pattern over time. Missing one planned repetition appears far less damaging than abandoning the stable cue response pattern over time. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the 12 week habit study found about missed opportunities Why habit growth is gradual rather than all or nothing How to resume without redesigning the whole routine self improvement that works stable contexts missed days Introduction Missing one planned repetition does not appear to break a developing habit or send habit formation back to day one. The best known direct evidence comes from Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ 12 week study of everyday habit formation. When participants missed a single opportunity after several successful repetitions, the resulting change in automaticity was very small, and their progress resumed when they performed the behaviour again. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That finding matters because habit formation is better understood as gradual learning , not as maintaining an unbroken streak. Each repetition in a stable context can strengthen the relationship between a cue and a response. An isolated miss means one fewer learning opportunity; it does not erase the associations created by earlier repetitions. The more important distinction is between an occasional lapse and becoming sufficiently inconsistent that the cue response pattern is no longer being reinforced reliably. Exactly how much consistency is necessary, however, remains uncertain. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College LondonAugust 4, 2009... Published: August 4, 2009 What the 12 week study found about missed opportunities Lally and colleagues recruited 96 volunteers and asked each to choose a new health related behaviour that could be performed once each day in response to a clear daily cue. Examples included eating fruit with lunch, drinking water with lunch and running for 15 minutes before dinner. Participants attempted the behaviour for 84 days and repeatedly rated how automatic it felt. Eighty two participants supplied enough data for the main analysis. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers were specifically interested in whether missing an opportunity interrupted the development of automaticity. For this analysis, they used a fairly precise definition of a miss: the participant had failed to perform the behaviour after performing it on each of the preceding three opportunities. They identified 140 such omissions among 55 participants . [studylib.net] studylib.net How Habits Form: Real World Habit Formation Study July 16, 2009... Published: July 16, 2009 Immediately after one of these misses, participants' automaticity scores fell by an average of only 0.29 points on the study's automaticity scale — a very small change. Where the researchers also had the necessary data after the participant resumed the behaviour, the automaticity score was actually 0.55 points higher than it had been before the omission; that increase was not statistically significant. [studylib.net] studylib.net How Habits Form: Real World Habit Formation Study July 16, 2009... Published: July 16, 2009 The researchers then compared those episodes with stretches in which participants performed the behaviour on three consecutive opportunities. Across those uninterrupted sequences, automaticity increased by an average of 0.79 points . In other words, the increase surrounding a single missed opportunity was broadly comparable with the increase seen during uninterrupted repetition. The authors concluded that one missed opportunity did not materially affect habit formation. [SciSpace] scispace.com how are habits formed modelling habit formation in the real 4iq5e623zp SciSpace(PDF) How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world (2010) Phillippa Lally 1734 CitationsOctober 1, 2010... Published: October 1, 2010 That conclusion is narrower than the popular claim that “missing days doesn't matter”. The study tested isolated omissions after recent successful repetitions . It did not establish that lengthy gaps, repeated missed opportunities or highly irregular performance are harmless. Indeed, the researchers found that more consistent performance was associated with better fitting habit development curves, and Lally later emphasised that very inconsistent participants did not successfully develop habits in the study. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... How to Break Unwanted Habits and How to Create New Desired Ones with Adam Grant and Wendy Wood Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Why habit growth is gradual rather than all or nothing The missed day result makes more sense once habit formation is viewed as a learning curve rather than a streak. Lally's participants did not suddenly cross from “no habit” to “habit” on a particular day. Their reported automaticity tended to rise relatively quickly at first and then increase by progressively smaller amounts as it approached a plateau. Researchers describe this as an asymptotic curve : repetition continues to contribute, but each later repetition generally produces a smaller incremental change than repetitions earlier in learning. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... There was also enormous variation between individuals. Among participants whose data could be modelled, estimated times to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days . A later account of the study reports a median of about 66 days among those whose curves fitted the model. Crucially, this was not a universal deadline: the original analysis itself demonstrated that people followed markedly different trajectories. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis reinforces that broader point. Across 20 studies involving 2,601 participants, the few studies that directly estimated time to habit formation reported medians of roughly 59–66 days and means of 106–154 days, with individual estimates ranging from 4 to 335 days. The review also cautioned that much of the evidence base had a high risk of bias and that relatively few studies had directly measured the time required to reach automaticity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. So there is little scientific reason to interpret a missed Tuesday as “back to day one”. Automaticity is a continuously varying measure, not a counter that resets when a repetition is omitted. The cue response learning established by previous repetitions does not appear to disappear simply because one opportunity passes without the response. Reviews of habit research describe habits as learned context behaviour associations strengthened through repetition in particular situations. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The more defensible interpretation is therefore: One isolated miss: the Lally study found no material disruption to the trajectory of automaticity. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Frequent repetition: remains important because habit learning depends on repeated opportunities to associate the response with its context. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Persistent inconsistency: is more concerning, although research has not established a simple threshold such as “never miss twice” or “perform the behaviour at least 80% of the time”. UCL's summary of the original work explicitly says that the required level of consistency was not known. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College LondonAugust 4, 2009... Published: August"},{"title":"Routine vs Time","url":"/routine-vs-time/","description":"Linking a habit to an existing routine can work as well as using a fixed clock time when the cue reliably recurs.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Stable Contexts","level":3,"text":"Routine vs Time Should a Habit Follow a Routine or a Clock? Stable Contexts Routine vs Time Routine vs Time Should a Habit Follow a Routine or a Clock? Linking a habit to an existing routine can work as well as using a fixed clock time when the cue reliably recurs. Linking a habit to an existing routine can work as well as using a fixed clock time when the cue reliably recurs. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case policy intervention What routine based and time based cues have in common What the 84 day randomised trial found Choosing the cue that survives your normal schedule self improvement that works stable contexts routine vs time Introduction A habit does not have to happen at exactly the same clock time to acquire a stable cue. Linking a new behaviour to an existing routine — “after breakfast, take my vitamins” or “after brushing my teeth, floss” — can work about as well as linking it to a fixed time such as 8:00 am, provided the chosen cue reliably occurs and the behaviour is repeatedly performed when it does. The strongest direct evidence comes from an 84 day randomised trial in which routine based and time based plans produced similar increases in automaticity . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 Explanatory illustration 1 That result changes the practical question. Rather than asking whether routines are inherently better than clock times, it is more useful to ask which cue will remain dependable in your actual life. A routine anchor can accommodate a breakfast that shifts from 7:30 to 9:00; a clock cue can be excellent when 9:00 itself is reliably actionable. In either case, repeated enactment at the chosen cue appears to matter more than belonging to the “right” cue category. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... What routine and time cues have in common A routine based cue is an event in an existing sequence: finishing breakfast, brushing your teeth, arriving home or making the first coffee of the morning. A time based cue specifies a point in time: “at 8:00 am” or “at 6:30 pm”. Both can provide the stable context needed to repeatedly pair a cue with a behaviour. The distinction is therefore smaller than it can sound. Suppose someone wants to drink a glass of water every morning. “Drink it after breakfast” ties the behaviour to another action, whereas “drink it at 9:00 am” ties it to the clock. If breakfast reliably finishes around 9:00 anyway, those plans may expose the person to almost the same real world context. The researchers behind the principal randomised trial explicitly raised this possibility when interpreting their results: participants allocated to clock time cues may have selected times that already coincided with regular routines. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... There is nevertheless a meaningful cognitive difference. Event based prospective memory tasks require remembering to act when an external event occurs, while time based tasks require acting at a specified time and can involve monitoring the passage of time or checking a clock. Laboratory prospective memory research confirms that monitoring differs between event and time based intentions, although these experiments should not be treated as direct tests of long term habit formation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Looking for cues over time: A study on self initiated monitoring in event based and time based prospective memory P... This gives routine anchors a plausible practical advantage when schedules move around: the event itself can announce the opportunity. “After I finish lunch” remains interpretable whether lunch ends at 12:30 or 1:45. But plausibility is not the same as evidence that routine cues always form stronger habits. The head to head trial did not find that superiority. Habit Stacking How to Use Existing Habits to Build New Ones (EFFECTIVE!) Channel: Vihan Chelliah Open on YouTube What the 84 day randomised trial found Jan Keller and colleagues tested the comparison directly in a randomised controlled trial published in the British Journal of Health Psychology . They recruited 192 adults aged 18–77 and randomly assigned them to create either a routine based or time based plan for a self selected everyday healthy eating behaviour. Participants then completed daily assessments for 84 days, reporting whether they had enacted their plan and how automatic the behaviour felt. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 The study was designed around an interesting prior expectation. Earlier observational research had given routine cues reasons to look promising. In a study of 1,244 Australian adults, for example, greater consistency of routine cues was associated with stronger physical activity automaticity, whereas consistent time cues were associated with physical activity behaviour but not automaticity. Because that research was cross sectional, however, it could not establish that choosing a routine cue caused stronger habit formation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cue Consistency Associated with Physical Activity Automaticity and Behavior PubMedApril 11, 2015... Published: April 11, 2015 The randomised trial did not confirm a routine cue advantage. Automaticity increased over time in both conditions, as did enactment of the cue behaviour plans, but the researchers found no significant between group difference. In this experiment, attaching a healthy eating behaviour to an existing routine was therefore not demonstrably better or worse than attaching it to a particular time. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... What did stand out was enactment. People who more frequently carried out the behaviour in response to their planned cue were more likely to show increasing automaticity. The researchers found this relationship both within individuals over time and between individuals. Multiple omissions, by contrast, were associated with relatively flat automaticity trajectories. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... That finding is more useful than turning the experiment into another “how many days?” rule. Among participants whose habit development followed the study's successful asymptotic pattern, the median estimated time to reach 95% of peak automaticity was 59 days. Individual estimates ranged much more widely, from 4 to 335 days. A 2024 systematic review subsequently placed the Keller trial within a still small experimental literature and likewise concluded that the time needed for health habit formation varies substantially. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is an important qualification: the trial was a comparison between two planning strategies, not proof that either strategy was superior to making no plan. There was no no planning control group. The measures of enactment and automaticity were also self reported, and only 135 of the original 192 participants supplied enough longitudinal data to be retained in the principal analyses. The authors additionally noted that the sample contained many women and younger participants, limiting how confidently the result can be generalised. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... So the appropriate conclusion is fairly narrow but useful: for the self selected nutrition behaviours studied, routine anchors and clock times performed similarly at group level. It is not evidence that cue choice never matters for any person, behaviour or schedule. Explanatory illustration 2 When a routine anchor earns its flexibility Routine cues become particularly attractive when the event is dependable but its timing is not. Someone who always eats breakfast but does so at 7:00 on workdays and 9:30 at weekends may find “after breakfast” more robust than “at 7:15”. The cue moves with the relevant part of the day instead of becoming detached from it. There is some supporting evidence that placement within an existing sequence can matter. In a small exploratory study of 50 people learning to floss, participants were instructed to floss either before or after tooth brushing. Those"},{"title":"Ego Depletion","url":"/ego-depletion/","description":"Large preregistered replications found little support for a strong, general ego-depletion effect, though the scientific debate remains open.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","level":3,"text":"Ego Depletion Does Willpower Really Run Out Like a Tank? The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more... Ego Depletion Ego Depletion Does Willpower Really Run Out Like a Tank? Large preregistered replications found little support for a strong, general ego depletion effect, though the scientific debate remains open. Large preregistered replications found little support for a strong, general ego depletion effect, though the scientific debate remains open. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence Why the ego depletion idea became influential What large preregistered replications found Why the debate is not fully settled self improvement that works willpower myths ego depletion Introduction Ego depletion is the claim that exerting self control now temporarily reduces the capacity available for self control later. It gave psychology one of its most memorable metaphors: willpower behaves like a limited tank or muscle, so resisting one temptation leaves less strength for the next . For years, experiments and an influential meta analysis appeared to support that picture. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest later evidence makes the simple version much harder to defend. A 23 laboratory preregistered replication found an effect of only d = 0.04, with its confidence interval spanning zero. A subsequent 36 site preregistered project likewise found no significant confirmatory effect ( d = 0.06). Another multilab study did find depletion, but the effect was small ( d = 0.10). [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect PubMed... That does not establish that demanding self control can never impair later performance. It does mean there is weak support for the stronger everyday story in which ordinary acts of restraint reliably drain a single, general purpose reservoir of willpower. The useful distinction is between feeling mentally fatigued , which is real, and assuming that fatigue proves an underlying self control resource has literally been used up. Why the willpower tank idea became so influential The classic ego depletion experiment uses a sequential task paradigm . Participants first perform either a task requiring self control or a comparatively undemanding control task. They then complete a different task assumed to require self control. If the first group subsequently performs worse, the difference is interpreted as depletion. This design produced an appealing result because it appeared to travel across domains. In principle, suppressing an habitual response on one task could make someone less persistent, attentive or restrained on an apparently unrelated second task. That fitted the proposed “strength model”: different acts of self control were thought to draw upon the same finite resource. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis PubMedJuly 1, 2010... Published: July 1, 2010 The case seemed particularly strong after Martin Hagger and colleagues published a 2010 meta analysis covering 83 studies. It reported a significant ego depletion effect and concluded that the findings offered preliminary support for the strength model. The effect subsequently cited from that literature was substantial, around d = 0.62. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis PubMedJuly 1, 2010... Published: July 1, 2010 That result helped turn a laboratory hypothesis into an intuitive account of everyday behaviour. If resisting biscuits, concentrating through a tedious meeting and remaining patient with an irritating colleague all consume the same scarce resource, then failure late in the day seems straightforward: the tank is approaching empty. But the metaphor was always more precise than the underlying mechanism. Saying that self control has been “used up” raises an obvious question: what exactly has been consumed? Researchers at one point proposed glucose as a physiological substrate. Later analyses did not support the crucial predictions of that account. A 2016 meta analysis found no support for the claims that self control exertion meaningfully lowered glucose, that remaining glucose predicted subsequent self control, or that ingesting glucose restored depleted control. A separate analysis concluded that the glucose literature had weak evidential value and showed possible publication or reporting bias. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Testing the role of glucose in self control: A meta analysis Testing the role of glucose in self control: A meta analysis PubMedDecember 1, 2016... Published: December 1, 2016 The failure of the glucose explanation does not by itself disprove ego depletion. A psychological effect can exist even when an early explanation for it is wrong. But it removes one seemingly concrete reason to interpret willpower as a consumable fuel. Episode 26: Willpower and Ego Depletion with Roy Baumeister Channel: The Free Will Show Open on YouTube What large preregistered replications found The bigger problem emerged when researchers reconsidered how strong the basic behavioural effect actually was. In 2014, Evan Carter and Michael McCullough reanalysed the earlier meta analytic evidence using methods intended to detect small study and publication biases. Whereas the previous literature suggested a robust medium sized effect, their analyses found strong signals of publication bias and estimates compatible with no depletion effect. A more extensive 2015 series of meta analytic tests by Carter and colleagues again challenged the limited resource interpretation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Publication bias and the limited strength model of self control: has the evidence for ego depletion been overestimated? PubMedJul... This mattered because a published literature can exaggerate an effect even without misconduct. Small experiments produce noisy estimates; positive findings may be more likely to be published than null findings; researchers may have flexibility in analytical decisions; and an accumulation of individually significant studies can therefore look more decisive than the underlying phenomenon really is. Preregistration offered a stronger test. Researchers specify important hypotheses and analyses before seeing the results, reducing opportunities for hindsight driven analytical choices. Multilaboratory projects add another advantage: instead of asking whether one research team can obtain an effect in one setting, the same hypothesis is tested across many sites and much larger samples. The first major result was striking. In 2016, 23 laboratories with 2,141 participants followed a standardised ego depletion protocol. The estimated effect was: d = 0.04, 95% CI [−0.07, 0.15]. In other words, the estimated difference was extremely small and the confidence interval included zero. The project therefore failed to provide reliable evidence for the predicted depletion effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect PubMed... That replication immediately generated an important objection: perhaps the chosen task did not induce depletion effectively enough. The project used a version of an “e crossing” task in which participants had to follow rules about responding to words containing the letter e . Critics argued that the particular implementation might not have created the habitual response that participants were subsequently required to override strongly enough. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. Researchers therefore had good reason not to treat one unsuccessful protocol as the final word. A later preregistered project approached the problem differently. Thirty six laboratories and 3,531 participants used one of two procedures intended to manipulate self control. Importantly, the manipulation did change participants' experience: those in depletion conditions reported greater effort, frustration and fatigue. Yet the preregistered confirmatory analysis again found no significant downstream performance effect: d = 0.06, 95% CI [−0.02, 0.14]. A Bayesian analysis using the researchers' informed alternative hypothesis found the observations approximately four times more likely under the null model. An exploratory analysis that ignored preregistered exclusion criteria did yield a statistically significant effect, but it was still only d = 0.08. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That combination is especially informative. Making people feel more effortful and fatigued did not reliably produce the substantial loss"},{"title":"Habit Timing","url":"/habit-timing/","description":"Habit automaticity usually develops over weeks or months, with wide variation between people and behaviors rather than one universal deadline.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more...","level":3,"text":"Habit Timing How Long Does a Habit Really Take? The Idea That Inconsistent People Simply Need “more Willpower” Habit Timing Habit Timing How Long Does a Habit Really Take? Habit automaticity usually develops over weeks or months, with wide variation between people and behaviors rather than one universal deadline. Habit automaticity usually develops over weeks or months, with wide variation between people and behaviors rather than one universal deadline. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence What researchers mean by habit automaticity Why formation times vary so widely What one missed repetition actually means self improvement that works willpower myths habit timing Introduction There is no scientifically established 21 day deadline for forming a habit. Modern habit research instead finds a gradual process that commonly unfolds over weeks or months , with large differences between people and behaviours. The best known real world study found a median of about 66 days to approach peak automaticity , but individual estimates ranged from 18 to 254 days . A 2024 systematic review found an even broader range across health behaviour studies: 4 to 335 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That variation is more important than replacing the 21 day myth with a new slogan such as “habits take 66 days”. Habit formation is not a countdown to a switch being flipped. Researchers generally study how repeated behaviour becomes increasingly automatic in response to a recurring cue or context. Early repetitions may require deliberate effort; later ones can be initiated with less conscious deliberation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 For self improvement, this changes the question. Instead of asking, “Have I been disciplined for enough days?”, it is more useful to ask whether repeated performances in a sufficiently stable context are strengthening the cue response link. Where the 21 day rule came from The familiar number did not originate in a controlled experiment on habit formation. It can be traced to plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz's 1960 self help book Psycho Cybernetics . Maltz discussed observations such as patients becoming accustomed to their appearance after surgery and people adjusting to a new home. Crucially, he described a minimum of about 21 days for a perceptible change in a “mental image”; he was not reporting evidence that arbitrary behaviours become automatic on day 21. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. The distinction matters because getting used to a change and learning an automatically cued behavioural response are not the same phenomenon. University College London researchers have specifically noted that the popular rule may partly reflect a loss of the distinction between habituation — becoming accustomed to something — and habit formation. [UCL Blogs] blogs.ucl.ac.uk UCL BlogsJune 2012 UCL ‘Health Chatter’: Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health Blog... Even Maltz's original wording was more cautious than the rule that subsequently entered popular culture. “Minimum” became, in effect, a deadline. A loose observation about psychological adjustment became the much stronger claim that repeating any behaviour for three weeks makes it habitual. Habit research does not support that claim. What researchers mean by habit automaticity In contemporary behavioural research, calling something a habit usually means more than saying that it happens frequently. A habit involves an association through which a recurring contextual cue can activate a learned response with increasing automaticity . Repetition matters because repeated pairings strengthen that association. Experimental work published in 2023, for example, found that greater practice strengthened measurable cue response associations, which in turn supported performance without corresponding improvements in participants' intentions or motivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 Researchers therefore often measure automaticity rather than waiting for a participant to declare that a habit has suddenly “formed”. Instruments such as the Self Report Behavioural Automaticity Index ask about experiences associated with automatic behaviour — for example, whether an action occurs without conscious remembering or much thought. These measures are imperfect, not least because they rely on self report, but they allow habit strength to be treated as a continuum rather than a yes or no state. [IAAP Journals] iaap journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com IAAP JournalsCan—and should—automaticity be self‐reported using a single item? A secondary analysis of 16 datasets Gardner 2024 App... That distinction explains why asking for the exact day on which a habit forms can be misleading. Automaticity normally develops as a learning curve . Repetition initially produces relatively large gains; as the behaviour becomes increasingly automatic, additional repetitions tend to produce smaller gains towards a plateau. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Someone on day 40, in other words, need not be either “without a habit” or “fully habitual”. The useful behaviour may already require less prompting than it did on day five while still having considerable room to become more automatic. Day 5: Why 21 Days Is a Lie — The Real Science of Habits (Lally 2010) Channel: Local Ai Hub Open on YouTube Why 66 days is not the new 21 days The figure most often used to correct the 21 day claim comes from Phillippa Lally and colleagues' influential real world study. Ninety six volunteers chose an eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour and attempted to perform it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Participants recorded both performance and their subjective automaticity. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... For participants whose development could be modelled appropriately, the estimated time required to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity plateau varied dramatically: 18 to 254 days . The frequently quoted central estimate was about 66 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That makes “66 days to form a habit” a poor replacement for “21 days to form a habit”. The study's main lesson is arguably the spread around the number , not the number itself. More recent evidence reinforces that point. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis examined 20 studies involving 2,601 participants across behaviours including exercise , diet, water consumption and dental flossing. Only four included studies directly reported time to habit estimates. Their median estimates were 59–66 days , while reported means ranged from 106 to 154 days . Across the relevant studies, individual formation times extended from 4 to 335 days . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The review also comes with important limitations. Eleven of the 20 included studies were rated at high risk of bias, and the evidence base was uneven across behaviours. The authors therefore describe the findings as emerging evidence rather than establishing a universal timetable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The defensible summary is consequently not “science says 66 days”. It is: habit automaticity often takes substantially longer than three weeks, and there is no single formation time that applies reliably across people and behaviours. Why formation times vary so widely The enormous range becomes less surprising once habit formation is understood as learning rather than calendar time. A “day” only contributes to the process insofar as the relevant behaviour and cue are actually paired. Explanatory illustration 2 Repetitions matter more than dates A randomised study of 192 adults illustrates the point. Participants selected a nutrition behaviour and linked it either to a daily routine or to a specific time. Among those who successfully developed the habit, median time to peak automaticity was 59 days . But whether the cue was routine based or time"},{"title":"Setup Creep","url":"/setup-creep/","description":"A useful shutdown setup ends once the first task, next action, materials and memory cues are ready, rather than becoming another project.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Work Prep","level":3,"text":"Setup Creep When Tomorrow Prep Becomes Today's Procrastination Work Prep Setup Creep Setup Creep When Tomorrow Prep Becomes Today's Procrastination A useful shutdown setup ends once the first task, next action, materials and memory cues are ready, rather than becoming another project. A useful shutdown setup ends once the first task, next action, materials and memory cues are ready, rather than becoming another project. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention How preparation expands beyond its useful purpose The minimum setup needed for an easier restart A practical stopping rule for end of day preparation self improvement that works work prep setup creep Introduction End of day preparation is useful only while it makes tomorrow easier to start. Once the first task, first concrete action, necessary materials and a reliable memory cue are ready, further organising can become a disguised form of procrastination: work that feels responsible but no longer meaningfully reduces tomorrow’s starting friction. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because planning genuinely can help. Specific implementation plans improve goal initiation, and concrete plans for unfinished work can reduce intrusive thoughts about it. But the research does not imply that more planning is always better. Reviews of procrastination explicitly identify excessive planning as one way action regulation can go wrong, while research on external reminders shows that reminders themselves have costs and should be used selectively. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The practical policy is therefore simple: prepare for resumption, not for certainty . End the shutdown routine when tomorrow’s first useful action can begin without another planning session. How preparation expands beyond its useful purpose Preparation has an unusually convincing disguise. Renaming folders, improving a task system, rearranging tomorrow’s calendar and researching a better workflow all look more productive than obvious avoidance. The problem is not that these activities are inherently useless. It is that their value depends on whether they remove a real obstacle to subsequent action. Research on procrastination provides a useful warning here. A scoping review of work related procrastination describes procrastination partly as impaired action regulation: difficulties can occur in evaluating, planning, carrying out or completing work. Crucially, this can involve excessive planning , with planning becoming more important than doing the task itself. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That makes “I am preparing” an unreliable test of whether preparation is worthwhile. A better question is: what decision or obstacle will this preparation remove tomorrow? Opening the correct spreadsheet and writing “Next: reconcile the three unmatched invoices” has an identifiable payoff. Spending another 20 minutes redesigning the project dashboard may not. The risk becomes greater when preparation promises relief from an uncomfortable task. Procrastination research increasingly treats delay not simply as poor time management but as a form of short term mood regulation: when a task feels difficult, frustrating or aversive, avoiding it can provide immediate emotional relief while shifting the cost to the future self. [DOI] doi.org Procrastination and the Priority of Short‐Term Mood Regulation: Consequences for Future Self Sirois 2013 Social and Personality... An elaborate preparation ritual can fit that pattern without looking like avoidance. Instead of confronting the uncertain draft, difficult analysis or awkward decision, a person can continue working on the safer problem of how work should be organised . Perfectionistic concerns can reinforce the same tendency. A meta analysis involving roughly 10,000 participants found a positive association between procrastination and perfectionistic concerns, while a seven day diary study found that perceived discrepancies between actual and ideal performance helped explain the relationship. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryA Meta‐analytic and Conceptual Update on the Associations Between Procrastination and Multidimensional Perfectionism... Applied cautiously to end of day setup, this suggests a recognisable failure mode: “ready enough to restart” quietly becomes “everything must be perfectly arranged before I can stop”. That is setup creep. Its defining feature is not how long the routine takes but whether additional effort is still reducing a genuine initiation barrier. How to Fix Procrastination with Neuroscience (And Why You do it) Dr. Sid Warrier Channel: Dr Sid Warrier Open on YouTube The minimum setup needed for an easier restart The strongest evidence for preparation supports specificity, not elaborateness. Gollwitzer and Sheeran’s meta analysis of 94 independent tests found that implementation intentions — plans specifying when, where and how an intended behaviour will occur — produced a medium to large overall improvement in goal attainment and helped with initiating action. The mechanism involves making the relevant cue more accessible and strengthening the connection between that cue and the intended response. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... For end of day preparation, that points towards a deliberately small package: Choose the restart target. Identify the task you intend to resume first rather than reprioritising your entire workload. Specify one observable next action . “Continue budget” is vague; “open the August forecast and check the three flagged assumptions” is actionable. Put the required material within reach. Leave the correct document, dataset, notebook or physical material easy to locate. Externalise anything you genuinely need to remember. Record the unresolved thought, constraint or question rather than expecting tomorrow’s memory to reconstruct it. Stop. Do not treat successful setup as permission to start optimising the rest of tomorrow. The memory step can be extremely small. Research on “intention offloading” — using external aids to carry future intentions — finds that reminders such as calendars, notes, alerts and strategically positioned objects can substantially improve fulfilment of delayed intentions. Importantly, the same review frames reminder setting as a cost benefit decision: reminders consume time and effort too, and setting them for everything would create unacceptable overhead. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Outsourcing Memory to External Tools: A Review of ‘Intention Offloading’ PMC... That is a useful model for the whole shutdown routine. A cue earns its place when it saves more future effort than it costs to create. A note reading “Next: verify whether the 4.2% figure includes refunds” may save tomorrow’s self several minutes of reconstruction. Copying every possible future action into three different productivity systems probably does not. The same principle applies to materials. “Materials ready” does not require an immaculate desk or inbox zero. It means that whatever blocks the first action has been removed. If tomorrow starts with editing a presentation, having the current presentation and source figures identifiable is enough. Sorting unrelated downloads or choosing new folder icons does nothing for that restart. Explanatory illustration 2 Why a small plan can be enough There is a particularly relevant finding in research on unfinished goals. Masicampo and Baumeister found that unfinished goals could produce intrusive thoughts and interfere with unrelated tasks, but having participants make specific plans for completing those goals eliminated those cognitive effects in their experiments. Participants did not have to finish the underlying goal first; a credible plan for later action was enough to reduce interference. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Consider it done! Plan making can eliminate the cognitive effects of unfulfilled goals PubMed... This is important for shutdown routines because it challenges the intuition that peace of mind requires comprehensive closure. Often the work itself cannot be completed today. The useful substitute is not exhaustive replanning but sufficient closure about how work will resume . Workplace evidence points in the same direction. Unfinished tasks are associated with poorer psychological detachment from work, and research on incomplete work goals suggests that concrete plans for when, where and how they will be resolved can help people disengage. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Psychological detachment matters because off job recovery depends partly on being able to stop engaging mentally and"},{"title":"Mental Closure","url":"/mental-closure/","description":"Giving unfinished work a specific continuation point may make it easier to stop mentally rehearsing the task during leisure time.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Work Prep","level":3,"text":"Mental Closure Can a Restart Plan Help You Switch Off? Work Prep Mental Closure Mental Closure Can a Restart Plan Help You Switch Off? Giving unfinished work a specific continuation point may make it easier to stop mentally rehearsing the task during leisure time. Giving unfinished work a specific continuation point may make it easier to stop mentally rehearsing the task during leisure time. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Why unfinished work follows people into leisure time What research on specific completion plans found Creating closure without continuing to work late self improvement that works work prep mental closure Introduction Unfinished work does not have to be finished before you can mentally leave it. A more realistic stopping strategy is to give the task a credible continuation point: record what remains, decide where and when you will resume, and specify the first concrete action. Research suggests that this kind of planning can reduce the cognitive pull of incomplete goals and, for some employees, improve psychological detachment after work. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Consider it done! Plan making can eliminate the cognitive effects of unfulfilled goals PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 That matters because unfinished tasks are unusually good at following people into leisure time. A 2026 meta analysis found that having unfinished work was associated with more work related thinking outside working hours, particularly affective rumination. Earlier diary studies linked unfinished tasks with weekend rumination and poorer sleep. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unfinished work tasks and work related thoughts during off job time: meta analysis of the Zeigarnik effect in a work recovery conte... The useful distinction is therefore between finishing the work and finishing today's decisions about the work . A restart plan aims for the second. It creates enough closure to stop now while also lowering the friction of starting again tomorrow. Why unfinished work follows you home An incomplete task leaves an unresolved goal. Instead of becoming irrelevant when the laptop closes, that goal can remain cognitively accessible: the mind keeps returning to what has not yet been achieved, what could be forgotten or what must happen next. The strongest recent summary comes from a 2026 meta analysis specifically examining unfinished work and off job thoughts. Across 17 between person samples involving 2,473 participants and 14 within person samples involving 12,129 observations, unfinished tasks were positively associated with work related thoughts during non work time. The estimated association was stronger between people (ρ =.382) than within the same person across different occasions (ρ =.247), and associations were strongest when researchers measured affective rumination. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unfinished work tasks and work related thoughts during off job time: meta analysis of the Zeigarnik effect in a work recovery conte... That finding strengthens an evidence base built from diary studies. Syrek and colleagues followed 59 employees for 12 weeks, matching 357 Friday and Monday observations. When employees ended a week with more unfinished tasks, they experienced more affective rumination, which in turn was associated with impaired weekend sleep. Employees who accumulated higher levels of unfinished work over the three month period also reported greater weekend sleep impairment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A separate five week diary study of 89 employees likewise examined unfinished tasks alongside time pressure. Even after accounting for time pressure, feeling that the week's tasks had not been completed was associated with perseverative thinking and poorer sleep; perceived expectations from leaders could intensify those relationships. In other words, unfinished work is not necessarily just a proxy for being busy. The sense of unresolved work appears to matter in its own right. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Unfinished tasks foster rumination and impair sleeping Pub Med Unfinished tasks foster rumination and impair sleeping This does not mean that every thought about work is harmful. Occupational research distinguishes distressing affective rumination from more constructive forms of thinking, such as problem solving pondering and positive reflection. That distinction matters: the problem is not literally having any thought connected with work, but being unable to disengage because unresolved goals repeatedly reclaim attention. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Why You Need a Shutdown Ritual: The Power of Separating Work and Personal Life Channel: Scott D. Clary Success Story Podcast Open on YouTube What specific completion plans actually changed The important experimental finding is that an unfinished goal may not need to be completed to become less mentally intrusive. It may be enough to decide credibly how it will be completed. Masicampo and Baumeister tested this idea across several laboratory studies published in 2011. They first reproduced several consequences of unfulfilled goals: unfinished goals increased intrusive thoughts during unrelated reading, made goal related words more mentally accessible and, in one experiment, interfered with performance on an unrelated anagram task. Participants who were allowed to formulate specific plans for pursuing their unfinished goals no longer showed the same activation and interference effects. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Consider it done! Plan making can eliminate the cognitive effects of unfulfilled goals PubMed... One detail makes the finding especially relevant to everyday planning. The effect was not simply produced by writing something reassuring on paper. Reduction in intrusive thoughts was related to the earnestness of the plan: participants who subsequently carried out their plans were also the ones who showed less intrusion. A plausible interpretation is that the mind responds to a plan that genuinely advances goal pursuit, rather than treating any to do list entry as closure. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Consider it done! Plan making can eliminate the cognitive effects of unfulfilled goals PubMed... Workplace research moved the idea closer to the end of day problem. Brandon Smit followed 103 employees pursuing 1,127 work goals and found that employees had greater difficulty detaching from incomplete than completed goals, particularly when those goals were highly valued. In an experimental component, making plans to resolve incomplete goals increased psychological detachment among employees with personal tendencies that ordinarily made detachment difficult. The practical prescription from the study was notably specific: end of day plans should describe where, when and how an unfinished work goal will be completed. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. Smit and Barber subsequently examined a daily planning intervention designed to direct attention away from incomplete work goals. Their research focused on the familiar problem that heavy workloads make detachment harder. The workload–detachment relationship was moderated by attentional factors, supporting the idea that what happens to attention after an unfinished workday can be changed rather than being an inevitable consequence of having too much to do. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is also broader intervention evidence, although it should not be mistaken for proof that restart planning alone will work for everyone. A 2021 meta analysis covering 30 studies and 34 interventions, with 3,725 participants, found that interventions intended to improve psychological detachment produced a statistically significant average benefit of d = 0.36 . Those interventions varied considerably, so this figure describes the wider detachment intervention literature rather than the effect size of an end of day restart plan specifically. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 2 Creating closure without working late The practical lesson is not to extend the working day until every open loop disappears. For many jobs that is impossible: knowledge work continuously generates drafts, unanswered questions, dependencies and tasks that span several days. Trying to obtain closure by completing everything can simply turn recovery time into more work. Instead, use the final few minutes of work to convert an unresolved task into a restartable task . The plan should answer enough questions that tomorrow's self does not need to reconstruct today's thinking. A useful restart note contains four pieces of information: Current position: where you actually stopped, rather than a vague description of the whole project. Next action : the first observable thing you will do on returning. Restart cue: when or under what circumstance you"},{"title":"Next Action","url":"/next-action/","description":"A concrete restart note can turn an unfinished project into an action you can begin without reconstructing yesterday's thinking.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Work Prep","level":3,"text":"Next Action What Should Tomorrow's First Action Look Like? Work Prep Next Action Next Action What Should Tomorrow's First Action Look Like? A concrete restart note can turn an unfinished project into an action you can begin without reconstructing yesterday's thinking. A concrete restart note can turn an unfinished project into an action you can begin without reconstructing yesterday's thinking. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Turning broad tasks into visible actions Examples for writing, analysis, coding and administration How much detail a restart note actually needs self improvement that works work prep next action Introduction A useful end of day restart note should make tomorrow’s first move executable, not merely remind you that a project exists. “Continue report” preserves the project name; “Open the survey workbook, filter to July respondents, and check whether the age totals match Table 3” preserves an action. The second version matters because it reduces the amount of project reconstruction required before useful work can resume. Explanatory illustration 1 Several strands of research support this approach, although there is not a large research literature testing “restart notes” as a named productivity technique. Experiments on interrupted work show that preparing for resumption and preserving useful contextual cues can shorten the disruption caused by a break. Research on implementation intentions finds that specifying what action will happen in response to a future cue improves goal initiation. Studies of external reminders likewise show that remembering improves when an intention is stored in the environment rather than left entirely to memory. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Preparing to resume an interrupted task: effects of prospective goal encoding and retrospective rehearsal ScienceDirect... The practical policy is therefore simple: before stopping unfinished work, leave one visible instruction describing the first physical or digital action you expect to perform when you return. Turning broad tasks into visible actions The crucial distinction is between a project description and a restart instruction . “Finish budget”, “work on presentation” and “deal with supplier problem” describe desired outcomes. They still force tomorrow’s worker to determine where to begin. A restart instruction moves that decision to today, while the project’s state is still readily available in memory. Research on implementation intentions provides the clearest general model. Peter Gollwitzer and Paschal Sheeran’s meta analysis examined 94 independent tests of plans that connect a specified future situation with a specified response — essentially deciding in advance, “when this happens, I will do that”. Across the studies, implementation intentions had a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment and specifically helped with initiating goal directed behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... A restart note need not be a formal if–then statement, but it can exploit the same principle. Compare: Tomorrow: work on quarterly analysis. with: When I reopen this workbook: check the three missing March values against the raw export. The latter has already settled the first behavioural choice. It identifies both the cue — reopening the workbook — and the response. Importantly, this does not mean planning tomorrow’s entire session. The intervention is deliberately smaller: decide the first action while the information needed to make that decision is still fresh. Interruption research makes this especially relevant to unfinished knowledge work. In an experiment by J. Gregory Trafton and colleagues, participants who received a short warning before an interruption used that interval to prepare and subsequently resumed the suspended task more quickly than participants interrupted immediately. The researchers distinguished between remembering the state of the task and encoding what should be done when work resumed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Preparing to resume an interrupted task: effects of prospective goal encoding and retrospective rehearsal ScienceDirect... Helen Hodgetts and Dylan Jones found a related effect: preparation before interruption reduced resumption costs, while contextual cues helped people retrieve suspended goals. Altering those cues after the interruption made recovery harder. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Contextual cues aid recovery from interruption: the role of associative activation PubMed... These were laboratory tasks rather than overnight office projects, so the findings should not be treated as a direct trial of end of day notes. They do, however, support the underlying design principle: preserve the information tomorrow will need to recover the suspended goal. Tired of Taking Work Home? You Need this Routine Channel: Focus Café Open on YouTube What should tomorrow’s first action look like? A strong next action has three characteristics: it is observable, it points to the relevant object or location, and it is small enough to start without another round of planning. “Think about the problem” usually fails this test because the worker must still decide what thinking consists of. “List the three explanations that could account for the discrepancy” passes it. The action should normally begin with a concrete verb: open, compare, rewrite, test, call, check, extract, insert, send, reconcile or review. The point is not linguistic neatness. A concrete verb forces the note writer to decide what tomorrow’s behaviour actually is. There is useful evidence that the content of reminders matters. In experiments on prospective memory — remembering to perform an intended future action — researchers compared reminders containing different information. Reminders that specified both the target cue and the associated action improved prospective memory performance, whereas target only or action only reminders did not produce the same benefit in one experiment. The benefits of reminders were also greater under heavier memory loads. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Benefits from prospective memory offloading depend on memory load and reminder type PubMedOctober 6, 2022... Published: October 6, 2022 That finding maps neatly onto restart notes. “Table 3” identifies a target but not what to do with it. “Check totals” identifies an action but not where. “Table 3: check its totals against the July export” carries both pieces. A practical test is to imagine encountering the note tomorrow after having forgotten most of today’s working context. Could you obey it immediately? If the answer is “I would first need to work out what I meant”, the note is not finished. Examples across everyday knowledge work The right level of specificity depends on the work, but the underlying transformation is consistent: replace a broad intention with the first manipulable object and action. Writing: Instead of “continue methods section”, leave “Rewrite the sampling paragraph using the three figures highlighted in Smith; then add the citation.” Analysis: Instead of “investigate conversion decline”, leave “Filter the weekly conversion table to mobile traffic and compare weeks 27–30 with the same weeks last year.” Coding: Instead of “fix login bug”, leave “Run the failing expired token test, inspect the refresh token branch, and confirm whether the exception occurs before the retry.” Administration: Instead of “sort invoices”, leave “Open the August invoice folder and match the five unassigned payments against the bank export.” Presentation work: Instead of “finish slides”, leave “Replace the placeholder chart on slide 8 with the regional revenue chart and check that its date range ends in July.” Research: Instead of “read papers on retention”, leave “Read the results section of the saved retention paper and record its sample size, outcome measure and effect estimate in the evidence table.” These instructions are intentionally modest. They do not attempt to specify every subsequent action. Once work is moving, the next decision can often be made with the project open and its context restored. The restart note is aimed at the unusually fragile transition between not working on the project and working on it again . That distinction also prevents a restart note from turning into elaborate daily planning. Research on workplace planning suggests that planning can support daily performance, but its effects depend on the work environment and interruptions. A two week experience sampling study, for example, distinguished ordinary time management planning from contingent planning for anticipated interruptions and found different relationships with engagement and performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When daily planning improves employee performance: The importance of planning type, engagement, and"},{"title":"Average Effects","url":"/average-effects/","description":"A positive pooled effect can hide large differences across people, settings, behaviours, and study designs.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Evidence Limits","level":3,"text":"Average Effects Why a Positive Average Effect May Not Predict Your Result Evidence Limits Average Effects Average Effects Why a Positive Average Effect May Not Predict Your Result A positive pooled effect can hide large differences across people, settings, behaviours, and study designs. A positive pooled effect can hide large differences across people, settings, behaviours, and study designs. Self Improvement That Works evidence single mechanism mechanism What a pooled effect actually represents How heterogeneity changes practical interpretation Why prediction intervals can be more informative self improvement that works evidence limits average effects Introduction A positive average effect does not mean that the same technique will produce that effect for you. Meta analysis combines results across studies, so its headline number answers a population level question: what was the average difference across the studies included? It does not directly answer the individual question: what will happen if I try this? Cochrane explicitly warns that, when effects vary between studies, a random effects meta analysis estimates the centre of a distribution of effects rather than a single effect shared by everyone. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters unusually much in self improvement research. Behaviour change studies can differ in their participants, goals, intervention intensity, comparison groups, follow up periods and outcome measurements. A technique can therefore have a credible positive average while producing large gains in some circumstances, little change in others and occasionally results compatible with harm. The practical question is not simply whether the pooled effect is positive, but how much variation sits behind that average and how closely the evidence resembles your situation . What a pooled effect actually represents A meta analysis usually gives more weight to studies that estimate their effects more precisely and combines them into a summary estimate. Under a random effects model, the studies are allowed to have different underlying effects. The resulting pooled number is therefore an estimate of their average effect , conditional on the studies and statistical model being used. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... That is valuable evidence, but it is easy to translate it into a stronger claim than the analysis supports. Suppose a meta analysis of a self regulation technique reports a standardised mean effect of 0.40. That does not mean that every participant improves by 0.40 standard deviations, nor that a new user should expect precisely that improvement. It means that the studies collectively support an average difference of that approximate magnitude. Real self improvement evidence illustrates the distinction. A meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions encouraging people to monitor their goal progress improved goal attainment by an average standardised effect of d = 0.40. Yet the same review found larger effects when progress was physically recorded and when outcomes were made public. The intervention labelled “progress monitoring” therefore did not operate as an invariant treatment: how monitoring was implemented mattered . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Implementation intentions — plans that link a situation to a response, commonly framed as “if X happens, then I will do Y” — provide another example. A well known meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported an average effect of d = 0.65 on goal achievement, while also finding significant variation that prompted analyses of moderators. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... The sensible interpretation is that implementation intentions have evidence of benefit across many studies. The number 0.65 should not be converted into a personal forecast. There is another important distinction. Variation between studies is not identical to variation between people . A meta analysis might show that effects differ across universities, workplaces, delivery formats or measurement methods without establishing exactly which individuals benefit. Conversely, similar study averages can conceal different individual responses within each study. Research on heterogeneous treatment effects emphasises that estimating the average effect in a population and determining the best intervention for one person are fundamentally different statistical problems. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. What is Heterogeneity? Channel: Terry Shaneyfelt Open on YouTube Heterogeneity changes what “it works” means The technical term for variation in effects across studies is heterogeneity . Cochrane distinguishes clinical diversity — differences in participants, interventions and outcomes — from methodological diversity, such as differences in study design, measurement and risk of bias. Either can produce statistical heterogeneity, in which observed intervention effects vary more than would be expected from sampling error alone. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... For self improvement, those sources of variation are not minor details. A behaviour change technique can be embedded in very different programmes. A reminder on a phone, weekly coaching and a carefully designed intervention may all contain “goal setting” or “self monitoring”, but they do not necessarily represent equivalent experiences. A systematic review of interventions promoting physical activity and healthier eating among adults with overweight or obesity makes the point concrete. Across 50 short term outcome reports, the pooled effect was 0.37, but heterogeneity was substantial at I ² = 71.3%. For 32 long term reports, the average fell to 0.24 and I ² remained 59.4%. Goal setting and self monitoring were associated with outcomes, but intervention characteristics and reporting bias also helped explain differences between studies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The I ² statistic is often treated as a simple “heterogeneity score”, but it needs care. Cochrane notes that its importance depends on the magnitude and direction of effects and on the strength of evidence for heterogeneity; estimates are particularly uncertain when few studies are available. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... More importantly for an ordinary reader, I ² does not directly say whether a technique is likely to help you . Heterogeneity can arise for several reasons that have different practical meanings: The intervention really works differently in different circumstances. A technique may depend on motivation, opportunities, feedback , social context or how faithfully it is used. Studies may be testing meaningfully different versions of the technique. “Mindfulness”, “goal setting” or “self monitoring” can describe interventions differing greatly in duration and delivery. Outcomes may differ. Improving steps per day is not the same outcome as improving long term fitness, and reducing a questionnaire score is not necessarily equivalent to a meaningful change in everyday functioning. Study methods can create apparent variation. Differences in measurement, comparison conditions, missing data, study quality and reporting bias can alter observed effects even without genuine differences in efficacy. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... This is why unexplained heterogeneity should reduce the precision of a practical claim. A random effects model does not make heterogeneity disappear. Cochrane specifically cautions that such a model incorporates variation statistically but is not a substitute for investigating why results differ. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane Chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta analyses Cochrane... Explanatory illustration 2 A narrow confidence interval can still hide wide variation One of the easiest meta analytic results to misread is a narrow 95% confidence interval around a pooled effect. A confidence interval around a random effects mean primarily describes uncertainty"},{"title":"Mechanism Gap","url":"/mechanism-gap/","description":"Many self-improvement studies show behaviour change without proving that the proposed psychological mechanism actually caused it.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Evidence Limits","level":3,"text":"Mechanism Gap Do Self Improvement Studies Prove Why Their Techniques Work? Evidence Limits Mechanism Gap Mechanism Gap Do Self Improvement Studies Prove Why Their Techniques Work? Many self improvement studies show behaviour change without proving that the proposed psychological mechanism actually caused it. Many self improvement studies show behaviour change without proving that the proposed psychological mechanism actually caused it. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single dataset dataset evidence The difference between outcome effects and mechanism evidence What the self regulation meta review found Why untested mechanisms weaken confident advice self improvement that works evidence limits mechanism gap Introduction A self improvement technique can change behaviour without proving the psychological reason it supposedly works. That distinction is easy to lose. A study may find that goal setting, feedback or self monitoring improves an outcome, then describe the result in terms of greater self regulation. But unless researchers actually measure the proposed self regulatory process and test whether changing it predicts the behavioural change, the mechanism remains a hypothesis rather than an established explanation. Explanatory illustration 1 A systematic meta review of 66 meta analyses makes the scale of this problem unusually clear. Although almost all of the reviews compared intervention components associated with self regulation, only 6% directly examined whether change in a self regulation mechanism predicted change in behaviour . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Regulation Mechanisms in Health Behaviour Change: A Systematic Meta Review of Meta Analyses, 2006–2017 PMCMarc... That is an important qualification for evidence based self improvement: knowing that a technique helps is not the same as knowing why it helps. An outcome effect is not mechanism evidence Consider an intervention that asks people to set goals and then finds that they exercise more. The experiment may provide evidence that the intervention caused additional exercise. It does not automatically demonstrate the longer causal story: goal setting improved a particular self regulatory capacity, and that improvement caused the additional exercise. Those are separate questions. A convincing mechanism test needs to establish the links along the proposed pathway. Researchers need a defined and measurable mechanism, evidence that the intervention changes that mechanism — often called target engagement — and evidence connecting the resulting change in the mechanism to the subsequent change in behaviour. The US National Institutes of Health's Science of Behavior Change programme developed its experimental medicine approach precisely around this distinction. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The NIH Science of Behavior Change Program: Transforming the science through a focus on mechanisms of change PMC... The distinction matters because behaviour change techniques can have several plausible routes to the same outcome. Recording daily exercise might increase awareness of progress, make a goal more salient, provide feedback, create a sense of accountability or simply serve as a reminder. An intervention's success cannot, by itself, tell researchers which of these explanations is responsible. There is a related classification problem. Behaviour change techniques (BCTs) are things an intervention does — such as prompting goal setting, giving feedback or asking someone to monitor behaviour. Mechanisms of action are the processes through which those techniques are hypothesised to affect behaviour . A synthesis of 277 published intervention articles found many proposed links between BCTs and mechanisms, but its authors emphasised that there was no widely shared understanding of exactly which mechanisms individual techniques operate through. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavior Change Techniques and Their Mechanisms of Action: A Synthesis of Links Described in Published Intervention L... A plausible theoretical link is useful for forming a hypothesis; it is not a substitute for testing it. CLIMBR Checklist for Investigating Mechanisms in Behavior change Research Channel: Science Of Behavior Change Open on YouTube The meta review found a large mechanism gap Emily Hennessy and colleagues' systematic meta review provides the clearest numerical snapshot. The researchers searched literature published from January 2006 to August 2017 and ultimately included 66 meta analyses quantitatively examining aspects of self regulation in health behaviour interventions. The underlying behaviours included areas such as physical activity , diet, medical adherence, sleep, self care and substance use. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Regulation Mechanisms in Health Behaviour Change: A Systematic Meta Review of Meta Analyses, 2006–2017 PMCMarc... The headline contrast was striking: 97% of the meta analyses assessed self regulation through comparisons involving intervention components. Only 6% — four meta analyses — directly examined whether changes in self regulation predicted behavioural change . Those direct tests covered only a narrow set of proposed mechanisms: self efficacy, frequency of self monitoring and cognitive bias. Review quality was itself uneven. Only 27% reported a protocol and 11% used GRADE, a system for assessing certainty of evidence; across AMSTAR 2 methodological criteria, the reviews averaged 45.45%. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self Regulation Mechanisms in Health Behaviour Change: A Systematic Meta Review of Meta Analyses, 2006–2017 PMCMarc... This does not mean that 94% of the literature was worthless or that the interventions did not work. It means something more specific: most of the synthesis literature was better equipped to identify potentially useful intervention components than to establish the psychological mechanisms responsible for their effects. That distinction is especially important because the component evidence was not uniform. The meta review found promising results for techniques including personalised feedback, goal setting and self monitoring, but no component was consistently successful across all the health behaviours and populations in which it had been studied. Some components were supported only by lower quality reviews. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulation mechanisms in health behavior change: a systematic meta review of meta analyses, 2006 2017 PubMed... A statement such as “self monitoring can improve some outcomes” can therefore be substantially better supported than a sweeping explanation of why self monitoring works. Explanatory illustration 2 The rare direct tests show why mechanism testing matters The small collection of direct tests was not merely a technical footnote. It produced examples in which measuring the proposed mechanism changed what researchers could reasonably conclude. Progress monitoring provides one of the stronger cases. A meta analysis by Benjamin Harkin and colleagues included 138 randomised studies and 19,951 participants . Interventions intended to promote monitoring produced a very large increase in the frequency of progress monitoring ( d = 1.98) and a more modest improvement in goal attainment ( d = 0.40). Crucially, the researchers went beyond showing both effects independently: changes in monitoring frequency mediated the intervention's effect on goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That provides more informative evidence than simply observing that programmes containing self monitoring outperform programmes without it. It supports a pathway in which the intervention changes monitoring and the change in monitoring helps account for improved goal attainment. Even then, mediation evidence needs careful causal interpretation, but it moves the evidence much closer to the proposed mechanism. Cognitive bias modification provides an instructive counterexample. A 2016 meta analysis of 25 randomised trials in alcohol problems and smoking found that the interventions produced a moderate post treatment change in the targeted cognitive biases ( g = 0.60), yet there was no significant post treatment effect on addiction outcomes ( g = 0.08) or craving ( g = 0.05). More importantly for the mechanism claim, greater changes in cognitive bias were not significantly associated with greater changes in addiction outcomes. Risk of bias was also high or uncertain across much of the evidence. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Open source on plos.org. This is exactly the kind of result that mechanism testing can reveal: an intervention can successfully move its proposed psychological target without producing the behavioural result expected from that theory. The story has continued to develop rather than resolving into a"},{"title":"Visible Tracking","url":"/visible-tracking/","description":"Tracking goals tends to help more when progress is physically recorded or shared, showing that monitoring design matters as much as monitoring itself.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Evidence Limits","level":3,"text":"Visible Tracking Why Visible Progress Tracking Can Improve Goal Follow Through Evidence Limits Visible Tracking Visible Tracking Why Visible Progress Tracking Can Improve Goal Follow Through Tracking goals tends to help more when progress is physically recorded or shared, showing that monitoring design matters as much as monitoring itself. Tracking goals tends to help more when progress is physically recorded or shared, showing that monitoring design matters as much as monitoring itself. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention What randomized studies found about self monitoring Why recording and public reporting may strengthen effects Where tracking can become excessive or misleading self improvement that works evidence limits visible tracking Introduction Self monitoring is one of the better supported ideas in the self improvement toolkit, but the evidence points to a more specific lesson than simply “track your goals”. Monitoring tends to work better when progress leaves a visible trace: a written log, recorded measurement, step count or other record that can be reviewed. Effects have also been larger when progress is reported to somebody else rather than kept entirely private. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest broad evidence comes from a meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions designed to increase progress monitoring produced a moderate improvement in goal attainment, with an average effect of d = 0.40. Crucially, the benefit was larger when monitoring information was physically recorded and when outcomes were reported or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That makes visible tracking a useful example of why implementation matters in evidence based self improvement. The intervention is not merely “pay attention to your goal”. A record can make discrepancies difficult to forget, while reporting can add accountability. Neither mechanism, however, means that more tracking is always better. What randomised studies found about self monitoring Benjamin Harkin and colleagues' 2016 Psychological Bulletin meta analysis provides unusually broad experimental evidence because it restricted its main analysis to studies in which participants were randomly allocated to an intervention promoting progress monitoring or to a control condition. The 138 studies covered nearly 20,000 participants and a variety of goals, although health related goals were particularly prominent. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The interventions had a very large effect on how frequently people monitored progress ( d = 1.98, 95% confidence interval 1.71 to 2.24), but the effect on actually attaining goals was substantially smaller: d = 0.40 (95% CI 0.32 to 0.48). Changes in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the improvement in goal attainment. In plain terms, interventions successfully got people to check their progress much more often, and this increased monitoring was associated with a meaningful but considerably more modest improvement in achieving the goal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That distinction matters. A tracking system can be extremely successful at generating entries, check ins or measurements without producing an equally dramatic change in the outcome that ultimately matters. The evidence supports monitoring as an aid to goal pursuit, not as a guarantee of success. The meta analysis also produced a particularly useful implementation finding. Monitoring in private had an estimated effect of d = 0.19 on goal attainment, whereas monitoring in public was associated with d = 0.55 and reporting monitoring information with d = 0.47. Physically recording the information was associated with an effect of d = 0.43, compared with d = 0.29 when information was not recorded. When goal attainment itself was measured objectively, the corresponding recorded versus unrecorded estimates were d = 0.57 versus d = 0.23. [Library] 1library.net Does Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence... These are moderator findings across sets of studies, rather than clean estimates from one enormous experiment independently randomising people to “visible” and “invisible” tracking. They should therefore be interpreted more cautiously than the overall randomised comparison between monitoring interventions and controls. The authors themselves called for direct experiments comparing reported with unreported monitoring to establish the mechanisms more firmly. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. The Science of Habit Tracking: Why Visible Progress Doubles Follow Through Channel: Habits, Backed by Science Open on YouTube Why a visible record may be more useful than a mental check The basic self regulation logic is straightforward. Monitoring supplies information about the difference between the present state and a desired state. If the target is 8,000 steps and the display says 4,300 late in the afternoon, the discrepancy can prompt an adjustment. Without sufficiently frequent feedback , people may simply fail to notice that behaviour is drifting away from the target. The Harkin meta analysis supports this feedback loop account because interventions that changed monitoring frequency subsequently improved goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Recording adds something beyond noticing the number in the moment. Harkin and colleagues proposed that a physical record may strengthen the encoding and later retrieval of progress information. A diary, spreadsheet, calendar mark or stored digital reading also gives the person something that can be examined across time. It becomes harder for yesterday's shortfall—or improvement—to disappear from memory. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. A record can therefore turn an impression such as “I have been exercising quite regularly” into a sequence that can be inspected. The useful feature is not necessarily paper rather than pixels: the relevant distinction is between transient awareness and information that is actually recorded and available for review. Indeed, physical activity monitors provide a particularly concrete modern version of this feedback loop. A 2022 BMJ systematic review of 121 randomised controlled trials involving 16,743 participants found that physical activity monitor interventions that provided feedback increased overall physical activity by a standardised mean difference of 0.42, equivalent in the authors' analysis to roughly 1,235 additional steps per day. The certainty of evidence differed by outcome, and the authors cautioned that publication bias might mean the physical activity effect was overestimated. [BMJ] bmj.com bmj 2021 068047 bmj 2021 068047 A separate review of step count monitoring found 70 randomised trials and reported increases of about 1,126 steps per day at four months or less and 464 at one year in its preferred change from baseline analysis. The estimated effect was much less certain at two years, illustrating a recurring problem with tracking interventions: an immediately visible number can change behaviour without establishing that the same benefit will persist indefinitely. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Public reporting adds accountability, but the mechanism is not settled Making progress visible to somebody else changes the intervention again. A private spreadsheet primarily supplies information to the person pursuing the goal. A weekly report to a coach, group or other observer adds a social audience. Harkin and colleagues proposed several possible reasons why public or reported monitoring might strengthen goal pursuit: greater public commitment, accountability, concern about how one's performance is presented to others, and—in experiments—the possibility of demand effects, where participants alter behaviour because they infer what researchers expect. Their meta analysis established the moderator pattern, but it did not establish which of these explanations causes the stronger effect. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. This distinction guards against turning “public tracking works better” into another oversized self help rule. Posting every goal on social"},{"title":"Workout Location","url":"/workout-location/","description":"A slightly less impressive gym or workout space may support more exercise when it is easier to reach and simpler to use.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Exercise","level":3,"text":"Workout Location Is the Best Gym Simply the One You Will Reach? Exercise Workout Location Workout Location Is the Best Gym Simply the One You Will Reach? A slightly less impressive gym or workout space may support more exercise when it is easier to reach and simpler to use. A slightly less impressive gym or workout space may support more exercise when it is easier to reach and simpler to use. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic population context How accessibility changes the effort required to exercise Comparing commute friendly gyms with higher friction options Designing an immediately available home workout space self improvement that works exercise systems workout location Introduction The best gym is often not the gym with the best equipment. It may be the place you can reach with the least disruption to an ordinary day. A modest gym beside work, a weights room on the route home, or a small exercise area already set up in the house can turn a workout from a separate expedition into an immediately available action. Explanatory illustration 1 Research does not support a simplistic rule that the nearest facility automatically produces the most exercise. Studies of neighbourhood recreational facilities are mixed, and convenience cannot replace a programme that is safe, suitable and enjoyable. But the evidence consistently makes accessibility part of the behavioural equation: long journeys, awkward transport, inaccessible buildings and unavailable equipment create barriers, while easy access and usable facilities remove them. A large scoping review of fitness centre research, for example, identified long travel time or distance as a barrier and easy access as a facilitator among adults without physical disabilities. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The useful question is therefore not simply, “Which gym is best?” It is: which location makes starting exercise easiest on the days when enthusiasm is ordinary rather than exceptional? Home Gym vs Gym Membership — Which Is ACTUALLY Worth It? Channel: Explaining Health Like You're Five Open on YouTube How accessibility changes the effort required to exercise A 45 minute workout is not necessarily a 45 minute behavioural task. If it requires a 20 minute journey each way, parking, changing clothes, finding equipment and travelling home, the real commitment may approach 90 minutes. The same training done in a gym downstairs from an office, in a nearby leisure centre or with equipment already available at home has a different practical cost. That distinction matters because location creates demands before the exercise begins. Distance adds travel time; a car dependent facility adds traffic and parking; limited opening hours add scheduling constraints; crowded equipment can add waiting; and poor physical accessibility can make the facility unusable altogether. The 2021 scoping review of 102 papers on fitness centre participation found that adults without physical disabilities commonly encountered long transport time or distance as a barrier, whereas easy access appeared repeatedly as a facilitator. Flexible opening hours also mattered. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The effect is particularly stark for people with physical disabilities, for whom “nearby” and “accessible” are not interchangeable. The same review identified poor transport, stairs, unsuitable doors, inaccessible changing facilities and non adjustable equipment among barriers. A separate systematic review covering 533 fitness centres found shortcomings across every accessibility domain it assessed, concluding that physical and system level barriers continued to limit participation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The accessibility of fitness centers for people with disabilities: A systematic review PubMedMay 24, 2018... Published: May 24, 2018 This makes convenience broader than mileage. A useful workout location has at least three kinds of accessibility: Geographical access: it is close to home, work or a journey already being made. Operational access: it is open when the person can realistically train, without excessive waiting or setup. Functional access: the space, changing facilities and necessary equipment can actually be used by that individual. A facility can score well on one and badly on another. The gym 300 metres away is not especially convenient if its opening hours clash with work. A home workout is not frictionless if the equipment has to be excavated from a cupboard and furniture moved before every session. There is also an important limit to the evidence. Research on the wider built environment does not show that simply putting recreational facilities closer to everybody automatically increases activity. A European systematic review found no consistent relationship between access to recreational facilities and several physical activity domains, while a US review of objectively measured activity and park proximity found positive, null and mixed results. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Relationship between the physical environment and different domains of physical activity in European adults: a system... More recent built environment research likewise warns that studies differ considerably in how both environmental access and physical activity are measured. [Springer] link.springer.com Objectively measuring the association between the built environment and physical activity: a systematic review and reporting fram... So proximity should be treated as a friction reducing opportunity , not a causal guarantee. People still have to use what is nearby. Best Ways to Build Better Habits & Break Bad Ones James Clear Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube When the less impressive gym is the better choice Consider two otherwise suitable gyms. Gym A is a ten minute walk from work and lies directly on the journey home. Gym B has better machines, more classes and a swimming pool, but requires a 25 minute drive in the opposite direction. If somebody reliably uses Gym B, its extra facilities may justify the journey. But for a person whose main failure point is getting started, Gym A has an important advantage: fewer things must go right before training begins. Leaving work can flow directly into walking to the gym. There is less opportunity for a delayed meeting, traffic, bad weather, an errand or the temptation to sit down at home to derail the session. This is the practical significance of fitness centre research identifying both easy access and flexible opening hours as facilitators. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. It also fits the wider built environment literature, although that evidence should not be overstated. A systematic review concerned with causal evidence found that greater proximity and a greater mixture of destinations generally supported physical activity, and noted longitudinal evidence in which moving to an area with more recreational facilities was associated with changes in moderate to vigorous activity. The authors nevertheless emphasised the difficulty of establishing causality because people partly choose environments that suit their existing preferences. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)In search of causality: a systematic review of the relationship between the built environment and physical activity a... For older adults, the environmental signal is somewhat clearer. A systematic review and meta analysis of 100 articles found positive associations between physical activity and several environmental features, including recreational facilities, overall access to destinations, walkability and public transport. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Built environmental correlates of older adults' total physical activity and walking: a systematic review and meta analysis PubMed... An umbrella review published later reached a broadly compatible conclusion but stressed that much of the underlying evidence remained cross sectional, meaning association should not be mistaken for proof that adding a nearby facility causes a particular person to exercise. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An Umbrella Review of the Best and Most Up to Date Evidence on the Built Environment and Physical Activity in Older A... The sensible comparison, then, is not “cheap gym versus premium gym” or even “near versus far” in isolation. It is total usable value after friction is included . When choosing between plausible locations, ask which one is easiest to use during the actual week: Count the whole journey. Compare door to door time, not just kilometres. Include parking, public transport connections, changing and the return trip. Look for route compatibility. A facility between work and home may impose less behavioural cost than an equally distant facility requiring a special journey. Check the hours you will"},{"title":"Session Records","url":"/session-records/","description":"A simple record of completed sessions can separate actual exercise from plans, impressions, and optimistic memory.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Exercise","level":3,"text":"Session Records Are You Tracking Exercise or Remembering It Optimistically? Session Records Session Records Are You Tracking Exercise or Remembering It Optimistically? A simple record of completed sessions can separate actual exercise from plans, impressions, and optimistic memory. A simple record of completed sessions can separate actual exercise from plans, impressions, and optimistic memory. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Why intentions and completed sessions are easy to confuse Choosing the lightest useful exercise record Using wearables and logs without making tracking the workout self improvement that works exercise systems session records Introduction An exercise plan says what was supposed to happen; a session record says what actually happened. That distinction is surprisingly important. Physical activity questionnaires and retrospective estimates are vulnerable to imperfect recall and reporting bias, and research comparing self reports with device measurements often finds only modest agreement. A simple contemporaneous record — for example, date, activity and minutes completed — gives a routine a much firmer evidence trail. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A comparison of direct versus self report measures for assessing physical activity in adults: a systematic review P... Explanatory illustration 1 The useful question is therefore not “Do I think I exercise regularly?” but “Which sessions can I point to?” The record need not become a second fitness hobby. Paper diaries, calendar ticks, phone notes and automatically recorded wearable data can all make behaviour visible. Evidence also suggests that self monitoring can contribute to increasing physical activity, particularly when it is connected to goals or feedback , although monitoring alone is not uniformly effective. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effective techniques in healthy eating and physical activity interventions: a meta regression PubMed... For a routine already built around reliable cues and convenient opportunities, that gives the record a narrow but valuable job: reveal whether the intended cue to exercise sequence is repeatedly producing completed sessions. Why intentions and completed sessions are easy to confuse A person can have a well designed routine without consistently performing it. “I run on Monday, Wednesday and Friday” might describe a genuine pattern, an aspiration, or a mixture of successful weeks and missed sessions. Without a record, those possibilities are difficult to distinguish retrospectively. Research on physical activity measurement explains part of the problem. A systematic review covering 187 studies with comparable self report and direct measure data found generally low to moderate correlations between the two approaches. Self report sometimes produced higher and sometimes lower estimates, depending on the activity and measurement method. Recall error and socially desirable responding are among the recognised sources of disagreement. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A comparison of direct versus self report measures for assessing physical activity in adults: a systematic review P... Another systematic review examining physical activity questionnaires found that reliability — obtaining similar answers repeatedly — was generally better than validity against objective measures. In other words, a questionnaire can produce reasonably consistent answers without necessarily reconstructing activity particularly accurately. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A systematic review of reliability and objective criterion related validity of physical activity questionnaires PMC... The length of the recall period matters too. In one study of 121 young adults, accelerometers recorded an average 3.2 hours of moderate to vigorous physical activity per week. A one week recall produced 2.2 hours, while a one year recall produced 13.7 hours. The authors concluded that the one year measure substantially overestimated total activity and that the shorter recall performed much better for estimating an individual's absolute activity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Evaluation of two self report measures of physical activity with accelerometry in young adults PMCJanuary 12, 2012... Published: January 12, 2012 That particular numerical discrepancy should not be treated as a universal correction factor, but it illustrates why “I am usually quite active” is weak evidence about what happened during particular weeks. A session record changes the question from retrospective reconstruction to accumulated observations. If the intended routine is three sessions each week, six weeks of dated records can answer basic questions that memory handles poorly: Were there actually 18 sessions? Were Mondays reliable but Fridays repeatedly missed? Did the routine survive busy weeks? Did “three times a week” quietly become once or twice? That is the branch specific value of tracking. The record is not principally there to prove fitness, calories burned or athletic performance. It provides evidence about routine execution . How To Track Your Workouts Setting Up a Training Journal Channel: FightCamp Open on YouTube Choose the lightest record that answers the question A useful exercise record does not need to capture everything measurable about exercise. In fact, excessive detail creates another behaviour that must itself be maintained. If the purpose is simply to discover whether the planned routine happens, the minimum useful dataset is usually small: Date — when the session happened. Activity — enough description to identify what counted, such as “walk”, “gym” or “cycle”. Completion — a tick, yes/no field or equivalent indication that the intended session occurred. Duration or another simple threshold , where necessary — useful when the definition of a session requires, for example, at least 20 minutes. That is enough to distinguish a completed session from an intention. Optional details such as distance, sets, repetitions, heart rate, pace, perceived exertion or calories are useful only when they answer another genuine question. They are not prerequisites for knowing whether the routine occurred. There is precedent for deliberately simple records in physical activity research. In the 488 participant Get Moving randomised trial, the paper diary group received a pocket sized diary and recorded physical activity of at least ten minutes, including whether activity occurred, its type, start time and duration. Other groups used an accelerometer with web based graphical feedback, with or without additional virtual coaching. [Springer] link.springer.com Protocol for Get Moving: a randomised controlled trial to assess the effectiveness of three minimal contact interventions to prom... The eventual trial results are a useful warning against assuming that more recording automatically produces more exercise. There was no overall statistically significant difference between the four groups in physical activity energy expenditure or fitness; the paper diary group did not show the increases seen in the other groups, and the tracker plus coaching group improved physical activity energy expenditure more than the diary group. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Effectiveness of Minimal Contact Interventions: An RCT PubMed Central (PMC)Effectiveness of Minimal Contact Interventions: An RCT PMC... A record can therefore reveal behaviour without necessarily being a powerful intervention by itself. That distinction matters. Measurement and behaviour change are related but not identical jobs. A calendar tick can be perfectly adequate evidence that Tuesday's run happened even if the tick itself does nothing to make someone run. The Training Journal for Weightlifting: What, Why & How Channel: Catalyst Athletics Open on YouTube What self monitoring can — and cannot — change There is nevertheless substantial evidence that making physical activity visible can form part of effective behaviour change. A meta regression covering 122 evaluations and 44,747 participants found that self monitoring accounted for more between study variation in effectiveness than the other individual behaviour change techniques examined. Interventions combining self monitoring with at least one additional self regulation technique, such as goal setting or feedback, performed better on average than interventions without that combination. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effective techniques in healthy eating and physical activity interventions: a meta regression PubMed... More recent synthesis gives a more qualified picture. A meta review of self regulatory behaviour change techniques found mixed results for physical activity self monitoring: some reviews reported benefits and others did not, and the quality of the underlying reviews was"},{"title":"Starting Rituals","url":"/starting-rituals/","description":"A repeatable pre-workout ritual can make starting exercise more automatic even when the workout itself changes from day to day.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Exercise","level":3,"text":"Starting Rituals Why Starting Exercise Matters More Than Repeating Every Detail Starting Rituals Starting Rituals Why Starting Exercise Matters More Than Repeating Every Detail A repeatable pre workout ritual can make starting exercise more automatic even when the workout itself changes from day to day. A repeatable pre workout ritual can make starting exercise more automatic even when the workout itself changes from day to day. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Exercise instigation versus workout execution Building a stable pre workout sequence Keeping the start consistent while varying the session self improvement that works exercise systems starting rituals Introduction A workout does not have to become identical from day to day for exercise to become easier to start. The more useful target for repetition may be the beginning of the session : encountering a familiar cue, carrying out the same short preparation sequence and reaching an exercise ready state. Research distinguishes this process — exercise instigation or preparation — from execution, meaning the workout itself. Instigation habit has predicted exercise frequency more strongly than habitual execution in observational work, while intervention studies increasingly suggest that practising preparation can help people exercise more often. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 That makes a starting ritual a practical form of self improvement: standardise the few minutes before exercise, not necessarily the next 30 or 60. Trainers can go on after the same daily transition; a bottle can be filled; a mat can be unfolded; then the actual session can remain flexible. Starting matters differently from doing Exercise looks like one behaviour in a diary — “went to the gym” or “ran for 30 minutes” — but psychologically it can contain distinct phases. Instigation concerns selecting and initiating exercise. Execution concerns progressing through the actions that make up the workout once it has begun. Habit researchers have argued that these phases need not become automatic to the same degree. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... A particularly relevant study by L. Alison Phillips and Benjamin Gardner followed 123 healthy adults using surveys and a month of electronic daily diaries. Instigation habit strength was the only unique habit measure that predicted exercise frequency: execution habit strength and simply performing the same exercise pattern from session to session did not uniquely predict frequency. Changes in exercise frequency were also associated with changes in instigation habit rather than execution habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 That finding changes what “consistent exercise” can mean. Someone need not run the same route, perform the same lifts or follow the same video every Tuesday. The repeatable unit can instead be something like: finish work → change clothes → fill water bottle → put on trainers → begin Once that sequence reaches the point where exercise has started, the person might run, cycle, lift weights or take an online class. The ritual provides consistency where consistency has particular behavioural value — at the transition from not exercising to exercising. A second study sharpened this distinction by separating preparation from performance . Among 181 adults recruited from nine gyms and recreation centres, both intention and preparatory habit strength predicted exercise over six weeks, whereas performance habit did not. Temporal consistency was the strongest predictor among the proposed habit antecedents in both phases. The authors concluded that focusing on a consistent preparatory routine could help establish exercise habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The role of habit in different phases of exercise The role of habit in different phases of exercise PubMed... These studies are important but should not be overread. Much of the foundational evidence is observational and uses self reported habit measures, so it cannot prove that making a ritual automatic will by itself cause long term exercise adherence. A systematic review of longitudinal physical activity research found the instigation/preparation literature promising but still too small to confirm the proposed advantage conclusively. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. How to Start Exercise Habit Using The 2min Rule From The Book \"Atomic Habits\" Channel: Dr. Srikanth Nithyanandam, MD Open on YouTube Build a stable pre workout sequence A useful starting ritual has two jobs. First, it makes the beginning recognisable: there is a clear situation in which preparation starts. Second, it makes the route from that situation to exercise short and repeatable. This fits the broader mechanism of habit formation. Habits are learned associations in which recurring contexts increasingly prompt a behavioural response with less deliberation. For exercise, the ritual can supply a compact sequence of cues and actions even when the eventual workout is complex. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... The practical design is therefore deliberately modest: 1. Choose a dependable starting cue. Prefer something that already occurs reliably: finishing breakfast, closing a work laptop, arriving home or returning from the school run. 2. Attach a small preparation action. Change into exercise clothes, put on trainers, unfold the mat or open the exercise programme. 3. Keep the sequence short. Each additional choice creates another point at which the transition can stall. Decide equipment and basic logistics beforehand where practical. 4. End the ritual with an unmistakable first exercise action. Walk outside, start the warm up, begin the first set or press play on the class. Preparation should lead into exercise rather than becoming an elaborate routine of its own. 5. Repeat the cue to start sequence even when the workout varies. The stable behaviour is the transition into exercise. Planning can reinforce this structure. Implementation intentions specify the situation and intended response — essentially, “when X happens, I will do Y”. A meta analysis of 26 physical activity studies found small to moderate positive effects at both post intervention and follow up. A later review of 13 randomised trials found a more qualified picture: the overall pooled effect was not statistically significant, but studies that reinforced implementation intentions showed a significant benefit, and planning for barriers also appeared useful. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. A ritual therefore works better as a concrete response to a cue than as a motivational slogan. “Exercise after work” still leaves room for negotiation. “When I shut my laptop on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, I change into the clothes beside my desk and start my five minute warm up” specifies what the transition actually looks like. Explanatory illustration 2 Keep the start consistent while the session changes One of the most useful consequences of the instigation versus execution distinction is that variety inside the workout need not destroy the starting habit . Phillips and Gardner explicitly distinguished patterned exercise — repeatedly doing the same exercise actions — from habitual instigation. Patterned exercise itself did not uniquely predict exercise frequency in their study. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 This matters because rigid workout repetition can clash with ordinary exercise needs. Strength programmes progress, runners change routes, weather alters outdoor plans, people sometimes have only 15 minutes rather than an hour, and enjoyment may depend on variety. A starting ritual can absorb those changes by putting stability one level earlier in the behavioural sequence. Consider three days that all begin after the same end of work cue: On Monday, changing clothes and putting on trainers leads to a 30 minute run. On Wednesday, the same preparation leads to a short home strength session. On Friday, it leads to a walk because time or energy is limited. The exercise content differs, but the behavioural transition remains recognisable. That is closer to what the instigation evidence implies than insisting that every"},{"title":"Fallback Workouts","url":"/fallback-workouts/","description":"A prepared backup session can keep an exercise routine intact when travel, overtime, or other predictable barriers block the default plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Exercise","level":3,"text":"Fallback Workouts What Should Happen When Your Normal Workout Falls Apart? Exercise Fallback Workouts Fallback Workouts What Should Happen When Your Normal Workout Falls Apart? A prepared backup session can keep an exercise routine intact when travel, overtime, or other predictable barriers block the default plan. A prepared backup session can keep an exercise routine intact when travel, overtime, or other predictable barriers block the default plan. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention Choosing barriers that deserve a backup plan Designing a short home session that is ready in advance Using fallbacks without quietly replacing the main routine self improvement that works exercise systems fallback workouts Introduction A fallback workout is a pre decided session for the days when the normal exercise plan becomes impractical: a late finish at work, travel, bad weather, childcare trouble or another predictable disruption. Its job is not to reproduce the full gym session at home. It is to prevent an ordinary obstacle from turning “not today” into the default response. Explanatory illustration 1 The idea has a useful basis in behavioural research. “Coping planning” means identifying likely barriers and deciding in advance what to do when they occur. Across randomised trials, planning interventions produce modest improvements in physical activity , although effects vary considerably between studies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effectiveness of Planning Interventions for Improving Physical Activity in the General Population: A Systematic R... Meanwhile, recent habit research specifically warns that changing circumstances and competing responsibilities can disrupt the cues that normally start physical activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Maintaining habitual physical activity by overcoming disruptive competing actions: mechanisms and interventions PubMedDecember 19... The practical policy is therefore simple: keep the preferred workout as Plan A, but give the most predictable failures a small, specific Plan B that can start immediately at home. Which barriers deserve a backup plan? A fallback is most useful for a barrier that is both predictable and capable of cancelling the normal session . The point is not to construct contingencies for every imaginable inconvenience. It is to notice the handful of situations that repeatedly defeat an otherwise workable routine. This distinction mirrors the difference between action planning and coping planning in exercise research. Action planning specifies when, where and how exercise will happen. Coping planning goes a step further by anticipating difficulties and specifying how they will be handled. In a longitudinal study of 354 people, coping planning appeared particularly relevant to maintaining physical activity among people who were already active. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Beyond behavioural intentions: planning mediates between intentions and physical activity PubMedJune 2, 2007... Published: June 2, 2007 In an earlier randomised study involving 211 cardiac rehabilitation patients, participants given both action and coping planning subsequently reported more exercise than those given action planning alone or standard care. That clinical population should not be generalised directly to every exerciser, but it illustrates the underlying intervention clearly: planning for obstacles can add something that merely scheduling the intended behaviour does not. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Action plans and coping plans for physical exercise: A longitudinal intervention study in cardiac rehabilitation PubMed... For an ordinary exercise routine, useful triggers might therefore look like this: If overtime makes the gym impractical , do the prepared home session after closing the laptop. If travelling removes access to normal equipment , use the equipment free version in the hotel room or accommodation. If childcare or another responsibility prevents leaving home , switch to the home session rather than postponing the decision until later. If severe weather blocks an outdoor session , use the indoor alternative at the normal workout time. Notice what is absent: “If I do not feel motivated, do something easier.” That is a much looser rule. A fallback works best as a response to an identifiable constraint, not as an unrestricted permission slip to downgrade any demanding session. There is also little value in building elaborate alternatives for rare events. If a barrier has cancelled the routine several times, it deserves attention. If it has never occurred, preparing for it may simply make the exercise system more complicated. 10 MIN FULL BODY BEGINNER WORKOUT No Equipment Rowan Row Channel: Rowan Row Open on YouTube Make the home session ready before it is needed The worst time to invent a backup workout is the evening when the original plan has already fallen apart. At that point the person has to recognise the disruption, decide whether exercise is still worthwhile, search for exercises, choose repetitions, find equipment and determine how long the substitute should last. The fallback is supposed to remove those decisions, not relocate them. A useful home session can therefore be surprisingly plain. For an adult who is already familiar with the movements and for whom they are appropriate, a pre written example might be: 1. Two minutes: brisk marching, stair walking or another simple warm up. 2. Six to twelve minutes: two or three rounds of familiar movements such as squats or sit to stands, press ups against a suitable surface, lunges or another leg exercise, and a familiar trunk exercise. 3. Two to five minutes: brisk stair climbing, marching or another aerobic movement if space and circumstances permit. 4. Stop when the predetermined session is complete. That is an illustration, not a universal prescription. Exercise selection and intensity need to suit the person's ability, health and experience. The important design features are more general: few movements, little or no equipment, a fixed sequence and a clear finish point . Home exercise does not have to mean “exercise that does not count”. The NHS lists resistance bands and body weight movements such as press ups among ways to strengthen muscles at home. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk physical activity guidelines for adults aged 19 to 64 May 22, 2024... Published: May 22, 2024 Home based resistance training has also been studied as a genuine training method rather than merely an emergency substitute; systematic reviews in older adults, for example, have found improvements in strength, although supervised or facility based programmes may have advantages and those findings cannot automatically be extended to every population. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Unsupervised home based resistance training for community dwelling older adults: A systematic review and meta analysis of randomize... Short is a feature, not a loophole A common mistake is to make the fallback nearly as demanding logistically as Plan A. A 50 minute gym workout replaced by a complicated 45 minute home programme has preserved most of the original barrier. A deliberately short fallback has a different objective. It creates a response that remains feasible after the day has already gone wrong. Current UK guidance also removes an old reason for dismissing short activity: moderate to vigorous activity does not have to occur in bouts of at least ten minutes to contribute towards physical activity totals. Small bouts accumulated across the day and week can provide benefits. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UKJuly 10, 2026... Published: July 10, 2026 The World Health Organization similarly states that physical activity of any duration is associated with health benefits and that some activity is better than none. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That does not mean five or ten minutes is nutritionally equivalent, so to speak, to the workout it replaced. Volume, intensity and exercise type still matter. UK guidance recommends that adults accumulate at least 150 minutes of moderate intensity activity, 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or an equivalent combination each week, alongside muscle strengthening work on at least two days. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UKJuly 10, 2026... Published: July 10, 2026 A ten minute fallback should therefore be understood as ten useful minutes, not as a magical substitute for every longer session. Its behavioural value may be larger than its duration suggests, however, because it supplies an executable response at exactly the point where the normal routine has become unavailable. Explanatory illustration 2 Preserve the cue even when the workout changes Fallback"},{"title":"Minimum Start","url":"/minimum-start/","description":"Defining a tiny successful start lets you preserve the exercise routine without committing in advance to the full workout.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Exercise","level":3,"text":"Minimum Start How Small Should Your Minimum Workout Start Be? Exercise Minimum Start Minimum Start How Small Should Your Minimum Workout Start Be? Defining a tiny successful start lets you preserve the exercise routine without committing in advance to the full workout. Defining a tiny successful start lets you preserve the exercise routine without committing in advance to the full workout. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention Separating starting criteria from performance criteria Examples for walking, running, and strength training When a minimum start should remain small instead of expanding self improvement that works exercise systems minimum start Introduction A minimum workout start is the smallest pre decided action that counts as beginning a planned exercise session on a difficult day. It is not a miniature fitness target and it does not need to deliver the benefits of the full workout. Its job is narrower: when motivation is low, make the threshold for responding to the usual exercise cue small enough that starting remains realistic. Explanatory illustration 1 For a walker, that might mean putting on walking shoes and going outside for five minutes. For a runner, it could mean changing into running kit and beginning an easy five minute warm up. For strength training, it might mean setting up and completing the first warm up set. After that minimum has been completed, continuing is optional. This distinction matters because research on exercise habits suggests that instigating a workout and executing the workout are different behavioural problems . The evidence does not establish a scientifically optimal five minute, one set or one kilometre minimum. Instead, it supports a practical policy: define the start separately from the performance standard, keep that start easy to execute under predictable barriers, and allow the full session to remain the goal without making it the price of beginning. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Separate starting criteria from performance criteria The most useful way to set a minimum is to maintain two standards rather than lowering one standard . The starting criterion answers: What do I have to do when my exercise cue occurs for this routine to have begun? The performance criterion answers: What workout am I normally trying to complete? On an ordinary day, the two flow together. On a difficult day, they can separate. Suppose Tuesday's planned workout is a 40 minute run. The performance criterion can remain 40 minutes while the minimum start is simply: change into running clothes, leave the house and run or walk easily for five minutes. Reaching five minutes does not magically make a five minute outing equivalent to the planned run. It means the person has successfully carried out the start policy . At that point, they can continue, shorten the session or stop. That distinction fits an important finding from exercise habit research. In a study of 123 healthy adults who reported exercise through daily electronic diaries for a month, L. Alison Phillips and Benjamin Gardner distinguished instigation habit — automatically deciding or preparing to exercise — from execution habit , the automatic performance of the exercise sequence itself. Instigation habit was the only unique habit predictor of exercise frequency. The study was observational rather than a trial of minimum workouts, so it cannot prove that tiny starts cause better adherence. But it provides a strong reason to treat “getting the workout started” as a behavioural target in its own right. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 Gardner and colleagues subsequently developed the distinction beyond exercise, describing habitual instigation as a cue triggering the selection and initiation of an action, while habitual execution concerns progression through its component actions. Their results again indicated that instigation was more closely associated with behaviour frequency. For a minimum workout policy, the implication is useful: standardise the doorway into exercise without requiring the whole workout to be standardised or automatic . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMed... This also explains why “do at least 30 minutes” is often a poor definition of a minimum start. Thirty minutes is a duration target. It says how much exercise to perform, not what observable action constitutes beginning. A better start criterion occurs near the front of the sequence: shoes on and outside; onto the treadmill and moving; at the rack with the warm up set begun. How to Stop Procrastinating by Using the Two Minute Rule Atomic Habits by James Clear (Chapter 13) Channel: Kyky's TALK BUFFET Open on YouTube How small should the minimum be? There is no research backed universal number of minutes, steps, repetitions or sets that constitutes the ideal minimum workout start. Claims that everyone should use exactly two minutes, five minutes or one set should therefore be understood as rules of thumb rather than established physiological or behavioural thresholds. A more defensible test is functional: the minimum should be small enough that you would normally still do it on a low motivation day, but substantial enough to constitute unmistakable entry into the exercise sequence . “Think about running” is too early. “Complete the entire programmed run” defeats the purpose. “Put on my kit, go outside and start the warm up” sits between them. Planning research provides support for making this response concrete. A 2022 systematic review and meta analysis covering 41 randomised trials, with 35 included in the meta analysis, found that physical activity planning interventions produced a small to moderate improvement in activity compared with control conditions. These interventions typically specify when, where or how action will occur; some also use coping plans for anticipated barriers. An earlier systematic review likewise concluded that implementation intention approaches appeared to promote physical activity, particularly when plans addressed obstacles. Neither literature specifically validates “minimum workouts”, but both support deciding the response to a difficult situation before that situation occurs. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effectiveness of Planning Interventions for Improving Physical Activity in the General Population: A Systematic R... A useful policy can therefore be written in advance: When my normal workout cue occurs and I strongly want to skip the session, I will complete my minimum start. After completing it, I may stop without treating the day as a failure. The last sentence matters. If “five minutes” secretly means “once I start, I must force myself through 45 minutes”, the five minute rule ceases to be a genuine minimum. It becomes a bargaining tactic whose real cost is still the full workout. Public health guidance also makes room for small amounts of activity. The UK Chief Medical Officers state that adults should aim to be active every day, that any activity is better than none and that more is better, while the World Health Organization advises inactive adults to begin with small amounts and gradually increase frequency, intensity and duration. These recommendations concern health enhancing physical activity rather than habit design, but they help avoid the false conclusion that a shortened session is physically worthless. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UKJuly 10, 2026... Published: July 10, 2026 Explanatory illustration 2 Minimum starts for walking, running and strength training A minimum start works best when it is attached to the existing cue and normal exercise sequence . It should not require inventing a second complicated workout for bad days. For walking , a normal plan might be a 30 minute brisk walk after lunch. A workable minimum could be: put on walking shoes, leave the building and walk for five minutes . Someone beginning from very low activity might simply walk to a predetermined nearby point and back. NHS guidance explicitly encourages people to start gradually, and current Better Health advice emphasises that every minute counts while people build towards recommended weekly activity levels. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk How to be more active Better Health NHSFebruary 17, 2023... Published: February 17, 2023 For running , the minimum should usually preserve the act of starting without demanding normal pace or distance: change into running kit, go outside and complete five minutes of"},{"title":"Temptation Bundling","url":"/temptation-bundling-f128a0/","description":"Saving an absorbing pleasure for workouts can turn exercise into the gateway to something you already want.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Immediate Rewards","level":3,"text":"Temptation Bundling Can Temptation Bundling Make Exercise Easier to Repeat? Immediate Rewards Temptation Bundling Temptation Bundling Can Temptation Bundling Make Exercise Easier to Repeat? Saving an absorbing pleasure for workouts can turn exercise into the gateway to something you already want. Saving an absorbing pleasure for workouts can turn exercise into the gateway to something you already want. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case policy intervention How temptation bundling works What the gym audiobook experiments found How to build a pairing that lasts self improvement that works immediate rewards temptation bundling Introduction Temptation bundling makes exercise more immediately rewarding by reserving something genuinely enjoyable for the workout itself. Instead of asking the distant benefits of fitness to outweigh the immediate appeal of staying on the sofa, the method changes the choice: going to the gym becomes the way to hear the next chapter of an absorbing audiobook, watch a favourite programme or enjoy another compatible pleasure. The reward arrives during the behaviour rather than weeks or years later. Explanatory illustration 1 The best evidence comes from two field experiments built around audiobooks and gym attendance. The first found a large initial increase when access to tempting novels was physically restricted to the gym, although the effect weakened over time. A later, much larger experiment found smaller but more persistent gains when gym members were taught to create their own bundles. Together, they suggest that temptation bundling can make exercise easier to repeat, while also showing that the pairing has to remain tempting and credible if it is to last. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 How temptation bundling works A temptation bundle combines a “want” with a “should” : an activity that is attractive immediately with one whose most important benefits arrive later. In the original exercise research, the want was listening to a page turning audiobook and the should was going to the gym. The crucial feature was not merely having entertainment available while exercising. Access to the entertainment was tied to exercise, so wanting to continue the story could create an additional reason to return. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 That distinction separates temptation bundling from a conventional reward. “Exercise today and watch television afterwards” puts a prize behind the workout. “I watch this particular programme only while walking on the treadmill” makes exercise the gateway to the prize and allows both experiences to happen together. This matters because the workout acquires some of the attraction of an activity the person already wants to do. The researchers argued that combining the two can also reduce the sense that the indulgence is wasted time and distract from some of exercise's unpleasantness. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 Restriction is therefore part of the mechanism. If the audiobook is equally available on the sofa, during the commute and in bed, there is no need to exercise to discover what happens next. Reserving it for workouts preserves its motivational value. Advice from Wharton based on the research similarly emphasises that temptation bundling works best when the indulgence can genuinely be restricted to the activity that needs extra motivation. [Wharton Knowledge] knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu Wharton Knowledge Get It Done: Two Techniques to Fast track Your Goals Wharton KnowledgeGet It Done: Two Techniques to Fast track Your Goals Knowledge at WhartonFebruary 28, 2022... Published: February 28, 2022 This does not mean the reward must be an audiobook. The useful design principle is compatibility : the pleasure should make the workout nicer without preventing the exercise itself. An engaging podcast or television series can fit steady cardio; favourite music may fit running or resistance training. Entertainment that demands constant visual attention is a much poorer match for activities where attention to surroundings, technique or safety matters. The research establishes the principle more strongly than it establishes any particular medium as universally best. Temptation Bundling The Key to Building Consistent Habits Channel: Vihan Chelliah Open on YouTube What the gym audiobook experiments found The original experiment by Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp turned the idea into an unusually literal commitment device. The researchers recruited 226 people at a university fitness facility and randomly allocated them to three groups. One group received iPods containing four tempting audio novels that could be accessed only at the gym . A second group received the novels on their own devices and was encouraged to impose the gym only rule on itself. The control group received a US$25 bookshop gift certificate of comparable value and encouragement to exercise. Gym entry records supplied an objective measure of attendance rather than relying on participants to remember their workouts. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The novels were deliberately chosen for their ability to pull listeners onwards. Participants selected from 82 pre tested titles, including popular page turners such as The Hunger Games , The Da Vinci Code , Twilight and The Help . That detail is central rather than decorative: a bundle has little motivational force if the supposedly tempting half is something the person feels indifferent about. [Wharton Knowledge] knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu Wharton Knowledge Using 'The Hunger Games' to Encourage Healthier Choices Wharton KnowledgeUsing 'The Hunger Games' to Encourage Healthier Choices Knowledge at WhartonNovember 19, 2013... Published: November 19, 2013 Initially, participants whose audiobook access was physically restricted to the gym visited 51% more frequently than controls . Participants who were merely encouraged to restrict their listening visited 29% more frequently than controls . In the first week, the control group averaged about 0.75 gym visits; regression adjusted increases were 0.48 visits for the restricted access group and 0.27 for the self restriction group. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: an Evaluation of Temptation Bundling by Katherine L. Milkman, Julia A. Minson, Kevin Vol... The striking percentage should not be mistaken for evidence of a permanent 51% improvement. The treatment effect declined substantially over the nine week experiment, particularly around the Thanksgiving interruption. Across the seven weeks before Thanksgiving, the estimated increase attributable to the full treatment was about 0.31 visits per week, falling to about 0.21 when the whole treatment period was considered. The authors explicitly concluded that the intervention's benefits tapered over time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 That decline is one of the experiment's most useful findings for habit design. The researchers considered the possibility that time away from the audiobook reduced craving for the story, weakening its ability to pull people back to the gym. Whatever the exact explanation, the interruption shows that temptation bundling is not a magical switch that permanently transforms motivation. Its leverage depends partly on the “want” continuing to feel like a want. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 There was another revealing result. At the end of the study, 61% of participants were willing to pay something for a month of gym only access to an iPod containing a tempting audiobook even though unrestricted access would otherwise have been available free . In other words, many participants placed value on having their future choices constrained. That supports the interpretation of temptation bundling not merely as entertainment but as a commitment device: restricting an attractive option can be useful precisely because unrestricted access makes it too easy to consume the pleasure without doing the exercise. [National Bureau of Economic"},{"title":"Component Cooking","url":"/component-cooking/","description":"Cooking flexible components together can turn dinner into a quick assembly choice instead of a fresh planning and cooking task.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Meal Prep","level":3,"text":"Component Cooking Batch Components Without Eating the Same Meal Meal Prep Component Cooking Component Cooking Batch Components Without Eating the Same Meal Cooking flexible components together can turn dinner into a quick assembly choice instead of a fresh planning and cooking task. Cooking flexible components together can turn dinner into a quick assembly choice instead of a fresh planning and cooking task. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Building a small set of mix and match meal components Combining grains vegetables proteins and sauces through the week Avoiding repetition and unused leftovers self improvement that works meal prep component cooking Introduction Component cooking is a form of meal preparation that does not decide every dinner in advance. Instead, it prepares a small set of versatile building blocks — perhaps a grain, one or two vegetables, a protein or pulse, and a couple of sauces or flavourings — and leaves the final combination until mealtime. The important distinction is that the evening choice becomes “Which of these ready components shall I combine?” rather than “What shall I cook from scratch?” Explanatory illustration 1 That makes component cooking particularly useful when the aim is to remove daily food decision making without committing to five identical portions. There is good observational evidence that meal planning is associated with greater dietary variety and better diet quality, although this does not establish cause and effect. Direct research comparing component cooking with conventional batch cooking is much thinner. Its strongest case therefore comes from a combination of established findings about planning, time pressure and healthy eating, plus the practical mechanics of making several meals from the same prepared ingredients. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Build a small set of components, not a week's menu The basic unit of component cooking is not a recipe but a reusable ingredient with several possible destinations . University of Kentucky nutrition guidance describes the method as preparing individual meal elements and combining them in different ways. Its examples span carbohydrates such as brown rice, quinoa, potatoes and wholegrain breads; proteins including beans, lentils, eggs and tofu as well as meat and fish; vegetables; and flavour components such as hummus, pesto, salsa, dressings and spices. [Human Resources University of Kentucky] hr.uky.edu Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources A workable batch therefore does not need to be large. For example: Base: brown rice, quinoa, potatoes or another grain or starch. Vegetables: a tray of roasted peppers, onions and courgettes, plus something fresh or frozen for contrast. Protein: lentils, chickpeas, tofu, eggs, chicken or another protein that suits the household. Flavour: two substantially different sauces, dressings or seasoning combinations. Quick extras: wraps, salad leaves, herbs, yoghurt, nuts, seeds or other ingredients that need little or no preparation. This structure has an important advantage over cooking five complete meals. The expensive decisions — what food to buy, which ingredients need cooking, whether there will be a useful protein and vegetables available — have already been made. The remaining choice is deliberately small. That is consistent with research on implementation intentions , in which a desired behaviour is tied to a predetermined situation or response. A meta analysis of 23 studies found a moderate effect for implementation intentions intended to increase healthy eating, and a later meta regression covering 70 interventions also found a significant, though smaller, overall effect. Component cooking is not itself an implementation intention intervention, so those findings should not be treated as direct evidence for the method. They do, however, support the broader mechanism of translating a vague intention such as “eat better this week” into an easier, pre arranged action. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do implementation intentions help to eat a healthy diet? A systematic review and meta analysis of the empirical evidence PubMedFe... High Protein Vegan Meal Prep! 🍂 Fall Falafel Bowl Channel: Steamy Vegan Open on YouTube How the same components become different meals Variety comes less from cooking more ingredients than from changing format, flavour and texture around a stable core. A batch of lentils, rice and roasted vegetables need not produce three servings of the same grain bowl. On one evening, rice, vegetables and lentils could become a warm bowl with a yoghurt herb dressing. On another, the vegetables and lentils could go into a wrap with leaves and a sharper sauce. Later, rice can become the base of a quick stir fry with an additional frozen vegetable and egg. The ingredients overlap substantially, but the eating experience changes because the assembly and seasoning change. This is where sauces and finishing ingredients do disproportionate work. Preparing every component with a highly specific flavour can lock it into one meal; keeping the main components relatively adaptable allows the final seasoning to establish the identity of the dish. Practical component cooking guidance consequently treats dressings, pesto, salsa, hummus, sauces, spices and seasoning blends as a component category in their own right. [Human Resources University of Kentucky] hr.uky.edu Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources The meal format can change too. University of Kentucky's component cooking examples include bowls, stir fries, tacos and quesadillas, wraps, salads, pasta dishes, egg dishes and open faced toast. That list illustrates the central mechanism: the cook is not trying to invent a new recipe each evening, but routing already prepared food into one of a small number of familiar formats. [Human Resources University of Kentucky] hr.uky.edu Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources There is also no nutritional requirement for every individual assembly to reproduce a perfect plate. The NHS Eatwell Guide explicitly says that dietary balance does not have to be achieved at every meal; it can be considered across a day or even a week. That makes a component system particularly compatible with weekly thinking. Across the available components, it is possible to make vegetables, higher fibre starchy foods and varied protein sources easy defaults without requiring exactly the same proportions every evening. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk The Eatwell Guide November 29, 2022... Published: November 29, 2022 Variety without restoring the planning burden There is an apparent paradox here. More combinations create variety, but too many combinations can recreate the decision problem that meal preparation was supposed to solve. A fridge containing twelve unrelated prepared foods may simply replace “What shall I cook?” with “What am I supposed to do with all this?” The solution is bounded variety : enough components to create noticeably different meals, but few enough that the available combinations remain obvious. One grain or starch, one main protein, two or three vegetables and two contrasting flavour options can already produce multiple combinations. Convenient components — canned pulses, frozen vegetables, bagged leaves, wholegrain wraps or ready made hummus — can extend the system without creating another batch cooking job. University of Kentucky explicitly includes such prepared foods within its component approach rather than assuming that everything must be made from scratch. [Human Resources University of Kentucky] hr.uky.edu Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources Human Resources University of Kentucky Component cooking Human Resources This matters because preparation time is a genuine constraint, not simply a motivation problem. In a population study of 1,319 adults, people who spent less time preparing and cleaning up food were more likely to be working adults who prioritised convenience; greater preparation time was associated with more frequent consumption of foods including vegetables and fruit, while spending less than an hour a day on preparation was associated with more fast food use and spending on food away from home. The study was cross sectional, so it cannot show that cooking time itself caused the healthier pattern. It nevertheless makes the practical constraint clear: a system designed for real life has to compete on convenience. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Time Spent on Home Food Preparation and Indicators of Healthy Eating PMC... Component cooking attempts to do"},{"title":"Freezer Backup","url":"/freezer-backup/","description":"A ready freezer meal can keep one late or chaotic evening from becoming an entirely open food decision.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Meal Prep","level":3,"text":"Freezer Backup The Freezer Meal That Saves a Bad Evening Meal Prep Freezer Backup Freezer Backup The Freezer Meal That Saves a Bad Evening A ready freezer meal can keep one late or chaotic evening from becoming an entirely open food decision. A ready freezer meal can keep one late or chaotic evening from becoming an entirely open food decision. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Why disrupted schedules create risky food decision points Choosing one fallback meal that is genuinely easy to use Keeping emergency meals visible stocked and realistic self improvement that works meal prep freezer backup Introduction An emergency freezer meal is useful for a very specific moment: the evening when the original dinner plan has failed and hunger arrives before a replacement plan does. A meeting overruns, a train is delayed, childcare changes or the intended ingredients never get cooked. At that point, the freezer meal turns an open question — “What shall I eat now?” — into a small execution task: take out one known meal and heat it. Explanatory illustration 1 That matters because time scarcity is associated with less home food preparation and greater use of fast food and ready prepared food, while meal planning is associated with better diet quality and food variety. These studies do not prove that a freezer meal itself improves health, but they support the practical principle behind it: food decisions become harder to execute as time and preparation requirements increase. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Time scarcity and food choices: an overview Time scarcity and food choices: an overview PubMedMay 15, 2006... Published: May 15, 2006 The strongest freezer backup system is therefore not an enormous batch cooking project. It is one or two meals that are genuinely acceptable, complete enough for dinner, easy to identify and quick enough to use on the evening when everything else has gone wrong. Why disrupted evenings become risky decision points The important feature of a disrupted evening is not simply that somebody is busy. It is that the planned sequence between work, shopping, preparation and eating has broken. A dinner that would normally require 30 minutes of cooking may suddenly be competing with alternatives that require almost no preparation at all. Research on time scarcity gives this problem some empirical grounding. A review in Appetite linked perceived lack of time with reduced home preparation, more fast food and greater consumption of convenience or ready prepared foods. Research involving employed mothers also found that time pressure and convenience orientation were negatively related to confidence in preparing a healthy evening meal, although time pressure itself did not independently predict how healthy the meals were. That qualification is important: a hectic evening does not automatically produce a poor dinner, but it can make meals requiring substantial last minute effort less competitive. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Time scarcity and food choices: an overview Time scarcity and food choices: an overview PubMedMay 15, 2006... Published: May 15, 2006 An emergency freezer meal changes that competition. The comparison is no longer between “cook the intended meal” and “find something easier”. It becomes a choice between several easy options, one of which has already been selected and paid for. There is also a useful connection with research on implementation intentions — plans specifying what somebody will do when a particular situation occurs. A meta analysis of 23 studies found that these plans had a moderate effect on increasing healthier eating behaviours and a smaller effect on reducing unhealthy ones. The evidence concerns implementation intentions generally rather than freezer meals specifically, so it should not be stretched into a claim that frozen dinners have a proven behavioural effect. But it does suggest why a rule such as “when the evening plan collapses, I heat the freezer meal” is more actionable than “when I am busy, I will try to eat well”. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do implementation intentions help to eat a healthy diet? A systematic review and meta analysis of the empirical evidence PubMedFe... The distinction is subtle but useful. The backup meal does not demand that a tired person successfully reject every takeaway, snack or convenience option. It makes one acceptable meal immediately available. 6 Dump and Go Instant Pot Recipes That Save Hours Every Week Channel: Freezer Meals 101 Open on YouTube Choose one fallback meal you will actually use The most effective emergency meal is not necessarily the meal with the most ambitious nutritional profile. It has to survive contact with the situation for which it was designed. A freezer full of worthy dishes that nobody wants after a difficult day is not much of a decision removal system. Nor is a frozen meal that requires overnight defrosting when disruption usually becomes apparent at 7 pm. For this particular purpose, usability is part of meal quality . A good candidate normally has four characteristics: It is genuinely liked. The backup needs to compete with appealing alternatives rather than feel like a punishment for failing to follow the original plan. It reheats with little attention. Soups, stews, curries, chilli style dishes, lentil dishes and many pasta bakes can work because they tolerate freezing and reheating reasonably well. It is stored in useful portions. One or two person portions avoid having to thaw a large batch merely to rescue one dinner. It is sufficiently complete on its own. A meal that still requires chopping several ingredients, cooking two side dishes and making a sauce has preserved too much of the original problem. “Complete” need not mean that every frozen portion perfectly reproduces the proportions of the Eatwell Guide. UK guidance explicitly says those proportions describe eating across a day or even a week rather than requiring every individual meal to match them. As a practical benchmark, however, a freezer dinner can combine a substantial vegetable component, a starchy carbohydrate — preferably higher fibre or wholegrain where practical — and a source of protein such as beans, lentils, fish, eggs, meat or another alternative. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The Eatwell Guide: how to use in promotional material The Eatwell Guide: how to use in promotional material That makes dishes such as lentil and vegetable curry with rice, bean chilli, vegetable rich pasta bake, chicken and vegetable casserole, or a substantial soup with an easy accompaniment plausible backups. The purpose of these examples is structural rather than prescriptive: each can put most of dinner into one reheating operation. Explanatory illustration 2 Homemade is not the only legitimate backup A commercially prepared frozen meal can perform the same decision removing function. The trade off is that “ready meal” does not tell you very much about nutritional quality. UK research has found considerable variation. A study of 166 supermarket ready meals found that the category overall tended to be high in saturated fat and salt, but one fifth of the meals examined were low in fat, saturated fat, salt and sugar; healthier labelled ranges performed considerably better. A much larger 2019 survey covering 1,681 UK ready meals found median values per serving of 434 kcal, 14.8 g fat, 5.0 g saturated fat and 1.8 g salt. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The nutritional content and cost of supermarket ready meals. Cross sectional analysis PMCSeptember 1, 2015... Published: September 1, 2015 Nor does “home cooked” automatically mean nutritionally superior. An earlier BMJ comparison found that neither the supermarket ready meals studied nor recipes by television chefs met all of the nutritional recommendations used by the researchers; the chef recipes actually contained more energy, fat and saturated fat and less fibre per portion. More recent UK research comparing 54 ready meals with home cooked equivalents nevertheless found disadvantages for ready meals overall, including higher free sugar and higher cost in the comparisons studied. [BMJ] bmj.com Nutritional content of supermarket ready meals and recipes by television chefs in the United Kingdom: cross sectional study The BMJD... The sensible conclusion is not that ready meals are healthy or unhealthy as a class. It is to select the actual product. NHS guidance recommends using front of pack information to compare similar foods, favouring more green and amber indicators and fewer red ones where possible; it also cautions that a manufacturer's stated portion may differ from the amount somebody actually eats. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk Food labels Food labels For an emergency reserve, a reasonably balanced commercial frozen meal that reliably gets eaten may therefore be more useful than an idealised homemade option that repeatedly runs out or"},{"title":"Ingredient Prep","url":"/ingredient-prep/","description":"Prepping vegetables, grains and proteins in advance can cut evening effort without locking the week into identical meals.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Meal Prep","level":3,"text":"Ingredient Prep How Ingredient Prep Makes Dinner Easier Meal Prep Ingredient Prep Ingredient Prep How Ingredient Prep Makes Dinner Easier Prepping vegetables, grains and proteins in advance can cut evening effort without locking the week into identical meals. Prepping vegetables, grains and proteins in advance can cut evening effort without locking the week into identical meals. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Which ingredients save the most time when prepared ahead How to prep components without sacrificing variety Where ingredient prep stops saving effort self improvement that works meal prep ingredient prep Introduction Ingredient prep makes weeknight dinners faster by doing the slow, repetitive work before the hungry end of day moment. Instead of preparing complete meals in advance, the useful middle ground is to wash or cut sturdy vegetables, cook selected grains or pulses, portion proteins and keep a few flexible components ready to combine. Dinner still changes from night to night, but much of its friction has already disappeared. Explanatory illustration 1 This matters because time is a genuine constraint on home cooking. In a population study of 1,319 adults, people spending the least time on food preparation were more likely to prioritise convenience, eat fast food more frequently and consume vegetables and fruit less often. UK time use research adds an important qualification: food preparation competes with sleep, leisure and other activities, so simply telling people to spend more time cooking can impose its own costs. Ingredient prep offers a practical compromise. It does not make cooking effortless, and direct trials isolating this particular technique are limited. Its value is more straightforward: move washing, chopping, cooking and portioning to a convenient period so that a later meal requires fewer steps. Which ingredients save the most time when prepared ahead? The best candidates are not necessarily the ingredients that can be prepared early. They are the ones that remove a meaningful bottleneck later without deteriorating badly in storage. Sturdy vegetables are often high value prep. Washing, trimming and cutting carrots, peppers, broccoli, cauliflower and similar vegetables can remove several small tasks from dinner. A container of ready to cook vegetables can go directly into a roasting tray, frying pan, curry or soup. But cutting is not neutral: research on fresh cut produce shows that damaging plant tissue increases physiological stress and can accelerate moisture loss, softening, browning, flavour changes and microbial deterioration. The practical implication is to prepare only as far ahead as quality permits rather than automatically chopping every vegetable for the whole week. Cooked grains can remove a larger block of active waiting time. Preparing a grain in advance means a weekday bowl, stir fry or side dish does not have to begin with measuring water and waiting for a pan to cook. Rice, however, needs particular care. The Food Standards Agency (FSA) advises that rice intended for later use should be chilled quickly — within one hour — kept refrigerated and used within 24 hours; reheated rice should be steaming hot throughout and should not be reheated more than once. A grain prep habit is useful only if its storage routine is equally reliable. Pulses offer an especially flexible component. A batch of lentils, chickpeas or beans can move between salads, soups, curries, wraps and grain bowls. There is no requirement to start with dried pulses, however. Tinned beans and lentils remove soaking and cooking entirely. NHS linked guidance recognises dried and tinned pulses as relatively inexpensive protein options, while NHS guidance counts pulses towards fruit and vegetable intake. In a system designed to reduce effort, opening and draining a tin may sometimes be a better intervention than adding another Sunday cooking job. Proteins are useful to portion when portioning removes genuine weekday work. A larger cooked batch can be divided for later meals, while raw ingredients can be organised according to their storage instructions and use by dates. Food safety matters more than extracting the maximum number of refrigerator days: the FSA advises eating ordinary cooked leftovers within 48 hours or freezing them, and keeping a refrigerator at 0–5°C. The common feature is leverage. Chopping one onion in advance saves little if the later recipe still demands 25 minutes of preparation. Having the vegetables, grain and main protein already usable can change the character of the task: dinner becomes assembly plus a short cooking step rather than a cooking project begun from zero. Time Saving Meal Prep Tips for a Hassle Free Cooking My Weekend Meal Prep Routine Channel: Simplify Your Space Open on YouTube How to prep components without sacrificing variety Preparing ingredients rather than finished dishes separates efficiency from repetition . The same neutral components can lead to meals that taste and feel different because the final combination, cooking method and seasoning remain undecided. Suppose one preparation session produces roasted or chopped vegetables, cooked lentils and another ready to use protein. Those components do not have to become three identical boxes. Vegetables might go into a pasta dish one evening and a quick omelette another. Lentils could become a warm side, a soup addition or part of a salad. A protein can move between a wrap and a grain bowl. What has been predetermined is the labour, not necessarily the meal. That flexibility matters because planning need not mean reduced dietary variety. In a large French observational study involving 40,554 adults, people who reported meal planning were more likely to fall into the highest quartile for overall food variety as well as the highest quartile for adherence to dietary guidance. The study cannot establish that planning caused those differences, but it undermines the assumption that advance preparation inevitably produces a monotonous diet. A workable component system therefore tends to have three features: Prepare neutral bases more heavily than finished combinations. Cook the lentils; decide later whether they become a curry, salad or soup. Leave fast flavour decisions until dinner. Sauces, herbs, spices, dressings and garnishes can change the direction of ingredients whose labour intensive preparation has already happened. Prep according to the difficult night, not an imaginary perfect week. If Tuesday and Thursday are consistently rushed, having components ready for those evenings may deliver more value than preparing seven days of food. This resembles an implementation intention — converting a broad intention into a response that is easier to execute in a particular situation. A meta analysis of 23 studies found that implementation intentions had a moderate effect on increasing healthier foods in the diet (Cohen's d = 0.51) and a smaller effect on reducing unhealthy eating ( d = 0.29). Ingredient prep was not itself the intervention studied, so those effect sizes should not be attributed to meal preparation . The useful connection is behavioural: arranging for a desired food to be readily available can be more actionable than relying on a general intention to make a good choice later. Explanatory illustration 2 Prep the bottleneck, not everything A common failure mode is turning meal prep into another demanding self improvement project. Two hours of Sunday chopping are not automatically a success because Tuesday's dinner takes ten minutes. The relevant calculation includes the preparation session, containers, washing up, refrigerator space and discarded food. That constraint is visible in wider research on cooking. UK time use data covering 6,143 people found that those doing more “foodwork” — work required to feed a household — allocated time differently across the rest of their day. Among women in particular, more foodwork was associated with less time for personal care, socialising and hobbies. The researchers explicitly warned that attempts to encourage more home food preparation should consider such unintended consequences. The more efficient question is therefore: what repeatedly makes dinner slow? If the answer is chopping, prepare vegetables. If it is waiting for grains or pulses, batch cook those. If handling and portioning the main ingredient creates the obstacle, organise that. If the actual problem is having no food in the house, ingredient prep is downstream of the real problem and will not solve it. Convenience foods can also be legitimate components of this system. Frozen vegetables, tinned pulses and other minimally demanding staples can eliminate preparation rather than merely moving it to another day. NHS guidance notes that fresh, frozen and tinned vegetables can all contribute towards fruit and vegetable intake. The goal is not to maximise the proportion of dinner"},{"title":"Minimum Prep","url":"/minimum-prep/","description":"Frozen vegetables, tinned pulses and pre-cooked staples can support a good diet when they make an acceptable meal easier to assemble.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Meal Prep","level":3,"text":"Minimum Prep Healthy Eating Without Cooking Everything Yourself Meal Prep Minimum Prep Minimum Prep Healthy Eating Without Cooking Everything Yourself Frozen vegetables, tinned pulses and pre cooked staples can support a good diet when they make an acceptable meal easier to assemble. Frozen vegetables, tinned pulses and pre cooked staples can support a good diet when they make an acceptable meal easier to assemble. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Why more home cooking is not always better Convenience foods that reduce preparation without opening the decision Building a minimum prep meal system that still covers dietary basics self improvement that works meal prep minimum prep Introduction Healthy eating does not require cooking every meal from basic ingredients. Frozen vegetables, tinned pulses, microwaveable wholegrains, plain yoghurt, tinned fish, prepared salads and similar foods can remove most of the chopping, cooking and washing up while still supplying the building blocks of a balanced diet. The useful distinction is not simply between “home made” and “convenient”, but between convenience foods that make nutritious foods easier to eat and products whose convenience comes with an unfavourable nutritional profile. Explanatory illustration 1 A particularly revealing analysis of UK adults found that some people maintained high quality diets despite obtaining relatively little energy from home prepared food. Their diets were not identical to those of people who prepared more food at home: they contained somewhat more sugar and sodium, but also more fruit and low fat dairy and less red meat. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Comparison of individuals with low versus high consumption of home prepared food in a group with universally high die... For a meal system designed to remove the daily food decision, that is an important finding. The target need not be maximum cooking. It can be minimum preparation consistent with a good overall diet . Why more home cooking is not always better There is a genuine association between home preparation and diet quality, but it is easy to turn that association into a stronger rule than the evidence supports. An analysis of 6,364 adults in the UK National Diet and Nutrition Survey for 2008–16 found that, on average, only 26.5% of participants' dietary energy came from food classified as home prepared. Each 10 percentage point increase in home prepared food was associated with 1.2 times the odds of being in the highest fifth for adherence to the Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) dietary pattern. Because the study was cross sectional, however, it could not establish that cooking itself caused the better diet. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Home prepared food, dietary quality and socio demographic factors: a cross sectional analysis of the UK National Diet... The more revealing comparison came from the same survey data. Researchers selected 1,063 adults who already had high quality diets and divided them according to their reliance on home prepared food. Of these, 442 combined high diet quality with low home preparation , while 621 combined high diet quality with high home preparation. After adjustment for demographic differences, the researchers found no significant difference between the groups in DASH diet quality score, reported energy intake or prevalence of obesity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Comparison of individuals with low versus high consumption of home prepared food in a group with universally high die... That comparison matters because it separates two questions that are often treated as one. “Do people who cook more tend to eat better?” and “Must someone cook a great deal to eat well?” are not equivalent. The survey evidence supports an association for the first proposition but contradicts the idea that extensive home preparation is a necessary condition for the second. The authors explicitly concluded that home food preparation should not be presented as a prerequisite for a high quality diet. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Comparison of individuals with low versus high consumption of home prepared food in a group with universally high die... Nor does “home made” guarantee a nutritionally superior meal. A 2012 BMJ study compared 100 supermarket ready meals with 100 main course recipes from five bestselling books by UK television chefs. Neither group met all the nutritional recommendations used by the researchers, but the chef recipes contained more energy, fat and saturated fat and less fibre per portion than the ready meals. Ready meals performed particularly poorly on sodium. The study was a comparison of selected products and recipes, not proof that ready meals are generally healthier than home cooking, but it demonstrates why preparation method is a poor nutritional shortcut: what the meal contains matters more than whether somebody cooked it from scratch . [BMJ] bmj.com Nutritional content of supermarket ready meals and recipes by television chefs in the United Kingdom: cross sectional study The BMJD... This is especially relevant when self improvement advice inadvertently makes healthy eating harder. Someone who believes a proper dinner requires peeling, chopping, cooking and cleaning may abandon the intended meal when time or energy is scarce. A person willing to microwave frozen vegetables, open a tin of chickpeas and heat a pre cooked grain has a much shorter route from intention to eating. Cooking for One or Two People: Let the Grocery Store Do the Work Channel: Alberta Health Services Open on YouTube Convenience foods can remove preparation without removing nutrition “Processed” and “convenient” cover foods with radically different nutritional characteristics. Freezing peas and manufacturing a confectionery bar both involve food processing, but treating them as nutritionally equivalent because neither is completely unprocessed is not useful. The NOVA processing classification itself places plain frozen fruit and vegetables, grains and pulses among unprocessed or minimally processed foods; tinned vegetables and legumes made with added salt fall into its processed category. More complex industrial formulations can fall into the ultra processed category. Whatever one thinks of using processing classifications to predict health, the categories illustrate a simple point: freezing, cooking, canning and packaging do not automatically turn a useful staple into nutritionally poor food . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The UN Decade of Nutrition, the NOVA food classification and the trouble with ultra processing PMCMarch 21, 2017... Published: March 21, 2017 Frozen vegetables are a particularly strong example. The NHS explicitly counts fresh, frozen and tinned fruit and vegetables towards the recommended five portions a day. An 80 g serving of fresh, tinned or frozen fruit or vegetables counts as a portion. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk Eating a balanced diet June 30, 2026... Published: June 30, 2026 Research comparing fresh, frozen and “fresh stored” produce has also challenged the assumption that fresh produce is invariably nutritionally superior: in a two year analysis, some nutrients declined during five days of refrigerated storage, and frozen produce sometimes compared favourably with the fresh stored equivalent. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Tinned pulses solve a different preparation problem. Dried chickpeas and beans can be inexpensive and nutritious, but they require soaking or substantial cooking time. A tin turns the same basic food category into something that can go directly into a salad, soup, grain bowl or microwave meal. Pulses provide protein and fibre and are specifically included among the protein foods recommended by the NHS. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk Eating a balanced diet June 30, 2026... Published: June 30, 2026 Where sodium is a concern, choosing lower salt versions helps; research on canned beans has also found that draining and then rinsing them significantly reduces sodium. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. The same principle extends beyond vegetables and beans. A useful minimum prep cupboard, fridge and freezer can contain foods whose processing has already performed the tedious step: Vegetables and fruit: frozen peas, spinach, broccoli, mixed vegetables or berries; tinned tomatoes and sweetcorn; prepared salad; fresh fruit that requires no preparation. Pulses and other proteins: tinned lentils, chickpeas and beans; eggs; plain yoghurt; tofu; unsalted nuts; tinned fish where appropriate. The NHS notes that tinned and smoked fish can be comparatively salty, so the label still matters. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk Eating a balanced diet June 30, 2026... Published: June 30, 2026 Starchy"},{"title":"Adaptive Prompts","url":"/adaptive-prompts/","description":"A reminder is more useful when it arrives in a workable moment than when it follows the same schedule regardless of context.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / No Magic Formula","level":3,"text":"Adaptive Prompts When Should a Behaviour Reminder Actually Arrive? No Magic Formula Adaptive Prompts Adaptive Prompts When Should a Behaviour Reminder Actually Arrive? A reminder is more useful when it arrives in a workable moment than when it follows the same schedule regardless of context. A reminder is more useful when it arrives in a workable moment than when it follows the same schedule regardless of context. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention Why fixed time prompts often miss the moment How context can guide whether and when to intervene Balancing timely support with interruption self improvement that works no magic formula adaptive prompts Introduction Adaptive reminders beat fixed daily prompts when the usefulness of a reminder depends strongly on what is happening at that moment . A prompt to walk, practise a skill or complete a reflection is more valuable when there is a genuine opportunity to act and less valuable when the person is already busy, driving, already doing the target behaviour or simply unable to respond. Just in time adaptive interventions, or JITAIs, formalise this idea by using changing information such as recent behaviour, time, location and apparent availability to decide whether, when and sometimes what to prompt. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMCSeptember 12, 2025... Published: September 12, 2025 Explanatory illustration 1 The important qualification is that adaptive is not automatically better. Research shows promising short term effects and clear evidence that receptivity varies with context, but comparisons do not establish a universal advantage for sophisticated context aware prompting. Sensors can misread situations, frequent notifications can become burdensome, and simple schedules sometimes work perfectly well. The practical advantage comes from selective intervention : prompting when there is both a reason to intervene and a workable opportunity to respond. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A systematic review of just in time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) to promote physical activity PMCApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 Why fixed time prompts often miss the moment A daily reminder at 7 pm has one considerable virtue: simplicity. It is predictable, easy to implement and requires almost no personal data. For behaviours naturally tied to a stable time — taking an evening measurement, completing a diary before bed or preparing tomorrow's lunch — that may be all that is needed. The weakness appears when the behaviour depends on circumstances that move around. \"Take a ten minute walk\" is not equally actionable at 3 pm every day. One day the recipient may be sitting at home after hours of inactivity; another day they may already be walking; on another they may be driving or in a meeting. A fixed schedule treats these very different states as equivalent. HeartSteps, a much studied physical activity JITAI, illustrates the alternative. Its activity suggestions were tailored using time of day , weekday versus weekend, location and weather. More importantly, the system could withhold a suggestion when the participant was judged unavailable — for example, while driving, already walking or running, or immediately after an activity bout. Across 7,540 assessed decision points in the trial, participants were classified as unavailable at 19.6% of them. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Efficacy of Contextually Tailored Suggestions for Physical Activity: A Micro randomized Optimization Trial of HeartSt... That figure captures something a fixed reminder cannot do: sometimes the best reminder is no reminder . The six week HeartSteps micro randomised trial repeatedly randomised 44 adults at potential intervention moments, allowing researchers to estimate the immediate effect of prompting rather than merely compare people who happened to use an app more or less often. Averaged across study days and suggestion types, sending a suggestion was associated with a 14% increase in steps over the following 30 minutes, although this overall comparison narrowly missed conventional statistical significance. Walking specific suggestions produced a statistically significant 24% increase, equivalent to 59 additional steps on average. Their effects were substantially stronger early in the intervention and declined with time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Efficacy of Contextually Tailored Suggestions for Physical Activity: A Micro randomized Optimization Trial of HeartSt... That declining effect is important for self improvement systems. A reminder policy should not assume that because a prompt helped on day three, sending the same kind of interruption indefinitely remains worthwhile. Micro randomised trials are particularly useful here because repeated randomisation can reveal whether an intervention's effect changes as someone gains experience with it. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Micro randomised trial Micro randomised trial Susan Murphy Experimental Design & ML in Mobile Health The Frontiers of Machine Learning Channel: National Academy of Sciences Open on YouTube How context can decide whether to intervene A useful adaptive reminder policy separates three questions that fixed schedules tend to collapse into one: Does the person currently need support? Is this a good opportunity for the target behaviour? And can the person realistically engage with this particular prompt? Recent JITAI research describes the last question as receptivity : the conditions under which somebody can effectively engage with a particular intervention. Receptivity is not simply \"the phone is available\". Someone might be perfectly capable of reading a notification but unable to perform what it asks. A walking suggestion during a meeting is therefore different from a one second informational notification during the same meeting. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMCSeptember 12, 2025... Published: September 12, 2025 Evidence suggests that these conditions really do vary within people. In a six week study of 189 participants receiving physical activity interventions through the Ally digital coach, researchers found significant associations between momentary receptivity and contextual factors including day and time, phone interaction, physical activity, location and battery status. Participant characteristics also mattered. Models attempting to detect receptivity improved substantially over a biased random classifier benchmark, although prediction was far from perfect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Exploring the State of Receptivity for m Health Interventions Exploring the State of Receptivity for mHealth Interventions PubMedSeptember 14, 2020... Published: September 14, 2020 For a practical behaviour system, this points towards a hierarchy of increasingly adaptive policies: 1. Fixed prompt: \"Remind me to walk at 3 pm.\" 2. Personalised schedule: \"Remind me during the afternoon period when I usually have a break.\" 3. Context filtered prompt: \"Consider reminding me then, but not if I am driving or already active.\" 4. Opportunity sensitive prompt: \"Prompt me when I have been inactive and there appears to be a workable opportunity for a short walk.\" 5. Learning policy: Adjust the decision rule over time according to which kinds of prompts actually lead this individual to act. The sophistication should earn its keep. The purpose is not to collect as much contextual data as possible; it is to distinguish moments in which intervention has different expected value. That distinction is supported by the broader evidence base, but with important uncertainty. A 2019 systematic review found 14 distinct physical activity JITAIs across 19 papers. They commonly triggered sedentary breaks or suggested activity at opportune moments, and users generally found them acceptable, but behavioural effects were mixed and none of the studies was adequately powered to establish effects conclusively. Technological reliability and getting messages there at genuinely timely moments were themselves recurring problems. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A systematic review of just in time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) to promote physical activity PMCApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 A larger 2024 systematic review covering 45 studies similarly found considerable variation in how JITAIs were built. Decision points ranged from every minute to once a day, while decision rules ranged from simple conditions involving one variable to more elaborate contextual algorithms. The reviewers judged frequent decision points, device measured tailoring variables supplemented by user input, preference tailored"},{"title":"Dynamic Fit","url":"/dynamic-fit/","description":"Useful personalization responds to changing circumstances rather than assigning a person to one fixed type and treating it as destiny.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / No Magic Formula","level":3,"text":"Dynamic Fit Personalization Works Better When It Can Change No Magic Formula Dynamic Fit Dynamic Fit Personalization Works Better When It Can Change Useful personalization responds to changing circumstances rather than assigning a person to one fixed type and treating it as destiny. Useful personalization responds to changing circumstances rather than assigning a person to one fixed type and treating it as destiny. Self Improvement That Works mechanism bounded topic mechanism Why static personality labels are a weak control system Which changing signals can guide support How adaptive personalization avoids rigid user types self improvement that works no magic formula dynamic fit Introduction Personalisation works best when it behaves less like a personality verdict and more like a responsive control system. A useful behaviour system can begin with what it knows about a person, but it should keep updating from what is happening now: recent behaviour, available time, current environment, progress, setbacks, receptivity and whether previous support actually helped. Research on adaptive interventions increasingly formalises exactly this idea. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions: Where Are We Now and What Is Next? PMCSeptember 12, 2025... Published: September 12, 2025 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. Saying “you are an introvert, so use this routine” turns an average tendency into a prescription. An adaptive system instead asks whether this particular person, in this particular situation, currently has the need and opportunity for a particular form of support. Personality and other relatively stable characteristics can still provide useful background information, but they should not become destiny. Behaviour varies substantially within the same person across situations and time, while adaptive intervention research shows how changing signals can be used to alter the timing, content or intensity of support. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)TraitEnactments as Density Distributions: The Role of Actors, Situations, and Observers in Explaining Stability and V... Why static personality labels are a weak control system A stable trait and a fixed behavioural type are not the same thing. Personality research finds meaningful, persistent differences between people, but it also finds extensive variation within the same person. Experience sampling studies have repeatedly observed people expressing different levels of trait like behaviour from one occasion to another. In one line of research, within person variation in trait enactment was so large that people differed from themselves across occasions more than people differed from one another; observer rated research also found substantial within person variability rather than the phenomenon being merely an artefact of self report. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)TraitEnactments as Density Distributions: The Role of Actors, Situations, and Observers in Explaining Stability and V... Situations are part of the explanation. Research on person by situation interactions shows that people's responses can differ markedly according to the circumstances they encounter. Modern state and trait models explicitly allow situational fluctuations rather than treating every observation as an uncomplicated expression of an enduring characteristic. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews A Theory of States and Traits—Revised Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsA Theory of States and Traits—Revised Annual ReviewsMarch 28, 2015... Published: March 28, 2015 This creates a practical problem for self improvement systems built around types. Suppose a questionnaire classifies someone as highly organised. That may be informative about their average tendencies, but it does not tell a system whether they can plan effectively after a poor night's sleep, whether an unusually busy week has overwhelmed their normal routine, or whether they already know exactly what to do and simply lack an opportunity to do it. The intervention decision concerns the person at this moment , not merely the person's average description. Categorical labels can make the problem worse by introducing artificial boundaries. A historical National Research Council review of the Myers Briggs Type Indicator, for example, noted that continuous scale reliability could be acceptable while stability of the resulting type classifications was much weaker: across 11 samples cited in the review, only 24–61% retained the same type, with a median of 40%. That is a particularly clear illustration of why converting dimensions into fixed boxes can discard useful information. [National Academies] nationalacademies.org Open source on nationalacademies.org. The alternative is not to pretend enduring differences do not exist. Traits, preferences, abilities, constraints and past behaviour can all be useful inputs. The stronger principle is narrower: do not confuse a useful prior with a permanent decision rule . A person's history can initialise personalisation; new evidence should be able to change it. Walter Mischel’s Personality Theory Simplified Marshmallow Experiment & CAPS Model Channel: Mister Simplify Open on YouTube Which changing signals can guide support Just in time adaptive interventions, usually shortened to JITAIs, provide a concrete model of how this can work. Rather than selecting one intervention package at the beginning and leaving it unchanged, a JITAI makes repeated decisions. Researchers describe four central ingredients: decision points at which support might be offered, intervention options that could be selected, tailoring variables describing the person's current situation, and decision rules connecting those signals to an action. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Healt... For a self improvement system, the useful signals fall into a few practical categories: Recent behaviour: Has the intended behaviour already happened? Is performance improving, flat or deteriorating? Did the person act after the previous prompt? Current state: Relevant signals might include reported stress, mood, fatigue, craving, confidence or motivation, depending on the behaviour and whether measuring them is justified. Opportunity: Is there actually time and a suitable setting to act? A walking suggestion during a free lunch break has a different value from the same suggestion while somebody is driving. Context: Time of day , day of week, location, weather or the immediate social environment can sometimes change what is feasible. Receptivity: Can and will the person engage with support now? Recent ignored prompts or excessive intervention frequency may indicate that another notification would add burden rather than value. Response history: Which forms of support have previously been followed by useful action for this person, in circumstances resembling the present ones? These signals need not all be collected, and collecting more data is not automatically better. A 2024 scoping review identified 62 JITAIs across behaviours including physical activity , diet, substance use and treatment adherence. More than half relied solely on self reported information for tailoring, while 13 used passive monitoring alone; importantly, 44% of studies did not clearly specify their data processing techniques. The field therefore demonstrates both the possibilities of dynamic personalisation and the distance still to travel before sophisticated sensing can automatically be equated with reliable personalisation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Personalized interventions for behaviour change: A scoping review of just‐in‐time adaptive interventions PMCNovembe... The key design question is not “How much can we know about this person?” but “What changing information would alter a useful decision?” If knowing someone's location cannot legitimately change the intervention, location data add privacy cost without necessarily adding behavioural value. Who are you, really? The puzzle of personality Brian Little TED Channel: TED Open on YouTube A walking prompt shows what dynamic fit means HeartSteps, an experimental mobile physical activity intervention, provides a useful concrete example. Its activity suggestions were tailored using factors including location, weather, time and day. Crucially, the system also considered whether someone was available for an intervention. Participants were treated as unavailable when, for example, they were already walking or running or were driving. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is a different philosophy from demographic or personality matching. The useful question is not “What sort of person"},{"title":"Feedback Fit","url":"/feedback-fit/","description":"Feedback can improve follow-through, but its value depends on timing, format, behaviour and whether the person can act on it.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / No Magic Formula","level":3,"text":"Feedback Fit When Does Feedback Help Instead of Distract? No Magic Formula Feedback Fit Feedback Fit When Does Feedback Help Instead of Distract? Feedback can improve follow through, but its value depends on timing, format, behaviour and whether the person can act on it. Feedback can improve follow through, but its value depends on timing, format, behaviour and whether the person can act on it. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism What feedback adds to self monitoring Why no single feedback format wins everywhere How to test whether feedback is changing behaviour self improvement that works no magic formula feedback fit Introduction Feedback helps self improvement when it makes a useful gap visible and helps a person decide what to do next. But that does not mean that more feedback, more often, is automatically better. Across research on goal pursuit, health behaviour, learning and performance, the recurring finding is conditional effectiveness : feedback works differently depending on what it measures, when it arrives, where it directs attention and whether the recipient can translate it into action. [nih.gov] nih.gov Pub Med Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedDoes monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for personalised behaviour systems. A step counter, weekly review, coach, progress graph or app notification is not valuable merely because it produces information. The useful question is whether the feedback changes the next relevant behaviour. Sometimes a simple signal is enough. Sometimes detailed guidance helps. Sometimes additional feedback merely repeats what the person already knows, arrives too late to be useful or pulls attention away from the task itself. The evidence therefore supports designing feedback around fit and actionability rather than maximum volume . What feedback adds to self monitoring Self monitoring and feedback perform related but different jobs. Monitoring tells you what happened: how many workouts you completed, how long you studied or whether you kept to a planned behaviour. Feedback interprets that information against some standard, goal or previous performance. In a useful feedback loop, the comparison exposes a discrepancy that can influence the next action . There is strong evidence for the first part of that loop. A meta analysis of 138 studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase monitoring of goal progress substantially increased monitoring itself and produced a moderate improvement in goal attainment. The effect on attainment was larger when progress was physically recorded and when outcomes were reported or made public. Crucially, increased monitoring statistically mediated the improvement in goal attainment. [PubMed] nih.gov Pub Med Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedDoes monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016 Adding feedback can provide further value, but the evidence is more qualified. A 2024 systematic review of feedback added to self monitoring in diet, physical activity and weight interventions identified 19 studies involving 3,261 participants. For physical activity, feedback produced a small additional benefit over self monitoring alone, with a standardised effect size of 0.29. Yet results concerning different ways of generating and presenting feedback were mixed, and heterogeneity prevented comparable meta analyses for many of the other questions. Many included studies also had a high risk of bias. [DOI] doi.org Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and weight: a systemat... That combination of findings is more useful than either “feedback works” or “feedback is overrated”. Making progress visible is a well supported behaviour change mechanism; adding interpretation can help, but the design of that interpretation matters. Consider a person trying to walk more. A daily step count already answers one useful question: what did I do? A message saying “6,800 steps” adds little if the person can already see that number. “You are 1,200 steps short of today's target” makes the discrepancy clearer. “A 12 minute walk after lunch would close most of that gap” goes further by connecting the discrepancy to a feasible response. These are all technically forms of information about performance, but they impose very different demands on the recipient. Research on just in time diet and physical activity interventions illustrates the distinction. A systematic review of 31 studies found that almost all used personalised feedback and most linked feedback to goals, but only five supplied what the researchers classified as actionable feedback — explicit guidance about when, where or how to perform the desired behaviour. Among the relatively small subset of studies that directly tested behavioural efficacy, successful interventions commonly featured continuously available, goal oriented or actionable feedback. The review could not establish a universal winning combination, but it highlighted a practical weakness of many systems: they describe performance without necessarily helping the person alter it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Why Feedback Feels Threatening (And How to Respond Calmly) Channel: Sharon McLaughlin MD Open on YouTube Why no single feedback format wins everywhere The strongest warning against a “more is better” approach comes from the broader performance literature. In a landmark meta analysis covering 607 effect sizes and 23,663 observations, Avraham Kluger and Angelo DeNisi found that feedback interventions improved performance on average, with an effect size of 0.41. Yet more than one third of the feedback interventions reduced performance . [DOI] doi.org The effects of feedback interventions on performance: A historical review, a meta analysis, and a preliminary feedback intervention th... Their Feedback Intervention Theory proposed an important mechanism for this variation: feedback changes where attention goes. Feedback focused on the task can help someone learn what needs correcting. Feedback that pushes attention towards the self — one's competence, identity or feelings about being evaluated — can divert cognitive resources from doing the task. In their analysis, feedback became less effective as attention moved away from task processes and towards self related processes. [DOI] doi.org The effects of feedback interventions on performance: A historical review, a meta analysis, and a preliminary feedback intervention th... That helps explain why feedback should not be treated as a substance for which a larger dose must produce a larger benefit. Its effect depends partly on what the message makes salient . “Your last three practice attempts missed because you started too quickly” supplies task relevant information. A judgement that mainly communicates “you are doing badly” may create a discrepancy without providing a useful route for closing it. Frequency is not a universal dial It is tempting to solve feedback design by choosing an ideal frequency: instantaneous, daily, weekly or after every attempt. Research does not justify such a universal rule. Motor learning research provides a particularly clear caution. One influential idea held that giving feedback after every practice attempt might improve performance during practice while creating dependence that harms later learning. A 2022 meta analysis of 61 papers, however, found no significant overall advantage for reduced feedback frequency and substantial unexplained heterogeneity. The researchers concluded that robust evidence for a general reduced frequency principle was lacking. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Meta analysis of the reduced relative feedback frequency effect on motor learning and performance ScienceDirectMeta analysis of the reduced relative feedback frequency effect on motor learning and performance ScienceDirect Other tasks can favour very frequent feedback. A systematic review and meta analysis of resistance training, for example, found beneficial effects of feedback on several acute performance measures and reported that higher frequency feedback, including feedback after each repetition, could benefit immediate performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The apparent contradiction is exactly the point. The right frequency depends on what someone is trying to accomplish. Immediate information about bar velocity during a strength training session has a different function from repeated notifications about a long term lifestyle goal."},{"title":"Habit Timing","url":"/habit-timing-557839/","description":"Habit formation follows a familiar pattern, but the time needed for automaticity can vary dramatically from person to person.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / No Magic Formula","level":3,"text":"Habit Timing Why There Is No Universal Habit Timeline No Magic Formula Habit Timing Habit Timing Why There Is No Universal Habit Timeline Habit formation follows a familiar pattern, but the time needed for automaticity can vary dramatically from person to person. Habit formation follows a familiar pattern, but the time needed for automaticity can vary dramatically from person to person. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the 12 week habit study actually measured Why automaticity develops at different speeds How to use habit timelines without chasing magic numbers self improvement that works no magic formula habit timing Introduction There is no scientifically established number of days after which a new behaviour suddenly becomes a habit. The famous 18 to 254 day range comes from a real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, who followed people attempting to establish everyday eating, drinking and activity behaviours. Their central finding was not that everyone should aim for 66 days, or even that every habit must fall between 18 and 254 days. It was that habit automaticity developed at markedly different speeds in different people and behaviours . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. A fixed deadline can turn normal variation into apparent failure: if a behaviour still requires effort after three weeks, or even three months, that does not by itself mean the strategy has failed. The more useful question is whether repeated behaviour in a sufficiently consistent context is becoming progressively easier and more automatic. How to stop failing at forming new habits Rob Stein TEDxWestminster Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube What the 12 week study actually measured The study behind the numbers, How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world , recruited 96 volunteers . Each chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted to perform it every day in the same context for 12 weeks — for example, doing something after breakfast. Participants recorded whether they had performed the behaviour and completed daily measures of its automaticity . Eighty two supplied enough data to be included in the main analysis. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This is important because the researchers were not simply counting consecutive successful days and declaring a habit complete. They were interested in automaticity : the extent to which the behaviour came to occur with less conscious deliberation. In contemporary habit research, habitual behaviour is generally understood as behaviour that becomes automatically activated by associated contextual cues through learning and repetition. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com What is habit and how can it be used to change real‐world behaviour? Narrowing the theory‐reality gap Gardner 2024 Social an... The researchers modelled each participant's changing automaticity using an asymptotic curve . In plain English, this means they expected early repetitions to produce relatively large gains in automaticity, followed by progressively smaller gains as the person approached a plateau. Later research continues to describe this as a useful model of habit development: repetition does not necessarily add the same amount of habit strength every day. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Brit... For participants whose data could be modelled this way, the researchers estimated when each person reached 95% of their eventual automaticity plateau . The resulting estimates ranged from 18 to 254 days , with a central estimate of about 66 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That makes “66 days to form a habit” a misleading simplification. Lally herself has subsequently stressed that the figure should not be interpreted as a universal deadline. [University of Surrey] surrey.ac.uk University of SurreyDoes it really take 66 days to form a habit? We asked the expert, Dr Pippa Lally University of SurreyJanuary 21, 2026... Published: January 21, 2026 There is another easily missed limitation. The study ran for only 84 days, yet some fitted curves projected that participants would need considerably longer than that to reach 95% of their plateau. In other words, 254 days was a modelled estimate rather than 254 days of directly observed habit formation for that individual . Moreover, the asymptotic model did not adequately represent everyone: later methodological discussion notes poor model fit for 43 of the 82 participants with sufficient data. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Brit... So the study is better understood as evidence for a variable process than as a stopwatch for habits. PAN Podcast 6 Dr. Phillippa Lally Habits and Health Channel: PAN Physicians Association for Nutrition Open on YouTube Why automaticity develops at different speeds The enormous gap between 18 and 254 days makes more sense once habit formation is treated as learning rather than calendar accumulation. What matters is not simply that another midnight has passed. The person has another opportunity to perform a particular response in the presence of a sufficiently consistent cue, strengthening the association between that situation and the behaviour. The original study found that consistent performance was associated with better fitting habit formation curves . It also found something practically reassuring: missing one opportunity did not materially derail the automaticity building process. Habit formation therefore appears more forgiving than “never break the chain” advice implies. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Several factors help explain why the number of calendar days can differ so much. The behaviour itself matters. Drinking a glass of water is a relatively simple action; an exercise routine demands more steps, time and effort. A later clinical explanation of the original findings notes that automaticity peaked more quickly for simple actions such as drinking water than for more elaborate routines. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The context matters. Habit learning depends on associations between situations and responses, so repetition under stable circumstances should be different from performing the same nominal behaviour amid constantly changing cues. A 2022 study tested this more directly and found, across both an experimental student sample and observational app data, that greater context stability predicted greater automaticity as well as better attainment of repetition goals. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. People matter too. Individuals differ in what they choose, how consistently they perform it and how their daily environments support repetition. A 2024 systematic review found that habit development was associated with factors including behaviour type, frequency, timing, personal choice and behavioural regulation. The evidence base remains relatively small, however, so these should not be converted into another rigid formula. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Later studies reinforce the central point rather than narrowing everything to 66 days. The 2024 systematic review found four studies reporting time to habit estimates. Median estimates included 59 and 66 days , while reported means in other studies ranged from 106 to 154 days . Across the evidence reviewed, individual estimates extended from 4 to 335 days . The authors also cautioned that much of the underlying literature had a high risk of bias and that comparatively few studies directly estimated formation time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The 18 to 254 day result should therefore not be interpreted as defining the minimum and maximum possible human habit formation times . It is the observed/modelled range from one influential dataset , not a biological boundary. Explanatory illustration 2 The curve matters more than the deadline The most useful part of the Lally study may be the shape of the learning curve rather than any"},{"title":"Sitting Trackers","url":"/sitting-trackers/","description":"Trials of sedentary-behaviour programmes found clearer short-term effects when monitoring used objective tools and targeted sitting directly.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Track Progress","level":3,"text":"Sitting Trackers Do Objective Trackers Help People Sit Less? Track Progress Sitting Trackers Sitting Trackers Do Objective Trackers Help People Sit Less? Trials of sedentary behaviour programmes found clearer short term effects when monitoring used objective tools and targeted sitting directly. Trials of sedentary behaviour programmes found clearer short term effects when monitoring used objective tools and targeted sitting directly. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence What the sedentary behaviour meta analysis found Objective monitoring versus less direct tracking Why matching the tracker to the target behaviour matters self improvement that works track progress sitting trackers Introduction Objective trackers appear to help people sit less, at least in the short term, and the most revealing evidence comes from trials that treated sitting itself as the behaviour to change. A 2019 systematic review and meta analysis of adult interventions found that programmes incorporating self monitoring reduced total sedentary time by roughly 34 minutes a day more than control conditions . More importantly for progress tracking, the benefit was concentrated in studies using objective monitoring devices rather than subjective self recording. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction makes sedentary behaviour a useful test case for self improvement. Sitting is repetitive, easily overlooked and surprisingly difficult to reconstruct from memory. A sensor can make an otherwise largely automatic behaviour visible while there is still time to change it. But the evidence does not show that simply wearing any activity tracker reliably solves the problem. The clearest results came when the measurement matched the target: track sitting if the goal is to sit less . What the sedentary behaviour meta analysis found The key dataset is Sofie Compernolle and colleagues' 2019 review in the International Journal of Behavioral Nutrition and Physical Activity . The researchers examined interventions in adults that deliberately used self monitoring to reduce sedentary behaviour. Nineteen intervention studies involving 2,800 participants met the review's eligibility criteria. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Across the relevant studies, self monitoring interventions produced a statistically significant reduction in total sedentary time, with a standardised effect size of Hedges' g = 0.32 (95% confidence interval 0.14 to 0.50). Expressed in a more intuitive unit, the intervention groups reduced their total sedentary time by an average of 34.37 minutes per day more than control groups . For occupational sedentary time, the pooled standardised effect was larger, g = 0.56 , although its confidence interval was considerably wider. The interventions did not produce a significant overall effect on the number of breaks in sedentary behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The headline result is useful, but the subgroup comparisons are more informative for someone deciding how to track progress . The researchers tested whether results differed according to factors including intervention duration, age, health status, the purpose of the monitoring tool and whether monitoring was subjective or objective. The method of self monitoring was the only statistically significant moderator of total sedentary time effects. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Interventions using objective self monitoring devices produced a significant effect of g = 0.40 (95% CI 0.19 to 0.60). Interventions relying on subjective monitoring produced essentially no detectable effect: g = −0.02 (95% CI −0.29 to 0.26). The difference between these subgroups was itself statistically significant. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is a much more specific finding than saying that \"tracking works\". In these trials, automatically measured behavioural feedback was associated with clearer short term reductions in sitting than asking people to monitor the behaviour subjectively . There was a second revealing comparison. Programmes focused only on sedentary behaviour had a significant effect of g = 0.45 (95% CI 0.15 to 0.75), whereas programmes simultaneously targeting sedentary behaviour and physical activity had a smaller effect that did not reach statistical significance. The difference between those categories was suggestive rather than conclusive—the moderator test had a p value of 0.09—but it points in the same practical direction: tightly defining the behaviour may make feedback easier to act upon. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The Best Accelerometers and Activity Trackers for Research in 2025 📊🔬 Video Channel: Physical Activity Researcher Podcast Open on YouTube Objective monitoring versus less direct tracking \"Objective tracker\" did not mean one standardised gadget. The interventions in the review used a varied collection of technologies, including the Gruve, Lumoback, Jawbone Up24, Fitbit One, Garmin Vivofit 2, SitFIT, a Shimmer accelerometer paired with a smartphone application and a sensor equipped Darma cushion . Other studies used pedometers, while subjective approaches included logbooks and questionnaires. Intervention periods ranged from one week to one year. [Springer] link.springer.com Effectiveness of interventions using self monitoring to reduce sedentary behavior in adults: a systematic review and meta analysi... The important distinction is therefore not a particular brand. It is the difference between requiring someone to estimate and record their behaviour and having a device continuously detect relevant movement or posture and turn it into feedback. That matters unusually strongly for sitting. A person can probably remember whether they went for a run this morning. Reconstructing every period spent sitting across an entire working day is another problem. A large systematic review comparing self report with device measurement illustrates the scale of that difficulty. Across 123 studies and more than 55,000 participants, self report measures underestimated overall sedentary time by an average of about 105 minutes per day compared with device measures. Results varied substantially between instruments, and better designed diaries and questionnaires performed better than simple single item estimates, but recall was clearly an imperfect substitute for direct measurement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A comparison of self reported and device measured sedentary behaviour in adults: a systematic review and meta analysis PubMedMarc... That does not prove that measurement accuracy caused the stronger behavioural effects in Compernolle's meta analysis. It does, however, provide a plausible explanation. Objective monitoring can remove some of the memory and estimation burden from the feedback loop. Instead of asking, \"How much do I think I sat today?\", the user can respond to a recorded pattern of sitting as it accumulates. Some interventions made that feedback particularly immediate. The SitFIT feasibility trial, for example, tested a pocket worn monitor that gave sedentary men real time information about either sedentary or upright time alongside their step counts. Participants generally regarded it as practical and motivating. The study was small—only 40 men—and was designed for feasibility rather than a definitive test of effectiveness, so its modest changes should not be overinterpreted. It nevertheless demonstrates what behaviour specific monitoring looks like in practice: the tracker exposes the behaviour during everyday life rather than asking for a retrospective estimate. [BMJ Open Seminars] bmjopensem.bmj.com BMJ Open SeminarsFeasibility of a real time self monitoring device for sitting less and moving more: a randomised controlled trial BMJ... Another example comes from the SMArT Work workplace intervention. Participants received baseline feedback from a thigh worn activPAL monitor showing sitting, prolonged sitting, standing and stepping. They then set goals specifically around sitting less. A sensor equipped cushion could provide continuing sitting time feedback and vibrate after a user selected period of prolonged sitting. At 12 months, the intervention group sat 83 minutes less during work hours per day than the control group. [BMJ] bmj.com Effectiveness of the Stand More AT (SMArT) Work intervention: cluster randomised controlled trial The BMJOctober 10, 2018... Published: October 10, 2018 That trial should not be read as evidence that the tracker alone caused an 83 minute reduction. SMArT Work was deliberately multicomponent: it also included education, goal setting, action planning, coaching and changes to the workplace, including height adjustable desks or desk platforms. It is better"},{"title":"Sleep Debt","url":"/sleep-debt-7a1eaf/","description":"Several nights of restricted sleep can steadily worsen attention even when each individual night feels manageable.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep Foundation","level":3,"text":"Sleep Debt Why Repeated Short Nights Quietly Erode Attention Sleep Foundation Sleep Debt Sleep Debt Why Repeated Short Nights Quietly Erode Attention Several nights of restricted sleep can steadily worsen attention even when each individual night feels manageable. Several nights of restricted sleep can steadily worsen attention even when each individual night feels manageable. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case event time window How attention deficits accumulate across restricted nights What the 14 day sleep restriction experiment found Why extra waking hours can become a false productivity gain self improvement that works sleep foundation sleep debt Introduction Repeated short nights can quietly erode attention even when no single night feels disastrous. The important variable is not simply how tired someone feels the next morning, but what happens when a modest sleep shortfall is repeated. Controlled experiments show that restricting sleep night after night can produce progressively more failures of sustained attention, slower responses and less stable performance. Crucially, subjective sleepiness does not always worsen at the same rate, so feeling as though you have “got used to” a short sleep schedule is not reliable evidence that your attention has adapted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Explanatory illustration 1 For self improvement, that distinction matters. Taking an hour or two from sleep can appear to create more time for work, study or personal goals. But if the same trade is made repeatedly, some of those extra waking hours may be bought at the cost of progressively less dependable attention during the hours that remain. The classic 14 day sleep restriction experiment makes this accumulation unusually clear. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... How attention deficits accumulate across restricted nights Sleep restriction does not have to mean staying awake all night. In laboratory research, it usually means giving participants a shorter nightly sleep opportunity than they would normally receive and repeating that schedule for several days. This is closer to an ordinary pattern such as staying up late to finish work and still getting up at the usual time. One of the most sensitive measures of the resulting impairment is the psychomotor vigilance test (PVT). Participants watch for unpredictable signals and respond as quickly as possible. The task is deliberately simple: there is little to learn and few complicated decisions to make. What it exposes is whether someone can maintain stable attention without periodically drifting into abnormally slow responses or lapses. Under repeated sleep restriction, those lapses tend to become more frequent. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMCAugust 15, 2007... Published: August 15, 2007 This makes chronic restriction different from judging the consequences of one mediocre night. A 2024 systematic review of 44 studies found that even a single restricted night impaired sustained attention on average, increasing both reaction times and attentional lapses, although effects on several other cognitive domains were not statistically significant. Repeated restriction adds another dimension: impairment can continue accumulating as successive short nights are added. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis PubMedApril... A separate seven day dose response experiment illustrates the same pattern. Sixty six healthy adults were assigned sleep opportunities of three, five, seven or nine hours a night. PVT performance deteriorated most strongly under severe restriction. With five hours in bed, attentional lapses increased across the restriction period; seven hours produced a smaller impairment in response speed, while the nine hour group remained around baseline. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The precise trajectory therefore depends on the amount of sleep, the measure used and the individual. It should not be reduced to a claim that everyone loses a fixed amount of attention for every hour of missed sleep. There are large, repeatable differences in vulnerability between people. Research involving hundreds of healthy adults has found substantial individual variation in how vigilant attention deteriorates during chronic restriction, and common characteristics such as personality, intelligence, chronotype and several demographic measures have done little to predict who will be most vulnerable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That variability does not overturn the central result. It means that the rate of deterioration differs. Repeatedly reducing sleep still creates a risk that attention is worsening across days, and a person cannot safely assume from ordinary self observation that they belong to a particularly resilient group. Fatigue as a challenge in 24/7 operations how to survive and thrive Channel: University of South Australia Open on YouTube What the 14 day experiment found The clearest single case comes from Hans Van Dongen and colleagues' 2003 laboratory experiment. Forty eight healthy adults aged 21–38 took part in tightly controlled protocols. In the chronic restriction experiment, participants were assigned four, six or eight hours of time in bed each night for 14 consecutive days . The researchers repeatedly measured attention and other aspects of cognitive performance and compared the results with a separate experiment involving total sleep deprivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The difference between the groups grew with time. Participants given eight hour sleep opportunities remained comparatively stable, while those restricted to four or six hours developed cumulative, dose dependent performance deficits. In other words, six hours was not simply associated with one initial drop followed by complete adaptation. Cognitive impairment continued to build over the two weeks. Four hour nights produced still greater deterioration. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The comparison with all night sleep deprivation made the size of that accumulation easier to grasp. By the end of the experiment, repeated restriction to six hours or less produced cognitive performance deficits that, depending on the measure and condition, reached levels comparable with as much as one to two nights of total sleep deprivation. This does not mean that six hours of sleep is equivalent to pulling an all nighter on any particular morning. The finding concerns deficits accumulated over many consecutive restricted nights. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The PVT results were particularly revealing. The researchers' modelling found that behavioural alertness lapses were approximately linearly related to cumulative wakefulness beyond about 15.8 hours per day. Their interpretation was that sleep debt could be understood partly as an accumulating cost of additional wakefulness: repeatedly extending the waking day without enough compensating sleep progressively increased vulnerability to attentional failure. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Later experimental work has strengthened the conclusion that short sleep itself matters, rather than the phenomenon being merely a consequence of staying awake for exceptionally long stretches. In a 32 day inpatient study designed to keep individual waking episodes below 16 hours, chronic short sleep still substantially worsened reaction time performance and increased attention lapses . Participants' self reported alertness, meanwhile, did not deteriorate uniformly alongside objective performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Chronic sleep curtailment, even without extended ( 16 h) wakefulness, degrades human vigilance performance PubMed... Episode 175: Hans Van Dongen on how fatigue and sleep loss lead to cognitive deficits Channel: TheIHMC Open on YouTube The dangerous gap between feeling adapted and performing adapted Perhaps the most practically important part of the 14 day study was the mismatch between"},{"title":"Attention Lapses","url":"/attention-lapses/","description":"Sleep loss often makes performance unstable, producing brief lapses and unusually slow responses rather than uniform slowing.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep Foundation","level":3,"text":"Attention Lapses Why Tired Brains Fail in Brief, Costly Lapses Sleep Foundation Attention Lapses Attention Lapses Why Tired Brains Fail in Brief, Costly Lapses Sleep loss often makes performance unstable, producing brief lapses and unusually slow responses rather than uniform slowing. Sleep loss often makes performance unstable, producing brief lapses and unusually slow responses rather than uniform slowing. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism What unstable attention looks like under sleep loss Why vigilance tasks reveal lapses so clearly How brief lapses disrupt studying, work and routine transitions self improvement that works sleep foundation attention lapses Introduction Sleep loss does not simply make attention uniformly slower. Its more distinctive effect is to make attention unstable . A tired person can respond normally for many moments, then suddenly produce an unusually slow response, miss an obvious signal or briefly lose the thread of what they are doing. Sustained attention — the ability to keep responding reliably over time — is therefore one of the cognitive abilities most consistently disrupted by insufficient sleep. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement because consistency , not peak capability, often determines whether intentions become actions. Someone may still be perfectly capable of understanding a lecture, writing good code or completing an administrative task after poor sleep. The problem is that this capability becomes less dependable from one moment to the next. Experiments describe this pattern as state instability : normal or near normal performance becomes increasingly interspersed with brief failures as pressure for sleep competes with the effort to remain awake and engaged. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained attention performance during sleep deprivation: evidence of state instability PubMed... What unstable attention looks like under sleep loss The clearest signature of sleep loss is found not just in average reaction time but in the shape of people's responses. When well rested, healthy young adults performing a vigilance task tend to produce a relatively concentrated distribution of reaction times. After sleep loss, that distribution develops a long tail: the slowest responses become dramatically slower and lapses become more numerous, while many of the fastest and ordinary responses remain comparatively intact. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 This means that describing a tired person as simply “20% slower”, for example, would miss much of what is happening. They may be nearly as quick as usual on one trial and exceptionally slow on the next. In a classic experiment that repeatedly tested people during 88 hours without sleep, both average reaction time and within person variability increased markedly. Participants continued to produce timely responses, but these were increasingly mixed with omissions and premature responses. The researchers described the resulting condition as an unstable state that could fluctuate over seconds. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained attention performance during sleep deprivation: evidence of state instability PubMed... The phenomenon is not restricted to extraordinary periods without sleep. In the influential Van Dongen laboratory experiment, healthy adults given either four, six or eight hours in bed per night for 14 days showed a dose dependent pattern. Attention lapses accumulated progressively in the four and six hour conditions, whereas they remained comparatively uncommon with eight hours in bed. Crucially, subjective sleepiness did not deteriorate in parallel with objective vigilance performance. People could therefore become progressively worse at maintaining attention without feeling proportionately worse. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... There is an important qualification. Very mild restriction does not inevitably produce a measurable vigilance deficit in every experiment. A randomised study of 93 healthy young adults that removed one hour from habitual sleep for six nights found an effect on working memory capacity but not on its sustained attention measure. Sleep loss exists on a continuum, and the detectable effect depends on its severity, duration, timing, the task used and individual susceptibility. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cumulative mild partial sleep deprivation negatively impacts working memory capacity but not sustained attention, response inhibiti... Study uncovers how mental fatigue causes the brain to zone out Channel: Medical Dialogues Open on YouTube Why vigilance tasks expose lapses so clearly Much of what researchers know about this instability comes from the psychomotor vigilance test (PVT) . The standard version lasts about ten minutes. A visual stimulus appears after unpredictable intervals, and the participant responds as quickly as possible. Traditionally, a reaction taking more than 500 milliseconds is counted as an attentional lapse. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 The task sounds almost absurdly easy, and that is part of its value. It demands little specialist knowledge, has little learning effect and gives people few strategic tricks with which to disguise deteriorating alertness. The challenge is simply to remain ready for the next unpredictable signal. Sleep loss reliably produces slower responses, more lapses, more false starts and a larger time on task effect — performance becomes less reliable as the test continues. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Maximizing Sensitivity of the Psychomotor Vigilance Test (PVT) to Sleep Loss PMCMay 1, 2011... Published: May 1, 2011 This helps separate sleep related vigilance failure from a broader claim that a tired brain becomes equally bad at everything. It does not. Some complex tasks permit people to compensate temporarily through strategy, motivation, extra effort or prior skill. The repetitive PVT strips much of that away and exposes whether attention can remain continuously available. Reviews consequently describe vigilant attention as especially sensitive to sleep deprivation. [Nature] nature.com Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review NeuropsychopharmacologyJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Nor are PVT lapses merely an artefact of staring at a screen until the eyes close. Experiments comparing visual and auditory versions found broadly similar sleep loss effects in both modalities, including increased variability, slowing and lapses. The findings indicate that the problem extends beyond visual distraction or eyelid closure to a more general instability in maintaining responsiveness. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is another reason vigilance tests are revealing: sleep pressure and time on task can compound one another. Maintaining attention becomes harder during a prolonged task even when rested, but sleep deprivation magnifies that vigilance decrement. A person can therefore begin a monotonous activity reasonably well and become substantially less dependable as it continues. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PubMed... What happens during a lapse The older “lapse hypothesis” treated these failures as brief reductions in arousal. Later work produced a more detailed picture. Sleep deprivation increases the physiological pressure for sleep, but wake promoting circadian signals and deliberate effort are still operating. Performance can consequently oscillate as these influences compete. The result is not necessarily a clean switch from awake to asleep; it is unstable wakefulness in which successful responses and failures alternate. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep deprivation, vigilant attention, and brain function: a review PMCJune 8, 2019... Published: June 8, 2019 Brain imaging and electrophysiological studies suggest that this instability can reach surprisingly local levels. In one unusual experiment, researchers recorded individual neurons through intracranial electrodes in 12 patients undergoing monitoring for epilepsy surgery. Participants performed a visual categorisation vigilance task before and after sleep deprivation."},{"title":"Effort Cost","url":"/effort-cost-ff323c/","description":"Insufficient sleep can make demanding tasks feel more effortful and reduce willingness to keep investing effort toward a goal.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep Foundation","level":3,"text":"Effort Cost Why Hard Tasks Feel Harder After Too Little Sleep Sleep Foundation Effort Cost Effort Cost Why Hard Tasks Feel Harder After Too Little Sleep Insufficient sleep can make demanding tasks feel more effortful and reduce willingness to keep investing effort toward a goal. Insufficient sleep can make demanding tasks feel more effortful and reduce willingness to keep investing effort toward a goal. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism How sleep loss changes perceived effort When incentives can partly compensate What this means for demanding self improvement plans self improvement that works sleep foundation effort cost Introduction Too little sleep can change a difficult task in two ways at once: it can reduce the cognitive capacity available to do the task, and it can make spending that capacity feel less worthwhile. Laboratory studies suggest that sleep deprived people become more reluctant to choose cognitively demanding work, discount rewards more sharply when those rewards require sustained effort, and experience attentional effort as more costly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. A person may still possess the underlying skill to study, write, plan or solve a difficult problem, yet be less willing to keep paying the mental cost required to use it. The result can look like a motivation problem: choosing the easier task, abandoning a demanding session early or needing a much stronger incentive to persist. Rewards can sometimes draw out extra effort temporarily, but the evidence does not support treating motivation as a complete substitute for sleep. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance PubMedApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 What is Cognitive Effort? – Andrew Westbrook (Striving & Doing Workshop, April 2021) Channel: Effort Project Neuchâtel Open on YouTube How sleep loss changes perceived effort One useful way to understand motivation is as a cost benefit decision. A demanding action offers some expected benefit — finishing an assignment, earning money, making progress towards a goal — but it also carries an effort cost. As that cost rises, the benefit has to become more valuable before the action is worth choosing. Researchers call this effort discounting : rewards become subjectively less attractive when obtaining them requires more work. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Sleep Deprivation Alters Effort Discounting but not Delay Discounting of Monetary Rewards PMCJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 Sleep deprivation appears to shift this calculation against cognitively demanding options. In a 2013 Sleep study, researchers compared choices after normal sleep with choices after a night without sleep. Participants could obtain different monetary rewards by completing different amounts of effortful work, such as typing letter strings backwards. After sleep deprivation, participants showed greater effort discounting: they became more inclined to sacrifice some reward in exchange for doing less work. Importantly, the same experiments found no corresponding change in delay discounting — the preference for a smaller reward now rather than a larger reward later. The result therefore did not look like an indiscriminate change in every kind of reward based choice. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation alters effort discounting but not delay discounting of monetary rewards PubMedJune 1, 2013... Published: June 1, 2013 A later experiment examined the mechanism using sustained attention. Participants performed a vigilance task after normal sleep and after sleep deprivation, with different payments available for fast responses. They then made choices involving rewards for maintaining attentional performance for periods ranging from one to 30 minutes. Longer tasks naturally reduced the attractiveness of the reward, but sleep deprivation made that duration related discounting stronger. Performance and pupil measurements also shifted with incentives, leading the researchers to interpret sleep deprivation as increasing the perceived cost of sustained attentional effort rather than simply switching motivation off altogether. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep deprivation increases the costs of attentional effort: Performance, preference and pupil size PubMed... This produces a more useful explanation than saying that tired people simply become “lazy”. The evidence points to an interaction between capacity and allocation . Sleep loss reduces the resources available for reliable cognitive performance, while effort allocation processes determine how much of those scarcer resources a person is prepared to mobilise for the task in front of them. A major review of this literature argues that sleep related performance deterioration can therefore contain both an involuntary capacity limitation and a motivational component in which people withdraw effort as its subjective cost rises. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance PubMedApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 There is also evidence that people can feel themselves compensating without fully restoring performance. In a study of 15 healthy young men undergoing five nights with five hours in bed, subjective effort increased during sleep restriction while alertness and motivation generally fell. Participants who suffered larger drops in motivation tended to show worse aspects of sustained attention performance. Yet reported effort itself was not a straightforward indicator of preserved performance: feeling that one is trying harder does not mean sleep related impairment has disappeared. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Changes in Subjective Motivation and Effort During Sleep Restriction Moderate Interindividual Differences in Attentio... Why Bad Sleep Makes You Spend More, Save Less, and Risk Everything Channel: VOID MIND Open on YouTube The effect may be especially cognitive The claim should not be broadened into “sleep deprivation makes people unwilling to do anything difficult”. One of the more informative experiments directly compared cognitive and physical effort and found a more selective effect. In that 2022 study, 24 healthy young adults completed two laboratory conditions: three consecutive nights with only a three hour sleep opportunity, and a rested condition with nine hours available each night. Participants first learnt tasks spanning six levels of cognitive or physical effort. They then chose between options requiring different amounts of effort for different rewards. The researchers deliberately trained performance to very high levels before the choice phase, helping to separate being able to perform an effort level from being willing to choose it . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation Sleep Restriction Reduces Cognitive but Not Physical Motivation PubMed... Sleep restriction reduced willingness to choose greater cognitive effort, particularly beyond the lowest effort level. It did not produce an equivalent reduction in willingness to exert physical effort. Analyses also indicated that the cognitive result could not simply be explained by participants believing that they were less likely to succeed at harder levels. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That finding is particularly relevant to self improvement plans built around mentally demanding activities: studying after work, learning a language, writing, deliberate practice, complicated financial administration or completing a project requiring sustained concentration. After inadequate sleep, the barrier may not merely be poorer execution once the activity begins. The high effort option itself can become less attractive. The physical finding also supplies an important boundary. It comes from one small controlled study and should not be taken to mean that sleep loss never affects physical effort, exercise or perceived exertion. Rather, it shows why “motivation” should not be treated as one undifferentiated quantity. Sleep restriction can alter willingness to invest effort differently depending on what kind of effort is required. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 2 When incentives can partly compensate Reduced willingness to exert effort is not the same thing as complete inability. If sleep deprivation raises the subjective price of cognitive work, increasing the value of the outcome can sometimes make paying that price"},{"title":"Plan Around Sleep","url":"/plan-around-sleep/","description":"Protecting enough sleep can make ambitious routines more realistic by preserving the attention and follow-through they depend on.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep Foundation","level":3,"text":"Plan Around Sleep Should Sleep Come Before Another Productivity Habit? Sleep Foundation Plan Around Sleep Plan Around Sleep Should Sleep Come Before Another Productivity Habit? Protecting enough sleep can make ambitious routines more realistic by preserving the attention and follow through they depend on. Protecting enough sleep can make ambitious routines more realistic by preserving the attention and follow through they depend on. Self Improvement That Works governance bounded topic policy intervention What adult sleep duration guidance recommends Why cutting sleep for productivity can backfire How to design routines around adequate rest self improvement that works sleep foundation plan around sleep Introduction For most healthy adults, sleep should usually come before another productivity habit when the new habit would require routinely cutting sleep. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM) and Sleep Research Society recommend seven or more hours of sleep a night on a regular basis , while the NHS says adults need seven to nine hours on average . Individual needs vary, but a self improvement plan built around persistent short sleep is asking attention, learning and follow through to operate under an avoidable constraint. [AASM] Protect a realistic sleep window first. Open source on aasm.org. Explanatory illustration 1 This changes how an ambitious routine should be designed. The question is not simply whether an extra hour awake could accommodate exercise , study, meditation or a side project. It is whether taking that hour from sleep leaves the following day's behaviour reliable enough to make the trade worthwhile. Controlled sleep restriction research suggests that repeated short nights can progressively impair performance, while people may not accurately appreciate the extent of that deterioration. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The practical rule is therefore to treat adequate sleep as a constraint on the plan , rather than as whatever time remains after everything else has been scheduled. What adult sleep guidance actually recommends The widely quoted seven hour figure is better understood as a lower boundary for regular sleep than as a universal target. The AASM/Sleep Research Society consensus states that adults should regularly obtain at least seven hours to promote optimal health. The panel also emphasised individual variation in sleep need, influenced by genetic, behavioural, medical and environmental factors. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Recommended Amount of Sleep for a Healthy Adult: A Joint Consensus Statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicin... UK guidance is similarly resistant to a one size fits all number. The NHS says everyone needs different amounts of sleep and gives seven to nine hours as the average adult requirement. Persistent daytime tiredness can be a sign that a person is not getting enough. [nhs.uk] nhs.uk Insomnia Insomnia That distinction matters for self improvement planning. “I get seven hours” is not proof that sleep has been optimised, just as sleeping less than seven hours does not diagnose the reason for someone's difficulties. Duration is only one dimension of sleep, and sleep disorders, shift work and other circumstances can complicate the picture. But for an otherwise healthy adult voluntarily trimming an ordinary night's sleep to make room for more goals, seven hours is a useful warning line rather than an expendable block of time. There is also a difference between an occasional short night and making sleep restriction part of the operating model. A late evening before a deadline is not equivalent to a routine that requires six hours every weekday. The latter repeatedly exposes the person to the very condition their improvement system is supposed to overcome: reduced capacity to perform consistently. Effects of Sleep Deprivation on Attention and Mood Channel: The BioClock Studio Open on YouTube Why borrowing from sleep can make the plan worse The seductive arithmetic of sleep sacrifice is straightforward. If waking at 5.30 am creates an extra hour for study or training, that appears to produce seven additional productive hours each week. The problem is that the calculation values waking time while assuming that the quality of those waking hours stays constant. Laboratory evidence gives good reason not to make that assumption. In a well known controlled experiment, 48 healthy adults were assigned to four, six or eight hours of time in bed for 14 consecutive nights, with separate participants undergoing total sleep deprivation. Neurobehavioural deficits accumulated across days in the four and six hour conditions. Importantly, subjective sleepiness did not track the continuing deterioration in objective performance particularly well. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... That finding makes chronic sleep restriction especially relevant to self improvement. A plan can feel as though it is becoming normal precisely while performance is becoming less dependable. “I have got used to six hours” may describe adaptation to the feeling of the schedule rather than evidence that the cognitive cost has disappeared. The US National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute explicitly identifies the idea that people can learn to function on little sleep without negative effects as a myth. [NHLBI, NIH] nhlbi.nih.gov NHLBI, NIHSleep Deprivation and Deficiency What Are Sleep Deprivation and Deficiency? NHLBI, NIHMarch 24, 2022... Published: March 24, 2022 The most consistent vulnerability is attention. A meta analysis of 61 sleep restriction studies found negative effects across cognitive processing, including sustained attention, executive functioning and long term memory. A newer meta analysis examining a single restricted night likewise found increased sleepiness, slower sustained attention responses and more attentional lapses, although several other cognitive outcomes did not show significant deterioration. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The neurocognitive consequences of sleep restriction: A meta analytic review PubMed... That qualification is important. Sleep loss does not make every mental function collapse equally, and it does not mean that every short night destroys productivity. Rather, it makes some of the capacities required for dependable execution less robust. For a self improvement system, reliability can matter more than peak performance: missing cues, drifting away from a study task or repeatedly making small errors can undermine a routine even if the person remains capable of impressive work for periods of time. Explanatory illustration 2 Motivation does not fully solve the problem The constraint is not purely cognitive. A review of sleep deprivation, effort and performance found evidence that sleep loss can make cognitive performance feel more effortful and reduce willingness to invest effort. Stronger external incentives can induce greater effort and partially compensate for impairment, but they do not simply erase the underlying effects of sleep loss. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance Sleep deprivation, effort allocation and performance PubMedApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 Experimental work has also found reduced willingness to exert cognitive effort following sleep restriction, without an equivalent reduction in physical effort motivation in that study. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This is particularly relevant to routines involving studying, writing, deliberate practice or difficult administrative work. A schedule may create more nominal time for those activities while simultaneously making engagement with them less attractive. Real world evidence points in the same broad direction, although observational workplace studies cannot establish causation as cleanly as controlled experiments. In a study of 4,188 employees at four US companies, groups classified as having insomnia or insufficient sleep syndrome showed worse productivity, performance and safety outcomes than comparison groups. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The cost of poor sleep: workplace productivity loss and associated costs Pub Med The cost of poor sleep: workplace productivity loss and associated costs The lesson is not that sleep automatically produces achievement. It is that motivation techniques, discipline and productivity systems operate on top of a variable level of cognitive capacity. Repeatedly reducing that capacity and then adding motivational machinery to compensate is an unnecessarily fragile design. Sleep Toolkit: Tools for Optimizing Sleep & Sleep Wake Timing Channel: Andrew"},{"title":"Hidden Impairment","url":"/hidden-impairment/","description":"People can get used to feeling sleepy while their objective attention and reaction performance continue to deteriorate.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Sleep Foundation","level":3,"text":"Hidden Impairment Can You Feel Fine and Still Be Sleep Deprived? Sleep Foundation Hidden Impairment Hidden Impairment Can You Feel Fine and Still Be Sleep Deprived? People can get used to feeling sleepy while their objective attention and reaction performance continue to deteriorate. People can get used to feeling sleepy while their objective attention and reaction performance continue to deteriorate. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism Why subjective sleepiness can level off How objective vigilance keeps worsening Why self assessment is a weak test of adequate sleep self improvement that works sleep foundation hidden impairment Introduction Yes. You can feel reasonably alert while repeated short nights are making your attention and reaction performance progressively less reliable. The important distinction is between subjective adaptation and performance adaptation . In controlled sleep restriction studies, people often report a sharp increase in sleepiness at first, followed by relatively little further change. Objective measures of vigilant attention, however, can continue to deteriorate across subsequent days. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... Explanatory illustration 1 That mismatch matters because “I feel fine” is an intuitively appealing but weak test of whether someone is adequately rested for dependable performance. The problem is not necessarily overwhelming drowsiness. It can instead appear as occasional slow reactions, missed signals and unstable attention amid long stretches in which the person feels and functions normally. For self improvement, that creates a hidden failure mode: someone may conclude that they have successfully adapted to less sleep just as their capacity for consistent follow through is declining. Why subjective sleepiness can level off The clearest demonstration comes from a landmark laboratory experiment led by Hans Van Dongen. Forty eight healthy adults were studied under controlled conditions. Groups were given four, six or eight hours of time in bed per night for 14 consecutive days, while another experiment examined total sleep deprivation. Participants repeatedly rated their own sleepiness and completed objective cognitive tests. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... The subjective and objective measures followed strikingly different trajectories. People restricted to four or six hours initially felt sleepier, but their sleepiness ratings subsequently increased only modestly. The ratings also failed to distinguish clearly between the four hour and six hour conditions. Objective cognitive performance, by contrast, showed cumulative, dose dependent deterioration. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... A later review of these experiments describes the divergence particularly clearly: after a week or two of restricted sleep, participants could be substantially impaired while reporting only moderate or slight sleepiness. In other words, what appeared to adapt was largely the subjective experience , not the underlying capacity being tested. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMCAugust 15, 2007... Published: August 15, 2007 This is easy to misinterpret in everyday life. If the first few short nights feel unpleasant but the same schedule becomes subjectively easier after several days, the natural conclusion is that the body has “got used to” six hours of sleep. The laboratory evidence shows why that inference can fail. Feeling less bothered by a state does not demonstrate that the performance consequences of that state have disappeared. Reviews of sleep loss research continue to distinguish the plateau seen in self reported sleepiness from the accumulating deficits found in vigilant attention. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Predicting and mitigating fatigue effects due to sleep deprivation: A review PMCAugust 5, 2022... Published: August 5, 2022 Nor should “subjective” be taken to mean useless. Sleepiness and alertness ratings contain genuine information about a person's current state. The narrower point is that their relationship with performance is imperfect and changes under sleep restriction. A person may correctly recognise that they are somewhat tired while still failing to appreciate how impaired their attention has become. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Prediction of Vigilant Attention and Cognitive Performance Using Self Reported Alertness, Circadian Phase, Hours since Awakening, and... Fatigue as a challenge in 24/7 operations how to survive and thrive Channel: University of South Australia Open on YouTube How objective vigilance keeps worsening The contrast is especially visible on the psychomotor vigilance test (PVT), a simple reaction time task widely used in sleep research. Signals appear at unpredictable intervals and the participant responds as quickly as possible. Because the task repeatedly demands sustained attention, sleep loss tends to show up not merely as uniformly slower performance but as an increasing number of unusually slow responses or attentional lapses. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Behavioral and Physiological Consequences of Sleep Restriction PMCAugust 15, 2007... Published: August 15, 2007 In Van Dongen's 14 day experiment, these vigilance deficits accumulated steadily in the four and six hour sleep conditions even though subjective sleepiness did not accumulate in parallel. Reviews of the experiment report that two weeks at six hours of time in bed produced deficits on cognitive measures comparable to roughly one night of total sleep deprivation, while four hour nights produced still larger impairment. The eight hour condition remained comparatively stable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic... That general dose response pattern is not confined to one experiment. In another controlled study, Gregory Belenky and colleagues assigned 66 participants to three, five, seven or nine hours of time in bed for seven days. PVT performance deteriorated most dramatically in the three hour group; the five and seven hour groups also showed impaired performance, although some measures reached a reduced plateau. The nine hour group remained around baseline. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryPatterns of performance degradation and restoration during sleep restriction and subsequent recovery: a sleep dose‐re... More unusually designed research has helped separate chronic short sleep from the simple effect of staying awake for an exceptionally long stretch. In a 32 day inpatient experiment, participants experiencing chronic sleep restriction were never scheduled to remain awake for more than 15.33 hours at once. Despite avoiding unusually long individual bouts of wakefulness, chronic short sleep still increased attentional lapses roughly fivefold and substantially degraded reaction time performance, without uniformly reducing participants' reported alertness. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Chronic sleep curtailment, even without extended ( 16 h) wakefulness, degrades human vigilance performance PubMedJune 5, 2018... Published: June 5, 2018 This is why the phrase “I can still function” sets a low bar. Sleep restricted performance is not necessarily a continuous state of obvious incompetence. Much of the time a person may respond normally. What becomes less dependable is the ability to sustain that performance without intermittent failures. Sleep loss research describes increased lapses of sustained attention as one of its most robust neurobehavioural effects. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Sleep Deprivation and Neurobehavioral Dynamics Pub Med Central (PMC)Sleep Deprivation and Neurobehavioral Dynamics For attention and follow through, reliability is precisely what matters. Reading nine paragraphs successfully does not cancel losing the thread on the tenth. Answering most messages correctly does not make the overlooked attachment harmless. Completing most steps of a routine does not compensate for repeatedly missing the transition at which action was required. These examples are illustrations rather than direct experimental outcomes, but they capture the practical significance of a vigilance deficit: average competence can coexist with less consistent performance . Explanatory illustration 2 The mismatch can change"},{"title":"Waiting Periods","url":"/waiting-periods/","description":"A next-day review can weaken a purchase urge more effectively when you leave the shopping environment instead of continuing to browse.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Spending Systems","level":3,"text":"Waiting Periods When Does Waiting Actually Stop an Impulse Buy? Spending Systems Waiting Periods Waiting Periods When Does Waiting Actually Stop an Impulse Buy? A next day review can weaken a purchase urge more effectively when you leave the shopping environment instead of continuing to browse. A next day review can weaken a purchase urge more effectively when you leave the shopping environment instead of continuing to browse. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention What longer purchase delays appear to change Why leaving the shopping environment matters How to build a next day review rule self improvement that works spending systems waiting periods Introduction Waiting can reduce an impulse purchase, but the evidence does not support a magical universal rule such as “always wait 24 hours”. What seems to matter is whether the delay actually separates the shopper from the state that produced the urge. In University of Michigan experiments, an approximately 25 hour postponement reduced both the felt urge to buy impulsively and purchase intention, whereas a ten minute delay imposed while participants remained on Amazon did not significantly reduce impulse purchases or impulsive spending. Every participant in that short delay condition continued shopping while waiting. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction makes a next day rule more useful than a checkout countdown. For a non essential purchase, the practical intervention is: save the item, leave the shop or close the shopping app, do something unrelated, and make a fresh decision the next day. The waiting period works best as a change of context, not merely as elapsed time. Explanatory illustration 3 What longer purchase delays appear to change The most directly relevant experimental evidence comes from Carol Moser's University of Michigan research on designing e commerce systems that support self control . The work tested postponement alongside reflection and distraction rather than assuming that delay itself would necessarily work. In one online experiment, participants reconsidered purchases after roughly 25 hours. Both their reported impulse buying urge and their intention to purchase declined. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce That result is important because an impulse buying urge is not identical to a completed purchase. Researchers commonly distinguish the sudden desire to buy from the subsequent behaviour: a person can feel a strong urge without acting on it. Earlier mall research likewise found that browsing, affect, available time and individual impulse buying tendencies feed into the urge and ultimately into whether an impulse purchase occurs. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Impulse buying: Modeling its precursors Impulse buying: Modeling its precursors ScienceDirect... A useful waiting rule therefore does not have to make an attractive product unattractive. It only has to weaken the urgency enough for an ordinary purchasing decision to replace the immediate one. Moser's findings also provide an unusually useful comparison with a much shorter intervention. In a laboratory study involving Amazon shopping, a ten minute delay did not produce a statistically significant reduction in the number of impulse products purchased or the amount spent impulsively. Participants did not actually disengage: all continued shopping during those ten minutes. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep BlueData Set Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce ID: m039k5047 Deep Blue Data... The sensible interpretation is not that precisely 25 hours is an evidence established optimum. The experiments compared different settings and designs, rather than running a clean contest between ten minutes, one hour, 12 hours, 24 hours and several days. The evidence is therefore too thin to declare a scientifically optimal waiting period. What it does support is the more useful distinction between a delay long enough for everyday life to intervene and a nominal delay during which the person remains immersed in shopping. Moser's conclusion was correspondingly conditional: postponement can help when it lasts long enough for natural distractions to cool the impulse, or when a shorter interval is deliberately occupied by reflection or distraction rather than further browsing. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep BlueData Set Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce ID: m039k5047 Deep Blue Data... This also explains why “sleep on it” is a reasonable personal policy without needing to claim that 24 hours is a special psychological threshold. A next day decision is simply an easy way to create substantial temporal and contextual separation. Your Brain's Hijacked: 7 Ways to Stop Impulse Spending NOW Channel: Sisters For Financial Independence Open on YouTube Why leaving the shopping environment matters An impulse buying urge is partly a response to what is happening now . Retail environments contain cues designed, intentionally or otherwise, to keep attention on products and make purchasing appealing: promotions, displays, attractive presentation, easy navigation and enjoyable browsing. A major meta analysis found that impulse buying reflects both internal characteristics and external marketing stimuli, with communication and price stimuli among the important retail influences. [Springer Link] link.springer.com Springer LinkImpulse buying: a meta analytic review Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science Springer Nature LinkJuly 9, 2019... Published: July 9, 2019 A systematic review similarly found that discounts and other immediate rewards can accelerate decisions, while online visual appeal and other website cues can stimulate pleasure, arousal and impulsive purchasing. [DOI] doi.org Impulse buying: A systematic literature review and future research directions Impulse buying: A systematic literature review and future research directions Redine 2023 International Journal of Consumer Stud... Online shopping does not remove the environment; it relocates it to the screen. Research on electronic stores has linked design and ease of navigation with shopping enjoyment , browsing and impulse buying behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect The role of atmospheric cues in online impulse buying behavior The role of atmospheric cues in online impulse buying behavior ScienceDirect... Consequently, “wait ten minutes” and “stop shopping for ten minutes” are behaviourally different instructions. Imagine seeing trainers reduced from £120 to £75. One version of a waiting rule says that checkout is blocked for ten minutes, but during those minutes you can inspect photographs, read reviews, compare colourways, notice “customers also bought” recommendations and search for similar trainers. The other says to save the link and close the site until tomorrow. Only the second reliably removes the continuing stream of shopping cues. That distinction is consistent with the wider evidence on browsing. The classic Beatty and Ferrell model connected browsing activity with felt impulse buying urges and eventual purchases, while later research has repeatedly treated browsing and shopping enjoyment as important parts of the impulse buying process. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Impulse buying: Modeling its precursors Impulse buying: Modeling its precursors ScienceDirect... The University of Michigan short delay experiment makes the point unusually concrete: keeping people inside the shopping process may preserve precisely the state that the waiting period is supposed to interrupt. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Leaving also creates room for competing thoughts that are unlikely to appear while attention is fixed on the product. During Moser's delay research, participants who changed their minds described considerations such as finding a cheaper or more useful alternative and recognising, after waiting, that they did not need or strongly want an item. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control This is the practical value of cooling an impulse: the original desire gets another chance to compete with price, usefulness, existing possessions and other priorities. There is a useful parallel in research on formal consumer cooling off periods. A qualitative study of people rescinding timeshare purchases found that reconsideration could involve reassessing value and financial"},{"title":"Live Feedback","url":"/live-feedback/","description":"Seeing how much money remains before checkout can connect one purchase to the rest of the month's spending plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Spending Systems","level":3,"text":"Live Feedback Why Spending Feedback Works Better Before Checkout Spending Systems Live Feedback Live Feedback Why Spending Feedback Works Better Before Checkout Seeing how much money remains before checkout can connect one purchase to the rest of the month's spending plan. Seeing how much money remains before checkout can connect one purchase to the rest of the month's spending plan. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Why after the fact budgeting misses the decision moment What real time balance feedback adds How to track one problem category at purchase time self improvement that works spending systems live feedback Introduction Real time spending feedback works on a simple principle: financial information is more useful for impulse control while a purchase can still be changed than after the money has gone. A monthly budget reviewed at home may show that restaurant, clothes or hobby spending is too high, but it cannot undo yesterday's checkout. A live figure — such as “£84 left for eating out this month” — connects the item in front of the buyer to the rest of the spending plan. Explanatory illustration 1 The evidence does not support the stronger claim that displaying a balance automatically makes everybody spend less. Effects vary with the type of feedback and the shopper. But field experiments, banking studies and consumer research converge on a useful distinction: information delivered near a consequential decision can change behaviour in ways that delayed summaries often do not. [fca.org.uk] fca.org.uk March 11, 2015... Published: March 11, 2015 Why Your Brain Thinks a Credit Card Is Free Money Channel: Max & Money Open on YouTube Why after the fact budgeting misses the decision moment Traditional budgeting separates two activities that need to meet: planning and purchasing. People decide in advance what they would like to spend, then encounter individual buying decisions in completely different settings. The US Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) found precisely this gap in its research on spending management. Consumers often wanted budgets and spending plans, yet did not regularly compare actual spending with those plans when making purchases. Tracking could feel burdensome, so the budget was not necessarily present when a decision had to be made. [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau] consumerfinance.gov Consumer Financial Protection BureauConsumer insights on managing spending Consumer Financial Protection BureauFebruary 28, 2017... Published: February 28, 2017 Banking evidence offers a useful comparison between delayed and timely information. In an analysis involving data from two large UK banks, the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) found that annual account summaries did not produce detectable changes in overdraft charges, balances or switching. By contrast, customers signing up for text alerts or mobile banking experienced reductions in unarranged overdraft charges of about 5–8%; customers using both were associated with a larger reduction. Because customers were not randomly assigned to all of these services, that particular comparison should not be read as pure experimental proof that timing caused the difference. It does, however, show why merely supplying financial information is not enough. [FCA] fca.org.uk March 11, 2015... Published: March 11, 2015 Stronger evidence comes from subsequent UK overdraft alert interventions. FCA research covering large scale bank populations found that automatically enrolling customers into alerts for unarranged overdrafts and unpaid items substantially reduced the corresponding charges. Later field experiments involving more than one million current account customers found that real time alerts warning of arranged overdraft use also reduced charges, whereas evidence for more generic low balance warnings was weak or mixed. [FCA] fca.org.uk Open source on fca.org.uk. These studies concern overdrafts rather than discretionary impulse purchases, so they should not be treated as direct evidence that a checkout balance will stop someone buying trainers or ordering a takeaway. Their relevance is more specific: information becomes actionable when it arrives close enough to the financial consequence for the person to respond . The FCA describes this as “ just in time ” disclosure. Its research found that customers could still transfer money, cut non essential spending or take another action before the unwanted outcome became unavoidable. [FCA] fca.org.uk FCAFinancial Conduct Authority October 19, 2025... Published: October 19, 2025 That distinction separates feedback from bookkeeping. A notification saying that £60 was spent yesterday improves the historical record. A display saying that only £75 remains in this month's discretionary allowance before a £45 purchase asks a different question: is this item worth consuming 60% of what remains? Why we make bad financial choices even when we know better Your Money and Your Mind Channel: TED Open on YouTube What a live balance adds before checkout The most useful feedback is not necessarily an ordinary bank balance. A current account containing £1,400 can make a £70 purchase look comfortable even if £1,250 is already needed for rent, bills, food and other commitments. For impulse control, the more meaningful number is often spendable money after planned commitments , or the amount remaining in the particular category where overspending occurs. That was the logic behind a CFPB prototype specifically designed around point of purchase feedback. Researchers tested a payment card concept whose display could show how much remained after accounting for monthly bills, expenses and savings goals. Users could view remaining amounts for the month, week and day, alongside a budgeting app giving wider context. [GovInfo] govinfo.gov GOVPUB FR PURL gpo153581 February 2017August 16, 2025... Published: August 16, 2025 Interest was striking: more than 90% of the consumers questioned expressed interest in a tool or app supplying this kind of feedback, and respondents expected it to help with impulse spending, budgeting and uncertainty about their finances. But the limitation is important. The CFPB's prototype research included a diverse but non representative online sample of 144 respondents, followed by in person testing with nine consumers using mock transactions. It was not a real world trial demonstrating that the prototype reduced spending . The results establish perceived usefulness and help identify a plausible design, not an effect size. [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau] consumerfinance.gov Consumer Financial Protection BureauConsumer insights on managing spending Consumer Financial Protection BureauFebruary 28, 2017... Published: February 28, 2017 More direct behavioural evidence comes from grocery shopping. A field experiment by Natalia Montinari, Emma Runnemark and Erik Wengström randomly allocated shoppers at two stores to use mobile self scanners or shop conventionally. Scanning gave shoppers a running total as they selected products — unusually immediate feedback because the cumulative cost was visible before the final checkout. Across shoppers overall, the effect on expenditure was negative but statistically insignificant. Among participants classified as having low self control , however, using the scanner significantly reduced both expenditure and the number of items purchased. [Lund University] portal.research.lu.se nd UniversitySelf Scanning and Self Control: A Field Experiment on Real Time Feedback and Shopping Behavior Lund University... That heterogeneous result matters for self improvement. A tool designed to interrupt impulsive spending does not have to make every consumer spend less to be useful. Its relevant test is whether it helps people whose existing intentions are repeatedly defeated during the purchase process. The grocery experiment suggests that real time totals may be particularly useful for at least some shoppers who recognise that self control is a problem. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com Self Scanning and Self Control: A Field Experiment on Real Time Feedback and Shopping Behavior by Natalia Montinari, Emma Runnemark... There is also a warning against treating “show a number” as a universal nudge. Experiments on smart shopping carts found different reactions among shoppers depending on whether they were operating under a spending constraint. Real time feedback did not simply push expenditure down across the board; some budget shoppers spent more, partly by choosing more national brand products, while other shoppers reduced expenditure by substituting store brands. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Smart Shopping Carts: How Real Time Feedback Influences Spending Sage Journals Smart Shopping Carts: How Real Time Feedback Influences Spending The practical conclusion is therefore narrower and stronger than “balances prevent impulse buying”. Feedback needs a meaningful"},{"title":"Saved Cards","url":"/saved-cards/","description":"Removing saved card details adds a small obstacle that can make payment more noticeable before an impulsive purchase is completed.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Spending Systems","level":3,"text":"Saved Cards Can Deleting Saved Cards Slow Impulse Spending? Spending Systems Saved Cards Saved Cards Can Deleting Saved Cards Slow Impulse Spending? Removing saved card details adds a small obstacle that can make payment more noticeable before an impulsive purchase is completed. Removing saved card details adds a small obstacle that can make payment more noticeable before an impulsive purchase is completed. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why friction disappears with stored payment details What payment salience research can and cannot prove Practical ways to make checkout less automatic self improvement that works spending systems saved cards Introduction Deleting saved card details can slow impulse spending, but the claim needs to be kept modest. The useful mechanism is not that typing a card number is inherently therapeutic. It is that stored credentials, autofill and one click purchasing remove actions that once separated “I want this” from “I have bought this”. Restoring even a little checkout friction can create a decision point at exactly the stage where an impulsive purchase would otherwise become automatic. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest direct evidence comes from research on one click buying: when an online retailer reduced checkout to a single click, customers subsequently bought more often and purchased more items. Broader payment research also finds that less salient, cashless forms of payment can weaken the psychological sense of parting with money. What researchers have not established is a precise causal estimate for the personal finance tactic “delete your saved cards”. That makes it best understood as a low cost environmental intervention: deliberately make an unwanted behaviour slightly harder rather than relying on willpower at the moment of temptation. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023... Published: February 22, 2023 Why You Can't Stop Spending Money (It's Not What You Think) Channel: Stackers University Open on YouTube Why stored cards remove a useful pause A conventional online card payment contains several small pieces of work: finding the card, entering its number, checking the expiry date and security code, and confirming the transaction. None is difficult. Collectively, however, they consume time and attention. Saving the card moves that work into the past. Autofill reduces it further; one click systems can make payment almost disappear from the shopping sequence. That reduction in effort is deliberate. The UK Competition and Markets Authority's review of online choice architecture notes that businesses can increase sales by shortening the interval between seeing an item and buying it, including by encouraging shoppers to save payment details and enabling one click purchases. The same review stresses that convenient design is not inherently harmful: smoother online purchasing can genuinely benefit consumers. The issue for self control is narrower. A feature optimised to remove obstacles from an intended purchase also removes obstacles from an unintended one. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Evidence review of Online Choice Architecture and consumer and competition harm Evidence review of Online Choice Architecture and consumer and competition harm The importance of those obstacles is unusually visible in Murat Unal and Young Hoon Park's study, “Fewer Clicks, More Purchases”. Using 35 months of quasi experimental data surrounding an online retailer's introduction of one click buying, they found that adoption increased purchases persistently. Customers bought more frequently and bought more items, with purchasing expanding across product categories. Because this was a real retailer rather than a hypothetical shopping exercise , it provides particularly relevant evidence that reducing checkout effort can change actual purchasing behaviour. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023... Published: February 22, 2023 That study does not show that every extra checkout field prevents an impulse purchase, nor does reversing one click checkout necessarily reverse every effect the researchers observed. Their analysis also found increased site visits and page views among adopters, so the mechanism was richer than simply “one fewer click caused one more purchase”. Still, it demonstrates something important for a personal spending system: checkout convenience is not behaviourally neutral. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023... Published: February 22, 2023 Stored credentials are already an established part of this convenience infrastructure. In the Reserve Bank of Australia's 2019 Consumer Payments Survey, about 45% of respondents had used stored card details for an online payment during the previous year. The researchers described this as part of a trend towards increasingly seamless payments and noted that consumers might effectively “set and forget” their payment method. [Reserve Bank of Australia] rba.gov.au online and automated payments Reserve Bank of AustraliaOnline and Automated Payments RDP 2020 06: Consumer Payment Behaviour in Australia: Evidence from the 2019 Con... For someone repeatedly making regretted purchases, deleting the saved card changes that default. The sequence becomes: urge → checkout → payment obstacle → decision → purchase rather than: urge → checkout → tap → purchase The obstacle is valuable because it arrives before the transaction is irreversible, not because entering sixteen digits is difficult in itself. Friction Maxxing: The Psychological Trick to Stop Impulse Spending with Hanna Horvath Channel: Moolala: Money Made Simple Bruce Sellery Open on YouTube Payment can become less noticeable as it becomes easier Checkout friction and payment salience are related but distinct ideas. Friction concerns how much action is required to complete the transaction. Payment salience concerns how strongly the buyer experiences the fact that money is being surrendered. Decades of research suggest that the form of payment can influence spending. In a well known 2001 experiment involving genuine transactions, Drazen Prelec and Duncan Simester found that participants instructed to pay by credit card could be willing to pay more than participants instructed to use cash. The authors reported effects as large as 100% in some experimental conditions, although that striking figure should not be treated as a general estimate of how much cards increase everyday spending. [CiNii Research] cir.nii.ac.jp CiNii ResearchAlways Leave Home Without It: A Further Investigation of the Credit Card Effect on Willingness to Pay CiNii ResearchFebru... Priya Raghubir and Joydeep Srivastava subsequently investigated what happens when payment feels less transparent. Across several experiments, participants were more willing to spend under less cash like payment conditions. Importantly, the researchers could reduce differences between payment forms by making the act of parting with money more salient. In one experiment, asking people to estimate the individual components of an expense rather than think about the total holistically eliminated the observed cash credit difference. [PubMed] nih.gov Pub Med Monopoly money: the effect of payment coupling and form on spending behavior PubMedMonopoly money: the effect of payment coupling and form on spending behavior PubMed A 2024 meta analysis provides a useful corrective to drawing too much from individual experiments. Lachlan Schomburgk, Alex Belli and Arvid Hoffmann synthesised 392 effect sizes from 71 papers spanning more than four decades. They found a statistically significant but small overall tendency for people to spend more with cashless payment methods than with cash. The size of the effect varied with circumstances, and the authors found that it had weakened over time. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Less cash, more splash? A meta analysis on the cashless effect Less cash, more splash? A meta analysis on the cashless effect ScienceDirect... That qualification matters. “Cards make people overspend” is too crude. Modern consumers are highly accustomed to cards and digital payments, and payment behaviour varies by person, purchase and setting. Deleting stored card details therefore should not be presented as a way to recreate some universally powerful “pain of paying” effect. More recent mobile payment experiments underline that complexity. Research has found higher purchase intentions with mobile rather than cash payment, but the proposed mechanisms are not limited to reduced pain. A 2024 study found that reduced pain of paying mediated the mobile payment effect for higher"},{"title":"Short Delays","url":"/short-delays/","description":"A brief delay may do little if the shopper keeps browsing, because the tempting context remains active throughout the pause.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Spending Systems","level":3,"text":"Short Delays Why a Ten Minute Shopping Pause May Not Work Spending Systems Short Delays Short Delays Why a Ten Minute Shopping Pause May Not Work A brief delay may do little if the shopper keeps browsing, because the tempting context remains active throughout the pause. A brief delay may do little if the shopper keeps browsing, because the tempting context remains active throughout the pause. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single case aspect event time window What the ten minute delay experiment found How continued browsing undermines the pause Better ways to change both time and context self improvement that works spending systems short delays Introduction A ten minute shopping pause sounds sensible: interrupt the checkout, wait for the urge to weaken, then decide with a clearer head. But the best direct experiment on this specific tactic found that a ten minute delay did not significantly reduce either the number of impulse products bought or the amount spent impulsively. The revealing detail was what happened during the pause: every participant in the delayed group continued shopping for at least part of it. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Explanatory illustration 1 That makes the experiment useful for self improvement precisely because the intervention failed. A pause is not automatically a cooling off period. If ten extra minutes are spent comparing alternatives, seeing recommendations and adding products to a basket, the shopper remains inside the environment stimulating the urge. The more useful rule is therefore not simply wait before buying , but wait away from shopping . For short delays, changing what occupies attention may matter as much as adding minutes. The 24 Hour Rule That Silently Kills Impulse Spending Channel: Capital Cognition Open on YouTube What the ten minute experiment found Carol Moser's 2020 University of Michigan doctoral research examined several interventions intended to help people control online impulse buying. The programme included an approximately 25 hour postponement experiment, a ten minute in laboratory delay involving Amazon purchases, and experiments involving reflection and distraction. The underlying question was practical: what kind of friction actually helps once somebody already wants to make an impulsive purchase? [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control with E commerce The ten minute study provides an unusually direct test of the familiar advice to impose a short waiting period. Participants in the delay condition could shop, but completion of their Amazon purchases was held up for ten minutes. Researchers compared outcomes including the number of impulsive purchases, dollars spent impulsively and the proportion of basket additions ultimately purchased. The delay produced no statistically significant reduction in the number of impulse products purchased or in impulsive spending. [School of Information] si.umich.edu School of Information Virtual dissertation defense: Carol Moser umsi School of InformationVirtual dissertation defense: Carol Moser umsiMarch 17, 2020... Published: March 17, 2020 This result should not be read as proof that waiting never works. Within the same research programme, an approximately 25 hour postponement reduced both the reported urge to buy impulsively and purchase intention. The experiments differed in more than duration, so they do not establish a universal threshold somewhere between ten minutes and 25 hours. They instead expose a weakness in treating elapsed time as the active ingredient by itself. [School of Information] si.umich.edu School of Information Virtual dissertation defense: Carol Moser umsi School of InformationVirtual dissertation defense: Carol Moser umsiMarch 17, 2020... Published: March 17, 2020 Moser also cautioned that the laboratory setting may have influenced behaviour. Participants were relatively captive and might have felt expected to remain engaged with the task; somebody encountering the same barrier at home could simply put down the device. Ten minutes also leaves fewer opportunities than 25 hours for ordinary life — a meal, sleep, work or a conversation — to draw attention away from the desired product. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control Deep BlueImpulse Buying: Designing for Self ControlOctober 18, 2025... Published: October 18, 2025 The appropriate conclusion is therefore narrower than “ten minutes is useless”. It is that an unguided ten minute delay, while shopping remains available, is weak evidence of meaningful friction . The intervention stopped payment temporarily without reliably stopping the shopping process that preceded it. How to Stop IMPULSE BUYING and the Power of Delaying Shopping Decisions (my experience & best tips) Channel: Conscious Consumerism Open on YouTube How continued browsing undermines the pause The striking finding was that 100% of participants exposed to the ten minute delay continued to browse alternative products for at least some of the waiting period . Some participants said the extra time enabled them to look at other items or options; one reported that it allowed them to add more to their basket. Moser consequently described a case against “unguided postponement”: merely preventing checkout can create additional shopping time rather than genuine psychological distance from the purchase. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control Deep BlueImpulse Buying: Designing for Self ControlOctober 18, 2025... Published: October 18, 2025 That interpretation is consistent with a wider body of research linking browsing and exploratory shopping to impulsive outcomes. Research in the Journal of Consumer Research , using laboratory and field experiments, found that broader visual attention can produce a more exploratory shopping mindset, increasing exploration and ultimately affecting unplanned purchasing; people with greater impulsive buying tendencies were especially susceptible. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicExploratory Shopping: Attention Affects In Store Exploration and Unplanned Purchasing Journal of Consumer Research Oxford... Other mobile commerce research has found that hedonic browsing — browsing for enjoyment rather than simply completing a practical task — positively influences the urge to buy impulsively. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Understanding impulse buying in mobile commerce: An investigation into hedonic and utilitarian browsing ScienceDirect... This distinction explains why ten minutes can be ten minutes in name but not ten minutes of cooling off. Imagine somebody wants a pair of trainers and activates a ten minute checkout lock. They then spend those ten minutes looking at another colour, checking the “customers also bought” section, comparing a discounted model and reading enthusiastic reviews. The transaction has been delayed, but attention has never left the purchase. The shopper has also encountered several fresh opportunities to want something. Research on online commerce has long identified exposure as part of this problem. An analysis in the Journal of Computer Mediated Communication argued that browsing increases exposure to triggering shopping stimuli, while prolonged exposure can interfere with self observation and restraint. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn the Negative Effects of E Commerce: a Sociocognitive Exploration of Unregulated on line Buying Journal of Computer Media... More recent research on social commerce similarly finds relationships between impulse buying and features such as visual appeal, ease of use, passing time and serendipitous discovery. Its survey based design cannot prove that every extra minute of browsing causes another impulsive purchase, but it reinforces the point that a digital shopping environment is not psychologically neutral space in which to “cool down”. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers How technical and situational cues affect impulse buying behavior in social commerce? Evidence from bored consumersO... Explanatory illustration 2 When boredom becomes another shopping cue There is a second failure mode. An enforced ten minute wait can become boring, especially if it provides no alternative activity. Moser reported that some participants considered the delay too long or boring and noted the awkward implication: impulse shopping itself can be used as relief from boredom. [Deep Blue] deepblue.lib.umich.edu Deep Blue Impulse Buying: Designing for Self Control Deep BlueImpulse Buying: Designing for Self ControlOctober 18, 2025... Published: October 18, 2025 That connection is supported beyond this experiment. A Swedish qualitative study of"},{"title":"Category Budgets","url":"/category-budgets/","description":"Tracking one trouble category can make repeated overspending easier to spot while keeping the system simple enough to use consistently.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Spending Systems","level":3,"text":"Category Budgets Should You Budget Only Your Worst Spending Category? Spending Systems Category Budgets Category Budgets Should You Budget Only Your Worst Spending Category? Tracking one trouble category can make repeated overspending easier to spot while keeping the system simple enough to use consistently. Tracking one trouble category can make repeated overspending easier to spot while keeping the system simple enough to use consistently. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why narrow tracking can be easier to act on How category limits interact with impulse purchases What to do when the budget is repeatedly exceeded self improvement that works spending systems category budgets Introduction A full household budget is not always the best starting point for recurring impulse spending. If takeaway meals, clothes, beauty products, hobby purchases or another discretionary category repeatedly causes regret, tracking that one category can create a simpler and more usable constraint. The aim is not to account for every pound. It is to make one troublesome stream of spending visible before its many small purchases disappear into the rest of the month. Explanatory illustration 1 There is good evidence for the underlying mechanism, but not strong evidence that “budget only your worst category” is universally superior to comprehensive budgeting. Research shows that consumers commonly think and spend in categories, that tracking can become burdensome, and that budgets influence decisions most usefully when they are visible at the point of choice. [oup.com] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicMental Budgeting and Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 1, 1996... Published: June 1, 1996 That makes a single category budget best understood as a targeted self control tool : identify the recurring trouble spot, set a realistic limit from actual spending, check the remaining amount before purchases, and review repeated overruns rather than simply resetting the number. Why We Make Bad Choices: The Secret Power of Mental Accounting Channel: Easy Marketing School Open on YouTube Why narrow tracking can be easier to act on The case for narrow tracking begins with a mundane problem: budgeting requires attention. Research by the US Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) found that people often want to manage spending but exceed their intentions anyway. Participants described budgeting and expense tracking as overwhelming or troublesome, and even people with budgets did not necessarily compare current spending with those budgets regularly. [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau] consumerfinance.gov Open source on consumerfinance.gov. That matters because a budget that exists only in a spreadsheet opened once a month cannot interrupt many purchases. Reducing the tracking task from “categorise my financial life” to “keep an accurate running total of takeaway spending” removes information that may be useful for broader financial planning, but it also reduces the amount of work needed for this particular behavioural intervention. The CFPB itself advises people who find comprehensive tracking overwhelming to start small, such as reviewing one week of expenses at a time. [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau] consumerfinance.gov Consumer Financial Protection BureauBudgeting: How to create a budget and stick with it Consumer Financial Protection BureauJune 5, 2019... Published: June 5, 2019 Category based thinking is not an artificial budgeting trick. In a 2022 study using a nationally representative survey of 3,826 US adults, more than 90% of people who budgeted reported tracking spending through distinct categories. People differed substantially in how finely they divided those categories: some used little more than “necessities” and “discretionary”, while others separately tracked expenses such as dining out, entertainment and clothing. [Squarespace] static1.squarespace.com How consumers budget How consumers budget... Earlier experimental research by Chip Heath and Jack Soll helps explain why those divisions can influence purchases. Their work on “mental budgeting” found that consumers allocate money to expense categories and consider spending already charged to a category when making subsequent decisions. A purchase that clearly belongs to a nearly exhausted category can therefore compete psychologically with other purchases in the same category. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicMental Budgeting and Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 1, 1996... Published: June 1, 1996 For recurring impulse spending, this suggests an important design choice: make the category narrow enough that the purchases you want to control obviously belong in it . “Discretionary spending” may be too broad if the actual problem is frequent clothes shopping. Conversely, creating separate limits for tops, shoes, accessories and coats introduces bookkeeping that may defeat the purpose. Explanatory illustration 3 How to Stop Impulse Spending (Without Shame or Deprivation) Channel: Inspired Budget Allison Flores Baggerly Open on YouTube How a category limit interrupts impulse purchases A category budget works differently from a general intention such as “spend less online”. The limit supplies a reference point: instead of evaluating a £35 purchase largely on whether the item seems worth £35, the buyer can also ask what £35 does to the month's remaining clothing allowance. That distinction is particularly useful when repeated purchases look harmless in isolation. A £12 order, £18 purchase and £25 purchase each invite a separate justification. A running category total places them in the same frame. Research on mental accounting indicates that consumers do not always treat money as perfectly interchangeable and that expenditure assigned to a category can affect subsequent consumption within it. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicMental Budgeting and Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 1, 1996... Published: June 1, 1996 The crucial part, however, is seeing the information before deciding . CFPB research found that people often failed to use their budgets during spending decisions. More than 90% of consumers in its qualitative research expressed interest in tools providing real time spending feedback , and participants believed such information could help them curb impulse spending and stay within budgets. These findings primarily show demand and perceived usefulness rather than proving a particular app will reduce expenditure. [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau] consumerfinance.gov Open source on consumerfinance.gov. Research on financial self control more broadly supports using concrete strategies instead of expecting willpower alone to carry the decision. A 2021 meta analysis covering 29 studies and 12 financial self control strategies found an overall medium sized beneficial effect on spending or saving outcomes. Strategies represented in the underlying literature included organising expenses into categories and tracking expenditure. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is an important warning here. More feedback does not automatically mean less spending. Experiments involving real time grocery spending feedback found different effects for different shoppers: feedback actually increased expenditure among budget constrained shoppers in those experiments, partly through purchases of more national brands, while reducing expenditure among non budget shoppers through substitution towards store brands. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Smart Shopping Carts: How Real Time Feedback Influences Spending Sage Journals Smart Shopping Carts: How Real Time Feedback Influences Spending So “£73 remaining” should not be interpreted as “£73 available to spend”. That turns the ceiling into a target. The useful question is not “Can I fit this purchase under the limit?” but “Knowing what I have already spent and what remains, do I still want to make this purchase?” This is why a single category budget fits naturally alongside a purchase review step. Before a non essential purchase in the chosen category: 1. Check the running total. Include pending transactions and purchases made through different payment methods. 2. Calculate the effect of this purchase. A £40 item with £55 remaining is not merely a £40 decision; it leaves £15 for the rest of the budget period. How to Stop Impulse Spending Without Guilt or Extreme Budgeting Channel: Michela Allocca Open on YouTube Explanatory illustration 2 3. Apply the existing pause rule. Put the item on a wish list or wait before buying rather than treating available budget as permission. MoneyHelper similarly recommends both budgeting and pausing before non essential purchases when addressing impulse spending. MaPS 4. Record the purchase promptly if it goes ahead. The running"},{"title":"Flexible Starts","url":"/flexible-starts/","description":"A restart plan tied to the first usable work block can remain actionable when meetings and interruptions make fixed schedules unreliable.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Work Prep","level":3,"text":"Flexible Starts When Tomorrow's Morning Refuses to Follow the Plan Work Prep Flexible Starts Flexible Starts When Tomorrow's Morning Refuses to Follow the Plan A restart plan tied to the first usable work block can remain actionable when meetings and interruptions make fixed schedules unreliable. A restart plan tied to the first usable work block can remain actionable when meetings and interruptions make fixed schedules unreliable. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Why rigid restart plans can fail Planning around usable work windows instead of perfect schedules Preparing a first task that can survive interruptions self improvement that works work prep flexible starts Introduction For workers whose mornings are routinely broken up by meetings, messages, handovers or urgent requests, a restart plan should not depend on starting at an ideal time. “At 09:00 I will work on the proposal” is brittle: once 09:00 disappears, the plan has lost its cue. A more resilient version attaches a concrete next action to the first usable work block : “When I get my first uninterrupted 20 minute window, I will open the proposal at the pricing section and resolve the three comments already marked there.” Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is supported by two relevant research strands. Studies of daily work planning find that ordinary time management plans become less useful as interruptions increase, whereas contingent planning —anticipating disruption and deciding how to respond—holds up better. Research on interrupted tasks also shows that preserving cues about where and how to resume can reduce the cognitive work of reconstructing a task afterwards. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When daily planning improves employee performance: The importance of planning type, engagement, and interruptions PubMedNovember... The practical aim, therefore, is not to protect a perfect morning. It is to make the important work easy to restart when the morning inevitably changes. Why rigid restart plans can fail A fixed time plan can look admirably specific while containing a hidden weakness: its usefulness depends on the predicted schedule surviving contact with the real workday. That matters particularly in jobs where colleagues, customers, managers, patients or operational problems can claim the first hour without much warning. A two week experience sampling study of employees makes this distinction unusually relevant. Researchers separated daily planning into time management planning —prioritising tasks and deciding when and how to perform them—and contingent planning , in which workers anticipate possible interruptions and prepare responses. Time management planning was associated with better engagement and performance, but its benefits weakened on days containing more interruptions. The benefits associated with contingent planning were not similarly dependent on low interruption levels. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When daily planning improves employee performance: The importance of planning type, engagement, and interruptions PubMedNovember... That does not mean calendars or time blocking are useless. It means they solve a different problem. A schedule answers, “When should I work on this?” A restart plan for an interruption heavy morning must also answer, “What happens if that time disappears?” This exposes the weakness in plans such as: Brittle: “Start the analysis at 09:00.” Better: “Use the first 30 minute gap after the morning meetings to check the three anomalous rows already flagged in the analysis.” Brittle: “Write for an hour before email.” Better: “At the first usable writing window, open the draft at ‘Customer impact’ and turn the four notes underneath it into a paragraph.” Brittle: “Finish the forecast first thing.” Better: “When I next have a quiet block, reopen the forecast at the assumptions sheet and verify staffing costs before changing anything else.” The change is small but important. The second kind of plan still specifies a cue and an action, but the cue is an event or opportunity that can recur , rather than a single clock time that can be missed. This is consistent with the broader evidence on implementation intentions: plans linking a recognisable situation to a specified response can improve goal enactment. A 2006 meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, while a much larger review published in 2024, covering 642 independent tests, found beneficial effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes and reported stronger effects when plans actually used a contingent if–then structure. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... There is an important limit to that evidence. Implementation intentions are not proof that any detailed schedule will work. Indeed, a field experiment involving 877 gym users found no benefit from simply asking people to specify the days and times they intended to exercise repeatedly over the following fortnight. Specificity alone is therefore not magic. The useful lesson for disrupted mornings is narrower: specify an actionable response to a cue that is realistically likely to occur, rather than making success depend on one predicted moment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The limits of simple implementation intentions: Evidence from a field experiment on making plans to exercise ScienceDirect... Refocusing After Interruptions Channel: Kelli Risse Open on YouTube Plan around the first usable work window A “usable work window” is not necessarily the first empty space on the calendar. It is the first period long and suitable enough for the prepared next action. That definition makes the restart plan responsive to the day rather than tied to an imaginary version of it. Suppose tomorrow begins with a 09:00 team meeting. You intend to work on a difficult report at 09:30, but the meeting runs over, somebody asks for help immediately afterwards, and at 10:15 another call appears. Under a rigid plan, the planned start has already failed. The worker must now decide whether to squeeze in the report, postpone it, choose something easier or redesign the morning. A flexible restart plan removes part of that replanning: “At my first quiet block of at least 25 minutes, I will open the findings draft, read the final paragraph for context, and write the explanation of the retention drop using the chart already prepared.” The important feature is not the wording. It is that three decisions have been made in advance: what opportunity qualifies, which task resumes, and what action starts it . This approach also avoids an easy mistake: making the cue too vague. “When I get time, work on the report” leaves almost everything unresolved. Does a ten minute gap count? What part of the report? Is there enough time to begin? What must be opened first? A useful restart plan reduces those questions rather than merely recording an aspiration. The recent implementation intention review is helpful here because it distinguishes different kinds of cues, including time and place cues and task juncture cues . That supports a broader conception of planning than simply assigning actions to clock times. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... For interruption heavy work, practical cues can therefore be transitions such as “when the client call ends”, “after the urgent request is handed off” or “when the first 30 minute gap appears”. There is also a reason not to over engineer the system. If tomorrow's plan contains a complicated tree of contingencies—“if the 09:00 meeting moves, do A, unless B arrives, in which case do C…”—the plan itself becomes another object to interpret. The objective is to reduce decisions at restart, not construct a miniature scheduling algorithm. Usually one primary rule and, where genuinely necessary, one fallback are enough. Explanatory illustration 2 Prepare a first task that survives interruption Choosing the right work window solves only half the problem. The task itself must also be restartable. Interruption research uses the term resumption lag for the delay between finishing an interruption and successfully getting back into the original task. Laboratory research by J. Gregory Trafton and colleagues found that people who received an eight second warning before being interrupted prepared more and subsequently resumed the primary task faster than people interrupted immediately. Related experiments found that cues associated with the interrupted task could reduce the delay in getting back to work. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Preparing to resume an interrupted task: effects of prospective goal encoding and retrospective rehearsal ScienceDirect... The underlying problem is partly one of"},{"title":"Workspace Cues","url":"/workspace-cues/","description":"Files, notes and strategically placed objects can carry intentions into tomorrow instead of leaving prospective memory to do all the work.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Work Prep","level":3,"text":"Workspace Cues Can Your Workspace Remember Tomorrow's Task? Work Prep Workspace Cues Workspace Cues Can Your Workspace Remember Tomorrow's Task? Files, notes and strategically placed objects can carry intentions into tomorrow instead of leaving prospective memory to do all the work. Files, notes and strategically placed objects can carry intentions into tomorrow instead of leaving prospective memory to do all the work. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why future intentions are easy to forget Turning files, notes and objects into reminders Choosing cues that remain obvious when work resumes self improvement that works work prep workspace cues Introduction A workspace can do part of tomorrow’s remembering for you. Instead of ending the day with an unfinished task held mainly as a mental intention — “carry on with the analysis”, “send that document”, “check those figures” — you can encode the intention into something you are likely to encounter when work resumes: the open file, a note beside the relevant passage, an object placed somewhere unusual, or a deliberately positioned digital document. Explanatory illustration 1 Researchers call the broader strategy intention offloading : creating an external cue so that remembering a future action does not depend entirely on prospective memory. Reviews find that external reminders can substantially improve fulfilment of delayed intentions, particularly when memory load or distraction makes unaided remembering less dependable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Cognitive Offloading Cognitive Offloading PubMedAugust 16, 2016... Published: August 16, 2016 The practical point is not that every unfinished task needs a reminder. It is that a well designed workspace can preserve the thread of work across an overnight interruption. The best cue is noticeable when work resumes, connected to the task it represents, and specific enough to recover the intended next action without another round of reconstruction. Why future intentions are easy to lose Remembering something you have already learnt and remembering that you need to do something later are different problems. The latter is prospective memory : remembering to execute an intention when the appropriate time or situation arrives. Unlike an ordinary memory question, there may be nobody prompting you to retrieve the information. You have to notice that the moment for action has arrived and recover what you intended to do. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Focal/nonfocal cue effects in prospective memory: monitoring difficulty or different retrieval processes? PubMed... That distinction matters when work crosses a boundary such as an evening, weekend or meeting. At 17:30, the significance of a spreadsheet, half written paragraph or unresolved question may be completely obvious because its context is active in your mind. Tomorrow morning, you may remember the project while failing to retrieve the precise state in which you left it: which discrepancy mattered, what you had ruled out, or what you intended to try next. Prospective memory experiments also show why simply deciding to remember is an imperfect strategy. Remembering future intentions competes with whatever else occupies attention. In Sam Gilbert's experiments on intention offloading, participants became more likely to create reminders when they had more intentions to hold in memory and when interruptions were introduced. Crucially, those were also circumstances in which unaided memory performed worse. Across four experiments involving 1,196 participants, setting external reminders improved performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Strategic offloading of delayed intentions into the external environment Strategic offloading of delayed intentions into the external environment PubMedNovember 18, 2014... Published: November 18, 2014 More recent work reaches a similar conclusion for time based intentions. In experiments where people had to remember an action while simultaneously performing a working memory task, reminders increased accuracy and became more attractive as the delay grew longer. [Springer] link.springer.com Strategic reminder setting for time based intentions: Influence of metacognition, delay length, and cue visibility Memory & Cog... This suggests a useful interpretation of end of day preparation. Leaving a cue is not merely organising the desk. It changes the memory problem. Rather than requiring tomorrow’s self to spontaneously retrieve an intention, the environment presents information capable of prompting retrieval. Why Your Ancestors' Brains Weren't Built for To Do Lists Channel: Inertiology: The Science of Getting Stuck Open on YouTube Turning files, notes and objects into reminders An external reminder works because some part of the intention is moved into the environment. The principle is broader than conventional alerts. Research on cognitive and intention offloading explicitly includes diaries, lists, sticky notes, digital alerts and strategically positioned physical objects. [Springer] link.springer.com Outsourcing Memory to External Tools: A Review of ‘Intention Offloading’ Psychonomic Bulletin & Review Springer Nature LinkJu... A workspace therefore offers several natural places to store an unfinished intention. Put the instruction where the work will happen. If a report is unfinished, a note inside the report saying “Next: reconcile Table 2 with the June spreadsheet” carries more useful information into tomorrow than a separate task saying “continue report”. For code, the equivalent might be a comment beside the failing function. For research, it could be a note attached to the source that still needs checking. Use position as information. A document deliberately placed at the top of a working folder, a paper left on the keyboard or an item placed beside the bag that must leave with you can function as a cue because its location makes it difficult to encounter the workspace without encountering the reminder. Gilbert's experimental intention offloading paradigm deliberately models this idea: participants could reposition items in their environment so that their later location prompted the required action. The researchers compare the mechanism with placing an object by the front door so that it reminds you to take it when leaving. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Strategic offloading of delayed intentions into the external environment PMC... Preserve state as well as task identity. There is an important difference between remembering that a project is unfinished and recovering where to restart it . A workspace cue is especially useful when it captures the latter. “Budget” identifies a project; “Next: find why contractor costs differ by £4,800 between these two sheets” restores a working problem. That distinction follows from prospective memory research separating the need to notice that an action is required from the need to remember what that action is. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Strategic offloading of delayed intentions into the external environment PMC... A useful cue can therefore contain both components: the trigger and the action . “When I reopen this file, check the July total against invoice 184” does more than record information. It links the situation tomorrow will present with the behaviour you want that situation to elicit. That resembles an implementation intention , an if then plan linking a specified future cue with an intended response. Research has repeatedly found that such encoding can improve prospective remembering, apparently in part by strengthening the association between cue and response. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Implementation intentions facilitate prospective memory under high attention demands PubMedJune 1, 2008... Published: June 1, 2008 The effect should not be overstated as perfectly automatic: experiments have also found that implementation intentions do not eliminate performance losses under heavy cognitive demands. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. The safer conclusion is that a strong cue action link helps retrieval; it does not make attention irrelevant. Explanatory illustration 2 What makes a workspace cue hard to miss? The temptation is to equate an effective reminder with a conspicuous reminder. Prospective memory research suggests a more useful distinction: what matters is not merely whether a cue is visually striking, but whether normal activity directs attention towards the feature that carries the intention. Research comparing focal and non focal prospective memory cues illustrates this. A focal cue is processed naturally as part of what someone is already doing; a non focal cue requires attention to a feature that the ongoing activity would not otherwise emphasise. Experiments have found that focal cues can support relatively strong prospective remembering without the same degree of continual"},{"title":"Greyscale Phones","url":"/greyscale-phones/","description":"Making a phone screen less visually vivid can reduce measured screen time, although that alone does not guarantee better wellbeing or performance.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Design Around Temptation","level":3,"text":"Greyscale Phones Does Making Your Phone Boring Reduce Screen Time? Design Around Temptation Greyscale Phones Greyscale Phones Does Making Your Phone Boring Reduce Screen Time? Making a phone screen less visually vivid can reduce measured screen time, although that alone does not guarantee better wellbeing or performance. Making a phone screen less visually vivid can reduce measured screen time, although that alone does not guarantee better wellbeing or performance. Self Improvement That Works implementation single case policy intervention How greyscale changes the phone's visual environment What the preregistered screen time experiment found Why lower screen time is not the same as better wellbeing self improvement that works design around temptation greyscale phones Introduction Making a smartphone screen greyscale can reduce how long people use it. The strongest evidence comes from field experiments in which participants actually changed their phones rather than merely reporting what they thought they would do. A preregistered randomised experiment with 112 participants found that greyscale produced an immediate, significant reduction in objectively measured screen time, while a separate two week study found a reduction of about 20 minutes a day. Earlier research with university students reported reductions of roughly 38–40 minutes a day. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Digital Strategies for Screen Time Reduction: A Randomized Field Experiment PubMedDecember 26, 2022... Published: December 26, 2022 Explanatory illustration 1 The attraction is that greyscale changes the environment rather than demanding a fresh act of self control every time the phone comes out. Apps still work and nothing is blocked; the screen is simply less visually vivid. That makes greyscale a useful example of environmental design for self improvement: alter the cue or reward before the habitual behaviour unfolds. But the evidence also gives an important warning. Less screen time is an outcome in itself, not proof of better productivity, sleep or wellbeing. How greyscale changes the phone's visual environment Greyscale removes colour saturation while preserving the phone's basic functionality. Photographs, notification badges, games, social media feeds and app icons remain visible, but their colour differences disappear. Research on visual attention has long established that colour can affect where people direct their gaze; experiments comparing colour and greyscale versions of the same scenes have found substantial differences in fixation patterns. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov What's color got to do with it? The influence of color on visual attention in different categories PubMedOctober 23, 2008... Published: October 23, 2008 That provides a plausible mechanism for greyscale, but it should not be overstated. Researchers studying smartphone interventions generally describe greyscale as reducing visual salience or gratification, rather than demonstrating that a particular colour causes compulsive checking. In a 2026 crossover feasibility trial involving medical students, the researchers framed the intervention as reducing the visual salience of non essential stimuli while leaving useful smartphone functions available. Greyscale was associated with about 28 fewer minutes of screen time per day. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers A cross over feasibility trial of smartphone grayscale mode in medical studentsJuly 6, 2026... Published: July 6, 2026 This distinction matters. Greyscale is not an app blocker and does not impose a hard limit. A person can still spend an hour scrolling through a feed in black and white. Instead, it changes the small incentive surrounding the behaviour. The phone becomes a little less stimulating to look at, potentially making it easier to notice that an intended 30 second check has turned into aimless browsing. The pattern within earlier research is consistent with that interpretation. In a study of 161 undergraduates, 73 participants used greyscale for eight to ten days. Their total daily screen time fell substantially, with reductions particularly evident in social media and internet browsing use, while video screen time did not show the same effect. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. In other words, removing colour did not make every form of phone use disappear equally. It appears better suited to weakening some forms of visually driven, discretionary engagement than to preventing activities people have a specific reason to continue. Forget Apple's 'Screen Time' Controls Go Grayscale Channel: The Wall Street Journal Open on YouTube What the preregistered experiment found The particularly useful test is Laura Zimmermann and Michael Sobolev's preregistered randomised field experiment. It involved 112 participants and compared three approaches over several weeks: greyscale as a design friction intervention, self imposed time limits as a goal setting intervention, and self monitoring as the control. The groups were broadly similar at baseline, including in estimated daily screen time. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsRAPID COMMUNICATIONSApril 21, 2026... Published: April 21, 2026 The contrast between the interventions is important. Greyscale produced an immediate, significant reduction in objectively measured screen time relative to the control group . Setting time limits also reduced use, but its effect was smaller and developed more gradually. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsDigital Strategies for Screen Time Reduction: A Randomized Field Experiment Laura Zimmermann, Michael Sobolev, 2023... That makes the experiment unusually relevant to environment design . A time limit asks the user to formulate and follow a goal: I intend not to spend more than this much time on my phone. Greyscale changes the experience of using the device itself. Once enabled, it continues operating whether or not the user is consciously thinking about a screen time goal. Other studies point in the same broad direction, although their designs differ. Holte and Ferraro's university student study found a daily reduction of roughly 38–40 minutes among greyscale users. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. A German experimental study subsequently asked participants to use greyscale for at least 24 hours and also found substantially lower reported smartphone use duration, although only 92 of the initial 240 participants completed the second questionnaire, making it weaker evidence than the randomised field experiment. [DOI] doi.org Suffering from problematic smartphone use? Why not use grayscale setting as an intervention! – An experimental study ScienceDirectMa... More recently, a 2026 two period crossover study of 51 medical students compared each participant's experience under normal colour and greyscale conditions. Mean daily screen time was about 347 minutes in colour versus 319 minutes in greyscale, a difference of approximately 28 minutes. Because this was a small feasibility study with self reported adherence and retrospective reporting of phone recorded screen time figures, it is better treated as supportive evidence than as a definitive estimate of the effect. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers A cross over feasibility trial of smartphone grayscale mode in medical studentsJuly 6, 2026... Published: July 6, 2026 Taken together, these studies make a reasonably consistent case for a modest behavioural effect. They do not establish that everyone will save a predictable number of minutes. Samples have generally been fairly small and often student heavy, interventions have been short, and study designs and screen time measurements differ. The defensible conclusion is narrower: making the screen greyscale can reduce smartphone use, at least over the short periods studied. Explanatory illustration 2 Checking can survive even when screen time falls One result is especially revealing for habitual phone use. Cynthia Dekker and Susanne Baumgartner tracked 84 participants for two weeks, using the first week as baseline and the second as a greyscale intervention. Average daily screen time fell from about 287 minutes to 268 minutes — roughly 20 minutes — but the number of daily phone unlocks barely changed: approximately 80 before and 80 during greyscale. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. That separates two behaviours that are easily conflated. Greyscale may shorten what happens after someone unlocks the phone without necessarily stopping the initial checking impulse. A person can still reflexively reach for the device, unlock it and look; the diminished visual environment may simply make continued browsing less rewarding. For self improvement, this is a useful limitation rather than a reason to dismiss the technique. If the problem is unexpectedly losing 20 minutes after opening an app, reducing"},{"title":"Temptation Bundling","url":"/temptation-bundling-56f089/","description":"Restricting an enjoyable activity to exercise can make workouts more attractive, but the effect may weaken when routines are disrupted.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Design Around Temptation","level":3,"text":"Temptation Bundling Can You Make Exercise Easier to Want? Design Around Temptation Temptation Bundling Temptation Bundling Can You Make Exercise Easier to Want? Restricting an enjoyable activity to exercise can make workouts more attractive, but the effect may weaken when routines are disrupted. Restricting an enjoyable activity to exercise can make workouts more attractive, but the effect may weaken when routines are disrupted. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case policy intervention How temptation bundling changes immediate rewards What the gym audiobook experiment found Why disruptions can weaken the effect over time self improvement that works design around temptation temptation bundling Introduction Temptation bundling makes exercise easier to want by reserving something immediately enjoyable for the workout itself. Instead of relying only on distant benefits such as better health or fitness, the strategy gives exercise a reward available now: the next chapter of a gripping audiobook, for example. The crucial feature is the link between the two — ideally, the enjoyable activity is unavailable unless the exercise happens. Explanatory illustration 1 The best known experiment found a striking short term effect when researchers literally kept participants' tempting audiobooks at the gym. But it also revealed an important weakness: the advantage faded with time and was largely lost after a Thanksgiving closure interrupted the routine. A much larger later experiment found smaller but more durable gains under less restrictive, more realistic conditions. Together, the studies suggest that temptation bundling can help, but should be understood as a way of redesigning exercise's immediate reward rather than as a permanent cure for inconsistent motivation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 How temptation bundling changes the reward for exercise Exercise presents an awkward timing problem. Many of its strongest reasons — improved cardiovascular health, greater fitness or changes in body composition — arrive gradually. The costs are immediate: getting changed, travelling to the gym, exerting yourself and giving up time that could be spent on something more immediately pleasurable. Temptation bundling changes that timing without trying to make the delayed benefits arrive sooner. It attaches a \"want\" with immediate appeal to a \"should\" whose principal payoff lies in the future. Researchers describe this as a response to present bias , our tendency to give disproportionate weight to rewards and costs occurring now. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... That distinguishes a genuine temptation bundle from simply rewarding yourself occasionally. If an audiobook is available whenever you want it, it may make a workout more pleasant when you happen to exercise, but it provides little reason to start exercising. If the rule becomes \"I only listen to this series while exercising\", wanting the next chapter creates an additional reason to begin the next session. Exercise has effectively become the gateway to something desirable. There is broader evidence for the importance of this immediate reward mechanism. Research by Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach found that immediate rewards such as enjoyment were more strongly associated with persistence in long term goals than delayed rewards, even though people commonly reported pursuing activities such as exercise because of those delayed benefits. Other experiments found that directing people towards the more enjoyable exercise could increase persistence compared with concentrating on which option was most useful for their longer term goal. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 This gives temptation bundling a particularly useful role within environment design . The environment is not merely removing a temptation. It is relocating the temptation so that indulging in it supports the desired behaviour . Entertainment that might otherwise compete with a workout becomes part of the reason to do one. The Science of Why You Keep Failing Your Goals (And How to Fix It) Channel: Think Fast Talk Smart Open on YouTube What the gym audiobook experiment actually found The original field experiment by Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp, published in Management Science , turned this idea into an unusually literal commitment device. The study recruited 226 students, faculty and staff at a large north eastern US university in 2011. Participants already belonged to the university gym and typically exercised using stationary aerobic equipment. They were randomly assigned to three groups. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The full temptation bundling group selected several highly tempting audio novels. The audiobooks were loaded onto an iPod that researchers kept in a locked locker at the university gym. Participants could use it during exercise but could not take it home. In other words, the researchers did not merely advise them to save the stories for workouts: they engineered the environment so that continuing the story required returning to the gym. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 An intermediate group also received tempting audiobooks and was encouraged to restrict listening to exercise, but its members kept their own devices and therefore had to enforce the rule themselves. The control group received a Barnes & Noble gift card chosen to be perceived as similarly valuable, alongside reminders about the health benefits of exercise. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 At the beginning, the differences were substantial. After adjusting for relevant baseline measures, the full treatment group made an estimated 0.48 additional gym visits per week in the first week , a 51% increase relative to the control group's estimated 0.94 visits. The intermediate intervention produced an estimated 29% increase, although that result was weaker statistically. Across the seven weeks before Thanksgiving, participants made an average of 7.8 gym visits in the full treatment group, 6.5 in the intermediate group and 6.1 in the control group. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The comparison between the two treatment groups is particularly revealing. The strongest version did more than provide entertainment: it created an external restriction. Participants could not casually break their own rule on a quiet evening at home because the audiobook was physically inaccessible. The intermediate version depended on self enforcement and produced a smaller initial difference. That fits the wider environment design principle that changing access can be more dependable than repeatedly asking yourself to make the preferred choice. There was also evidence that people valued the restriction rather than merely tolerating it. At the end of the experiment, 61% chose to pay for gym only access to tempting audiobooks when offered the opportunity. In effect, many participants were willing to sacrifice unrestricted access to entertainment because they believed the restriction could help them exercise. [PubsOnLine] pubsonline.informs.org Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling Management ScienceNovember 6, 2013... Published: November 6, 2013 The study nevertheless has limits. It was small and drew from a university population already holding gym memberships and generally accustomed to stationary aerobic exercise. Gym attendance was recorded, but the researchers could not directly establish how much exercise participants completed during every recorded visit. The authors therefore treated the findings as an initial field demonstration rather than evidence that temptation bundling would work identically for everyone or every form of exercise. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central"},{"title":"Cue Placement","url":"/cue-placement/","description":"A cue placed at the start of a behaviour removes memory steps and makes immediate action more likely than a reminder seen somewhere else.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Cues","level":3,"text":"Cue Placement Where Should You Put a Behaviour Cue? Useful Cues Cue Placement Cue Placement Where Should You Put a Behaviour Cue? A cue placed at the start of a behaviour removes memory steps and makes immediate action more likely than a reminder seen somewhere else. A cue placed at the start of a behaviour removes memory steps and makes immediate action more likely than a reminder seen somewhere else. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why the action start location matters Examples for reading, walking, practice, and planning Reducing the gap between seeing and starting self improvement that works useful cues cue placement Introduction The best place for a behaviour cue is usually the point where the desired action can begin. Put the book on the chair where you will read it, the walking shoes beside the door you will leave through, the instrument at the place where you can pick it up and play, or the planning sheet on the desk where tomorrow’s plan will be made. The aim is not merely to remember the behaviour earlier in the day; it is to encounter the cue when the next useful response is immediately available. Explanatory illustration 1 Research does not establish a universal rule that every cue must sit literally beside the first object used. But several evidence streams converge on the same practical principle. Point of decision prompts work when people meet them at a behavioural choice, prospective memory research shows advantages when cues are closely tied to the activity in progress, and habit research finds that repeating actions in stable contexts strengthens automaticity . [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of decision prompts to increase stair use. A systematic review update PubMed... For self improvement, that means thinking less about where a reminder is easiest to display and more about where seeing it can turn directly into doing . Why the action start location matters A reminder can succeed at attracting attention while still fail at producing behaviour. Imagine seeing a note saying “walk tonight” on the bathroom mirror in the morning. The intention has been recalled, but walking cannot conveniently begin. Between the reminder and the walk lie hours of other activity, another transition, and another requirement to remember what was intended. A cue at the front door works differently. If the shoes, coat or walking prompt is encountered as you reach the point where leaving is possible, remembering and beginning are brought together. This is the distinction that matters: a memory cue can make an intention come to mind, whereas an action start cue is encountered when the response can actually be initiated. Prospective memory research helps explain why reducing this separation is useful. Prospective memory is the ability to remember to carry out an intended action later. Doing that involves maintaining an intention, detecting the relevant opportunity and initiating the response. Research shows that monitoring for future action cues can itself consume attention, while cues that fit what a person is already attending to can support less effortful retrieval. In experiments, strategic monitoring has been particularly effective when ongoing activity naturally directs attention towards the relevant contextual information. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Cue Focality Influences Strategic Prospective Memory Monitoring PMC... This does not prove that a book on an armchair will outperform a book elsewhere by a particular percentage. Laboratory prospective memory tasks are not identical to home habit design. They do, however, support a useful design principle: make the intended action easier to retrieve from the situation in which it belongs rather than requiring continuous mental monitoring for it . Implementation intention research reaches a related conclusion from another direction. An implementation intention specifies the situation in which a response should occur — effectively linking an “if” cue to a “then” action. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, and later work indicated that the mechanism involves both increased accessibility of the specified cue and a stronger cue response association. A much larger 2024 review, covering 642 independent tests, continued to find beneficial effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes and found larger effects for contingent if then plans. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... Physical cue placement can be understood as an environmental version of the same logic. Instead of repeatedly thinking, “When I reach the desk, I should practise”, the practice materials waiting on that desk can help make the situation itself carry part of the instruction. Notes in the Margins: Cue Based Planning Channel: Karen McGregor Open on YouTube The clearest real world model: prompt at the decision Stair use research provides an unusually concrete demonstration because researchers can place a prompt at a particular location and then observe what people actually do. Point of decision prompts are signs positioned near stairs, lifts or escalators where people choose how to move between floors. A Community Guide systematic review update covering 16 qualifying peer reviewed studies concluded that there was strong evidence that such prompts increased stair use. The intervention is explicitly defined by its placement at or near the behavioural decision: people encounter the message when taking the stairs is an available response. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of decision prompts to increase stair use. A systematic review update PubMed... That timing is important. A poster about physical activity in a distant corridor might improve awareness, but it leaves the reader to remember the message at the later choice. A sign beside the lift can convert the same broad intention — “I ought to move more” — into a present opportunity: the stairs are here; I can take them now . The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention describes these signs as reminders about an immediate opportunity to be physically active. [CDC] cdc.gov encouraging physical activity Strategies for Prompts to Encourage Physical Activity Physical Activity CDCJanuary 27, 2025... Published: January 27, 2025 Message specificity can sharpen that effect. In a university worksite study, the specific message condition produced about twice the odds of stair use compared with baseline (OR 2.04, 95% CI 1.46–2.84), and significantly greater odds than a general message condition (OR 1.57, 95% CI 1.13–2.20). Both signs were presented in the decision point area near the stairs and lift; only the specific message significantly increased stair use over baseline. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The results indicate that a specific message may be more effective at promoting stair use.Read more... There are limits to what this evidence can establish. Stair studies compare prompts with no prompts or different messages much more often than they experimentally compare the same cue at several distances from the action. Indeed, the Community Guide review identified the possible “critical distance” between stairs and lifts as a question still requiring research. [CDC Stacks] stacks.cdc.gov Stacks Point of Decision Prompts to Increase Stair Use Stacks Point of Decision Prompts to Increase Stair Use It would therefore be too strong to claim that behavioural science has identified an exact optimal number of centimetres or seconds between cue and action. The better supported conclusion is narrower: prompts can influence behaviour when they are encountered at the relevant choice, and this provides a strong model for personal cue design. Explanatory illustration 2 What this looks like in everyday self improvement The practical test is simple: after encountering the cue, what is the first physical action required, and can it happen here? A well placed cue makes the answer obvious. Reading: Instead of putting “read more” on a general to do list, leave the chosen book at the place where reading normally starts — for example, on the armchair, bedside table or commuting bag. An open book or visible bookmark can make the next response even more specific: sit down and read the next page. Walking: Put walking shoes, coat or another distinctive walking cue at the exit you actually use. The important location is not simply “somewhere visible in the house”; it is the transition point at which putting on the shoes and leaving can follow immediately. This mirrors point of decision prompting: the cue arrives as the behavioural choice becomes available. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of decision prompts to increase stair use. A systematic review update PubMed... Practice: Place the instrument, sketchbook,"},{"title":"Reminder Overload","url":"/reminder-overload/","description":"Adding more reminders is not always better because prompts that are vague, poorly placed, or constantly visible can become easy to overlook.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Cues","level":3,"text":"Reminder Overload When Do Environmental Reminders Stop Getting Noticed? Useful Cues Reminder Overload Reminder Overload When Do Environmental Reminders Stop Getting Noticed? Adding more reminders is not always better because prompts that are vague, poorly placed, or constantly visible can become easy to overlook. Adding more reminders is not always better because prompts that are vague, poorly placed, or constantly visible can become easy to overlook. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism Why noticeability depends on contrast and relevance How vague or badly timed prompts lose practical value Using fewer cues with clearer actions and locations self improvement that works useful cues reminder overload Introduction Repeated reminders can stop working precisely because they remain present. A note on the fridge, a poster above a desk or the same phone alert every afternoon may initially interrupt routine and bring an intention to mind. With repeated exposure, however, familiar stimuli tend to capture less attention. Research on habituation describes this as a reduction in responding to repeated stimulation, while field studies of behavioural prompts show that an intervention can produce a strong initial effect that later decays. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Novelty, attention, and challenges for developmental psychology PMCAugust 1, 2013... Published: August 1, 2013 Explanatory illustration 1 The practical lesson is not that reminders are useless. Well placed prompts can change behaviour, sometimes substantially. The problem is treating visibility as equivalent to noticeability. A useful environmental cue needs to stand out, appear when action is possible and point towards something sufficiently specific to do. Adding more permanent reminders can instead create a landscape of familiar signals that the brain learns it can safely ignore. Why contrast and relevance determine what gets noticed Attention is selective. If every object in view received the same continuing scrutiny, ordinary environments would be overwhelming. Habituation provides one way of filtering predictable information: responses to a repeatedly encountered stimulus decline, while a sufficiently different or novel stimulus can recover attention. Research on visual attention has found that attentional capture itself can habituate, and that the filtering of familiar stimuli can become tied to the context in which they repeatedly appear. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Short term and long term plasticity in the visual attention system: Evidence from habituation of attentional capture PubMed... That helps explain the familiar experience of no longer really seeing a reminder that remains in the same place. A sticky note reading “READ” may be conspicuous when first attached to a monitor. After dozens of encounters in which nothing consequential happens, it becomes part of the monitor. The eyes may still receive the visual information, but the cue is less successful at interrupting whatever else is occupying attention. Real world prompt research shows both sides of this effect. A meta analysis of 50 studies of signs encouraging stair use found a median absolute increase of 2.2 percentage points after signage was introduced and an overall adjusted odds ratio of 1.52 for stair use. In other words, prompts clearly can influence behaviour. [BMJ Open] bmjopen.bmj.com BMJ OpenIs there sufficient evidence regarding signage based stair use interventions? A sequential meta analysis BMJ OpenNovember 1, 2017... Published: November 1, 2017 But effectiveness at introduction does not establish that an unchanged cue will retain the same force indefinitely. A particularly revealing field experiment followed 205 people in three Paris Metro stations during an eight week stair promotion intervention. Point of decision prompts produced an immediate effect on stair use, but that effect peaked and decayed. The researchers found no evidence that the intervention produced a lasting change in stair use habits and suggested that people eventually became accustomed to the cues and ceased noticing them sufficiently to influence the decision. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Getting more people on the stairs: The impact of point of decision prompts PubMed... That distinction matters for self improvement. Repetition of the desired behaviour can strengthen a routine; repetition of an ignored reminder can simply make the reminder familiar. Seeing “stretch” 40 times is not equivalent to stretching 40 times. 39. Habituation in Psychology – Why We Stop Noticing Things Channel: MedStudyHub Open on YouTube Vague prompts lose practical value Noticeability is only part of the problem. Even a reminder that successfully attracts attention may be weak if it does not make the next action obvious. A university worksite study illustrates the difference. Researchers recorded 2,997 stair or lift choices while comparing a general stair promotion message with a more specific one. The specific sign significantly increased the odds of stair use relative to baseline, with an odds ratio of 2.04, and also performed significantly better than the general message. The general sign alone did not significantly increase stair use. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsPoint of Decision Signs and Stair Use in a University Worksite Setting: General versus Specific Messages Mary R. Eckhardt... Research on prospective memory — remembering to carry out an intention later — likewise suggests that cue specificity can matter. A 2023 experimental study found prospective memory accuracy consistently favoured specific cues over general ones. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A thorough examination of cue specificity and task appropriateness in defining focal and nonfocal prospective memory tasks PubMed... Implementation intention research takes the same basic problem seriously by connecting an identifiable situation to a defined response: when a specified situation occurs, perform the specified behaviour. Research has found that such plans can improve detection of opportunities to act. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. For an environmental reminder, therefore, compare: “Be healthier” on the fridge with fruit already washed and visible at the point where a snack is chosen. “ Exercise !” on a bedroom wall with walking shoes beside the door used for the planned lunchtime walk. “Read more” permanently on the desk with the intended book placed on the pillow before the evening routine. “Plan tomorrow” on a general to do list with the planning notebook open beside the object that reliably marks the end of the working day. The stronger examples reduce interpretation. They make the cue and the opportunity to act overlap. This does not mean every prompt needs lengthy instructions. Quite the opposite: specificity often means narrowing the behaviour. “Do ten squats after brushing my teeth” gives the cue one job. “Improve fitness” asks a reminder to reactivate an entire goal, choose an activity, decide when to perform it and overcome whatever friction follows. Explanatory illustration 2 Bad timing turns reminders into dismissals A reminder can also fail because it appears when the requested behaviour cannot sensibly happen. A prompt to practise guitar during a commute may successfully remind someone of the goal but cannot initiate the behaviour. By the time the person reaches the guitar, the prompt is gone and another context has taken over. This is why point of decision prompts are an instructive model. Signs encouraging stair use are placed where people actually choose between stairs and a lift or escalator. A systematic review covering 16 qualifying studies found strong evidence that these prompts increased stair use. Current US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention guidance similarly describes them as prompts delivered in specific places where an opportunity to be active is immediately available. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Timing affects digital reminders too. In a 28 day mobile health study, 64 adults pursued goals such as walking more, eating vegetables or eating more slowly while researchers manipulated reminder schedules. Reminders improved daily goal achievement overall, but their effects varied with their frequency and distribution; success was also related to how recently a reminder had been acknowledged. The study supports the broader point that reminders have a schedule and a context, rather than functioning as an unlimited dose of motivation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. More generally, repeated prompts can become costly when they repeatedly demand attention without supplying useful new information. Evidence from clinical decision support systems offers an unusually clear demonstration. In a study of 112 primary care clinicians, the likelihood of accepting a reminder fell by about 30% for each additional"},{"title":"Specific Prompts","url":"/specific-prompts/","description":"Specific prompts can outperform vague encouragement when they appear exactly where a person can choose and begin the intended action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Cues","level":3,"text":"Specific Prompts Do Specific Reminders Work Better Than General Ones? Useful Cues Specific Prompts Specific Prompts Do Specific Reminders Work Better Than General Ones? Specific prompts can outperform vague encouragement when they appear exactly where a person can choose and begin the intended action. Specific prompts can outperform vague encouragement when they appear exactly where a person can choose and begin the intended action. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic policy intervention What makes a prompt specific enough to act on What stair use studies found about message wording Turning vague reminders into clear environmental instructions self improvement that works useful cues specific prompts Introduction A reminder is most useful when it does more than say that a goal matters. At a decision point, a specific prompt can identify the action to take, make its benefit concrete and appear while the action is still possible . Research on stair use provides an unusually clear test of this distinction. In one university building, a specific message about the calories burned by taking the stairs increased stair use, while a general encouragement to “burn calories” and “get healthy” did not. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of Decision Signs and Stair Use in a University Worksite Setting: General Versus Specific Messages PubMedMarch 26, 2014... Published: March 26, 2014 Explanatory illustration 1 But specificity is not simply a licence to add more words. Other field research shows that a detailed message can lose its advantage when people are moving quickly and have little time to read. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Prompts to Increase Stair Climbing in Stations: The Effect of Message Complexity Request PDFSeptember 1, 2012... Published: September 1, 2012 The practical lesson for self improvement is therefore narrower and more useful: put a brief, concrete instruction where the relevant choice occurs, and make the next action immediately obvious. What makes a prompt specific enough to act on? A general reminder keeps a broad goal mentally available: “be healthier”, “ exercise more” or “remember to study”. A specific environmental prompt reduces the distance between that goal and an available behaviour: “take the stairs”, “open the practice questions” or “put your phone here before bed”. The distinction matters because a decision point is a short window. When someone reaches a lift and staircase, for example, they do not need a general explanation that physical activity is desirable. They need information capable of influencing the choice available now . This is the logic of a point of decision, or point of choice, prompt: place the cue near the competing options rather than somewhere the person cannot immediately act on it. The Community Preventive Services Task Force's review of 16 qualifying studies concluded that there was strong evidence that such prompts increase stair use, although the absolute changes were generally modest. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of decision prompts to increase stair use. A systematic review update PubMed... Placement can be as important as persuasive content. In a study across four university buildings, researchers recorded 14,138 choices between stairs and lifts. Putting a motivational intervention inside the lifts produced no significant increase in stair climbing. When a point of choice prompt was added where people selected their method of ascent, stair use increased significantly, with an odds ratio of 1.23. The researchers concluded that visibility when the behavioural choice is actually being made appeared necessary for changing behaviour in this setting. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubPrompt before the choice is made: Effects of a stair‐climbing intervention in university buildings Lewis 2012 British Jo... This gives “specific” two related meanings. Behavioural specificity tells people what they can do: take the stairs rather than the lift. Outcome specificity makes the reason concrete: how much activity, energy expenditure or another relevant benefit is associated with that choice. Research supports both ideas, but also shows that more detailed information is not automatically more effective. What Is Implementation Intention For Building Habits? The Time Management Pro Channel: The Time Management Pro Open on YouTube What stair use studies found about message wording The most direct comparison comes from a university worksite study by Mary Eckhardt, Jacqueline Kerr and Wendell Taylor. Researchers observed 2,997 stair or lift choices in an eight storey building across a two week baseline period, two weeks with a general message and two weeks with a specific message. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Point of Decision Signs and Stair Use in a University Worksite Setting: General Versus Specific Messages PubMedMarch 26, 2014... Published: March 26, 2014 The contrast was unusually clean. The general prompt said “Burn calories. Get healthy” . The specific version told passers by that walking upstairs burns almost five times as many calories as riding a lift. Compared with baseline, the general message did not produce a statistically significant change: its odds ratio was 1.13, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.84 to 1.52. The specific message produced significantly greater odds of stair use than baseline, at 2.04, and also outperformed the general message directly, at 1.57. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That result fits earlier work by Oliver Webb and Frank Eves. In structured interviews with 1,200 people, they compared relatively general descriptions of stair climbing with messages describing specific consequences. Participants found consequence focused messages more persuasive. The researchers also found evidence that validating the information presented could increase its persuasive appeal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Promoting stair climbing: effects of message specificity and validation Promoting stair climbing: effects of message specificity and validation PubMedFebruary 1, 2007... Published: February 1, 2007 There is an important qualification, however. “More specific” can also mean “longer and harder to process”. Amanda Lewis and Eves tested this in a UK railway station with 48,697 observed stair or escalator choices. One message stated that regular stair climbing protects the heart; the more specific version added a target of seven minutes per day. Both increased stair climbing after pedestrian traffic was taken into account: the estimated odds ratios were 1.07 for the simpler message and 1.10 for the more complex one. There was no straightforward victory for added detail. Crucially, the complex message became less effective during busier periods, whereas the simple message was not similarly impaired by traffic volume. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Prompts to Increase Stair Climbing in Stations: The Effect of Message Complexity Request PDFSeptember 1, 2012... Published: September 1, 2012 So the evidence does not support a rule that every prompt should contain a number, explanation or detailed benefit. It supports a more discriminating principle: specificity helps when it makes the relevant action or consequence clearer without demanding more attention than the situation allows . Explanatory illustration 2 Specificity works best when it reduces the decision Environmental prompts have limited time to do their job. In a quiet workplace, someone may have enough attention to process a sentence about the consequences of taking the stairs. A commuter leaving a crowded train may have only seconds. The railway station findings are therefore especially useful because they expose a trade off that is easy to miss: informational precision and cognitive simplicity are not always the same thing. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Prompts to Increase Stair Climbing in Stations: The Effect of Message Complexity Request PDFSeptember 1, 2012... Published: September 1, 2012 That trade off also explains why directional information can be valuable. A person may already be willing to take the stairs but not know where they are. A prompt saying where the stairs are removes an immediate practical uncertainty instead of attempting to increase motivation. A systematic review of stair use interventions reported that combining motivational and directional signs in worksites increased stair climbing in 83% of the studies examining that approach, although the review also cautioned that evidence for worksite interventions overall was more limited than evidence from public settings and that external validity was often poorly reported. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Stair use interventions in worksites and public settings Pub Med Stair use interventions in worksites and public settings Public health practice reflects the same concern with actionability. The US Centers for Disease Control and"},{"title":"Cue Failure","url":"/cue-failure/","description":"If the behaviour never comes to mind, the cue may be too vague, unstable or poorly placed to trigger action consistently.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fix the System","level":3,"text":"Cue Failure Why Your Habit Cue Keeps Failing Fix the System Cue Failure Cue Failure Why Your Habit Cue Keeps Failing If the behaviour never comes to mind, the cue may be too vague, unstable or poorly placed to trigger action consistently. If the behaviour never comes to mind, the cue may be too vague, unstable or poorly placed to trigger action consistently. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Signs that the cue is the real bottleneck How context stability affects automaticity Replacing vague cues with reliable events self improvement that works fix the system cue failure Introduction When a planned habit repeatedly fails because the behaviour simply does not come to mind, the cue is a prime suspect. A useful cue is not merely a reminder written into a plan; it is a recognisable event or context that occurs reliably enough for the brain to learn an association between that situation and the intended response. Habit research describes this process as repeated behaviour becoming linked to recurring contextual cues. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... Explanatory illustration 1 The practical implication is important: forgetting does not necessarily mean the goal needs more motivation. The cue may be too vague, may occur inconsistently, may be easy to overlook, or may have become detached from the context in which the habit was learned. Research on intentional habit building finds that stable contexts are associated with greater automaticity and better repetition of the intended behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Troubleshooting cue failure therefore means asking a narrow question: does the intended trigger reliably appear, get noticed and bring the behaviour to mind? If not, strengthening commitment while leaving the trigger unchanged addresses the wrong part of the system. Signs that the cue is the real bottleneck The clearest sign of cue failure is discovering the missed behaviour later and thinking, “I completely forgot.” That is different from noticing the opportunity and deciding not to act. If “read after breakfast” comes to mind but you choose to check messages instead, the cue has done at least part of its job. If reading never enters awareness until lunchtime, cueing is a more plausible bottleneck. This distinction follows from the way habits are understood in behavioural research. Habits are not simply behaviours performed frequently; they involve learned associations through which contextual features can activate a response. Experiments and daily life research have found that performance contexts can trigger strong habitual responses even when people's current goals are not doing the triggering. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... For troubleshooting purposes, several patterns particularly implicate the cue: The behaviour is remembered after the opportunity has passed. You remember the vitamins on the train rather than at breakfast, or remember your stretches after getting into bed. The habit works only on unusually orderly days. A cue such as “in the morning” may accidentally work when mornings follow one predictable sequence but disappear when that sequence changes. You depend on remembering to look for the reminder. A note hidden in an app or an alarm routinely dismissed during another activity is technically present but functionally weak. The trigger itself varies. “After lunch” is unreliable if lunch happens at a desk one day, in a café the next and not at all on another. A change of setting suddenly breaks an established routine. Moving house, changing jobs, travelling or rearranging the normal sequence of a day can remove cues that previously prompted behaviour without much thought. That last pattern has direct experimental support. In research following university students through a change of university, established behaviours such as exercise , newspaper reading and television viewing were disrupted when relevant features of their performance contexts changed. Where important contextual features remained similar, habitual behaviour was more likely to persist. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... This makes context change a useful diagnostic test. If a routine once felt almost effortless but suddenly requires active remembering after a change in schedule or location, the loss of its old cues may explain more than a sudden loss of self discipline. The Power of Habit: Why We Do What We Do in Life and Business Channel: Microsoft Research Open on YouTube How context stability affects automaticity Habit automaticity develops through repeated pairing. A context is encountered, a behaviour follows, and repeated experience strengthens the association between them. Eventually, encountering relevant contextual features can help bring the response to mind with less deliberate decision making. Reviews of habit research therefore emphasise repetition in recurring contexts rather than repetition alone. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMed... A particularly relevant longitudinal study tested this principle directly. Marco Stojanovic and colleagues followed intentional habit building using two datasets. In one study, 95 university students generated 2,482 recorded study habit repetitions over six weeks; participants were instructed either to keep their time and location stable or deliberately vary them. A second dataset covered 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions from 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and higher attainment of habit repetition goals. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The result does not mean every habit must happen at precisely the same minute in precisely the same chair. “Context” can include location, time, preceding actions and other recurring features of the situation. Habit theory specifically recognises physical settings and preceding actions as features from which context response associations can develop. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... What matters for cue troubleshooting is recognisable recurrence . Consider the difference between: Weakly specified: “I will practise Spanish in the evening.” More stable: “When I put my dinner plate in the dishwasher, I will practise Spanish.” The first requires the person to decide when “evening” has reached the appropriate moment and to retrieve the intention while doing something else. The second attaches the behaviour to an observable transition already occurring in the day. There is an important qualification. A routine based cue is not automatically superior to a clock time. In a randomised controlled trial involving 192 adults who selected an everyday nutrition behaviour, participants linked the behaviour either to a daily routine or to a specific time and reported on it for 84 days. Both approaches produced increases in plan enactment and automaticity, with no significant group level advantage for one cue type. What strongly predicted automaticity was repeatedly performing the behaviour in response to the chosen cue. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 So the useful rule is not “never use time cues”. It is choose whichever cue you can encounter and act upon consistently . Seven o'clock can be excellent for someone whose day reliably permits action at seven; it can be a poor trigger for someone who is variously commuting, cooking, working late or exercising at that hour. Explanatory illustration 2 Why a cue can stop working A cue can fail even after it once worked because the cue response relationship depends on the circumstances in which it was learned. Modern habit research characterises habit memories as context response associations formed through repeated, rewarding action in stable contexts. Altering those contexts can reduce the capacity of familiar stimuli to activate the usual response. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Open source on psychologicalscience.org. That produces several distinct forms of apparent “forgetfulness”. The cue becomes unstable. A person starts"},{"title":"Better Choices","url":"/better-choices/","description":"When several activities offer similar long-term benefits, choosing the one you actually enjoy can make persistence easier.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Immediate Rewards","level":3,"text":"Better Choices Pick the Habit Version You Actually Enjoy Immediate Rewards Better Choices Better Choices Pick the Habit Version You Actually Enjoy When several activities offer similar long term benefits, choosing the one you actually enjoy can make persistence easier. When several activities offer similar long term benefits, choosing the one you actually enjoy can make persistence easier. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Enjoyment versus instrumental usefulness Examples in exercise, study and food How to choose between equally useful options self improvement that works immediate rewards better choices Introduction When several habits can deliver roughly the same long term benefit, the version you genuinely enjoy is often the better choice. The reason is practical rather than indulgent: enjoyment gives the behaviour an immediate payoff, while benefits such as better health, stronger knowledge or improved nutrition may take months or years to become visible. Research on long term goals has found that immediate rewards such as enjoyment predict persistence more strongly than delayed rewards, even though people commonly say the delayed benefit is their main reason for pursuing the goal. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 This does not mean that the most enjoyable activity is automatically the most effective. The useful rule is narrower: first identify options that genuinely serve the goal; then, where several are good enough, favour the one you look forward to doing. Exercise , study and food all offer room for this kind of choice. The aim is not to make self improvement effortless. It is to stop treating unnecessary unpleasantness as evidence that a habit is working. Enjoyment and usefulness are different tests A useful habit has to pass two tests. Instrumental usefulness asks whether the behaviour actually advances the goal. Immediate enjoyment asks whether there is something rewarding about doing it now. A strong habit can satisfy both. That distinction matters because people can overvalue either side. Choosing purely for long term usefulness may produce an impressive routine that is repeatedly avoided. Choosing purely for enjoyment may produce an activity that feels good but does little for the intended outcome. For example, an enjoyable but extremely easy workout may not provide the training stimulus required for a particular fitness goal; similarly, a pleasant hour rereading familiar notes is not necessarily equivalent to a more demanding hour of retrieval practice. The research on immediate rewards makes the case for considering enjoyment, not for ignoring effectiveness. Woolley and Fishbach found that people tended to cite delayed rewards as their reason for pursuing exercise and study, yet immediate rewards were more closely associated with persistence within sessions and across longer periods. A useful interpretation is that the delayed benefit helps answer “Is this goal worth having?” , while the immediate experience helps answer “Will I keep doing the work?” [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 This suggests a better optimisation problem than simply looking for the theoretically “best” habit. Establish a threshold of usefulness first. Once several options clear that threshold, enjoyment becomes a legitimate decision criterion because adherence affects the amount of useful behaviour actually completed. A slightly less optimised routine performed consistently can outperform a theoretically superior one that exists mostly on a timetable. How to become 37.78 times better at anything Atomic Habits summary (by James Clear) Channel: Escaping Ordinary (B.C Marx) Open on YouTube Exercise makes the choice especially visible Exercise is an unusually clear example because there are many legitimate ways to be active. World Health Organization guidance counts physical activity undertaken through leisure, transport, work and household activities, and notes that different types and domains of activity can provide health benefits. Adults can accumulate aerobic activity through options as varied as brisk walking, cycling, running, sport and other forms of movement, while muscle strengthening activity adds benefits of its own. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov WHO Guidelines on Physical Activity and Sedentary Behaviour NCBI Bookshelf... That creates room for preference. Someone who dislikes running does not generally need to decide between running and inactivity. Depending on the goal, brisk walking, cycling, swimming, dancing or a sport may offer a workable route to regular aerobic activity. The UK Chief Medical Officers' guidance makes the implementation point unusually explicit: lasting change is more likely when activity is enjoyable, useful or incorporated into everyday routines, and it identifies walking, cycling, wheeling, sport, dancing and running as valuable forms of activity. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UKJuly 10, 2026... Published: July 10, 2026 The behavioural evidence supports taking those preferences seriously. A meta analysis of 114 independent adult samples found a positive association between affective judgements — constructs including enjoyment, affective attitude and intrinsic motivation — and physical activity, with a summary correlation of 0.42. A later systematic review found that a more positive affective response during moderate intensity exercise was linked to subsequent physical activity. These findings are not proof that simply selecting your favourite exercise will guarantee adherence, but they make the experience of exercising relevant rather than cosmetic. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A review and meta analysis of affective judgments and physical activity in adult populations PubMed... There is also no universal winner on enjoyment. A 2021 review, for example, found that moderate continuous exercise produced a more positive post exercise affective response than high intensity interval training (HIIT), while HIIT scored higher on enjoyment. That apparent contradiction is useful: people may enjoy different features of an activity — comfort, variety, intensity, challenge or time efficiency — and a population average cannot tell an individual which form will feel most sustainable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Exercisers' Affective and Enjoyment Responses: A Meta Analytic and Meta Regression Review PubMedJune 10, 2021... Published: June 10, 2021 So the practical comparison is not “Which exercise is scientifically best?” in the abstract. It is “Which options meet the demands of my goal, and which of those would I willingly repeat?” If cardiovascular fitness is the target, compare credible aerobic options. If strength is the target, compare forms of resistance training. Do not substitute an enjoyable activity that misses the goal entirely; choose enjoyably within the set that serves it . Explanatory illustration 2 Study methods need a usefulness floor Studying requires a little more caution because methods that feel easiest can also create an illusion of learning. Reviews of learning science consistently support retrieval practice — actively trying to recall material — and spaced practice, while passive strategies such as repeated rereading and highlighting tend to have weaker or less consistent evidence. A 2022 review describes spacing and retrieval practice as well supported ways to enhance learning across domains and ages, and a 2025 systematic review likewise found retrieval practice consistently more beneficial than simple restudy. [Nature] nature.com Open source on nature.com. “Choose what you enjoy” therefore should not become permission to replace effective study with whatever feels most comfortable. Instead, preserve the effective mechanism and make choices inside it . A student who needs retrieval practice, for instance, could choose among flashcards, self written quizzes, answering questions with a friend, practising past paper questions or trying to explain material from memory before checking the source. Spacing can likewise be implemented through different schedules and tools. The enjoyable variable might be the format, subject order, location, social setting or type of challenge rather than the underlying learning principle. Research on motivation regulation supports this idea of modifying a task to increase interest. Interest enhancement can involve changing how an activity is"},{"title":"Enjoyment","url":"/enjoyment/","description":"People may choose goals for future benefits, yet immediate enjoyment often better predicts how long they keep going.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Immediate Rewards","level":3,"text":"Enjoyment Why Enjoyment Often Keeps Good Habits Going Immediate Rewards Enjoyment Enjoyment Why Enjoyment Often Keeps Good Habits Going People may choose goals for future benefits, yet immediate enjoyment often better predicts how long they keep going. People may choose goals for future benefits, yet immediate enjoyment often better predicts how long they keep going. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What persistence studies found Why future benefits still matter Designing habits around immediate enjoyment self improvement that works immediate rewards enjoyment Introduction People often choose long term goals because of what those goals promise later: better health, stronger qualifications, financial security or some other future improvement. Yet when researchers look at what predicts whether people actually keep doing the necessary activity, a different factor repeatedly stands out: how rewarding the activity feels while it is happening . Explanatory illustration 1 The clearest evidence comes from a five study programme by psychologists Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach. Across New Year's resolutions, studying, exercise and healthy eating, immediate rewards such as enjoyment, interest, fun and taste were more strongly associated with persistence than delayed rewards such as usefulness, importance and future health. Their pooled analysis estimated a substantially larger relationship between immediate rewards and persistence than between delayed rewards and persistence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals PubMed... This does not mean future benefits are irrelevant. Rather, the research points to a useful division of labour: future benefits can give people a reason to choose a goal; immediate enjoyment can help them keep doing it. How to Set the Right Goals and Stay Motivated Ayelet Fishbach TED Channel: TED Open on YouTube What persistence studies found Woolley and Fishbach deliberately tested the same basic prediction in several settings and across different timescales. Their central question was not simply whether people liked enjoyable activities. It was whether immediate rewards predicted actual persistence after accounting for how valuable people considered the delayed rewards. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley The New Year's resolution study provides a particularly intuitive example. In January, the researchers recorded resolutions from participants recruited online. Two months later, 96 participants completed a follow up survey. They rated the immediate experience of pursuing their resolution — whether it was enjoyable, engaging and positive — as well as delayed benefits such as whether it was important, useful or potentially life changing. They then reported how successfully they had stuck to the resolution. Immediate rewards significantly predicted persistence to that point; delayed rewards did not. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley That distinction became more striking when the researchers examined what participants expected to do in the future. Delayed rewards did help predict expectations of future persistence. In other words, people could look ahead and reasonably believe that an important goal would keep motivating them. Their behaviour up to that point, however, was more closely associated with how rewarding pursuing the resolution actually felt. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley Studying showed a similar pattern. Researchers approached 79 undergraduate and postgraduate students working in a university library, with 68 subsequently reporting when they stopped studying. Students were much more likely to say that they came to the library to work on important material than enjoyable material — 95% versus 60% in a separate measure reported in the study. Yet enjoyment of the material was the stronger predictor of how long they actually continued working. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley Exercise produced an especially concrete behavioural measure. Eighty gym goers rated how important it was that their workout be enjoyable and fun and how important it was that it be useful for keeping them in shape. The researchers then recorded how long they exercised on their chosen cardio machine. Participants actually rated the delayed benefit more highly on average: usefulness for keeping in shape scored 4.91 on the study's scale, compared with 3.93 for having fun. Nevertheless, valuing immediate enjoyment predicted workout duration, while valuing the delayed benefit did not. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley The result was not confined to one off sessions. In another study, 85 museum visitors reported how fun they found exercise and how important they considered it, alongside their typical weekly exercise over the previous three months. Fun predicted hours exercised, whereas perceived importance did not. The same participants rated the taste and health importance of green vegetables; taste was associated with how many meals containing green vegetables they had eaten over the previous week, while perceived health importance was not significantly associated after accounting for taste. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley That finding is particularly revealing because participants plainly understood the long term purpose of these behaviours. More than 91% said they exercised primarily for delayed benefits, and roughly 83% said the same about eating green vegetables. The behaviour could therefore be chosen for its future payoff while its continuation was more closely associated with its present experience . [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley The science of getting motivated Ayelet Fishbach TEDxChicago Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube The pooled result is stronger than any single study Individual behavioural studies of this size can produce noisy estimates, so the most useful result is the authors' random effects meta analysis across nine regression estimates drawn from the five studies and pilot data. It compared the associations between persistence and immediate versus delayed rewards while statistically controlling each type of reward for the other. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley The pooled standardised regression coefficient for immediate rewards was β = 0.35 (95% confidence interval 0.28–0.42). Delayed rewards also had a statistically significant pooled association, but it was much smaller: β = 0.09 (95% confidence interval 0.02–0.16). The difference between the two predictors was statistically significant. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley That nuance matters. A simplified retelling might say that long term benefits do not motivate people, but the evidence does not support that conclusion. In the individual studies, delayed rewards generally failed to predict persistence significantly once immediate rewards were considered. When evidence was pooled, however, delayed rewards did have a small positive relationship with persistence. The result is therefore about relative predictive strength , not the complete irrelevance of future consequences. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley Nor should these results be read as proof that enjoyment causes all of the observed differences. Much of this particular research programme measured naturally occurring differences in enjoyment and persistence rather than randomly assigning people to enjoy an activity more or less. The authors themselves considered alternative explanations, including the possibility that people who value positive experiences choose different activities. [Kaitlin Woolley] kaitlinwoolley.com Kaitlin Woolley There is, however, complementary experimental evidence. In separate research, Woolley and Fishbach found that directing people towards the most enjoyable rather than the most useful exercise, focusing attention on immediate rather than delayed rewards, and adding an immediate positive element such as music could increase persistence in health or academic activities. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicFor the Fun of It: Harnessing Immediate Rewards to Increase Persistence in Long Term Goals Journal of Consumer Research O... A later series of experiments also found that receiving rewards sooner rather than later could increase intrinsic motivation — the extent to which the activity itself feels interesting or enjoyable — even when reward magnitude was considered separately. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 Explanatory illustration 2 Why future benefits still matter The studies reveal a gap between choosing a goal and executing it repeatedly . Delayed"},{"title":"Reward Pitfalls","url":"/reward-pitfalls/","description":"Points, treats and financial incentives can help, but poor reward design may become costly, distracting or incompatible with the goal.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Immediate Rewards","level":3,"text":"Reward Pitfalls When Habit Rewards Start Working Against You Immediate Rewards Reward Pitfalls Reward Pitfalls When Habit Rewards Start Working Against You Points, treats and financial incentives can help, but poor reward design may become costly, distracting or incompatible with the goal. Points, treats and financial incentives can help, but poor reward design may become costly, distracting or incompatible with the goal. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism When external rewards are useful Common ways rewards create competing costs How to keep incentives compatible with the goal self improvement that works immediate rewards reward pitfalls Introduction External rewards can make a delayed benefit habit easier to repeat, but they become counterproductive when the incentive starts competing with the reason for doing the habit. A reward can cost more than the behaviour is worth, make an already enjoyable activity feel like paid work, encourage people to optimise the rewarded metric rather than the real goal, or create a routine that collapses as soon as the payments, points or treats disappear. Explanatory illustration 1 That does not mean rewards are inherently harmful. Research finds that financial and tangible incentives can produce meaningful behaviour change, particularly when the target behaviour is initially unattractive or infrequent. The problem is reward design . A useful incentive adds an immediate benefit without cancelling the delayed one. A badly chosen incentive can make the numbers look better while leaving the underlying habit weaker — or even offsetting the benefit it was supposed to produce. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)When Do Financial Incentives Reduce Intrinsic Motivation? Comparing Behaviors Studied in Psychological and Economic L... The Overjustification Effect Channel: Psy vs. Psy Open on YouTube When external rewards are useful External incentives make most sense when the immediate experience of a worthwhile behaviour is not rewarding enough to compete with easier alternatives. In that situation, money, points, privileges or small treats can alter the short term calculation: going for the walk, attending the gym or completing the study session now produces something desirable rather than merely promising a distant benefit. There is good evidence that this can work. A systematic review and meta analysis of personal financial incentives for habitual health behaviours found that incentives increased behaviour change, with effects detectable for some time after programmes began. Experimental exercise research likewise shows that incentives can increase activity while they are available. In one influential gym experiment, Gary Charness and Uri Gneezy found that paying previously irregular gym users to attend repeatedly increased attendance even after the intervention, suggesting that incentives can sometimes help people cross the difficult initiation stage into more regular behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. But persistence after incentives disappear is much less reliable. A workplace experiment by Heather Royer, Mark Stehr and Justin Sydnor found that a four week financial incentive produced only small lasting increases in gym use by itself. Another experiment with new gym members found only small effects during six weeks of incentives and no effect on subsequent attendance trajectories. Across the wider literature, short term behavioural gains are more dependable than evidence that temporary payments reliably create lasting habits. [AEA Publications] pubs.aeaweb.org Open source on aeaweb.org. That distinction matters for self improvement. A reward need not permanently transform motivation to be useful: getting someone through the difficult first month may itself have value. But if the plan assumes that thirty rewarded repetitions will automatically become an unrewarded habit, the evidence gives good reason for caution. How Incentives Lead to Better Performance. Uri Gneezy. Channel: Vienna Behavioral Economics Network Open on YouTube How rewards start creating competing costs The central test is not simply whether a reward increases the target behaviour. It is whether the whole arrangement moves the person towards the underlying goal . Several common reward designs can fail that test. The reward can become the reason for acting. The possibility that external rewards undermine intrinsic motivation has produced a long running research dispute. A major meta analysis by Edward Deci, Richard Koestner and Richard Ryan, covering 128 studies, concluded that expected tangible rewards could reduce subsequent free choice engagement and reported interest under several conditions. Other meta analyses have reached more qualified conclusions: Judy Cameron and colleagues argue that harmful effects are not pervasive and are concentrated particularly where an expected tangible reward is attached to an already interesting activity without being meaningfully tied to performance. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A meta analytic review of experiments examining the effects of extrinsic rewards on intrinsic motivation PubMed... For practical habit design, the disagreement is more useful than a blanket rule that rewards either “destroy motivation” or “always work”. A review specifically examining financial incentives and health behaviour found strong evidence for undermining effects when tangible rewards were applied to simple tasks for which motivation was already high, but noted that many health behaviours begin from the opposite position: people are not doing enough of them in the first place. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When do financial incentives reduce intrinsic motivation? comparing behaviors studied in psychological and economic literatures P... The risk is therefore greatest when an external reward is unnecessarily imposed on something a person already values for its own sake. Someone who has started to enjoy running may gain little from turning every run into a transaction worth £5. The payment can shift attention from “I run because I like being a runner” towards “Is this run worth £5?”, creating a motivational problem that did not previously exist. The rewarded metric can displace the real objective. Incentives focus attention on whatever earns the reward, but measurable behaviour is often only a proxy for what matters. Ten thousand recorded steps are not synonymous with fitness; hours at a desk are not synonymous with learning; pages read are not synonymous with understanding. Evidence from performance incentives outside personal habits demonstrates the mechanism clearly. In a natural field experiment involving factory workers, a bonus for measurable output increased attention to the incentivised quantity at the expense of non incentivised quality. [National Bureau of Economic Research] nber.org Open source on nber.org. The personal habit equivalent is easy to recognise: once points are awarded for a number, hitting the number can quietly replace pursuing the purpose behind it. This is especially relevant to gamified habit systems. Points, badges, streaks and leaderboards can provide feedback and immediate reinforcement, but reviews of health gamification note that studies commonly combine several game elements, making it difficult to identify which component produces an effect or whether improvements persist. The existence of an engaging score should not be mistaken for evidence that the score accurately represents the desired outcome. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The reward itself can offset the benefit. “I exercised, so I deserve this” is a particularly literal example. If every workout earns a large dessert or every productive week earns an expensive purchase, the incentive may impose a cost in the same domain in which the habit is supposed to help. Experimental evidence shows that this is more than a hypothetical concern. In one study, people performed the same amount of exercise but were told that they had expended either 50 or 265 calories. Those given the higher estimate subsequently consumed more food, largely through greater cookie consumption — consistent with the exercise providing a perceived licence to eat. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A broader scoping review found that energy dense post exercise eating was particularly associated with experiencing exercise as less enjoyable, less autonomous or as hard work. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Recent UK evidence illustrates the relevance outside the laboratory. A 2026 survey of 1,054 adults who exercised weekly found that 24% reported eating more in response to exercise, most often afterwards; stronger beliefs about food as an exercise reward were associated with this compensatory eating. Because the study was observational and relied on self"},{"title":"Reward Timing","url":"/reward-timing/","description":"A reward delivered close to an activity may strengthen its positive association more than the same reward delivered much later.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Immediate Rewards","level":3,"text":"Reward Timing Why Immediate Rewards Beat Delayed Treats Reward Timing Reward Timing Why Immediate Rewards Beat Delayed Treats A reward delivered close to an activity may strengthen its positive association more than the same reward delivered much later. A reward delivered close to an activity may strengthen its positive association more than the same reward delivered much later. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why timing can matter more than reward size The associative explanation Practical ways to shorten the reward delay self improvement that works immediate rewards reward timing Introduction For a habit with delayed benefits, when a reward arrives can change how the activity itself feels . A pleasant consequence delivered during an activity or immediately afterwards is more tightly connected to what you have just done than the same consequence arriving much later. Experimental research finds that earlier rewards can increase enjoyment , interest and willingness to continue an activity — effects that are not explained simply by the reward being perceived as more valuable because it arrives sooner. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. A delayed treat can function as compensation: I endured the workout, so tonight I get something nice. An immediate reward has a better chance of becoming part of the experience: this is what working out feels like . The practical aim, therefore, is not necessarily to make rewards bigger. It is often to shorten the psychological distance between the behaviour and something positive . Surprising Lessons From the Science of Motivation: Ayelet Fishbach — The GCA Podcast, Episode 266 Channel: Crisp Open on YouTube Why timing can matter more than reward size The clearest evidence comes from research by Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach specifically comparing earlier and later rewards. Across experiments involving activities including watching, working and reading, participants who expected or received rewards sooner reported greater intrinsic motivation — meaning greater enjoyment or interest in doing the activity for its own sake. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 One experiment makes the distinction unusually concrete. Participants completed a spot the difference task and could receive the same $0.60 bonus either immediately or one month later. Those promised the immediate bonus reported greater intrinsic motivation. More tellingly, when subsequently offered the opportunity to continue without further compensation, 84.2% of the immediate reward group chose to continue, compared with 70.3% of the delayed reward group and 52.1% of a no bonus group. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. This suggests something more interesting than the familiar idea that people simply prefer money now to money later. In another experiment, Woolley and Fishbach independently varied reward timing and reward size . Earlier rewards increased intrinsic motivation, whereas making the bonus larger did not produce the same effect. Participants offered an immediate bonus were also more likely to continue reading when no further bonus was available: 73.3% continued, versus 54.5% among those expecting the delayed reward. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 A later series of three studies by researchers at South China Normal University tested this timing versus size explanation again. Their third study found a significant effect of immediate rather than delayed reward on intrinsic motivation, while the difference between larger and smaller rewards was not statistically significant. The authors concluded that reward timing exerted an effect independent of reward magnitude. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Do Immediate External Rewards Really Enhance Intrinsic Motivation? PMC... For habit design, this changes the question. Instead of asking, “What sufficiently large reward can I promise myself?” , it may be more useful to ask, “What positive experience can happen right alongside this behaviour?” A favourite playlist during a run may therefore have an advantage that a nicer but remote weekend treat does not: the music is temporally embedded in running. Science can help you change behavior for the better, with Katy Milkman, PhD Speaking of Psychology Channel: American Psychological Association Open on YouTube The associative explanation Woolley and Fishbach proposed that immediacy strengthens the mental association between an activity and its reward. They describe this as greater activity goal association : when the positive consequence arrives close to the behaviour, the activity and reward are experienced as more closely connected. Their experiments found that this stronger perceived association mediated the relationship between earlier rewards and intrinsic motivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 Consider two versions of the same tedious reading session. In one, you make a good coffee, sit somewhere comfortable and listen to pleasant background music while reading. In the other, you promise yourself an enjoyable evening several hours after finishing. Both contain positive experiences, but their relationship to reading is different. In the first arrangement, pleasure repeatedly coincides with reading. In the second, the pleasure belongs much more obviously to what happens after reading. The experiments suggest that this distinction can affect evaluation of the activity itself. When participants received an immediate rather than delayed bonus for reading, they reported greater enjoyment and interest in the reading. Yet reward timing did not similarly increase their ratings of how important the external outcome was. In other words, bringing the reward forward appeared to alter the positive experience of doing the task , rather than merely making its eventual payoff seem more important. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation It's about time: Earlier rewards increase intrinsic motivation PubMedJune 1, 2018... Published: June 1, 2018 This fits a wider pattern in goal persistence research. Woolley and Fishbach found that immediate rewards such as enjoyment predicted persistence in studying, exercise , healthy eating and New Year's resolutions more strongly than delayed benefits did, despite people saying that the delayed benefits were major reasons for pursuing those goals. Their interpretation is that long term outcomes can be important for choosing a goal, while the immediate experience has particular influence over whether someone keeps doing the activity. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 The mechanism should not be overstated as a universal law of habits. These experiments mostly examine motivation and persistence in bounded tasks rather than proving that every immediately rewarded behaviour will become an automatic long term habit. Research on motor learning does, however, provide separate evidence that temporal proximity can matter for learning: in an experiment with 60 participants, a one second reward delay produced different learning dynamics and better final performance than a six second delay. That study concerned motor skill reinforcement rather than everyday habit formation, so it supports the importance of timing without establishing an exact timetable for personal rewards. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Reward timing matters in motor learning Reward timing matters in motor learning PubMedApril 25, 2022... Published: April 25, 2022 Explanatory illustration 2 Make the reward part of the habit The strongest practical application is to move enjoyment into the behaviour wherever possible , rather than attaching all pleasure to a later celebration. Research on long term goals has found that choosing an exercise because it is enjoyable rather than merely useful, choosing the tastier healthy option, and adding music to an otherwise effortful activity can increase"},{"title":"Better Motivation","url":"/better-motivation/","description":"Acting from personal value or genuine interest is linked with better persistence, but even strong autonomous motivation does not eliminate execution problems.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Motivation Limits","level":3,"text":"Better Motivation Does the Type of Motivation Matter More? Motivation Limits Better Motivation Better Motivation Does the Type of Motivation Matter More? Acting from personal value or genuine interest is linked with better persistence, but even strong autonomous motivation does not eliminate execution problems. Acting from personal value or genuine interest is linked with better persistence, but even strong autonomous motivation does not eliminate execution problems. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism Autonomous versus pressured motivation What exercise research says about persistence Why better motivation still needs behavioural support self improvement that works motivation limits better motivation Introduction For long term self improvement, why you act appears to matter, not just how strongly you want the outcome . Research based on self determination theory distinguishes autonomous motivation — acting because a behaviour is enjoyable, personally important or consistent with one's values — from controlled motivation, where action is driven more by rewards, demands, guilt or pressure. Across exercise , health behaviour and personal goal research, the more autonomous forms are generally associated with better persistence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Self Determination Theory Applied to Health Contexts: A Meta Analysis Self Determination Theory Applied to Health Contexts: A Meta Analysis PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 That does not mean every lasting habit must be enjoyable. Exercise undertaken because someone genuinely values being healthy can be autonomously motivated even when the workout itself is unpleasant. Nor does autonomous motivation solve execution automatically. Intervention effects are generally modest, and research linking autonomous motivation with goal progress also points to planning as part of the pathway. The practical lesson is therefore narrower than “find your passion”: having reasons you genuinely endorse can make persistence more likely, but those reasons still need behavioural support to survive everyday friction . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self determination theory interventions for health behavior change: Meta analysis and meta analytic structural equation modeling of... Edward Deci Self Determination Theory Channel: The Brainwaves Video Anthology Open on YouTube Autonomous versus pressured motivation Self determination theory treats motivation as differing in quality , rather than reducing it to a single quantity such as “high” or “low”. At one end, intrinsic motivation means doing something because the activity itself is interesting or enjoyable. Identified regulation is also relatively autonomous: the activity may not be pleasurable, but the person accepts its importance and chooses it for personally meaningful reasons. Someone might dislike running yet willingly train because physical health matters deeply to them. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Exercise, physical activity, and self determination theory: A systematic review PMCJune 22, 2012... Published: June 22, 2012 Controlled motivation is different. External regulation involves incentives, punishment, demands or other outside contingencies. Introjected regulation moves some of that pressure inside the person: “I have to do this or I'll feel guilty”, for example. The distinction is therefore not simply internal versus external motivation. A reason can originate outside the activity — improving health, gaining a qualification or mastering a useful skill — and nevertheless become something the person genuinely endorses. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Student Motivation and Associated Outcomes: A Meta Analysis From Self Determination Theory PubMed... This distinction helps explain why apparently similar levels of effort can have different longer term implications. A large meta analysis of student motivation, covering 344 samples and 223,209 participants, found that identified regulation was particularly strongly related to persistence, while intrinsic motivation was associated with success and well being. External regulation was not associated with persistence or performance. Introjected motivation presents a more complicated picture: it was positively associated with persistence and performance goals, but also with indicators of poorer well being. Pressure, in other words, can sometimes move behaviour without producing the same overall pattern as personally endorsed motivation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Student Motivation and Associated Outcomes: A Meta Analysis From Self Determination Theory PubMed... Research on people's personal goals points in a similar direction. Across three studies and a meta analysis, Koestner and colleagues found autonomous motivation substantially related to goal progress, whereas controlled motivation was not. Importantly, the relationship between autonomous motivation and progress involved implementation planning . That finding already places an important qualification on motivational advice: better reasons for pursuing a goal may encourage effective goal striving, but translating those reasons into concrete action remains part of the process. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Autonomous motivation, controlled motivation, and goal progress Autonomous motivation, controlled motivation, and goal progress PubMed... Self Determination Theory: The Secret to Unlocking Better Motivation Channel: John Spencer Open on YouTube What exercise reveals about persistence Exercise provides an unusually useful test because starting and maintaining it are different problems. A person may initially exercise for a medical recommendation, appearance goal or approaching event, but months of repeated participation require motivation to survive competing demands, discomfort and declining novelty. A systematic review of 66 exercise and physical activity studies found a broadly consistent positive relationship between autonomous motivation and exercise behaviour. More interestingly, the pattern differed with time: identified regulation tended to predict initial or shorter term adoption more strongly, whereas intrinsic motivation tended to become more important for longer term adherence . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Exercise, physical activity, and self determination theory: A systematic review PMCJune 22, 2012... Published: June 22, 2012 That distinction makes intuitive and practical sense without requiring the claim that enjoyment is everything. When beginning exercise, “this matters to my health” can provide a sufficiently compelling reason to start. For sustained participation, however, finding forms of activity that provide some inherent satisfaction — enjoyment, challenge, mastery or an appealing experience — may remove part of the recurring motivational cost. The 2012 review also found competence satisfaction positively associated with participation, suggesting that feeling increasingly capable may help create a more sustainable motivational experience. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Exercise, physical activity, and self determination theory: A systematic review PMCJune 22, 2012... Published: June 22, 2012 Longitudinal research offers a particularly concrete example. A 2020 study followed new fitness centre members for 30 consecutive weeks and used actual attendance rather than relying solely on people's recollections. Maintained autonomous exercise motivation predicted continued attendance; the study also found that participants overestimated attendance when reporting it themselves. That matters methodologically because persistence research based only on self report can make behaviour look more consistent than objective records suggest. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Achievement motive, autonomous motivation, and attendance at fitness center: A longitudinal prospective study ScienceDirect... There is also evidence extending beyond a few months. In a randomised weight management study of women, autonomous motivation for exercise predicted three year weight loss outcomes, with physical activity playing an important role in the relationship. The result does not prove that autonomous motivation by itself produced three years of persistence, but it strengthens the case that motivational quality can remain relevant well beyond the initial enthusiasm of starting a programme. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Exercise autonomous motivation predicts 3 yr weight loss in women Exercise autonomous motivation predicts 3 yr weight loss in women PubMed... The broader evidence has continued to support the association while also exposing its limits. A 2018 systematic review of 35 exercise studies concluded that autonomy supportive interpersonal behaviour, psychological need satisfaction and more autonomous regulation were relevant to exercise persistence, but stressed"},{"title":"Motivation Swings","url":"/motivation-swings/","description":"Motivation can rise and fall within the same person, changing how rewarding an effortful action feels from one moment to the next.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Motivation Limits","level":3,"text":"Motivation Swings Why Motivation Feels Different From Day to Day Motivation Limits Motivation Swings Motivation Swings Why Motivation Feels Different From Day to Day Motivation can rise and fall within the same person, changing how rewarding an effortful action feels from one moment to the next. Motivation can rise and fall within the same person, changing how rewarding an effortful action feels from one moment to the next. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How motivation fluctuates within one person Why rewards and effort feel different across moments Why plans that require enthusiasm are fragile self improvement that works motivation limits motivation swings Introduction Motivation is not a fixed amount of willingness that a person carries from one day to the next. It fluctuates within the same person, and those fluctuations can change whether an effortful action seems worth doing. A workout, difficult piece of study or demanding household task can therefore look attractive at one moment and disproportionately burdensome at another even when the objective task and payoff have barely changed. Explanatory illustration 1 Recent real world research provides unusually direct evidence for this mechanism. Repeated smartphone measurements found that when people felt more motivated than usual for themselves, they were more willing to choose greater effort for greater reward. Computational analysis suggested something more specific than a general feeling that effort had become easier: higher motivation made the prospective reward more valuable . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 That distinction matters for long term self improvement. A plan built around what seems worthwhile during a motivational high is being designed under conditions that will not reliably persist. Andrew Westbrook Dopamine Promotes Cognitive Effort Channel: Virtual Dopamine Open on YouTube How motivation fluctuates within one person Much motivation research has traditionally compared people: one person is described as highly motivated, another as less motivated. For behaviour change, however, there is another important comparison — you today versus you at another moment . Hewitt and colleagues examined precisely this issue in a 15 day microlongitudinal study. Participants repeatedly reported their current state through their smartphones and periodically completed a game in which they chose between routes offering different combinations of monetary reward and physical effort. The published analysis included 129 participants after exclusions, 3,344 momentary state assessments and 845 decision making games. This dense sampling made it possible to distinguish relatively stable differences between people from fluctuations occurring within each person. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 Those within person changes were substantial and meaningful. At times when participants reported greater motivation than was normal for them, they were more likely to select options requiring greater physical effort in exchange for greater reward. This relationship remained after accounting for stable individual differences and other covariates. In other words, the result was not merely that generally energetic people both said they were motivated and acted motivated. The same individual's effort choices shifted alongside their changing motivational state. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 Motivation also moved alongside other everyday states. Within individuals, it was positively associated with happiness and the previous night's sleep quality and negatively associated with fatigue. Yet motivation was not simply interchangeable with feeling happy: the researchers found that effort based choices were specifically coupled with motivational state rather than happiness after modelling the relevant variables. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 That makes daily variability more than background noise. Someone can genuinely value an exercise goal, writing project or course while experiencing sizeable changes in how willing they are to pay the immediate effort required to pursue it. The finding should not be stretched too far. The study involved adults aged 18–45 and used a short, gamified physical effort task rather than months of exercise, studying or dieting. It demonstrates a mechanism of fluctuating effort based choice, not a precise prediction of how much any particular habit will vary. The broader effort discounting literature is also methodologically heterogeneous: researchers use different definitions and manipulations of physical and cognitive effort, limiting simple generalisations across tasks. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressA systematic review of effort discounting research in humans: Current knowledge, recommendations, and future di... BEACON Seminar: Striatal dopamine and the costs and benefits of cognitive effort Channel: Experimental Psychology Dept, University of Oxford Open on YouTube Why rewards and effort feel different across moments An effortful choice can be understood as a trade off. The brain is not evaluating a reward in isolation; it is evaluating something closer to reward minus the subjective cost of obtaining it . This phenomenon is commonly studied as effort discounting: as required effort rises, the subjective value of a prospective reward generally falls. Research has demonstrated this with both physical and cognitive demands. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressA systematic review of effort discounting research in humans: Current knowledge, recommendations, and future di... That creates two obvious ways motivation could fluctuate. On a low motivation day, the same task might seem more costly: the workout feels harder . Alternatively, its payoff might carry less weight: finishing the workout does not seem worth much right now . Both routes could produce the same observable behaviour — choosing the easier option — while arising from different underlying valuations. The 2025 longitudinal study was able to separate these possibilities computationally. Higher momentary motivation predicted greater reward sensitivity , whereas the evidence did not support a corresponding change in effort sensitivity. In practical terms, when participants were more motivated, the rewards available in the task carried more subjective value, tipping choices towards the high effort option. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 The temporal results strengthen that interpretation, although they do not establish causation. Higher state motivation preceded increased reward sensitivity at a later assessment, suggesting that motivation was not merely a label participants applied after making an energetic choice. Because motivation itself was not experimentally manipulated, the authors appropriately stop short of claiming definitive causal proof. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day fluctuations in motivation drive effort based decision making PMCMarch 25, 2025... Published: March 25, 2025 This provides a useful way to understand a familiar self improvement experience. Imagine that running for 30 minutes produces roughly the same long term benefits on Tuesday and Thursday. On Tuesday, those benefits — satisfaction afterwards, fitness progress or keeping a promise to yourself — may feel sufficiently valuable to justify getting changed and going outside. On Thursday, the distant payoff may carry less motivational weight. Nothing about the long term goal needs to have changed. The momentary valuation has. Effort itself is dynamic too. Laboratory work on persistence shows that fatigue accumulating during physical work can alter subsequent effort based decisions. Müller and colleagues modelled two fatigue processes: one rose with effort and could partly recover during rest, while another accumulated more gradually as work continued. Both reduced willingness to work for reward. [Nature] nature.com Neural and computational mechanisms of momentary fatigue and persistence in effort based choice Nature CommunicationsJuly 28, 2021... Published: July 28, 2021 Sleep can alter the equation as well, although not necessarily uniformly. In a"},{"title":"Intention Gap","url":"/intention-gap/","description":"Daily-life studies show that intending to exercise often fails to produce activity when the actual opportunity to act arrives.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Motivation Limits","level":3,"text":"Intention Gap Why Exercise Intentions So Often Stay Intentions Motivation Limits Intention Gap Intention Gap Why Exercise Intentions So Often Stay Intentions Daily life studies show that intending to exercise often fails to produce activity when the actual opportunity to act arrives. Daily life studies show that intending to exercise often fails to produce activity when the actual opportunity to act arrives. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence What momentary activity studies found Where intention breaks down before action What improves the odds of follow through self improvement that works motivation limits intention gap Introduction A sincere intention to exercise is not the same thing as exercising when the opportunity actually arrives. Studies that repeatedly ask people about their intentions during ordinary days, then measure what they do over the next few hours, make this gap unusually visible. In one accelerometer study, adults reported short term intentions to be active at 41% of prompts, yet completed at least ten minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity after only 16% of the prompts where they had intended to act. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Momentary assessment of physical activity intention behavior coupling in adults PMC... Explanatory illustration 1 That finding should not be read as a universal 84% failure rate. Other momentary studies have produced substantially smaller gaps, partly because they define intentions, activity and successful follow through differently. The stronger conclusion is simpler: intention predicts exercise, but does not guarantee its enactment even over the next few hours . The practical problem in self improvement is therefore not merely creating enough motivation. It is making an intended action sufficiently specific, feasible and resilient to survive the moment in which it must begin. Explanatory illustration 3 What momentary activity studies found Much of the traditional intention–behaviour literature asks whether somebody who intends to be physically active now is active days, weeks or months later. Ecological momentary assessment (EMA) changes the question. Participants receive repeated smartphone prompts during normal life, report what they intend to do over the near future, and researchers then examine activity during the following hours. This allows the same person to supply many intention–action episodes rather than being classified simply as a generally motivated or unmotivated individual. The distinction matters because exercise is enacted one occasion at a time. A person may have a strong general commitment to becoming fitter while repeatedly encountering individual moments in which today's intended walk, run or gym visit does not happen. Across longer term physical activity research, a 2023 systematic review covering 25 independent samples and 29,600 participants classified 33.0% of all participants as unsuccessful intenders and 38.7% as successful intenders. Among people who intended to be active, the estimated intention–behaviour gap was 47.6%. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The intention behaviour gap in physical activity: a systematic review and meta analysis of the action control framework PubMedJul... Momentary research shows what that broad statistical gap can look like during everyday life. A particularly revealing study by Jaclyn Maher and colleagues followed 116 low active adults through three four day EMA periods. Participants reported near term physical activity intentions and wore accelerometers; the researchers looked for at least ten minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity (MVPA) during the three hours following each prompt. Short term intentions were reported at 41% of prompts, but qualifying activity followed just 16% of occasions on which an intention had been reported. By that deliberately strict definition, 84% of intention episodes were not followed by the measured activity criterion. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Momentary assessment of physical activity intention behavior coupling in adults PMC... The study also exposed an important distinction between forming an intention and enacting it . Participants were more likely to intend to be active in the morning and at weekends, but time of day and weekday versus weekend did not significantly predict whether an already formed intention became activity. In other words, circumstances associated with saying “I will exercise” were not necessarily the same circumstances associated with actually doing it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Momentary assessment of physical activity intention behavior coupling in adults PMC... An EMA study of women aged 40–60 with elevated cardiovascular disease risk found an even more striking mismatch under its own measurement rules. Participants received five smartphone surveys per day for ten days while wearing accelerometers. They achieved their exercise intentions on only 13% of occasions on which intentions were set, and the within person association between intended minutes of MVPA and observed MVPA during the following three hours was modest. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These figures are not interchangeable. A later three week EMA study of 35 adults, based on 2,341 completed prompts, reported that intentions were not enacted in about 25% of relevant episodes — a much smaller short term gap. Its authors suggested that intentions formed close to the opportunity for action may sometimes be easier to fulfil because people can take immediate circumstances into account. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Within person association of volitional factors and physical activity: Insights from an ecological momentary assessment stud... The apparent disagreement is useful rather than troublesome. “I intended to exercise but did not” depends on exactly what counts as an intention, how soon behaviour is assessed, what intensity or duration qualifies as exercise, whether activity is self reported or objectively recorded, and who is being studied. The momentary literature therefore does not justify attaching one universal percentage to failed exercise intentions. It does justify treating the intention–action transition as a measurable behavioural event rather than assuming that motivation automatically carries a person across it. The Intention Behavior Gap Why You Can’t Do What You Say You Want to Do Channel: Garage Gym Athlete Open on YouTube Where intention breaks down before action Momentary studies help separate two questions that ordinary resolutions tend to blur: Do I want to exercise? and Will I initiate exercise in this particular situation? An intention answers the first much better than the second. One reason is that a general intention leaves operational details unresolved. “I want to exercise this afternoon” still permits several decisions at the critical moment: when exactly to start, what activity to do, where to do it, how long to continue, and what to do if work runs late or another demand intervenes. Evidence from the 2,341 prompt EMA study is consistent with this interpretation. When participants already had an intention, the probability of subsequent activity was higher on occasions when their plans were more specific and their self efficacy — their confidence that they could perform the behaviour — was stronger. Intrinsic motivation was also positively associated with enactment; planning specificity and intrinsic motivation predicted greater activity duration and intensity as well. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Within person association of volitional factors and physical activity: Insights from an ecological momentary assessment study Pub... Momentary emotional state can matter too, although the evidence is not uniform enough to reduce follow through to “being in a good mood”. In Maher and colleagues' study, an intention was more likely to be followed by measured activity when a participant's positive affect was higher than was usual for that same person . The estimated odds of enactment were more than twice as high on those higher positive affect occasions. Negative affect, fatigue and energy did not show equivalent significant associations with intention enactment in that analysis. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Momentary assessment of physical activity intention behavior coupling in adults PMC... The midlife women study likewise found that the strength of the intention–activity relationship varied with momentary positive affect and body satisfaction. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. These findings illustrate why a person's average motivation can conceal the important part of the story. Someone can be “motivated to exercise” in a questionnaire while the probability of acting on Tuesday at 6 pm differs from the"},{"title":"Habit Limits","url":"/habit-limits/","description":"Stable routines can help habits form, but changing environments and complex behaviours prevent automaticity from becoming a universal substitute for motivation.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Motivation Limits","level":3,"text":"Habit Limits When Habit Advice Promises Too Much Motivation Limits Habit Limits Habit Limits When Habit Advice Promises Too Much Stable routines can help habits form, but changing environments and complex behaviours prevent automaticity from becoming a universal substitute for motivation. Stable routines can help habits form, but changing environments and complex behaviours prevent automaticity from becoming a universal substitute for motivation. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context Why repetition does not guarantee effortless action Evidence that context effects are not uniform Which behaviours remain vulnerable to disruption self improvement that works motivation limits habit limits Introduction Habit formation can make self improvement less dependent on moment to moment motivation, but it does not make motivation obsolete. The important qualification is context . Psychological habits are learned links between cues and responses: repetition can make a behaviour easier to initiate when familiar cues recur, but that automaticity is not necessarily portable to a different schedule, place, social setting or version of the task. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceHabits, Goals, and Effective Behavior Change Current Directions in Psychological Science APS... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because real life is not perfectly repetitive. Work patterns change, people travel or move house, family responsibilities shift, and behaviours such as exercising or studying require many variable decisions. Research supports using stable contexts to build habits, while also showing substantial variation in automaticity, in which cues matter, and in whether automaticity translates into maintained behaviour. Habit is therefore best understood as a way to reduce some recurring motivational demands , not as a mechanism that eventually makes any important behaviour effortless everywhere. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Why repetition does not guarantee effortless action The familiar advice to “repeat something until it becomes automatic” leaves out a crucial condition. Repetition is most useful for habit formation when the behaviour and its cues recur together. Habit researchers commonly describe the resulting mechanism as a learned cue–response association: encountering a familiar context can generate an impulse towards the previously repeated action without requiring a fresh decision about what to do. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... This means automaticity is not simply a property acquired by a behaviour after enough repetitions. It is partly a relationship between a behaviour and the circumstances in which it has been learned . Fifty repetitions scattered unpredictably across different situations need not produce the same learning as fifty repetitions reliably following a particular cue. A six week study of 95 university students illustrates the point. Students assigned to build study habits at consistent times and places were compared with students told to vary their times and rooms. Across this experiment and a second dataset drawn from a habit building app, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. In the experimental study, the relationship between fluctuations in context stability and automaticity appeared only in the group whose overall context had been kept relatively stable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 More recent experimental evidence comes from walking. In a randomised study of 127 insufficiently active working adults, both consistent context and varied context planning increased walking during the four week intervention, but only the consistent context planners developed and retained greater walking automaticity. Having a more routine daily schedule also helped some participants develop automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Confirming the Causal Role of Consistent Contexts in Developing a Walking Habit: A Randomized Comparison With Varied Contexts PubMed... These findings support stable routines, but they do not support the stronger promise that sufficient repetition eventually makes behaviour universally effortless. Even under favourable conditions, habit formation varies markedly between people and behaviours. In the influential real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues, the estimated time to reach 95% of an individual's eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days among participants whose data fitted the model. Automaticity also approached an individual plateau rather than increasing indefinitely. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis reinforces that variability. Across 20 health habit studies involving 2,601 participants, the few studies reporting formation times produced median estimates of roughly 59–66 days and mean estimates of 106–154 days, with individual values spanning 4–335 days. Eleven of the 20 studies were rated at high risk of bias, so even these figures should not be treated as a universal timetable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical lesson is more useful than a “21 day” or “66 day” rule: repetition can progressively reduce the amount of deliberate initiation a behaviour requires, but there is no research supported point at which an arbitrary behaviour becomes permanently motivation free. The secret habits that control your life Wendy Wood Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Context effects are not uniform “Keep the context consistent” sounds straightforward until context has to be defined. A cue could be a clock time, location, person, mood, preceding activity or recurring event. Different behaviours may become linked to different combinations of these cues. That complication appears clearly in physical activity research. In a study of 1,244 Australian adults, greater consistency in routine and mood was associated with greater physical activity automaticity, whereas greater consistency in time and people present was associated with physical activity behaviour itself. Location was not the universal master cue that simple habit advice might lead someone to expect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cue Consistency Associated with Physical Activity Automaticity and Behavior PubMedApril 11, 2015... Published: April 11, 2015 A smaller 2024 prospective study produced another mixed pattern. Habit more strongly mediated the relationship between previous and later physical activity when participants reported consistency in time of day , type of activity and mood. Consistency in place, people present and position within a routine did not show the same moderating effect. The sample contained only 68 university students, so these particular cue rankings should not be generalised broadly; their importance is that they challenge the assumption that every form of contextual consistency operates identically. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There are also findings that do not neatly support a context stability to habit pathway. An ecological momentary assessment study followed 104 older adults for ten days while continuously measuring their movement. Stable physical contexts were associated with more moderate to vigorous physical activity, and aspects of context stability also related to sedentary behaviour. Yet none of the measured context stability scores predicted participants' reported habit strength for physical activity or sedentary behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. More recent longitudinal research likewise suggests that the relevant cue can be individual rather than universal. A study following 202 older adults across three data collection periods found context stability was more consistently related to changes in habit strength than to changes in actual movement behaviour, while associations depended on whether people were adopting or maintaining behaviour and on which cue was most stable for the individual. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. So “use a consistent cue” remains sensible advice, but the evidence does not justify turning it into “always do the behaviour at exactly the same time and place”. For some behaviours, a functional cue such as “after breakfast” may be more useful than 7.30 am; for others, the preceding routine, social situation or internal state"},{"title":"Stable Contexts","url":"/stable-contexts-a605ff/","description":"Repeating a behaviour in a stable context can strengthen automaticity, reducing how much fresh deliberation is needed each time.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Motivation Limits","level":3,"text":"Stable Contexts Can a Stable Context Make Habits Easier? Motivation Limits Stable Contexts Stable Contexts Can a Stable Context Make Habits Easier? Repeating a behaviour in a stable context can strengthen automaticity, reducing how much fresh deliberation is needed each time. Repeating a behaviour in a stable context can strengthen automaticity, reducing how much fresh deliberation is needed each time. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention Why recurring contexts can become behavioural cues What study and walking experiments found How consistency can reduce repeated decision making self improvement that works motivation limits stable contexts Introduction A stable context can make a new behaviour easier to repeat because the surroundings, timing or preceding routine can gradually become cues for the action itself. Instead of repeatedly asking, “Will I do this now?”, a person who always studies after breakfast, walks after lunch or stretches after brushing their teeth gives the brain a recurring cue–response pattern to learn. Habit researchers call the resulting sense that an action happens with less conscious initiation automaticity . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 The practical point is not that motivation becomes unnecessary. Motivation is still important for choosing a useful behaviour and getting enough repetitions underway. The advantage of context consistency is that those repetitions can gradually shift some control away from fresh deliberation. Experiments on study habits and walking now provide stronger evidence that keeping contexts relatively stable can accelerate this process, although automaticity does not guarantee that behaviour will persist under every circumstance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Why recurring contexts can become behavioural cues Habit theory treats a context as more than a backdrop. When a behaviour repeatedly follows particular features of a situation, associations can develop between those features and the response. “Context” can include a physical place, a time of day , people who are present, an emotional state or — importantly — another action that reliably happens immediately beforehand. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... That last category explains why “after breakfast” can be a useful habit context even when breakfast does not occur at exactly 7:30 every morning. The preceding routine provides an event that can recur despite some clock time variation. Likewise, arriving at a desk, putting the children to bed or finishing lunch can potentially provide a recognisable starting condition for the next behaviour. The essential ingredient is not a magical location or hour; it is repeated pairing between sufficiently recognisable circumstances and the intended response. This distinction matters because repetition alone and repetition in a recurring context are not quite the same intervention . If someone reads for 20 minutes every day but does so at unpredictable times and places, they accumulate practice. If they repeatedly read immediately after breakfast in the same chair, they also repeatedly expose themselves to cues that can become associated with starting to read. The theoretical expectation is that the second pattern provides more opportunity for cue dependent automaticity to develop. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... A well known real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates the process. Ninety six volunteers selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context — such as after breakfast — for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally rose rapidly at first and then more slowly towards a plateau. Among participants for whom the researchers could model the process, estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days. Missing a single opportunity did not materially derail habit formation. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Those results are useful partly because they undermine the popular idea that a habit appears after a fixed number of days. The more defensible target is consistent cue–behaviour repetition , not completing an arbitrary streak. A 2024 systematic review likewise concluded that habit formation times vary substantially between people and behaviours, while identifying repeated performance and stable contexts among the factors relevant to developing automaticity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. How Does Habit Formation Impact UX Design Choices Channel: IxDF Interaction Design Foundation Open on YouTube What study and walking experiments found The particularly useful evidence comes from studies that deliberately changed context consistency rather than merely observing people who already had strong routines. In a 2022 investigation, Marco Stojanovic and colleagues followed 95 university students building study habits over six weeks. Participants generated 2,482 logged habit repetitions. One group was told to keep the time and place of the behaviour stable, while another was instructed to vary them. A second dataset examined 308 habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across the datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of the intended repetition goal; automaticity partially mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is an important qualification inside that experiment. Simply belonging to the assigned stable context group did not by itself produce a statistically significant difference in automaticity. Instead, the day to day degree of context stability predicted automaticity, particularly among participants operating in generally stable contexts. The authors therefore argued that context needs to be specified with enough precision: a vague plan such as doing something “at home after school” may still encompass considerable variation from one repetition to the next. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. A newer randomised study provides more direct evidence from physical activity . Jane Ebert and Xin Yao Lin recruited 127 insufficiently active working adults and compared three conditions over four weeks: planning daily walking in consistent contexts, planning it in varying contexts, and a control condition without required planning. Both planning groups increased their walking during the intervention, but only the consistent context group developed and subsequently maintained increased walking automaticity at the four week follow up. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Confirming the Causal Role of Consistent Contexts in Developing a Walking Habit: A Randomized Comparison With Varied Contexts PubMed... That separation is revealing. Planning helped people walk more during the active intervention whether the contexts were stable or varied. Context consistency made the distinctive difference to whether walking came to feel more automatic. In other words, a strategy can successfully increase behaviour in the short term without necessarily producing the same degree of habit learning. The walking experiment also identified an intriguing natural advantage: people whose everyday schedules were already more routine were better positioned to develop automaticity. Among control participants who received no planning requirement, those with more routine schedules developed greater automaticity, with the effect mediated by walking in more consistent contexts. A predictable daily structure may therefore supply useful cues even without an elaborate habit building system. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Confirming the Causal Role of Consistent Contexts in Developing a Walking Habit: A Randomized Comparison With Varied Contexts PubMed... Explanatory illustration 2 Consistency helps, but the evidence is not absolute Stable context should not be turned into another simplistic self improvement rule. Some observational findings are less tidy than the experimental results. For example, a 2021 study followed 104 older adults for ten days using repeated assessments and activity monitoring. Greater stability of some contexts was associated with behaviour — including more moderate to vigorous physical"},{"title":"Fallback Workouts","url":"/fallback-workouts-311c0d/","description":"A shorter backup version of a normal workout can preserve continuity when weather, travel, overtime or low energy disrupts the plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Physical Capacity","level":3,"text":"Fallback Workouts What Should Your Workout Become on a Bad Day? Physical Capacity Fallback Workouts Fallback Workouts What Should Your Workout Become on a Bad Day? A shorter backup version of a normal workout can preserve continuity when weather, travel, overtime or low energy disrupts the plan. A shorter backup version of a normal workout can preserve continuity when weather, travel, overtime or low energy disrupts the plan. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why rigid routines break under ordinary disruptions How to define a realistic minimum version When to use the fallback without letting it replace progression self improvement that works physical capacity fallback workouts Introduction A fallback workout is a deliberately smaller version of the activity you normally planned to do, used when weather, travel, overtime, disrupted childcare or an unusually low energy day makes the full session impractical. Its purpose is not to pretend that five or ten minutes equals a complete training session. It is to prevent an ordinary disruption from automatically becoming a day of no movement at all. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is supported by two useful strands of evidence. Modern physical activity guidance no longer requires aerobic activity to occur in bouts of at least ten minutes: shorter bouts count, and some activity is better than none. At the same time, behavioural research suggests that planning specifically for obstacles can help translate an intention to exercise into action. [doi.org] doi.org bjsports 2020 102955 World Health Organization 2020 guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviourDecember 1, 2020... Published: December 1, 2020 For self improvement, the practical lesson is simple: build the bad day version before the bad day arrives. The normal workout remains the programme; the fallback is its continuity mechanism. Why rigid routines break under ordinary disruptions A routine can become easier to repeat when it is attached to stable circumstances. Research on physical activity habits supports the importance of contextual cues, and a randomised walking study found that exercising in consistent contexts helped participants develop greater automaticity than planning walks in varied contexts. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The influence of context stability on physical activity and sedentary behaviour habit and behaviour: An ecological momentary assess... That is useful on normal days, but it exposes a weakness too: real life does not preserve those circumstances indefinitely. Work runs late. A train is cancelled. Rain makes an outdoor route unpleasant. A hotel has no useful gym. A family responsibility consumes the evening. Research on physical activity maintenance explicitly identifies changes in circumstances and disruptive work or personal responsibilities as events capable of breaking the contextual links on which habits depend. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Maintaining habitual physical activity by overcoming disruptive competing actions: mechanisms and interventions PubMedDecember 19... The fragile response is to define success so narrowly that only the original plan qualifies. If the plan says “45 minutes at the gym after work”, an unexpected 30 minute delay can make the remaining 15 minutes seem pointless. Current guidance provides good reason not to treat it that way. The UK Chief Medical Officers state that small bouts, including those shorter than ten minutes, provide benefits, while WHO guidance says physical activity of any bout duration can contribute to health and that adults who are below recommendations should begin with small amounts and increase gradually. [gov.uk] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UK... There is also direct evidence that very short activity can have physiological value. A 2025 review and expert consensus covering 27 systematic reviews and 135 studies concluded that bouts of accumulated exercise lasting ten minutes or less can be a useful way of increasing activity and interrupting prolonged sitting. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Short bouts of accumulated exercise: Review and consensus statement on definition, efficacy, feasibility, practical applications, a... Observational studies of vigorous intermittent lifestyle physical activity have likewise associated brief bursts embedded in everyday life with lower mortality and cardiovascular risk, although such findings cannot prove that the short bouts themselves caused the lower risk. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A newer US cohort found a similar association but warned that existing illness and reverse causation could partly explain it . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. None of this means that two minutes of stair climbing is physiologically equivalent to the planned run. It means that the choice on a disrupted day is not “proper workout or worthless activity”. There is meaningful territory in between. The Least Amount You Can Train and Still See Results Mind Pump 2826 Channel: Mind Pump Show Open on YouTube How to define a realistic minimum version The most useful fallback preserves the type of action while aggressively reducing whatever has become difficult: duration, volume, equipment, travel or complexity. Someone who normally walks or runs might use a brisk ten minute walk. Someone who strength trains might do one compact round of familiar multi joint movements. Someone stranded in a hotel might use stairs or a short equipment free routine rather than searching for a replacement gym. This is better designed in advance than improvised while tired. Planning interventions have a modest but meaningful evidence base in physical activity: a systematic review and meta analysis of 41 randomised trials, including 5,439 participants, found greater physical activity in planning groups than controls, although effects varied considerably between studies. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Effectiveness of Planning Interventions for Improving Physical Activity in the General Population: A Systematic Review and Meta... A separate review of implementation intention interventions found that plans were particularly useful when they incorporated ways of managing expected obstacles and were reinforced over time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That makes a fallback well suited to an if–then rule . Instead of deciding from scratch at 8.30 pm whether exercise is still “worth it”, decide earlier: if overtime removes my normal gym session, I will walk briskly for ten minutes before going home . A large 2024 meta analysis across 642 tests of implementation intentions found that contingent if–then formats were generally more effective than weaker forms of planning, particularly when people were already motivated to pursue the underlying goal. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. A practical fallback can therefore be built around four constraints: Make starting almost frictionless. Remove the journey, complicated equipment, lengthy setup and unnecessary exercise choices. A fallback that still requires 30 minutes of preparation has not solved the disruption. Keep it recognisably connected to the normal routine. A runner might walk briskly, use stairs or run briefly; a strength trainee might retain a few familiar movement patterns with fewer sets. Set the minimum before motivation collapses. “Ten minutes”, “one round” or “one set of the main exercises” gives the decision a clear endpoint. “Do whatever I can” still requires negotiation. Allow completion to count. Once the predetermined fallback has been performed, the person has fulfilled the bad day plan. Continuing is optional rather than required. There is no universal scientifically established number of minutes that constitutes the ideal fallback. The correct minimum depends on fitness, normal training, goals and the nature of the disruption. Public health recommendations provide targets for overall activity; they do not prescribe a special “ consistency workout” for difficult days. The fallback is therefore best understood as a behavioural design built on evidence that smaller amounts of activity still matter, rather than as a distinct clinical exercise prescription. [DOI] doi.org bjsports 2020 102955 World Health Organization 2020 guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviourDecember 1, 2020... Published: December 1, 2020 Explanatory illustration 2 What a fallback can preserve — and what it cannot For someone whose main concern is general activity, a shorter session can contribute directly to the week's total. The removal of the old ten minute minimum is important here: five minutes of brisk walking does not become physiologically irrelevant simply because it falls short of an arbitrary session length. [PubMed Central (PMC)]"},{"title":"Pattern Spotting","url":"/pattern-spotting/","description":"Repeated misses at the same time or under the same conditions can reveal a scheduling problem rather than a motivation problem.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Track Progress","level":3,"text":"Pattern Spotting What Repeated Misses Can Tell You About Your Plan Track Progress Pattern Spotting Pattern Spotting What Repeated Misses Can Tell You About Your Plan Repeated misses at the same time or under the same conditions can reveal a scheduling problem rather than a motivation problem. Repeated misses at the same time or under the same conditions can reveal a scheduling problem rather than a motivation problem. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Finding recurring conditions behind missed actions Separating plan design problems from vague self judgment Changing timing, context or fallback actions from the record self improvement that works track progress pattern spotting Introduction A missed session is not automatically evidence of weak motivation . When misses keep clustering at the same time, place or circumstance, they can be evidence that the plan itself is poorly matched to real life . Three abandoned Tuesday evening workouts after late meetings tell you something different from three misses scattered randomly across two months. Explanatory illustration 1 That is where progress tracking becomes diagnostic rather than merely numerical. Recording whether a planned action happened — alongside a few practical details about when and under what conditions it failed — can reveal recurring friction that memory tends to compress into “I wasn't disciplined enough”. Behaviour change research supports treating capability and opportunity, not just motivation, as causes of behaviour, while planning research shows the value of specifying when, where and how an action will happen and anticipating obstacles. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions PMCApril 2... The useful question after repeated misses is therefore not simply “How do I make myself try harder?” It is “What does the pattern say about the conditions I have chosen?” Look for conditions, not just a failure count A completion percentage tells you how often the plan worked. The pattern of misses can tell you where the plan breaks . Suppose someone plans four 30 minute study sessions each week and completes only half of them. “50% adherence” is useful feedback , but it hides the most actionable information. If nearly every morning session happens while nearly every session planned immediately after work is missed, the problem is concentrated in one context. Moving those sessions may be more sensible than demanding more commitment. This interpretation fits established behaviour change models. The COM B model treats behaviour as the product of interacting capability, opportunity and motivation . Opportunity includes external conditions that make an action possible or prompt it. A recurring collision with childcare, work, transport, unavailable facilities or another commitment can therefore be a genuine behavioural constraint rather than merely an attitude problem. NICE similarly recommends examining social and environmental barriers and reviewing behavioural goals in light of experience. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 For practical tracking, this means that a miss becomes much more informative when attached to a small amount of context. Useful fields might be: Planned: Tuesday, 18:00, 30 minute run Result: missed Condition: meeting finished at 18:20; hungry on arriving home Next comparable session: Thursday, 18:00 Result: missed Condition: work overran again After several repetitions, “I keep skipping running” becomes a more precise diagnosis: “18:00 on working days is unreliable because my finish time is unreliable.” The record has identified something the next plan can actually change. The same reasoning applies beyond exercise . A person may repeatedly fail to write when working in a noisy shared room, miss language practice on commuting days, or abandon Sunday meal preparation whenever the weekend includes travel. The important signal is not that one particular session failed. It is that the same condition predicts failure repeatedly . Behavior Change Wheel & COM B Designing Effective Public Health Interventions Channel: Makerere Epidemiology and Statistical center Open on YouTube Repeated misses can expose a plan design problem Specific planning generally helps people turn intentions into behaviour. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect of implementation intentions — plans linking a situation to a response — on goal attainment. A much larger 2024 review covering 642 tests likewise found beneficial effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with stronger effects when plans used an explicit contingent or “if–then” structure. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... But specificity does not make an unrealistic plan realistic. “I will exercise at 18:00 after work” is precise; it is still badly designed if work routinely finishes at 18:30. Tracking lets specificity meet reality. There are several recurring patterns worth treating as plan information rather than immediately translating them into self judgement. Misses concentrated at one time of day . If early sessions repeatedly happen and late sessions repeatedly fail, timing is an obvious candidate. The original plan may be competing with fatigue, unpredictable work, family responsibilities or other established routines. Misses concentrated in one location or transition. “Study at the library” can fail because getting there adds enough travel friction to make a short session impractical. “Exercise straight after arriving home” can fail because going home itself cues dinner, childcare or relaxation. Misses whenever another event runs late. This is a dependency problem. A plan whose start time depends on an unreliable preceding event inherits that event's variability. Misses under predictable obstacles. Bad weather, travel days, caring responsibilities and late shifts need not be surprises after they have appeared repeatedly. Once an obstacle is recurrent, a plan that continues to ignore it is under specified. Misses mainly when the full version feels impossible. A 45 minute planned session may repeatedly disappear on busy days even though a 10 minute version would have been feasible. That suggests a missing fallback rather than necessarily a missing intention. Research on physical activity planning illustrates the distinction between action planning and coping planning particularly clearly. Action planning specifies what, when, where or how; coping planning anticipates barriers and specifies ways of dealing with them. A meta analysis of more than 150 effect sizes found associations between both forms of planning and physical activity, while a 2025 meta analytic structural equation analysis of 57 samples and 19,883 participants found coping planning to be an important pathway between intention and exercise behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Spontaneous and experimentally induced action planning and coping planning for physical activity: A meta analysis ScienceD... The broader lesson is not that every missed session requires a complicated contingency system. It is that a repeated obstacle deserves promotion from “excuse” to planning variable . Explanatory illustration 2 Separate a flawed plan from vague self judgement Tracking is especially useful because people are poor at diagnosing a pattern from isolated memories. A difficult week can produce a sweeping conclusion — “I'm terrible at sticking to things” — even when the record shows that six of eight planned actions happened and both failures occurred under the same unusual constraint. That distinction matters because the two interpretations prescribe different responses. “I lack discipline” is broad, difficult to test and offers little guidance. “Sessions planned after my Wednesday late shift have failed four weeks running” is narrow, observable and changeable. Behaviour change guidance reflects this problem solving orientation. NICE recommends self monitoring alongside action planning, coping plans and regular review of goals, explicitly describing behavioural goals as something that should be reconsidered in light of experience. [NICE] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 A systematic review of planning interventions for people with chronic conditions likewise identified self monitoring and problem solving among techniques associated with effective longer term interventions. [BMJ Open] bmjopen.bmj.com BMJ OpenBehaviour change techniques that constitute effective planning interventions to improve physical activity and diet behaviour for... A"},{"title":"Progress Avoidance","url":"/progress-avoidance/","description":"People sometimes avoid uncomfortable progress information, removing the feedback that could prompt corrective action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Track Progress","level":3,"text":"Progress Avoidance Why We Avoid Checking Progress When Behind Track Progress Progress Avoidance Progress Avoidance Why We Avoid Checking Progress When Behind People sometimes avoid uncomfortable progress information, removing the feedback that could prompt corrective action. People sometimes avoid uncomfortable progress information, removing the feedback that could prompt corrective action. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism The ostrich problem in goal monitoring Why uncomfortable feedback is easy to avoid Keeping missed targets visible enough to act on self improvement that works track progress progress avoidance Introduction Progress tracking has an awkward weakness: the moment feedback becomes most useful can also be the moment people least want to see it. When spending is running over budget, workouts have been missed or a project is slipping, checking the numbers can turn a suspected shortfall into an undeniable one. Avoiding the tracker offers immediate emotional relief, but it also removes information that could trigger corrective action. Explanatory illustration 1 Researchers call this the “ostrich problem” : deliberately avoiding, delaying or discounting available information about progress towards a goal. It is not simply forgetting to track. The distinctive problem is motivated avoidance — not looking because the answer may be uncomfortable, inconvenient or demanding. [DOI] doi.org DOI‘The Ostrich Problem’: Motivated Avoidance or Rejection of Information About Goal Progress Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psyc... This creates an important qualification to the advice to “track your progress”. Tracking only improves self regulation when the feedback loop remains open during bad weeks as well as good ones. The practical challenge is therefore not merely making progress measurable, but making disappointing measurements tolerable and useful enough that people continue to look. The ostrich problem in goal monitoring Imagine someone who has set a monthly spending limit. Halfway through the month, they suspect they have overspent. Opening the banking app could clarify the situation while there is still time to cancel purchases or reduce discretionary spending. Yet precisely because the balance might confirm a problem, postponing the check can feel attractive. Thomas Webb, Betty Chang and Yael Benn used the “ostrich problem” to describe this broader failure of goal monitoring. Their review distinguishes active monitoring — deliberately seeking information such as checking an account — from information that becomes apparent without deliberate effort. Avoidance can occur at either stage: a person can decline to obtain the information or receive it and then discount, ignore or reinterpret what it means. [DOI] doi.org DOI‘The Ostrich Problem’: Motivated Avoidance or Rejection of Information About Goal Progress Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psyc... That distinction separates progress avoidance from ordinary measurement problems. Someone who cannot obtain reliable information is not necessarily avoiding it. Nor is every imperfect metric evidence of avoidance. But if a student counts pages read because testing their actual understanding would expose weak progress, an apparently respectable measure can become a substitute that protects them from the more diagnostic one. Webb and colleagues explicitly identify this sort of motivated substitution as a possible form of the ostrich problem. [compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online Library... The risk matters because progress monitoring is not just record keeping. A large meta analysis covering 138 randomised studies and 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase monitoring substantially increased monitoring frequency and produced a smaller but meaningful improvement in goal attainment; increases in monitoring statistically mediated improvements in attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Avoiding feedback therefore interrupts a mechanism that can otherwise help translate intentions into action. Why You Avoid Bad News (Ostrich Effect) Channel: Boonmind Open on YouTube Why uncomfortable feedback is easy to avoid The simplest explanation is also an important one: not knowing can feel better right now . Information avoidance has been defined more broadly as behaviour intended to prevent or delay acquiring available but potentially unwanted information. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Information Avoidance: Who, What, When, and Why Sage JournalsInformation Avoidance: Who, What, When, and Why Kate Sweeny, Darya Melnyk, Wendi Miller, James A. Shepperd, 2010... Goal tracking creates a particularly clear version of that conflict because the information often carries an evaluation: actual behaviour is being compared with something the person wanted, promised or expected themselves to do. The ostrich problem framework describes competing motives. Accurate feedback serves self assessment and self improvement: knowing that you are behind can help you change course. But the same information may threaten self enhancement or conflict with an existing view of yourself. Someone can therefore simultaneously want an accurate answer for tomorrow's benefit and want to avoid that answer for today's comfort. [compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online Library... Later empirical work made those motives more concrete. In one study, participants recalled occasions when they had avoided monitoring personal goals. The most strongly endorsed explanations included believing that the information would require an unwanted action or change of beliefs, knowing that progress was poor, and experiencing negative emotions when thinking about the goal or working towards it. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Why Do People Act Like the Proverbial Ostrich? Investigating the Reasons That People Provide for Not Monitoring Thei... This helps explain why avoidance can intensify when a goal is going badly. A progress check does not merely say, “you are behind”. It can imply, “your current approach is not working”, “you need to do something difficult”, or “your earlier assumptions were wrong”. Ignorance temporarily postpones all three conclusions. There is another wrinkle. People can rationalise avoidance in apparently practical terms. In the prospective second study by Webb and colleagues, declining an opportunity to monitor progress was associated with doubts about the accuracy and usefulness of the available information; perceived control over goal attainment also predicted avoidance. The reasons people retrospectively said were important were therefore not identical to the factors that best predicted subsequent monitoring behaviour. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Why Do People Act Like the Proverbial Ostrich? Investigating the Reasons That People Provide for Not Monitoring Thei... That makes the ostrich problem difficult to recognise in oneself. “I don't want to know because I'm behind” may instead become “that number doesn't tell me much anyway”. Sometimes that criticism will be legitimate. The useful question is whether the metric really has stopped informing decisions or whether its apparent uselessness increases precisely when it begins delivering unwelcome news. Explanatory illustration 2 Avoidance protects mood by sacrificing feedback Progress avoidance is not irrational in the narrow sense that it has no benefit. Its benefit is simply concentrated in the present. Not checking can postpone disappointment, embarrassment, anxiety or the obligation to make a difficult decision. The cost arrives later. This is why information avoidance is best understood as a trade off rather than as simple laziness. Research on the ostrich problem proposes that people can face a self control conflict between proximal concerns — avoiding the unpleasant implications of negative feedback — and the more distant objective of improving performance. [compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Webb 2013 Social and Personality Psychology Compass Wiley Online Library... Recent research in health information avoidance also supports a broader defensive interpretation: across two studies involving five samples and 1,850 participants, avoidance and derogation of unwanted information behaved as related self protective strategies. If uncomfortable information was not avoided, people could instead defend themselves by discounting it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Information avoidance: An interchangeable strategy of self protection Information avoidance: An interchangeable strategy of self"},{"title":"Accountability","url":"/accountability-ee899e/","description":"Progress monitoring tends to work better when results are reported to another person rather than kept entirely private.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Track Progress","level":3,"text":"Accountability Does Reporting Your Progress Improve Follow Through? Track Progress Accountability Accountability Does Reporting Your Progress Improve Follow Through? Progress monitoring tends to work better when results are reported to another person rather than kept entirely private. Progress monitoring tends to work better when results are reported to another person rather than kept entirely private. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention What experiments found about reported versus private progress Why accountability may change effort and commitment Low pressure ways to build a useful progress check in self improvement that works track progress accountability Introduction Reporting progress to another person can make tracking more effective than keeping the same information entirely private. The best broad evidence comes from a meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 people: progress monitoring interventions improved goal attainment overall, but the effect was substantially larger when people reported their monitoring results to someone else or monitored them publicly. Privately monitored and reported progress had an average effect size of d = 0.47 , compared with d = 0.19 when privately monitored information stayed private. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Explanatory illustration 1 That does not mean that broadcasting a goal guarantees success, or that everyone needs a demanding “accountability partner”. Reporting appears useful because it changes what happens to the record: somebody else will see it, making missed actions harder to quietly reinterpret or forget. The most defensible practical approach is therefore modest: track a concrete behaviour, send a brief factual update to a supportive person at an agreed interval, and use the conversation to decide what happens next. Reported progress performed better than private tracking The 2016 Psychological Bulletin meta analysis by Benjamin Harkin and colleagues provides the clearest evidence specific to this question. It distinguished three arrangements: monitoring in a genuinely public setting; monitoring privately and then reporting the information to at least one other person; and monitoring privately without reporting it. Examples included weighing in front of a weight loss group, privately measuring something and telephoning the result to a researcher, and privately measuring it without telling anybody. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. The differences were notable. Public monitoring produced an average goal attainment effect of d = 0.55 , although this estimate came from only three studies and therefore deserves caution. Private monitoring followed by reporting, represented by a much larger body of evidence, produced d = 0.47 across 95 studies. Private monitoring that was not reported produced d = 0.19 across 14 studies. The reported versus unreported difference was statistically significant. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. This is more informative than simply observing that highly motivated people tend to find accountability partners. The meta analysis included studies in which participants were randomly allocated to interventions intended to increase progress monitoring. Across the full evidence base, those interventions increased monitoring substantially ( d = 1.98 ) and improved goal attainment ( d = 0.40 ); increases in monitoring frequency statistically mediated improvements in attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... There is an important limitation, however. The reported versus private figures are moderator findings across different studies and intervention designs , not the result of one enormous experiment that randomly assigned thousands of otherwise identical people to “tell someone” or “keep it secret”. Harkin and colleagues explicitly called for direct comparisons that could identify whether accountability itself causes the extra benefit and which psychological mechanism explains it. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Smaller experiments reinforce the need for that caution. In a randomised pilot involving 69 patients using an app to conduct skin self examinations, some participants had an accountability partner who received monthly progress reports. Self examination increased across the study, from 58% at baseline to 83% after six months, but there was no significant difference between intervention groups : adding an accountability partner did not outperform the app alone in this small trial. Satisfaction was also lowest in the accountability partner only group. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Piloting the Use of Smartphones, Reminders, and Accountability Partners to Promote Skin Self Examinations in Patients with Total Bo... So “accountability always works” is too strong. A better reading is that reported monitoring is a promising amplifier of tracking, with strong meta analytic evidence of association between intervention design and larger effects, but individual accountability arrangements do not reliably add value in every setting . How To Improve The Odds Of Reaching Your Goals Channel: The Art of Improvement Open on YouTube Why another person can change the meaning of a tracker A private tracker supplies information. A reported tracker supplies information plus a social consequence . If you planned three study sessions and completed one, a private record can be closed and forgotten. If you have agreed to send “1/3 completed” to somebody on Sunday, the discrepancy has an audience. Harkin and colleagues proposed several possible explanations for the larger effects of reported and public monitoring: stronger public commitment, a feeling of personal accountability, concern about how one presents oneself to other people, and, in experiments, the possibility that participants respond to what they think researchers expect. The meta analysis could not establish which mechanism was responsible. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. More recent research helps fill in part of that picture. In four longitudinal studies involving 1,035 participants , Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler and Tayler Wells examined what happened when people told others about personal goals. In one observational study, disclosure predicted receiving more support and putting in greater goal pursuit effort. In two studies that instructed participants to share a goal, sharing led to greater support from other people, which was in turn associated with greater effort and progress. In a fourth study, telling a person who was physically present increased subsequent goal pursuit effort, although it did not increase reported support in that particular experiment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Goals Out Loud: Telling Others About a Goal Increases Support Received and Facilitates Goal Pursuit PubMedOctober 27, 2025... Published: October 27, 2025 That evidence concerns goal disclosure , rather than repeated progress reporting, so the two should not be treated as identical interventions. It nevertheless identifies a useful interpersonal pathway: once another person knows what you are trying to do, that person can potentially encourage you, remind you, discuss obstacles or otherwise make the goal more socially present. [DOI] doi.org Goals Out Loud: Telling Others About a Goal Increases Support Received and Facilitates Goal Pursuit Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler, Ta... Publicity can also increase commitment after things go wrong. Three experiments reported by Jinfeng Jiao and Catherine Cole found that people high in “self monitoring” — here meaning sensitivity to social presentation, rather than simply recording progress — were more likely to persist following a public goal failure than a private one. The public/private distinction did not produce the same effect among low self monitors. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe Effects of Goal Publicity and Self‐Monitoring on Escalation of Goal Commitment Jiao 2020 Journal of Consume... This suggests an important boundary: social visibility does not exert the same motivational force on everybody. Explanatory illustration 2 Accountability is not the same as announcing a goal The useful distinction is between telling somebody what you intend to do and showing somebody what actually happened . A one off announcement — “I am going to run three times a week” — creates visibility around the intention. A reporting system creates repeated contact with evidence: “I planned three runs; I completed two.” The latter preserves the feedback function of progress tracking while adding another person as an audience. That"},{"title":"Evidence Size","url":"/evidence-size/","description":"Randomised studies suggest tracking improves goal attainment, but the estimated benefit shrinks under some bias adjustments.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Track Progress","level":3,"text":"Evidence Size How Much Does Progress Tracking Actually Help? Track Progress Evidence Size Evidence Size How Much Does Progress Tracking Actually Help? Randomised studies suggest tracking improves goal attainment, but the estimated benefit shrinks under some bias adjustments. Randomised studies suggest tracking improves goal attainment, but the estimated benefit shrinks under some bias adjustments. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the 138 study meta analysis found Why the estimated effect ranges from modest to meaningful Heterogeneity, publication bias and limits of the evidence self improvement that works track progress evidence size Introduction Progress tracking does improve goal attainment, but the best broad estimate is meaningful rather than dramatic . The strongest experiment based synthesis is Benjamin Harkin and colleagues’ meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions designed to increase progress monitoring improved subsequent goal attainment by an average standardised effect of d = 0.40 , with a 95% confidence interval of 0.32 to 0.48. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 There is an important qualification. Statistical adjustment for possible publication bias reduced the estimated effect to d = 0.19 , while a separate analysis restricted to better powered studies produced d = 0.33 . [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence.July 4, 2026... Published: July 4, 2026 So the evidence supports tracking as a useful self regulation tool, but not a guaranteed or enormous boost. A sensible reading is that its average benefit probably lies somewhere between small and moderate , with substantial variation depending on how tracking is done and what goal is being pursued. What the 138 study meta analysis found The Harkin meta analysis is unusually useful for answering “does tracking actually help?” because it focused on experiments rather than simply comparing habitual trackers with non trackers. Participants had to be randomly allocated to an intervention intended to increase monitoring or to a control condition, and the study had to measure both the resulting change in monitoring and subsequent goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The intervention worked extremely well at changing the immediate behaviour it targeted: progress monitoring frequency increased by d = 1.98 (95% CI 1.71 to 2.24). The effect on the thing people ultimately cared about — goal attainment — was considerably smaller at d = 0.40 (95% CI 0.32 to 0.48). [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence White Ros... That distinction is important. Tracking can produce a very large change in how much people track without producing an equally large change in what they achieve . A diary, pedometer, checklist or other monitoring intervention is therefore not a twofold increase in success or a 40% improvement in somebody's outcome. Cohen's d is a standardised difference between groups , not a percentage increase in the probability of succeeding. The result was also reasonably similar when the researchers separated different kinds of goal attainment. Among studies measuring behaviour itself, such as whether people performed a desired action, the pooled effect was d = 0.37 across 35 studies and 5,518 participants. Among studies measuring outcomes, the estimate was d = 0.44 across 86 studies and 10,593 participants. Both groups of studies nevertheless showed substantial variation. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. There was another reason to take the relationship seriously. Across the studies for which the required data could be obtained, increases in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the effect of the interventions on goal attainment: interventions predicted more monitoring, greater monitoring predicted better attainment, and accounting for increased monitoring reduced the direct intervention–attainment association. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. This does not establish every link in the causal mechanism beyond doubt, but it fits the proposition the experiments were designed to test: getting people to monitor more often appears to be part of why monitoring interventions improve results . Goal Setting Is a Hamster Wheel. Learn to Set Systems Instead. Adam Alter Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Why the effect ranges from modest to meaningful An average of d = 0.40 is best understood as a moderate shift across a diverse collection of experiments, not as a promise about what will happen to one person's particular goal. The meta analysis itself provides useful clues about why results differed. Recording mattered. In the authors' moderator analysis, studies in which progress was physically recorded had an average goal attainment effect of d = 0.43 , compared with d = 0.29 where monitoring was not recorded. The difference between those categories was statistically significant. [Scribd] scribd.com Monitoring Goal Progress and Attainment PDF Goal Goal Setting Monitoring Goal Progress and Attainment PDF Goal Goal SettingOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 Reporting progress also mattered. Monitoring that remained private and was not reported produced an average effect of d = 0.19 . When privately monitored progress was nevertheless reported to somebody , the estimate was d = 0.47 . The small group of three studies classified as public monitoring produced d = 0.55, although its confidence interval was very wide, so that particular number should not be treated as a precise estimate of the advantage of public accountability. [Scribd] scribd.com Monitoring Goal Progress and Attainment PDF Goal Goal Setting Monitoring Goal Progress and Attainment PDF Goal Goal SettingOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 The American Psychological Association's summary of the research consequently highlighted recording and reporting as conditions associated with larger effects. [American Psychological Association] apa.org American Psychological AssociationFrequently monitoring progress toward goals increases chance of successOctober 28, 2015... Published: October 28, 2015 These subgroup findings help explain why “tracking works” is too crude a conclusion. A mental check in that disappears immediately may not be equivalent to maintaining a written record. Nor is silently noticing progress necessarily equivalent to knowing that the result will be reported. The overall d = 0.40 combines interventions with quite different levels of structure and accountability. At the same time, these moderator results require more caution than the headline randomised comparison. Participants were randomised to monitoring interventions versus controls, but the 138 studies were not collectively one enormous experiment randomly assigning people to “recorded” versus “unrecorded” or “reported” versus “unreported” tracking. Those comparisons describe differences between sets of studies , so they are suggestive about what makes tracking stronger rather than definitive estimates of the causal contribution of each feature. Explanatory illustration 2 Publication bias pulls the headline estimate down The most important reason not to present d = 0.40 as the final word is the meta analysis's own bias analysis. Comparing published with unpublished research initially looked reassuring. The 129 published studies produced a goal attainment estimate of d = 0.40 , while the nine unpublished studies produced d = 0.42 — essentially no difference. However, a statistical test for funnel plot asymmetry, Egger's regression, was significant for goal attainment. That raised the possibility that smaller or unfavourable results were underrepresented in the available literature. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. The researchers therefore applied a “trim and fill” analysis, a method that estimates how the pooled result might change if apparently missing studies were added to make the distribution more symmetrical. It imputed 31 additional goal attainment effect sizes and reduced the estimate from d = 0.40 to d = 0.19 (95% CI 0.10 to 0.28). [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the"},{"title":"Too Small","url":"/too-small/","description":"Making a habit easier can improve repeatability, but the minimum still needs to contain a real dose of the behaviour you want to build.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Small Habits","level":3,"text":"Too Small When a Tiny Habit Becomes Too Tiny Small Habits Too Small Too Small When a Tiny Habit Becomes Too Tiny Making a habit easier can improve repeatability, but the minimum still needs to contain a real dose of the behaviour you want to build. Making a habit easier can improve repeatability, but the minimum still needs to contain a real dose of the behaviour you want to build. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism Why ease improves perceived feasibility The difference between a starting ritual and meaningful practice Setting a minimum that advances the underlying aim self improvement that works small habits too small Introduction Making a habit easier is often sensible: a behaviour that survives busy, tired and inconvenient days gets more chances to be repeated. But “smaller” is not automatically “better”. A minimum habit becomes too small when it reliably preserves the ritual of starting while delivering little or none of the behaviour that the person actually wants to develop. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because habit formation and goal progress are not the same thing. Repeating an action in a stable context can strengthen automaticity , but the action being automatised is the action actually performed. Research on habits distinguishes automatically initiating a behavioural episode from automatically executing its substantive components. A person can therefore become excellent at opening a book, putting on running shoes or opening a writing document without necessarily accumulating much reading, exercise or writing. The useful minimum is consequently not the smallest action imaginable. It is the smallest repeatable action that still contains a meaningful dose of the behaviour being built. Why ease improves feasibility There is good reason to reduce the difficulty of a new habit. Habit formation depends substantially on repetition in recurring contexts. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues' real world study, participants chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition, but the estimated time to reach 95% of its eventual level varied greatly, from 18 to 254 days among participants whose trajectories could be modelled. Missing a single opportunity did not materially disrupt the process. A behaviour that is too demanding for ordinary life can therefore undermine itself. If the full version requires unusual motivation, abundant time or ideal circumstances, there may simply be too few repetitions for a dependable cue–behaviour relationship to develop. Clinical habit formation guidance has accordingly recommended manageable, realistic changes rather than ambitious behaviours that are difficult to repeat. Reducing the minimum can also make initiation less dependent on deliberation. Implementation intentions — plans that connect a situation to a specific response, such as “when X happens, I will do Y” — have been shown to improve action initiation and goal attainment. A meta analysis covering 94 independent tests reported a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, while experimental work has found that such plans can facilitate initiation even under cognitive load. This gives small habits their legitimate advantage. “After breakfast, read for ten minutes” contains fewer opportunities for negotiation than “find time today to study properly”. A modest action with a clear cue is easier to enact repeatedly. But ease solves an initiation problem . It does not establish that the resulting dose is adequate for the underlying objective. How to become 37.78 times better at anything Atomic Habits summary (by James Clear) Channel: Escaping Ordinary (B.C Marx) Open on YouTube Starting is not the same as practising The crucial failure mode appears when the minimum is reduced until its main accomplishment is triggering the beginning of an activity. Habit research provides a useful distinction between habitual instigation and habitual execution . Instigation concerns automatically selecting and starting an action; execution concerns automatically performing the sequence of actions that follows. Research by Benjamin Gardner and colleagues found that habitual instigation predicted behavioural frequency, whereas habitual execution did not show the same relationship. Later work across behaviours of different complexity likewise found habitual instigation to be the more important predictor of frequency. That makes a starting ritual valuable, but also exposes its limitation. Consider four deliberately extreme minima: opening a novel and immediately closing it; putting on running shoes without doing any physical activity ; opening a language learning application but completing no exercise; creating a blank document and calling the writing habit complete. Each action can potentially become cue driven and automatic. Yet each trains a behaviour that is importantly different from the stated objective. The book opening habit does not by itself provide reading practice; the shoes do not provide exercise; the blank document does not develop written output. This is not an argument that every repetition must be large. It is an argument about what counts as the target response . Habit research generally describes habits as context linked responses strengthened through repeated performance. If the repeated response is merely preparation, preparation is what receives the repetitions. A starting ritual can still be useful when it reliably leads onwards. Opening the document may be an excellent cue for writing; putting on trainers may remove friction from going outside. But in that case its value lies in being a gateway , not in being an adequate substitute for the behaviour beyond the gate. Explanatory illustration 2 The minimum needs a real dose The phrase “too small to matter” needs care because there is no universal minimum dose across self improvement behaviours. In some domains, surprisingly small quantities genuinely count. Physical activity is a particularly important counterexample to the idea that short automatically means worthless. The World Health Organization removed the old requirement that moderate to vigorous physical activity had to occur in bouts of at least ten minutes to count. Its guidelines state that some physical activity is better than none, that activity of any duration can contribute, and that inactive adults can begin with small amounts before increasing frequency, intensity and duration. But the same evidence also demonstrates why “anything counts” should not be confused with “amount does not matter”. WHO describes a dose–response relationship in which greater volumes of activity are associated with greater benefits, while recommending 150–300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week, or its vigorous equivalent, for substantial health benefits in adults. One minute of walking is therefore not equivalent to a week containing 150 minutes simply because both are preferable to complete inactivity. Skill development makes the same distinction from another direction. Research on deliberate practice emphasises repeated performance of tasks designed to improve particular aspects of performance, combined with feedback , evaluation and opportunities for refinement. The wider deliberate practice literature contains genuine disputes about definitions and how much expertise it explains, so it should not be turned into a simplistic formula. Yet the basic point relevant here is modest: performing the skill is different from performing a ritual adjacent to the skill . For someone learning a musical instrument, taking the instrument from its case may reduce the barrier to practice. It is not itself practice of timing, technique or repertoire. For someone learning a language, opening a vocabulary deck may be a useful cue, but recalling several words supplies a more defensible minimum because it actually exercises retrieval. The meaningful minimum contains at least some of the mechanism through which improvement is expected to occur. The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Beware of optimising the streak Tiny habits can create a measurement problem as well as a behavioural one. Once success is defined as completing an exceptionally easy minimum, a perfect record can coexist with weak progress. Imagine that the goal is to read more deeply. A person defines success as “read one sentence per day” and completes it for 100 consecutive days. The streak accurately records 100 repetitions of the minimum. What it cannot establish is that the person has read a meaningful quantity, improved concentration or developed the capacity for sustained reading. This matters because repetition can strengthen habits even when the behaviour's connection to a larger goal is imperfect. Contemporary habit research emphasises that habits emerge as rewarding responses are"},{"title":"Missed Days","url":"/missed-days-1120cb/","description":"One missed opportunity need not derail habit formation, so the useful skill is resuming at the next available cue without overcompensating.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Small Habits","level":3,"text":"Missed Days What to Do After Missing a Habit Small Habits Missed Days Missed Days What to Do After Missing a Habit One missed opportunity need not derail habit formation, so the useful skill is resuming at the next available cue without overcompensating. One missed opportunity need not derail habit formation, so the useful skill is resuming at the next available cue without overcompensating. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What habit research says about a single missed opportunity Why perfect streaks are a fragile measure of consistency Resuming normally instead of compensating with extra effort self improvement that works small habits missed days Introduction Missing one day does not mean a habit has gone back to zero. The best direct evidence comes from a 12 week real world habit formation study: participants who missed a single opportunity did not materially disrupt the growth of automaticity , whereas broader inconsistency was more concerning. The practical response is therefore simple: resume the ordinary version of the habit at the next appropriate cue rather than trying to compensate for the miss . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters because habit formation is built from repeated cue–behaviour associations, not from maintaining an aesthetically perfect calendar. A streak can motivate repetition, but research also shows that highlighting a broken streak can reduce subsequent engagement. Treating one missed day as a major failure may therefore create a second problem that the original miss did not: reluctance to restart. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 Katy Milkman: What I've Learned from Applying The Fresh Start Effect Channel: John R. Miles Open on YouTube What one missed opportunity actually does The most useful dataset for this question is Phillippa Lally and colleagues' study of 96 volunteers. Participants selected an eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour and attempted to perform it once a day in the same context for 12 weeks. They recorded both whether they had performed the behaviour and how automatic it felt. The researchers then modelled changes in automaticity over the 84 day study. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The headline result is unusually reassuring: missing one opportunity to perform the behaviour did not materially affect the habit formation process . A later account by researchers involved in this work describes automaticity gains as resuming after an occasional missed performance. In other words, the learning trajectory did not behave like a counter that reset to zero when somebody missed Tuesday. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That does not mean frequency is irrelevant. In the same dataset, greater consistency of performance was associated with better fitting habit development trajectories, and UCL's account of the research explicitly distinguishes an isolated miss from being “very inconsistent”. The study did not establish a precise minimum percentage of opportunities that must be completed. Its narrower finding is stronger and more defensible: occasional misses appear compatible with habit formation; persistent inconsistency should not be assumed to be harmless . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This fits the underlying mechanism. Psychological habit research describes habits as cue–response associations acquired through context dependent repetition. Repeatedly acting in a particular situation strengthens the association between that situation and the response, so that encountering the context increasingly prompts the behaviour with less deliberation. Experimental work has since provided further evidence that practice strengthens cue–response associations rather than merely strengthening people's intentions to act. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour PMC... A missed opportunity is therefore better understood as one repetition that did not occur , not as the destruction of all previous learning. Established habit associations are not generally thought to shift dramatically because of an infrequent counter habitual response, and the Lally dataset provides direct real world evidence that a single omission need not seriously alter the formation trajectory. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... There is an important limit to how far this finding can be stretched. Lally and colleagues studied daily health related behaviours in a relatively small sample, and only 62 participants produced automaticity trajectories that could be modelled. A 2024 systematic review likewise concluded that the habit formation literature remains limited and that the degree of repetition consistency required is still unclear. “One miss is not disastrous” is evidence based; “it does not matter how often you miss” is not. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Get Back on Track With Habits using 3 Simple Tricks Channel: Ljubi Productivity Open on YouTube Why a perfect streak is the wrong scorecard A streak answers one narrow question: how many consecutive scheduled performances have occurred? Habit strength answers a different question: how automatically does a particular context evoke the behaviour? Those measures can overlap, but they are not interchangeable. Research on logged streaks makes the difference particularly clear. Jackie Silverman and Alixandra Barasch studied streak tracking across seven experiments involving domains including exercise , language learning and games. They found that highlighting an intact streak increased subsequent engagement compared with highlighting a broken streak. Crucially, the effect could occur even when participants' actual preceding behaviour was identical: changing how the behavioural history was represented changed what people did next. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 That makes streaks potentially useful motivational devices, but also fragile ones. In one experiment described by the researchers, participants performed the same four strength exercises, yet some were shown a log in which the fourth exercise appeared not to have registered. When subsequently given a choice, 66.23% of those shown an intact streak selected another strength exercise, compared with 57.86% of those shown the apparently broken streak. The behavioural history was effectively the same; the displayed continuity differed. [INSEAD Knowledge] knowledge.insead.edu INSEAD KnowledgeConsumer Streaks Are Motivating – The Key Is Keeping Them Alive INSEAD KnowledgeJune 22, 2022... Published: June 22, 2022 The broader experiments found that people came to treat preserving the logged streak as a goal in its own right. The disadvantage appeared after a break: highlighting a broken streak reduced engagement relative to highlighting an intact one, with the effect stronger when people attributed the break to themselves and weaker when they had an opportunity to “repair” it. [UD Space] udspace.udel.edu UD Space On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions UD Space On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions This does not establish that streak tracking is bad. An unbroken run of repetitions can encourage another repetition, which is useful during habit formation. The problem is making the streak the definition of success. If the display changes from “37” to “0”, the habit itself has not necessarily undergone an equivalent transformation. The habit learning evidence and the streak research point to different processes: one missed opportunity need not materially derail automaticity, while perceiving a streak as broken can nevertheless weaken motivation to continue. [Wiley Online Library]"},{"title":"Choice Overload","url":"/choice-overload/","description":"More options are not automatically harmful; reducing choice helps most when uncertainty or complexity interferes with an action already chosen.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fewer Decisions","level":3,"text":"Choice Overload When Fewer Choices Help and When They Do Not Fewer Decisions Choice Overload Choice Overload When Fewer Choices Help and When They Do Not More options are not automatically harmful; reducing choice helps most when uncertainty or complexity interferes with an action already chosen. More options are not automatically harmful; reducing choice helps most when uncertainty or complexity interferes with an action already chosen. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Why large choice sets do not always reduce performance The conditions that make choice overload more likely Separating useful exploration from poorly timed reconsideration self improvement that works fewer decisions choice overload Introduction Choice overload is real, but the popular rule that “fewer choices are better” is not. Research instead points to a conditional effect: large choice sets become most troublesome when the options are difficult to compare, the chooser is unsure what they want, the decision itself is demanding, or the immediate goal is to finish rather than explore. When those conditions are absent, additional options can be harmless or positively useful. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicCan There Ever Be Too Many Options? A Meta Analytic Review of Choice Overload Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academi... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. Reducing choices is most defensible not as a general lifestyle principle, but as a way of protecting execution once a direction has already been chosen. Exploring several exercise programmes while deciding what suits you may be useful. Reopening that comparison when it is time for today's workout may simply reintroduce uncertainty at the point where action matters. The research therefore supports a narrower principle: preserve useful choice during exploration, but reduce unnecessary reconsideration during execution. Why large choice sets do not always reduce performance The modern choice overload debate is often traced to Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper's 2000 experiments. In their well known field study, shoppers encountered a display containing either six or 24 varieties of jam. Across the paper's three experiments, smaller sets were associated with greater likelihood of acting or completing an optional task, and in some settings with greater satisfaction afterwards. The findings challenged the appealing assumption that adding options must make people better off. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med When choice is demotivating: can one desire too much of a good thing? When choice is demotivating: can one desire too much of a good thing? PubMed... But subsequent research made the story substantially less universal. A 2010 meta analysis by Benjamin Scheibehenne, Rainer Greifeneder and Peter Todd assembled 63 experimental conditions from 50 published and unpublished experiments, involving 5,036 participants. Its average choice overload effect was virtually zero . Importantly, however, effects varied considerably between studies. Some experiments showed overload; others showed no meaningful disadvantage from greater choice, and some found benefits from having more alternatives. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicCan There Ever Be Too Many Options? A Meta Analytic Review of Choice Overload Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academi... That result changes the question. Instead of asking whether six choices are inherently better than 24, it is more useful to ask what the chooser has to do with those choices. A later meta analysis by Alexander Chernev, Ulf Böckenholt and Joseph Goodman addressed precisely that issue. It analysed 99 observations from 53 studies involving 7,202 participants and concluded that the effect of assortment size depended systematically on four factors: choice set complexity, decision task difficulty, preference uncertainty and the chooser's decision goal . Once those moderators were considered, the authors found evidence for an overall overload effect, in contrast with the virtually zero average reported by the earlier meta analysis. [DOI] doi.org Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta‐analysis Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta‐analysis Chernev 2015 Journal of Consumer Psychology Wiley Online Library... The two meta analyses are therefore less contradictory than they first appear. The 2010 result warns against treating option count itself as a reliable cause of poor decisions. The later analysis argues that overload becomes more predictable once the conditions surrounding the choice are taken seriously. Together they support a conditional rather than universal account: the number of alternatives matters partly because of what those alternatives require from the person choosing among them. Episode 027: Too Many Choices with Dr. Sheena Iyengar Channel: Alliance for Decision Education Open on YouTube When fewer choices are most likely to help The 2015 meta analysis provides a particularly useful framework for deciding when reduction is worth attempting. All four of its proposed moderators were statistically significant: overload became more likely as the choice set became more complex, the decision became more difficult, preferences became less certain and an effort minimising goal became more prominent. [DOI] doi.org Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta‐analysis Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta‐analysis Chernev 2015 Journal of Consumer Psychology Wiley Online Library... Complex options create more trouble than merely numerous ones. Ten alternatives that can be compared on one familiar dimension may be easier to handle than five alternatives involving several awkward trade offs. Greifeneder, Scheibehenne and Kleber experimentally investigated this distinction and argued that choice complexity reflects not only the number of alternatives but also the number and structure of attributes distinguishing them. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Less may be more when choosing is difficult: Choice complexity and too much choice ScienceDirect... Gourville and Soman demonstrated a related distinction between alignable and non alignable assortments. An alignable set might contain otherwise similar products differing mainly along one comparable dimension. A non alignable set instead forces trade offs across different features. Across three studies, they found evidence that larger non alignable assortments could increase effort and anticipated regret, whereas larger alignable assortments could accommodate diverse preferences without producing the same penalty. Simplifying the information presentation could reduce or eliminate the overchoice effect. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org Overchoice and Assortment Type: When and Why Variety Backfires Marketing ScienceAugust 1, 2005... Published: August 1, 2005 That is important because simply counting options can mistake the symptom for the mechanism. Consider choosing a running route. Six routes presented only by distance — 2 km, 3 km, 4 km and so on — impose a relatively straightforward comparison. Six workout programmes varying simultaneously in duration, equipment, intensity, progression, training philosophy and weekly frequency pose a qualitatively different decision. Cutting the first list from six to three may accomplish little. Curating the second can materially reduce the comparison problem. Uncertain preferences make large sets harder to exploit. Chernev's experiments found that people with an articulated “ideal point” — a clearer idea of the attributes they wanted — were more likely to prefer larger assortments. When people know what they are seeking, additional options increase the chance of finding a close match and can therefore become an advantage rather than a burden. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Product assortment and individual decision processes Product assortment and individual decision processes PubMedJuly 1, 2003... Published: July 1, 2003 This produces an important limit on choice reduction advice. A large library of books is not necessarily overwhelming to someone who knows they want a short history of nineteenth century Britain. The same library is a different decision environment for someone whose criterion is merely “I ought to read something useful”. The latter problem is not simply an excess of books; it is an excess of alternatives combined with an underspecified preference. Task difficulty changes the value of variety. The harder it is to compare alternatives, obtain the relevant information or make the necessary trade offs, the more likely additional choice is to impose costs. Evidence from healthcare illustrates the general mechanism. In an experiment involving hypothetical selection of primary care doctors, increasing decision complexity could reduce decision quality, with consumer skills and decision making style explaining only part of the deterioration."},{"title":"Decision Fatigue","url":"/decision-fatigue/","description":"Pre-deciding useful actions can reduce friction without assuming that every earlier choice drains a fixed reserve of willpower.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fewer Decisions","level":3,"text":"Decision Fatigue Does Decision Fatigue Really Explain Why Planning Helps? Fewer Decisions Decision Fatigue Decision Fatigue Does Decision Fatigue Really Explain Why Planning Helps? Pre deciding useful actions can reduce friction without assuming that every earlier choice drains a fixed reserve of willpower. Pre deciding useful actions can reduce friction without assuming that every earlier choice drains a fixed reserve of willpower. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence What classic ego depletion theory claimed What large replication studies found Stronger reasons to reduce decisions at action time self improvement that works fewer decisions decision fatigue Introduction Reducing daily choices can be useful, but the familiar explanation — that every decision drains a fixed reservoir of willpower — is much less secure than popular “decision fatigue” advice often suggests. Classic ego depletion experiments proposed that acts of choice and self control consume a common limited resource. Early studies and a 2010 meta analysis appeared to support that account, but later bias analyses and two large preregistered multi laboratory projects found effects close to zero. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ego depletion: is the active self a limited resource? Ego depletion: is the active self a limited resource? PubMed... Explanatory illustration 1 That does not make pre deciding actions pointless. It changes the reason for doing it. A stronger evidence based case is that a good plan removes ambiguity at the moment of action, makes the relevant cue easier to recognise and links that cue to a response already chosen. In practical self improvement, “decide beforehand” remains sensible advice without requiring the metaphor of a willpower battery. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... What classic ego depletion theory actually claimed The influential version of ego depletion theory emerged from experiments in the late 1990s. Its central claim was unusually broad: different acts of deliberate control could depend on a shared, limited resource, so exerting control in one activity would temporarily impair performance on another. One early experiment, for example, had participants resist tempting chocolates and eat radishes instead; those participants subsequently gave up sooner on unsolvable puzzles. Other experiments connected emotion suppression, thought suppression and deliberate choice with poorer performance or persistence on a later task. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Self control as limited resource: regulatory depletion patterns Self control as limited resource: regulatory depletion patterns PubMed... This became the “strength model” of self control. A 2000 review compared self regulation to muscular exertion: using self control was proposed to consume some limited capacity, leaving less available for the next demand. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulation and depletion of limited resources: does self control resemble a muscle? PubMed... That framework supplied an intuitive explanation for an everyday experience — after a demanding day, resisting temptation or making another difficult decision can certainly feel harder. The theory became directly relevant to decision reduction through experiments by Kathleen Vohs and colleagues. Their 2008 paper reported that participants who made repeated choices about consumer products or university courses subsequently showed poorer performance on measures including physical stamina, persistence, procrastination and arithmetic. A field component also found that shoppers reporting more previous active decision making showed less self control afterwards. The authors interpreted the findings as evidence that choosing and self control draw on the same limited resource. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Making choices impairs subsequent self control: a limited resource account of decision making, self regulation, and active initiati... The resulting practical story was compelling: every choice costs a little willpower, so eliminating minor decisions should preserve that resource for important ones. But that story makes a stronger scientific claim than the practical advice actually needs. It assumes not merely that repeated difficult decisions can be tiring, but that choosing causes subsequent self control impairment because a general purpose resource has been depleted. Initially, the quantitative literature appeared to support that interpretation. A prominent 2010 meta analysis concluded that ego depletion produced a medium to large effect, conventionally reported as d = 0.62. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis Ego depletion and the strength model of self control: a meta analysis PubMed... For several years, that estimate helped make the limited resource model look like a well established foundation for advice about conserving willpower. Roy Baumeister Willpower: Self Control, Decision Fatigue, and Energy Depletion Channel: TheIHMC Open on YouTube Large replications changed the picture The first major warning concerned the published evidence itself. Carter and McCullough reanalysed the earlier literature using techniques intended to detect small study and publication effects. They found strong indications of publication bias and estimated that, after correction, the depletion effect could be indistinguishable from zero. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A subsequent series of meta analytic tests by Carter, Kofler, Forster and McCullough reached a similarly sceptical conclusion. Using more restrictive criteria and methods intended to address limitations of the earlier evidence base, the researchers found very little support for a depletion effect as conventionally measured in laboratory experiments. They argued that the results strongly challenged the claim that self control behaves as though it relies on a limited psychological or physical resource. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The dispute then moved from reanalysing old studies to conducting much larger, preregistered tests. In 2016, Martin Hagger and colleagues coordinated 23 laboratories using a standardised ego depletion procedure, producing a combined sample of 2,141 participants. The estimated effect was d = 0.04, with a 95% confidence interval from −0.07 to 0.15. In other words, the estimate was tiny and statistically compatible with no depletion effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego Depletion Effect PubMed... That replication did not settle every methodological dispute. Critics argued, among other things, that its initial depletion manipulation might not have been sufficiently demanding, illustrating a recurring difficulty in this literature: a null result is harder to interpret when researchers disagree about whether the experimental procedure successfully induced the proposed state in the first place. Reviews of the controversy note that many earlier studies had not included strong manipulation checks either. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. A second major preregistered effort therefore matters. Published in 2021, it involved 36 laboratories and 3,531 participants and deliberately used a “paradigmatic replication” approach rather than simply repeating the earlier standardised procedure. Its confirmatory estimate was again very small, d = 0.06, and non significant. Bayesian analysis found the observed data four times more likely under the null hypothesis than under the researchers' specified depletion hypothesis. An exploratory analysis using the full sample produced a statistically significant d = 0.08, but that is still far smaller than the effect suggested by the influential earlier literature. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A Multisite Preregistered Paradigmatic Test of the Ego Depletion Effect A Multisite Preregistered Paradigmatic Test of the Ego Depletion Effect PubMed... These results do not prove that previous mental effort can never affect later behaviour. Indeed, proponents of ego depletion theory continue to argue that the original model should be refined rather than abandoned, with greater attention to the strength and duration of the initial exertion, fatigue and conservation of effort. A 2024 review by Baumeister and colleagues makes precisely that case. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. The narrower conclusion is more defensible: large preregistered studies do not support treating a sizeable, automatic depletion of a general willpower resource as an established fact. “Decision fatigue” is broader than a willpower battery There is another"},{"title":"Exact Timing","url":"/exact-timing/","description":"Choosing an exact date and time can improve follow-through when an important opportunity is easy to postpone or miss.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fewer Decisions","level":3,"text":"Exact Timing When an Exact Time Makes Follow Through More Likely Fewer Decisions Exact Timing Exact Timing When an Exact Time Makes Follow Through More Likely Choosing an exact date and time can improve follow through when an important opportunity is easy to postpone or miss. Choosing an exact date and time can improve follow through when an important opportunity is easy to postpone or miss. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case dataset evidence What the workplace vaccination experiment tested Why date and time planning outperformed a date alone Where exact scheduling is most useful in everyday goals self improvement that works fewer decisions exact timing Introduction Choosing an exact date and time can make follow through more likely when an important action is easy to postpone and the opportunity to do it is brief. The clearest real world demonstration comes from a large workplace experiment on influenza vaccination: employees who were merely reminded about available clinics were less likely to attend than employees prompted to write down exactly when they would go. Crucially, specifying both the date and time worked better than specifying the date alone. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PubMedJune 28, 2011... Published: June 28, 2011 Explanatory illustration 1 The lesson is not that every goal belongs in a tightly controlled calendar. It is more specific: when you already intend to do something, there is a known opportunity to act, and missing that opportunity means delay or failure, deciding the precise moment beforehand can remove a consequential decision from the moment itself. That makes exact scheduling particularly relevant to appointments, applications, registrations and other short lived opportunities. How to Change: Katy Milkman Channel: Rotman School of Management Open on YouTube What the workplace vaccination experiment tested In 2009, Katherine Milkman and colleagues ran a randomised field experiment with 3,272 employees of a large Midwestern US utility company. The employees were eligible for free influenza vaccinations at on site workplace clinics. Clinics were held across 62 work locations between 13 October and 16 November, with availability varying substantially: at some sites the clinic operated for only part of one day, while others had clinics lasting several days. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PMC... Everyone received a mailing containing information about the vaccination clinics, including the relevant dates, times and locations. The experiment therefore did not simply compare people who knew when clinics were available with people who did not. Instead, it tested whether getting employees to convert that information into a personal plan changed behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PMC... The experimental mailings added a small planning prompt. Depending on assignment, employees were given space to write down either the date on which they intended to receive the vaccination or both the date and time . A control group received the clinic information without this extra planning exercise . The intervention did not make vaccination compulsory, offer an additional financial incentive or automatically book anybody into an appointment; it simply asked some employees to make one scheduling decision in advance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PMC... That apparently minor distinction produced the central result. The control group's vaccination rate was 33.1%. In the paper's regression adjusted headline comparison, prompting employees to record a date alone increased vaccination by 1.5 percentage points, a difference that was not statistically significant. Prompting them to record a date and time increased vaccination by 4.2 percentage points, and that difference was statistically significant. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PubMedJune 28, 2011... Published: June 28, 2011 The raw rates tell the same basic story: 33.1% of the control group, 35.6% of the date planning group and 37.1% of the date and time group were vaccinated. The precise estimates vary depending on the statistical specification because clinic characteristics were not perfectly balanced across the experimental groups, which is why the researchers reported adjusted analyses as well as raw comparisons. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PMC... The useful finding is therefore not merely that reminders help. Everyone was reminded. What differed was whether recipients were prompted to turn an available opportunity into a personally selected moment for action. Implementation Intentions Increase Goal Completion by 40% Channel: Sam Martin Peak Performance Open on YouTube Why date and time beat a date alone A date still leaves an unresolved decision. “I will get vaccinated on Thursday” can become Thursday morning, then lunchtime, then later in the afternoon. Each shift preserves the intention while postponing execution. “Thursday at 11.30” supplies a much sharper answer to the question of when action begins. This fits the broader research on implementation intentions : plans that connect a desired action to a particular future situation. A major meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found that implementation intentions had an overall medium to large positive effect on goal attainment. A newer meta analysis covering 642 independent tests likewise found positive effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with larger effects when plans used a contingent if–then structure, when motivation was already high and when plans were rehearsed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... One plausible reason specificity matters is prospective memory — remembering to carry out an intention later while ordinary life is competing for attention. A systematic review and meta analysis of 36 comparisons found that implementation intentions produced a medium sized overall improvement in prospective memory performance. The practical point is that a plan does more than record what somebody wants: it can help make the intended response retrievable when its cue arrives. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: A systematic and meta analytic review ScienceDirectMarch 30... The vaccination experiment cannot, by itself, prove exactly which psychological process caused its effect. The researchers did not experimentally isolate memory, procrastination, calendar conflict and other possible mechanisms. It is therefore safer to say that precise scheduling reduces ambiguity about execution than to claim that it works through one proven mechanism. Reviews of planning interventions similarly argue that concrete scheduling can both structure action and make the intention easier to recall at the relevant moment. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsBeyond good intentions: Prompting people to make plans improves follow through on important tasks Todd Rogers, Katherine L... There is another important clue in the workplace data. The date and time prompt had a larger effect at locations where the vaccination clinic was available for only one day than at locations with multi day clinics. The researchers interpreted this as evidence that planning prompts may be especially valuable when the opportunity to act is fleeting. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using implementation intentions prompts to enhance influenza vaccination rates PMC... That result makes intuitive sense without requiring a claim that precision is always superior. If a clinic runs for five days, failing to go at 11.30 on Tuesday still leaves Wednesday, Thursday and Friday. If it runs for one afternoon, repeated postponement can exhaust the opportunity altogether. Exact scheduling has more potential value when “later” is a particularly dangerous alternative. Explanatory illustration 2 Where exact scheduling is most useful in everyday goals The workplace study supports a fairly narrow practical rule: use exact scheduling when the goal is already chosen but execution can disappear into a short window of opportunity . This is different from trying to timetable every aspect of daily life. Good candidates share several features. The action is concrete rather than vague; you genuinely intend to perform it; there is a"},{"title":"Action Sequences","url":"/action-sequences/","description":"Deciding the first few steps before a recurring task begins can prevent fresh deliberation from interrupting execution halfway through.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fewer Decisions","level":3,"text":"Action Sequences Preplan the Next Steps Before Hesitation Returns Fewer Decisions Action Sequences Action Sequences Preplan the Next Steps Before Hesitation Returns Deciding the first few steps before a recurring task begins can prevent fresh deliberation from interrupting execution halfway through. Deciding the first few steps before a recurring task begins can prevent fresh deliberation from interrupting execution halfway through. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Why one trigger may not eliminate later decision points How short predetermined sequences keep behavior moving Where to stop scripting and leave room for judgment self improvement that works fewer decisions action sequences Introduction Preplanning a task should not stop at the first move when the task predictably contains later moments of hesitation. A useful action sequence settles a small run of decisions in advance: “When I finish breakfast, I open the document, read the last paragraph, write the next rough paragraph, then check messages.” The point is not rigid scheduling. It is to prevent each transition from reopening the question of what to do next. Explanatory illustration 1 Research on implementation intentions — specific plans linking a recognisable cue to a response — supports the underlying principle. Such plans can make intended actions easier to retrieve and initiate, including under cognitive load, and can help protect ongoing goal pursuit from distraction. But the evidence also supplies an important limit: more plans are not automatically better. Multiple or competing plans can interfere with one another, and highly scripted responses can become a liability when circumstances require an unexpected switch. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation PubMed... For self improvement that works , the practical target is therefore a short, coherent sequence through predictable decision points , not a script for the whole day. Why one trigger may not eliminate later decision points An initial cue solves an initiation problem. It does not necessarily solve a continuation problem. Suppose someone has already decided, “At 7 pm, I will start studying at my desk.” That removes one important choice. Yet five minutes later the person may encounter another set of questions: Which chapter comes first? Should I reread notes or attempt questions? Do I look up this unfamiliar term now? Is this a sensible point to check my phone? The original plan successfully got the person into the chair but left the subsequent transitions undecided. That distinction is consistent with the broader implementation intention literature. The US National Cancer Institute's behavioural research guidance notes that complex goals can involve many behaviours and self regulatory problems, making more than one if–then plan potentially useful. It also specifies demanding conditions for doing this well: plans should be precise, feasible, useful for the goal and non overlapping. If a person still has to deliberate about the appropriate response when the cue occurs, much of the advantage of deciding beforehand has been lost. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Cancer ControlImplementation Intentions Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences (DCCPS)... This matters because remembering what comes next is itself a cognitive task. A meta analysis of 36 comparisons examining implementation intentions and prospective memory — remembering to carry out an intended future action — found an overall medium sized improvement in prospective memory performance. The estimated effect was d = 0.508 overall; among healthy young adults it was d = 0.445. The review also cautioned against assuming that the process is completely automatic: some conditions showed a small cost to performance on the ongoing task. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: a systematic and meta analytic review PubMedMarch 30, 2015... Published: March 30, 2015 Experiments under high cognitive load reinforce that qualification. Brandstätter, Lengfelder and Gollwitzer found that implementation intentions could facilitate rapid action initiation even when participants faced substantial cognitive demands. Later work by McDaniel and Scullin, however, found that implementation intention encoding did not prevent prospective memory performance from declining under high demand. Preplanning can make the intended response more available; it does not make human attention unlimited. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation PubMed... The implication for action sequences is specific. Do not merely pre decide how a recurring task begins. Pre decide the next few transitions that repeatedly cause the task to stall. How short predetermined sequences keep behaviour moving The most useful sequence converts predictable transition points into cues for the next concrete action. Rather than “work on the report for an hour”, a person might use: How to Automate Your Focus: The Science of If Then Rules Channel: The Research File Open on YouTube 1. Open the report and read the final paragraph from yesterday. 2. Write the missing paragraph in rough form. 3. Add the supporting reference already saved in the notes. 4. Only after those steps, decide what the next section needs. The fourth step is deliberately different. It restores judgement after the part of the task where the appropriate path was predictable. Research on implementation intentions offers a plausible explanation for why the first three steps can help. If–then planning links a critical situation with a goal directed response, increasing the accessibility of the cue and strengthening the connection between cue and action. Reviews describe the resulting control as a form of “strategic automaticity ”: the choice is made deliberately beforehand so that encountering the planned situation can prompt a comparatively immediate response later. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Metacognition in action: the importance of implementation intentions Metacognition in action: the importance of implementation intentions PubMed... A learning experiment provides an unusually close example of predetermined transitions within an ongoing activity. Researchers gave learners implementation intentions such as responding to the completion of a paragraph by looking for corresponding information in an accompanying picture. Across two experiments, implementation intentions improved multimedia learning outcomes; plans that coordinated relevant text, picture and integration processes were particularly effective. The experiment does not prove that every personal routine should become a chain of steps, but it demonstrates the more precise principle: a transition inside an activity can itself become the cue for a useful next action . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. That approach also addresses something beyond forgetting. Ongoing goal pursuit has to survive competing thoughts and temptations. Field experiments involving dieting and athletic goals found that implementation intentions aimed at troublesome internal states helped participants shield their goal pursuit from interference. Other experiments found that planned goal striving was better protected from disruptive internal states even when the plans identified opportunities for goal directed action rather than explicitly specifying how to suppress the disruption. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. In practice, this suggests three especially useful places to preplan a next step: after completing a predictable subtask, after encountering a familiar sticking point, and when a familiar distraction appears . A writing sequence might say, “After drafting the opening, make the argument bullets; after the bullets, draft the weakest section; if I want to check messages before that is finished, record the urge and continue.” Each clause closes a decision point that otherwise tends to invite renegotiation. The useful mechanism should not be reduced to “saving willpower”. Preplanning can instead change the cognitive structure of the task: the relevant event is more clearly identified, a response has already been selected, and less fresh interpretation is required before continuing. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Promoting the translation of intentions into action by implementation intentions: behavioral effects and physiologica... Explanatory illustration 2 Plan the transitions where hesitation actually occurs A sequence works best when it is built around observed friction rather than an imaginary ideal routine. Start with a recurring task that already matters and"},{"title":"Remembering","url":"/remembering/","description":"A plan can fail even when motivation is intact because the intended action never comes to mind when the opportunity appears.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fewer Decisions","level":3,"text":"Remembering Why Good Intentions Vanish at the Crucial Moment Fewer Decisions Remembering Remembering Why Good Intentions Vanish at the Crucial Moment A plan can fail even when motivation is intact because the intended action never comes to mind when the opportunity appears. A plan can fail even when motivation is intact because the intended action never comes to mind when the opportunity appears. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How prospective memory differs from forming an intention Why recognizable cues make intended actions easier to retrieve Ways to design action moments that are harder to miss self improvement that works fewer decisions remembering Introduction A self improvement plan can fail even when the goal is still wanted and motivation has not disappeared. The failure may occur because, when the opportunity to act finally arrives, the intention simply does not come to mind . Psychologists call the capacity to remember and carry out a previously formed future intention prospective memory . It is what allows “I must do that later” to become action at the appropriate moment. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: A systematic and meta analytic review ScienceDirectMarch 30... Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters because remembering an intention is not the same as remembering to retrieve it. You can know perfectly well that you planned to stretch after lunch, send a message when you reach the office or prepare tomorrow's lunch before leaving the kitchen, yet miss the relevant opportunity while absorbed in something else. Research suggests that prospective remembering improves when the action is attached to a recognisable cue, when that cue fits naturally with what will already command attention, and when reminders make the action moment difficult to overlook. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineImplementation intentions forge a strong cue–response link and boost prospective memory performance: Journal of Co... Forming an intention is not remembering to act Ordinary memory is often discussed as though the main problem were retaining information. Prospective memory poses an additional problem: something must prompt retrieval at the right time . Researchers commonly distinguish several stages, including forming an intention, retaining it during a delay, recognising the relevant cue, retrieving the intention and executing it. A breakdown at the retrieval stage can therefore produce an odd but familiar experience: the intention returns to mind perfectly clearly, but only after its useful moment has passed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The effect of implementation intention on prospective memory: A systematic and meta analytic review ScienceDirectMarch 30... That is why strengthening motivation or repeatedly thinking about a goal at planning time does not necessarily solve this particular failure. In one experiment comparing prospective memory strategies, participants using an if/when–then implementation intention performed better than controls, whereas ordinary rehearsal of the same cue and response did not produce a significant benefit. Because the content was held constant, the result points towards the importance of how the future cue and action are linked rather than mere repetition of the intention. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineImplementation intentions forge a strong cue–response link and boost prospective memory performance: Journal of Co... An earlier experiment with older adults illustrates the distinction particularly well. Participants who formed detailed implementation intentions were more than twice as likely to initiate an intended behaviour than people who had merely received or rehearsed the instruction. Yet implementation intentions did not improve another task in which the necessary response was already prompted by salient cues. The planning intervention was especially useful where remembering depended on self initiation. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceImplementation Intentions and Facilitation of Prospective Memory Psychological Science APS... This reframes some apparently motivational failures. Suppose you sincerely decide in the morning that, when you get home, you will put your running shoes on before sitting down. At 18:15 you enter the house while reading a message, put your bag away, walk into the kitchen and begin making dinner. At 21:00 you remember the plan. Nothing about that sequence demonstrates that you secretly preferred not to exercise . The more immediate failure occurred earlier: the intention was not retrieved while the opportunity to begin was available. Prospective Memory: Remembering to do stuff (and how to improve it) Channel: Psy vs. Psy Open on YouTube Why recognisable cues make intentions easier to retrieve The most useful distinction for designing these moments is between focal and nonfocal cues . A cue is relatively focal when noticing its relevant feature is already part of what you are doing. A classic laboratory example is looking for a particular word while performing a task that already requires processing words. A nonfocal cue asks you to notice a feature that the ongoing activity does not naturally make you process — for example, detecting a particular syllable while concentrating on the meaning of words. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Prospective Memory and Aging: Evidence for Preserved Spontaneous Retrieval with Exact but Not Related Cues PMC... This seemingly technical distinction maps neatly onto everyday plans. “When I finish brushing my teeth, I will take my medication” provides an event that is already being processed because you are performing it. “At some point while getting ready, remember the medication” supplies much less retrieval support. Likewise, “when I put my lunch plate in the sink, I will fill my water bottle” has a concrete encounter built into it; “drink more water this afternoon” largely leaves remembering to you. Experimental findings suggest why this matters. Research supporting the multiprocess account of prospective memory finds that focal cues can sometimes trigger relatively spontaneous retrieval, whereas nonfocal cues tend to require more deliberate monitoring. In experiments comparing the two, participants showed interference with their ongoing activity when watching for nonfocal cues but could perform well with focal cues even without comparable evidence of continuous monitoring. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Focal/Nonfocal Cue Effects in Prospective Memory: Monitoring Difficulty or Different Retrieval Processes? PMC... A neuroimaging meta analysis likewise found different patterns of brain activity for focal and nonfocal prospective memory demands, consistent with nonfocal retrieval placing greater reliance on top down processes. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of cue focality on the neural mechanisms of prospective memory: A meta analysis of neuroimaging studies PMC... A 2026 systematic review of 34 electroencephalography studies adds further detail. It found evidence for multiple stages — including preparatory attention, cue detection, intention retrieval and post retrieval control — rather than a single prospective memory mechanism. Nonfocal cues were associated with greater retrieval related and post retrieval control activity, while early effects were also influenced by how perceptually salient a cue was. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do we remember the future? A systematic review of ERP components in focal and nonfocal prospective memory tasks Scienc... The practical implication is not simply “make cues noticeable”. A bright cue can attract attention, but a good action cue also needs to retrieve the correct intention . The useful design question is: What event will I reliably encounter at the moment when this action becomes possible, and will encountering it naturally make the plan relevant? Make the cue retrieve the action Implementation intentions apply this principle deliberately. Instead of encoding only an outcome such as “exercise today”, the person specifies a future situation and response: when situation Y occurs, I will perform action X . The point is to make Y function as a retrieval route to X. Laboratory evidence supports that interpretation. Research has found that implementation intentions can increase the accessibility of the specified cue and strengthen its association with the planned response. In one study designed to separate these processes from simple familiarity and rehearsal, implementation intention participants responded more quickly to the critical cue and showed evidence of a strengthened cue–response link. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open"},{"title":"Spending Friction","url":"/spending-friction/","description":"Removing saved payments, one-click checkout and retailer prompts can add just enough friction to interrupt automatic spending.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Design Around Temptation","level":3,"text":"Spending Friction Can Extra Checkout Steps Curb Impulse Buying? Design Around Temptation Spending Friction Spending Friction Can Extra Checkout Steps Curb Impulse Buying? Removing saved payments, one click checkout and retailer prompts can add just enough friction to interrupt automatic spending. Removing saved payments, one click checkout and retailer prompts can add just enough friction to interrupt automatic spending. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why low friction checkout encourages repeat purchasing Which purchasing conveniences are worth removing How to add friction without blocking useful shopping self improvement that works design around temptation spending friction Introduction Adding a little friction to online shopping can interrupt an impulse before it becomes a purchase. The principle is straightforward: if saved cards , one click checkout and retailer prompts make buying almost automatic, selectively reversing those conveniences creates a moment in which the shopper has to decide again. This is not merely intuition. A 35 month study of an online retailer found that adopting one click buying increased purchasing by making customers buy more often and purchase more items, with effects that persisted over time. [PubsOnline] informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science PubsOnlineFewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023 Explanatory illustration 1 The useful target is therefore not maximum inconvenience. It is a small interruption between wanting and paying : typing payment details, signing back into an account, reviewing a basket or waiting before completing an unplanned order. Experimental research on online shopping also suggests that postponement and reflection can reduce purchase impulsivity in environments containing scarcity and social proof prompts. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org University Press Dark patterns in online shopping: do they work and can nudges help mitigate impulse buying? Behavioural Public Policy Cambridge University PressDark patterns in online shopping: do they work and can nudges help mitigate impulse buying? Behavioural Publi... For someone whose problem is spontaneous rather than necessary spending, carefully chosen checkout friction can make the online environment support the decision they would prefer to make after the immediate urge has passed. How to Stop Impulse Buying in 24 Hours Channel: Jack Explains Money Open on YouTube Why low friction checkout encourages repeat purchasing Online retailers have good commercial reasons to remove checkout steps. Every forgotten password, form field and confirmation page gives a customer another opportunity to abandon a transaction. But the same efficiency has a behavioural consequence: it reduces the distance between an initial impulse and an irreversible purchase. The clearest evidence comes from Murat Unal and Young Hoon Park's study, [Fewer Clicks, More Purchases] informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science PubsOnlineFewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023 , published in Management Science . Using quasi experimental data covering 35 months around an online retailer's introduction of one click buying, the researchers found that customers adopting the feature subsequently purchased more frequently and bought more items. The effect was economically significant and persistent rather than a short lived novelty. Their clickstream analysis also indicated greater engagement with the retailer, including more visits and page views, and purchases spreading across more product categories. [PubsOnline] informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science PubsOnlineFewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023 That finding matters because one click purchasing does more than save a few seconds. With an address and payment method already stored, the sequence can shrink from “I like this” → “Do I want to spend £45?” → “find my card” → “enter the details” → “confirm” into something much closer to “I like this” → “buy”. A consumer trying to reduce spontaneous purchases can use the same mechanism in reverse. Removing a saved payment method does not forbid the purchase; it makes the intention survive one additional test. There is a second mechanism at work: payment becomes less salient as it becomes more seamless . Research on the “pain of payment” — the psychological discomfort associated with giving up money — has repeatedly found relationships between payment characteristics and spending. Reviews note that less transparent and more psychologically decoupled payment methods can make the financial outflow less prominent during the purchasing decision. The exact psychological mechanism remains contested, however: recent studies have not consistently found that consciously experienced payment discomfort fully explains why cashless methods can increase purchasing. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Why does mobile payment promote purchases? Revisiting the pain of paying, and understanding the implicit pleasure via... That qualification is important. There is stronger direct evidence that reducing checkout effort can increase purchasing than there is evidence that deleting a saved card will produce a predictable reduction in any particular person's spending. The practical strategy is best understood as a plausible reversal of a demonstrated commercial mechanism, not as a guaranteed behavioural treatment. How to Avoid Impulse Spending When You Have ADHD Channel: How to ADHD Open on YouTube Which purchasing conveniences are worth removing The best friction targets are conveniences that disproportionately help with purchases you later question. Removing every useful shopping feature would simply create irritation and encourage workarounds. A better approach is to identify where an impulse currently has an unusually short path to completion. Remove one click purchasing first. If a retailer allows an item to move almost directly from product page to confirmed order, disabling that option restores a meaningful decision point. The one click evidence makes this the most directly supported target: reducing steps increases purchasing, so this is precisely the convenience to question when excessive purchasing is the problem. [PubsOnline] informs.org Pubs Online Fewer Clicks, More Purchases Management Science PubsOnlineFewer Clicks, More Purchases Management ScienceFebruary 22, 2023 Delete stored payment details from high risk retailers. Having to retrieve a card and enter its details is modest friction, but that is the point. A purchase you planned to make can still be completed in a minute or two. A late night, boredom driven or sale triggered purchase now has time to lose momentum. Where browsers or digital wallets automatically refill payment information, removing the retailer's saved card alone may not create much friction, so the whole payment path needs to be considered. Stay signed out of shops associated with habitual browsing. Accounts often combine stored addresses, payment information, order histories, recommendations and rapid checkout. Signing in only when there is something specific to buy separates browsing from purchasing without preventing either. Turn off retailer generated purchase triggers. Friction is not confined to the checkout itself. The UK's Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) identifies prompts and reminders, scarcity messages and personalised offers as forms of online choice architecture capable of influencing consumer behaviour. Its evidence review finds particularly substantial evidence around scarcity and popularity claims, which can increase urgency, purchasing intentions and interaction with products. [GOV.UK] At the trigger: Open source on gov.uk. Unsubscribing from sale emails, disabling retailer push notifications and removing shopping apps that mainly generate browsing occasions therefore complements checkout friction: fewer impulses enter the purchase funnel in the first place. Do not let a preselected option make the decision for you. UK Department for Business and Trade research examined 558 consumer journeys and found defaults on 49% of the sampled websites and apps. In a subsequent experiment involving 5,889 participants, people encountering a preselected default were 60–70% more likely to choose the more expensive option than those seeing no default; importantly, many observed defaults in the prevalence study actually favoured cheaper or similarly priced choices, so defaults are not inherently harmful. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping The prevalence and potential harm of defaults in online shopping The practical lesson is narrower: treat preselection as something to review rather than as a recommendation. This distinction helps prevent “friction” becoming an indiscriminate rule. A saved"},{"title":"Notification Batching","url":"/notification-batching/","description":"Receiving notifications in scheduled batches may reduce interruptions without the anxiety some people experience when alerts disappear entirely.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Design Around Temptation","level":3,"text":"Notification Batching Is Batching Notifications Better Than Turning Them Off? Design Around Temptation Notification Batching Notification Batching Is Batching Notifications Better Than Turning Them Off? Receiving notifications in scheduled batches may reduce interruptions without the anxiety some people experience when alerts disappear entirely. Receiving notifications in scheduled batches may reduce interruptions without the anxiety some people experience when alerts disappear entirely. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single policy policy intervention What the three group notification experiment tested Attention, productivity and perceived phone control Why total notification removal can backfire self improvement that works design around temptation notification batching Introduction Notification batching is a middle path between letting a phone interrupt you whenever an app chooses and turning alerts off altogether. Instead of delivering each notification immediately, the phone holds ordinary alerts and releases them at predictable times. The strongest direct experiment on this approach found that three scheduled batches a day were associated with better attention, mood, perceived productivity and control over the phone than normal continuous delivery, while hourly batching offered little benefit. Completely suppressing alerts, meanwhile, increased anxiety and fear of missing out (FoMO). [DOI] doi.org Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 1 That makes batching an unusually interesting form of environment design . It does not require repeatedly deciding whether to ignore each buzz. It changes when the temptation appears. At the same time, it preserves reassurance that messages will arrive soon enough — potentially avoiding a weakness of the more severe advice to switch everything off. What the notification experiment actually tested The central evidence comes from a two week randomised field experiment by Nicholas Fitz, Kostadin Kushlev and colleagues, published in Computers in Human Behavior in 2019. The researchers recruited 237 participants and used a custom Android application to control when notifications became visible. Rather than simply comparing notifications with no notifications, they created four conditions. [DOI] doi.org Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being ScienceDirect... Participants either received notifications normally, received accumulated notifications once every hour, received them in three batches at 9am, 3pm and 9pm, or received no notification alerts at all and had to look for notifications themselves. Participants followed their assigned system for two weeks, completed daily surveys, and had aspects of their phone behaviour recorded by the research application. [Springer Nature Communities] communities.springernature.com Open source on springernature.com. That four way comparison matters because it tests two different ideas. One is simply reducing the number of interruptions . The other is making interruptions predictable . If either were sufficient, hourly batching should have delivered much of the benefit. It did not: apart from participants feeling less interrupted by notifications, the hourly condition generally looked similar to ordinary notification delivery. [DOI] doi.org Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being ScienceDirect... Three batches a day produced a different pattern. Compared with normal delivery, participants in that condition reported greater attentiveness and concentration, a better mood, greater perceived productivity and more control over their phones. They also unlocked their phones less frequently and felt less interrupted. Reported effect sizes included fewer phone unlocks (Cohen's d = −0.60), lower inattention ( d = −0.65), greater concentration ( d = 0.54), greater perceived control ( d = 0.58) and greater perceived productivity ( d = 0.57). [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. The result therefore does not support the loose rule that “grouping notifications is good”. Batch frequency appears important. Moving from an unpredictable stream to an interruption every hour may leave the basic rhythm of distraction largely intact. Moving most alerts into a few well spaced windows changes the structure of the day much more substantially. [DOI] doi.org Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being ScienceDirect... What Are Strategies For Batching Digital Notifications At Work? Channel: Learn As An Adult Open on YouTube Why batching can protect attention A notification can impose an attentional cost even when you resist opening it. In a controlled experiment published in the Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance , Cary Stothart and colleagues found that receiving a mobile phone notification significantly disrupted performance on an attention demanding task despite participants not interacting with the phone. The observed distraction was comparable in magnitude to effects associated with actively using a phone for calls or text messages. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification PubMedJune 29, 2015... Published: June 29, 2015 This makes the familiar advice to “just ignore the notification” incomplete. The decision not to respond happens after the alert has already captured some attention . A vibration may trigger questions about who contacted you or whether the message matters; a banner may reveal enough information to redirect thought even if it disappears seconds later. Batching intervenes earlier. During the protected interval, there is no new alert to resist. At the scheduled delivery point, several interruptions that would otherwise have occurred independently are consolidated into one episode. The intervention therefore targets the arrival of temptation rather than demanding better resistance after every arrival. Related workplace research strengthens the case that reducing notification triggered interruptions can matter, although it does not establish that batching is always the best method. A 2023 field experiment involving 247 participants compared a day with communication notifications disabled against normal conditions. Reducing notification caused interruptions benefited performance and reduced strain, with effects varying according to FoMO and “telepressure” — the felt pressure to remain responsive to messages. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of task interruptions caused by notifications from communication applications on strain and performance PMC... The useful distinction is between phone use and phone initiated interruption . Batching does not necessarily prevent someone from voluntarily opening an app every five minutes. What it can do is reduce the number of occasions on which the device itself initiates the contest for attention. iPhone Secret Setting: Schedule Your Notification Summary! Channel: ITJungles Open on YouTube Attention, productivity and perceived phone control One of the most useful findings in the Fitz study is not simply that participants said they felt better. Three times daily batching was also associated with fewer phone unlocks. That suggests the intervention changed at least one observable behaviour rather than producing only a subjective impression of improved focus. [Springer Nature Communities] communities.springernature.com Open source on springernature.com. Perceived control is also worth separating from raw screen time. A person who intentionally spends 20 minutes replying to messages at an expected time is having a different experience from someone whose attention is repeatedly redirected by alerts throughout those same hours. The batching experiment found greater perceived control in the three times daily group, which fits the idea that predictable delivery shifts some control over timing from applications back to the user. [DOI] doi.org Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being Batching smartphone notifications can improve well being ScienceDirect... But the evidence should not be stretched into a claim that notifications explain smartphone overuse in general. A more recent preregistered randomised controlled trial of 205 participants tested a week of disabling notifications and collected objectively logged smartphone behaviour. Turning notifications off did not significantly reduce screen time or checking frequency, nor did it improve perceived control, productivity, distraction or several other measures of digital well being. Participants did report weaker perceived"},{"title":"Routine or Time","url":"/routine-or-time/","description":"Linking a behaviour to a recurring routine can work about as well as using a fixed time, provided the cue is dependable and the action is repeated.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Cues","level":3,"text":"Routine or Time Should a Habit Follow a Routine or a Clock? Useful Cues Routine or Time Routine or Time Should a Habit Follow a Routine or a Clock? Linking a behaviour to a recurring routine can work about as well as using a fixed time, provided the cue is dependable and the action is repeated. Linking a behaviour to a recurring routine can work about as well as using a fixed time, provided the cue is dependable and the action is repeated. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence How routine based and time based cues differ What the randomized nutrition trial found Choosing the cue that recurs most reliably self improvement that works useful cues routine or time Introduction For building a habit, there is no good evidence that “after breakfast” is inherently better than “at 9 am”. The strongest direct comparison found that routine based cues and clock time cues produced similar increases in habit automaticity over 12 weeks. What mattered more was whether people repeatedly performed the intended behaviour when their chosen cue occurred. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is useful for self improvement. A routine cue links an action to another recurring event — “after breakfast, I will eat a piece of fruit”. A time cue links it to the clock — “at 9 am, I will eat a piece of fruit”. Either can provide the stable environmental context from which a habit develops. The practical question is therefore less “Which type of cue is scientifically superior?” and more “Which cue will I reliably encounter and act on?” How routine and time cues differ A routine based cue is an event in the ordinary sequence of the day: getting out of bed, finishing breakfast, brushing your teeth, arriving at your desk or clearing away dinner. The event signals that the new behaviour should follow. A time based cue uses a particular clock time instead: 8 am, 1 pm or 9.30 pm. There are plausible reasons to expect routine cues to have an advantage. An established routine is already something a person repeatedly encounters without having to watch the clock. Habit researchers have therefore suggested that event based cues may be easier to detect than time cues, which can require conscious time monitoring. A routine can also tolerate some variation in scheduling: if breakfast happens at 7.30 one morning and 8.15 the next, “after breakfast” still identifies the relevant moment. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... Earlier observational evidence appeared consistent with that idea. A study of 1,244 Australian adults examined the consistency of several cues surrounding physical activity , including time, location, people, mood and routines. More consistent routine cues were associated with greater physical activity automaticity , whereas consistent time cues were associated with the amount of physical activity rather than its automaticity. Because this was observational rather than experimental evidence, however, it could not establish that choosing a routine cue actually causes a stronger habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cue Consistency Associated with Physical Activity Automaticity and Behavior PubMedApril 11, 2015... Published: April 11, 2015 That uncertainty made a direct randomised comparison particularly useful. How to Create New Habits and Change Existing Habits The Habit Loop Channel: Wisdom for Life Open on YouTube What the randomised nutrition trial found The clearest head to head test was published by Jan Keller and colleagues in the British Journal of Health Psychology in 2021. The prospectively registered study recruited 192 adults aged 18–77 from the general population in Germany and randomly allocated 96 to each of two planning conditions. Participants chose a healthy nutrition behaviour they wanted to develop and were instructed to connect it either to a daily routine or to a particular time. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... The contrast was deliberately simple. Someone in the routine condition might plan the behaviour for after breakfast ; someone in the time condition might plan it for 9 am . Both groups received a brief intervention of about five minutes and were asked to carry out their chosen behaviour in the planned context every day for 12 weeks. Daily questionnaires across 84 days measured whether they enacted the plan and how automatic the behaviour was becoming. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... The predicted superiority of one cue type did not emerge. At the group level: automaticity increased over time in both conditions; [nature.com] nature.com Source details in endnotes. enactment of the planned behaviour increased in both conditions; there was no significant between group difference favouring routine cues or time cues; repeatedly performing the behaviour in response to the planned cue predicted greater automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 In other words, “after breakfast” and “at 9 am” were both viable ways to organise the repeated behaviour. The important ingredient was not which wording appeared in the plan, but whether the person repeatedly completed the action when that cue arrived. Explanatory illustration 2 Habit formation was highly individual The same dataset also cautions against expecting habits to click into place after a standard number of days. Among participants whose habit development trajectories could be modelled as successful asymptotic curves, the median estimated time to reach 95% of peak automaticity was 59 days . Individual estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days . Some participants showed continuous progress, others discontinuous development, and some did not successfully form the intended habit during the study. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... That broad variation echoes the influential earlier study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues. In that research, participants repeated an eating, drinking or activity behaviour in a consistent daily context for 12 weeks. Estimated times to reach 95% of maximum automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days, and greater consistency of performance was associated with better fitting habit development curves. Missing a single opportunity did not materially derail the process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Taken together, these studies shift attention away from finding a supposedly perfect trigger. Habit formation is better understood as repeated learning in a sufficiently consistent context than as the result of choosing a special kind of cue. Why the result does not prove that cues never matter The nutrition trial supports equivalence within its tested circumstances, not the stronger claim that routine and time cues must work identically for every behaviour and every person. It involved self selected healthy eating behaviours, relied on self reported enactment and automaticity, and had no no planning control group. The authors also noted that their sample contained comparatively many women and younger participants, limiting how confidently the results can be generalised to other populations. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... There was also some inevitable fuzziness between the two cue categories. All retained participants in the time condition supplied a valid clock time, but only 55 of 65 retained participants in the routine condition supplied a cue that the researchers classified as an actual routine; several instead entered a general time of day or an internal state such as feeling stressed. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... More fundamentally, routine and time cues often overlap in ordinary life. Breakfast may usually happen around 8 am; a lunch break may reliably begin around 1 pm. A person intending to act “after breakfast” is therefore exposed to a bundle of"},{"title":"Stable Contexts","url":"/stable-contexts-452254/","description":"Repeating the same action in a stable place or situation can strengthen context-response links until starting requires less deliberate thought.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Cues","level":3,"text":"Stable Contexts Why Repeating a Habit in the Same Context Helps Useful Cues Stable Contexts Stable Contexts Why Repeating a Habit in the Same Context Helps Repeating the same action in a stable place or situation can strengthen context response links until starting requires less deliberate thought. Repeating the same action in a stable place or situation can strengthen context response links until starting requires less deliberate thought. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How context response associations strengthen Evidence linking stable place and time with automaticity Keeping enough consistency without making life rigid self improvement that works useful cues stable contexts Introduction Repeating a desired behaviour in a stable context can make it progressively easier to start without a fresh decision each time. The important ingredient is not simply repetition: it is repetition in relation to recurring cues. When an action repeatedly follows the same situation — finishing breakfast, arriving at a particular desk, getting home from work — the situation can become associated with the response. Encountering it then increasingly brings the action to mind or creates an impulse to begin. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... Explanatory illustration 1 This is why stable contexts matter for self improvement. They provide predictable opportunities for the brain to learn a context response relationship. Longitudinal studies show automaticity rising when behaviours are repeatedly performed in consistent circumstances, although the speed and strength of this process vary substantially between people and behaviours. More recent work directly manipulating context stability strengthens the case: people building habits in more stable settings reported greater automaticity and better completion of their intended actions. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The useful principle is therefore not “live by an inflexible timetable”. It is to give a developing behaviour enough contextual consistency for its triggers to become familiar. How context response associations strengthen A new behaviour usually begins under deliberate control. You decide to read, practise a language or review tomorrow's priorities, remember that decision at the relevant moment, and then make yourself start. Habit formation changes part of that sequence. Through repeated pairing, features of the situation become associated in memory with the response that has repeatedly followed them. Contemporary habit research therefore describes habits as forms of automaticity in which recurring contextual cues can activate previously learned responses. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... Imagine deciding to read for ten minutes after breakfast. Initially, breakfast ending has little special relationship with reading: you have to remember your plan. If you repeatedly finish breakfast and then read, however, elements of that recurring situation can acquire predictive value. Finishing the meal, sitting in the same chair, seeing the book or simply reaching that point in the morning routine may increasingly activate the reading response. That does not mean the environment literally forces the behaviour. Rather, the balance of control changes. The behaviour can become quicker to retrieve and less dependent on repeatedly asking, “Should I do this now?” Experiments examining established habits have found that strong habitual behaviours are particularly responsive to cues associated with their previous performance, including locations, while immediate goals exert less influence than people sometimes assume. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... This explains why cue specific repetition is more informative than repetition in the abstract. Drinking water twenty times in twenty unrelated circumstances gives you twenty performances, but relatively little repeated pairing with any one situation. Drinking water after the same recurring morning activity repeatedly gives both the behaviour and a recurring cue opportunities to become associated. Research on a broad range of everyday behaviours supports this distinction. A study examining behavioural frequency, contextual stability and rewards found that contextual stability was independently associated with greater automaticity, alongside frequency and reward. The relationship also varied with perceived behavioural complexity, underlining that context is an ingredient in habit formation rather than a universal formula that produces identical results for every action. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beha... The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Stable contexts predict greater automaticity One of the foundational real world studies followed 96 volunteers who chose an eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour and attempted to perform it daily in the same context, such as after breakfast, for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition towards a plateau rather than rising indefinitely at a constant rate. There was considerable individual variation, which is why the study's often repeated “66 days” figure should not be interpreted as a universal deadline for forming a habit. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That study demonstrated automaticity developing under consistent context instructions, but it did not isolate context stability experimentally. A 2022 study by Marco Stojanovic, Axel Grund and Stefan Fries addressed that question more directly. In its first study, 95 university students completed 2,482 repetitions of newly chosen study habits over six weeks. One group was instructed to keep the place and time of performance stable, while another was instructed to vary rooms and times. The researchers also analysed a separate dataset covering 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions from 218 habit building app users. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Across the two datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and higher attainment of the goal set for each habit repetition. Automaticity partially mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment. In other words, the results were consistent with part of the practical advantage of a stable context coming from the behaviour becoming more automatic. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... There is an important qualification. The experimental results did not imply that every tiny increase in consistency produces an equal gain. In the group deliberately operating with highly variable places and times, the expected context stability effect was not evident in the same way. The authors argued that a sufficiently recognisable stable context may first be needed before small variations in its stability become meaningful. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... Nor does every field study find a simple stability to habit relationship. An ecological momentary assessment study of physical activity and sedentary behaviour found relationships between context stability and behaviour, but its context stability measures did not predict habit strength. The researchers concluded that the behavioural associations they observed did not appear to be explained by habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The influence of context stability on physical activity and sedentary behaviour habit and behaviour: An ecological momentary assess... This is a useful limit on the claim: stable contexts are well supported as part of habit learning, but their effects depend on how context, behaviour and automaticity are defined and measured. Explanatory illustration 2 The cue can be a routine, not just a clock “Same context” does not necessarily mean “exactly 7.15 am in exactly the same chair”. Context can contain physical location, preceding actions, other people, time of"},{"title":"Next Steps","url":"/next-steps/","description":"Feedback becomes more useful when it links what happened to a realistic action you can take next.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Feedback","level":3,"text":"Next Steps When Tracking Tells You What to Do Next Useful Feedback Next Steps Next Steps When Tracking Tells You What to Do Next Feedback becomes more useful when it links what happened to a realistic action you can take next. Feedback becomes more useful when it links what happened to a realistic action you can take next. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism From recorded behaviour to a useful comparison What makes a recommendation genuinely actionable Turning repeated shortfalls into small experiments self improvement that works useful feedback next steps Introduction Tracking becomes actionable when it does more than report a number. Useful feedback compares what actually happened with a meaningful reference point, identifies a gap or pattern that can still be changed, and turns that information into a realistic next action . In other words, the mechanism is not simply record → notice but record → compare → interpret → act → review . Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because monitoring alone can improve goal attainment, but feedback is not automatically helpful. A meta analysis of 138 studies found that interventions increasing progress monitoring improved goal attainment, particularly when progress was physically recorded or reported to someone else. Yet research on feedback also shows substantial variation: poorly targeted feedback can distract, discourage or leave people knowing they are off course without knowing how to respond. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The practical aim, then, is not to extract ever more information from a tracker. It is to make the next decision easier. From recorded behaviour to a useful comparison A record becomes feedback when it is interpreted against something. “6,200 steps today” is data. “6,200 steps against my 7,500 step target” introduces a discrepancy. “Most of the missing 1,300 steps occur on days when I work from home” makes the discrepancy diagnostically useful. “On my next home working day, I will walk for 15 minutes after lunch” turns it into an action. This comparison process sits at the heart of self regulation accounts of behaviour. Monitoring allows people to detect differences between current behaviour and a goal or standard; that discrepancy can then prompt an adjustment. Modern behaviour change taxonomies therefore distinguish self monitoring from techniques such as drawing attention to a discrepancy, reviewing goals and changing the strategy in response to progress. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Linking behaviour change techniques to mechanisms of action: Using the Theory and Techniques Tool alongside the Behav... The reference point matters. A useful comparison might be against: a behavioural target , such as completing four planned study sessions this week; your own recent baseline , such as averaging 20 minutes more sleep than last month; an action plan , such as whether the intended lunchtime walk actually happened; a recurring context , such as consistently spending more on takeaway food on late working days. The important point is that the comparison should reveal something a person can influence. A broad outcome can be too distant from the behaviour producing it. If someone wants to write a book, for example, repeatedly seeing “manuscript unfinished” offers little guidance. Tracking whether three protected writing sessions happened this week creates a much shorter path from feedback to correction. This is also why feedback should usually stay close to the task rather than becoming a judgement about the person. Kluger and DeNisi's influential meta analysis of 607 feedback effects found that feedback improved performance on average, but more than a third of effects were negative. Their Feedback Intervention Theory proposed that feedback becomes less useful when attention shifts away from the task towards self focused concerns. The result is an important warning for self improvement: “you missed three sessions” and “your plan is failing at this particular point” contain information that can be acted on; “you lack discipline” does not. [EBSCO OpenURL] openurl.ebsco.com EBSCO OpenURLThe effects of feedback interventions on performance: A historical review, a meta analysis, and a preliminary feedback inter... Why an Empty Cell Made Her Run: Psychology of Self Monitoring Channel: Mind Decoded Open on YouTube What makes a recommendation genuinely actionable “Do better tomorrow” is technically a recommendation, but it does not reduce uncertainty about what to do. Actionable feedback needs to specify a response that can be performed in the person's real circumstances. A systematic review of 31 just in time diet and physical activity interventions illustrates how often this ingredient is missing. Although 97% used personalised feedback and 78% used goal oriented feedback, only 16% provided feedback classified as actionable. Just nine studies directly tested feedback's effect on behaviour, and four reported significant improvements; continuously available, goal oriented or actionable feedback appeared in three of those four successful studies. The authors treated these characteristics as promising rather than proof of a uniquely effective formula because the studies were too heterogeneous to isolate their individual effects. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That evidence suggests a useful test: after reading the feedback, could you say what you will actually do differently? If not, the feedback probably stops too early. Consider three versions of feedback from the same study log: Record: “You studied for 2 hours this week.” Comparison: “Your plan was 4 hours, so you completed half of the intended study time.” Actionable feedback: “Both missed sessions were planned for late evenings. For the next week, test one 30 minute session immediately after dinner on Tuesday and Thursday instead.” Only the third version converts the shortfall into a change that can be tried and subsequently measured. Crucially, it does not pretend to know that evening timing caused the missed sessions. It treats the pattern as a hypothesis worth testing. Explanatory illustration 2 Make the action specific enough to start A strong next step usually answers some combination of what, when, where and under what circumstances . NICE guidance on individual behaviour change similarly recommends combining feedback and monitoring with goals and planning, including action plans that specify the situation or time in which behaviour will occur. It also recommends reviewing behavioural goals in light of experience and making further plans according to previous progress. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 “If–then” plans are one particularly well studied way of closing this gap. Instead of “I will exercise more”, an implementation intention might say: “If I finish work before 6.30 pm on Monday, Wednesday or Friday, then I will walk for 20 minutes before going home.” A 2006 meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large overall effect of implementation intentions on goal attainment. A much larger 2024 synthesis covering 642 independent tests found positive effects across behavioural, cognitive and affective outcomes, with stronger effects when plans used a contingent if–then structure, when people were highly motivated and when plans were rehearsed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Tracking can make these plans more intelligent because it supplies the “if”. Rather than inventing a trigger arbitrarily, repeated observations can reveal where behaviour actually breaks down: after late meetings, on unstructured weekends, during travel, or when a planned task has no fixed starting cue. Keep the recommendation proportional to the evidence Personalisation is useful only when the data justify it. A single missed workout may be noise; four weeks of consistently missing the same Friday session is a pattern worth investigating. The tracker should therefore support decisions without encouraging false precision. Evidence on feedback formats reinforces that caution. A corrected systematic review and meta analysis of 19 self monitoring studies involving 3,261 adults examined feedback about diet, physical activity and weight. It found evidence that adding feedback could improve some outcomes, but comparisons among different feedback generation and presentation approaches were mixed. There is not good evidence for a universal “best” personalised message, dashboard or algorithm that can automatically prescribe the right response for everyone. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. The safer and often more useful role for"},{"title":"Immediate Enjoyment","url":"/immediate-enjoyment/","description":"Adding an immediate pleasure can make a worthwhile activity easier to start and sustain even when its main benefits are far away.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Temptation Bundling","level":3,"text":"Immediate Enjoyment Why Enjoyment Matters More Than Distant Rewards Temptation Bundling Immediate Enjoyment Immediate Enjoyment Why Enjoyment Matters More Than Distant Rewards Adding an immediate pleasure can make a worthwhile activity easier to start and sustain even when its main benefits are far away. Adding an immediate pleasure can make a worthwhile activity easier to start and sustain even when its main benefits are far away. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How present bias makes beneficial activities easy to postpone Why enjoyment during the activity can improve persistence Choosing pleasures that complement rather than replace the goal self improvement that works temptation bundling immediate enjoyment Introduction Difficult beneficial habits often have a timing problem: the effort is immediate, while the payoff is remote. A workout is tiring now but may improve health months or years from now; studying requires concentration now while qualifications and career benefits arrive later. Immediate enjoyment changes that motivational arithmetic. When some pleasure occurs during the activity itself, the present contains a reason to continue rather than merely a cost to endure. Explanatory illustration 1 Research increasingly supports a useful distinction: distant benefits can help people decide which goals matter, but immediate rewards such as enjoyment are often more closely tied to whether they actually persist. Experiments on exercise and studying, research on New Year’s resolutions, and longer term studies of intrinsic motivation all point in this direction. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals PubMed... Temptation bundling exploits precisely this mechanism by moving some of a worthwhile activity's reward into the moment when the difficult behaviour has to happen. Why distant benefits lose to today's effort Present bias describes the tendency to give disproportionate weight to outcomes that occur now. This creates an especially awkward problem for beneficial activities whose cost and benefit arrive at different times. Physical activity , for example, requires an immediate expenditure of time and effort while many of its most important health benefits are delayed. A field study of employees found that greater present bias was associated with lower physical activity, although the observational relationship should not be treated as proof that present bias alone caused inactivity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Association between time preference, present bias and physical activity: implications for designing behavior change i... The practical difficulty is not necessarily that people undervalue their future. Someone may genuinely care about being healthier next year and still prefer the sofa tonight. The decision made at the planning stage — “I want to exercise regularly” — is therefore different from the repeated decisions required to enact it. Each individual session again pits an immediate inconvenience against a largely delayed benefit. That distinction helps explain an initially surprising set of findings from Kaitlin Woolley and Ayelet Fishbach. Across studies involving New Year’s resolutions, studying, exercising and other goal directed activities, people often reported pursuing their goals for delayed benefits, yet immediate rewards such as enjoyment were stronger predictors of persistence. Their meta analysis similarly found immediate rewards more closely associated with actual persistence than delayed rewards. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Immediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Sage JournalsImmediate Rewards Predict Adherence to Long Term Goals Kaitlin Woolley, Ayelet Fishbach, 2017November 29, 2016... Published: November 29, 2016 This does not make future benefits irrelevant. They can supply the reason for choosing a destination: better health, greater knowledge or another valued outcome. But once a person is repeatedly performing the behaviour, the experience of doing it becomes important. A distant reward can answer “Why is this worth doing?” without necessarily supplying a compelling answer to “Why should I keep doing it right now?” The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Enjoyment changes the experience of persistence The important mechanism is more specific than simply “reward yourself”. Immediate enjoyment occurs while a person is paying the cost of goal pursuit. It can make the activity itself more attractive, so persistence requires less reliance on repeatedly choosing a future payoff over a present discomfort. Woolley and Fishbach tested this idea experimentally. In one gym study, participants selected from equally effortful exercises either according to which they expected to enjoy or according to which seemed most useful. Those directed towards immediate enjoyment subsequently completed more sets. Other experiments in their research found similar benefits from making academic work more immediately enjoyable, including adding music and other pleasant features while students worked. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicFor the Fun of It: Harnessing Immediate Rewards to Increase Persistence in Long Term Goals Journal of Consumer Research O... That finding matters because it reverses a common intuition about serious goals. People may assume that the “best” beneficial activity is the option that maximises its instrumental value: the most efficient workout, the most useful study technique or the most austere version of a healthy routine. But if an optimised activity is sufficiently unpleasant that it is repeatedly avoided or abandoned, its theoretical advantage may never be realised. An option that is both effective enough and pleasant enough to repeat can have a behavioural advantage. The broader evidence from exercise motivation points in the same direction, although it comes from a somewhat different theoretical tradition. A systematic review of 66 studies based on self determination theory found consistent positive relationships between autonomous motivation and physical activity, with intrinsic motivation — doing something partly because the experience itself is satisfying — tending to be especially relevant to longer term exercise adherence. The authors also cautioned that much of this literature was observational and that evidence differed across particular motivational constructs. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Exercise, physical activity, and self determination theory: a systematic review PubMedJune 22, 2012... Published: June 22, 2012 A 2025 review of systematic reviews likewise identified intrinsic and other autonomous forms of regulation as positive determinants of physical activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The pattern has now been tested over longer periods and beyond exercise. A 2025 Psychological Science paper followed personal resolutions across an entire year and found that intrinsic motivation predicted adherence better than extrinsic motivation, even though people often established resolutions for instrumental reasons. The researchers found related patterns in China, in objectively recorded walking over two weeks, and in an experiment where increasing intrinsic rather than extrinsic motivation improved engagement with a health goal. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Adherence to Personal Resolutions Across Time, Culture, and Goal Domains Adherence to Personal Resolutions Across Time, Culture, and Goal Domains PubMed... Together, these findings suggest a division of motivational labour. Long term value can be powerful for choosing a beneficial goal; enjoyment can be disproportionately important for continuing the behaviours that produce it. 104. How to Change: Building Better Habits and Behaviors (And Getting Out of Your Own Way) Channel: Stanford Graduate School of Business Open on YouTube Temptation bundling puts reward inside the activity Temptation bundling is a particularly clear application because the enjoyable component accompanies the difficult one. Rather than promising yourself entertainment after exercise, for example, you might reserve an absorbing audiobook for walking or gym sessions. The pleasure and the beneficial behaviour then occur together. The original field experiment by Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp paired tempting audiobooks with gym attendance. Participants given gym only access to the audiobooks initially visited the gym 51% more frequently than controls, while those encouraged to impose the restriction themselves initially visited 29% more frequently. The effects declined over time and were disrupted particularly strongly around the Thanksgiving break, so the experiment does not show that immediate enjoyment automatically creates a permanent"},{"title":"Context Cues","url":"/context-cues/","description":"In a commercial-gym study, simply receiving an audiobook appeared to prompt some people to pair listening with exercise even without explicit instructions.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Temptation Bundling","level":3,"text":"Context Cues Can the Temptation Teach the Strategy Itself? Temptation Bundling Context Cues Context Cues Can the Temptation Teach the Strategy Itself? In a commercial gym study, simply receiving an audiobook appeared to prompt some people to pair listening with exercise even without explicit instructions. In a commercial gym study, simply receiving an audiobook appeared to prompt some people to pair listening with exercise even without explicit instructions. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case population context Why audiobook only participants also changed their behaviour What researchers meant by information leakage Using context to make a useful pairing feel obvious self improvement that works temptation bundling context cues Introduction Yes — sometimes the reward appears capable of teaching the temptation bundling strategy by itself. The clearest evidence comes from a large commercial gym experiment in which members joined an exercise programme and received a free audiobook. Some were explicitly taught to pair enjoyable media with workouts; others simply received the audiobook. Surprisingly, the audiobook only group also changed its exercise behaviour. The researchers' explanation was information leakage : in the setting of a programme designed to increase exercise, an audiobook was not interpreted as a random present. Many people inferred that it was there to be enjoyed while working out. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 1 That makes this study unusually useful for practical self improvement. It suggests that an environment can sometimes communicate a behavioural strategy without spelling it out. But the finding is more limited than saying that rewards automatically create habits. The reward had to make sense alongside the target activity, the evidence for the mechanism is partly indirect, and explicit teaching still seems to have added something — particularly over longer periods. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. Why audiobook only participants also changed their behaviour The 2020 study by Erika Kirgios and colleagues grew out of earlier temptation bundling research in which tempting audio novels were deliberately paired with gym visits. The original experiment made that relationship unmistakable: some participants could access their chosen audiobooks only at the gym, while another group was encouraged to reserve them for workouts. Gym attendance initially increased substantially, although the effects weakened over time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The later StepUp study asked a subtler question: does someone actually need to be taught the strategy? It was conducted with members of 24 Hour Fitness. In the main randomised comparison, 2,334 eligible participants were assigned either to receive a free audiobook plus an explanation and encouragement to temptation bundle , or simply to receive the free audiobook without that explanation. The programme itself was explicitly about exercising more regularly. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. Explicit instruction produced only a modest advantage over the audiobook alone. During the four week intervention, teaching bundling increased the probability of making at least one weekly gym visit by about 2.2 percentage points relative to simply providing the audiobook, a marginally significant difference; ten weeks afterwards the difference was 3.2 percentage points and statistically significant. The researchers found much less evidence that the extra instruction increased the number of weekly visits. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. That relatively small difference became interesting because the supposedly minimal treatment was not necessarily psychologically minimal. Giving someone an audiobook while enrolling them in an exercise programme creates a fairly obvious possible relationship between the two things: perhaps I am meant to listen to this while exercising . In other words, the audiobook may have functioned simultaneously as a reward and as a clue about how to use the reward. The broader comparison strengthened that interpretation. The researchers incorporated another StepUp condition whose participants received the programme's other features but no audiobook. Across the full analysis of 6,792 participants, simply receiving the audiobook was associated with more gym attendance: the authors summarised the estimated increase in gym visits as 11% during the four week programme and 18% during the following four weeks. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. This does not prove that every extra workout resulted from audiobook listening. Only 13% of the audiobook only participants actually downloaded the free audiobook through the programme, for example, and the field experiment did not track whether people listened during their workouts. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. That limitation matters. The behavioural result is evidence that the apparently simple gift did something useful; identifying why required additional experiments. Katy Milkman Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling Channel: PennLDIvideo Open on YouTube What researchers meant by information leakage “Information leakage” here has nothing to do with privacy or confidential data. It describes information that people infer from the way a choice, message or object is presented, even though nobody states that information explicitly. Earlier decision research showed why such inferences can be reasonable. A speaker's choice between apparently equivalent descriptions can reveal something about the speaker's reference point or evaluation. Sher and McKenzie described how wording can therefore carry an “implicit recommendation”: the literal information may be equivalent while the choice of presentation communicates additional clues. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Information leakage from logically equivalent frames Information leakage from logically equivalent frames PubMedDecember 20, 2005... Published: December 20, 2005 Kirgios and colleagues applied the same idea to behaviour change design. Consider the difference between receiving an audiobook in three settings: a general birthday present; a reading programme; a programme specifically intended to get you exercising. The physical reward can be identical, but its apparent purpose changes with its source. In the third case, the gym context supplies a missing instruction: use this with exercise . That inference can lead someone towards temptation bundling without anyone having to explain the term. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. The researchers first checked whether real gym goers actually made that inference. A preregistered follow up survey showed 75 gym goers the materials seen by StepUp participants who had received an audiobook but no bundling instructions. In open ended responses, 60 of the 75 — 80% — spontaneously said that the audiobook was intended for entertainment at the gym. The same proportion subsequently selected gym entertainment when given explicit response options. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. That is an important detail. Participants were not merely agreeing with a temptation bundling suggestion after researchers put it in front of them. Four out of five produced the gym connection in their unrestricted answers. Explanatory illustration 2 The context mattered more than the audiobook A stronger test came from a preregistered experiment with 602 participants. Everyone was asked to imagine receiving the same kind of gift — a free audiobook — but its context varied. One group imagined joining a 28 day programme designed to build exercise habits; the other imagined joining a 28 day virtual book club designed to build reading habits. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. The difference was striking. In the exercise programme condition, 66.2% believed the programme designers most wanted them to listen to the audiobook while exercising. In the reading programme condition, only 2.64% thought so. Participants in the exercise condition also reported being significantly more likely to use the audiobook while exercising. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Open source on upenn.edu. Statistical analysis was consistent with the proposed mechanism: the exercise programme"},{"title":"Record Timing","url":"/record-timing/","description":"Recording a habit soon after it happens reduces the amount of repeated behaviour you must reconstruct from memory later.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Written Records","level":3,"text":"Record Timing Should You Log Habits Right After They Happen? Written Records Record Timing Record Timing Should You Log Habits Right After They Happen? Recording a habit soon after it happens reduces the amount of repeated behaviour you must reconstruct from memory later. Recording a habit soon after it happens reduces the amount of repeated behaviour you must reconstruct from memory later. Self Improvement That Works evidence single mechanism mechanism Why delayed reconstruction weakens accuracy What momentary assessment research suggests Choosing a practical recording window self improvement that works written records record timing Introduction Logging a habit soon after it happens is usually a better basis for judging progress than trying to reconstruct the whole week from memory. The advantage is not that an immediate tick in a tracker is infallible. It is that the recording task is smaller: instead of remembering several similar episodes, deciding which belonged to the relevant period and summarising them afterwards, you record one recent event while its details are still comparatively accessible. Explanatory illustration 1 Research on ecological momentary assessment (EMA) — repeated measurement of behaviour or experience in everyday life — was developed partly to reduce the recall problems created by longer retrospective reports. Reviews consistently identify reduced dependence on memory as one of its central advantages. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Ecological Momentary Assessment Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsEcological Momentary Assessment Annual ReviewsApril 27, 2008... Published: April 27, 2008 For self improvement, that supports a simple principle: where practical, turn “How many times did I do this last week?” into a series of small records made during the week. Is Inner Speech Universal? Descriptive Experience Sampling & Myths of the Mind Russell Hurlburt Channel: Dr Tevin Naidu Open on YouTube Why delayed reconstruction weakens accuracy An end of week question can look deceptively simple: “How many times did I exercise ?” Yet answering it requires more than retrieving a stored total. Unless the person has already been counting, they must search memory for individual episodes, distinguish one similar day from another and then construct a weekly estimate. Repeated behaviours are particularly awkward because individual instances can resemble one another. A Monday walk, Wednesday walk and Friday walk may not leave three neatly labelled memory traces. Retrospective reports can therefore be influenced by what happened recently, what was unusually noticeable and what fits the respondent's present state or general impression. Reviews of experience sampling research describe recency, salience and mood congruent remembering among the processes that can separate retrospective reports from reports made closer to experience. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using Experience Sampling Methods/Ecological Momentary Assessment (ESM/EMA) in Clinical Assessment and Clinical Resea... The practical danger is not merely forgetting. Reconstruction can change the pattern that the record is supposed to reveal. Someone trying to establish four study sessions a week, for example, might reach Sunday remembering Tuesday's particularly productive session and Saturday's long session, then conclude that the week was broadly successful. Four tiny entries made as sessions occurred could instead show that those were the only two sessions. The near event record has reduced the problem from reconstructing a seven day pattern to recording four individual yes or no events. Research comparing momentary and retrospective measurements demonstrates that these are not necessarily interchangeable views of the same period. In a study of 68 people with chronic pain, correspondence between weekly recall and aggregated momentary reports was moderate to high when comparing different people, but within person correspondence was low. That distinction matters for self improvement: the important question is often not whether one person is generally more active than another, but whether your own behaviour improved this week compared with last week. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Understanding recall of weekly pain from a momentary assessment perspective: absolute agreement, between and within person consist... Retrospective summaries can also give disproportionate weight to distinctive experiences. Research on weekly pain found that greater variability in momentary pain was associated with higher recalled pain relative to the average of the momentary reports, consistent with a peak or salience effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Variability of momentary pain predicts recall of weekly pain: a consequence of the peak (or salience) memory heuristic PubMed... The finding concerns pain rather than habits, so it should not be mechanically generalised to every behaviour. It does, however, illustrate the underlying measurement problem: remembering a period is not always equivalent to averaging what actually occurred during it. Even shortening recall from a week to a day does not eliminate reconstruction entirely. Two studies comparing repeated momentary ratings with end of day ratings found a memory–experience gap for negative affect; the day's peak and most recent negative affect also predicted the retrospective rating. At the same time, correspondence was reasonably good on some measures, showing why the evidence does not support an absolutist rule that every entry must be instantaneous. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How was your day? Convergence of aggregated momentary and retrospective end of day affect ratings across the adult life span PubM... Sensors, Methods and Tools for Ecological Momentary Assessment Channel: Danish Sound Cluster Open on YouTube What momentary assessment research suggests EMA provides the closest research analogue to near event habit logging. Rather than asking participants for one broad report after a long interval, researchers repeatedly ask about current or very recent behaviour in normal daily settings. The classic review by Saul Shiffman, Arthur Stone and Michael Hufford identifies minimising recall bias as a core reason for using the method. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Ecological momentary assessment Ecological momentary assessment PubMed... A particularly relevant 2025 study compared near momentary physical activity reports with seven day retrospective reports while 258 adults also wore accelerometers. Participants answered questions five times a day, with the near momentary questions covering roughly the previous five to 120 minutes, and later supplied end of week reports covering the same week. For sedentary time, the near momentary reports were closer in magnitude to the accelerometer measurements than the weekly recall reports were. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Seeking to improve criterion and construct validity of retrospective self reports of physical activity with brief rep... That result is useful but also supplies an important warning against overselling habit trackers. Both near momentary and retrospective self reports showed limitations: correlations with accelerometer measurements were generally low to modest, and self reports overestimated physical activity relative to the device measurements. Near event self report therefore removes some dependence on long term recall; it does not transform subjective recording into objective observation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2022 systematic review makes the same qualification more broadly. Across 32 studies comparing mobile EMA with more objective measures of health behaviour or environmental events, reported correspondence varied enormously — from 1.8% to 100% — and the reviewers could not identify one set of factors that reliably produced high accuracy. Their conclusion was that EMA can provide accurate measurements, not that it necessarily does so. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For personal habit records, the defensible inference is therefore narrower than “real time tracking is accurate”. It is that recording closer to behaviour removes much of the need to reconstruct a long sequence later. Other errors remain: a person can misunderstand what counts as a session, forget to log an event, exaggerate a duration or deliberately record what they hoped to do rather than what they did. There is striking evidence that apparently detailed diaries can themselves become retrospective reports when entries are postponed. In a study of people with chronic pain asked to complete paper diaries three times daily for 21 days, submitted diary cards suggested that 90% of scheduled entries had been completed within the required 15 minute window. Hidden electronic monitoring of when the paper diary was actually opened put verified"},{"title":"66 Day Study","url":"/66-day-study/","description":"The 66-day figure was a midpoint in a study where estimated habit formation ranged from 18 to 254 days.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Habit Timelines","level":3,"text":"66 Day Study Does Science Really Say Habits Take 66 Days? Habit Timelines 66 Day Study 66 Day Study Does Science Really Say Habits Take 66 Days? The 66 day figure was a midpoint in a study where estimated habit formation ranged from 18 to 254 days. The 66 day figure was a midpoint in a study where estimated habit formation ranged from 18 to 254 days. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence How the 12 week study measured automaticity Why 66 days was never a universal deadline What the 18 to 254 day range changes self improvement that works habit timelines 66 day study Introduction The famous claim that it takes 66 days to form a habit comes from a real and influential study, but “66 days” was never a universal deadline. Phillippa Lally and colleagues followed people attempting new everyday health behaviours for 12 weeks and modelled how their sense of automaticity changed with repetition. Among participants whose habit growth curves could be modelled well, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days , with a median of 66 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction changes the lesson considerably. The study did not discover a new magic number to replace the 21 day myth . It found that habit formation is a gradual, highly variable learning process : automaticity generally rises quickly at first, then improves by progressively smaller amounts as it approaches a plateau. The range, the shape of that curve and the study's modelling limitations are at least as important as its much repeated midpoint. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... How to build a habit in 66 days Channel: EJ Mina Open on YouTube How the 12 week study measured automaticity Lally and colleagues recruited 96 volunteers and asked each to choose a new eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour that they wanted to perform once a day in a consistent situation. An example of such a context was doing the behaviour “after breakfast”. Participants then attempted their chosen behaviour for 12 weeks, or 84 days , recording both whether they had performed it and how automatic it felt. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This matters because the researchers were not simply counting consecutive successful days. Their central outcome was automaticity : the extent to which performing the behaviour came to feel less dependent on conscious deliberation or remembering . They calculated this using selected items from the Self Report Habit Index (SRHI), a questionnaire designed to measure characteristics associated with habitual behaviour. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers expected automaticity to follow an asymptotic curve . In plain English, that means repetition produces substantial gains early on, but each additional repetition tends to add less than the previous one. Eventually the curve approaches a personal plateau. There is therefore no obvious scientific moment when a behaviour flips overnight from “not a habit” to “habit”. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... To put a number on the process, the researchers estimated when each person's curve reached 95% of its predicted plateau , rather than waiting for some categorical “habit formed” event. Later research using the same approach has explicitly distinguished these two quantities: the time required to approach stable automaticity and the eventual level of automaticity reached. Someone can therefore develop automaticity relatively quickly without necessarily ending up with the same habit strength as somebody else. [BPS PsycHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS PsycHubHabit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 Britis... There is another important detail hidden by the simple “96 people, 66 days” retelling. Of the 96 volunteers, 82 provided sufficient data for analysis . The nonlinear model could be fitted for 62 of those people, and the researchers judged it a good fit for 39 . More consistent performance was associated with better model fit. In other words, the famous timing estimate emerged from individual trajectories that behaved sufficiently like the hypothesised learning curve; the study did not observe every one of its 96 participants smoothly becoming habitual after roughly two months. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Making and Breaking Habits Dr. Pippa Lally 24 Channel: The Mental Wellbeing College Open on YouTube Why 66 days was never a universal deadline For the well fitting trajectories, the median estimated time to reach 95% of the automaticity plateau was 66 days . A median is the middle observation in an ordered set, not a prescription for every person. More revealingly, the individual estimates stretched from 18 days at the low end to 254 days at the high end . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That spread is the central finding obscured when “median of 66 days” becomes “habits take 66 days”. The slow end was more than fourteen times the fast end. Lally herself has subsequently stressed that the real message of the research was the large variability in how long habit formation took , rather than a universal 66 day rule. [University of Surrey] surrey.ac.uk Open source on surrey.ac.uk. There is also a methodological reason to interpret the longest estimates carefully. The experiment lasted only 84 days , yet the fitted curves produced estimates extending as far as 254 days. The researchers were fitting mathematical learning curves to the observed automaticity scores and estimating when those curves would reach 95% of their asymptotes. Consequently, values beyond the 84 day observation period were model based projections , not three, six or eight months of directly observed daily data. The study's abstract itself describes the procedure as nonlinear regression fitted to each participant's scores over the 84 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This does not make the 254 day result meaningless. It means the correct interpretation is narrower: some participants' observed trajectories were consistent with habit development taking substantially longer than the study itself lasted. The evidence supports large variation far better than it supports treating either 66 or 254 as a precise personal forecast. The 66 day figure is also specific to what this experiment studied: self selected eating, drinking and physical activity behaviours performed daily in a repeated context . It did not establish the formation time for every behaviour people casually call a habit, from studying and saving money to complex workplace routines. A 2024 systematic review found that even within health behaviours, relatively few studies had directly estimated time to habit formation, reinforcing the need for caution about universal numbers. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 2 What the 18 to 254 day range changes The range changes the practical interpretation from “persist for 66 days” to “expect your own learning curve” . At day 30, one behaviour might already be close to its eventual automaticity while another may still require substantial conscious effort. Neither outcome contradicts the original study. The study also produced a useful finding about imperfect consistency . Missing one opportunity to perform the target behaviour did not materially disrupt the habit formation process. That is different from saying consistency does not matter: participants who were very inconsistent were less successful, and more consistent performance was associated with better fitting habit growth curves. The evidence therefore distinguishes an occasional lapse from chronic non performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of"},{"title":"Timeline Range","url":"/timeline-range/","description":"Across health-habit studies, individual formation estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days, making any single countdown misleading.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Habit Timelines","level":3,"text":"Timeline Range Why Habit Formation Timelines Vary So Widely Habit Timelines Timeline Range Timeline Range Why Habit Formation Timelines Vary So Widely Across health habit studies, individual formation estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days, making any single countdown misleading. Across health habit studies, individual formation estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days, making any single countdown misleading. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence What the 4 to 335 day range represents Why averages hide individual differences How to interpret two to five month estimates self improvement that works habit timelines timeline range Introduction Habit formation can take weeks or months because a habit is not simply a behaviour repeated for a certain number of days. It is a learned link between a recurring cue and a response that becomes increasingly automatic through repetition. The speed of that learning varies substantially between people, behaviours and contexts. Modern health habit research therefore produces a distribution of timelines rather than a universal countdown. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com What is habit and how can it be used to change real‐world behaviour? Narrowing the theory‐reality gap Gardner 2024 Social an... Explanatory illustration 1 The clearest recent synthesis found individual estimates ranging from 4 to 335 days . In the small number of studies that directly estimated formation time, medians were 59–66 days , while means in other studies reached 106–154 days . Those figures make “two months” a useful description of some study results, but not a deadline. For self improvement that works , the important question is not whether a behaviour has crossed an arbitrary day count, but whether repeated performance in a recognisable context is actually becoming easier and more automatic. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. What the 4 to 335 day range actually represents The unusually wide range comes into focus in a 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of health related habit formation. The review included 20 studies and 2,601 participants covering behaviours such as physical activity , healthy eating, water consumption and flossing. Yet only four studies directly reported a mean or median time to habit formation . Across those studies, individual estimates extended from four days at the shortest to 335 days at the longest. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That 4 to 335 day span should not be read as though researchers discovered two biological types of people, one who needs four days and another who needs nearly a year. It combines observations from different people and health behaviours, using particular definitions and statistical methods for deciding when sufficient automaticity had developed. The systematic review itself warns that the evidence base for formation time remains small: 11 of its 20 included studies were rated as having a high risk of bias, and most did not directly estimate the time required to form a habit. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. One of the studies behind the extreme range illustrates another important qualification. In a randomised trial of 192 adults choosing an everyday nutrition behaviour, participants linked the behaviour either to a daily routine or to a specific time and reported on it for 84 days. Among participants classified as successfully forming a habit, the median modelled time to reach 95% of peak automaticity was 59 days , but individual estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days . Crucially, only 27 of the 117 participants whose automaticity trajectories were modelled — about 23% — met the study's criterion for successful habit formation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 So even the endpoints need context. “Four days” does not establish that four days normally suffices, while “335 days” does not establish that difficult habits require nearly a year. The striking result is the dispersion : people following broadly comparable habit building instructions can develop automaticity at markedly different rates, and some attempts do not fit a clean successful formation trajectory at all. [DOI] doi.org Habit formation following routine‐based versus time‐based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial Keller 2021 British Journa... The Science of How Your Brain Forms Habits (and How to Take Control) Channel: Dr. Tracey Marks Open on YouTube Why the average hides what happens to individuals The best known demonstration of this problem predates the systematic review. Phillippa Lally and colleagues asked 96 volunteers to choose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and repeat it daily in the same context for 12 weeks. Rather than treating habit formation as an on/off event, the researchers repeatedly measured automaticity and fitted learning curves to individual participants. For those whose data could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This is why the often repeated figure of roughly 66 days is easy to misuse. It describes the centre of a highly variable set of estimates; it does not mean that people are generally unhabituated on day 65 and habitual on day 66. The underlying model describes automaticity as increasing towards a plateau, with the size of further gains diminishing as the association becomes established. Habit formation is therefore better understood as a learning curve than as a finish line. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Averages can also obscure differences between behaviours. Drinking water after lunch is not necessarily equivalent, as a learning task, to establishing an exercise routine. The 2024 review found evidence that habit strength varied with factors including frequency, timing, behaviour type, personal choice and behavioural regulation. That makes it risky to take an average obtained across several behaviours and turn it into a promise for one particular behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A stretching experiment makes the point particularly clearly. Forty eight students were followed for 90 days while trying to make a daily stretch habitual. Participants were assigned to stretch either after waking or before bed. Modelled learning curves projected automaticity at approximately 106 days for the morning group and 154 days for the evening group . Even for essentially the same target action, changing when it was performed was associated with a substantially different estimated timescale. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit PubMed... Explanatory illustration 2 Why elapsed days are only a rough measure of learning Calendar time is an imperfect proxy for what produces a habit. Current habit theory emphasises context consistent repetition : a recurring cue becomes associated with a response, eventually allowing that cue to prompt an impulse to act with less deliberate decision making. Thirty calendar days can therefore contain very different amounts and qualities of habit learning. Someone who performs a behaviour reliably after the same cue has a different learning history from someone who performs it sporadically under changing circumstances. [Compass] compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. The nutrition trial supports this distinction. Repeatedly carrying out the planned behaviour in response to its cue predicted greater automaticity. The researchers found no significant advantage of linking the behaviour to a routine cue rather than a clock time cue; what mattered strongly was repeated enactment of the cue behaviour plan. Participants also showed diverse trajectories, including successful formation, discontinuous progress and failure to develop the expected automaticity pattern. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 Separate longitudinal research strengthens the case that the stability of the setting matters. In one study, university students attempted to build study habits for six weeks while being instructed either to keep their performance context stable or to vary places and times. A second dataset examined naturally occurring"},{"title":"Activity Evidence","url":"/activity-evidence/","description":"Trials suggest that adding feedback to activity tracking produces a modest improvement beyond recording behaviour by itself.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Feedback","level":3,"text":"Activity Evidence How Much Does Feedback Add to Activity Tracking? Useful Feedback Activity Evidence Activity Evidence How Much Does Feedback Add to Activity Tracking? Trials suggest that adding feedback to activity tracking produces a modest improvement beyond recording behaviour by itself. Trials suggest that adding feedback to activity tracking produces a modest improvement beyond recording behaviour by itself. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What feedback versus monitoring trials actually tested The corrected estimate for added physical activity Why the effect is meaningful but not large self improvement that works useful feedback activity evidence Introduction Physical activity research provides an unusually direct test of whether feedback adds anything to self monitoring. A wearable can record movement for everyone while researchers vary what participants are shown or told about those records. That makes it possible to compare tracking plus feedback with tracking that is otherwise similar but receives no additional feedback. Explanatory illustration 1 The best focused synthesis suggests that feedback does add something, but considerably less than an early version of the evidence implied. A corrected 2024 meta analysis estimated a small positive effect on physical activity, Cohen’s d = 0.29 (95% CI 0.16 to 0.43) , when feedback was added to self monitoring. [Springer Link] link.springer.com Springer LinkCorrection: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity... Individual trials help make that abstract result tangible: points based app feedback has produced extra moderate to vigorous activity, while a more recent wearable experiment found about 7% more activity during feedback periods. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A smartphone \"app\" delivered randomized factorial trial targeting physical activity in adults PubMedMarch 2, 2017... Published: March 2, 2017 The useful conclusion is therefore restrained. Recording activity is not the whole intervention. Making those records visible, interpretable or evaluative can improve behaviour further, but the average incremental benefit is modest rather than transformative. What Feedback Versus Monitoring Trials Actually Tested The critical comparison is easy to lose in the much larger literature on fitness trackers. A study showing that people given a wearable become more active than people receiving usual care cannot tell us whether feedback itself is responsible: the wearable may simultaneously introduce self monitoring, goals, reminders, rewards and other behaviour change techniques. The 2024 review by Rebecca Krukowski, Andrea Denton and Laura König was designed to deal with this problem. It included randomised controlled trials in which participants self monitored diet, physical activity or weight and researchers either added feedback or compared different forms of feedback. Across the full review, 24 articles representing 19 studies and 3,261 participants met the eligibility criteria. Crucially, intervention groups had to differ in feedback rather than in a whole package of unrelated treatment components. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... For physical activity, the review could pool nine comparisons from six studies that provided sufficiently comparable data. The feedback itself was not one standard treatment. Depending on the trial, it could mean graphical displays, website feedback, text messages, computer generated reports or other responses to the participant's recorded behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... That variation matters. “Feedback” can describe anything from seeing progress plotted against a target to receiving a message interpreting that progress. For example, a 2016 UK experiment randomised 124 insufficiently active participants to goal setting alone, goal setting plus self monitoring, or goal setting plus self monitoring and feedback. The last group received feedback on recorded activity; objectively measured activity was higher than in the goal setting only group and marginally higher than in the self monitoring group. The direct feedback versus self monitoring contrast was d = 0.33 , although its 95% confidence interval of −0.13 to 0.78 crossed zero. [DOI] doi.org An experimental test of control theory‐based interventions for physical activity An experimental test of control theory‐based interventions for physical activity Prestwich 2016 British Journal of Health Psycho... A different 12 week factorial trial involving 116 low active adults tested a smartphone self monitoring app with optional goal setting and points based feedback modules. Everyone increased moderate to vigorous physical activity by more than 11 minutes per day, but participants receiving points based feedback achieved still higher activity and more favourable app use and psychosocial outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A smartphone \"app\" delivered randomized factorial trial targeting physical activity in adults PubMedMarch 2, 2017... Published: March 2, 2017 This is an important distinction: the question is not whether tracking can work, but whether a feedback layer can produce an incremental gain when tracking is already present . BCT workshop 2021 Session 1: The Behaviour Change Technique Taxonomy (BCTTv1) Channel: UCL Centre for Behaviour Change Open on YouTube The Corrected Estimate for Added Physical Activity The central numerical result needs particular care because the published estimate was corrected shortly after publication. The original January 2024 article reported a pooled feedback effect of d = 0.73 , suggesting a fairly substantial advantage. In February, the authors issued a correction after discovering that two standard errors had been treated as standard deviations in the meta analysis. Recalculation reduced the pooled effect to d = 0.29 , with a 95% confidence interval from 0.16 to 0.43 and p [Springer Link] link.springer.com Springer LinkCorrection: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity... That correction changes the interpretation substantially. A Cohen's d of 0.29 represents a small standardised difference: people receiving feedback were, on average, more active than comparable people monitoring their behaviour without that added feedback, but the two groups still overlapped considerably. The revised analysis also produced low statistical heterogeneity (I² = 9.07%) , rather than the very high heterogeneity reported in the erroneous calculation. A trim and fill analysis did not identify missing studies suggestive of publication bias, although that diagnostic is necessarily limited with such a small evidence base. [Springer Link] link.springer.com Springer LinkCorrection: Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity... The review's study by study findings reinforce the need for modest language. Six studies across its wider set of feedback versus no feedback comparisons reported benefits of feedback on outcomes such as physical activity, diet goals, self monitoring or weight, but benefits were not universal. For physical activity specifically, the authors noted that only about half of the relevant studies individually reported a significant advantage from adding feedback. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... There is also a useful complementary benchmark from a broader 2022 systematic review asking what happens when anything is added to physical activity self monitoring . Across 75 studies included in its post intervention meta analysis, additional components produced about 926 extra steps per day compared with self monitoring controls; at follow up, the difference was 413 steps per day . Human counselling and prescribed goals were associated with larger differences than some app , website and print based additions. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis PMCSeptember 7, 2022... Published: September 7, 2022 Those figures should not be treated as the effect of feedback alone, because the added components included goals, counselling, incentives, texts, websites and structured exercise . They nevertheless support the same broader point: self monitoring can be a useful base, and additional behaviour change components can yield further gains. Explanatory illustration 2 A Cleaner Wearable Test Shows the Same Direction A 2024 field experiment"},{"title":"Human vs Algorithm","url":"/human-vs-algorithm/","description":"Current studies do not show that human coaches or algorithmic feedback consistently outperform the other across self-monitoring tasks.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Feedback","level":3,"text":"Human vs Algorithm Do Humans Give Better Feedback Than Algorithms? Useful Feedback Human vs Algorithm Human vs Algorithm Do Humans Give Better Feedback Than Algorithms? Current studies do not show that human coaches or algorithmic feedback consistently outperform the other across self monitoring tasks. Current studies do not show that human coaches or algorithmic feedback consistently outperform the other across self monitoring tasks. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic policy intervention What direct human versus algorithm comparisons found Where personalised algorithmic feedback looked promising Why no clear winner has emerged self improvement that works useful feedback human vs algorithm Introduction For self monitoring, the evidence does not support a simple rule that human feedback is better than algorithmic feedback, or vice versa. The relatively small number of direct comparisons points in different directions. An early internet weight loss trial found automated tailored feedback matched human email counselling over three months but fell behind it by six months. A more recent trial found that adding human coaching to an automated monitoring and feedback system produced greater weight loss. Yet a 2025 randomised trial found a fully automated artificial intelligence programme non inferior to a human coached diabetes prevention programme on a composite outcome covering weight, physical activity and blood glucose. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkA Randomized Trial Comparing Human e Mail Counseling, Computer Automated Tailored Counseling, and No Counseling in an Interne... Explanatory illustration 1 That mixed record matters for self improvement because “human versus algorithm” is not really a contest between two uniform treatments. Algorithms range from fixed rules to systems that adapt messages from tracked behaviour; human support ranges from brief emails to recurring coaching. What seems to matter is whether feedback interprets self monitoring data well enough, arrives when it can influence action and keeps people engaged over time. What Direct Human versus Algorithm Comparisons Found One of the clearest early comparisons came from a 2006 randomised trial of 192 adults in an internet weight loss programme. Everyone received basic behavioural weight loss material, while participants were assigned to no counselling, weekly computer automated tailored feedback or weekly human email counselling. Both feedback conditions used participants' monitoring of weight, diet and activity to guide subsequent messages. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkA Randomized Trial Comparing Human e Mail Counseling, Computer Automated Tailored Counseling, and No Counseling in an Interne... At three months, the distinction between human and automated feedback barely mattered to the outcome: participants completing follow up had lost an average of 6.1 kg with human counselling and 5.3 kg with automated feedback, versus 2.8 kg with no counselling. The human and automated groups did not significantly differ from each other. By six months, however, the trajectories had separated. Average losses were 7.3 kg with human counselling, 4.9 kg with automated feedback and 2.6 kg without counselling; human counselling was then significantly better than either alternative. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkA Randomized Trial Comparing Human e Mail Counseling, Computer Automated Tailored Counseling, and No Counseling in an Interne... This is an important warning against judging feedback systems only by their initial effect. An algorithm can use logged data to recognise whether someone met a target and return an appropriate prewritten response cheaply and consistently. A human can do that too, but can also interpret unusual circumstances, respond to questions and vary the conversation when adherence starts to weaken. The 2006 trial did not isolate which of these differences caused the longer term divergence, so it cannot establish a general human advantage. It does show that short term equivalence does not guarantee that automated feedback will remain equally effective. A newer test reached a similar conclusion in a different design. A 2024 randomised trial assigned 400 adults with overweight or obesity either to a wireless feedback system alone or to that same system plus human coaching. The system collected activity and weight through a tracker and wireless scale and returned daily smartphone feedback about progress; coaches in the second condition could see participants' monitoring data and provided weekly telephone support. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com Open source on jamanetwork.com. At six months, the system alone group had lost an average of 2.8 kg, compared with 4.8 kg in the group that also received coaching. The trial was designed to test whether the automated system was non inferior — close enough to coaching according to a prespecified margin — and it failed that test. Differences were similar at three and 12 months. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com Open source on jamanetwork.com. But this was not a clean contest between a person and an algorithm supplying otherwise identical feedback. Human coaching was added to an existing automated system, giving that group more support overall. The appropriate conclusion is therefore narrower: in this setting, automated self monitoring feedback did not make the additional human contact redundant. Is ChatGPT the Secret to Easy Weight Loss? Channel: Manuel Villacorta The Caliente Kitchen Show Open on YouTube A Newer AI Trial Complicates the Picture The strongest recent counterexample comes from a pragmatic randomised trial reported in JAMA in 2025. Researchers assigned 368 adults with prediabetes and overweight or obesity to referral either to a fully automated AI powered Diabetes Prevention Program or to a programme delivered remotely by human coaches. Unlike many earlier digital studies, this was a genuine automated versus human comparison rather than an app versus nothing study. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkAn AI Powered Lifestyle Intervention vs Human Coaching in the Diabetes Prevention Program: A Randomized Clinical Trial Tria... The automated system was more sophisticated than the rule based feedback typical of early digital interventions. It combined actively recorded information, including weight and meal logs, with passively collected information such as activity and location. A reinforcement learning algorithm adapted the content and timing of prompts according to patterns of engagement. It could, for example, connect a person's previous behaviour and current circumstances with a prompt about physical activity, eating or weighing. It did not use a large language model. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkAn AI Powered Lifestyle Intervention vs Human Coaching in the Diabetes Prevention Program: A Randomized Clinical Trial Tria... At 12 months, 31.7% of people assigned to the AI programme and 31.9% assigned to human coaching achieved the study's primary composite outcome. That outcome required maintaining blood glucose below the diabetes threshold while also achieving a specified improvement in weight, physical activity or glycated haemoglobin. The difference met the study's prespecified criterion for non inferiority. Initiation was also higher after referral to AI: 93.4% began the programme, compared with 82.7% of those referred to human coaching. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkAn AI Powered Lifestyle Intervention vs Human Coaching in the Diabetes Prevention Program: A Randomized Clinical Trial Tria... This is unusually strong evidence that human involvement is not intrinsically necessary for every effective feedback loop. If monitoring data are collected continuously and the system can personalise when and how it responds, automation can remove practical constraints associated with scheduled human contact. Yet a 2026 analysis of participants' experiences in the same trial reveals why declaring an algorithmic victory would be equally premature. AI participants started sooner — a median of 11 days after referral versus 26 days with human coaching — and engagement was higher and more evenly spread over the year. But participants rated the human programme more favourably across measures of acceptability, with particularly large differences in satisfaction. Among people assigned to AI, 46.5% said they would have preferred a human coach; among those assigned to human coaching, 31.5% would have preferred a fully automated programme. [Nature] nature.com Patient engagement, acceptability, and preference of artificial intelligence versus human coaching for diabetes prevention npj Di... The striking result is therefore not that people preferred algorithms. They generally did not. It is that the automated system could achieve comparable measured outcomes while offering faster access and sustained engagement, even though the human"},{"title":"Right Timing","url":"/right-timing/","description":"Feedback may be most useful when it arrives while there is still time to change the behaviour it is tracking.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Feedback","level":3,"text":"Right Timing Does Feedback Work Better Before the Moment Passes? Useful Feedback Right Timing Right Timing Does Feedback Work Better Before the Moment Passes? Feedback may be most useful when it arrives while there is still time to change the behaviour it is tracking. Feedback may be most useful when it arrives while there is still time to change the behaviour it is tracking. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Why delayed summaries can miss the decision window What just in time intervention studies have found Balancing immediacy with useful recommendations self improvement that works useful feedback right timing Introduction Just in time feedback is most useful when it reaches a person before the opportunity to change the tracked behaviour has closed . A daily activity summary at bedtime can explain why a step goal was missed; a prompt in mid afternoon, when walking is still possible, can potentially change the result. That distinction turns feedback from retrospective information into an intervention. Explanatory illustration 1 Digital trackers make this possible because phones and wearables can repeatedly measure behaviour, compare it with a target and deliver support within the same decision window. Research on these systems is promising but not definitive. Reviews of just in time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) find evidence of behaviour change, particularly for physical activity and sedentary behaviour, while also finding small studies, inconsistent designs and substantial uncertainty about which timing rules work best. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical implication is narrower than “faster feedback is always better”. Effective timing means finding a moment when three things overlap: the tracker has detected something worth changing, an actionable opportunity still exists, and the person is sufficiently receptive to do something about it. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicJust in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Health Behavi... Why delayed summaries can miss the decision window Self monitoring data have different useful lifetimes. Some information remains actionable for weeks: a monthly spending pattern can inform next month's budget. Other information becomes obsolete quickly. A warning that someone has been sitting continuously can prompt them to stand now; the same observation tomorrow cannot interrupt yesterday's sitting. Similarly, discovering at 10 pm that only 4,000 of an intended 8,000 steps have been completed leaves fewer realistic options than discovering the shortfall at 3 pm. Researchers therefore distinguish ordinary feedback from genuinely just in time support. A 2018 systematic review of diet and physical activity interventions defined just in time feedback as feedback provided from within one minute to no more than one day after assessment, depending on the behaviour and goal. Weekly summaries were explicitly outside its definition. The important principle was not a universal number of minutes, but matching the feedback interval to the period in which the behaviour could still usefully be influenced. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. More developed JITAIs go further. Instead of merely displaying an up to date number, they repeatedly reach decision points at which the system decides whether support is currently warranted. Information such as recent activity, location or other contextual signals can become “tailoring variables”, which determine whether a prompt is sent and what it recommends. The goal is to recognise temporary states of vulnerability — such as prolonged sitting — or opportunity, such as a convenient chance to walk. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Just in Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs) in Mobile Health: Key Components and Design Principles for Ongoing Healt... This changes the role of self monitoring. Consider three versions of feedback about the same low activity day: Retrospective: “You walked 5,200 steps yesterday.” Current but non actionable: “You have walked 5,200 steps today.” Just in time and actionable: “You are 1,800 steps short and have time for a 15 minute walk before dinner.” The final message does something the first two do not: it connects monitoring to an available response. The precise recommendation is illustrative, but the distinction is supported by the research literature. In the 2018 review of 31 diet and physical activity studies, 30 provided personalised feedback and 24 provided goal oriented feedback, yet only five provided what the reviewers classified as actionable feedback . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Just in Time Feedback in Diet and Physical Activity Interventions: Systematic Review and Practical Design Framework PubMedMarch 2... That scarcity matters. Speed without direction can simply deliver uncertainty more quickly. A vibration announcing that activity is low does not necessarily tell someone what to do, while a recommendation that arrives after the relevant opportunity has disappeared is actionable in wording but not in practice. Good just in time feedback therefore depends on both content and timing . Video 1: Introduction to JITAI with Susan Murphy Channel: methodsctr Open on YouTube What just in time intervention studies have found Evidence that this approach can change behaviour has accumulated, although the certainty of that evidence needs careful qualification. The 2018 systematic review provides an early snapshot. Among 31 just in time diet and physical activity studies, only nine specifically tested whether providing feedback changed behaviour, and four of those nine reported significant improvements. Three of those four successful studies used feedback that was continuously available, goal oriented or actionable. The reviewers regarded these features as promising, but the studies were too heterogeneous for a meta analysis and did not allow them to determine which feedback characteristic was responsible for the results. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2019 systematic review applied a stricter definition of JITAI: support had to respond to a real time need, adapt its content or timing using information collected by the system, and be system triggered. It identified 14 distinct physical activity JITAIs, six evaluated in randomised studies. Typical interventions either prompted people after sedentary periods or suggested activity when an opportunity arose. Acceptability was generally encouraging, but behavioural results were mixed, and none of the randomised studies was adequately powered to detect intervention effects reliably. Sensor reliability and getting messages to people at the intended time were themselves implementation problems. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A systematic review of just in time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) to promote physical activity PMCApril 3, 2019... Published: April 3, 2019 Later evidence is more encouraging but still calls for restraint. A 2023 meta analysis pooled 21 effect sizes from studies involving 592 participants and reported a medium overall effect on objectively measured behaviour, with Hedges' g of 0.77 (95% confidence interval 0.32 to 1.22). The authors nevertheless emphasised that JITAI evaluation remained at an early stage and called for better randomised trials and better information about non adherence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Using a complexity science approach to evaluate the effectiveness of just in time adaptive interventions: A meta analysis PubMed... A broader 2024 systematic review identified 45 JITAI studies concerned with physiological health outcomes, mostly physical activity and sedentary behaviour. Designs varied enormously: systems made intervention decisions at intervals ranging from roughly a minute to a day, and tailoring could depend on sensor measurements, self reports or combinations of contextual information. The authors judged overall study quality to be mainly weak, but found frequent decision points, device measurements supplemented by user input, user tailored intervention options and relatively simple decision rules among the more promising design features. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Just in Time Adaptive Interventions for Behavior Change in Physiological Health Outcomes and t... Together, these findings support a useful distinction. There is reasonable evidence that just in time approaches can influence behaviour. There is much less evidence that researchers have discovered the universally optimal moment, frequency or form of feedback. The implementation problem is therefore not solved simply by switching notifications to “instant”. Explanatory illustration 2 HeartSteps shows why the moment"},{"title":"More Is Not","url":"/more-is-not/","description":"More messages, detail or visual complexity do not reliably improve outcomes, and extra feedback can help adherence without changing the main goal.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Useful Feedback","level":3,"text":"More Is Not Why More Feedback Is Not Always Better Useful Feedback More Is Not More Is Not Why More Feedback Is Not Always Better More messages, detail or visual complexity do not reliably improve outcomes, and extra feedback can help adherence without changing the main goal. More messages, detail or visual complexity do not reliably improve outcomes, and extra feedback can help adherence without changing the main goal. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism Daily versus weekly feedback Simple displays versus richer contextual feedback When adherence improves but outcomes do not self improvement that works useful feedback more is not Introduction More feedback is not automatically better feedback. Making a self monitoring system more frequent, detailed or visually sophisticated can improve awareness and sometimes keep people recording their behaviour, but those gains do not reliably translate into better performance on the underlying goal. Randomised studies of physical activity and weight management show a recurring pattern: daily feedback can beat weekly feedback in some settings, yet additional messages can reach diminishing returns; richer visual and contextual displays can fail to outperform simple information; and extra reminders can substantially increase tracking without producing significantly greater weight loss. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement. The purpose of feedback is not to maximise the amount of information a person receives. It is to make the next useful adjustment easier. Once feedback already provides a clear comparison with a meaningful target, adding frequency or complexity may contribute little — and sometimes improves the monitoring process more than the behaviour being monitored. Daily feedback does not have a simple advantage The case for frequent feedback is intuitive. If someone learns today that they are falling behind on walking, studying or another repeated behaviour, they still have time to respond. Waiting until the end of the week may turn feedback into a retrospective report rather than a useful prompt. There is experimental support for that logic. In Project Step, a small randomised trial involving insufficiently active adults with overweight or obesity, participants received adaptive walking goals and either daily or weekly feedback. Those receiving daily feedback increased their steps more than those receiving weekly feedback. The result shows that frequency can matter when the feedback concerns a behaviour that can be adjusted repeatedly and quickly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Project Step: A Randomized Controlled Trial Investigating the Effects of Frequent Feedback and Contingent Incentives on Physical Ac... But the wider evidence does not establish a rule that daily is superior to weekly. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis covering 19 self monitoring and feedback studies found only two studies that directly investigated feedback frequency. It concluded that results on the best way to generate and present feedback were mixed, with substantial heterogeneity preventing a pooled comparison of different frequencies and formats. In other words, the evidence base is much thinner than the ubiquity of daily app notifications might suggest. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Impact of feedback generation and presentation on self monitoring behaviors, dietary intake, physical activity, and w... The long running SMART weight management trial illustrates why the answer depends on what outcome is measured. Participants using a personal digital assistant could receive short, tailored daily messages in addition to the feedback built into a broader behavioural programme. Across related analyses, daily feedback was associated with better self monitoring and larger reductions in reported energy intake, yet the primary 24 month comparison found no significant difference in percentage weight change between the paper diary, electronic diary and electronic diary plus feedback groups. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Using mHealth Technology to Enhance Self Monitoring for Weight Loss A Randomized Trial PMCJuly 1, 2012... Published: July 1, 2012 There can also be a saturation point. In a three month smartwatch experiment, insufficiently active young adults received between zero and six randomly timed prompts per day. Step counts rose with the number of prompts to self monitor up to roughly three per day, after which extra prompts produced minimal or reduced benefit. The number of behavioural feedback prompts, meanwhile, was not associated with daily step counts. More opportunities to intervene therefore did not create a linear dose response relationship. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical question is consequently not “How often can feedback be delivered?” but “How often does new information create a useful opportunity to act?” A daily signal can be valuable for daily walking because the person can still take another walk. Repeating essentially the same message several more times that day may add no new decision value. Why Feedback Is Making You Worse (And What to Do Instead) Adam Grant Channel: Washington Speakers Bureau Open on YouTube Richer displays can inform without changing behaviour Digital self monitoring makes it easy to assume that more dimensions, graphs and contextual information will produce better decisions. The Feedback, Awareness and Behaviour (FAB) study in Cambridgeshire put that assumption to a particularly clear test. The trial randomised 466 adults to no immediate feedback or one of three progressively richer versions of personalised physical activity feedback . “Simple” feedback gave participants their average physical activity level alongside reference categories and information about activity guidelines. “Visual” feedback added graphs of heart rate and movement across the day. “Contextualised” feedback added estimates showing how familiar activities such as walking, cycling and housework could increase activity, together with material intended to support goal setting. The design deliberately made each successive condition more information rich. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Yet personalised feedback did not significantly change objectively measured physical activity, self reported activity or intention to become more active. It did improve participants’ awareness of their activity level, but the simple, visual and contextualised versions did not differ significantly in their behavioural effects. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That is an important failure mode for self monitoring: better understanding is an intermediate outcome, not necessarily the goal itself . A graph may correct a mistaken belief about how active someone is. Additional context may explain what the graph means. Neither necessarily removes the practical barrier that prevents that person from exercising tomorrow. A later UK intervention pushed sophistication considerably further. MIPACT provided primary care patients at risk of cardiovascular disease or type 2 diabetes with personalised, multidimensional physical activity feedback through a digital system and web app, supported by health trainer sessions. Feedback covered several dimensions rather than relying on a single activity number. At 12 months, however, the trial found no meaningful improvement in moderate to vigorous activity, overall physical activity level or sedentary time compared with usual care; for the principal outcomes, the groups were effectively equivalent. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There is an instructive design detail behind that study. During development, interviews with patients and health professionals found that patients generally preferred simple messages to more complex or abstract visualisations. The eventual platform therefore offered accessible graphical formats and additional guidance rather than assuming that information richness was inherently desirable. [Springer] link.springer.com Open source on springer.com. These findings do not show that visualisation is useless. A richer display may be valuable when it exposes a pattern that a single number hides — for example, distinguishing long sedentary periods from insufficient vigorous exercise . But richness earns its place only when the extra dimension changes interpretation or suggests a different action. Complexity added for its own sake can turn a feedback tool into a better description of the problem without making the solution easier. Explanatory illustration 2 Adherence can improve while the main outcome does not One"},{"title":"Flexible Cues","url":"/flexible-cues/","description":"A cue works best when it recurs in real life, so flexible event-based cues can outperform exact clock times when daily schedules vary.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Small Habits","level":3,"text":"Flexible Cues Choose Habit Cues Your Real Schedule Repeats Small Habits Flexible Cues Flexible Cues Choose Habit Cues Your Real Schedule Repeats A cue works best when it recurs in real life, so flexible event based cues can outperform exact clock times when daily schedules vary. A cue works best when it recurs in real life, so flexible event based cues can outperform exact clock times when daily schedules vary. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic population context Why stable context helps habits become automatic When exact times become fragile cues Using recurring daily events as more durable anchors self improvement that works small habits flexible cues Introduction When your days keep changing, the most useful habit cue may be something that happens reliably rather than something that happens at a reliable hour. “At 7.30 am” is a workable cue if 7.30 am repeatedly finds you in the right circumstances. For a shift worker, frequent traveller, parent with variable mornings or anyone whose working day moves around, “after I brush my teeth”, “when I finish lunch” or “after I close my laptop for the day” may preserve more of the context that habit learning needs. Explanatory illustration 1 The important distinction is not simply clock cue versus routine cue . Research shows that both can support habit formation. The deeper requirement is repeated enactment in a sufficiently stable context: a cue has to recur, be noticed and lead to the behaviour often enough for the association to strengthen. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 For an irregular life, flexibility therefore means finding stability inside the variability , not changing the cue every day. Why stable context helps habits become automatic Psychological accounts of habit describe it as an association through which a recurring context can increasingly prompt a response without a fresh decision each time. In other words, repetition alone is not the whole story. Repetition in relation to recurring cues is what gives the environment an increasing role in getting the behaviour started. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... That helps explain why context stability matters. In a 2022 study, Marco Stojanovic and colleagues tested this directly. In one experiment, 95 university students built study habits for six weeks; one group was instructed to vary rooms and times, while another kept the performance context stable. A second study examined 308 habits recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of the goal for each habit repetition. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Older research demonstrates the same principle from the opposite direction. Wendy Wood and colleagues followed university students through a move to a new university and examined exercising, television watching and newspaper reading. Established behaviours were more likely to be disrupted when important features of their usual performance contexts changed. Habits survived changes better when relevant aspects of the old context remained similar. [USC Dornsife] dornsife.usc.edu Dornsife Journal of Personality and Social Psychology USC DornsifeJournal of Personality and Social PsychologyJune 20, 2005... Published: June 20, 2005 For someone with an irregular schedule, this creates an apparent problem. If Monday begins at home at 6 am, Tuesday in a hotel at 8 am and Wednesday after a night shift, there may be no single stable combination of time, place and surrounding activity available every day. But context does not have to mean a particular room at a particular clock time. Habit researchers treat contextual cues more broadly: they can include locations, preceding actions, other people and recurring events. Practical habit formation guidance has therefore long recommended cues embedded in existing routines, such as taking an action after breakfast or when beginning a lunch break. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 The useful question becomes: what part of my day stays recognisably the same even when its timing changes? The Science of Making & Breaking Habits: How to Change Your Life in 1 Month Channel: Mel Robbins Open on YouTube When exact times become fragile cues An exact clock time is not inherently a bad habit cue. The strongest direct comparison of clock based and routine based cueing actually cautions against claiming that event cues are universally superior. In a 2021 randomised controlled trial, Jan Keller and colleagues assigned 192 adults to link a self selected nutrition behaviour either to a daily routine or to a particular time. Participants then completed daily assessments for 84 days. Automaticity and plan enactment increased in both conditions, with no significant group level advantage for routine cues over time cues. Among participants whose habit formation followed the expected successful trajectory, the median modelled time to reach 95% of peak automaticity was 59 days. Most importantly for practice, repeatedly carrying out the behaviour in response to the planned cue predicted increasing automaticity. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryHabit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial UCL Discovery... So “always use an event, never use a time” would go beyond the evidence. A person who predictably takes a break at 10.30 am may do perfectly well with a 10.30 cue. The problem arises when clock stability and life stability diverge . Suppose you plan five minutes of mobility work at 7 am. On a normal office day, 7 am finds you awake and at home. After a late shift it finds you asleep; during travel you may be in transit; on a weekend you may simply wake later. The clock continues to produce 7 am with perfect reliability, but the circumstances that make the behaviour feasible have disappeared. This distinction also makes sense from research on prospective memory — remembering to carry out a future intention. Time based intentions require a person to monitor time, whereas event based intentions can be retrieved when an external event occurs. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That literature is not identical to habit research, so it should not be treated as proof that event cues necessarily create stronger habits. It does, however, illuminate one practical cost of a clock cue: before an association becomes automatic, “do this at 10 am” may require some degree of remembering or checking that 10 am has arrived. A routine event can instead carry the reminder with it. Finishing breakfast, putting away a toothbrush, getting into a car or shutting down a computer produces a perceptible transition in the environment. Habit formation guidance accordingly recommends cues that are sufficiently noticeable and encountered consistently, and identifies established daily routines as convenient starting points. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 For variable schedules, then, the comparison is better framed this way: “At 7 am” is stable in clock time but may occur in radically different practical circumstances. “After my first meal” can move several hours while retaining the same position in the day's behavioural sequence. “When I arrive home” can survive changing finishing times, although it fails on days when you do not return home in the usual way. “After I brush my teeth before sleep” can travel across shifts and time zones if that existing behaviour itself remains dependable. The best cue is the one whose real world recurrence most closely matches the opportunities you actually have to perform the habit. Explanatory illustration 2 Use daily events as durable anchors For an irregular schedule, choosing a flexible cue starts with observation rather than an ideal timetable. Look for events that already recur across different versions of your day. Meals, hygiene routines, opening or closing a work session, arriving somewhere, making a first drink, plugging in a phone, changing clothes and preparing for sleep are candidates only if they genuinely recur in your life. Then make the relationship between cue and action specific. “ Exercise sometime after work”"},{"title":"Behaviour Reports","url":"/behaviour-reports/","description":"Accountability becomes clearer when another person sees whether specific planned actions happened, rather than hearing a general self-assessment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Accountability","level":3,"text":"Behaviour Reports What Should You Actually Report for Accountability? Behaviour Reports Behaviour Reports What Should You Actually Report for Accountability? Accountability becomes clearer when another person sees whether specific planned actions happened, rather than hearing a general self assessment. Accountability becomes clearer when another person sees whether specific planned actions happened, rather than hearing a general self assessment. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism Why completed behaviours make better reports Setting the measure before the week begins Keeping reports short without hiding missed actions self improvement that works accountability behaviour reports Introduction For accountability, report what you did , not how committed, busy or “on track” you felt. A useful report compares a behaviour agreed in advance with a behaviour that can be observed afterwards: “I planned four 30 minute study sessions; I completed three” is more informative than “I worked quite hard this week”. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because accountability needs something another person can actually check. Research on progress monitoring finds that monitoring improves goal attainment on average, with stronger effects when the information is recorded and reported to somebody else or made public. Meanwhile, supportive accountability research argues that expectations work best when they are clear, agreed beforehand and focused on processes rather than left ambiguous. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The practical principle is therefore simple: define the behaviour before the reporting period, count what happened, and report the result without quietly changing the measure afterwards. Why completed behaviours make better reports A vague progress report mixes together several different things: intention, effort, interpretation and action. Someone can sincerely say “I’m taking my fitness seriously” without revealing whether any planned exercise happened. Even “I had a good week” leaves the listener unable to distinguish five completed workouts from one unusually energetic session. Behaviour reports remove much of that ambiguity. Consider the difference: Intention: “I’m going to read more.” General self assessment: “I did fairly well with reading this week.” Outcome: “I want to finish 20 books this year.” Completed behaviour report: “I planned to read for 25 minutes on five evenings and did it on four.” The last version gives an accountability partner information that can be compared with the original commitment. It also preserves partial success: four out of five is neither an undefined “pretty good” nor a binary failure. This fits the broader evidence on progress monitoring. Harkin and colleagues analysed 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions designed to increase monitoring substantially increased monitoring frequency and produced a smaller but meaningful improvement in goal attainment (standardised effect size d = 0.40). Importantly for social accountability, effects on goal attainment were larger when progress was reported or made public and when monitoring information was physically recorded. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The study does not establish that a particular weekly reporting format is uniquely effective. Its more defensible implication is that accountability benefits from having actual progress information to communicate. A report such as “3/4 sessions completed” preserves information that “I’m still committed” does not. Report the controllable action, not only the result Completed behaviours are particularly useful when the eventual outcome is only partly under personal control. “Lose 1 kg this week”, “get a job” or “receive an A” can be affected by factors beyond the person's immediate actions. By contrast, submitting three applications, completing four planned training sessions or doing five scheduled revision blocks describes behaviour the person can perform directly. The supportive accountability model developed by David Mohr, Pim Cuijpers and Kenneth Lehman makes this distinction explicit. It argues for clear expectations that concentrate on processes , are established with the participant and are understood before accountability occurs. Its examples distinguish outcome accountability, such as symptom severity, from process accountability, such as completing specified exercises or logging into an intervention. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth Interventions PMC... There is an important limit to that principle. Research comparing process and outcome accountability does not show that process accountability universally produces better performance. A 2022 meta analysis covering nine experiments and 1,080 participants found no overall superiority for either approach; task complexity moderated the results. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effect of Outcome vs. Process Accountability Focus on Performance: A Meta Analysis PMCApril 27, 2022... Published: April 27, 2022 For ordinary personal commitments involving straightforward, repeatable actions, however, behaviour counts have a narrower advantage: they tell the accountability partner unambiguously whether the agreed action occurred. That is a reporting advantage, not a claim that outcomes never matter. How Accountability Partners Keep You Committed Atomic Habits Book Summary Chapter 17 (James Clear) Channel: Alexander Emmanuel Open on YouTube Set the measure before the week begins A behaviour report only becomes a meaningful accountability device when its definition precedes the behaviour. Otherwise, the target can move to accommodate whatever happened. Suppose someone agrees simply to “work on my novel”. At the end of the week, researching a location, rearranging notes, thinking about a character on the train or writing 2,000 words could all retrospectively qualify as success. If the advance commitment was “three 45 minute drafting sessions by Sunday”, there is much less room to redefine success after seeing the result. This is one reason specificity matters. Goal setting research has long contrasted concrete goals with vague instructions such as “do your best”, although specificity should not be treated as a magic ingredient independent of difficulty, task knowledge and other conditions. Research separating specificity from goal level shows that the two properties should not simply be conflated. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Separating the effects of goal specificity from goal level Separating the effects of goal specificity from goal level ScienceDirectApril 1, 1989... Published: April 1, 1989 The relevant lesson for accountability is narrower: the reporting criterion needs enough precision for two people to agree afterwards whether it was met . Research on implementation intentions reinforces the value of defining action in advance. Implementation intentions specify when, where or under what circumstances a goal directed behaviour will occur. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests found a medium to large positive effect on goal attainment ( d = 0.65), although implementation intentions are a planning technique rather than evidence specifically about social reporting. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... They nevertheless illustrate the same useful transition from “I intend to do X” towards a pre specified action that can later be assessed. For a weekly accountability report, a workable measure usually answers three questions: 1. What counts? “A study session” means at least 30 minutes of uninterrupted practice questions. 2. How many were planned? Four sessions between Monday and Sunday. 3. What will be reported? Number completed out of four, rather than a general judgement about whether the week went well. The denominator matters. Reporting “three workouts” sounds positive in isolation. If three were planned, it means full completion; if six were planned, it means half. Planned versus completed preserves the information accountability needs. There is also value in deciding the reporting arrangement beforehand. The supportive accountability model warns that introducing accountability only after an action has occurred can encourage self justification rather than useful reflection. It therefore recommends making expectations and accountability procedures explicit in advance. [PubMed Central (PMC)]"},{"title":"Real World Effects","url":"/real-world-effects/","description":"A large commercial-gym trial found smaller but more durable exercise gains than the dramatic early university experiment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Temptation Bundling","level":3,"text":"Real World Effects How Much Does Temptation Bundling Actually Help? Real World Effects Real World Effects How Much Does Temptation Bundling Actually Help? A large commercial gym trial found smaller but more durable exercise gains than the dramatic early university experiment. A large commercial gym trial found smaller but more durable exercise gains than the dramatic early university experiment. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence The large commercial gym experiment and its behavioural measures How its effect sizes compared with the university trial What smaller but longer lasting gains mean in practice self improvement that works temptation bundling real world effects Introduction The strongest real world test of temptation bundling suggests that it helps exercise , but by much less than the dramatic headline from the original university experiment. In a field experiment involving 6,792 members of the commercial chain 24 Hour Fitness, researchers tested a lightweight version: participants could receive a free audiobook and, in one condition, were explicitly encouraged to save tempting entertainment for workouts. The intervention raised measures of gym attendance by roughly 10–14%, with effects detectable for as long as 17 weeks after the four week programme. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... Explanatory illustration 1 That is important for self improvement because the trial traded experimental control for realism. Nobody locked participants' entertainment inside a gym. People had ordinary memberships, could access their audiobook elsewhere and had to enforce the rule themselves. The resulting gains were smaller than those in the pioneering university study, but they survived in a setting much closer to everyday life. The evidence therefore points towards temptation bundling as a modest behavioural aid rather than a transformation: useful precisely because a relatively light intervention can shift repeated behaviour at all. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... Big Brains Podcast: The science behind forming better habits, with Katy Milkman Channel: The University of Chicago Open on YouTube The commercial gym test Erika Kirgios and colleagues embedded their temptation bundling experiment in StepUp, an exercise promoting programme run with 24 Hour Fitness. Participants were existing adult gym members rather than university students recruited into an unusually controlled study. The researchers could measure behaviour objectively through gym check ins and had as much as 52 weeks of pre intervention attendance data for participants, giving them a substantial baseline against which to assess changes. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. The crucial comparison involved three approaches. A control group participated without receiving an audiobook. A second group received a code for a free audiobook but no explicit lesson in temptation bundling. A third received the audiobook and was encouraged to use entertainment strategically with exercise — including reserving enjoyable media for workouts. This was a deliberately low touch intervention: unlike the original experiment, the researchers did not physically prevent participants from listening elsewhere. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... That distinction makes the experiment unusually informative. A behavioural technique can work impressively when researchers create the perfect conditions for it yet prove impractical when people must administer it themselves. Here, participants had to remember the rule, resist breaking it and decide when and how to combine entertainment with exercise. The study was therefore testing not merely whether an enforced bundle can alter behaviour, but whether the basic idea can survive contact with ordinary consumer choice. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. The behavioural outcomes were actual gym visits rather than participants' intentions or recollections. Across the study, pairing an audiobook with encouragement to temptation bundle increased the probability of making at least one weekly gym visit by about 10–14% and average weekly workouts by about 10–12%, with benefits observed during the programme and in follow up analyses extending as far as 17 weeks afterwards. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment ScienceDirect... There is an important qualification hidden behind those averages. Only about 14% of participants in the explicit temptation bundling condition actually downloaded their free audiobook, almost identical to the 13% download rate among those simply offered one. Meanwhile, 78.6% of participants in the explicit condition signed a pledge to temptation bundle, and fewer than a third said audiobooks were their preferred entertainment for pairing with exercise. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. This means the experiment should not be read as showing that thousands of people faithfully listened to novels only on treadmills and consequently exercised more. The treatment was an offer, an idea and a set of prompts delivered at scale, with substantial variation in how people engaged with it. That messiness is a limitation if the question is the pure physiological effect of listening to an audiobook while exercising. It is a strength if the question is what happens when a behavioural strategy is offered to real people who remain free to ignore, adapt or imperfectly follow it. The Science of Change with Katy Milkman Channel: Young and Profiting Open on YouTube Why the gains were smaller than the university result The contrast with the original temptation bundling experiment is striking. Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp's earlier university study randomly assigned 226 participants, of whom 215 attended the intake session. In its strongest condition, participants selected tempting audio novels that were loaded onto an iPod stored in a monitored locker at the university gym. If they wanted the next chapter, they had to return. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 At the beginning of that experiment, the researchers estimated that the enforced bundle group made 0.48 additional gym visits per week relative to a regression estimated 0.94 visits in the control condition — a 51% increase. Participants merely encouraged to impose the restriction themselves initially showed a smaller 29% increase. The effects then weakened as the weeks passed, with a particularly conspicuous disruption around the Thanksgiving break. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The later commercial trial produced no comparable 51% surge. Instead, its roughly 10–14% improvements are more plausible as an estimate of what a scalable, lightly administered programme might achieve. The two experiments did not test identical interventions or populations, so their percentages should not be treated as a clean replication in which one simply supersedes the other. But the difference is informative: enforcing access to the temptation can produce a powerful initial incentive, whereas asking people to create and maintain their own rule yields a smaller behavioural change. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 There is another difference that matters just as much: durability. The original study's strongest treatment weakened by about 0.07 gym visits per week as its nine week intervention progressed, and the Thanksgiving interruption substantially eroded the advantage. By contrast, the commercial gym study found detectable treatment effects in follow up analyses extending 17 weeks beyond its four week intervention. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014... Published: February 1, 2014 The large trial therefore changes what the evidence means. The most eye"},{"title":"Reward Restriction","url":"/reward-restriction/","description":"Keeping the enjoyable activity unavailable elsewhere can preserve its pull and make the beneficial activity more tempting next time.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Temptation Bundling","level":3,"text":"Reward Restriction Should You Lock Your Temptation to the Habit? Temptation Bundling Reward Restriction Reward Restriction Should You Lock Your Temptation to the Habit? Keeping the enjoyable activity unavailable elsewhere can preserve its pull and make the beneficial activity more tempting next time. Keeping the enjoyable activity unavailable elsewhere can preserve its pull and make the beneficial activity more tempting next time. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention Why unrestricted rewards lose motivational value What the gym only audiobook condition changed Practical ways to create a commitment constraint self improvement that works temptation bundling reward restriction Introduction Restricting the enjoyable half of a temptation bundle can make the whole arrangement stronger. If the next chapter, favourite podcast or television episode is available whenever you want it, there is little reason to do the difficult beneficial activity to get it. Reserve that pleasure for the workout, walk or other chosen activity, however, and anticipation itself can become part of the motivation to begin. Explanatory illustration 1 The clearest evidence comes from Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp's gym experiment. Participants whose tempting audiobooks were physically kept at the gym initially attended substantially more often than controls, and more often than participants who merely promised themselves not to listen elsewhere. The result does not prove that every reward should be locked away. It does show why restriction is more than an incidental detail of temptation bundling: exclusivity preserves something worth coming back for. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 Changing Behavior With 'Temptation Bundling' Channel: PennLDIvideo Open on YouTube Why unrestricted rewards lose motivational value A temptation bundle needs a link between two activities: doing this beneficial thing gives me access to that enjoyable thing . Unrestricted access weakens that link. If you listen to the gripping audiobook on the sofa tonight, its unresolved story can no longer provide the same reason to exercise tomorrow. In that sense, consuming the temptation separately spends some of the incentive that the bundle was supposed to save. Restriction therefore changes the role of the enjoyable activity. It is no longer merely entertainment that happens to coincide with exercise. It becomes a benefit conditional on exercise. This resembles a commitment device : a voluntary arrangement made in advance to protect a longer term intention against a more tempting choice later. Reviews of commitment devices distinguish harder constraints, which make deviation genuinely difficult, from softer commitments that rely more heavily on a person's willingness to follow a rule. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews Commitment Devices Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsCommitment Devices Annual ReviewsSeptember 4, 2010... Published: September 4, 2010 Temptation bundling has an unusual advantage over many conventional commitment devices. A commitment contract might punish failure by taking away money. A restrictive bundle need not impose a new punishment. Instead, it relocates an existing pleasure so that access to it pulls the person towards the desired behaviour. Milkman and colleagues explicitly described this as a distinctive form of commitment device: it can address under consumption of the beneficial “should” activity and over consumption of the immediately pleasurable “want” at the same time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 There is an important second mechanism. Restriction can preserve anticipation . The original researchers noted that repeated engagement with the tempting fiction could generate desire to continue it, whereas distance from it could reduce its allure. That helps explain why a bundle is vulnerable to long interruptions: after participants stopped listening over the Thanksgiving break, the experimental advantage dropped sharply. The researchers interpreted this pattern as consistent with the idea that continued contact with the enjoyable activity was helping sustain its motivational pull. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 So the useful rule is not simply “deny yourself fun”. It is more precise: do not routinely consume elsewhere the particular pleasure that you are relying on to make the beneficial activity attractive . The Science of Change with Katy Milkman Channel: Young and Profiting Open on YouTube What the gym only audiobook condition changed The original field experiment provides an unusually clean illustration because it compared different degrees of restriction. Participants chose from pre tested, tempting audio novels. In the strongest treatment, four chosen novels were loaded onto a loaned iPod Shuffle that remained in a monitored locker at the university gym. Participants could therefore listen when they visited the facility but could not simply take the device home. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 A second group received tempting audiobooks on their own devices. They were encouraged to impose the same “gym only” rule on themselves, but nothing physically prevented them from listening elsewhere. A control group received a similarly valued bookshop gift certificate and encouragement to exercise. The comparison matters because the strongest treatment did not merely tell participants about temptation bundling: it altered their environment so that violating the restriction was much harder. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 At the beginning of the intervention, the enforced gym only group made a regression adjusted 0.48 additional gym visits per week relative to the control group, a 51% increase over the estimated control attendance rate. The self restriction group made 0.27 additional visits, equivalent to a 29% increase, although that comparison was only marginally statistically significant. The enforced condition also produced significantly more attendance than the self restriction condition early in the study. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 That difference is the strongest direct evidence for locking the temptation to the habit, but it deserves careful interpretation. The experiment did not isolate restriction as the only difference between two otherwise perfectly identical interventions: the enforced group also used a loaned device stored at the gym, whereas the intermediate group used personal devices and had to police its own rule. The results therefore support the practical advantage of an enforced bundle over a self enforced one, rather than establishing a precise universal effect size for restriction itself. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 Nor did the lock make the intervention permanently effective. Both treatment effects weakened across the nine week study, particularly around Thanksgiving. The enforced treatment's advantage declined by about 0.07 visits per week for each additional week of the intervention. Restriction can protect the reward from premature consumption, but it cannot guarantee that the reward will remain exciting indefinitely or that a routine will survive major disruptions. [PubMed Central (PMC)] nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PubMed Central (PMC)Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling PMCFebruary 1, 2014 One"},{"title":"Routine Breaks","url":"/routine-breaks/","description":"A holiday or other interruption can sharply weaken a bundle when the pairing depends on a stable place, device or routine.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Temptation Bundling","level":3,"text":"Routine Breaks Why a Break Can Undo Temptation Bundling Routine Breaks Routine Breaks Why a Break Can Undo Temptation Bundling A holiday or other interruption can sharply weaken a bundle when the pairing depends on a stable place, device or routine. A holiday or other interruption can sharply weaken a bundle when the pairing depends on a stable place, device or routine. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single case aspect event time window What happened after the Thanksgiving interruption Why place dependent bundles can be fragile Designing pairings that can survive disrupted routines self improvement that works temptation bundling routine breaks Introduction Temptation bundling can make a difficult beneficial activity easier to start by attaching an immediate pleasure to it. But the method has an important weak point: the bundle may depend on the very routine it is supposed to strengthen. In the original gym experiment, participants with gym only access to tempting audiobooks initially exercised substantially more than controls, but the advantage declined with time and deteriorated particularly sharply around Thanksgiving, when the university gym closed and normal routines were interrupted. Explanatory illustration 1 That episode matters because it shows that a successful bundle is not necessarily a durable habit. When the enjoyable activity, the beneficial activity and the cues connecting them all depend on one place or schedule, a holiday, trip, illness or closure can remove the whole behavioural set up at once. Evidence from exercise and habit research suggests that such interruptions can suppress behaviour even after ordinary life resumes. Changing Behavior With 'Temptation Bundling' Channel: PennLDIvideo Open on YouTube What happened after the Thanksgiving interruption Katherine Milkman, Julia Minson and Kevin Volpp's original field experiment followed 226 university gym users. Participants in the strongest temptation bundling condition chose four highly tempting audio novels and received them on an iPod that was stored at the gym. They could therefore continue their chosen stories only by returning to exercise. Another group received the audiobooks on their own devices and was encouraged to restrict listening to workouts, while a control group received an equivalently valued gift certificate. The early result was striking. In the first week, the regression adjusted gym attendance advantage for the full treatment group was 0.48 visits per week over the control group's estimated 0.94 visits — a 51% increase. Across the seven weeks before Thanksgiving, participants in the full treatment made an average of 7.79 gym visits, compared with 6.51 in the intermediate group and 6.11 among controls. Then the calendar intervened. The university gym closed for Thanksgiving and students went home. The neat arrangement linking a particular place, an exercise session and exclusive access to an audiobook was temporarily impossible to perform. After the interruption, the earlier treatment advantage largely disappeared. In intervention week eight, average attendance was 0.75 visits in the full treatment group versus 0.99 among controls; in week nine it was 0.47 versus 0.67. Those figures should not be read as proof that Thanksgiving reversed the treatment — the study was not designed to randomise people to receiving or avoiding the holiday — but they make the interruption an important failure point in the experiment. The weakening had actually begun before the break. Across the nine week study, the estimated full treatment effect fell by about 0.07 gym visits per week, a statistically significant attenuation. Thanksgiving therefore should not be treated as the sole explanation for declining effectiveness. Rather, it appears to have accelerated or exposed a vulnerability that was already developing. Milkman later described the interruption as a period of forced separation from the tempting stimulus: the effects had been robust for roughly seven weeks, but the Thanksgiving closure disrupted the pattern. That retrospective interpretation fits the observed data, while also highlighting the study's limitation: the holiday was a naturally occurring interruption, not a separately controlled experimental treatment. Katy Milkman Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling Channel: PennLDIvideo Open on YouTube Why place dependent bundles can be fragile The Thanksgiving result becomes more understandable when temptation bundling is viewed as a cue dependent system rather than simply a motivational trick. In the full treatment, several pieces lined up: go to the familiar gym, obtain the stored device, exercise and hear the next part of the story. Repetition could make those elements mutually reinforcing. But a closure removed both the opportunity to exercise in that setting and access to the exclusive temptation. That is consistent with the broader science of behavioural routines. Habits develop partly through repeated behaviour in recurring contexts, so changes in location, timing and other environmental cues can weaken the connection between a situation and the usual response. Research on habit discontinuity has found that context changes can interrupt established behaviours precisely because familiar cues stop appearing in their normal configuration. Exercise provides particularly relevant evidence. A study of 1,210 members of Danish fitness centres examined what happened around the Easter holiday. Immediately after Easter, gym attendance fell by 0.24 visits per week, or about 12.25% relative to the week before the holiday. The decline was especially pronounced among working age members and people who had previously exercised at least twice a week. Although that study did not test temptation bundling, it strengthens the interpretation that holidays themselves can disrupt exercise routines rather than merely providing neutral pauses in them. There is an important nuance, however. Context stability is not a simple switch that guarantees habit formation. An ecological momentary assessment study of 104 older adults found that more stable physical contexts were associated with greater moderate to vigorous physical activity , but its measures of context stability did not predict self reported physical activity habit strength. More recent longitudinal research has found context stability to be more consistently related to changes in habit strength, while also suggesting that the particular cue that matters can differ from person to person. So the safest interpretation is not that changing place automatically destroys a temptation bundle. It is that a bundle concentrated around one configuration of cues has fewer routes by which it can continue when that configuration disappears. There is another vulnerability specific to temptation bundling: the temptation itself can become inaccessible at exactly the wrong moment . Ordinary exercise habits may at least be transferable — someone whose gym closes can run outdoors or use another gym. In the original full treatment condition, however, the novels were physically stored at the university gym. Exercising elsewhere during Thanksgiving could not reproduce the bundle. The commitment device that made cheating difficult also made the intervention less portable. That creates a genuine design trade off. Strong restrictions protect the exclusivity of the reward: if a gripping audiobook is available only during exercise, wanting the next chapter can create a reason to work out. Yet the tighter the restriction is tied to a particular venue or device, the more easily a disruption can make the whole arrangement unavailable. A looser rule is more portable, but also easier to break by consuming the temptation on the sofa. Explanatory illustration 2 Designing pairings that can survive disrupted routines The Thanksgiving episode suggests that the strongest practical bundle is not necessarily the one with the strictest possible restriction. It is one that preserves the crucial rule — this pleasure accompanies this beneficial behaviour — while allowing the behaviour to move between realistic circumstances. For example, “I listen to this series only at my usual gym” is highly specific but brittle. “I listen to this series only while exercising” preserves exclusivity while allowing a hotel gym, outdoor walk, stationary bike or temporary alternative to keep the association alive. The appropriate degree of flexibility depends on the activity: broadening the rule so far that almost any situation qualifies would eventually remove the commitment value that makes the bundle useful. Three design principles follow from the evidence: Bind the reward to the behaviour more than to the venue. Where practical, make the defining condition “while exercising”, “while doing household administration” or another beneficial action rather than “at this particular building”. The original experiment"},{"title":"Small Adjustments","url":"/small-adjustments/","description":"Changing one plausible source of friction at a time makes it easier to learn which redesign actually improves a failing routine.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Adapt the Plan","level":3,"text":"Small Adjustments Why Small Routine Changes Are Easier to Test Adapt the Plan Small Adjustments Small Adjustments Why Small Routine Changes Are Easier to Test Changing one plausible source of friction at a time makes it easier to learn which redesign actually improves a failing routine. Changing one plausible source of friction at a time makes it easier to learn which redesign actually improves a failing routine. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention How to identify the smallest change that targets the failure Why changing several variables at once obscures what worked When repeated small tests justify a larger redesign self improvement that works adapt the plan small adjustments Introduction When a routine keeps failing in the same way, the most useful response is usually not a complete reset. It is a minimum useful adjustment : the smallest practical change that plausibly targets the recurring point of failure while leaving enough of the routine unchanged to see whether the change helped. This turns redesign into a simple experiment rather than another burst of reinvention. Explanatory illustration 1 The principle is not that smaller changes are automatically better. It is that targeted changes are easier to interpret . Behaviour change frameworks recommend diagnosing whether a problem lies in capability, opportunity or motivation and matching the intervention to that problem, while NICE guidance explicitly recommends reviewing goals in light of experience, tailoring interventions and using small, manageable changes to daily routines. [Springer] link.springer.com The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions Implementation Science... For a repeatedly failing routine, that suggests a practical rule: identify one plausible source of friction, change one relevant feature, keep the rest reasonably stable, and observe whether the same failure becomes less frequent. Escalate only when these focused tests stop producing useful improvements. Find the smallest change that targets the failure A minimum useful adjustment begins with a specific failure , not a vague judgement that the routine “isn't working”. The useful question is: What repeatedly happens immediately before the routine breaks down? The COM B model provides a compact way of narrowing the possibilities. It treats behaviour as depending on capability — being able to perform it; opportunity — having a physical and social environment that permits it; and motivation — the reflective and automatic processes that energise or direct it. Importantly, the model links different diagnoses to different kinds of intervention rather than assuming that every behavioural problem needs more motivation. [Springer] link.springer.com The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions Implementation Science... For an ordinary routine, the diagnosis can be much simpler than the formal framework: The routine is forgotten: strengthen or relocate its cue. Instead of “read every evening”, try “after I put my dinner plate in the dishwasher, I read for ten minutes”. It starts but is repeatedly abandoned: reduce its minimum size. A planned 45 minute exercise session might become a 15 minute minimum rather than disappearing altogether on busy days. The timing repeatedly collides with something else: change the time while retaining the activity, place and target. Preparation creates the bottleneck: prepare the necessary equipment or materials in advance rather than changing the activity itself. A predictable obstacle defeats the plan: add one specific fallback rule for that obstacle. The environment keeps pulling behaviour elsewhere: alter the relevant part of the environment — for example, moving distracting devices rather than constructing an entirely new productivity system. These are deliberately narrow interventions. Environmental restructuring, prompts, action plans and enablement are established categories within behaviour change research; the important implementation decision is to select the one that corresponds to the observed barrier. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The behaviour change wheel: A new method for characterising and designing behaviour change interventions PMC... A useful test of “minimum” is therefore not whether the adjustment feels trivial. Ask instead: If my diagnosis is right, is this change large enough to affect the failure? Moving a workout from 18:00 to 17:45 is not useful if the recurring obstacle is an unpredictable commute lasting until 19:00. Moving it to the morning might be. The smallest intervention still has to reach the suspected cause. BJ Fogg The key to make positive habits (Shortened Talk) Channel: QuickTalks Open on YouTube Make the adjustment specific enough to test Vague redesigns are difficult to evaluate because they leave considerable discretion at the moment when the old routine usually fails. “Try harder to study earlier” does not specify what will actually happen. “On weekdays, after breakfast, I will study at the kitchen table for 20 minutes” changes the situation more concretely. Research on implementation intentions — plans linking a recognised situation to a predetermined response in an “if–then” structure — supports this kind of specificity. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment. A much larger 2024 review covering 642 independent tests found positive effects across cognitive, emotional and behavioural outcomes, with stronger effects when plans used a contingent if–then format, motivation was high and the plans were rehearsed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... That evidence does not mean every failing routine can be repaired by writing an if–then sentence. It shows why a precise adjustment is more actionable than a general intention . If the recurring failure is “I skip the routine whenever the meeting runs late”, a testable adjustment might be: “If I am still at work at 18:00, I do the 15 minute version when I get home instead of cancelling the session.” The change now contains an observable trigger and response. After several relevant occasions, it is possible to ask whether that rule actually improved completion. “Be more disciplined”, by contrast, provides almost nothing that can be tested or refined. Why changing everything hides what worked Suppose an evening study routine fails repeatedly. In response, someone simultaneously switches from evenings to mornings, changes rooms, buys a new planning app, reduces sessions from an hour to 25 minutes, starts using website blocking software and introduces a reward for completion. If studying improves, what should be retained? The earlier time might have solved the problem. The shorter duration might have mattered. Perhaps removing online distraction did the work. Or the apparent improvement might simply reflect a temporary surge of attention associated with starting a new system. This is the central informational advantage of minimum useful adjustments: fewer simultaneous changes reduce ambiguity about what produced the improvement . The logic resembles, without being equivalent to, single case experimental research. Formal single case designs repeatedly measure an individual's behaviour while an intervention is systematically manipulated, allowing the individual to serve as their own control. Researchers use such designs precisely because repeated measurement and controlled changes can help isolate whether an intervention affected the target behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Single Case Experimental Design: A New Approach for Non invasive Brain Stimulation Research? PMCMay 31, 2021... Published: May 31, 2021 Research on technology based behavioural interventions likewise notes that single case experiments can help isolate the active elements of a larger treatment package. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Single Case Experimental Designs to Evaluate Novel Technology Based Health InterventionsFebrua... An everyday routine experiment is far less controlled and should not be mistaken for scientific proof. Sleep, workload, illness, weather, deadlines and countless other variables continue changing. But the experimental logic remains useful: if you alter six things simultaneously, you voluntarily discard much of your ability to learn from the outcome . That matters beyond the first improvement. If the redesigned routine later deteriorates, knowing which adjustment previously helped makes further adaptation easier. A wholesale redesign leaves behind a package; a sequence of targeted tests leaves behind knowledge. Explanatory illustration 2 Keep stable"},{"title":"Failure Windows","url":"/failure-windows/","description":"A routine deserves redesign when the same situation repeatedly predicts failure, not simply because one attempt went badly.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Adapt the Plan","level":3,"text":"Failure Windows When Repeated Failures Mean Your Routine Needs Redesign Adapt the Plan Failure Windows Failure Windows When Repeated Failures Mean Your Routine Needs Redesign A routine deserves redesign when the same situation repeatedly predicts failure, not simply because one attempt went badly. A routine deserves redesign when the same situation repeatedly predicts failure, not simply because one attempt went badly. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic event time window How to separate one off lapses from recurring failure windows Which recurring conditions are informative enough to act on Why local diagnosis is more useful than global self judgment self improvement that works adapt the plan failure windows Introduction A routine deserves redesign when failure becomes predictable . One missed workout, abandoned study session or disrupted morning routine may be noise: illness, travel, an unusual deadline or chance can defeat an otherwise workable plan. But when the same behaviour repeatedly fails in the same kind of window — after late meetings, on low sleep mornings, during the hour before dinner, or whenever the usual location is unavailable — the repetition is evidence about the routine itself. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because behaviour is strongly shaped by context. Research on habits shows that recurring contextual cues can support automatic behaviour, while changes in those cues can disrupt it. Research on goal monitoring likewise finds that recording progress can improve goal attainment. The useful move is therefore not to count failures indiscriminately, but to ask whether they cluster under identifiable conditions . A cluster points towards a local design weakness that can be tested and changed. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... How to separate a one off lapse from a recurring failure window A failure window is best understood as a recurring combination of circumstances in which a planned behaviour becomes substantially less reliable. The important feature is not merely repetition. It is conditional repetition : the failures keep appearing when particular conditions recur. Suppose someone intends to exercise four times a week and misses three sessions in a month. Those three misses alone reveal relatively little. If one happened during illness, another during travel and another because the gym unexpectedly closed, there may be no common mechanism to redesign. But if all three were Wednesday evening sessions following a late day at work, that clustering carries much more information. “I sometimes miss exercise” has become “this routine repeatedly breaks in this particular situation”. This is why monitoring can be more useful when it records circumstances rather than simply producing a streak or success percentage. A major meta analysis covering 138 studies and 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase progress monitoring increased goal attainment, with a mean effect of d = 0.40; effects were larger when progress was physically recorded. The study did not establish a universal rule for how many repeated failures constitute a broken routine, but it supports the broader value of making performance visible rather than relying on impressions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... For diagnosing failure windows, a compact record can capture four things: what was planned, whether it happened, what conditions were present, and where the routine broke down . After several comparable opportunities, the question becomes whether failures disproportionately coincide with one condition. That approach also protects against a common reasoning error: treating any repetition as proof of a universal personal limitation. Even time of day is more complicated than familiar claims about “running out of willpower”. A large naturalistic study following two student samples of 8,867 and 8,754 people found evidence of fatigue during prolonged work on a specific task, but negligible evidence that ability generally deteriorated across the day. A recurring evening failure may therefore be real without proving that people simply possess less self control at night. The relevant mechanism could instead be sleep related fatigue, competing obligations, hunger, environmental cues or the particular task itself. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A pre registered naturalistic observation of within domain mental fatigue and domain general depletion of self contro... The Science of Good and Bad Habits with Dr. Wendy Wood The Brainy Business podcast ep 428 Channel: The Brainy Business Open on YouTube Which recurring conditions are informative enough to act on? The strongest signal is a condition that is specific, repeated and plausibly connected to the behaviour . “I have been inconsistent lately” is too broad to diagnose. “I have skipped the planned study session on four of the last five evenings when I arrived home after 19:30” identifies a situation that can actually be redesigned. Several kinds of recurring condition are particularly informative: Time and sequence: the behaviour repeatedly fails at a particular time or after another activity, such as studying after dinner or exercising after a long commute. Place: success is reliable in one location but deteriorates elsewhere. Competing events: late meetings, childcare, social plans or other predictable commitments repeatedly consume the same window. Missing cues: the behaviour works when attached to a stable event but disappears when the cue varies. Task size: starting is reasonably reliable, but completion repeatedly fails when the planned session requires more time or effort than the available window permits. Disruption and recovery: an interruption is unavoidable, but the routine repeatedly fails to restart afterwards. Context deserves particular attention because habits are not simply behaviours that have been repeated many times. They involve learned associations between contexts and responses. Research examining students who moved to a new university found that habitual exercising, newspaper reading and television watching were disrupted when relevant features of their usual performance contexts changed. A later UK study of 18,053 people similarly found evidence consistent with the “habit discontinuity” hypothesis: changing context can weaken established behavioural patterns and create an opportunity for more intentional control. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Changing circumstances, disrupting habits PubMed... The reverse is also important. In a 2022 study of intentional habit building, researchers examined 95 students completing 2,482 study habit repetitions and a separate dataset containing 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions. Greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better repetition goal attainment in both datasets. That does not mean every useful routine must happen at exactly the same time and place. It does mean that repeatedly failing whenever the usual context disappears is diagnostically meaningful: the routine may depend more heavily on that context than its designer realised. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMed... Recent work reinforces the point that context should be treated as part of the behavioural system rather than as scenery around it. Experimental research has found that practice strengthens cue response associations, while reviews of habitual physical activity identify changed circumstances and competing responsibilities as routes through which established patterns can be disrupted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 Explanatory illustration 2 The pattern should identify the weak link Once a failure window is visible, the next question is not simply “How do I try harder there?” It is what exactly becomes unreliable inside that window? Consider three people who all fail to complete an evening study routine. The surface outcome is identical, but the diagnostic patterns could be completely different. One never begins because “study sometime after dinner” provides an unreliable cue. Another begins consistently but plans a 90 minute session when only 30 minutes are normally available. A third studies successfully unless work finishes late, at which point the transition from work to study disappears. Treating all three as a motivation problem would hide the evidence needed for redesign. This is where implementation intention research is useful. Implementation"},{"title":"Re Test","url":"/re-test/","description":"A routine change is only convincing when it survives repeated encounters with the same condition that defeated the old plan.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Adapt the Plan","level":3,"text":"Re Test Test Your New Routine Where the Old One Failed Adapt the Plan Re Test Re Test Test Your New Routine Where the Old One Failed A routine change is only convincing when it survives repeated encounters with the same condition that defeated the old plan. A routine change is only convincing when it survives repeated encounters with the same condition that defeated the old plan. Self Improvement That Works governance bounded topic dataset evidence Why the original failure condition is the right test environment How many repeated encounters can reveal a useful pattern What to change next when the redesign still fails self improvement that works adapt the plan re test Introduction A redesigned routine has not really passed its test because it worked on an easy Tuesday. The convincing test is whether it works when the condition that defeated the old routine returns: the late meeting, the tired evening, the disrupted commute, the distracting phone, the crowded house or another recurring obstacle. Re testing in that same failure condition turns self improvement from hopeful redesign into a small, practical experiment. Explanatory illustration 1 The important word is repeated . One successful encounter may be luck; one failure may be an unusually difficult day. What matters is the pattern across several comparable encounters. Research on habits, implementation intentions, progress monitoring and relapse prevention all points towards the importance of context: behaviour depends partly on the situations and cues in which it is attempted, and plans become more informative when evaluated against those situations rather than against an undifferentiated success rate. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Why test where the old routine failed? Suppose an evening exercise routine repeatedly disappeared whenever work ran late. You redesign it so that on late work days the target becomes a 20 minute home session rather than a trip to the gym. Completing that session on a quiet weekend proves very little about the redesign. The relevant question is what happens on the next several late work days. This is essentially a matched test : keep the troublesome situation recognisable while changing the response to it. The reasoning has strong parallels with relapse prevention research, where identifying a person's particular high risk situations and developing effective responses to those situations is central to the intervention. In that literature, the outcome of a high risk encounter depends substantially on whether an effective coping response can actually be enacted when the risk is present. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Relapse Prevention: An Overview of Marlatt’s Cognitive Behavioral Model PMC... The analogy should not be pushed too far. Evidence from treatment for addictive behaviours cannot establish that an ordinary study, exercise or productivity routine works in exactly the same way. But it provides a useful design principle: a response intended to solve a situational failure should eventually be evaluated in that situation . Habit research gives another reason to care about this distinction. In a six week study of 95 university students, involving 2,482 habit repetitions, participants assigned to build study habits in more stable contexts developed greater automaticity than those instructed to vary times and locations. A second dataset covering 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions showed the same general association between context stability, automaticity and goal attainment. The authors caution against broad generalisation, but the studies demonstrate something important for routine testing: context is not merely background scenery. Changing it can change how readily behaviour is performed. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 That means an overall completion figure can conceal the information that matters most. Imagine a redesigned study routine succeeds on 16 of 20 days. An 80% success rate sounds encouraging. But if all four failures occur on the four days when the person arrives home after 8 pm — precisely the condition the redesign was supposed to handle — the redesign has not solved its target problem. A better record distinguishes ordinary opportunities from target condition encounters : EncounterOld failure condition present?Redesigned response attempted?ResultUseful observationMondayNoYesCompletedRoutine worked normallyWednesdayYesYesCompletedShort version fitted available timeFridayYesYesFailedStarted too late after arriving homeTuesdayYesYesCompletedImmediate start workedThursdayYesNoFailedCue was missed The purpose is not to manufacture a scientific trial out of everyday life. It is to stop easy situations from masking the precise weakness the redesign was meant to repair. Drug Relapse: Why 60% Go Back (It's Not Willpower) Channel: MedHeads Open on YouTube How many repeated encounters reveal a useful pattern? There is no well supported universal rule saying that an ordinary personal routine must survive exactly three, five or ten difficult encounters before it can be declared robust. Behaviour change research does not provide such a general threshold, and presenting one would create false precision. NICE guidance instead recommends reviewing behavioural goals in light of experience, monitoring outcomes and tailoring subsequent plans to progress. It also explicitly combines action planning with coping plans intended to manage relapse. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 For personal self improvement, the practical answer is therefore to collect enough comparable encounters to distinguish a recurring result from an isolated event , without waiting indefinitely. This is better treated as an evidence quality question than as a magic number question. Three distinctions help. Count opportunities, not merely days. If the routine fails specifically when work runs late, seven calendar days containing only one late finish provide one meaningful stress test, not seven. Conversely, a month containing six late finishes provides six observations of the condition that matters. Record the severity of the condition. “Tired” can mean mildly fatigued after an ordinary day or exhausted after four hours' sleep. “Busy” can cover a normal workload and an exceptional crisis. Without this distinction, apparently repeated tests may not really be comparable. Look for direction, not perfection. A redesign can be useful without eliminating every lapse. If the old routine almost always collapsed under a particular condition and the new version now succeeds in most comparable encounters, that is meaningful evidence of improvement. If outcomes remain erratic, more observations or another adjustment may be warranted. Progress monitoring itself has a substantial evidence base. A meta analysis of 138 studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions designed to increase monitoring increased goal attainment, with stronger effects where progress was physically recorded or reported. That does not tell an individual exactly how many retests to perform, but it supports making the results visible rather than relying on retrospective impressions such as “I think the new routine is going better”. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMed... The resulting dataset can remain very small. For each encounter, record the condition , the planned response , whether the response started , whether it was completed sufficiently to count , and one brief explanation if it failed. Recording ten additional variables can create enough burden to become another reason the routine collapses. Explanatory illustration 2 Test the response, not your character Repeated stress testing is most useful when the unit being judged is the routine design . “The shortened workout failed on three of four late work nights” is actionable information. “I am bad at consistency ” is not. Relapse prevention research makes this distinction unusually explicit. Its cognitive behavioural model contrasts interpreting a lapse as evidence of a stable personal deficiency with identifying an ineffective coping response to a particular high risk situation. The latter interpretation creates a route towards changing the response when that situation appears again. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Relapse Prevention: An Overview of Marlatt’s Cognitive Behavioral Model PMC... For ordinary routines, this translates into a simple governance rule: define failure operationally"},{"title":"A Routine Can Fail At Several Different Points, And The...","url":"/a-routine-can-fail-at-several/","description":"Different routine failures call for different fixes because not starting, stopping early, missing the cue, and failing to restart are not the same problem.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Adapt the Plan","level":3,"text":"A Routine Can Fail At Several Different Points, And The... What Kind of Routine Failure Keeps Happening? Adapt the Plan A Routine Can Fail At Several Different Points, And The... A Routine Can Fail At Several Different Points, And The... What Kind of Routine Failure Keeps Happening? Different routine failures call for different fixes because not starting, stopping early, missing the cue, and failing to restart are not the same problem. Different routine failures call for different fixes because not starting, stopping early, missing the cue, and failing to restart are not the same problem. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How starting and completion failures differ How cue and obstacle failures expose different design problems How recovery failures turn one miss into a longer breakdown self improvement that works adapt the plan failure types Introduction A routine can fail at several different points, and the point of failure matters. Starting failure means the intended behaviour never gets under way. Completion failure means it begins but repeatedly breaks down before the intended dose or endpoint. Cue failure means the situation that was supposed to prompt action is absent, vague or overlooked. Obstacle failure occurs when a recurring interruption or constraint defeats an otherwise workable plan. Recovery failure happens afterwards: one miss is allowed to become several because there is no dependable route back. Explanatory illustration 1 These are not interchangeable versions of “poor discipline”. They describe different mechanisms, so they call for different adjustments. Habit research distinguishes the automatic triggering of an action from the automatic execution of its subsequent steps, while research on prospective memory , planning and interruption shows that remembering to act, carrying an action through and resuming it after disruption place different demands on a system. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMedMarch 18, 2016... Published: March 18, 2016 The practical implication for adaptive self improvement is simple: locate the break before redesigning the routine . Otherwise, an apparently sensible change can solve the wrong problem. Making and Breaking Habits Dr. Pippa Lally 24 Channel: The Mental Wellbeing College Open on YouTube Starting and completion failures are different problems Suppose someone intends to exercise for 30 minutes after work. On some weeks, the exercise never starts; on others, it starts reliably but ends after five or ten minutes. Counting both outcomes simply as “failed workouts” throws away useful diagnostic information. Research on habits provides a particularly helpful distinction. Benjamin Gardner and colleagues separate habitual instigation — automatically selecting and initiating an action — from habitual execution , in which the sequence of actions involved in performing it becomes automatic. In their prospective study of 229 students across flossing, snacking and breakfast consumption, measures of habitual instigation predicted behaviour frequency more consistently than measures of habitual execution. The authors therefore argued that maintaining behaviour may depend especially strongly on making its initiation habitual. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency PubMedMarch 18, 2016... Published: March 18, 2016 That distinction changes what should be adjusted. A starting failure points towards the transition into the routine. The person may have a sincere goal yet repeatedly fail to convert it into action at the relevant moment. The useful questions are therefore: What exactly is supposed to trigger the start? Does that trigger actually occur? Is the first action obvious? Is another behaviour winning the same moment? Implementation intentions address precisely this intention to action gap by specifying in advance that when situation Y occurs, behaviour X will begin. A meta analysis of 94 independent tests reported a medium to large overall effect on goal attainment, while a separate meta analysis of prospective memory research found that implementation intentions improved remembering to perform intended future actions. [uni konstanz.de] socmot.uni konstanz.de Social Psychology and MotivationImplementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta analysis of effects and processes Social Psychol... A completion failure , by contrast, says that initiation is already occurring. Repeatedly changing alarms or adding more reminders may therefore add little. The diagnostic question moves downstream: What happens after the start? For example, someone who consistently opens a textbook at 19:00 but abandons a planned 90 minute study session after 20 minutes does not primarily have a remembering problem. The intended session may be too long for the available energy or time; distractions may enter once work has begun; the task may contain an unclear next step; or a predictable interruption may terminate it. More generally, research on goal pursuit treats persistence as a distinct self regulatory issue rather than simply the reverse of starting. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Persistence and Disengagement in Personal Goal Pursuit Persistence and Disengagement in Personal Goal Pursuit PubMedJanuary 4, 2022... Published: January 4, 2022 The adjustment should therefore match the observed break: Never starts: strengthen or clarify the trigger, make the first action more definite, or reduce friction at initiation. Starts but consistently stops at roughly the same point: inspect workload, duration, sequencing and competing demands after initiation. Starts and completes only when circumstances are unusually favourable: the nominal routine may exceed what ordinary conditions can support. Gets interrupted and never returns: treat this as a resumption problem rather than automatically reducing the entire routine. The important unit of evidence is not merely whether the routine received a tick. It is where behaviour diverged from the plan . Why is it so hard to break a bad habit? Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Cue failures expose the trigger; obstacles expose the environment Cue failures can look like low motivation because their visible result is the same: nothing happens. Mechanistically, however, a cue failure occurs before the person has had a meaningful opportunity to carry out the intended response. Habit research describes habits as learned cue–response associations: recurring features of a context can activate behaviour with progressively less reliance on conscious deliberation. Experiments by David Neal and colleagues found that strong habits were triggered by performance contexts associated with past behaviour, rather than simply by participants' current goals. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com How do habits guide behavior? Perceived and actual triggers of habits in daily life ScienceDirect... This makes “when I have time” a weak routine specification. It requires the person to notice an ill defined state, decide that sufficient time exists, remember the intention and choose it over competing possibilities. “After I put my breakfast plate in the dishwasher, I take my medication” or “when I close my work laptop, I put on my running shoes” provides a more concrete event around which an association can form. Evidence on context stability supports this principle. Research on everyday habit building found greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals when behaviours were performed in more stable contexts. The authors emphasised that context can involve both place and position within the flow of the day, not merely a clock time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 In Lally and colleagues' well known real world study, participants repeated a chosen eating, drinking or activity behaviour in a consistent context; automaticity generally increased with repetition, although the speed of development varied considerably between individuals. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. A useful diagnostic test is therefore to ask what happened immediately before the intended behaviour . If the answer varies every day, the routine may not possess a stable cue at all. An obstacle failure is different because the cue may work perfectly. The person remembers, intends to act and perhaps even starts — but the same foreseeable condition repeatedly prevents successful performance. A late meeting eliminates the exercise window; childcare interrupts study; required equipment is elsewhere; or the phone repeatedly diverts attention. In that case, strengthening the cue treats the wrong"},{"title":"Fallback Mode","url":"/fallback-mode/","description":"A reduced version of a routine can preserve continuity when time or energy collapses without replacing the normal standard.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Adapt the Plan","level":3,"text":"Fallback Mode Build a Backup Routine for Days That Go Wrong Adapt the Plan Fallback Mode Fallback Mode Build a Backup Routine for Days That Go Wrong A reduced version of a routine can preserve continuity when time or energy collapses without replacing the normal standard. A reduced version of a routine can preserve continuity when time or energy collapses without replacing the normal standard. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context How to define a reduced version that still counts When fallback routines protect continuity How to stop the fallback from becoming the permanent default self improvement that works adapt the plan fallback mode Introduction A fallback routine is a preplanned, deliberately reduced version of a normal routine for days when time, energy or circumstances make the full version unrealistic. Its purpose is not to redefine success downwards. It is to preserve a workable link between the usual cue and the intended behaviour until normal conditions return. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because robust routines need to survive ordinary disruption. Behaviour change research supports planning for obstacles and relapse rather than assuming motivation and circumstances will remain constant. At the same time, there is little direct experimental evidence for the popular claim that doing an extremely tiny version of a habit is uniquely powerful because it “keeps the streak alive”. The stronger evidence concerns coping plans , implementation intentions, manageable changes and repetition in stable contexts. A sensible fallback system therefore uses a reduced routine as a practical application of those findings — while retaining a clear route back to the normal standard. [nice.org.uk] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 Define a reduced version that still counts The most useful fallback is not simply “do less”. It specifies in advance what happens when a recognisable obstacle makes the normal routine impractical. In behaviour change research, this resembles coping planning : anticipating barriers and deciding how to respond to them. NICE guidance similarly recommends explicit coping strategies for situations likely to undermine behaviour change and well rehearsed action plans that can be used when relapse threatens. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 A fallback can therefore be designed as a simple conditional rule: If the normal routine is blocked by a predictable bad day condition, I will do this smaller version instead. For example, someone whose normal routine is a 40 minute home workout might decide that an exceptionally late finish triggers a ten minute routine requiring no equipment. Someone normally studying for 45 minutes might use one focused 15 minute review when an unavoidable disruption consumes the evening. A writer expecting 1,000 words could switch to outlining the next section and writing one paragraph during a genuinely compressed day. The important design test is whether the reduced action preserves something meaningful about the original behaviour. “Open the textbook” may be too trivial if it involves no studying; “review ten flashcards” actually performs the target behaviour. Conversely, demanding 80 per cent of the normal routine defeats the purpose if the fallback remains too difficult under precisely the conditions for which it was created. Research does not establish a universal percentage or duration that makes a fallback effective. That threshold has to depend on the behaviour, the person and the disruption. NICE explicitly recommends tailoring behaviour change interventions to people’s capabilities and physical, economic and social environments rather than treating one prescription as universally appropriate. [Nice] nice.org.uk Recommendations Behaviour change: individual approaches Guidance NICEJanuary 2, 2014... Published: January 2, 2014 This produces a useful three part definition: Normal standard: what you ordinarily intend to do. Fallback trigger: a concrete condition that genuinely interferes with that standard, such as an unusually late work finish, travel or a severely compressed schedule. Reduced action: the smallest practical version that still contains meaningful performance of the intended behaviour. The trigger is particularly important. Without one, “fallback” can quietly become a choice made whenever the normal routine feels inconvenient. Act Like the Person Who Doesn’t Quit Channel: Grand Rising Open on YouTube When fallback routines protect continuity The strongest case for fallback mode arises when disruption is predictable enough to plan for but temporary enough that abandoning the routine altogether would be unnecessary. This is why coping planning is relevant. A systematic review of randomised trials found that prompting people to develop coping plans could support health related behaviour change, particularly when participants received support in forming those plans, although effects varied between studies. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineDoes planning how to cope with anticipated barriers facilitate health related behaviour change? A systematic revie... A broader meta analysis of planning and physical activity interventions found a small to medium overall benefit across 35 trials involving 5,439 participants, while also reporting substantial variation between studies. The lesson is not that a fallback guarantees adherence, but that specifying how behaviour will happen — including around obstacles — is more defensible than relying on an intention such as “I will try to exercise even when I am busy”. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effectiveness of Planning Interventions for Improving Physical Activity in the General Population: A Systematic R... Implementation intention research strengthens that case. A 2024 meta analysis covering 642 independent tests found benefits across behavioural, cognitive and affective outcomes, with stronger effects when plans used a contingent if–then structure, when motivation for the underlying goal was high and when plans were rehearsed. That makes “If my evening is unexpectedly compressed, then I will do my ten minute version” closer to the evidence than the vague instruction “do something, however small”. [DOI] doi.org The when and how of planning: Meta analysis of the scope and components of implementation intentions in 642 tests: European Review of... Fallbacks can be especially useful in three situations. First, there are temporary resource shortages : less time, access or usable attention than normal. Second, there are recurring high risk situations already visible in experience — for example, the routine repeatedly disappears on late shift days. Third, there are transitional disruptions , such as travel, when the ordinary setting or equipment is temporarily unavailable. The underlying principle is adaptation rather than perfection. Recent work on self regulatory flexibility describes successful regulation partly in terms of having a repertoire of strategies and matching them to the situation rather than expecting one strategy to work everywhere. A 2026 meta analytic review of goal adjustment likewise characterises goal striving flexibility as openness to modifying how one pursues a goal to fit capabilities, resources and situational demands, although the authors rate the accumulated evidence on goal adjustment as only low to moderate overall because of heterogeneity and heavy reliance on cross sectional research. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Self regulatory flexibility Self regulatory flexibility PubMedSeptember 2, 2024... Published: September 2, 2024 Continuity does not require a perfect streak One common justification for fallback routines deserves qualification: the idea that a single missed day will “break” a habit. Evidence does not support such a brittle model. In a well known real world habit formation study, 96 participants chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it in a consistent daily context for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repeated performance, but the time required varied greatly between people. Importantly, missing one opportunity to perform the behaviour did not materially disrupt the habit formation process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That finding changes the purpose of fallback mode. The goal need not be to protect an immaculate numerical streak at any cost. If someone has a fever, a genuine emergency or another circumstance in which the behaviour is inappropriate, skipping it can be entirely reasonable."},{"title":"Broken Streaks","url":"/broken-streaks/","description":"A broken streak can reduce motivation even when nearly all of the underlying behavioral progress remains intact.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Consistency","level":3,"text":"Broken Streaks Why a Broken Streak Can Make You Quit Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits Broken Streaks Broken Streaks Why a Broken Streak Can Make You Quit A broken streak can reduce motivation even when nearly all of the underlying behavioral progress remains intact. A broken streak can reduce motivation even when nearly all of the underlying behavioral progress remains intact. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism How intact and broken streak displays changed later behavior Why the streak can become a goal of its own How repairable streaks may reduce post lapse discouragement self improvement that works consistency broken streaks Introduction A broken streak can make a small lapse feel much larger than it really is. The missed workout, lesson or reading session costs one repetition; a streak counter can additionally turn it into the apparent loss of 20, 50 or 200 days of accumulated success. That distinction matters because the record can influence what someone does next even when their underlying behaviour has barely changed. Explanatory illustration 1 Experimental research provides unusually direct evidence for this effect. Across seven studies of exercise , language learning and games, people were more likely to continue an activity when an intact streak was highlighted than when a broken streak was highlighted. Crucially, some experiments held participants' actual previous behaviour constant. What changed was the way that behaviour appeared in the log. [DOI] doi.org On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 The danger, then, is not simply missing a day. It is allowing a display of discontinuity to turn a recoverable lapse into a reason not to return. How intact and broken streak displays changed later behaviour The clearest demonstration comes from research by Jackie Silverman and Alixandra Barasch published in the Journal of Consumer Research . Their studies investigated what happens when apps make consecutive behaviour conspicuous through logs and streak counters. Across the research programme, intact logged streaks encouraged subsequent engagement relative to broken ones, including when the participants' underlying behaviour did not differ. [DOI] doi.org On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 One exercise experiment makes the problem concrete. Participants completed four consecutive strength exercises. For some, however, the fourth exercise appeared not to have been logged, producing a visually broken streak. Participants then chose whether to perform another strength exercise or switch to stretching. Among those shown an intact streak, 66.23% chose another strength exercise, compared with 57.86% of those shown the broken streak. The odds of continuing the target behaviour were 1.43 times higher with the intact display. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 The striking part is what had not changed: the participants had actually performed the same four strength exercises. The researchers could therefore separate the psychological effect of the log from explanations such as having exercised less, developed less expertise or become more bored. Simply representing the same recent behaviour as intact rather than broken altered the next real behavioural choice. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 Language learning experiments produced the same general pattern. Highlighting an intact streak made participants more likely to continue the streak related activity, whereas drawing attention to a broken streak reduced persistence. Across the full programme, the effect appeared in a fitness field study and controlled experiments involving exercise, language learning and games. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academic... This does not establish that every broken streak causes someone to abandon a habit, or that streaks are generally harmful. Indeed, the same research shows why streaks are attractive: an intact one can encourage another repetition. The more precise criticism is that the motivational system is asymmetric. The display that helps while the chain remains intact can become a source of discouragement once the chain breaks. That distinction is especially important in habit building because the psychological reset shown by the counter need not correspond to a behavioural reset. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues' real world habit formation study, participants repeated a chosen eating, drinking or activity behaviour in a consistent context for 12 weeks. Missing one opportunity did not materially disrupt the development of automaticity . Very inconsistent repetition was a different matter, but a single miss was not equivalent to starting again from scratch. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A streak counter therefore measures something narrower than accumulated habit progress. It measures uninterrupted consecutiveness. The Real Reason You Can't Stay Consistent (Even When You Try) Channel: What’s The Next Level Open on YouTube Why the streak can become a goal of its own Silverman and Barasch's explanation is more specific than the familiar idea that people merely dislike seeing a zero. Their studies suggest that maintaining the logged streak becomes a separate goal in its own right . Participants were not only pursuing exercise, learning or game performance; once the sequence was made salient, they also began pursuing preservation of the sequence. Measures of the goal of maintaining the streak helped explain the effect of intact versus broken displays on subsequent behaviour. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academic... This creates two overlapping goals: Underlying goal: exercise regularly, learn a language, practise an instrument, read more often. Streak goal: keep the displayed sequence unbroken. For as long as they point in the same direction, the distinction barely matters. Wanting to exercise and wanting to preserve a 30 day exercise streak both encourage today's workout. Once a lapse occurs, however, the goals separate. Today's workout still advances fitness, but it can no longer save yesterday's perfect sequence. A source of motivation has therefore disappeared at exactly the moment when returning matters most. The experiments support this interpretation through participants' sense of accomplishment. Intact streaks increased feelings associated with progress towards the streak goal, while broken streaks reduced them. In the researchers' mediation analyses, sense of accomplishment helped explain later behavioural engagement. The effect was not simply a matter of participants experiencing more general negative emotion after a break. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 Responsibility for the break also mattered. In one experiment, the discouraging difference between intact and broken streaks became larger when participants attributed the break to themselves rather than to an external cause. This fits the goal failure account: an interruption interpreted as my failure carries different motivational information from an identical looking interruption blamed on a technical problem or other outside event. [DOI] doi.org On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 The value attached to the record could become surprisingly tangible. In one experiment, 45% of participants said they were willing to watch an advertisement to maintain their logged streak, while 43% were willing to do so to repair a broken one. The counter was therefore not functioning merely as neutral information about past behaviour; people were willing to incur an immediate inconvenience to preserve or restore it. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicOn or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford AcademicJune 30, 2022... Published: June 30, 2022 This is the central risk of streak based self improvement."},{"title":"Recovery Metrics","url":"/recovery-metrics/","description":"Completion rate, total repetitions, and speed of return can reveal progress that a flawless-streak counter hides.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Consistency","level":3,"text":"Recovery Metrics Track How Fast You Return, Not Just Streaks Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits Recovery Metrics Recovery Metrics Track How Fast You Return, Not Just Streaks Completion rate, total repetitions, and speed of return can reveal progress that a flawless streak counter hides. Completion rate, total repetitions, and speed of return can reveal progress that a flawless streak counter hides. Self Improvement That Works governance bounded topic policy intervention Why streak length can misrepresent accumulated progress Completion rates and rolling windows as alternative measures Using repeated slow returns to identify flaws in the habit system self improvement that works consistency recovery metrics Introduction A habit tracker should answer a more useful question than “How long is my unbroken streak?”: when life interrupts the behaviour, how reliably and how quickly do I return? A flawless streak records consecutiveness, but it can hide the difference between substantial participation with occasional short lapses and a pattern in which one missed day repeatedly becomes a week away. Explanatory illustration 1 Research supports treating the tracker as a feedback system rather than a pass–fail scoreboard. Habit formation depends on repeated behaviour in recurring contexts, and one missed opportunity did not materially disrupt automaticity in a foundational real world study. Meanwhile, experiments on behavioural logs show that merely displaying a streak as broken can reduce subsequent engagement. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... For long term self improvement, three measurements therefore belong together: completion rate, accumulated repetitions and recovery speed . Streak length can remain useful, but it should not have sole authority over whether the system appears to be working. Why streak length can misrepresent progress Consider two people trying to exercise five times a week for eight weeks. One completes every planned session for three weeks, misses a session and then largely stops. The other completes four of five sessions most weeks and usually returns immediately after a miss. The first person can boast the longer peak streak. The second has accumulated more useful repetitions and demonstrated a more resilient pattern. That distinction matters because habit learning is cumulative rather than an all or nothing process. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues' 12 week study, 96 participants selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour to perform daily in the same context. Automaticity generally increased along a curve rather than suddenly appearing after a particular run of consecutive days, and the estimated time to reach 95% of eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days among participants for whom the model fitted. Most importantly for tracking, missing one opportunity did not materially affect the habit formation process. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A 2024 systematic review covering 20 health habit studies and 2,601 participants reinforces the case against treating streak length as the master measure. The few studies that estimated formation time reported medians of 59–66 days and means of 106–154 days, with individual estimates ranging from 4 to 335 days. Frequency, timing, behavioural regulation and stable routines were among the factors associated with habit strength. The evidence base was limited and many studies had a high risk of bias, so these figures should not be converted into another rigid countdown. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Streaks are not useless. They can provide an immediate incentive to keep going. The problem arises when the tracker makes preserving the streak look equivalent to making behavioural progress . Silverman and Barasch demonstrated this problem across seven studies involving behaviours including exercise and language learning. People were more inclined to continue when a behavioural log highlighted an intact rather than a broken streak, even where underlying past behaviour was held constant. The representation of the record itself changed subsequent engagement. Allowing a broken streak to be perceived as repairable attenuated the effect. [DOI] doi.org On or Off Track: How (Broken) Streaks Affect Consumer Decisions Journal of Consumer Research Oxford Academic... A tracker designed for durable consistency should therefore preserve the motivational benefit of visible progress without telling users, implicitly, that a single interruption has erased it. Tracking Your Progress Without Becoming Obsessed Channel: Banq Official Open on YouTube Measure participation across a window The simplest alternative is not to abolish streaks but to place them beside a completion rate over a rolling period . Instead of asking whether every scheduled behaviour happened consecutively, ask what proportion of the planned opportunities were completed recently. For example, suppose a behaviour is planned once per day. A 28 day tracker might show: Completion rate: 24 of 28 planned repetitions, or 86%. Total repetitions: 73 since tracking began. Current streak: 4 days. Longest streak: 19 days. Typical recovery: 1 day after a missed opportunity. Longest recent interruption: 2 days. Those numbers describe substantially more than “current streak: 4”. The latter makes the record look newly restarted; the fuller dashboard shows that participation remains high and that interruptions have so far been contained. Rolling windows are particularly useful because they prevent ancient success from disguising recent deterioration. Someone who completed 170 of 200 opportunities overall might appear highly consistent even after struggling for the past month. A 28 day or four week completion rate exposes the change sooner. Conversely, one bad day does not make a generally successful month look like zero. There is good broader evidence for monitoring behavioural progress, although research has not established one universally optimal dashboard for personal habits. A meta analysis of 138 studies involving 19,951 participants found that interventions intended to increase monitoring of goal progress increased monitoring and improved goal attainment. Reviews of health behaviour interventions likewise identify self monitoring as a potentially useful behaviour change component, while also finding substantial variation across behaviours, populations and intervention designs. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. That qualification matters. “Track recovery speed” is a sensible measurement policy derived from evidence about habit formation, self monitoring and lapses; it is not a scientifically validated universal formula with a proven ideal recovery threshold. The purpose is diagnostic: to retain information that a binary streak counter discards. Explanatory illustration 2 Make speed of return a first class metric Completion rate describes how much behaviour happened. Recovery speed describes what happened after it did not happen . A practical definition is the number of scheduled opportunities between a lapse and the next successful repetition. If Monday's planned run is missed and Wednesday is the next scheduled run and is completed, the person has returned at the next opportunity. For a daily habit, a miss on Monday followed by completion on Tuesday is a one day recovery. Over time, the tracker can calculate a median or typical return time rather than overreacting to one unusual interruption. This produces a revealing distinction: High completion + fast recovery usually indicates a robust system. Interruptions occur, but they remain interruptions. High completion + slow recovery may indicate that good looking averages depend on long periods of success separated by damaging breaks. Lower completion + fast recovery can indicate that the behaviour is still difficult or badly specified, but individual misses are not cascading into abandonment. Lower completion + slow recovery is a stronger signal that the habit system itself needs attention. This approach shifts the policy of the tracker from punishing discontinuity to governing interruption . The objective after a miss becomes reducing the distance to the next successful repetition, not reconstructing yesterday's perfect record. That distinction also fits what is known about goal pursuit after setbacks. A meta analysis of 94 tests found that implementation intentions — advance “if–then” plans specifying when, where or how to act — had a medium to large positive effect on goal attainment. Experimental work has also found that such plans can promote repeated attempts after an"},{"title":"Next Cue","url":"/next-cue/","description":"The most useful response to a lapse is often to resume at the next planned cue instead of inventing a dramatic restart.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Consistency","level":3,"text":"Next Cue The Best Time to Restart Is the Next Cue Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits Next Cue Next Cue The Best Time to Restart Is the Next Cue The most useful response to a lapse is often to resume at the next planned cue instead of inventing a dramatic restart. The most useful response to a lapse is often to resume at the next planned cue instead of inventing a dramatic restart. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention What counts as the next normal opportunity Why compensatory punishment can disrupt the original routine How a smaller version can preserve continuity on difficult days self improvement that works consistency next cue Introduction The best default response to a missed habit is usually simple: wait for the next normal cue and do the habit then . If the plan is “walk after lunch”, missing Tuesday’s walk does not require a punishing evening workout, a new programme or a ceremonial restart next Monday. Wednesday’s lunch is already the recovery point. Explanatory illustration 1 That rule fits what habit research actually suggests. Habits develop as behaviours are repeatedly performed in response to recurring contextual cues. In a landmark real world study, one missed opportunity did not materially disrupt the development of automaticity ; gains resumed when participants returned to the behaviour. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical objective after a lapse is therefore not to erase the miss. It is to prevent one missing repetition from displacing the established cue–behaviour relationship. A useful recovery policy can be stated in advance: “If I miss one planned opportunity, I resume at the next normal opportunity.” What counts as the next normal opportunity? The “next cue” is the next occurrence of whatever normally tells you it is time to act. That distinction matters because psychological habits are not simply behaviours performed frequently; they are actions increasingly triggered by recurring contexts. A cue might be a time, place, preceding activity or another reliable feature of a routine. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMC... Suppose the habit is: read for 20 minutes after getting into bed; take a short walk after lunch; practise vocabulary on the train home; prepare tomorrow’s priorities after shutting down work; do a mobility routine after making morning coffee. If Monday’s opportunity disappears because of travel, illness, an emergency or simple forgetfulness, the next normal opportunity is generally the next bedtime, lunch, train journey, work shutdown or morning coffee . It is not necessarily the earliest moment at which the missed behaviour could physically be squeezed into the day. This is more than convenient wording. A random make up session may achieve the behaviour once, but the normal cue supplies another repetition of the relationship that the person is actually trying to learn. Research comparing routine based cues with time based cues found that both approaches could support increases in automaticity when people repeatedly enacted their plans. In that randomised trial of 192 adults, successful habit formers took a median of 59 days to reach peak automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 Context stability also appears important. Research on habit building has found that stable contexts support automaticity and goal attainment, consistent with the broader view that habits emerge through learned links between situations and responses. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal AttainmentJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 Returning at the next cue therefore restores something more specific than “discipline”: it restores another learning opportunity under the conditions in which the behaviour is meant eventually to become easier to initiate. The rule does need sensible interpretation. A cue that will not recur for a long time may justify a planned alternative. Missing a Saturday gym session need not mean doing nothing for seven days if the broader routine already includes an appropriate backup slot. But that backup is most useful when it is predefined , rather than improvised in frustration after every lapse. Atomic Habits: How to Get 1% Better Every Day James Clear Channel: APB Speakers Open on YouTube Why a missed habit does not need repayment After a miss, people can be tempted to compensate: double tomorrow’s session, impose an extra restriction, stay up late to complete the task or redesign the whole routine. That response can feel rigorous because it makes the lapse carry an immediate cost. For habit formation, however, it can solve the wrong problem. The original problem was one omitted repetition. Compensation can turn it into a second, harder behaviour. A 20 minute run becomes a 40 minute debt; ten pages of reading become twenty; one missed study session creates an obligation to sacrifice tomorrow evening. The recovery requirement is now more demanding than the routine that was supposed to become sustainable. There is no good general evidence that such punishment is required to preserve habit formation. Lally and colleagues' 12 week study followed 96 people attempting everyday eating, drinking or activity behaviours in consistent contexts. Among participants whose automaticity trajectories could be modelled, the time required to approach their eventual automaticity varied greatly, but missing one opportunity did not materially affect the formation process . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The better supported intervention is planning what to do when the opportunity or obstacle appears. Implementation intentions are “if–then” plans linking a recognisable situation with a chosen response. The US National Cancer Institute's research summary describes them as plans that connect critical cues or good opportunities to goal directed behaviour, helping people recognise and act on those situations. [Cancer Control] cancercontrol.cancer.gov Open source on cancer.gov. In physical activity research, planning interventions have produced a small to medium overall improvement in behaviour across randomised trials, although results vary considerably between studies and settings. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That suggests a cleaner recovery instruction than punishment: If today's cue passes and I do not perform the habit, then I will perform it when the next scheduled cue occurs. The distinction is important. The policy specifies re entry , not repayment. Explanatory illustration 2 Stop one lapse becoming several The most useful feature of a next cue rule may be that it settles a decision before disappointment occurs. After missing a habit, there is no need to decide whether the programme is still “working”, whether the streak has been ruined or when a fresh start should begin. The recovery action was chosen in advance. This resembles coping planning , in which people anticipate obstacles and decide how they will respond. A systematic review and meta analysis of implementation intention approaches to physical activity found support for planning interventions, particularly where plans addressed perceived obstacles and were reinforced over time. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A broader 2022 meta analysis covering 35 trials and 5,439 participants likewise found better physical activity outcomes in planning conditions than controls, while also reporting substantial heterogeneity between studies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. More recent meta analytic modelling has also highlighted coping planning — anticipating barriers and responses to them — as particularly relevant to translating exercise intentions into behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The useful shift is from asking “Will I ever miss?” to “What happens when I miss?” A practical policy can cover the common cases without becoming elaborate: 1. Normal day: perform the habit at its usual cue. 2. Cue arrives but the habit is impossible: use a predefined smaller version if one is appropriate. 3. Opportunity is missed entirely: do not manufacture a punishment session; return at the next normal cue. 4. The same cue repeatedly fails: treat that as information about the routine and redesign the cue rather than"},{"title":"One Miss","url":"/one-miss-a78025/","description":"Missing one planned repetition usually matters far less than abandoning the routine after the miss.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Consistency","level":3,"text":"One Miss Does One Miss Really Break a Habit? Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits One Miss One Miss Does One Miss Really Break a Habit? Missing one planned repetition usually matters far less than abandoning the routine after the miss. Missing one planned repetition usually matters far less than abandoning the routine after the miss. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What the Lally habit study found about isolated omissions Why one lost repetition is not a reset When occasional misses become frequent enough to matter self improvement that works consistency one miss Introduction Missing one planned day does not normally reset habit formation. The best known direct evidence comes from Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ real world habit study: participants who missed a single opportunity showed, at most, a small temporary interruption in the growth of automaticity , with no detectable longer term cost from that isolated omission. The researchers concluded that one missed opportunity did not materially affect habit formation. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That finding is narrower — and more useful — than the slogan that “missing days does not matter”. Habit formation depends on repeated behaviour in a recurring context, so omissions cannot become frequent without eventually reducing the repetitions that build the habit. The evidence supports a distinction between one miss and a pattern of missing . One lost repetition is usually a small interruption; persistent inconsistency can prevent the behaviour from becoming reliably automatic. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College LondonAugust 4, 2009... Published: August 4, 2009 6 How can I form a Lasting Habit? Dr Philippa Lally Channel: Healthy Habits Open on YouTube What the Lally study found about one missed day Lally and colleagues followed 96 volunteers for 12 weeks. Each participant chose an eating, drinking or activity behaviour — such as drinking water, eating fruit or exercising — and aimed to perform it every day in the same context, for example after breakfast. Participants recorded both whether they had performed the behaviour and their experience of its automaticity. Of the 96 recruits, 82 supplied sufficient data for analysis. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The central finding was that automaticity generally developed as an asymptotic curve rather than a straight line. In simple terms, early repetitions tended to produce comparatively large gains in automaticity; subsequent repetitions added progressively smaller gains as the behaviour approached its eventual plateau. For the 62 participants whose data could be fitted to the researchers' model, the estimated time required to reach 95% of that plateau ranged from 18 to 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers then examined occasions when somebody missed one planned performance. The comparison is unusually relevant to the everyday question of whether a single lapse “breaks” an emerging habit. Across two day periods, automaticity increased by an average of about 0.79 points when the behaviour was performed on both days, compared with about 0.55 points when there was a missed opportunity between performances. The difference was not significant enough to support the idea that one omission derailed formation. [SciSpace] scispace.com how are habits formed modelling habit formation in the real 4iq5e623zp how are habits formed modelling habit formation in the real 4iq5e623zp The original analysis also found that the timing of the omission within the habit building period did not significantly alter its effect. In other words, the data did not show that a single miss was especially destructive simply because it occurred early rather than late in the process. The study's overall conclusion was therefore deliberately modest: repetition is necessary, but some missed opportunities can occur without derailing habit formation . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... University College London summarised the practical result similarly: one missed opportunity did not significantly affect formation, whereas people who were very inconsistent in performing their chosen behaviour did not successfully establish habits. The researchers also cautioned that their study could not identify a precise minimum level of consistency required for success. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College LondonAugust 4, 2009... Published: August 4, 2009 Hack Your Habits for Lasting Change: Insights from Wendy Wood and Adam Grant Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Why one lost repetition is not a reset The result makes sense once habit formation is understood as an accumulation process rather than a streak counter. Repeatedly performing an action in a stable context strengthens the association between that context and the behavioural response. The important input is repeated experience of the cue–behaviour pairing; nothing in this model requires every opportunity to occur consecutively. More recent methodological work on habit formation likewise stresses that consistent repetition, rather than the mere passage of time , is the key determinant of development. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. Imagine someone trying to make drinking a glass of water after breakfast automatic. If they perform the behaviour on Monday through Thursday, forget on Friday and resume on Saturday, Friday supplies no new repetition. But the omission does not somehow delete the four preceding cue–behaviour pairings. Lally's data are important precisely because they tested whether an isolated omission produced evidence of such a lasting setback and found that it did not. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This also explains why translating habit formation into a rigid number of consecutive days can be misleading. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis covering 20 health habit studies and 2,601 participants found considerable variation in formation time. Among the relatively small number of studies that estimated it, reported medians were roughly 59–66 days and means 106–154 days, while individual estimates ranged from 4 to 335 days. Frequency of practice and contextual stability were among the factors associated with habit strength. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That review also provides an important caution against making too much of any single study. Eleven of its 20 included studies were judged to have a high risk of bias, and only four reported mean or median times to habit formation. The evidence is therefore much stronger for the general proposition that formation varies greatly between people and behaviours than for any precise rule about how many misses are harmless. [MDPI] mdpi.com Open source on mdpi.com. The most defensible interpretation is consequently not “miss whenever you like”. It is that habit progress is not binary . A missed repetition means losing one opportunity to strengthen automaticity, not returning automatically to day one. Explanatory illustration 2 When misses become frequent enough to matter The boundary becomes clearer when a single missed opportunity is compared with longer lapses. Lally and colleagues explicitly contrasted their result with Christopher Armitage's earlier 12 week study of 94 new gym members. Armitage monitored actual gym attendance and found that earlier attendance influenced subsequent participation; his analysis suggested that behaviour during the first five weeks was particularly important to later maintenance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The apparent tension between the studies largely disappears once the unit of measurement is considered. Lally examined one missed daily opportunity . In the gym study, lapses were measured in weekly blocks , so failing to attend"},{"title":"Stable Context","url":"/stable-context-d39ad9/","description":"Repeating a behavior in a similar setting can strengthen the link between the situation and the action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Consistency","level":3,"text":"Stable Context Why Doing It in the Same Context Helps Consistency Matters More Than a Perfect Streak Better Habits Stable Context Stable Context Why Doing It in the Same Context Helps Repeating a behavior in a similar setting can strengthen the link between the situation and the action. Repeating a behavior in a similar setting can strengthen the link between the situation and the action. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism What context stability means in everyday habit building Evidence linking stable contexts with stronger automaticity How timing, location, and preceding events can anchor repetition self improvement that works consistency stable context Introduction Stable contexts make habits easier to repeat because they allow the same surroundings, moments or preceding events to become reliable signals for the same action. When a behaviour repeatedly follows a recognisable cue — brushing teeth after breakfast, reading after getting into bed, or walking after lunch — the brain can learn an association between that situation and the response. With enough repetition, encountering the cue can begin to prompt the behaviour with less deliberate remembering and decision making. This cue–response learning is central to psychological accounts of habit. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 Explanatory illustration 1 That helps explain why consistency matters more than creating ideal conditions every time. The useful consistency is not simply an uninterrupted calendar streak. It is repeated pairing of a behaviour with a sufficiently recognisable context. Evidence from longitudinal habit building research suggests that greater context stability is associated with stronger automaticity and better repetition, although the evidence base remains smaller and less definitive than popular habit advice sometimes implies. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 What a stable context actually means In habit research, “context” is broader than physical location. It can include where you are, roughly when something happens, what you have just done, which objects are present and aspects of the social environment. A context is therefore better understood as the collection of cues surrounding an action rather than merely the room in which it occurs. Habit researchers have long argued that responses become associated with contextual features that repeatedly accompany their performance. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... Imagine someone trying to establish ten minutes of reading each day. “Read whenever I find time” allows the behaviour to occur in many different circumstances. Monday's reading might happen on the train, Tuesday's at a desk and Wednesday's on the sofa at an unrelated time. The behaviour is repeated, but no single situation consistently predicts it. “Read for ten minutes after getting into bed”, by contrast, repeatedly pairs reading with a recognisable event and place. Getting into bed can gradually become associated with picking up the book. The important difference is not that bedtime is intrinsically superior. It is that the cue is repeatedly available and connected to the same response. This is why context stability should not be confused with rigid scheduling. A context can be stable even when the clock time varies. “After I finish breakfast” may occur at 7.15 on one day and 8.00 on another while retaining the same relationship between an existing routine and the new behaviour. Researchers commonly distinguish such event based cues from purely time based cues, although evidence is not yet strong enough to declare one cue type universally superior. A recent discussion of the literature notes that some physical activity findings favour consistency in preceding events rather than time of day , while a dietary habit study found both event and time based approaches capable of supporting habit development. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL Discovery Psychology & Health UCL Discovery Psychology & Health Introduction to Good Habits, Bad Habits with Wendy Wood Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Why repetition in the same setting builds automaticity The key mechanism is cue–response association . Early in habit formation, carrying out an intended behaviour usually requires deliberate control: remembering the intention, recognising an opportunity and deciding to act. Repeating the action in connection with the same cues gives those cues predictive value. Psychological theories of habit propose that, through repeated experience, encountering the context can increasingly activate the associated response directly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems Pub Med Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems This is different from merely becoming skilled at an activity. A person can become highly proficient at doing press ups without automatically starting them each morning. Habit formation concerns, in part, the transition from “I need to remember to do this” towards the situation itself helping to bring the action to mind. Strong habits consequently reduce how much behavioural control has to come from an active decision on every occasion. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMedSeptember 10, 2015... Published: September 10, 2015 The classic real world study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates the process. Ninety six participants selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and were asked to perform it daily in the same context, such as after breakfast, for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition along a curved trajectory: early repetitions could produce substantial gains, while later gains became progressively smaller as automaticity approached a plateau. Among participants whose data fitted the model, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. The study cannot by itself tell us how the same behaviours would have developed in constantly changing contexts, because stable contextual repetition was built into its design. But it established an important real world pattern: automaticity can develop gradually when a behaviour is repeatedly performed following a consistent cue. More direct evidence arrived from Marco Stojanovic and colleagues in 2022. Their first study followed 95 university students over six weeks and covered 2,482 study habit repetitions. Participants were assigned either to keep the place and time of their study habit stable or to vary those circumstances. A second study analysed 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions recorded by 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals. The relationship between context stability and goal attainment was also partly mediated by automaticity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 That last result is particularly useful for understanding the mechanism. A stable context did not merely provide a convenient diary slot. The findings were consistent with the idea that repeated contextual pairing helped behaviour become more automatic, which in turn contributed to continued performance. The authors also argued that context may support not only habit instigation — getting started — but smoother execution once the behaviour has begun. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. Explanatory illustration 2 Timing, location and preceding events can all become anchors There is no requirement that every habit be tied to the same kind of cue. The practical question is which feature of everyday life can reliably precede the behaviour. Location can work because returning to the same physical environment repeatedly restores many cues at once. Sitting at a particular desk may cue study; entering a kitchen after work may cue preparing a particular snack. Research on habitual behaviour has repeatedly identified location as one of the contextual features capable of triggering well learned responses. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Time or part of the day can also provide regularity, particularly when daily schedules are predictable. Yet “at 7.00 pm” requires noticing that it is 7.00 pm. This is"},{"title":"Habit Timing","url":"/habit-timing-bbb315/","description":"Habit automaticity usually rises quickly at first and then slows toward a plateau, with formation times varying widely across people and behaviours.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Automaticity","level":3,"text":"Habit Timing Why Habits Do Not Form on a Fixed Day Automaticity Habit Timing Habit Timing Why Habits Do Not Form on a Fixed Day Habit automaticity usually rises quickly at first and then slows toward a plateau, with formation times varying widely across people and behaviours. Habit automaticity usually rises quickly at first and then slows toward a plateau, with formation times varying widely across people and behaviours. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence The shape of the automaticity learning curve Why estimates range from weeks to many months Better signs of progress than counting days self improvement that works automaticity habit timing Introduction There is no scientifically supported day on which a repeated behaviour suddenly becomes a habit. The best known real world dataset found something more useful: automaticity tends to rise rapidly at first, then improve by progressively smaller amounts as it approaches a plateau . In Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ study of daily eating, drinking and activity behaviours, the modelled time to reach 95% of each person’s eventual automaticity level ranged from 18 to 254 days , with a central estimate of about 66 days . [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That 66 day figure is therefore not a deadline or universal “habit formation time”. It describes the middle of a remarkably wide distribution in one influential dataset. Later research has reinforced the larger lesson: different behaviours and different people follow very different timelines, and some continue gaining automaticity for several months. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 For self improvement, the practical question is consequently not “Which day will my habit be finished?” but “Is the same cue increasingly bringing the behaviour to mind and making it easier to start?” How Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit? Science Explains Channel: Lifestyle Science Open on YouTube The automaticity curve rises fast, then flattens The landmark Lally study followed 96 volunteers who chose a new eating, drinking or physical activity behaviour and attempted it once each day in response to the same contextual cue, such as after breakfast. Participants recorded whether they performed the behaviour and rated how automatic it felt over 12 weeks. Researchers then fitted mathematical learning curves to individual participants rather than assuming everybody progressed at the same rate. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The resulting pattern was asymptotic . Imagine a curve that climbs steeply before gradually bending towards horizontal. Early repetitions can produce relatively large increases in automaticity. As learning accumulates, each additional repetition tends to add less. Eventually, further practice may still reinforce the behaviour, but measured automaticity changes comparatively little. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... That shape matters because it changes what “forming a habit” means. There is no obvious psychological switch between “not habitual” and “habitual”. Automaticity is strengthening along a continuum. To make the learning curve measurable, Lally and colleagues operationalised the plateau as the point at which a participant reached 95% of their modelled maximum automaticity . It was this threshold — rather than some universal state in which a habit becomes complete — that produced the famous figure of roughly 66 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Lally herself has subsequently stressed this distinction. In a 2026 University of Surrey interview, she cautioned against turning the original result into the claim that everybody forms a habit in 66 days: it was an average from that particular study, not a rule for individuals. [University of Surrey] surrey.ac.uk University of SurreyDoes it really take 66 days to form a habit? We asked the expert, Dr Pippa Lally University of SurreyJanuary 21, 2026... Published: January 21, 2026 The curve also explains an experience that can otherwise be discouraging. Progress may feel obvious during the first few weeks and much less dramatic later. That slowing does not necessarily mean habit formation has stalled. It is exactly what an asymptotic learning process predicts: large early gains followed by smaller gains as automaticity approaches its ceiling . The Truth About Habit Streaks (And Why You Were Lied To) Channel: Peak Performance AI Open on YouTube Why estimates run from weeks to many months The striking result in the original dataset is arguably not 66 days but the 18 to 254 day range . Even among people deliberately repeating relatively straightforward behaviours every day, the estimated time to reach 95% of maximum automaticity differed by more than seven months. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Subsequent studies have made a single fixed number even harder to defend. In a randomised trial of 192 adults building everyday nutrition habits, participants linked their chosen behaviour either to a routine or to a particular time. Among those who successfully formed habits, the median modelled time to peak automaticity was 59 days . Yet the reported individual range extended from 4 to 335 days , and only 23% of participants reached the study’s specified habit formation threshold. Repeatedly carrying out the plan was a key predictor of increasing automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 A different experiment illustrates how the behaviour and circumstances can stretch the curve. Forty eight students attempted a daily stretching exercise for 90 days, either after waking or before bed. Extrapolated learning curves placed automaticity at about 106 days for the morning group and 154 days for the evening group . The researchers investigated time of day physiology as a possible explanation, although a small experiment of this kind should not be treated as establishing a general rule that morning habits always form faster. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit PubMedNovember 1, 2017... Published: November 1, 2017 The broader evidence remains limited. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis covered 20 habit formation intervention studies involving 2,601 participants, but only four studies directly reported a time to habit formation . Their median estimates included 59 and 66 days, while mean estimates in other studies reached 106–154 days; individual estimates across the reviewed research ranged from 4 to 335 days. The authors also judged most included studies to have a high risk of bias. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants PubMed... So even “two months” should be understood as orientation, not prediction. The available datasets indicate why several variables can move the plateau: Behavioural demands matter. In the original work, simpler behaviours such as drinking water tended to reach peak automaticity faster than more elaborate actions such as doing 50 sit ups. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 Repetition matters more than calendar time alone. A day only provides an opportunity for learning; the cue–response association is strengthened when the intended response is actually performed. In the nutrition trial, repeated plan enactment predicted automaticity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial PubMedJanuary 6, 2021... Published: January 6, 2021 Stable contexts can strengthen learning. Research comparing habit repetitions across stable and variable contexts found greater"},{"title":"Under Load","url":"/under-load/","description":"Practised cue-response associations can help behaviour hold up when attention is stretched and deliberate control becomes harder.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Automaticity","level":3,"text":"Under Load Why Practised Habits Survive When Attention Is Busy Automaticity Under Load Under Load Why Practised Habits Survive When Attention Is Busy Practised cue response associations can help behaviour hold up when attention is stretched and deliberate control becomes harder. Practised cue response associations can help behaviour hold up when attention is stretched and deliberate control becomes harder. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism What cognitive load reveals about automatic responding How practice strengthened cue response associations in experiments Where automaticity can reduce reliance on deliberate control self improvement that works automaticity under load Introduction Practised habits can be especially useful when attention is occupied. If a familiar cue has repeatedly been followed by the same response, encountering that cue can begin to activate the response directly, reducing the need to hold a goal in working memory, reconsider what to do and consciously retrieve each step. That is why a well rehearsed routine may keep moving when someone is distracted, hurried or simultaneously dealing with another task. Reviews of habit research describe this rapid cue driven activation as a defining feature of habit memory. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems PubMedMarch 1, 2022... Published: March 1, 2022 Explanatory illustration 1 A particularly revealing experiment tested this idea by having people practise a 16 step virtual sushi making task and then repeat it while simultaneously attending to a distracting secondary task. More practice strengthened objectively measured cue–response associations, and stronger associations predicted more successful performance under this cognitive load. The important point for self improvement is not that habits make distraction harmless. It is narrower and more useful: practice can shift some of the work of initiating a familiar response away from moment to moment deliberation and towards cues that are already present. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Measuring context–response associations that drive habits Wiley Online LibraryMeasuring context–response associations that drive habits Labrecque 2024 Journal of the Experimental Analysis o... The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube What cognitive load reveals about automatic responding Mental distraction provides a useful test of automaticity because deliberate control has limited capacity. Imagine trying to perform a familiar routine while also keeping track of a conversation, remembering an instruction or monitoring something else. The secondary task competes for attention and working memory that could otherwise be used to recall the goal, work out the next action and check each choice. A genuinely well practised response has an advantage in that situation. Habit theories propose that repeated performance builds associations in memory between features of the context and particular responses. Once those associations are sufficiently accessible, seeing the relevant cue can rapidly bring the associated response to mind. Wood, Mazar and Neal characterise habit memory in part by this rapid activation: habitual responses can be activated irrespective of whether a person is currently deliberating about the goal that originally motivated them. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems Habits and Goals in Human Behavior: Separate but Interacting Systems PubMedMarch 1, 2022... Published: March 1, 2022 This creates an important contrast: Deliberation heavy action: cue → remember the goal → retrieve or choose the response → act. Practised cue response action: familiar cue → practised response becomes accessible → act. Real behaviour rarely falls perfectly into either category. Complex activities can combine deliberate decisions with highly practised components. Nor does automaticity necessarily mean complete unconsciousness. The useful distinction is how much online cognitive work is required to produce the next appropriate response. Practice can make some of those computations less demanding even while conscious supervision remains possible. A review by Adrian Haith and John Krakauer therefore cautions against treating skill, habit and reduced cognitive load as exactly the same phenomenon: practice can affect all three, but they can be partly dissociable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The multiple effects of practice: skill, habit and reduced cognitive load PMCMarch 1, 2018... Published: March 1, 2018 This distinction also explains why distraction can expose the difference between knowing what to do and having a response that is readily triggered. A person may understand a routine perfectly when concentrating on it yet fail to retrieve the right action when attention is divided. A stronger cue–response association provides another route to performance, one less dependent on reconstructing the plan at that moment. The Science of Good and Bad Habits with Dr. Wendy Wood The Brainy Business podcast ep 428 Channel: The Brainy Business Open on YouTube How practice strengthened cue response associations in experiments Labrecque, Lee and Wood's 2024 experiments offer unusually direct evidence because they did not simply ask participants whether a behaviour felt habitual. Participants learnt a computerised sushi making sequence containing 16 steps. Some received little practice; others repeated the sequence roughly ten times. The researchers then separately measured how quickly participants could identify the correct response after seeing a cue from the sequence. Faster correct responses served as an objective indicator of stronger cue–response associations. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Measuring context–response associations that drive habits Wiley Online LibraryMeasuring context–response associations that drive habits Labrecque 2024 Journal of the Experimental Analysis o... The final test deliberately made thoughtful retrieval harder. Participants had to reproduce the sushi sequence without the earlier instructions while a second screen played attention grabbing university football highlights. They were required to listen for the announcer's calls of a touchdown and remember the total, creating a genuine dual task demand rather than merely telling participants to hurry. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Measuring context–response associations that drive habits Wiley Online LibraryMeasuring context–response associations that drive habits Labrecque 2024 Journal of the Experimental Analysis o... More practice strengthened the measured cue–response associations. Crucially, those stronger associations predicted better reproduction of the practised sequence under cognitive load even after the analyses accounted for the amount of practice and participants' intentions to perform correctly. The researchers also examined other possible motivational explanations, including perceived ease, enjoyment , attitudes and fluency. Their central conclusion was that repetition aided performance by creating associations through which recurring contextual cues could activate practised responses, rather than simply making participants more determined to succeed. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Measuring context–response associations that drive habits Wiley Online LibraryMeasuring context–response associations that drive habits Labrecque 2024 Journal of the Experimental Analysis o... The study also contains a useful caution against oversimplification. In the second experiment, stronger cue–response associations were associated with better performance on the distracting secondary task as well, consistent with the idea that automaticity freed some capacity; that relationship did not appear in the first experiment. The evidence therefore supports reduced dependence on deliberation more strongly than a sweeping claim that habits always release a predictable quantity of spare attention. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Measuring context–response associations that drive habits Wiley Online LibraryMeasuring context–response associations that drive habits Labrecque 2024 Journal of the Experimental Analysis o... The experimental task was also far simpler and shorter lived than most important self improvement behaviours. Participants were university students practising a computerised sequence in controlled conditions. Ten repetitions of a sushi game cannot establish how much rehearsal is required to make exercise , studying or household routines robust to distraction. What the experiments contribute is a cleaner demonstration"},{"title":"Missed Days","url":"/missed-days-4dc92c/","description":"An occasional missed repetition does not erase habit learning, but repeated missed cue-response pairings reduce opportunities for automaticity to strengthen.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Automaticity","level":3,"text":"Missed Days Does Missing One Day Reset a Habit? Automaticity Missed Days Missed Days Does Missing One Day Reset a Habit? An occasional missed repetition does not erase habit learning, but repeated missed cue response pairings reduce opportunities for automaticity to strengthen. An occasional missed repetition does not erase habit learning, but repeated missed cue response pairings reduce opportunities for automaticity to strengthen. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single dataset dataset evidence What a single missed opportunity does to habit growth Why repeated omissions matter more than isolated lapses Using missed repetitions to diagnose a weak setup self improvement that works automaticity missed days Introduction Missing one day does not appear to reset a developing habit. The best known direct evidence comes from Phillippa Lally and colleagues’ 12 week study of 96 people building everyday eating, drinking or activity habits. Automaticity — the extent to which the behaviour felt as though it happened with little deliberate prompting — generally accumulated gradually, and missing a single opportunity did not materially alter that trajectory. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Explanatory illustration 1 That finding is reassuring, but it is easy to overextend. The study did not establish that missed repetitions never matter, that any number of gaps is harmless, or that a habit survives indefinitely without practice. Cue–response learning depends on repeated opportunities in which a familiar situation is followed by the intended action. An isolated omission is therefore better understood as one lost learning opportunity than as a reset. A recurring pattern of omissions is more consequential because it reduces the very repetitions through which automaticity develops. What a single missed opportunity does to habit growth The most useful evidence comes from Lally and colleagues because participants were studied while attempting to form real habits in everyday life rather than simply reporting habits they already possessed. Each participant chose a health related behaviour, such as drinking water, eating fruit or exercising, and specified a daily contextual cue such as “after breakfast”. They then reported their behaviour and automaticity over 84 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Automaticity did not behave like a streak counter. For participants whose data could be modelled successfully, it tended to follow an asymptotic learning curve: comparatively large gains occurred earlier, followed by progressively smaller gains as automaticity approached a plateau. The estimated time to reach 95% of that plateau varied enormously, from 18 to 254 days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The researchers also examined missed opportunities. Their conclusion was unusually clear: missing one opportunity to perform the behaviour did not materially affect habit formation . UCL’s account of the research similarly reports that one missed opportunity had no significant effect, whereas people who were highly inconsistent generally did not succeed in forming habits. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College London... That distinction matters. Suppose the intended pattern is: Breakfast → take vitamin and on Tuesday breakfast occurs but the vitamin is forgotten. The accumulated learning from Monday and all the preceding repetitions does not disappear on Tuesday. On Wednesday, breakfast can once again be followed by the vitamin, adding another cue–response pairing. A review translating the research into health practice notes that automaticity gains resumed after an occasional missed performance. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 A calendar streak and a learned habit are therefore different quantities. A streak can fall from 50 to zero overnight because that is how the counter is defined. There is no evidence from this dataset that habit automaticity behaves in the same all or nothing way. How Long Does It Take to Build a Habit? HINT: It's NOT 21 Days Channel: Develop Good Habits Open on YouTube Why repeated omissions matter more than isolated lapses The comforting “one missed day is fine” result should not be converted into “ consistency does not matter”. In fact, the broader result points in the opposite direction. Lally and colleagues found that greater consistency of performance was associated with a better fitting habit formation trajectory, while UCL’s summary explicitly distinguishes an occasional missed opportunity from being “very inconsistent”. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The reason follows from the mechanism being learned. A habit is not simply a record that an action has been performed many times. Habit research commonly defines it in terms of a response becoming automatically activated by recurring situational cues after repeated cue specific behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A review and analysis of the use of ‘habit’ in understanding, predicting and influencing health related behaviour P... If the cue repeatedly occurs without the desired response, there are simply fewer successful cue–response pairings from which that association can strengthen. Later longitudinal evidence reinforces the importance of the repetition context. In a 2022 study, researchers examined two datasets: 95 students completing 2,482 study habit repetitions and 218 app users contributing data on 308 habits and 2,368 repetitions. More stable contexts were associated with greater automaticity and better attainment of repetition goals in both datasets; in the student study, context stability was experimentally manipulated by asking one group to keep place and time stable and another to vary them. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 This does not provide a numerical rule such as “two misses are safe but three are harmful”. The evidence is not precise enough to support such a threshold. Even the researchers behind the original missed opportunity finding said they did not yet know what degree of consistency was necessary for habit formation. [University College London] ucl.ac.uk University College London How long does it take to form a habit? UCL News University College LondonHow long does it take to form a habit? UCL News UCL – University College London... That limitation is important because popular rules can sound more scientific than they are. Advice such as “never miss twice” may be a useful personal rule for preventing one lapse from turning into prolonged non performance, but the Lally dataset does not establish a special biological or psychological boundary at the second missed day. What the evidence supports is a more modest distinction: isolated misses appear tolerable; persistent inconsistency reduces the opportunities through which the desired response becomes automatic. Explanatory illustration 2 A missed day is not necessarily a missed cue There is another complication hidden by calendar based tracking. A “missed day” and a “missed cue–response opportunity” are not always the same event. Imagine someone intends to walk for ten minutes after lunch at work. On Saturday they do not go to work and their normal post lunch situation never occurs. That is different, from a cue learning perspective, from sitting in the usual workplace after lunch and repeatedly deciding not to walk. The original Lally study was explicitly organised around behaviour performed in response to a once daily cue, rather than around accumulating an abstract number of consecutive calendar days. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... This is one reason the evidence should not be translated too literally into streak rules. Habits are context sensitive. Research characterises automatic habits as cue contingent: relevant situations acquire the"},{"title":"Stable Contexts","url":"/stable-contexts-0d556e/","description":"Repeating the same action in a stable context gives recurring cues more opportunity to trigger the response with less deliberation.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Automaticity","level":3,"text":"Stable Contexts Why Stable Contexts Make Habits Easier to Trigger Automaticity Stable Contexts Stable Contexts Why Stable Contexts Make Habits Easier to Trigger Repeating the same action in a stable context gives recurring cues more opportunity to trigger the response with less deliberation. Repeating the same action in a stable context gives recurring cues more opportunity to trigger the response with less deliberation. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism What context stability means in everyday life What studies find when contexts stay stable or vary How to choose a context you can repeat reliably self improvement that works automaticity stable contexts Introduction Stable contexts make habits easier to trigger because they allow the same situation to predict the same response repeatedly. Doing an action 30 times in 30 different circumstances is therefore not necessarily equivalent to doing it 30 times after the same recurring event, in the same place or within the same part of a routine. In the second case, features of the situation can become reliable cues for the behaviour itself. Explanatory illustration 1 This is the important distinction between repetition and context dependent repetition . Habit learning is not simply practice accumulating with time; it involves learning a relationship between circumstances and a response. Research ranging from classic prospective studies to newer longitudinal work generally finds stronger automaticity when behaviour occurs in stable contexts, although the size and consistency of the effect varies between behaviours and studies. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... For self improvement, the practical implication is straightforward: when trying to make a useful behaviour more automatic, choose a recurring situation you can realistically reproduce rather than merely aiming to perform the behaviour whenever an opportunity appears. What context stability means in everyday life A context is stable when the circumstances surrounding repeated performances are sufficiently similar for recurring features to become predictive. Researchers have treated context as potentially including location, time, preceding actions, other people, mood and other aspects of the situation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Reflecting on non reflective action: An exploratory think aloud study of self report habit measures PMCJuly 22, 2013... Published: July 22, 2013 That means stability does not necessarily require reproducing every detail. Consider three ways of practising ten minutes of vocabulary study: Highly variable: study whenever there is spare time, sometimes on the train, sometimes in bed and sometimes during lunch. Clock based: study at 19:00 each evening. Routine based: study immediately after clearing away the evening meal. All three can produce repetition. The latter two additionally provide recurring contextual information that can become associated with starting the behaviour. The third may remain relatively stable even when dinner moves from 18:30 to 19:30, because the relevant relationship is “meal finished → vocabulary practice”, rather than a particular position on the clock. Research does not establish one universally superior kind of cue. A 2024 systematic review of health habit studies noted evidence for stronger habits among people whose target behaviour occurred in consistent contexts, while also reporting research finding no significant difference between time based and routine based cues in habit formation. [MDPI] mdpi.com Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its DeterminantsDecember 9, 2024... Published: December 9, 2024 The useful principle is therefore reliable recurrence , not a rule that every habit must happen at an identical clock time. How to Break Unwanted Habits and How to Create New Desired Ones with Adam Grant and Wendy Wood Channel: The Next Big Idea Podcast Open on YouTube Stability is about predictability, not perfect sameness A recurring event can provide stability even while superficial details change. “After breakfast” might still be recognisable when breakfast contains different food or happens somewhat later at weekends. Likewise, “when I arrive at my desk” can recur despite changes in weather, clothing or the emails waiting on the screen. This distinction matters because an attempt to reproduce an entire environment precisely can make a habit unnecessarily brittle. The aim is to preserve one or more dependable features that repeatedly accompany the behaviour. There is also evidence that the relevant feature can differ between people. A 2026 study following older adults found that context stability was more consistently related to changes in habit strength than to behaviour itself, but the useful contextual cue was not identical across participants. Among people adopting physical activity , habit gains were associated with whichever individual cue was most stable for that person. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Examining context stability, habits, and movement behaviors among behavioral adopters and maintainers: it's an individual vibe Pu... So “stable context” should not be interpreted as “everyone needs the same kind of cue”. It means that your recurring cue reliably predicts your recurring response . Why stable contexts strengthen automatic behaviour The mechanism can be understood as a learning problem. Suppose someone wants to read for 15 minutes every day. If reading follows breakfast each morning, the brain repeatedly encounters a similar relationship: breakfast finishes → reading begins Initially, breakfast finishing may do little by itself. The person still has to remember the plan and deliberately start reading. But repeated pairing gives features of that situation opportunities to become associated with the response. Habit theory proposes that sufficiently established contextual cues can then activate the learned response more readily, reducing dependence on renewed deliberation. Reviews describe habits accordingly as responses directly cued by contexts that have repeatedly covaried with previous performance. [My SCP] myscp.onlinelibrary.wiley.com My SCPThe habitual consumer Wood 2009 Journal of Consumer Psychology Wiley Online LibraryOctober 2, 2009... Published: October 2, 2009 Variable practice gives the environment a less consistent relationship to learn. If reading happens after breakfast today, on a train tomorrow, at lunch the following day and just before sleep after that, “reading” is being repeated but no single situation is repeatedly predicting its initiation. This explains why frequency alone is an incomplete description of habit. Danner, Aarts and de Vries found that the relationship between past behaviour, intention and subsequent behaviour depended on contextual stability: high frequency behaviour performed in stable contexts showed the pattern expected of stronger habits, whereas frequency alone was insufficient. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. The same principle also explains an everyday characteristic of established habits: changing surroundings can make a familiar action surprisingly easy to forget. Research on context disruption has found that changes such as moving home can weaken the influence of established habitual patterns, leaving behaviour more responsive to current intentions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Context is therefore not merely scenery surrounding a habit. It can be part of the information that retrieves it. Explanatory illustration 2 What happens when contexts stay stable or vary One of the clearest direct tests comes from Marco Stojanovic, Axel Grund and Stefan Fries. Their 2022 research examined context stability in two longitudinal datasets rather than simply assuming that repeated behaviour was habitual. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... In the first study, 95 university students formed study habits over six weeks, generating 2,482 recorded habit repetitions. Participants assigned to the stable context condition were instructed to keep the place and time of performance stable, while those in the variable context condition were instructed to change rooms and times. The manipulation produced a large difference in reported context stability. Across the data, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity, although the detailed results were more nuanced than a simple stable group versus variable group victory: the association between day level stability and automaticity was evident particularly within the generally stable condition, while the overall group factor itself"},{"title":"Cue Types","url":"/cue-types/","description":"Both clock times and existing routines can support habit formation when the chosen cue is encountered consistently and followed by the action.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Automaticity","level":3,"text":"Cue Types Should a Habit Follow a Time or Routine? Automaticity Cue Types Cue Types Should a Habit Follow a Time or Routine? Both clock times and existing routines can support habit formation when the chosen cue is encountered consistently and followed by the action. Both clock times and existing routines can support habit formation when the chosen cue is encountered consistently and followed by the action. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case policy intervention How time based and routine based cues differ What the 84 day randomised trial found Choosing the cue that fits your real schedule self improvement that works automaticity cue types Introduction A habit can be attached to a clock time — “at 8 am, drink a glass of water” — or to an existing routine — “after breakfast, drink a glass of water”. The strongest direct comparison suggests that neither type of cue is inherently better. In an 84 day randomised controlled trial, both time based and routine based plans supported increasing automaticity , with no significant difference between the groups. What mattered more was whether people actually performed the behaviour when their chosen cue occurred. Explanatory illustration 1 That changes the practical question. Rather than asking whether clocks or routines are universally superior, ask which cue will reliably appear in your life and give you a realistic opportunity to act. A precise time can work well in a regular timetable. A routine cue can accommodate a day that shifts by an hour or two. Either can support cue–response learning if it repeatedly precedes the intended behaviour. How time based and routine based cues differ A time based cue specifies when an action should happen by the clock: take a short walk at 1 pm, prepare tomorrow's lunch at 8 pm, or eat a piece of fruit at 10.30 am. A routine based cue attaches the new behaviour to an event already recurring in daily life: take the walk after lunch, prepare tomorrow's lunch after washing the dinner dishes, or eat the fruit after making morning coffee. Both approaches create a cue–behaviour plan. Their practical difference lies in what has to signal that it is time to act. A routine supplies an event that can itself become the trigger. Finishing breakfast is observable: breakfast ends, and the planned behaviour follows. A clock time plan depends on encountering or checking temporal information at approximately the appropriate moment. Research on prospective memory — remembering to carry out a future intention — distinguishes similarly between time based tasks and event based tasks. Time based prospective remembering can require monitoring the passage of time, whereas an event in the environment can provide an external retrieval cue. That difference gives routine cues an intuitively appealing advantage. “After breakfast” remains usable whether breakfast happens at 7.15 or 8.30, whereas “at 8 am” may collide with commuting, a meeting or an unusually late start. The authors of the direct habit trial noted earlier evidence suggesting that routine cues might allow greater situational flexibility and require less monitoring than time cues. But this does not establish a general superiority of routine cues. Clock time can itself be extremely stable, particularly when a person's day follows a fixed timetable or when clocks, watches and alarms make the time highly noticeable. More importantly, direct experimental evidence did not reproduce the expected routine cue advantage. Implementation Intention Explained: How to DOUBLE YOUR SUCCESS with a Goal or Habit Channel: Develop Good Habits Open on YouTube What the 84 day randomised trial found The clearest head to head test was conducted in Germany by Jan Keller and colleagues and published in the British Journal of Health Psychology . The prospectively registered trial enrolled 192 adults and randomly assigned 96 to each of two brief planning interventions. Participants selected a healthy nutrition behaviour they did not already perform in the proposed cue–behaviour combination. One group attached the behaviour to a daily routine , such as after breakfast. The other attached it to a specific time , such as 9 am. Participants were then asked to perform the behaviour in that planned context every day for 12 weeks. Daily questionnaires measured whether they had enacted the plan and their behavioural automaticity — the extent to which the behaviour felt as though it happened automatically. The headline result was unusually simple: both approaches worked similarly at group level . Automaticity increased over time in both conditions, as did plan enactment, and the researchers found no significant between group difference favouring routine based or time based planning. The more consequential difference was between people who repeatedly followed their plan and those who did not. On days when participants successfully enacted the behaviour in response to their planned cue, predicted automaticity was higher; across participants, people who enacted their plans more consistently also tended to develop greater automaticity. The descriptive figures make the similarity between cue types particularly tangible. Among the 135 participants retained for the main analyses, average plan enactment during the first 28 days was about 69% in the routine group and 67% in the time group. During days 57–84, the figures were about 68% and 71% respectively. These are not cleanly diverging trajectories in which one cue type obviously wins. The “59 days” result needs context Among participants classified as successfully forming a habit, the median modelled time to reach 95% of their automaticity plateau was 59 days. But individual estimates varied dramatically — from 4 to 335 days — so 59 should not be treated as a countdown after which a behaviour suddenly becomes habitual. There was another important qualification: only 27 participants were classified as showing successful habit formation according to the individual curve analyses, split almost evenly between routine cues (14) and time cues (13). The trial therefore offers useful evidence against a large, straightforward advantage for either cue type, but it is not proof that the two approaches are equivalent for every person, behaviour or schedule. The analysed sample also skewed young and female. Of the original 192 participants, 86.5% were women and the mean age was about 25, although ages ranged from 18 to 77. The intervention concerned self selected healthy eating behaviours, not every possible self improvement habit. Generalising the precise results to exercise , studying, sleep routines or complex skills therefore requires caution. Explanatory illustration 2 Consistency matters more than the label on the cue The trial points towards a more useful distinction than “time versus routine”: reliably encountered cue versus unreliable cue . That interpretation fits broader habit research on context stability. In a 2022 study, researchers examined intentional habit building in two datasets. In the first, 95 university students formed study habits over six weeks; one group was instructed to keep the time and place stable while another varied them. The second dataset covered 308 habits from 218 users of a habit building app. Across both datasets, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and better attainment of the intended habit repetition goals. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis of a six month longitudinal habit study also found context stability among the factors associated with habit strength, alongside behaviour frequency and several motivational and experiential variables. This does not tell us that a clock or routine is the better cue, but it reinforces the importance of repeatedly performing a behaviour under sufficiently similar circumstances for a cue–response relationship to develop. This helps explain why the same cue format can be excellent for one person and poor for another. “At 7 am” is stable for someone who reliably gets up at 6.30, but fragile for a shift worker whose waking time changes. “After breakfast” is stable for someone who eats breakfast every morning, but useless for someone who regularly skips it. The cue category is secondary to whether the cue actually occurs and whether the behaviour can realistically follow it. Choosing the cue that fits your real schedule A good cue should pass a simple practical test: Will I encounter this cue reliably, and when I do, can I normally perform the behaviour? The 84 day trial suggests that getting this pairing right is more important than choosing time based planning merely because it sounds precise or routine based planning because it sounds more “habit like”. Use a time cue when the relevant part of your schedule is genuinely clock driven. Taking a work break at 11 am, beginning a shutdown routine at 5.30 pm or doing a two minute posture exercise when a scheduled afternoon alarm sounds can all give the behaviour a"},{"title":"Habit Timing","url":"/habit-timing-129285/","description":"A stretching study estimated faster automaticity in the morning than evening, showing that timing can influence habit development.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Habit Timelines","level":3,"text":"Habit Timing Does the Time of Day Change Habit Formation? Habit Timelines Habit Timing Habit Timing Does the Time of Day Change Habit Formation? A stretching study estimated faster automaticity in the morning than evening, showing that timing can influence habit development. A stretching study estimated faster automaticity in the morning than evening, showing that timing can influence habit development. Self Improvement That Works evidence single case population context What the morning versus evening stretching study found Why timing might affect automaticity Why one timing result should not become another rule self improvement that works habit timelines habit timing Introduction The time of day may affect how quickly a repeated behaviour becomes automatic, but the evidence does not support a simple rule that morning habits are always easier to form than evening ones. The clearest direct evidence comes from one small 2017 experiment in which students repeated the same stretching exercise either after waking or before bed. Researchers estimated that automaticity developed substantially sooner in the morning group: about 106 days versus 154 days . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit PubMedNovember 1, 2017... Published: November 1, 2017 Explanatory illustration 1 That difference is interesting because the behaviour itself was held broadly constant while its place in the daily routine changed. The researchers proposed that higher morning cortisol helped explain the result. Yet the study was small, its habit formation dates extended beyond the 90 day observation period, and the morning and evening cues were not perfectly equivalent. Later research also suggests that routine stability, individual morning or evening preference and the nature of the behaviour may matter. The useful lesson is therefore about finding a favourable, repeatable context , not automatically moving every self improvement habit to breakfast time. [psy.uni hamburg.de] psy.uni hamburg.de fournier2017 healthpsych fournier2017 healthpsych Explanatory illustration 3 What the morning versus evening stretching study found Marion Fournier and colleagues recruited 48 students and randomly assigned them to practise a psoas iliac stretch once a day for 90 days. One group stretched in bed after waking; the other did so before sleeping. Participants recorded their behaviour and completed a daily measure of automaticity using a smartphone app, while saliva samples taken every 30 days allowed the researchers to measure cortisol. Forty two participants completed the experiment: 19 in the morning group and 23 in the evening group. Adherence was high, at 94.9%, and did not significantly differ between groups. [psy.uni hamburg.de] psy.uni hamburg.de fournier2017 healthpsych fournier2017 healthpsych The researchers fitted learning curves to each participant's automaticity scores and defined habit formation as reaching 95% of the person's modelled maximum automaticity. On that measure, the morning group was estimated to reach the threshold after an average 105.95 days , compared with 154.01 days for the evening group. Morning cortisol concentrations were also higher, averaging 2.16 ng/ml compared with 1.10 ng/ml in the evening group. Statistical mediation analysis suggested that cortisol accounted for part of the relationship between time of day and faster automaticity. [psy.uni hamburg.de] psy.uni hamburg.de fournier2017 healthpsych fournier2017 healthpsych There is an important detail behind those memorable numbers: participants were followed for only 90 days . The portions of the fitted curves after day 90 were extrapolations, including both average estimates of the point at which 95% automaticity was reached. In other words, researchers did not literally observe the average morning participant becoming habitual on day 106 and the average evening participant on day 154. They observed 90 days of changing automaticity and used those data to estimate where the curves would subsequently reach the specified threshold. [psy.uni hamburg.de] psy.uni hamburg.de fournier2017 healthpsych fournier2017 healthpsych That distinction matters when this study is used in discussions about how many days habits take to form. A 2024 systematic review identified only four studies that directly reported a mean or median time to health habit formation. It included the stretching study's 106 and 154 day figures but emphasised that the evidence base for habit formation timelines remains modest and heterogeneous. Of the 20 studies in the broader review, 11 were judged to have a high risk of bias. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. How optimizing circadian rhythms can increase healthy years Satchin Panda TEDxBoston Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube Why timing might affect automaticity The stretching experiment offers one biological explanation. Cortisol follows a strong daily rhythm: in healthy people, much of its secretion occurs around morning awakening, including a rapid rise during roughly the first 30–45 minutes after waking. Cortisol is involved in systems governing metabolism, cognition and responses to daily demands. [Endocrine Society] endocrine.org Society The Cortisol Awakening Response Endocrine Society Endocrine SocietyThe Cortisol Awakening Response Endocrine SocietyOctober 1, 2024... Published: October 1, 2024 Fournier and colleagues therefore proposed that performing the stretch during this higher cortisol period could favour the learning processes involved in automatic behaviour. Their mediation results were consistent with that possibility. Separate laboratory research has also connected cortisol responses with greater reliance on habitual rather than goal directed behaviour under some experimental conditions, although those studies investigate different processes and cannot by themselves prove that ordinary morning routines become habits faster. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit Effects of circadian cortisol on the development of a health habit PubMedNovember 1, 2017... Published: November 1, 2017 The biology is not the only plausible explanation, however. Morning and evening can provide different behavioural contexts. A habit becomes easier to trigger automatically when the relevant cues and surroundings recur reliably. Research specifically manipulating context stability has found that people building habits in more stable contexts reported greater automaticity; an additional observational dataset showed a similar association. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 For some people, mornings contain unusually predictable anchors: waking, getting out of bed, showering, making breakfast or starting work. Evening schedules may accumulate more variation from work finishing late, social plans, travel, tiredness or changing bedtimes. That makes it plausible that a morning behaviour could receive a cleaner and more consistently encountered cue. Researchers examining medication habits have explicitly proposed this morning stability explanation, although they also stress that it has not been established as a general mechanism. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicTime of Day Differences in Treatment Related Habit Strength and Adherence Annals of Behavioral Medicine Oxford AcademicJu... There is supporting evidence for a morning advantage in performing planned behaviours, but it should not be confused with direct evidence of faster habit formation. In one study of 92 people making fruit and vegetable action plans, enactment declined over the course of the day. Similarly, an observational analysis of 14,879 users of a meditation app found that morning meditation was associated with greater persistence: over the first 180 days, the probability of meditation declined by 63.4% for morning sessions compared with reductions of 67.8% to 74.5% at other times. Because users chose when to meditate, however, this cannot establish that morning timing caused better persistence or faster automaticity. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Nina Knoll ScienceDirect ScienceDirect Nina Knoll ScienceDirect Taken together, these findings point towards at least two plausible routes by which time of day could matter: circadian biology may alter the conditions under which automaticity develops, while daily schedules may make some time periods more reliable sources of cues than others . The existing evidence does not yet establish how much each contributes. The Optimal Morning Routine Andrew Huberman Channel: After Skool Open on YouTube Explanatory illustration 2 Why one timing result should not become another"},{"title":"21 Day Myth","url":"/21-day-myth/","description":"The famous 21-day rule grew from Maxwell Maltz's observations about adjustment, not experiments on everyday habit formation.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Habit Timelines","level":3,"text":"21 Day Myth Where Did the 21 Day Habit Rule Come From? Habit Timelines 21 Day Myth 21 Day Myth Where Did the 21 Day Habit Rule Come From? The famous 21 day rule grew from Maxwell Maltz's observations about adjustment, not experiments on everyday habit formation. The famous 21 day rule grew from Maxwell Maltz's observations about adjustment, not experiments on everyday habit formation. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single case aspect entity What Maxwell Maltz actually wrote How a minimum became a universal rule Why modern habit research rejects a fixed deadline self improvement that works habit timelines 21 day myth Introduction The famous claim that “it takes 21 days to form a habit” did not begin as the result of a habit experiment. Its most traceable source is Psycho Cybernetics , a 1960 self help book by plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz. Maltz described patients adapting to altered appearances after surgery, phantom limb experiences after amputation, and people becoming accustomed to a new home. From these observations, he proposed that perceptible change in a person’s mental image generally required “a minimum of about 21 days”. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. Explanatory illustration 1 That is substantially different from saying that repeating any behaviour for exactly three weeks makes it automatic. Maltz was discussing adjustment and self image, not reporting a controlled study of everyday habit formation. As the idea travelled through popular self help, crucial qualifications disappeared: “a minimum of about 21 days” became simply “21 days”, and psychological adjustment became “forming a habit”. Modern habit research measures something different and finds no universal three week deadline. [Scientific American] scientificamerican.com Scientific AmericanHow Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit? Scientific AmericanJanuary 24, 2024... Published: January 24, 2024 The 21 Day Habit Myth: What Science Really Says Channel: The Research File Open on YouTube What Maxwell Maltz actually wrote Maltz's original passage is revealing because it contains both the seed of the modern claim and the qualifications that later versions largely discarded. In Psycho Cybernetics , he advised readers trying his self image exercises to reserve judgement for at least three weeks . He then offered several observations to explain why. According to Maltz, the average plastic surgery patient took about 21 days to become accustomed to a changed face; a phantom limb persisted for roughly that period after amputation; and a new house took around three weeks to begin feeling like home. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. His conclusion concerned a change in a “mental image” , not the acquisition of an automatically cued behaviour. The wording was cautious even by the standards of his own argument: he referred to a minimum , said about 21 days, and described “commonly observed phenomena”. The passage presented no randomly assigned groups, systematic comparison of different behaviours or statistical estimate showing that habits reliably emerge after three weeks. Scientific American's account of the history likewise notes that no formal experiment was conducted to verify Maltz's 21 day observation. [Scientific American] scientificamerican.com Scientific AmericanHow Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit? Scientific AmericanJanuary 24, 2024... Published: January 24, 2024 The distinction matters because Maltz's examples were not actually equivalent phenomena. Becoming familiar with one's appearance after surgery, experiencing a phantom limb, feeling at home in a new house and learning a repeated behaviour involve different processes. Maltz grouped them together because he believed they illustrated changes in self image and psychological adjustment. The later 21 day rule went further by treating the common number as evidence of a general law governing behavioural habits. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. There is also an important linguistic distinction. Researchers at University College London have suggested that part of the confusion may involve habituation versus habit formation . Habituation can mean becoming accustomed to something; behavioural habit formation concerns developing a response that becomes automatically elicited by an associated situation. Maltz's examples largely concerned the former sort of “getting used to” experience, yet the popular rule came to be understood as a claim about the latter. [UCL Blogs] blogs.ucl.ac.uk UCL BlogsJune 2012 UCL ‘Health Chatter’: Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health Blog... The 21 Day Habit Myth. Maxwell Maltz Started a 70 Year Lie. Channel: What If Lab Open on YouTube How a minimum became a universal rule The mutation can be understood as a series of small but consequential simplifications: First, the lower bound disappeared. “A minimum of about 21 days” does not predict completion on day 21. Even on Maltz's own terms, it means meaningful adjustment might require three weeks or longer . Removing “minimum” converts a proposed floor into something resembling a finish line. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. Second, “about” disappeared. Maltz's wording acknowledged approximation. The popular version supplies a precise integer, giving an anecdotal observation the appearance of a measured scientific threshold. That precision is not present in the underlying evidence. [Penguin Random House Canada] penguinrandomhouse.ca Open source on penguinrandomhouse.ca. Third, the outcome changed. Maltz wrote about perceptible changes in mental image and adapting to altered circumstances. Popular retellings transformed this into a statement about habit formation : repeat an action for 21 days and it becomes habitual. UCL researchers explicitly describe the broad generalisation from Maltz's plastic surgery observations as unclear and distinguish his examples from the automatic responses studied by habit researchers. [UCL Blogs] blogs.ucl.ac.uk UCL BlogsJune 2012 UCL ‘Health Chatter’: Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health Blog... Finally, the scope expanded. A handful of observations became advice supposedly applicable to exercise , diets, productivity routines and other behaviour changes. By 2009, The Guardian was already describing the 21 day idea as pop psychology wisdom while reporting research that contradicted it; university and scientific explainers have subsequently continued to trace the claim back to Maltz. [theguardian.com] theguardian.com The GuardianThis column will change your life: How long does it really take to change a habit? Life and style The GuardianOctober 9... There is no well documented single moment at which one identifiable writer definitively changed Maltz's wording into the modern rule. Claims that a particular self help author or decade alone caused the transformation should therefore be treated cautiously. What the sources establish more securely is the direction of the distortion : an observation about adjustment entered popular self improvement culture and circulated in a much broader, more definite form. [UCL Blogs] blogs.ucl.ac.uk UCL BlogsJune 2012 UCL ‘Health Chatter’: Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health Blog... The simplified version also has an obvious psychological advantage as advice. Twenty one days feels substantial without seeming forbidding. Behavioural economist Colin Camerer told Scientific American that three days would sound implausibly short whereas a year would seem daunting; three weeks occupies an appealing middle ground. That does not demonstrate exactly why the myth historically spread, but it helps explain why the simplified claim has proved so durable once established. [Scientific American] scientificamerican.com Scientific AmericanHow Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit? Scientific AmericanJanuary 24, 2024... Published: January 24, 2024 Explanatory illustration 2 Why modern habit research rejects a fixed deadline Modern studies ask a more precise question than Maltz did. In behavioural research, forming a habit is generally associated with a behaviour becoming increasingly automatic in response to a recurring context . The relevant question is therefore not simply “How many consecutive days did someone perform this action?” but how repetition changes automaticity over time. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A landmark study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues illustrates why the 21 day rule fails this test. Ninety six volunteers selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted to perform it each day in the same context"},{"title":"Stable Cues","url":"/stable-cues/","description":"Repeating a behavior in the same recurring context strengthens the cue-response link that makes action increasingly automatic.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Habit Timelines","level":3,"text":"Stable Cues Do Habits Form Faster With the Same Cue? Habit Timelines Stable Cues Stable Cues Do Habits Form Faster With the Same Cue? Repeating a behavior in the same recurring context strengthens the cue response link that makes action increasingly automatic. Repeating a behavior in the same recurring context strengthens the cue response link that makes action increasingly automatic. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism How cues become linked to repeated actions Why consistent contexts matter more than raw repetition counts Ways to attach a behavior to a recurring event self improvement that works habit timelines stable cues Introduction Yes. Habits are more likely to become automatic when the same behaviour is repeatedly performed in response to a stable, recurring cue. The reason is not simply that consistency makes a routine easier to remember. Repeatedly pairing one context with one response strengthens a learned cue–response association, so encountering the cue increasingly prompts the action with less deliberate decision making. Experimental and longitudinal research supports this basic mechanism, although it does not establish a universal number of repetitions or days by which a stable cue will produce a habit. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters when thinking about habit formation timelines. Twenty repetitions scattered across unrelated situations are not necessarily equivalent to twenty repetitions after the same recurring event. Habit learning depends partly on what is being repeated together : the cue as well as the behaviour. This is why a useful habit plan is often not merely “I will do this every day”, but something more concrete such as “after breakfast, I will take my vitamins” or “after I put my toothbrush down, I will floss”. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 The Science of Good and Bad Habits with Dr. Wendy Wood The Brainy Business podcast ep 428 Channel: The Brainy Business Open on YouTube How cues become linked to repeated actions In habit research, a cue can be a feature of the situation that reliably accompanies an action: a location, time of day , preceding activity, object or other recurring element of a routine. As the same response repeatedly follows that context, learning builds an association between them. Eventually, encountering the context can activate the practised response without requiring the person to reconstruct the decision from scratch. This context–response account is central to influential psychological models of habit. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits—A Repeat Performance Sage JournalsHabits—A Repeat Performance David T. Neal, Wendy Wood, Jeffrey M. Quinn, 2006... Experimental work provides evidence for the mechanism rather than merely the correlation between frequent behaviour and strong habits . In two experiments involving practice of a sequential sushi making task, greater practice produced stronger measured cue–response associations. Those stronger associations, in turn, predicted better task performance when participants later worked under cognitive load. Practice did not appear to work by strengthening their intentions or motivation; the researchers concluded that repetition created associations through which recurring cues automatically activated practised responses. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Measuring context response associations that drive habits Measuring context response associations that drive habits PubMedDecember 4, 2023... Published: December 4, 2023 A stable cue therefore does two useful jobs. Initially it provides an identifiable opportunity to act: breakfast ends, the work laptop closes, or tooth brushing finishes. With sufficient consistent pairing, that same event can become part of the mechanism that prompts the behaviour automatically. The practical shift is from remembering what you intended to do towards responding to a familiar situation . Reviews of habit formation consequently describe context dependent repetition, rather than repetition alone, as the core learning process. [King's College London] kclpure.kcl.ac.uk King's College London Promoting habit formation King's College LondonPromoting habit formation King's College LondonMay 1, 2013... Published: May 1, 2013 This also explains why changing environments can expose how context dependent an established habit has become. A behaviour that seems effortless in its normal setting may require considerably more thought elsewhere because familiar contextual triggers are absent. Conversely, an old setting can sometimes elicit a habitual response even when it is no longer intended. Habit automaticity is therefore not simply a property stored inside the behaviour; it reflects a learned relationship between an action and the circumstances that have repeatedly accompanied it. [Wiley Online Library] myscp.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library The habitual consumer Wiley Online LibraryThe habitual consumer Wood 2009 Journal of Consumer Psychology Wiley Online LibraryOctober 2, 2009... Published: October 2, 2009 Wendy Wood: How Habits Guide Behavior Channel: USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences Open on YouTube Why consistent contexts matter more than raw repetition counts Counting repetitions misses an important feature of what the brain is learning. Suppose two people each perform a short study routine 30 times. One normally starts at the same desk at roughly the same point in the day. The other studies in changing rooms and at widely varying times. Both have repeated the target behaviour 30 times, but the first has also repeatedly paired that behaviour with a relatively predictable context. Habit theory predicts that this consistency should make it easier for contextual features themselves to acquire control over the response. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med A new look at habits and the habit goal interface A new look at habits and the habit goal interface PubMed... A 2022 study by Marco Stojanovic and colleagues tested context stability particularly directly. In one six week study, 95 university students building study habits were instructed either to maintain the same place and point in the day or deliberately vary those features. A second dataset covered 308 habits from 218 users of a habit building app. Across the two datasets, greater context stability predicted greater automaticity and better attainment of the participants' habit repetition goals; automaticity partly mediated the relationship between context stability and goal attainment. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... The result adds an important qualification, however. In the experimental study, simply assigning people to the stable context rather than variable context condition did not by itself produce every predicted between group effect. The clearer association appeared among repetitions where context was actually experienced as sufficiently stable. The authors therefore argued that context stability may operate less like an on/off switch and more like a condition that supports smoother automatic performance once sufficient consistency is present. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMC... Other real world research points in the same direction while also showing why the claim should not be overstated. In an eight week study of 199 people trying to make filling half their dinner plate with vegetables habitual, participants with greater average context stability also had greater average habit strength. But day to day increases in stability did not reliably predict stronger habit the following day. The study therefore supports stable contexts as one contributor to habit formation without showing a simple rule in which every unusually consistent repetition produces a measurable automaticity gain 24 hours later. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. There are also findings that do not fit a strong version of the context stability hypothesis. An ecological momentary assessment study of 104 older adults found relationships between some forms of contextual stability and physical or sedentary activity, but its measures of context stability did not predict self reported habit automaticity. Context may be harder to define for behaviours such as physical activity , which can legitimately occur in many places and social situations, than for"},{"title":"Missed Days","url":"/missed-days-17f23f/","description":"One missed opportunity usually does not destroy habit formation, but repeated interruptions need a clear rule for how the routine restarts.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fix the System","level":3,"text":"Missed Days How to Restart a Habit After You Miss Fix the System Missed Days Missed Days How to Restart a Habit After You Miss One missed opportunity usually does not destroy habit formation, but repeated interruptions need a clear rule for how the routine restarts. One missed opportunity usually does not destroy habit formation, but repeated interruptions need a clear rule for how the routine restarts. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic event time window Why one missed day is different from a failure pattern Creating a simple recovery rule Diagnosing repeated disruptions instead of resetting self improvement that works fix the system missed days Introduction Missing a day does not usually mean a habit has been erased. In one of the best known real world habit formation studies, participants repeated a chosen behaviour in a stable daily context for 12 weeks, and missing a single opportunity did not materially alter the habit formation process . What mattered more was repetition over time. Habit strength also developed at very different rates between individuals, rather than according to a universal deadline. [Wiley Online Library] Treat an isolated miss as a miss, not a reset. Open source on wiley.com. Explanatory illustration 1 The more important problem begins when an interruption becomes a pattern: one missed run becomes a week without running, or every late meeting breaks an evening study routine. At that point, “starting again” is not primarily a test of motivation. It is a troubleshooting problem. A useful recovery system distinguishes an isolated lapse from evidence that the original cue, time or behaviour no longer fits real life, then specifies exactly what happens at the next workable opportunity. [PubMed] nih.gov Pub Med Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedContext Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022 Why One Missed Day Is Different From a Failure Pattern Habit formation is better understood as repeated learning than as maintaining a perfect streak. Phillippa Lally and colleagues followed 96 people who selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and attempted it in the same context each day. Among participants for whom an automaticity curve could be modelled, the estimated time to reach 95% of their eventual automaticity ranged from 18 to 254 days . The study therefore offers little support for treating an arbitrary streak length as the mechanism that makes behaviour habitual. [Wiley Online Library] Treat an isolated miss as a miss, not a reset. Open source on wiley.com. This matters after a missed day because the intuitive response — “I have broken the habit, so I must start from zero” — overstates what happened. A missed opportunity removes one repetition; it does not necessarily undo all the learning produced by previous repetitions. Lally's study specifically compared changes in automaticity around performed and missed opportunities and concluded that one omission did not materially disrupt formation. [Wiley Online Library] Treat an isolated miss as a miss, not a reset. Open source on wiley.com. That finding should not be stretched into the stronger claim that missed days never matter. The same study found that more consistent performance was associated with better fitting habit development trajectories. More broadly, research characterises habits as responses acquired through repetition in recurring contexts. Repeated interruptions therefore matter because they reduce opportunities for reinforcement and may reveal that the supposedly recurring context is not actually stable enough to support the routine. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The practical distinction is consequently between a lapse and a systematic disruption . Missing Tuesday because of an exceptional emergency is mostly a recovery event. Missing most Tuesdays because work always finishes late is evidence about the design of the routine. The first calls for resumption; the second calls for diagnosis. The Real Reason You Can't Stay Consistent (Even When You Try) Channel: What’s The Next Level Open on YouTube Create a Recovery Rule Before You Need It The strongest recovery rule is usually simple enough that there is little to decide after the miss. Rather than promising vaguely to “get back on track”, specify the next cue and the action attached to it. For example: Exercise : “If I miss my planned Monday session, I do my normal session at Wednesday's scheduled opportunity.” Reading: “If bedtime reading is lost to a late night, I resume after getting into bed the following night.” Study: “If today's study block becomes impossible, I start with the first five minutes of tomorrow's scheduled block rather than trying to repay today's time.” Walking: “If bad weather prevents my lunchtime walk, the next normal lunchtime is the restart point.” This approach is consistent with research on implementation intentions : plans that connect a recognisable situation with a specified response — essentially, “if X happens, then I will do Y”. A major meta analysis covering 94 independent tests found that implementation intentions improved goal attainment overall and helped with problems including initiating action and protecting goal pursuit from disruption. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... For recovery, the useful adaptation is to plan not only for success but for interruption: “If the planned opportunity is missed, then at the next normal cue I resume.” Research on action and coping planning supports this distinction. Action plans specify when, where and how behaviour happens; coping plans anticipate obstacles and specify responses to them. In a randomised study of cardiac rehabilitation patients, participants given both action and coping planning subsequently reported more exercise than those receiving action planning alone or standard care. [PubMed] nih.gov Pub Med Action plans and coping plans for physical exercise: A longitudinal intervention study in cardiac rehabilitation PubMedAction plans and coping plans for physical exercise: A longitudinal intervention study in cardiac rehabilitation PubMed The recovery rule should generally restore the ordinary routine rather than create a punishment for missing it. Doubling tomorrow's workout, reading twice as many pages or studying for two hours because yesterday's hour was missed changes the cost of restarting. It can turn a manageable habit into an accumulating debt. The evidence for implementation intentions supports specifying an actionable response to the obstacle; it does not imply that missed repetitions have to be repaid. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes ScienceDirect... Restart at the Next Useful Cue “Tomorrow” is sometimes an unnecessarily vague restart point. Habits are associated with contexts: places, preceding actions, times and other recurring circumstances that become linked to behaviour through repetition. Wendy Wood and Dennis Rünger describe habits as responses formed through repeatedly acting in particular contexts, while experimental and observational work has found that greater context stability predicts stronger automaticity and better repetition of intended habits. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Psychology of Habit Psychology of Habit PubMed... That makes the next useful cue a better unit of recovery than the calendar streak. Suppose the habit is “floss after brushing my teeth at night”. If Tuesday is missed, the most straightforward recovery opportunity is Wednesday's night time brushing cue. There is little reason to floss at an unusual time on Wednesday morning merely to repair the streak if doing so weakens the relationship between the intended behaviour and its normal context. The same principle explains why travel, illness, holidays and schedule changes can be unusually disruptive. Research on students transferring universities found that established behaviours such as exercise and newspaper reading were more likely to survive when relevant aspects of their performance context remained stable. When the usual context changed, habitual performance was disrupted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Pub Med Changing circumstances, disrupting habits Large scale UK research has similarly found evidence that changes such as moving home can temporarily weaken the influence of existing habits. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. After several missed days caused by an exceptional interruption, then, the first repair to try is often deliberately"},{"title":"Start Friction","url":"/start-friction/","description":"When you notice the cue but still postpone action, small preparation steps may be making the desired behaviour harder to start than the alternative.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fix the System","level":3,"text":"Start Friction Why Starting the Habit Feels Harder Than It Should Fix the System Start Friction Start Friction Why Starting the Habit Feels Harder Than It Should When you notice the cue but still postpone action, small preparation steps may be making the desired behaviour harder to start than the alternative. When you notice the cue but still postpone action, small preparation steps may be making the desired behaviour harder to start than the alternative. Self Improvement That Works implementation single mechanism mechanism Mapping every step before the first real action Moving preparation outside the vulnerable moment Making the first action obvious and immediate self improvement that works fix the system start friction Introduction When you notice the cue for a habit but still postpone starting, the problem may be neither memory nor commitment. The habit may simply have too much start up friction : equipment has to be found, a space cleared, an app opened, a decision made, clothes changed, materials gathered or several small steps completed before the intended behaviour actually begins. Each step is modest, but together they make the alternative — often doing nothing or continuing an existing habit — easier. Explanatory illustration 1 This matters because effort influences choice, and habits become stronger through repeated behaviour in recurring contexts. Research on habit change therefore increasingly emphasises changing the environment and the ease of competing actions, rather than relying solely on stronger intentions. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Habits, Goals, and Effective Behavior Change Sage JournalsHabits, Goals, and Effective Behavior Change Wendy Wood, 2024June 3, 2024... Published: June 3, 2024 The practical repair is specific: move as much preparation as possible out of the moment when the habit is supposed to start, then make the first real action immediately available . The aim is not to eliminate all effort from the activity. It is to stop unnecessary effort from deciding whether the activity begins. Map Everything Before the First Real Action Start up friction is easy to underestimate because people tend to describe habits at too high a level. “Go for a run”, “practise guitar” and “study after dinner” sound like single behaviours. At the moment of action, however, each may actually be a chain. Consider an intended evening workout: 1. Notice that it is workout time. 2. Decide which workout to do. 3. Find suitable clothes. 4. Change clothes. 5. Find headphones. 6. Fill a water bottle. 7. Move furniture or prepare equipment. 8. Open an exercise programme. 9. Choose the session. 10. Start exercising. Only the final step is the behaviour the person believes they are postponing. The preceding nine are an activation sequence that has to succeed first. If the competing option is “sit down and open my phone”, the two behaviours have radically different start costs. That asymmetry is behaviourally important. Laboratory research consistently finds that greater anticipated physical or cognitive effort reduces the subjective attractiveness of rewards and affects which actions people choose. The exact mathematics of effort discounting varies by task, so it would be an overstatement to translate those experiments directly into a rule such as “every extra habit step reduces adherence by X per cent”. The defensible conclusion is simpler: effort is a genuine cost in action selection, even when the eventual outcome is desirable . [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Physical and cognitive effort discounting across different reward magnitudes: Tests of discounting models PMCJuly 3... Small procedural burdens can also have surprisingly large effects outside the laboratory. A field experiment involving small and medium sized businesses found that adding minor effort to the process for obtaining free environmental management items substantially reduced programme effectiveness. That setting is very different from personal habit formation, but it demonstrates the broader mechanism: seemingly trivial hassles can alter whether an intended action gets completed. [DOI] doi.org How sludge impairs the effectiveness of policy programs: a field experiment with SMEs Behavioural Public Policy Cambridge CoreNove... Audit the activation sequence, not the habit When a habit repeatedly stalls after its cue, write down what actually happens between “now is the moment” and “I am doing it” . Include steps that seem too trivial to matter. For a reading habit, the sequence might be: finish dinner → remember the plan → decide where to read → locate the book → find the page → move the laptop → put the phone somewhere else → sit down → begin the first paragraph. For morning exercise, it might include locating clothing, choosing a route, checking the weather and deciding what counts as today’s workout. Then mark each pre start step according to what it demands: Physical friction: fetching, carrying, changing clothes, unlocking, plugging in, travelling or setting up. Decision friction: choosing the workout, book, lesson, route, duration or starting point. Search friction: finding equipment, files, passwords, notes or instructions. Digital friction: logging in, navigating menus, dismissing notifications or locating the correct document. Transition friction: stopping one comfortable activity, moving elsewhere or switching from leisure to focused work. The useful diagnostic question is not “Is any one of these difficult?” It is “Which of these steps still has to happen after the cue?” Five easy preparatory actions can collectively create a poor starting system. This distinction also prevents the wrong repair. If you reliably notice “I should practise now” and then spend ten minutes avoiding getting the instrument out, adding another reminder targets a problem you do not have. The cue is already working. The bottleneck lies between cue detection and behavioural initiation. The 20 Second Rule A Simple Way to Change Your Habits Channel: Vihan Chelliah Open on YouTube Move Preparation Outside the Vulnerable Moment The highest value fix is often pre positioning : perform preparatory actions earlier, when they are not competing with the immediate temptation to postpone. Research on physical activity offers a useful illustration. In a longitudinal study of 338 participants, planning predicted preparatory behaviours, and those preparatory behaviours in turn predicted subsequent physical activity. The study was observational rather than an experiment proving that laying out clothes directly causes exercise, but it supports an important distinction: preparation is part of the pathway through which an intention becomes behaviour, not merely administrative detail surrounding it. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Planning and preparatory actions facilitate physical activity maintenance ScienceDirect... For a repeatedly failing habit, that suggests changing when preparation occurs: HabitPreparation left until start timePreparation moved earlierMorning runFind kit, choose route, locate headphonesKit and shoes laid out; default route already chosenGuitar practiceRetrieve guitar and music, choose exerciseGuitar accessible; music open at next exerciseEvening studyClear desk, find notes, decide subjectDesk ready; correct document already openHealthy lunchDecide what to eat and prepare ingredientsLunch prepared or ingredients portioned beforehandBedtime readingFind book, charge reading light, move phoneBook on pillow or bedside table; light ready The point is not ritual for its own sake. A preparation routine is useful only when it removes work from the later initiation point . This is also why organisation can occasionally become disguised friction. Elaborate productivity systems may add categories, tracking, app navigation and choices before meaningful work begins. If “write for 20 minutes” requires opening a task manager, reviewing priorities, selecting a project, starting a timer, choosing music and configuring a distraction blocker, the system intended to support the habit has acquired its own start up sequence. Explanatory illustration 2 Remove decisions before removing effort Physical setup is only part of the problem. A person can have running shoes beside the door and still stall over whether to run 3 km or 5 km, which route to take and what pace to aim for. Implementation intention research is relevant here. An implementation intention specifies a situation and a response in an if–then form — in effect, deciding in advance what will happen when a particular cue appears. Experimental work has found that these plans can make action initiation faster and more efficient once the specified situation occurs, including under cognitive load. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation Implementation intentions and efficient action initiation PubMed... A 2024 meta analysis"},{"title":"Bad Timing","url":"/bad-timing/","description":"A routine can fail repeatedly because its scheduled moment is unreliable, overloaded or mismatched to when you actually have capacity.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fix the System","level":3,"text":"Bad Timing Is Your Habit Scheduled at the Wrong Time? Fix the System Bad Timing Bad Timing Is Your Habit Scheduled at the Wrong Time? A routine can fail repeatedly because its scheduled moment is unreliable, overloaded or mismatched to when you actually have capacity. A routine can fail repeatedly because its scheduled moment is unreliable, overloaded or mismatched to when you actually have capacity. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context How to spot a timing mismatch Stable events versus aspirational schedules Testing a better time without changing the habit self improvement that works fix the system bad timing Introduction A habit can be perfectly sensible and still fail because it has been assigned to the wrong part of the day. If the intended moment is routinely swallowed by meetings, childcare, commuting, fatigue or unpredictable finishing times, repeated failure is evidence about the schedule, not necessarily the behaviour itself. Explanatory illustration 1 Habit research gives timing a specific role: repetition in a sufficiently stable context helps a cue become associated with an action. But “stable” does not have to mean obeying an exact clock time. A recurring event such as finishing breakfast can provide a usable cue, and some behaviours may benefit from flexibility rather than a narrow time window. Studies comparing routine based and clock based cues have found that both can support habit formation when people actually repeat the planned behaviour. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 The practical troubleshooting question is therefore not simply, “What time should I do this?” It is: Which recurring moment in my real life reliably gives this behaviour a chance to happen? How to spot a timing mismatch Bad timing usually leaves a pattern. The habit does not fail randomly; it repeatedly collides with the same conditions. A planned 6 pm workout disappears whenever work runs late. Reading before bed works until tiredness makes the phone or sleep easier. Morning study succeeds at weekends but not on school run weekdays. That pattern matters because habits are context sensitive. In a six week study of university students, researchers deliberately varied versus stabilised the times and places in which participants practised new study habits. Greater context stability was associated with greater automaticity and better attainment of the participants' repetition goals. A second dataset from a habit building app showed the same general relationship. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PMCJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 A useful timing diagnosis therefore looks beyond the scheduled hour. For seven to fourteen days, record four things: when the cue actually occurred, whether the habit started, what competed with it, and how much practical capacity you had at that point. You are looking for repeated collisions rather than isolated bad days. Strong signs of a timing mismatch include: The habit works when done earlier or later. The behaviour is feasible, but its assigned slot is not. The same interruption repeatedly wins. Meetings, cooking, commuting or caring responsibilities occupy the supposedly available period. The cue itself moves around. “After work” is unreliable if work ends anywhere between 5 pm and 8 pm. You remember the habit but continually postpone it. That distinguishes a capacity or scheduling problem from simple forgetting. Weekdays and weekends behave differently. A cue tied to the working day may disappear when the structure of the day changes. Your available energy does not match the task. A demanding exercise or study session may repeatedly meet the lowest capacity part of your day. This last point should not be turned into a universal claim that everybody must exercise, study or meditate in the morning. Evidence suggests that individual timing can matter. A 2024 experiment involving 317 young women found that behavioural engagement sometimes differed according to morning–evening preference: for exercise, morning oriented participants did better with morning cues and evening oriented participants with evening cues. The finding is useful evidence for matching rather than a rule that establishes one ideal time for everyone. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Evaluating the impact of individuals' morningness eveningness on the effectiveness of a habit formation intervention for a simple a... The simplest diagnostic test is counterfactual: if exactly the same habit were moved to another recurring moment, would most of these failures disappear? If the answer is plausibly yes, test timing before redesigning the behaviour itself. How to Stick to Habits and Routines Without Falling Off! Channel: How to ADHD Open on YouTube Stable events can beat aspirational schedules An aspirational schedule describes the day you would like to have: “I will write at 7 am”, “I will exercise at 6 pm”, “I will read at 9.30 pm”. A stable event describes something your existing life already produces: “after I make breakfast”, “when I arrive home”, or “after I put the children to bed”. The distinction matters because a clock time is useful only if life reliably leaves that time usable. A randomized controlled trial followed 192 adults for 84 days after assigning them to link an everyday nutrition behaviour either to a daily routine or to a specific time. Both approaches increased plan enactment and automaticity, with no significant advantage for one cue type over the other. Among participants who successfully developed habits, the estimated median time to peak automaticity was 59 days. Most importantly for troubleshooting, repeated enactment of the plan predicted automaticity. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk UCL DiscoveryHabit formation following routine based versus time based cue planning: A randomized controlled trial UCL Discovery... So “event based is always better than time based” goes beyond the evidence. A dependable 7.30 am may work perfectly well. The problem is false precision : choosing an exact time that repeatedly conflicts with the structure of the day. There is also a reason to be cautious about making routines too rigid. In a field experiment involving 2,508 employees with access to workplace gyms, researchers compared incentives for exercising within a self selected two hour window with incentives that rewarded exercise regardless of timing. The narrowly scheduled incentives increased exercise within the chosen window, as intended, but produced fewer gym visits overall than flexible incentives. After incentives ended, the routine incentive groups also exercised less than the flexible incentive groups. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Creating Exercise Habits Using Incentives: The Tradeoff between Flexibility and Routinization PMCJuly 1, 2021... Published: July 1, 2021 That result does not mean consistency is useless; substantial evidence associates stable contexts with habit development. It shows the trade off. A rigid schedule can strengthen regularity at one moment while making the behaviour less likely when that moment becomes unavailable. In ordinary life, where meetings overrun and trains are late, a habit needs enough consistency to be learnable without becoming so brittle that one blocked slot cancels the day. A practical compromise is often a stable anchor plus a bounded fallback . For example: walk after lunch when possible; if lunch is spent in a meeting, walk after finishing work. The primary cue remains consistent, but real life has somewhere to go when it fails. Explanatory illustration 2 Real life changes the cues Sometimes a habit appears to have mysteriously weakened when the more precise explanation is that its supporting context has vanished. In qualitative research on people developing health related habits, participants reported that routines established in work settings could be disrupted during weekends and holidays because their normal cues were absent; the habits often returned when the work context returned. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study Experiences of habit formation: a qualitative study PubMedAugust 1, 2011... Published: August 1, 2011 More broadly, research on “habit discontinuity” shows how sensitive established behaviours can be to contextual change. In an influential study of students transferring universities, behaviours such as exercising and newspaper reading were more likely to survive when relevant features of their previous performance context remained stable. Changing the circumstances disrupted habitual performance beyond what could be explained merely by changes in people's intentions."},{"title":"Shrink the Habit","url":"/shrink-the-habit/","description":"A smaller version of the behaviour can preserve repetition on difficult days without abandoning the larger performance goal.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Fix the System","level":3,"text":"Shrink the Habit When Your Habit Is Simply Too Big Fix the System Shrink the Habit Shrink the Habit When Your Habit Is Simply Too Big A smaller version of the behaviour can preserve repetition on difficult days without abandoning the larger performance goal. A smaller version of the behaviour can preserve repetition on difficult days without abandoning the larger performance goal. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic policy intervention Telling low motivation from excessive task demand Defining a minimum version that preserves repetition When to expand the behaviour again self improvement that works fix the system shrink the habit Introduction When a habit repeatedly starts and then collapses because the planned action is too long, difficult or demanding for the conditions in which it occurs, shrinking the behaviour is a practical design change rather than an abandonment of the goal. The aim is to preserve a repeatable version on low capacity days while keeping the larger performance target available when circumstances permit. Explanatory illustration 1 This distinction matters because habit formation depends on actually repeating behaviour in sufficiently stable contexts. Research also suggests that behavioural complexity and perceived difficulty can work against automaticity , while more complex established behaviours continue to require deliberate self regulation. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... The useful troubleshooting question is therefore not simply “How can I become more motivated?” but “What version of this behaviour reliably fits the capacity I have when the cue occurs?” The strongest evidence supports the underlying principles—repeatability, manageable complexity and graded progression—rather than any universal formula such as “one push up” or “two minutes”. A minimum habit should be small enough to survive difficult days, meaningful enough to count as the intended behaviour, and expandable without turning the minimum itself into an escalating obligation. Forget big change, start with a tiny habit: BJ Fogg at TEDxFremont Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube Is motivation low, or is the task simply too demanding? Low motivation and excessive task demand can look identical from the outside: the planned behaviour does not get completed. The pattern of failure helps distinguish them. A 45 minute workout that is enthusiastically planned in the morning but repeatedly abandoned after ten minutes in the evening may not primarily be a commitment problem. The behaviour may simply exceed the time, energy or concentration normally available at its actual point of performance. Recent longitudinal evidence strengthens this interpretation. A 2026 preregistered reanalysis following 254 people for six months found that habit trajectories differed with behavioural complexity, frequency and context stability. In its multilevel analyses, greater perceived difficulty was negatively associated with habit strength, while greater behaviour frequency and feeling energised were positively associated with it. The study is observational rather than proof that making any behaviour easier will necessarily create a habit, but it makes perceived difficulty a legitimate variable to troubleshoot rather than something automatically attributed to deficient motivation. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsWhat Makes a Habit? Investigating Potential Determinants of Habit Formation Sebastian Bürgler, Marina Milyavskaya, Gail Mc... Complexity presents a related problem. In a study covering 25 behaviours among 459 participants, perceived behavioural complexity was negatively associated with automaticity. The researchers defined complexity partly in terms of behaviours involving more mental or physical steps, taking more time and requiring more planning. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Habits, Quick and Easy: Perceived Complexity Moderates the Associations of Contextual Stability and Rewards With Beh... A conceptual review similarly argues that complex behaviours cannot always be treated like single, uniform responses: they may contain multiple separable components, each with different demands. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Ramifications of behavioural complexity for habit conceptualisation, promotion, and measurement PubMedApril 6, 2022... Published: April 6, 2022 That gives repeated mid task collapse diagnostic value. Consider three attempts: “Read after breakfast” is repeatedly forgotten. Shrinking the reading session will not necessarily fix the missing cue. “Read for 45 minutes after breakfast” is started almost every day but commonly abandoned after 10 minutes. Duration is now a plausible bottleneck. “Go to the gym after work” succeeds on quiet days but repeatedly fails when work finishes late. A smaller fallback session may fit the variable capacity better than insisting that every performance must be the full workout. The distinction should not be overdrawn. Motivation, opportunity and task difficulty interact, and there is no simple diagnostic test that converts a few failures into a precise psychological cause. But repeated initiation followed by predictable collapse is a good reason to test a smaller specification of the behaviour before concluding that stronger motivation is the missing ingredient. Explanatory illustration 3 How small habits and a two minute rule can help your productivity Channel: CBS Mornings Open on YouTube Define a minimum that preserves the repetition The minimum version is best understood as a floor, not a new ceiling . If the performance goal is to read for 30 minutes, for example, the minimum might be reading two pages after breakfast. On a normal day, reading continues towards 30 minutes. On a difficult day, two pages still constitutes a completed repetition. That arrangement separates two jobs that are easily confused. The habit floor protects the recurring act of starting and performing the behaviour in its intended context. The performance goal determines how much work is desirable when sufficient capacity exists. The smaller version is therefore not a claim that two pages, five minutes of walking or one paragraph of writing is enough to achieve every larger outcome. It is a way of making repetition less brittle. This distinction fits the habit literature particularly well. Habit researchers define habits in terms of cue linked automatic responding learned through repetition, rather than simply as ambitious goals completed repeatedly. Reviews of applied habit formation consequently recommend selecting a simple action and repeating it in a consistent context. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 In Lally and colleagues' influential real world study, participants selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour and repeated it in the same daily context for 12 weeks. Automaticity generally increased with repetition, although the estimated time to reach 95% of each person's plateau varied enormously—from 18 to 254 days among those whose data fitted the model. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... A 2024 systematic review likewise found substantial variation in health habit formation and warned against treating a fixed number of days as a universal rule. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A workable minimum therefore has four useful properties: 1. It keeps the behaviour recognisable. “Open the textbook” may be an excellent cue or preparation step, but if the intended habit is studying, “answer one practice question” more clearly preserves the target behaviour. 2. It fits the bad but normal day. A minimum designed only for ideal conditions does not solve the capacity problem. It should survive ordinary fatigue, time pressure or competing demands. 3. Its completion rule is unambiguous. “Do a little exercise ” invites renegotiation; “walk for five minutes after lunch” makes completion observable. MINI HABITS by Stephen Guise Core Message Channel: Productivity Game Open on YouTube Explanatory illustration 2 4. It remains attached to the intended cue or context. Shrinking the amount while retaining “after breakfast”, “after lunch” or another stable event preserves the repeated context–behaviour pairing that habit theory regards as important. PubMed Central (PMC) An if–then rule can make the fallback explicit rather than leaving it to an exhausted person to negotiate in the moment: If I reach the normal cue and cannot reasonably do the full session, then I do the minimum version. Implementation intentions—plans"},{"title":"Metric Fixation","url":"/metric-fixation/","description":"A target can become counterproductive when people optimize the measured number while neglecting the purpose the metric was meant to serve.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goals vs Actions","level":3,"text":"Metric Fixation When Chasing the Number Starts Hurting the Goal Goals vs Actions Metric Fixation Metric Fixation When Chasing the Number Starts Hurting the Goal A target can become counterproductive when people optimize the measured number while neglecting the purpose the metric was meant to serve. A target can become counterproductive when people optimize the measured number while neglecting the purpose the metric was meant to serve. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism How targets narrow attention Why people may neglect unmeasured priorities Warning signs that a goal metric is taking over self improvement that works goals vs actions metric fixation Introduction A goal can improve performance by concentrating attention on what matters. The same mechanism can also become a liability: once a number becomes the dominant definition of success, people may optimise the number rather than the purpose it was supposed to represent. Researchers describe related problems as goal fixation, goal displacement and metric gaming. Goodhart’s law captures the basic measurement problem: when a measure becomes a consequential target, the behaviour surrounding that measure changes, potentially making it less informative. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. Explanatory illustration 1 For self improvement, gaming need not mean deliberate cheating. It can be as ordinary as choosing easy books to preserve a reading streak, doing low value tasks to hit a productivity count, or training in ways that improve a fitness app statistic while neglecting recovery. The important question is therefore not merely, “Did the number improve?” It is, “Does improving this number still mean that the underlying thing I care about is improving?” How targets narrow attention Goals work partly because they are selective. They tell the mind that one result deserves attention and effort. Research on “goal shielding” found that activating a strongly held focal goal can inhibit the accessibility of alternative goals. That can be useful: protecting one priority from distractions helps people persist. But the same selectivity becomes dangerous when the alternatives being screened out are actually important parts of success. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Forgetting all else: on the antecedents and consequences of goal shielding PubMed... This is why metric fixation is not simply a failure of discipline. It can emerge from successful discipline applied too narrowly. A runner intent on a weekly mileage target may continue adding kilometres when recovery would be wiser. Someone measuring productivity by tasks completed may become extremely efficient at clearing trivial tasks. A student targeting hours studied can spend the required time at a desk without checking whether the study method produces durable learning. The problem becomes particularly clear when performance has several dimensions but the target captures only one. Experimental work has found quantity–quality trade offs under some goal conditions: in six experiments reported by Janet Bavelas and Eric Lee, higher goals increased the number of responses on several creativity tasks while the average quality of responses moved further from the experimenters’ ideal. That does not establish a universal law that quantity targets reduce quality — other tasks can produce different results — but it illustrates the underlying danger of defining success on one dimension of a multidimensional activity. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Effects of goal level on performance: A trade off of quantity and qualityDecember 1, 1978... Published: December 1, 1978 A review of goal setting in the workplace describes the same practical issue: quantity goals can pull attention away from quality, while multiple goals do not automatically solve the problem because people may concentrate on dimensions that are easier to measure. [World of Labour] wol.iza.org World of LabourWorld of Labour Goal setting and worker motivation... The paradox is that this attentional narrowing is also one reason goals can work so well. Recent experiments on sustained attention, for example, have found that specific, difficult task goals can reduce behavioural lapses and improve performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Effects of goal setting on sustained attention and attention lapses Effects of goal setting on sustained attention and attention lapses PubMedOctober 23, 2023... Published: October 23, 2023 The sensible conclusion is therefore not that focus is harmful. It is that a goal directs attention towards whatever has been specified, not necessarily towards everything that ultimately matters . 6:08 When the Measure Becomes the Idol Goodhart’s Law & The Law of Distortion Channel: BASIRA Lab · Views: 101 · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why unmeasured priorities disappear Metrics are proxies. Body weight is not health. Words written are not good writing. Books completed are not knowledge. Hours worked are not useful output. Money saved is not financial wellbeing in its entirety. A proxy can be extremely useful while remaining incomplete. Trouble starts when the distinction between the proxy and its purpose fades. Research on public sector targets provides unusually visible examples because organisations leave records of how people react to measurement systems. In their study of targets in the English public health care system, Gwyn Bevan and Christopher Hood examined how performance regimes could produce gaming alongside genuine improvements. Their central warning was essentially that treating one measurable part as if it represented the whole creates predictable opportunities for distortion. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryWHAT’S MEASURED IS WHAT MATTERS: TARGETS AND GAMING IN THE ENGLISH PUBLIC HEALTH CARE SYSTEM BEVAN 2006 Public... Research on UK public spending controls likewise describes “creative categorisation”: when administrative numbers carried consequences, actors could change how expenditure was classified in ways that helped satisfy the numerical rules. The underlying spending had not necessarily changed in the way an uninformed observer of the headline measure might assume. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. This is the deeper meaning of metric gaming. There are at least three ways a target can appear to improve: Real improvement: the underlying objective genuinely gets better and the metric records it. Proxy optimisation: the measured feature improves, but other important dimensions stagnate or deteriorate. Measurement manipulation: the way activity is counted, classified or reported changes, producing a better number without corresponding substantive progress. Personal goals are usually less formal than institutional performance systems, but the logic transfers readily. Suppose the objective is to read more because you want greater knowledge. “Books finished” is an easy proxy. Once 50 books a year becomes the emotionally important target, however, shorter and easier books become disproportionately attractive. Nothing dishonest has happened. The metric has simply begun influencing the activity it was meant to observe. The same effect can become almost invisible with daily behaviours. A behaviour goal such as “write 500 words every morning” is more controllable than “publish a successful book”, which is one reason behaviour based goals are useful. But controllability does not guarantee validity. If 500 words becomes sacrosanct, a writer may resist deleting weak material, researching a difficult point or spending a session restructuring a chapter because those valuable activities do not increase the chosen count. The lesson is not to abandon behavioural measures. It is to remember their status: the behaviour is valuable because it serves a larger purpose, not because it can be counted . Explanatory illustration 2 When a target starts changing the rules of the game Metric fixation becomes more concerning when the target is difficult and missing it feels consequential. In a well known series of laboratory studies, Maurice Schweitzer, Lisa Ordóñez and Bambi Douma found that participants with unmet goals were more likely to engage in unethical behaviour than participants instructed simply to do their best. The effect appeared both with and without financial incentives and was particularly pronounced among people who narrowly missed their target. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. Later reviews have proposed several mechanisms connecting demanding performance goals with unethical behaviour, including reduced attention to moral considerations, moral disengagement, pressure on self regulation and greater risk taking. Incentives attached to goal attainment can increase the stakes further. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com"},{"title":"Learning Goals","url":"/learning-goals-301732/","description":"When a task is complex and the right strategy is still unclear, learning what works can matter more than chasing a demanding score.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goals vs Actions","level":3,"text":"Learning Goals When Should You Learn Before Chasing a Target? Goals vs Actions Learning Goals Learning Goals When Should You Learn Before Chasing a Target? When a task is complex and the right strategy is still unclear, learning what works can matter more than chasing a demanding score. When a task is complex and the right strategy is still unclear, learning what works can matter more than chasing a demanding score. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic population context Why hard targets work differently on complex tasks What learning goals ask you to improve first When to switch from strategy discovery to performance targets self improvement that works goals vs actions learning goals Introduction When a task is new, complex and strategically unclear, a demanding performance target can arrive too early. The immediate problem is not necessarily insufficient effort; it may be that you do not yet know how to perform well. In that situation, a learning goal — such as identifying effective strategies, mastering a technique or testing different approaches — can be more useful than insisting on a particular score. Explanatory illustration 1 This is an important qualification to the familiar finding that specific, difficult goals often improve performance. Research suggests that their benefits weaken when success depends heavily on acquiring knowledge and discovering strategies. Experiments on complex tasks have repeatedly found advantages for learning goals, although the effect is not universal. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... The practical principle is therefore not “avoid ambitious targets”. It is sequence the goals to match the problem : discover what works when the method is uncertain, then demand increasingly strong performance once an effective method is available. Why hard targets work differently on complex tasks A performance goal assumes, at least implicitly, that better results can be produced by directing more attention, effort and persistence towards the desired result. If you already know how to perform a task, that can be exactly what is needed. A runner with an established training system, for example, may benefit from a challenging time target because the basic methods are already understood. A novel complex task presents a different bottleneck. Performance may depend on working out which information matters, which actions should be combined, what sequence works and how the strategy should change when circumstances change. Simply trying harder does not solve those questions. Research on complex task learning has consequently found that the standard advantage of difficult, specific goals does not generalise straightforwardly from simpler tasks. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Goal Setting Effects on Implicit and Explicit Learning of Complex Tasks ScienceDirect... One of the clearest demonstrations came from Dawn Winters and Gary Latham. In an experiment involving 114 business students, participants performed either a simple or complex scheduling task. On the simple task, an outcome goal produced better performance than being told merely to do their best. On the complex version, however, participants given a learning goal performed significantly better than those given either an outcome goal or a do your best instruction. Crucially, the learning goal group also discovered and used more effective task strategies. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsThe Effect of Learning versus Outcome Goals on a Simple versus a Complex Task Dawn Winters, Gary P. Latham, 1996... That last finding helps explain why the distinction matters. A learning goal does not merely replace a stressful number with something easier. It changes where attention is directed . Seijts and Latham describe the appropriate learning goal as one focused on discovering effective strategies or processes when knowledge and skills still need to be acquired. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Open source on aom.org. A later complex business simulation study found the same broad pattern: a specific learning goal produced better performance than either a specific performance goal or a vague goal. Information search and self efficacy helped explain the learning goal's effect on performance. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsGoal Setting and Goal Orientation: An Integration of Two Different Yet Related Literatures Academy of Man... This makes the distinction especially relevant to self improvement. Suppose someone is learning public speaking. “Give an 8/10 presentation” establishes a standard, but a novice may have little idea what changes would produce that score. Early goals such as “test three ways of structuring the opening and compare audience responses” or “learn to deliver the first two minutes without reading from notes” direct effort towards building the capabilities from which later performance can emerge. Why Most Goals Fail (and How to Set Goals That Actually Stick) Channel: Mental Health Academy Open on YouTube What learning goals ask you to improve first A genuine learning goal concerns acquiring capability , not simply replacing an outcome target with a daily activity. That distinguishes it from a process or behavioural goal based on a strategy that is already known. Recent goal setting literature explicitly makes this distinction: process goals concern executing existing strategies, whereas learning goals concern acquiring the knowledge and skills needed to perform the task. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Effect of goal setting, attentional focus, and feedback on resistance training: Stage 1 registered report of a factorial ran... For a person who does not yet know the best route forward, useful learning goals might therefore ask them to: identify several strategies that could improve performance and test them; learn a particular technique well enough to use it reliably; discover which recurring errors are producing poor results; compare two plausible methods using the same feedback measure; seek information from stronger performers and extract specific practices to test; practise a difficult component separately until its mechanics are understood. The goal can still be specific and challenging. “Learn more about studying” is vague. “Test retrieval practice and rereading on comparable material this week, then compare what I can recall two days later” gives learning a concrete purpose. Likewise, “become better at sales” says little about what must be discovered; “identify and test three ways of handling the objection that most often ends my calls” creates an observable learning task. This is consistent with another scheduling experiment involving 96 participants. The task required participants to acquire knowledge in order to perform correctly. A specific, difficult learning goal produced higher performance than a do your best instruction, while a specific, difficult outcome goal performed worse than the do your best outcome condition. The number of task relevant strategies participants discovered was positively associated with their performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of distal learning, outcome, and proximal goals on a moderately complex task Seijts 2001 Journal of... The same principle has appeared beyond a single experimental design. A meta analysis of manipulated motivational states found an overall performance advantage for learning over performance goals, while indicating that the advantage may be concentrated in relatively complex tasks. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Performance effects of motivational state: a meta analysis Performance effects of motivational state: a meta analysis PubMed... For personal development, that suggests a useful diagnostic question before choosing a demanding metric: Is my main constraint motivation, or is it still method? If you already know what works and are failing to execute it, stronger behavioural commitments or performance targets may be appropriate. If you genuinely do not know which strategy works, measuring yourself relentlessly against an outcome does little to fill the knowledge gap. Explanatory illustration 2 Premature targets can compete with strategy discovery The problem with an early performance target is not simply that failure feels discouraging. Complex tasks consume attention. During early skill acquisition, the learner has to understand the task while simultaneously trying to perform it. A demanding result can direct scarce attention towards the performance gap precisely when that attention is needed for experimentation, information search and strategy formation. DeShon and Alexander's experiments on complex learning help qualify the mechanism. Difficult specific goals did not automatically improve"},{"title":"Noisy Outcomes","url":"/noisy-outcomes/","description":"Weight, investment balances and race times can move for reasons unrelated to whether today's planned behaviour was good.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goals vs Actions","level":3,"text":"Noisy Outcomes When Outcome Numbers Give You the Wrong Signal Goals vs Actions Noisy Outcomes Noisy Outcomes When Outcome Numbers Give You the Wrong Signal Weight, investment balances and race times can move for reasons unrelated to whether today's planned behaviour was good. Weight, investment balances and race times can move for reasons unrelated to whether today's planned behaviour was good. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why outcomes contain factors outside personal control How short term noise can mislead daily decisions Using outcome trends without abandoning behaviour feedback self improvement that works goals vs actions noisy outcomes Introduction Outcome measures are useful because they tell you whether your efforts are ultimately producing the result you want. The problem is that many outcomes are noisy : today’s number reflects not only your underlying progress, but also temporary influences that have little to do with whether today’s planned behaviour was sensible. Explanatory illustration 1 Body weight can rise because of water, glycogen or digestive contents even while fat loss is progressing. An investment balance can fall despite disciplined saving because markets moved. A race time can deteriorate despite improved fitness because the day was hotter, windier or preceded by poor sleep. Research confirms meaningful short term variation in all three cases. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Day to day variability in euvolemic body mass PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day variability in euvolemic body mass PMCNovember 13, 2023... Published: November 13, 2023 The practical consequence is not to abandon outcome measures. It is to use them at the timescale at which they become informative. Daily behaviour answers “Did I do what I intended?”; an outcome trend answers “Does this approach appear to be working?” Confusing those two feedback loops can make good behaviour look unsuccessful and random favourable fluctuations look like progress. Why outcomes contain factors outside personal control An outcome number is rarely a pure measurement of personal effort. A useful way to think about it is: observed outcome = underlying progress + temporary conditions + measurement variation The proportions differ between domains. With body weight, much of the short term disturbance is physiological. With investments, external market movements can dominate. With race performance, environmental and day specific physical conditions affect what a given level of fitness produces. Body weight can move faster than body tissue Scale weight is particularly good at demonstrating the problem because it looks precise. A digital scale might report 78.4 kg rather than 78 kg, encouraging the impression that a change to 78.8 kg represents equally precise information about fat gain. It does not. Research on normal day to day body mass variability found fluctuations of roughly 0.4 kg attributable largely to changes in water associated with factors including sodium and glycogen. The researchers noted that a tissue composition change of that magnitude within one day would be physiologically implausible. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Day to day variability in euvolemic body mass PubMed Central (PMC)Day to day variability in euvolemic body mass PMCNovember 13, 2023... Published: November 13, 2023 Glycogen adds another mechanism: glycogen stored in muscle is accompanied by substantial water, while changing intestinal contents also contributes to short term changes in measured mass. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Composition of two‐week change in body weight under unrestricted free‐living conditions PMC... For people who menstruate, there can be another systematic source of variation. A 2023 study following 42 women found body weight was about 0.45 kg higher during menstruation than during the first week of the cycle, with the difference largely attributable to increased extracellular water rather than a detected change in other body composition measures. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Changes in body weight and body composition during the menstrual cycle Changes in body weight and body composition during the menstrual cycle PubMedJuly 3, 2023... Published: July 3, 2023 This produces a common false signal. Someone can follow their intended eating and activity behaviours on Monday and Tuesday, then see a higher weight on Wednesday. If Wednesday's measurement is interpreted as a verdict on those behaviours, water movement can masquerade as failure. Repeated measurements solve a different problem from single measurements. A review of daily self weighing notes that although body water, glycogen and gastrointestinal contents create substantial daily variation, graphing measurements over time can reveal the underlying pattern in body tissue. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Daily Self Weighing to Control Body Weight in Adults: A Critical Review of the Literature PMCDecember 14, 2014... Published: December 14, 2014 The individual measurement is not useless; it becomes more useful when treated as one observation in a series. Why Weight Fluctuation Is Normal Nutritionist Explains... Myprotein Channel: Myprotein Open on YouTube Investment balances mix your decisions with the market's decisions An investment account makes the separation between behaviour and outcome even clearer. You control whether you make the planned contribution, how much you contribute and — within practical limits — your asset allocation. You do not control what other investors collectively decide those assets are worth tomorrow. FINRA describes volatility simply as the movement of market indexes and security prices up and down, noting that long term buy and hold investors may appropriately treat much of this movement as background noise. [FINRA] finra.org Volatility FINRA.org Volatility FINRA.org... The US Securities and Exchange Commission similarly advises investors considering changes during volatile periods to relate decisions to their financial goals, time horizon and tolerance for risk rather than treating market movements in isolation. [SEC] sec.gov Things to Consider Before You Make Investing Decisions Things to Consider Before You Make Investing Decisions... Suppose somebody contributes £500 to a diversified long term portfolio exactly as planned and the portfolio nevertheless finishes the month £300 below where it started. “My balance fell” and “I failed at my savings behaviour” are not equivalent statements. The first can be true while the second is false. That distinction matters because reacting to the noisy outcome can create a second order problem: changing a sound long term strategy because of a short term price movement. FINRA warns that market timing involves predicting such movements and carries the risk of exiting during a decline only to miss a subsequent recovery. [FINRA] finra.org What Is Market Timing? FINRA.org What Is Market Timing? FINRA.orgJune 10, 2025... Published: June 10, 2025 Frequent observation can make this temptation stronger; Vanguard notes that repeatedly checking a portfolio during volatile periods can magnify the perceived significance of short term movements and encourage reactive decisions. [Vanguard UK Investor] ukd ics.ecs.itlp.c1.vanguard.com UK Investor How often should I check my portfolio? UK Investor How often should I check my portfolio? The important qualification is that volatility is not always irrelevant. A person who needs the money soon faces a genuinely different risk from somebody investing over decades. The SEC explicitly treats time horizon as an important determinant of appropriate investment risk. [SEC] sec.gov Beginners' Guide to Asset Allocation, Diversification, and Rebalancing Beginners' Guide to Asset Allocation, Diversification, and Rebalancing “Ignore every fluctuation” is therefore too crude. The narrower lesson is that a short term balance movement should not automatically be interpreted as evidence about the quality of today's controllable behaviour. Race times measure fitness under particular conditions A finishing time also appears beautifully objective: 42:17 is unquestionably faster than 43:05. Yet the stopwatch measures performance under the conditions of that particular attempt , not fitness in isolation. A large study covering 1,258 endurance races in 42 countries found temperature to be the most important weather variable examined. Peak performance was more likely within favourable temperature ranges, while performance declined as wet bulb globe temperature moved away from the optimum range. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of Weather Parameters on Endurance Running Performance: Discipline specific Analysis of 1258 Races PubMed... Analysis of hundreds of thousands of Boston Marathon performances likewise found slower times as temperatures increased,"},{"title":"Process Goals","url":"/process-goals/","description":"Process goals direct attention to the next controllable action, which can be more useful during execution than thinking about the final result.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goals vs Actions","level":3,"text":"Process Goals Why Process Goals Help When It Is Time to Perform Goals vs Actions Process Goals Process Goals Why Process Goals Help When It Is Time to Perform Process goals direct attention to the next controllable action, which can be more useful during execution than thinking about the final result. Process goals direct attention to the next controllable action, which can be more useful during execution than thinking about the final result. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative single mechanism mechanism How process goals differ from outcome goals What sport research suggests about performance How process cues transfer to study, work and training self improvement that works goals vs actions process goals Introduction Process goals are useful during performance because they direct attention towards what can still be done now . An outcome goal such as winning a race, earning a high mark or delivering the best presentation in the room may provide motivation beforehand, but once execution begins, thinking repeatedly about that result does not tell you what action to take next. A process goal does: maintain the chosen rhythm, complete the next step of the calculation, listen fully before responding, or use the rehearsed opening. Explanatory illustration 1 Sport psychology has developed this distinction particularly clearly. Research generally supports process goals as a useful way to organise attention and action, and a 2022 systematic review and meta analysis found substantially larger performance effects for process goals than for performance or outcome goals. The important qualification is that process goals must fit the performer and task: too much conscious attention to technique can interfere with an already well learned skill. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: The performance and psychological effects of goal setting in sport: A systematic review and meta ana... How process goals differ from outcome goals Sport research commonly distinguishes three kinds of goals. An outcome goal concerns the result relative to somebody else, such as winning a match. A performance goal uses a self referenced standard, such as achieving a personal best time. A process goal concerns the behaviours, strategies or thought processes involved in executing the task. The categories are not necessarily competitors; an athlete can want to win, target a particular time and still concentrate on a process cue while performing. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. The distinction becomes especially useful at the moment of execution because the goals sit at different distances from action. A tennis player cannot directly execute “win this set”. Winning depends partly on the opponent and emerges from many individual points. The player can, however, execute a chosen serving routine, recover position after a shot or commit to the intended target. Process goals therefore reduce a distant result to an immediate job. This is more than the familiar distinction between controllable and uncontrollable outcomes. Process goals can function as attentional instructions. Instead of asking the performer to keep evaluating whether success is approaching, they specify where attention should go. A golfer might use a cue associated with the intended movement; a runner might attend to rhythm; a speaker might concentrate on delivering the next idea clearly rather than monitoring the audience for evidence of success. That distinction helps explain why outcome goals can remain valuable without being the best thing to think about continuously. “Finish in the top three” can determine what months of preparation are for. At the starting line, however, attention has a different job. The question changes from What result do I want? to What do I need to execute now? Goal Setting in Sport How to Reach Any Goal! (Sport Psychology) Channel: Inner Edge Performance Open on YouTube What sport research suggests about performance The evidence is encouraging, although it is not strong enough to justify the simple slogan that process goals always outperform every alternative. A 2022 systematic review and meta analysis synthesised 27 studies and reported the largest performance effect for process goals, with an effect size of d = 1.36 , compared with d = 0.44 for performance goals and d = 0.09 for outcome goals. Process goals also showed a large positive effect on self efficacy, the belief that one can successfully perform the required task. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: The performance and psychological effects of goal setting in sport: A systematic review and meta ana... Evidence specifically concerning attention fits the proposed mechanism. In the studies reviewed by Williamson and colleagues, process and performance goals both showed potential attentional benefits. One particularly relevant earlier study by Kingston and Hardy found that golfers receiving a process oriented intervention significantly improved their concentration, whereas the performance goal group did not show a comparable improvement. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: The performance and psychological effects of goal setting in sport: A systematic review and meta ana... Research on learning motor skills supplies another clue. Zimmerman and Kitsantas studied goal setting during dart throwing practice and found advantages when learners initially concentrated on process before subsequently shifting towards outcome. Later work in physical education did not reproduce a straightforward advantage of one goal type over another, but it did find that self recording improved performance and that goal setting groups made more technique related and adaptive judgements than controls. In other words, the evidence points towards a self regulation cycle — set an actionable target, execute it, observe what happened and adjust — rather than suggesting that merely attaching the label “process goal” guarantees improvement. [ERIC] eric.ed.gov EJ543828 Developmental Phases in Self Regulation: Shifting from Process Goals to Outcome Goals., Journal of Educational Psychology, 1997... There are good reasons to keep the size of the claimed benefit in perspective. A systematic review of applied sport interventions found only 27 eligible studies, with 63% containing fewer than 30 participants. Researchers also noted inconsistent implementation of goal setting theory and relatively few direct comparisons between process, performance and outcome goals. The later meta analysis likewise warned that including only published studies could inflate estimated effects because statistically significant findings are more likely to appear in the published literature. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. The defensible conclusion is therefore narrower than “process goals are proven to be best”. They are a promising and practically coherent way of directing attention towards execution , supported by experimental and applied evidence, but the precise advantage depends on the task, performer and way the goal is constructed. Explanatory illustration 2 When focusing on the process becomes too much There is an important complication: concentrating on a process is not automatically helpful merely because it is controllable. Consider an experienced golfer. During early learning, deliberately thinking about grip, alignment or another component of the movement may be useful. After thousands of repetitions, however, much of the movement has become automatic. Under pressure, consciously trying to control each component can interfere with that automatic coordination. This tension became known in the literature as the process goal paradox : process goals are supposed to help performers focus, yet a highly detailed process instruction can sometimes make a skilled performer focus on mechanics that normally run without conscious supervision. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Evidence for the effectiveness of holistic process goals for learning and performance under pressure ScienceDirect... Researchers consequently distinguished part process goals from holistic process goals . A part goal directs attention towards a particular technical component. A holistic goal uses a broader cue representing the movement as a whole — for example, the word “smooth” for the overall feel of a golf putt. The latter aims to direct attention without forcing the performer to consciously reconstruct an automated skill piece by piece. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Experiments by Mullen and colleagues provide support for this distinction. Across three experiments involving motor tasks under increased anxiety, performers using a holistic movement focus outperformed those using part oriented process goals. A later race driving experiment similarly"},{"title":"Progress Logs","url":"/progress-logs/","description":"Physically recording progress can make daily behaviour easier to evaluate and is associated with stronger goal attainment.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Goals vs Actions","level":3,"text":"Progress Logs Does Writing Down Progress Make Goals Work Better? Goals vs Actions Progress Logs Progress Logs Does Writing Down Progress Make Goals Work Better? Physically recording progress can make daily behaviour easier to evaluate and is associated with stronger goal attainment. Physically recording progress can make daily behaviour easier to evaluate and is associated with stronger goal attainment. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence What progress monitoring experiments found Why recorded behaviour gives faster feedback What to log without obsessing over results self improvement that works goals vs actions progress logs Introduction Writing down progress can make goals work better, but the strongest evidence supports a specific version of the idea: recording what you actually do, frequently enough to compare it with a target, can improve follow through . It is not the act of handwriting a goal that appears important. The useful ingredient is creating a record of progress that makes behaviour visible and reviewable. Explanatory illustration 1 The clearest evidence comes from a 2016 meta analysis of 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions that encouraged people to monitor progress produced a moderate improvement in goal attainment, and the benefit was larger when monitoring information was physically recorded. Crucially for behaviour based self improvement, the analysis also found that monitoring behaviour was especially effective at changing behaviour itself. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That makes a progress log more than a motivational diary. Used carefully, it is a feedback system: Did I perform the action I said I would perform? TEDxAtlanta Teresa Amabile The Progress Principle Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube What progress monitoring experiments found Benjamin Harkin and colleagues' meta analysis in Psychological Bulletin provides the most directly relevant dataset. The researchers included experiments in which participants were randomly assigned to an intervention intended to increase monitoring of goal progress or to a control condition. Across the 138 studies, the monitoring interventions substantially increased monitoring itself (standardised effect size d = 1.98) and improved subsequent goal attainment ( d = 0.40, 95% confidence interval 0.32 to 0.48). Changes in how frequently people monitored their progress statistically mediated the improvement in goal attainment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The studies covered different kinds of goals, with much of the evidence coming from health related behaviours such as physical activity , diet, weight management, smoking and management of medical conditions. That breadth is useful, but it also means the headline effect should not be interpreted as a guaranteed improvement of the same size for every personal goal. It is an average across varied interventions and populations. [American Psychological Association] apa.org American Psychological AssociationFrequently monitoring progress toward goals increases chance of successOctober 28, 2015... Published: October 28, 2015 For written progress logs, one moderator is particularly revealing. When participants physically recorded the information obtained through monitoring — for example, by entering it in a diary — the effect on goal attainment was larger ( d = 0.43) than when the information was monitored but not recorded ( d = 0.29). When researchers restricted the comparison to objectively measured goal attainment, the corresponding effects were d = 0.57 versus 0.23. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Does Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental EvidenceOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 That finding needs careful wording. The meta analysis does not establish that pen and paper possesses a special psychological advantage over every digital alternative. “Physically recorded” meant that monitoring information was recorded rather than merely observed; a structured digital log can serve the same basic self monitoring function. The evidence therefore supports leaving a record , not a claim that handwriting is inherently superior. There was another notable result. Monitoring that was reported to somebody else ( d = 0.47) or conducted publicly ( d = 0.55) was associated with larger effects on goal attainment than private monitoring ( d = 0.19). A private log can still be useful, but the result suggests why coaching reports, training diaries shared with a coach or other forms of accountability can sometimes add leverage. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Does Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental EvidenceOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 The Power of Written Goals + Accountability Channel: George Kao Open on YouTube Why recorded behaviour gives faster feedback A written log changes the information available to you. Without one, a statement such as “I have been studying quite consistently” depends heavily on memory and impression. With a simple record — “retrieval practice session completed: yes/no” — the question becomes empirical. Harkin and colleagues described self recording as a way to examine progress over time and potentially identify actions that help or hinder it. More generally, monitoring works by allowing the present state to be compared with some reference point: a desired target or previous performance. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence.July 4, 2026... Published: July 4, 2026 This matters especially when an outcome goal has been translated into controllable behaviours. Suppose the outcome is to finish a 10K faster. Recording the race time tells you whether the outcome eventually improved. Recording completion of planned training sessions tells you much sooner whether the behaviour intended to produce that improvement is happening. The meta analysis found a striking “matching” pattern. Interventions that prompted people to monitor behaviour had a large reliable effect on behaviour ( d = 0.79), but no reliable effect on outcomes in the smaller subset testing that relationship ( d = 0.14). Conversely, monitoring outcomes affected outcomes ( d = 0.62) but did not reliably change behaviour ( d = 0.17). [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Does Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental EvidenceOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 That distinction is easy to overlook. A person trying to save £5,000 can repeatedly inspect the bank balance, just as somebody trying to improve fitness can repeatedly inspect body weight or a race time. Those measurements answer an important question — is the result changing? — but they do not necessarily answer the more immediately actionable question: am I consistently performing the behaviour intended to change it? A behaviour log closes that information gap. “Transferred £100 on payday”, “completed the planned run”, “did 30 minutes of retrieval practice” and “wrote for 45 minutes” are observations about actions under comparatively direct control. They can therefore expose a breakdown in execution before the slower moving outcome makes the problem obvious. Research on people's natural monitoring habits also suggests that this is not automatic. In three studies of personal goals, Chang and colleagues found that people reported finding progress easier to monitor when goals were represented in quantifiable terms. Monitoring was harder for some domains, including work and study, where progress may be less immediately measurable than something such as weight or exercise . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Which Factors Are Associated with Monitoring Goal Progress? Which Factors Are Associated with Monitoring Goal Progress? PubMedMarch 24, 2017... Published: March 24, 2017 The practical implication is not that everything needs a sophisticated metric. Often a deliberately crude measure makes an otherwise fuzzy behaviour observable: session completed or not; minutes practised; applications submitted; pages revised; planned transfer made. Explanatory illustration 2 What to log without obsessing over results A useful progress log should make behaviour easier to evaluate, not turn self improvement into continuous surveillance. The research supports monitoring as a behaviour change technique, but it does not imply that more data, more elaborate dashboards or more frequent"},{"title":"Mental Boost","url":"/mental-boost/","description":"Even a single bout of exercise can produce modest short-term gains in attention, memory, processing and executive function.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Physical Capacity","level":3,"text":"Mental Boost What Happens to Your Thinking After Exercise? Physical Capacity Mental Boost Mental Boost What Happens to Your Thinking After Exercise? Even a single bout of exercise can produce modest short term gains in attention, memory, processing and executive function. Even a single bout of exercise can produce modest short term gains in attention, memory, processing and executive function. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism Which cognitive abilities improve after a single session How large and reliable the average effects appear to be Using movement before demanding mental work realistically self improvement that works physical capacity mental boost Introduction A short bout of exercise can sharpen some aspects of thinking soon afterwards. The effect is not dramatic, and it does not turn an ordinary working day into a state of exceptional mental performance, but the average result across a large research literature is positive: attention, executive function, memory and information processing can all improve modestly after a single exercise session. A 2025 meta review combining 30 previous meta analyses, 383 unique studies and 18,347 participants estimated an overall small to medium benefit, with the clearest effects when cognition was measured after exercise rather than during it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of acute exercise on cognitive function: A meta review of 30 systematic reviews with meta analyses PubMedJanuary 30, 2025... Published: January 30, 2025 Explanatory illustration 1 That makes exercise unusual among self improvement habits. Some of its value accumulates over months and years, but part of the return can arrive on the same day. For someone about to study, write, solve problems or return to mentally demanding work, a short movement break may therefore serve two purposes at once: physical activity in its own right and a temporary improvement in readiness for cognitive work. Which mental abilities improve after one session? The strongest recent overview is the 2025 Psychological Bulletin meta review of acute exercise — meaning one exercise session rather than a training programme. Across the meta analyses it examined, acute exercise improved overall cognitive performance by a standardised mean difference of 0.33. Benefits appeared across all five broad categories reported: attention (0.37), executive function (0.36), memory (0.23), information processing (0.20) and mixed or other cognitive measures (0.36). [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of acute exercise on cognitive function: A meta review of 30 systematic reviews with meta analyses PubMedJanuary 30, 2025... Published: January 30, 2025 Those categories translate into different everyday mental demands. Attention concerns selecting and maintaining focus. Executive functions include processes such as suppressing an inappropriate response, holding information in working memory and switching between rules or tasks. Information processing includes how efficiently someone detects, interprets and responds to information. Memory effects concern learning, storage or retrieval, depending on when exercise occurs relative to the material being learned. The pattern is important because the post workout effect is not simply “feeling more awake”. Controlled experiments use cognitive tasks and compare performance under exercise and control conditions. An individual participant data meta analysis of acute aerobic exercise, for example, found beneficial post exercise effects even after accounting for participants' performance before exercise. Interestingly, people who began with poorer cognitive performance tended to benefit more. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2024 Bayesian meta analysis provides a useful reality check. It synthesised 113 studies involving 4,390 healthy young adults and found only a small improvement in overall cognition, with an effect size of g = 0.13. The more conspicuous change was in reaction time: exercise made responses faster, whereas there was no credible overall improvement in accuracy. Within executive function tasks, the analysis found evidence of improvements in inhibition and working memory. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A systematic review and Bayesian meta analysis provide evidence for an effect of acute physical activity on cognition... This distinction between faster thinking and more accurate thinking is one of the most useful ways to understand the literature. Earlier meta analytic work likewise found that speed accounted for much of the cognitive benefit, particularly around moderate intensity exercise, while changes in accuracy were much smaller. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A post exercise boost should therefore not be interpreted as a temporary increase in intelligence. It is closer to a modest change in the efficiency with which some mental operations are performed. Grow your brain by moving your body—just 10 minutes a day Wendy Suzuki Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Why can moving your body change thinking so quickly? There is no single proven “exercise molecule” that explains the immediate cognitive effect. Exercise simultaneously alters arousal, neurotransmitter activity, circulation, metabolism and signalling between peripheral tissues and the brain. Researchers have several overlapping explanations, and the evidence is stronger for the existence of the cognitive effect than for any one complete causal account of it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The acute exercise cognition interaction: From the catecholamines hypothesis to an interoception model PubMedOctober 16, 2021... Published: October 16, 2021 One prominent mechanism involves catecholamines , particularly dopamine and noradrenaline. These neurotransmitters help regulate arousal, attention and cognitive control. Models of the acute exercise–cognition relationship propose that the physiological stimulation produced by exercise can move the brain into a state more favourable for processing salient information and exercising top down control. Moderate exercise has often fitted this account particularly well, although newer models emphasise that intensity, perceived effort, motivation and the cognitive task all interact rather than following one simple “more exercise equals more arousal equals better thinking” rule. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Developing the catecholamines hypothesis for the acute exercise cognition interaction in humans: Lessons from animal studies PubM... Exercise also changes cerebral blood flow and neurobiological signalling . Brain derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), a protein involved in synaptic plasticity, is frequently discussed in this context. Exercise can increase circulating BDNF and other signalling molecules, while greater cardiac output and changes in cerebral circulation may contribute to the brain's response. However, measuring BDNF in the blood does not straightforwardly reveal what is happening inside the brain, so it is premature to attribute the immediate cognitive improvement to BDNF alone. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Molecular mechanisms underlying physical exercise induced brain BDNF overproduction PMC... A 2026 scoping review of acute exercise, cognitive performance and neurobiological markers illustrates both the plausibility and the uncertainty. Studies have reported post exercise changes involving BDNF, lactate, catecholamines and prefrontal activation alongside improvements particularly in executive function and processing speed. But variation in exercise protocols, biomarker measurements and testing times makes it difficult to establish a neat causal chain from a particular biological change to a particular improvement in thinking. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The safest interpretation is therefore mechanistic but not reductionist: exercise briefly changes the physiological state in which cognition operates , and several interacting brain and body systems probably contribute to the resulting performance changes. Boost Attention & Memory with Science Based Tools Dr. Wendy Suzuki Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube How large and reliable is the boost? The evidence is convincing enough to say that there is an average acute benefit, but not convincing enough to promise a noticeable improvement every time someone exercises. Estimates vary substantially according to which studies, populations and cognitive tests are included. The 2025 meta review's overall estimate of 0.33 is encouraging, but the 2024 analysis restricted to healthy young adults produced a considerably smaller estimate of 0.13. An influential 2012 meta analysis of 79 studies also found a small overall effect, g = 0.097, including small benefits both immediately after exercise and following a delay. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of acute"},{"title":"Exercise and Sleep","url":"/exercise-and-sleep/","description":"Regular exercise can improve sleep quality and efficiency, making it easier to sustain concentration and effort elsewhere.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Physical Capacity","level":3,"text":"Exercise and Sleep Can Exercise Make Better Sleep Easier to Sustain? Physical Capacity Exercise and Sleep Exercise and Sleep Can Exercise Make Better Sleep Easier to Sustain? Regular exercise can improve sleep quality and efficiency, making it easier to sustain concentration and effort elsewhere. Regular exercise can improve sleep quality and efficiency, making it easier to sustain concentration and effort elsewhere. Self Improvement That Works evidence bounded topic dataset evidence What exercise research finds about sleep quality Why better sleep can increase capacity for other goals Building enough activity to help without exhausting yourself self improvement that works physical capacity exercise and sleep Introduction Exercise can make better sleep easier to sustain without turning sleep into another complicated self improvement project. Across randomised trials, regular exercise improves how people rate their sleep and produces smaller but measurable improvements in objective sleep efficiency — the proportion of time in bed actually spent asleep. A 2024 meta analysis of 81 randomised controlled trials involving 6,193 participants found that exercise improved both measures, while newer evidence suggests the people starting with poorer sleep may have the most to gain. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of exercise on sleep quality in general population: Meta analysis and systematic review PubMedNovember 12, 2024... Published: November 12, 2024 Explanatory illustration 1 The practical message is simpler than the research literature can make it appear. There is no clearly established single best workout, exact hour or elaborate exercise prescription that everyone needs for sleep. Walking, other aerobic activity, resistance exercise and mixed approaches have all shown benefits in research. For someone using physical activity to support studying, working or everyday responsibilities, the useful target is therefore repeatable activity that helps sleep without creating so much fatigue, scheduling difficulty or bedtime stimulation that the routine defeats its purpose. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Effects of different types of exercise on sleep quality based on Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index in middle aged and ol... 6 tips for better sleep Sleeping with Science, a TED series Channel: TED Open on YouTube What exercise research finds about sleep quality The strongest broad evidence supports a modest but genuine sleep benefit rather than a dramatic cure. In the 81 trial meta analysis, exercise reduced scores on the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) by an average of 1.77 points. The PSQI is a questionnaire covering aspects such as perceived sleep quality, time taken to fall asleep, sleep duration and daytime problems. Across trials that objectively measured sleep, exercise also increased sleep efficiency by an average of 4.81 percentage points. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of exercise on sleep quality in general population: Meta analysis and systematic review PubMedNovember 12, 2024... Published: November 12, 2024 Those two findings are worth distinguishing. People do not merely report feeling somewhat better about their sleep; objective measurements provide some support for an improvement too. At the same time, the effects are not identical across every sleep measure or every population. A 2026 systematic review and Bayesian meta analysis covering 200 trials and 23,523 adults found a beneficial average effect on subjective sleep quality but only a small improvement in objective sleep efficiency. It also judged the overall certainty of its evidence to be very low, reflecting limitations and variation across the underlying studies. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Determinants and interrelations of exercise effects on sleep quality: A multimethod meta analysis PubMed... This is one reason not to overinterpret rankings of particular workouts. The 2024 general population analysis ranked mind body exercise highest for subjective sleep quality and aerobic exercise highest for objective sleep efficiency. Another 2024 network meta analysis suggested that combined exercise might perform especially well under particular combinations of frequency, intensity and programme length, but its authors explicitly cautioned that the number of studies was limited. Among middle aged and older adults, a separate analysis instead ranked aerobic exercise highest for overall PSQI scores. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of exercise on sleep quality in general population: Meta analysis and systematic review PubMedNovember 12, 2024... Published: November 12, 2024 The disagreement is useful rather than disappointing: it argues against believing that sleep requires one optimised exercise formula. Different reviews include different populations, interventions and outcomes, and network rankings can make small or uncertain differences look more decisive than they are. The stable finding underneath them is that several forms of regular activity can help. There is also evidence that the starting point matters. The large 2026 review found baseline sleep quality to be one of the main determinants of response: people whose sleep was already reasonably good had less room to improve, whereas those beginning with poorer sleep were more likely to experience a clinically important change. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Determinants and interrelations of exercise effects on sleep quality: A multimethod meta analysis PubMed... This makes intuitive sense and puts the average effects in perspective. Exercise should not be expected to transform every good sleeper's night, but it may be more consequential when sleep is already a weak point. This Sucks, But It'll Help Seniors Sleep Better Instantly Channel: Sleep Doctor Open on YouTube Why better sleep can increase capacity for other goals The value of this effect becomes clearer when exercise is treated as support for other goals rather than another performance target. Sleep deficiency interferes with learning, concentration, reaction time, decision making and emotional regulation. The US National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute notes that people who are sleep deficient can take longer to complete tasks and make more mistakes at work or school. [NHLBI, NIH] nhlbi.nih.gov NHLBI, NIHSleep Deprivation and Deficiency What Are Sleep Deprivation and Deficiency? NHLBI, NIHMarch 24, 2022... Published: March 24, 2022 Experimental evidence makes the connection more concrete. A 2024 meta analysis of 44 studies found that even one night in which adults were restricted to a two to six hour opportunity for sleep substantially increased sleepiness and impaired sustained attention, including slower responses and more attentional lapses. It did not find significant impairment in every cognitive measure examined, so the lesson is not that one short night destroys mental performance. Rather, sustained attention — precisely the capacity needed to remain engaged with many ordinary tasks — appears particularly vulnerable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Impact of one night of sleep restriction on sleepiness and cognitive function: A systematic review and meta analysis PubMedApril... That creates a plausible pathway from movement to greater day to day capacity: regular activity → somewhat better sleep → fewer sleep related obstacles to sustained effort. The important word is somewhat . Exercise cannot guarantee concentration, motivation or academic and professional performance. Nor does evidence that exercise improves sleep prove that every downstream improvement in a person's work is caused by that sleep change. The useful conclusion is narrower: if physical activity makes sleep more efficient or consistently better, it may reduce one source of friction that makes other goals harder. This also changes how exercise itself can be evaluated. A 30 minute walk that never produces impressive fitness statistics can still be useful if it is easy to repeat and contributes to better nights and more functional days. In a self improvement system, that indirect return may matter more than maximising the difficulty of each session. Explanatory illustration 2 Building enough activity to help without exhausting yourself A sleep supporting exercise routine does not need its own complicated optimisation system. UK physical activity guidance recommends that adults accumulate at least 150 minutes of moderate intensity activity each week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a combination, while doing muscle strengthening activity on at least two days. But those figures are public health targets, not a minimum dose below which movement suddenly becomes useless. UK guidance explicitly says that any activity is better than none and notes that benefits are measurable at lower activity levels. WHO likewise advises inactive people to start with small amounts and increase frequency, intensity and duration"},{"title":"Too Much Exercise","url":"/too-much-exercise/","description":"More activity is not always more useful when fatigue, soreness or injury begins to undermine work, recovery and daily responsibilities.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Physical Capacity","level":3,"text":"Too Much Exercise When Does More Exercise Stop Helping Your Goals? Physical Capacity Too Much Exercise Too Much Exercise When Does More Exercise Stop Helping Your Goals? More activity is not always more useful when fatigue, soreness or injury begins to undermine work, recovery and daily responsibilities. More activity is not always more useful when fatigue, soreness or injury begins to undermine work, recovery and daily responsibilities. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic mechanism Why health benefits eventually flatten at higher activity volumes Signs that training is exceeding current capacity Balancing improvement, recovery and the rest of life self improvement that works physical capacity too much exercise Introduction Exercise supports a wider life only while the training itself remains compatible with that life. More activity can still improve fitness after a person has met basic health recommendations, but the returns become progressively smaller, while the costs in time, fatigue and recovery can continue to rise. Large cohort studies suggest that much of the mortality benefit is already captured at moderate activity volumes; beyond that point, doing more may still serve sporting goals, but it becomes harder to justify simply as “more health”. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov WHO Guidelines on Physical Activity and Sedentary Behaviour NCBI Bookshelf... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction is important for self improvement. The relevant question is not whether a high training volume is inherently bad. It is whether the current dose is producing enough useful adaptation to justify what it takes away from sleep, concentration, physical comfort, relationships and ordinary responsibilities. Persistent fatigue, declining performance, worsening sleep, recurrent injury or an inability to accommodate normal life demands are therefore not badges of commitment. They are evidence that training and recovery may no longer be in a productive balance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of the overtraining syndrome: joint consensus statement of the European College of Sport Scien... Why the health payoff eventually flattens Public health recommendations are deliberately expressed as ranges rather than as instructions to maximise exercise. The World Health Organization recommends that adults accumulate 150–300 minutes of moderate intensity aerobic activity each week, 75–150 minutes of vigorous activity, or an equivalent combination, as well as muscle strengthening activity on at least two days. Adults can exceed those aerobic amounts for additional benefits, but this does not imply that every extra hour produces the same return. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov WHO Guidelines on Physical Activity and Sedentary Behaviour NCBI Bookshelf... The dose response evidence makes the distinction clearer. A prospective study of 116,221 US adults followed for up to 30 years found that people reporting 150–299 minutes a week of moderate leisure time activity had substantially lower mortality than those doing none. Moving to 300–599 minutes was associated with some further reduction, but at 600 minutes or more there was no clear additional reduction in mortality. For vigorous activity, roughly 150–300 minutes a week was associated with near maximal mortality benefit in that study. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Long term leisure time physical activity intensity and all cause and cause specific mortality: a prospective cohort o... An earlier pooled analysis reached a similar broad conclusion from more than 660,000 adults. Mortality benefits reached a plateau at roughly three to five times the recommended minimum, but the incremental gain was modest compared with simply reaching the minimum: the estimated mortality risk reduction was about 31% around the recommended minimum and 39% at the apparent benefit threshold. Importantly, the study did not find excess mortality among people reporting ten or more times the minimum. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com JAMA NetworkLeisure Time Physical Activity and Mortality: A Detailed Pooled Analysis of the Dose Response Relationship Lifestyle Behavi... That last point prevents a common misreading. A flattening benefit curve does not mean that high volume exercise suddenly becomes dangerous. Nor does it mean that endurance athletes should stop training. High volume can be entirely rational when the goal is to run a marathon, improve cycling performance or compete in a sport. It simply changes the calculation. Once large health benefits have already been captured, another training session may offer a comparatively small additional health return while still demanding another hour of time and another dose of recovery. This is why “more exercise” and “better self improvement” eventually diverge. Someone training for performance may willingly accept fatigue and opportunity costs because performance is the objective. Someone exercising mainly to have more energy, think clearly, stay healthy and function well in everyday life has a different optimisation problem. For that person, exercise has begun to undermine its purpose when its marginal benefits are outweighed by its effects on the rest of the week. Large gains in minimal time: the minimal effective dose for hypertrophy│Dr. Brad Schoenfeld Channel: FoundMyFitness Clips Open on YouTube When training exceeds current capacity There is no universal number of weekly kilometres, gym sessions or training hours at which exercise becomes “too much”. Capacity varies with training history, age, intensity, nutrition, sleep, previous injury and what else is happening in a person's life. A workload that is manageable during a quiet month may be excessive during a period of poor sleep, demanding work or heavy caring responsibilities. Sports medicine therefore treats the relationship between load and recovery as more informative than exercise volume in isolation. The joint consensus statement from the European College of Sport Science and American College of Sports Medicine distinguishes useful short term overload from maladaptation. “Functional overreaching” can temporarily reduce performance before sufficient recovery produces improvement. If overload continues without adequate recovery, however, non functional overreaching and, in more severe cases, overtraining syndrome can develop. Persistent fatigue, performance decline and mood disturbance are characteristic concerns, although there is no single laboratory marker that reliably diagnoses overtraining syndrome. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of the overtraining syndrome: joint consensus statement of the European College of Sport Scien... For an ordinary exerciser, that makes patterns more useful than any magic threshold. Warning signs become more persuasive when several appear together or persist rather than resolving after normal recovery: Performance is falling despite continued effort. A difficult session is normal; a sustained decline while training remains high suggests that adding more load may not be producing adaptation. Fatigue is leaking into ordinary life. Training that repeatedly leaves someone too depleted to work effectively, study, care for family or complete routine tasks is consuming rather than supplying usable capacity. Normal soreness is becoming persistent pain or recurrent injury. Training creates stress that tissues must adapt to. Repeatedly increasing the demand faster than that adaptation occurs can turn a productive stimulus into a problem. Sleep is deteriorating. This matters particularly because sleep is itself part of the recovery process. A 2024 systematic review and meta analysis of endurance overreaching found a small but statistically significant reduction in objectively measured sleep efficiency after intensified training, although subjective sleep ratings did not show a significant change and the underlying studies had important risk of bias limitations. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Does overreaching from endurance based training impair sleep: A systematic review and meta analysis PMC... Rest stops feeling permissible. If missed sessions provoke disproportionate guilt or exercise repeatedly takes precedence over injury recovery and important commitments, the problem is no longer adequately described by weekly minutes alone. Research on exercise dependence identifies maladaptive, compulsive exercise as distinct from simply enjoying or doing a large amount of sport. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Exercise dependence: a systematic review Exercise dependence: a systematic review ScienceDirect... These signs are deliberately broader than muscle soreness. The real boundary is functional: training is exceeding current capacity when recovery can no longer keep pace and the resulting deficit starts appearing elsewhere. Explanatory"},{"title":"Start Smaller","url":"/start-smaller/","description":"Public-health targets are destinations, not minimum thresholds, so inactive adults can begin smaller and increase activity gradually.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Physical Capacity","level":3,"text":"Start Smaller You Do Not Need to Start at 150 Minutes Physical Capacity Start Smaller Start Smaller You Do Not Need to Start at 150 Minutes Public health targets are destinations, not minimum thresholds, so inactive adults can begin smaller and increase activity gradually. Public health targets are destinations, not minimum thresholds, so inactive adults can begin smaller and increase activity gradually. Self Improvement That Works implementation single policy policy intervention What the weekly activity guidelines actually recommend Why smaller increases still count How graded progression can turn a small start into a durable routine self improvement that works physical capacity start smaller Introduction You do not need to start at 150 minutes of exercise a week. For an adult who is currently doing little physical activity , public health guidelines are better understood as a destination to work towards than as an entry requirement. Both the World Health Organization (WHO) and the UK Chief Medical Officers explicitly say that some activity is better than none; WHO advises inactive people to begin with small amounts and gradually increase how often, how long and how intensely they are active. [NCBI] Increase one or more dimensions progressively. Open source on nih.gov. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters for self improvement that lasts. Starting below the guideline is not a failed version of an exercise programme. It can be the intended first stage: choose an amount that is manageable at current fitness, repeat it reliably, then build as capacity develops. Research on health outcomes and behaviour change supports this graded approach, while exercise safety guidance gives a further reason not to make a sudden jump from inactivity to demanding training. [bmj.com] bjsm.bmj.com British Journal of Sports MedicineNon occupational physical activity and risk of cardiovascular disease, cancer and mortality outcomes: a... Explanatory illustration 3 What the weekly guidelines actually recommend For adults, the current UK guidance is to aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a combination. Adults should also aim to strengthen the major muscle groups on at least two days each week. Moderate activity includes efforts such as brisk walking or cycling that increase breathing while still allowing conversation. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UK... The easily missed part is what comes immediately before those numerical targets. The UK Chief Medical Officers say adults should aim to be active every day and emphasise that “any activity is better than none”. The updated supporting evidence goes further: health benefits are measurable below 150 minutes a week, at lower intensities and even at lower frequencies. It specifically warns that threshold recommendations such as 150 minutes can appear to be a barrier to people who are starting from low activity levels. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK U K Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines UK Chief Medical Officers' physical activity guidelines GOV.UK... WHO takes the same approach. Its guidelines recommend 150–300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity a week, or 75–150 minutes of vigorous activity or an equivalent combination, but also state that adults falling short of those recommendations still benefit from being active. Such adults should start with small amounts and progressively increase frequency, intensity and duration. [NCBI] Increase one or more dimensions progressively. Open source on nih.gov. This changes how the target should be used. Someone presently doing virtually no deliberate activity does not have only two meaningful states — “meeting 150 minutes” and “failing”. Ten minutes of walking that was not happening before is an increase in activity. Thirty or sixty minutes across a week can be an intermediate stage rather than a disappointing score. The disappearance of the old idea that exercise must occur in bouts of at least ten minutes reinforces the point. US physical activity guidance removed its former ten minute bout requirement because evidence supports benefits from smaller amounts of moderate to vigorous activity accumulated through the day. [Health.gov] odphp.health.gov questions answers Activity Guidelines Questions & Answers odphp.health.govNovember 19, 2025... Published: November 19, 2025 The Best Beginner Workout: How to Start Exercising Safely! 30 Minute Full Length Home Routine Channel: jessicasmithtv Open on YouTube Why smaller increases still count The relationship between physical activity and health is not a cliff at 150 minutes. Large observational studies generally find a dose response curve: moving from very little activity to somewhat more is associated with meaningful reductions in health risk, with further activity adding benefits. A 2023 dose response meta analysis in the British Journal of Sports Medicine combined evidence from 196 articles representing 94 cohorts and more than 30 million participants. It found that the relationship between non occupational activity and several outcomes was non linear. In practical terms, the largest potential gains occurred when people with inactive lifestyles added relatively small amounts of activity. The authors estimated that appreciable population health benefits could arise from reaching only half the recommended level, while further gains continued towards and beyond the full guideline. As an observational synthesis, this cannot prove that every increment directly causes a particular reduction in an individual's risk, but its dose response pattern is important for interpreting public health targets. [British Journal of Sports Medicine] bjsm.bmj.com British Journal of Sports MedicineNon occupational physical activity and risk of cardiovascular disease, cancer and mortality outcomes: a... Device measured activity tells a similar story. A harmonised meta analysis of 36,383 mainly middle aged and older adults found lower all cause mortality among people doing more total activity, including light intensity activity. The relationship was non linear, with the largest difference appearing between the least active group and the next activity category. [BMJ] bmj.com Open source on bmj.com. Step count research provides an especially intuitive example. A 2025 systematic review and dose response meta analysis covering 57 studies found that even modest daily step totals were associated with better health outcomes. Compared with 2,000 steps a day, 4,000 was associated with substantially lower all cause mortality risk; around 7,000 steps was associated with sizeable improvements across several outcomes. The researchers did not present 7,000 as a magical cut off — risk generally continued to decline as steps increased — but argued that it could be a more achievable target than the familiar 10,000 step figure for some people. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. These studies should not be converted into a promise that adding a precise number of minutes or steps produces a precise personal benefit. They do support the more useful principle behind the guidelines: progress from inactivity has value before the full recommendation has been reached . That is why “every minute counts”, the wording used in current UK public health material, is more than motivational language. It reflects a shift away from treating exercise as valuable only after a prescribed threshold has been crossed. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Open source on gov.uk. What EXACTLY Is Progressive Overload? Channel: PictureFit Open on YouTube Explanatory illustration 2 Build the routine before chasing the number A graded start also makes sense as a behaviour change strategy. The immediate objective for someone who has been inactive is not simply to accumulate as many exercise minutes as possible in week one. It is to establish an amount of activity that can be repeated and then expanded. A systematic review and meta analysis of interventions for healthy inactive adults found that physical activity interventions produced small but significant improvements that persisted for at least six months. Among the behaviour change techniques associated with better outcomes were “graded tasks”: beginning with achievable demands and progressively increasing them. At longer term follow up, effective interventions also tended to include action planning, prompts or cues, practice and clear instruction. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis PubMed Central (PMC)Are physical activity interventions for healthy inactive adults effective in promoting behavior change and maintenanc... This does not prove that one particular progression formula is best for everyone. It does support a practical implementation pattern: 1. Establish the baseline. Start from what is actually happening now rather"},{"title":"Automatic Starts","url":"/automatic-starts/","description":"Complex routines may never become fully automatic, but making the first action cue-driven can still make them happen more reliably.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Small Habits","level":3,"text":"Automatic Starts Automate the Start, Not the Whole Routine Small Habits Automatic Starts Automatic Starts Automate the Start, Not the Whole Routine Complex routines may never become fully automatic, but making the first action cue driven can still make them happen more reliably. Complex routines may never become fully automatic, but making the first action cue driven can still make them happen more reliably. Self Improvement That Works mechanism single mechanism mechanism The difference between habitual starting and habitual execution Why complex routines still require deliberate regulation Choosing a first action that reliably launches the routine self improvement that works small habits automatic starts Introduction For a complex routine, the most realistic habit target may be the start rather than the whole sequence . A workout, study session, weekly planning ritual or meal preparation routine contains too many decisions, changing conditions and separate actions to become wholly automatic. Yet the cue that launches it can become much more automatic. Habit researchers call this distinction habitual instigation versus habitual execution : the first concerns automatically selecting and beginning an activity; the second concerns automatically progressing through its component steps. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction changes what “building a habit” can sensibly mean. Success does not require reaching a state where an elaborate routine runs without thought. Research suggests that complex behaviours continue to draw on deliberate self regulation even when their starts are strongly habitual. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The useful goal is narrower: make a reliable cue produce a reliable first action , then use ordinary deliberate control to do the rest . Starting automatically is not the same as doing everything automatically Consider going for a run. There are at least two ways in which it could feel habitual. At a familiar cue — perhaps finishing work — you might find yourself automatically beginning the running routine. That is habitual instigation. Alternatively, the individual actions after that point might flow with little conscious direction: changing clothes, putting on shoes, leaving the house, following a familiar route. That is habitual execution. Gardner, Phillips and Judah formalised this distinction in 2016, arguing that the same underlying habit process can operate at different levels of an action. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych HubHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency Gardner 2016... The distinction is more than terminology. In their prospective study of 229 students, measures of habitual instigation were consistently more predictive of subsequent frequency of flossing, snacking and eating breakfast than measures of habitual execution. The authors concluded that the relationship between habit and repeated behaviour appeared to be attributable mainly to instigation rather than automatic execution. [Open Research Surrey] openresearch.surrey.ac.uk Open Research SurreyHabitual instigation and habitual execution: Definition, measurement, and effects on behaviour frequency University... An earlier exercise study likewise found that habitual exercise instigation, rather than habitual execution, predicted how frequently healthy adults exercised. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Habitual exercise instigation (vs. execution) predicts healthy adults' exercise frequency PubMedJanuary 1, 2016... Published: January 1, 2016 A later study tested the idea directly across behaviours that differed in complexity. Participants considered drinking sugary drinks, checking a phone, using social media and using public transport, with public transport rated as the most complex. Habitual instigation predicted behaviour frequency across this range, whereas habitual execution did not. The researchers' interpretation was that complexity does not prevent a habit from helping to regulate whether an action episode gets started . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Habit and behavioural complexity: habitual instigation and execution as predictors of simple and complex behaviours Scienc... This provides a more useful model of a complex habit. Instead of imagining: cue → entire routine runs automatically think: cue → first action starts readily → deliberate and partly practised processes carry the routine forward The first link can become strong even when everything after it remains cognitively demanding. The Science of Making & Breaking Habits Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Why complex routines still need deliberate regulation Complexity creates demands that repetition cannot necessarily remove. A complex behaviour contains distinguishable steps, takes time, may require planning and effort, and often has to adapt to circumstances. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibrarySome habits are more work than others: Deliberate self‐regulation strategy use increases with behavioral complexity... A morning planning session, for example, may reliably begin when someone opens their laptop after breakfast, but what happens next depends on today's calendar, unfinished work, priorities and new information. Automatic execution of one invariant sequence would not even be desirable. Recent evidence makes this limitation unusually clear. Blair Saunders and Kimberly More recruited 239 UK adults and had them identify personally relevant simple and complex behaviours, both established and not yet established. Simple examples included handwashing and flossing; complex examples included moderate or vigorous exercise, active travel, preparing vegetables and recycling. Participants also reported the deliberate strategies they used to regulate these behaviours. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Strategy use increased as behavioural complexity increased. Importantly, that relationship persisted even for complex behaviours that participants reported starting with high automaticity . At particularly high levels of complexity, the usual difference in strategy use between habitually and non habitually instigated behaviour disappeared. In other words, an automatic start did not eliminate the need for deliberate regulation once the activity became sufficiently complicated. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That finding guards against a common misconception about successful routines. Having to think during a well established routine is not evidence that the habit has failed. A person can habitually begin exercising and still decide how hard to train; habitually begin studying and still choose which problem deserves attention; habitually start a weekly review and still reason carefully about next week's priorities. The automatic and deliberate parts can therefore complement one another. The habit reduces one recurring self regulation problem — getting into the activity at all — without pretending to remove the judgement required to perform it well. Explanatory illustration 2 Choose a first action that reliably launches the routine The practical challenge is deciding what, exactly, should become cue driven. “Exercise after work” is still an umbrella behaviour. A stronger habit start design specifies both a recognisable cue and an observable first response. For example: after putting the dinner plate in the dishwasher → open the language learning material; after making the first morning drink → open the planning document; after arriving home from work → change into exercise clothes; after brushing teeth in the evening → put the book on the bed and open it; after sitting at the desk for a scheduled writing session → open the working document. These examples deliberately stop near the beginning. The target is not to automate every subsequent decision. It is to reduce the gap between encountering the cue and entering the routine. There are two evidence based reasons for being precise. First, habits are learned through repeated associations between contexts and responses. In a six week study of new study habits, and a second dataset drawn from a habit building app, greater context stability was associated with greater automaticity and better attainment of the intended repetition goal. The researchers concluded that clearly specifying when and where a habit occurs is a useful starting point for establishing a stable context. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. Second, planning research shows that linking a specific situation to a specific response can help people translate intentions into action. A major meta analysis of 94 independent tests"},{"title":"Pressure Risks","url":"/pressure-risks/","description":"Reporting can stop helping when judgement, pressure or surveillance makes people hide failures, avoid check-ins or focus on pleasing the observer.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Accountability","level":3,"text":"Pressure Risks When Does Accountability Become Too Much Pressure? Pressure Risks Pressure Risks When Does Accountability Become Too Much Pressure? Reporting can stop helping when judgement, pressure or surveillance makes people hide failures, avoid check ins or focus on pleasing the observer. Reporting can stop helping when judgement, pressure or surveillance makes people hide failures, avoid check ins or focus on pleasing the observer. Self Improvement That Works critique risk bounded topic population context How judgement can distort progress reporting Keeping accountability focused on controllable processes Preserving visibility, autonomy and usefulness together self improvement that works accountability pressure risks Introduction Accountability becomes too much pressure when the cost of admitting a miss starts to compete with the purpose of reporting it. If a check in feels like a test of character, competence or worth, people have stronger reasons to soften bad news, omit awkward details, delay the conversation or concentrate on whatever will look best to the observer. At that point, accountability may still produce reports, but the reports become less useful. Explanatory illustration 1 This is not an argument against social accountability. Research shows that making goals known to other people can increase commitment, while well designed coaching can strengthen self monitoring without damaging the relationship. The important distinction is between supportive visibility and threatening evaluation . Accountability works best when another person can see what happened and help the individual respond to it; it becomes risky when failure carries enough judgement, embarrassment or consequence that managing the observer's impression becomes a competing goal. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When goals are known: The effects of audience relative status on goal commitment and performance PubMed... How judgement can distort progress reporting The basic vulnerability is familiar from research on social desirability bias: people do not always report behaviour as it happened when truthful disclosure could make them look bad. Reviews of sensitive question research find that respondents tend to underreport socially undesirable behaviour and overreport desirable behaviour, with misreporting influenced by the social situation rather than being simply a fixed tendency of dishonest individuals. Fear of embarrassment, exposure or repercussions can all affect what gets disclosed. [DOI] doi.org Determinants of social desirability bias in sensitive surveys: a literature review Quality & Quantity Springer Nature LinkNovember... An accountability check in can create a milder version of the same problem. Imagine agreeing to report five planned study sessions to a friend every Sunday. After completing only two, there are several possible responses: report two honestly; count a ten minute glance at the textbook as a “session”; explain at length why the week should not count; avoid the check in; or report a more flattering number. The more the relationship communicates that five means disciplined and two means disappointing, the more reporting itself acquires stakes. That does not mean ordinary accountability routinely causes lying. Direct evidence on dishonesty in informal self improvement partnerships is limited. But adjacent experimental evidence shows why the risk deserves attention. In a classic goal setting experiment, Maurice Schweitzer, Lisa Ordóñez and Bambi Douma found that participants who had not met specific goals were more likely to behave unethically than people instructed simply to do their best. The effect appeared even without financial incentives and was particularly pronounced when people narrowly missed the target. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Academy of Management JournalsGoal Setting as a Motivator of Unethical Behavior Academy of Management JournalJune 1, 2004... Published: June 1, 2004 Later research has reinforced the broader warning that intense performance goals can create unintended incentives. Reviews link demanding outcome goals with mechanisms including moral disengagement and greater willingness to take questionable shortcuts, while experimental work has found that repeated high performance goals can increase unethical behaviour over successive rounds. These studies largely concern workplaces and laboratory tasks rather than personal habit check ins, so they should not be treated as direct estimates of what happens between accountability partners. They do show, however, that making a target more consequential can change more than effort: it can change how people respond when their actual performance is inconvenient. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Immoral goals: how goal setting may lead to unethical behavior Immoral goals: how goal setting may lead to unethical behavior ScienceDirect... The distinction matters because a reporting system can appear successful while its information quality deteriorates . If the person begins redefining misses, selecting only favourable metrics or concealing lapses, the observer sees a tidier trajectory precisely when accurate feedback is most needed. Research on managers' expectations about performance reporting illustrates the concern: in four studies involving 738 managers, respondents generally recognised that performance goals and low performance could increase the likelihood of overstating accomplishments. [sjwop.com] sjwop.com Open source on sjwop.com. There is also newer evidence that uncertainty surrounding evaluation can matter. A 2025 series of six studies on performance reporting found that people who were particularly intolerant of uncertainty experienced more negative emotion when evaluation was uncertain, which in turn was associated with impulsive performance misreporting. The setting is organisational rather than personal self improvement, but it highlights a useful design principle: an accountability partner who reacts unpredictably can make disclosure itself stressful. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Open source on sciencedirect.com. Why Accountability Feels So Hard (And How to Make It Work for You) Channel: Zen Habits Open on YouTube Pressure can rise without anyone explicitly threatening you A partner does not have to issue punishments for accountability to become evaluative. Simply making a goal known to somebody can increase what psychologists call evaluation apprehension : concern about how that person will judge your performance. Experiments examining publicised goals found that goal commitment and subsequent performance increased when the audience was perceived as relatively high status, with evaluation apprehension helping to explain the effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When goals are known: The effects of audience relative status on goal commitment and performance PubMed... That is the useful and dangerous edge of social accountability. Caring what another person thinks can make “I will do it” more consequential. But the same concern can make “I did not do it” harder to say. More pressure is therefore not automatically better accountability. The risk becomes especially clear when monitoring starts resembling surveillance. A meta analysis covering 94 independent samples and 23,461 workers found no evidence that electronic performance monitoring improved performance overall, while monitoring was associated with greater stress. More transparent and less invasive monitoring was associated with more favourable attitudes. A separate meta analysis of 70 independent samples likewise found small increases in stress and decreases in job satisfaction, no overall performance relationship, and a small positive relationship with counterproductive work behaviour. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. Personal accountability is plainly different from workplace surveillance: a voluntarily chosen friend seeing a weekly habit total is not equivalent to an employer tracking workers electronically. The comparison is useful because it shows why visibility and control should not be treated as the same thing . Increasing the amount of information another person can see does not guarantee better behaviour, particularly if the monitoring reduces autonomy or feels invasive. Why learning to fail can teach us to thrive I Amy Edmondson I RSA REPLAY Channel: Royal Society of Arts Open on YouTube Keep accountability focused on controllable processes One of the clearest safeguards is to make the report primarily about actions the person can choose , rather than outcomes they can only partly control. The supportive accountability model developed by David Mohr, Pim Cuijpers and Kenneth Lehman argues that effective human support should involve clear expectations, participation by the person being supported and an emphasis on process rather than outcome accountability. It also stresses trust, benevolence and an understandable benefit to the"},{"title":"Goals vs Progress","url":"/goals-vs-progress/","description":"Announcing an aspiration and reporting completed work are different social acts, and research gives good reason not to treat them as interchangeable.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Accountability","level":3,"text":"Goals vs Progress Should You Tell People Your Goals or Your Progress? Accountability Goals vs Progress Goals vs Progress Should You Tell People Your Goals or Your Progress? Announcing an aspiration and reporting completed work are different social acts, and research gives good reason not to treat them as interchangeable. Announcing an aspiration and reporting completed work are different social acts, and research gives good reason not to treat them as interchangeable. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic mechanism Why public intentions can create premature recognition What newer goal disclosure studies add Turning aspiration announcements into evidence based check ins self improvement that works accountability goals vs progress Introduction Telling people what you intend to do and telling them what you have actually done are not the same form of accountability. An aspiration announcement — “I’m going to become a writer”, “I’m training for a marathon”, “I’m starting a business” — can create commitment or attract useful support. But in some circumstances, especially when the announcement itself signals a desired identity, social recognition may provide part of the psychological reward before the work has happened. A classic series of experiments found exactly that pattern for identity related intentions. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science When Intentions Go Public Association for Psychological ScienceWhen Intentions Go Public Psychological Science APS... Explanatory illustration 1 Reporting completed behaviour works differently. “I wrote 3,200 words this week” or “I completed three of my four planned runs” puts evidence into the social interaction. A large meta analysis found that deliberately increasing progress monitoring improved goal attainment, with larger effects when monitoring outcomes were reported to others or made public. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The practical lesson is therefore more precise than either “tell everyone your goals” or “keep your goals secret”. Use disclosure when it creates useful commitment or support, but make ongoing accountability revolve around evidence of action rather than recognition for an intended future. Why public intentions can create premature recognition The strongest reason for caution about announcing goals comes from Peter Gollwitzer and colleagues' 2009 paper When Intentions Go Public . Across four experiments, the researchers studied identity related behavioural intentions : actions connected with becoming the sort of person participants wanted to be. Their examples included law students intending to undertake activities associated with becoming successful lawyers. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science When Intentions Go Public Association for Psychological ScienceWhen Intentions Go Public Psychological Science APS... When other people noticed these identity relevant intentions, strongly committed participants subsequently acted on them less intensively than participants whose intentions had effectively gone unnoticed. The pattern appeared both in a week long field study and in laboratory experiments measuring whether people seized opportunities to work towards the identity. A fourth experiment supported the proposed mechanism: social acknowledgement of the intention produced a premature sense of possessing the desired identity. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science When Intentions Go Public Association for Psychological ScienceWhen Intentions Go Public Psychological Science APS... That mechanism matters because it explains why some announcements may feel suspiciously satisfying. Suppose someone wants to become a novelist. Saying “I’m writing a novel” does more than communicate a plan: listeners may begin treating that person as a writer. If being recognised as a writer was part of the reward the goal promised, some of that reward has arrived without another page being written. The result is often popularised too broadly as “science says never tell anyone your goals”. That is not what the experiments established. The 2009 research concerned identity related intentions , and the detrimental effect was found among participants strongly committed to the relevant identity goal. It does not demonstrate that every disclosed target — losing five kilograms, saving £2,000 or completing a course — automatically becomes less likely to happen. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological Science When Intentions Go Public Association for Psychological ScienceWhen Intentions Go Public Psychological Science APS... That limitation is crucial because other research has found beneficial effects from public commitment. A weight loss study, for example, reported that making commitments public was associated with greater motivation and better weight loss outcomes. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library Public commitment as a motivator for weight loss Wiley Online LibraryPublic commitment as a motivator for weight loss Nyer 2010 Psychology & Marketing Wiley Online LibraryDecembe... The research literature is therefore less contradictory once the social acts being studied are separated: receiving recognition for an identity, making yourself accountable for a target, recruiting support and reporting subsequent performance can produce different consequences. How To Actually Get Things Done (implementation intentions) Channel: Better Than Yesterday Open on YouTube What newer goal disclosure studies add Later work makes it difficult to defend a simple rule that goals should remain secret. In a four study investigation published in 2020, Howard Klein and colleagues found that the status of the audience influenced the consequences of disclosure. Sharing goals with someone perceived as higher in status increased commitment more than disclosure to a lower status audience, and that greater commitment could translate into better performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When goals are known: The effects of audience relative status on goal commitment and performance PubMed... This suggests that an announcement can sometimes create a genuine reputational stake rather than merely premature satisfaction. Telling a respected supervisor that you intend to submit a piece of work by Friday, for example, is psychologically different from broadcasting “big things coming” to followers who reward the aspiration itself. The content may concern the same future goal, but the audience and expected consequences differ. An even newer set of studies complicates the story further. Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler and Tayler Wells followed 1,035 participants across four longitudinal studies of personally important goals. Rather than concentrating on artificial laboratory targets, they examined ordinary goals and disclosure to other people, including close relationships. People who shared goals tended to report receiving more support and investing more effort; experimental instructions to share also produced evidence that increased support could contribute to greater pursuit effort. [DOI] doi.org Goals Out Loud: Telling Others About a Goal Increases Support Received and Facilitates Goal Pursuit Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler, Ta... The findings were not uniformly dramatic. In one preregistered study, being assigned to share rather than keep a goal private increased reported support but produced no significant direct difference in effort or subjective progress. There was, however, an indirect association between the sharing condition and effort through increased support. [DOI] doi.org Goals Out Loud: Telling Others About a Goal Increases Support Received and Facilitates Goal Pursuit Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler, Ta... This is useful precisely because it resists the tempting headline that merely saying a goal aloud makes it happen. The newer research also identifies a mechanism absent from the “premature recognition” story: other people can actually help . Once someone knows that you are trying to run a marathon, finish a qualification or change career, they can remind you, encourage you, provide information or resources, and respond to setbacks. In the Peetz studies, participants were particularly likely to report receiving support from people to whom they had disclosed the goal. [DOI] doi.org Goals Out Loud: Telling Others About a Goal Increases Support Received and Facilitates Goal Pursuit Johanna Peetz, Roger Buehler, Ta... So the historical comparison is not “old research says disclosure is bad; new research says it is good”. The better reading is that goal disclosure is not one"},{"title":"Social Visibility","url":"/social-visibility/","description":"Making progress visible to someone else can strengthen the feedback loop between what you intended to do and what you actually did.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Accountability","level":3,"text":"Social Visibility Why Does Someone Else Seeing Progress Matter? Accountability Social Visibility Social Visibility Why Does Someone Else Seeing Progress Matter? Making progress visible to someone else can strengthen the feedback loop between what you intended to do and what you actually did. Making progress visible to someone else can strengthen the feedback loop between what you intended to do and what you actually did. Self Improvement That Works evidence single dataset dataset evidence From private monitoring to socially consequential monitoring What the progress monitoring meta analysis found Why visibility helps without requiring public posting self improvement that works accountability social visibility Introduction Another person seeing your progress can improve follow through because it changes monitoring from a purely private act into one with social consequences. Instead of simply noticing whether you did what you intended, you know that the result will be visible to somebody else. Experimental evidence suggests that this distinction matters. Explanatory illustration 1 The strongest broad evidence comes from a meta analysis of 138 experimental studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions that increased progress monitoring improved goal attainment overall, but the effects were larger when monitoring results were reported to another person or made public than when they remained private. Importantly, reporting to just one other person was enough to show this pattern; the evidence does not imply that broadcasting progress publicly is necessary. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 That makes social visibility a potentially useful addition to self improvement: not because another person must police the goal, but because the evidence of what happened becomes harder to overlook, reinterpret or quietly abandon. The Psychology Of Human Motivation Ayelet Fishbach Channel: Chris Williamson Open on YouTube From private monitoring to socially consequential monitoring Progress monitoring already performs an important self regulatory function. It tells you where your behaviour or outcome currently stands relative to where you want it to be. In the 2016 meta analysis led by Benjamin Harkin and Thomas Webb, interventions designed to increase monitoring produced a very large increase in monitoring frequency, with an average effect size of d = 1.98. More importantly for actual goal pursuit, they produced a smaller but meaningful improvement in goal attainment, d = 0.40. Changes in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the improvement in attainment. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Social visibility appears to strengthen this basic feedback loop. The researchers distinguished three forms of monitoring: progress monitored publicly; progress monitored privately and then reported to at least one other person; and progress monitored privately without being reported. Their example of the middle category was deliberately ordinary: a person could weigh themselves privately and then telephone a research assistant with the result. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. That distinction matters because it separates being accountable to somebody from performing a goal in front of an audience . A private spreadsheet sent to a coach, a weekly step count given to a friend or a completed session total reported to a colleague can all make progress socially visible without making it broadly public. Why might that alter behaviour? Harkin and colleagues proposed several possible explanations, including public commitment, personal accountability and concerns about how one's performance will appear to others. The common feature is that the information no longer exists solely inside the person's private self assessment. Someone else can now observe whether actions and intentions match. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. This interpretation fits the broader idea of supportive accountability developed by David Mohr and colleagues. Their model defines accountability around an expectation that a person may have to account for actions or inaction. It argues that human support is particularly useful when expectations are clear and process focused and when the person receiving progress information is regarded as trustworthy, benevolent and credible. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth... The qualification is important. The social element is not simply “pressure”. Visibility can create a reason to keep tracking and acting, while the other person's role can make that information consequential. The supportive accountability model explicitly cautions against framing monitoring as threatening surveillance; it instead emphasises agreed expectations and a relationship that provides benefits to the person pursuing the goal. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth... Thinkers vs. Do ers: Why You Suck At Following Through Channel: HealthyGamerGG Open on YouTube What the progress monitoring meta analysis found The most revealing result for social accountability lies in the meta analysis's moderator analysis. When progress was monitored privately and not reported , the estimated effect on goal attainment was d = 0.19. When monitoring was conducted privately but reported to at least one other person , the effect was d = 0.47. Public monitoring produced an estimated d = 0.55. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Those figures should not be treated as though the researchers ran one giant experiment randomly assigning nearly 20,000 people to private, reported and public tracking. They did not. These are subgroup estimates across different experiments whose interventions had different characteristics. The underlying studies were also highly heterogeneous, and the authors noted that better quality studies generally tended to produce smaller effects. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. So the evidence supports a careful conclusion: progress monitoring interventions tended to work better when the resulting information became visible to someone else . It does not establish that visibility itself accounts for the entire difference between the subgroup effect sizes. That caution is particularly important for the public monitoring result. Only three studies, representing roughly 214 participants in the goal attainment analysis, fell into the public category. By contrast, the private but reported estimate drew on 95 studies and more than 12,000 participants. The evidence base is therefore much stronger for the ordinary act of reporting progress than for conspicuously pursuing a goal in front of a group. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. The authors themselves called for experiments directly comparing reported and unreported monitoring so researchers could identify whether mechanisms such as accountability, public commitment or presentational concerns actually explain the advantage. In other words, the meta analysis identifies a strong and useful pattern, but it does not settle exactly why social visibility works. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Another result reinforces the general idea that progress becomes more consequential when it leaves the realm of fleeting private thought. Physically recording monitoring information was associated with a larger goal attainment effect ( d = 0.43) than not recording it ( d = 0.29). The authors suggested that records may make progress easier to remember and harder to ignore or reinterpret. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk Open source on whiterose.ac.uk. Taken together, these findings suggest a progression from weak to stronger feedback: merely intending to improve provides little information; monitoring creates feedback; recording preserves that feedback; and reporting it gives the feedback a social audience. Explanatory illustration 2 A direct test of someone else seeing the record More recent experimental research offers a particularly clean example of this principle. A 2024 study in Obesity randomly assigned 322 adults taking part in a 12 week behavioural weight loss programme to conditions that differed in whether their coaches could access their digital self monitoring data. Everyone was asked to track weight, physical activity and food"},{"title":"Choosing a Partner","url":"/choosing-a-partner/","description":"Accountability is more likely to stay useful when expectations are agreed together and the person receiving reports is trusted rather than feared.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Accountability","level":3,"text":"Choosing a Partner When Does an Accountability Partner Actually Help? Choosing a Partner Choosing a Partner When Does an Accountability Partner Actually Help? Accountability is more likely to stay useful when expectations are agreed together and the person receiving reports is trusted rather than feared. Accountability is more likely to stay useful when expectations are agreed together and the person receiving reports is trusted rather than feared. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention Trust and legitimacy in supportive accountability Agreeing expectations without creating surveillance Responding to missed commitments with problem solving self improvement that works accountability choosing a partner Introduction An accountability partner helps when they make progress visible without turning visibility into surveillance . The best choice is usually someone you trust to want your goal to succeed, who respects that the goal remains yours, and who can respond to an imperfect week with curiosity rather than judgement. Research on “supportive accountability” describes much the same combination: clear expectations work best when the person receiving reports is regarded as trustworthy, benevolent and legitimate, and when monitoring has been agreed rather than imposed. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth... Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because accountability and control are not the same intervention. Research on goal support finds that autonomy supportive behaviour — listening, understanding the goal holder's perspective and preserving choice — is associated with better goal progress than more directive approaches, while controlling interpersonal styles are associated with poorer personal and relationship outcomes. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Distinguishing autonomous and directive forms of goal support: their effects on goal progress, relationship quality, and subjective... The useful partner therefore creates a reason to report honestly, not a reason to hide a bad result. Trust matters more than toughness A common intuition is that the ideal accountability partner is the person least likely to “let you off the hook”. That can be useful only up to a point. Accountability depends on the expectation that somebody will notice whether an agreed action happened; it does not require that person to punish, shame or interrogate you when it did not. The supportive accountability model developed by David Mohr, Pim Cuijpers and Kenneth Lehman is particularly useful here. It argues that the person holding someone accountable needs legitimacy . Part of that legitimacy is instrumental: they should have enough knowledge or experience to make their involvement credible when expertise matters. But another part is relational: the person should appear trustworthy, benevolent and genuinely concerned with the goal holder's interests. Demands from somebody perceived as illegitimate can backfire, particularly when they are experienced as attempts to control behaviour. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research JMIRJournal of Medical Internet Research Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth... For everyday self improvement, this suggests that the most impressive or disciplined person in your circle is not automatically the best partner. Someone can know a great deal about training, studying or business and still be a poor choice if admitting “I did none of it this week” feels unsafe. Conversely, a supportive friend does not need professional expertise when the job is simply to receive an agreed progress report and help you think through obstacles. Research on close relationships points in the same direction. Across three studies following personal goals for three months, Richard Koestner and colleagues distinguished autonomy support , characterised by empathic perspective taking, from directive support , which involved giving positive guidance. Autonomy support consistently predicted greater goal progress and was also associated with better relationship quality and wellbeing. Its association with progress was explained in part by stronger autonomous motivation — pursuing the goal because it felt personally chosen rather than externally imposed. Directive support showed only a marginal association with progress overall. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Distinguishing autonomous and directive forms of goal support: their effects on goal progress, relationship quality, and subjective... A later study of 710 people in romantic relationships made the distinction even clearer. Autonomy supportive, directive supportive and controlling behaviour emerged as distinguishable styles. Autonomy support predicted favourable outcomes including cooperation and perceived goal progress, whereas controlling behaviour predicted more goal related conflict and poorer relationship quality and wellbeing. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Empirically distinguishing interpersonal styles within romantic relationships: What is helpful or harmful when having a goal... So a useful screening question is not simply, “Will this person challenge me?” It is “Can I tell this person the unvarnished truth about my progress?” If the relationship makes failure embarrassing enough to conceal, the reporting mechanism starts losing the information it needs to work. How to choose the right accountability partner the person who can truly help you make change Channel: Dr. Haley Perlus on Sport & Performance Psychology Open on YouTube Agree expectations without creating surveillance Good accountability begins with a small agreement rather than an open ended licence to monitor somebody's life. The supportive accountability literature recommends establishing expectations early, making them specific and process oriented, involving the person being supported in setting them, and explaining why any monitoring is useful. Monitoring should have the person's agreement and should not carry an implied threat of negative consequences. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Supportive Accountability: A Model for Providing Human Support to Enhance Adherence to eHealth Interventions PMC... In practice, the agreement can be remarkably modest: What gets reported: an observable behaviour, such as “three runs completed”, “four writing sessions done” or “applications sent”. When: perhaps a Friday message or a ten minute Sunday call, rather than unpredictable checking. What the partner does: acknowledge the report, ask one or two useful questions and help troubleshoot when requested. What the partner does not do: demand evidence, chase repeatedly between check ins, contact other people about the goal or independently expand what is being monitored. Who owns changes: the goal holder can revise an unrealistic target; the partner can question the reasoning, but does not unilaterally raise the standard. This structure preserves the valuable part of social accountability — another person will know what happened — while putting boundaries around that person's authority. The partner is receiving information under an agreed arrangement, not becoming a supervisor. There is evidence that meaningful visibility can work without damaging rapport when it is embedded in a supportive relationship. In a 12 week randomised behavioural weight loss study, coaches either could or could not see participants' digitally recorded weight, eating and physical activity data. When coaches had access, participants reported greater supportive accountability and recorded eating and physical activity more consistently; average weight loss was 6.2% versus 5.3%. Importantly, the groups did not significantly differ in working alliance, treatment satisfaction or attendance. [DOI] doi.org Open source on doi.org. That study involved trained coaches in a structured programme, so it should not be treated as proof that giving a friend access to every metric is beneficial. Its more useful lesson is narrower: visibility itself need not feel hostile when people understand the arrangement and the person seeing the information is there to help . In ordinary accountability partnerships, sharing less information may be enough. A weekly total can create social visibility without exposing an entire diary, fitness dashboard or productivity log. The communication method should also fit the person rather than maximise contact for its own sake. A 2024 qualitative study of young people receiving adherence support found that phone coaching could build a stronger personal connection but was also more disruptive, while texts were easier and more flexible; participants' preferences varied considerably. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A partner who"},{"title":"Binary Tracking","url":"/binary-tracking/","description":"A binary record can answer the most important adherence question without turning self-monitoring into a burdensome data project.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Written Records","level":3,"text":"Binary Tracking When a Simple Tick Box Is Enough Written Records Binary Tracking Binary Tracking When a Simple Tick Box Is Enough A binary record can answer the most important adherence question without turning self monitoring into a burdensome data project. A binary record can answer the most important adherence question without turning self monitoring into a burdensome data project. Self Improvement That Works implementation bounded topic policy intervention What binary tracking captures well When extra metrics add little value When a richer record is justified self improvement that works written records binary tracking Introduction For a recurring habit, the most useful record is often the smallest one that can answer the question you will actually ask later: did I do the planned behaviour on this occasion, or not? A tick beside Monday’s walk, a yes/no entry after language practice, or a marked square for taking a daily medication creates evidence without requiring a miniature research project. Explanatory illustration 1 That simplicity is not a claim that more data are always useless. It is a rule about matching the record to the decision. If the goal is “practise guitar after dinner on Monday, Wednesday and Friday”, duration, mood and difficulty may add little to the first adherence question. Three planned occasions require three answers. Research on progress monitoring broadly supports recording behaviour, while habit formation studies routinely use simple daily occurrence measures because repetition in a stable context is itself important information. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 The important qualification is that the evidence specifically comparing binary habit trackers with richer tracking systems is limited. The stronger evidence supports self monitoring in general; studies using tick sheets and yes/no diaries show that binary recording is a workable implementation of that principle, not that a tick box is universally superior. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Self regulatory behavior change techniques in interventions to promote healthy eating, physical activity, or weight l... What binary tracking captures well Binary tracking works best when the intended behaviour has a clear definition and a clear opportunity. “Read for ten minutes before bed” can become a yes/no question. “Be more intellectual” cannot. The tracker therefore needs a behavioural threshold established in advance: what counts as done, and on which occasions is it supposed to happen? That produces a particularly legible measure of adherence . If the plan called for four sessions this week and three happened, the record says 3/4. If the behaviour was planned every weekday and the ticks repeatedly disappear on Fridays, the pattern is visible without reconstructing the week from memory. This is the distinctive value of a binary record: it preserves the occurrence pattern while discarding details that may not be needed. There is good reason to treat recording as more than clerical work. Harkin and colleagues' meta analysis covered 138 experimental studies and 19,951 participants. Interventions designed to increase monitoring increased goal attainment overall, and effects were larger when monitoring information was physically recorded. The review concerns progress monitoring broadly rather than tick box habit trackers specifically, so it cannot establish that binary tracking is the optimal format. It does support the underlying policy of turning progress into an external record instead of relying entirely on recollection. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta analysis of the experimental evidence PubMedFebruary 1, 2016... Published: February 1, 2016 Habit research provides a more concrete example. In Phillippa Lally and colleagues' well known 12 week field study, 96 volunteers selected an eating, drinking or activity behaviour to repeat daily in a consistent context and recorded whether they had performed it. Repetition was associated with increasing automaticity , although the rate varied greatly between individuals. Crucially, missing one opportunity did not materially derail the habit formation process. That finding is useful when reading a tick box record: an empty square is information about one missed repetition, not evidence that the entire effort has failed. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryHow are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world Lally 2010 European Journal of Social Psycho... Later habit studies have continued to use binary behaviour measures. A longitudinal field study published in Frontiers in Psychology , for example, asked participants each day whether they had performed their chosen behaviour and whether they had done so in their chosen context. A 2023 study of fruit consumption habits similarly used yes/no diary entries to record whether participants ate fruit after a designated meal. These designs illustrate exactly what binary measurement can preserve: whether the behaviour occurred and, when relevant, whether it occurred in the intended cueing context . [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers How to Form Good Habits? A Longitudinal Field Study on the Role of Self Control in Habit FormationMarch 27, 2020... Published: March 27, 2020 For ordinary self improvement, that suggests a compact rule: track the smallest unit that corresponds to the commitment. “Floss after brushing at night” needs one daily box. “Strength train three times each week” needs a record of the three intended sessions. “Study Spanish for 20 minutes after breakfast” can be recorded as done only when the predefined minimum is reached. The simplicity comes from deciding what counts beforehand, not from making the standard vague. How I plan my week goals, strategic to do lists, habit tracking + my FULL planning system 📔 Channel: Bec Stewart Open on YouTube When extra metrics add little value Tracking systems easily accumulate fields: duration, intensity, quality, difficulty, mood, location, streak length, notes and numerical scores. Each can be useful for a particular question. But collecting information merely because it might become interesting later changes the tracker from an adherence record into a data collection project. For a stable recurring behaviour, extra metrics add little when they would not change the next decision. Imagine a person whose immediate objective is simply to establish three weekly gym visits. If a 25 minute session and a 50 minute session both satisfy the current commitment, exact duration may be secondary. The first useful question is whether the planned visits are actually happening. Once that becomes reliable, training volume or progression may deserve separate measurement. This “minimum sufficient record” approach has indirect empirical support, although the evidence should not be overstated. In a small randomised pilot involving 72 adults, researchers compared standard calorie logging with a simplified system that monitored high calorie foods. After six months, the groups did not differ significantly in dietary tracking days, dietary intake or weight loss; 43.2% of the standard monitoring group and 42.9% of the simplified group achieved at least 5% weight loss. The intervention was about weight management rather than habit tick boxes, but it demonstrates an important principle: more detailed recording does not automatically produce a better outcome, and lower information monitoring can sometimes remain useful. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A pilot randomized trial of simplified versus standard calorie dietary self monitoring in a mobile weight loss interv... The broader evidence is appropriately cautious. A meta review covering 30 previous meta analyses and 409,185 participants found inconsistent evidence about individual self regulation techniques, including self monitoring, across diet, physical activity and weight interventions. The authors also stressed methodological problems with trying to isolate individual techniques from complex, multi component interventions. Binary tracking should therefore be treated as a practical implementation choice, not as a scientifically proven shortcut that guarantees behaviour change. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Nor is it safe to assume that every additional question necessarily destroys adherence. Reviews of ecological momentary assessment — repeated questionnaires completed during daily life — find high average compliance despite substantial variation in assessment schedules. One meta analysis of 477 articles reported average compliance of about 79%, while another systematic review found insufficient consistent evidence to identify"},{"title":"Tracking Effects","url":"/tracking-effects/","description":"Monitoring can sometimes change behaviour itself by making the action more noticeable and highlighting gaps between plans and performance.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Written Records","level":3,"text":"Tracking Effects Can Tracking a Habit Change the Habit Itself? Written Records Tracking Effects Tracking Effects Can Tracking a Habit Change the Habit Itself? Monitoring can sometimes change behaviour itself by making the action more noticeable and highlighting gaps between plans and performance. Monitoring can sometimes change behaviour itself by making the action more noticeable and highlighting gaps between plans and performance. Self Improvement That Works critique risk single mechanism mechanism What measurement reactivity means Why awareness may alter behaviour When tracking stops being passive observation self improvement that works written records tracking effects Introduction A habit log does not always sit outside the behaviour it records. Writing down a workout, checking a step count or answering a daily question about drinking can make that behaviour more noticeable and can sometimes change what happens next. Researchers call this measurement reactivity : the act of measuring a behaviour alters the behaviour, thoughts or feelings being measured. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Measurement Reactivity in a Randomized Clinical Trial Using Self Reported Data PMCNovember 16, 2016... Published: November 16, 2016 Explanatory illustration 1 For self improvement, that creates an important twist. A written record may make progress harder to misremember, but the record is not necessarily a neutral historical document. It can also function as a small intervention. Seeing “0 sessions this week” makes a gap between intention and performance unusually concrete; recording a successful day can reinforce attention to the goal. Experimental evidence supports a real effect of monitoring on goal attainment, although research specifically isolating mere measurement generally finds smaller and less consistent effects. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence. White Ro... The useful conclusion is therefore not that every habit tracker automatically improves behaviour. It is that once tracking repeatedly directs attention towards a habit and its relationship to a goal, it may stop being passive observation. What measurement reactivity means Measurement reactivity occurs when measuring something contributes to changing it. In behavioural research this creates a methodological problem: researchers may want to discover what people normally do, yet questionnaires, diaries, wearable devices or repeated prompts can make participants think about precisely the behaviour the researchers hoped merely to observe. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Measurement Reactivity in a Randomized Clinical Trial Using Self Reported Data PMCNovember 16, 2016... Published: November 16, 2016 The effect can arise with surprisingly little intervention. The closely related question–behaviour effect asks whether simply questioning people about a behaviour changes their subsequent behaviour. An updated systematic review covering 43 measurement versus no measurement studies and 104,388 participants found a statistically significant but small overall effect, with a standardised mean difference of 0.06. There was substantial variation between studies and evidence of publication bias, so the result is better understood as evidence that measurement can influence behaviour than as a promise that asking questions reliably changes habits. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Asking questions changes health related behavior: an updated systematic review and meta analysis PMC... Repeated digital monitoring provides another test. A 2022 systematic review of digital, in the moment measurement found small but meaningful pooled reactivity effects in the studies suitable for meta analysis, with Cohen's d values around 0.27–0.30. However, 81% of the included studies concerned physical activity indicators, while evidence for other behaviours was sparse and mixed. The authors concluded that digital momentary measurement can itself be reactive rather than offering a completely unobtrusive window onto everyday behaviour. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review and meta analysis of studies of reactivity to digital in the moment measurement of health behaviour PubMed... That distinction matters when interpreting a personal record. If someone starts logging daily walks and subsequently walks more, the log may accurately document the improvement while simultaneously helping to cause it. Those two functions are compatible: the record can be both evidence and intervention. Hawthorne Effect Explained Why People Change When They Are Being Watched Psychology in 5 Minutes Channel: 5 Minute Learnings Open on YouTube Why awareness may alter behaviour The most plausible pathway begins with attention . Habits are useful partly because familiar actions can occur with relatively little deliberate thought. Recording them reverses some of that invisibility. The person has to notice whether the target behaviour happened, classify it and often confront a number or mark representing the result. Research on measurement reactivity proposes several overlapping mechanisms rather than one settled explanation. Measurement can increase awareness of one's behaviour, make a health or behavioural goal more salient, trigger social desirability concerns, or expose a discrepancy between current and desired performance. Once that discrepancy is noticed, ordinary self regulation can follow: the person changes behaviour to reduce the gap. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Asking questions changes health related behavior: an updated systematic review and meta analysis PMC... This is closely related to why deliberate progress monitoring can work as a behaviour change technique. Harkin and colleagues' meta analysis examined 138 randomised studies involving 19,951 participants. Interventions intended to increase progress monitoring substantially increased monitoring itself and produced a moderate overall improvement in goal attainment ( d = 0.40). Changes in monitoring frequency statistically mediated the improvement, and effects were larger when monitoring information was physically recorded. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence. White Ro... Those findings should not be mistaken for a pure estimate of measurement reactivity: these were interventions designed to promote progress monitoring, not experiments in which every other possible influence of measurement was removed. But they demonstrate the practical overlap. The same act that produces a record — checking progress against a target and writing the result down — is also an established form of self regulation. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Does Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental EvidenceOctober 19, 2015... Published: October 19, 2015 A simple example shows why. Suppose the plan is to read for 30 minutes every evening. Without tracking, missing Tuesday may have little psychological presence by Friday. With a visible log, entering the first miss makes it explicit; entering a second produces a pattern. The tracker has not supplied motivation from nowhere. It has changed the information environment in which the next decision is made. Explanatory illustration 2 When a tracker becomes part of the habit Physical activity research provides unusually clear demonstrations because behaviour can be measured while varying how much feedback the participant receives. In one study, 63 adults first wore a sealed pedometer while believing that it was a body posture monitor. They subsequently experienced sealed, visible and visible plus diary conditions. Average activity was about 8,362 steps per day during covert monitoring but 9,635 during the condition in which participants could see the device and logged their steps. The diary condition produced significantly more steps than covert or sealed monitoring. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Increasing our understanding of reactivity to pedometers in adults Increasing our understanding of reactivity to pedometers in adults PubMed... A related study of 50 adults reported roughly 9,541 daily steps while the pedometer was sealed and its real purpose concealed, compared with 11,385 when participants could see the count and recorded it in an activity log. The design cannot identify which element — knowing the device's purpose, seeing the feedback or completing the log — caused how much of the increase, but that is precisely the practical point: making measurement visible and actionable can change the thing being measured. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med"},{"title":"Failure Patterns","url":"/failure-patterns/","description":"Dates of missed sessions can expose recurring weak points such as Fridays, weekends, locations or other predictable circumstances.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Written Records","level":3,"text":"Failure Patterns What Your Missed Sessions Can Reveal Written Records Failure Patterns Failure Patterns What Your Missed Sessions Can Reveal Dates of missed sessions can expose recurring weak points such as Fridays, weekends, locations or other predictable circumstances. Dates of missed sessions can expose recurring weak points such as Fridays, weekends, locations or other predictable circumstances. Self Improvement That Works governance bounded topic dataset evidence Turning omissions into usable evidence Spotting repeated day and context patterns Using patterns to revise the routine self improvement that works written records failure patterns Introduction A missed session is more useful when it has a date attached to it. Three missed workouts can look like ordinary inconsistency; three consecutive missed Fridays suggest a specific weakness in the routine. The same principle applies to study sessions, writing practice, budgeting reviews or any repeated behaviour: omissions can reveal whether failure is scattered or concentrated around particular days, places, times and disruptions. Explanatory illustration 1 That distinction matters because routines are context dependent. Research on habit formation finds that repeating behaviour in stable contexts supports automaticity and goal attainment, while changes in time, place and surrounding circumstances can make execution less reliable. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Context Stability in Habit Building Increases Automaticity and Goal Attainment PubMedJune 10, 2022... Published: June 10, 2022 A written record therefore does more than establish how many sessions happened. By retaining the dates and circumstances of the sessions that did not happen, it can expose where the routine repeatedly loses its support. Explanatory illustration 3 Turning omissions into usable evidence The simplest record — completed or missed, plus the date — already permits a useful question: are the omissions randomly distributed, or do they cluster? Adding one or two context fields can make the answer considerably more informative. A missed session entry might record the planned time or place and a short reason such as “worked late”, “travelling” or “usual room unavailable”. The aim is not to create an elaborate diary. It is to retain enough information to distinguish an isolated miss from a recurring failure condition. This approach fits the broader evidence for self monitoring. Harkin and colleagues' meta analysis covered 138 experimental studies with 19,951 participants and found that interventions which increased monitoring of goal progress also improved goal attainment. Effects were larger when monitoring information was physically recorded. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineDoes Monitoring Goal Progress Promote Goal Attainment? A Meta Analysis of the Experimental Evidence. White Ro... For diagnosing routine failures, recording has an additional practical advantage: it leaves individual observations available for comparison rather than compressing them into a later judgement such as “I wasn't very disciplined this month”. But an omission log should not be treated as a causal experiment. If someone misses four Friday workouts, the record establishes a Friday pattern; it does not establish why Fridays are difficult. Workload, social plans, fatigue, transport or the chosen workout time could all be responsible. The record is best treated as a small personal dataset that identifies where to investigate. Nor should one missed session be overinterpreted. Habit formation research indicates that an occasional missed opportunity need not derail the development of automaticity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit formation’ and general practice PMCDecember 1, 2012... Published: December 1, 2012 The more consequential signal is repetition: the same omission appearing under similar circumstances often enough that “bad luck” becomes an increasingly poor description. How to Stick to Habits and Routines Without Falling Off! Channel: How to ADHD Open on YouTube Spotting repeated day and context patterns Day of week differences are real enough that researchers routinely account for them when studying repeated behaviour. In one seven day accelerometer study of middle aged Irish adults, Sunday differed from every other day for sedentary behaviour and light activity, leading the researchers to conclude that day of week should not be ignored when measuring habitual activity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Number of Days Required to Estimate Habitual Activity Using Wrist Worn GENEActiv Accelerometer: A Cross Sectional Stu... UK Biobank accelerometer data from more than 96,000 participants likewise showed differences by time of day and smaller, but measurable, weekday versus weekend differences. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Large Scale Population Assessment of Physical Activity Using Wrist Worn Accelerometers: The UK Biobank Study PMCFeb... These population findings do not tell an individual that their Friday or Sunday is troublesome; they demonstrate why collapsing seven different days into a weekly average can conceal meaningful temporal structure. Similar clustering appears in adherence records. A 16 week behavioural weight loss study found that dietary self monitoring varied significantly across the week: participants were less likely to record their intake from Thursday through Sunday than on Tuesday, with the lowest odds on Saturday and Sunday. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Within week and within year patterns in self monitoring of dietary intake in adults with obesity participating in a b... An outdoor exercise programme produced a different weekday pattern, with its highest attendance on Tuesday and lowest on Friday. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Effect of Affective Exercise Experiences and Environmental Factors on Adherence to Outdoor Exercise Programs PM... The specific “bad day”, in other words, is not universal. That is precisely why an individual's own dated omissions matter more for routine diagnosis than a generic claim that weekends or Mondays are difficult. Place and preceding events can be equally revealing. A qualitative study asked 39 participants to take vitamin C daily for three weeks and then examined how they tried to remember. Some remembering strategies depended heavily on a single cue: one participant, for example, associated the tablet with charging a laptop and missed it when that usual event did not occur. More broadly, loosely structured strategies became vulnerable when participants left home earlier, failed to visit their normal location or otherwise departed from the expected routine. Participants who reported no misses described using multiple cues, whereas those with frequent misses lacked a structured remembering strategy. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That study is small and concerned a simple daily action, so it should not be generalised mechanically to every self improvement routine. Its value is diagnostic: a miss can identify the cue on which a behaviour was unknowingly dependent. “Missed on Tuesday” becomes more informative when Tuesday was also “worked away from home”; several such observations can reveal that the apparent weekday effect is really a location effect. Experimental and longitudinal habit research gives that interpretation stronger support. In a 2022 study, 95 university students built study habits for six weeks while researchers manipulated whether they performed them at stable or changing times and places; a second dataset examined 308 habits recorded by 218 app users. Across the two datasets, greater context stability predicted stronger automaticity and higher attainment of the goal for each repetition. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Open source on frontiersin.org. The practical implication is that missed session analysis should look beyond raw totals. A compact review might ask: Day: Do misses disproportionately fall on Fridays, Sundays or another recurring day? Time: Does the routine work in the morning but repeatedly disappear when postponed until evening? Place: Do sessions happen at home but fail when travelling, working elsewhere or using a different room? Preceding cue: Does the behaviour depend on finishing breakfast, leaving work or another event that is sometimes absent? Competing demand: Do omissions repeatedly coincide with late meetings, childcare, social plans or another predictable commitment? Disruption: Does the routine survive ordinary days but collapse during holidays, travel or schedule changes? The useful unit is not simply “missed”. It is missed under these circumstances . Explanatory illustration 2 Using patterns to revise the routine Once a pattern"},{"title":"Paper vs Digital","url":"/paper-vs-digital/","description":"Paper and digital systems can both preserve useful evidence, but engagement depends on which method people will keep using consistently.","breadcrumb":"What Actually Makes Self Improvement Stick? / Written Records","level":3,"text":"Paper vs Digital Paper or App: Which Habit Record Will Last? Written Records Paper vs Digital Paper vs Digital Paper or App: Which Habit Record Will Last? Paper and digital systems can both preserve useful evidence, but engagement depends on which method people will keep using consistently. Paper and digital systems can both preserve useful evidence, but engagement depends on which method people will keep using consistently. Self Improvement That Works historical comparative bounded topic population context What comparative self monitoring studies found Convenience, visibility and recording friction Choosing the format you will actually maintain self improvement that works written records paper vs digital Introduction Paper and digital habit records can both do the essential job: leave evidence of what you actually did. The more important question is which format will still be receiving entries after the novelty has worn off. Comparative research does not support a simple rule that apps always beat notebooks. Digital systems often improve adherence because they are carried everywhere, can time stamp entries, issue reminders and reduce some of the work of recording. Yet paper records can produce comparable data when people genuinely complete them, and some direct comparisons have found only modest differences between formats. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self Monitoring via Digital Health in Weight Loss Interventions: A Systematic Review Among Adults with Overweight or Obesity PubM... Explanatory illustration 1 The practical criterion, therefore, is persistence rather than sophistication. A beautifully designed tracker that is abandoned after ten days preserves less useful evidence than a pencil mark on a calendar maintained for six months. For self improvement, the best format is the one that makes recording sufficiently easy, visible and trustworthy that the record survives ordinary life. What comparative self monitoring studies found Some of the most striking paper versus digital evidence comes from diary research conducted before smartphones became commonplace. In a 2003 study of 80 adults with chronic pain, participants were asked to record symptoms three times a day for 21 days. The paper binders secretly contained sensors that registered when they were opened, while electronic diaries automatically time stamped entries. Paper participants reported completing 90% of entries within the required time window, but the instrumented binders indicated actual timely compliance of only 11%. On 32% of study days, the paper binder was never opened even though reported compliance for those days exceeded 90%. The electronic group achieved 94% verified compliance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Patient compliance with paper and electronic diaries Patient compliance with paper and electronic diaries PubMed... That experiment illustrates an important advantage of digital records: they can distinguish having a completed record from recording at the intended time . A paper diary can be filled in retrospectively and dated as though it were contemporary. A properly designed electronic system can preserve the actual time of entry and can prevent or expose backfilling. Where the purpose of tracking is to make progress harder to misremember, that difference matters: recreating last Tuesday's record on Friday partly reintroduces the memory problem that tracking was supposed to solve. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Patient non compliance with paper diaries PubMed Central (PMC)Patient non compliance with paper diaries PMCMay 18, 2002... Published: May 18, 2002 But that dramatic result should not be turned into a universal verdict against paper. A later series of three diary studies by Green and colleagues found much more reassuring results for paper and pencil methods. In one study, randomly signalled reports produced completion time patterns that the researchers concluded could not plausibly have been fabricated; in two others, paper and electronic diary data were, with minor exceptions, psychometrically and statistically equivalent. Paper records can therefore work well when people actually use them as intended. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Paper or plastic? Data equivalence in paper and electronic diaries Paper or plastic? Data equivalence in paper and electronic diaries PubMed... Other comparisons have similarly produced less decisive results. A study of people with Parkinson's disease found median completion of 88% with electronic diaries and 98% with paper, although strict adherence to scheduled recording times for the paper group fell to 78%. A 2020 six week randomised trial of 50 younger adults comparing a dietary app with a paper food diary found an average of 18.5 recording days in the app group versus 15.5 with paper, while changes in weight and other anthropometric measures did not differ significantly between groups. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Wireless real time electronic data capture for self assessment of motor function and quality of life in Parkinson's disease PubMed... The fairest reading is that the medium itself is not magic. Digital systems have useful capabilities, particularly time stamps and prompts, but people can maintain good paper records too. What becomes more interesting for self improvement is what happens when tracking continues for months rather than days. Turn Apple Reminders into the Perfect Habit Tracker (Daily Routine Hack) Channel: Nikki • Happy Habits Planners Open on YouTube Digital tracking often wins on sustained use Longer behavioural studies give digital recording a clearer advantage, although much of the strongest comparative evidence comes from weight management research rather than generic habit trackers. The six month My Meal Mate trial in the UK randomly assigned 128 adults to a smartphone app, website or paper diary. Participants using the smartphone recorded their diet for an average of 92 days, compared with 35 days for website users and 29 days for paper diary users. Six month study retention was also substantially higher in the smartphone group: 93%, versus 55% for the website group and 53% for the diary group. Crucially, however, recording declined over time in all three groups . Digital delivery reduced the attrition problem; it did not abolish it. [JMIR] jmir.org Journal of Medical Internet Research Journal of Medical Internet Research Adherence to a Smartphone Application for Weight Loss Compared to Website and Paper Diary: Pil... A larger 24 month trial offers a similarly useful qualification. The SMART trial assigned 210 adults to a paper record, a personal digital assistant (PDA), or a PDA that also supplied daily tailored feedback . Long term weight change did not differ significantly between the three groups, although the electronic groups showed a different adherence trajectory from the paper group. Across formats, participants who maintained substantially greater adherence to self monitoring lost more weight than those who rarely maintained it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Broader reviews point in the same direction. A 2020 meta analysis of 20 studies concluded that mobile health self monitoring interventions had higher adherence than paper recording, including in comparisons extending to six and twelve months. A separate systematic review covering 39 studies found that digital self monitoring engagement was higher than paper in 21 of 34 direct comparisons and lower in only two. Yet it also found that few digital interventions achieved engagement on at least 75% of days. Digital tracking, in other words, usually had the better persistence record without solving the underlying difficulty of sustained self monitoring. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That distinction is central to choosing a personal system. “Apps have higher average adherence in these studies” is not the same proposition as “you personally should use an app”. Population averages identify useful tendencies. A personal tracking system succeeds only if its particular combination of effort, visibility and feedback fits the circumstances in which you have to make the entry. Explanatory illustration 2 Convenience, visibility and recording friction Digital tracking has an obvious convenience advantage: for most smartphone users, the recording device is already present. There is no separate notebook to remember, entries can be made wherever the behaviour occurs, and software can perform work that paper cannot easily perform — calculating totals, storing histories, generating graphs, supplying feedback or prompting an overdue entry. Reviews of technology supported self monitoring have consequently suggested that electronic tools can reduce the burden of recording and support adherence. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The historical comparisons are revealing here because"}]};
